('1a90b305-14f9-5881-86f2-48d7d0f76638'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The normal PaCO2 range is:', '["45-55 mmHg","20-30 mmHg","35-45 mmHg","65-75 mmHg","55-65 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '35-45 mmHg', 'Normal arterial PaCO2 is 35-45 mmHg.'),
('32b4724e-494a-5f6d-851f-63d55b25cbe0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The normal plasma bicarbonate (HCO3-) concentration is:', '["36-40 mEq/L","30-35 mEq/L","16-20 mEq/L","10-15 mEq/L","22-28 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '22-28 mEq/L', 'Normal plasma HCO3- is 22-28 mEq/L.'),
('7ce53869-6306-513e-addd-49af02b9a4de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, Metabolic acidosis is characterized by:', '["Decreased pH, increased HCO3-","Increased pH, increased HCO3-","Increased pH, decreased HCO3-","Normal pH, decreased HCO3-","Decreased pH, decreased HCO3-"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased pH, decreased HCO3-', 'Metabolic acidosis: pH <7.35, HCO3- <22 mEq/L.'),
('9fea9a01-afe8-5d9a-958d-8ce31dc37fe9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In a surgical context, Metabolic alkalosis is characterized by:', '["Decreased pH, decreased HCO3-","Normal pH, increased HCO3-","Increased pH, decreased HCO3-","Decreased pH, increased HCO3-","Increased pH, increased HCO3-"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pH, increased HCO3-', 'Metabolic alkalosis: pH >7.45, HCO3- >28 mEq/L.'),
('d378cce3-269d-5713-b395-8f9c5ac30be2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Respiratory acidosis is characterized by:', '["Increased pH, decreased PaCO2","Decreased pH, increased PaCO2","Normal pH, increased PaCO2","Increased pH, increased PaCO2","Decreased pH, decreased PaCO2"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased pH, increased PaCO2', 'Respiratory acidosis: pH <7.35, PaCO2 >45 mmHg.'),
('c94975ba-ed30-5969-aa08-e1bc93a6462e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'With respect to management, Respiratory alkalosis is characterized by:', '["Decreased pH, increased PaCO2","Normal pH, decreased PaCO2","Increased pH, decreased PaCO2","Increased pH, increased PaCO2","Decreased pH, decreased PaCO2"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pH, decreased PaCO2', 'Respiratory alkalosis: pH >7.45, PaCO2 <35 mmHg.'),
('2fb09d1f-0bb5-5ea5-bd56-fe184d56010d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The kidneys compensate for respiratory acidosis by:', '["Decreasing HCO3- reabsorption","Increasing HCO3- reabsorption and acid excretion","Increasing CO2 elimination","Decreasing NH3 production","Increasing ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing HCO3- reabsorption and acid excretion', 'Kidneys increase acid excretion and HCO3- generation to compensate.'),
('48a7395b-a6d2-5cef-8d89-ff2cecbffc83'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis is:', '["Decreased O2 consumption","Increased CO2 production","No change","Decreased ventilation","Increased ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ventilation', 'Hyperventilation decreases PaCO2 to compensate for low HCO3-.'),
('69cce65b-6398-5bb2-a3f8-8f58dcdaa481'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The respiratory compensation for metabolic alkalosis is:', '["Hypoventilation","Hyperventilation","Increased O2 consumption","No change","Decreased CO2 production"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoventilation', 'Hypoventilation increases PaCO2 to compensate for high HCO3-.'),
('f20704d1-4141-59b5-87fa-8f5186b89435'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The renal response to acidosis includes all EXCEPT:', '["Increased NH4+ excretion","Increased HCO3- reabsorption","Increased H+ secretion","Increased phosphate excretion","Decreased NH3 production"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased NH3 production', 'NH3 production increases, not decreases, in acidosis to buffer H+.'),
('045e3acc-4d25-54b7-9a95-85c5d382782a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Titratable acid in urine consists mainly of:', '["Lactic acid","HCO3-","H2PO4-","NH4+","Free H+"]'::jsonb, 'H2PO4-', 'Phosphate buffers H+ in urine, forming the majority of titratable acid.'),
('4be1ae95-50df-5dd3-9080-72a4b25dd2af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The daily fixed acid load from metabolism is approximately:', '["10 mmol","200-300 mmol","600-700 mmol","400-500 mmol","50-100 mmol"]'::jsonb, '50-100 mmol', 'Normal metabolism produces ~50-100 mmol of non-volatile (fixed) acid per day.'),
('fc6aa56c-da87-52a4-a2e6-460c83c0c263'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The ammonium (NH4+) system buffers H+ in the:', '["Plasma","Renal tubule lumen","ECF","Bone","ICF"]'::jsonb, 'Renal tubule lumen', 'Ammonia (NH3) is secreted into tubular lumen where it buffers H+ to form NH4+.'),
('0d7fc804-37a7-56bc-9a6b-a12ee32b4af7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Renal ammoniagenesis primarily occurs in:', '["Distal tubule","Glomerulus","Proximal tubule","Thick ascending limb","Collecting duct"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal tubule', 'Proximal tubule cells metabolize glutamine to produce NH3 and HCO3-.'),
('5cb1f109-b126-5c76-bf74-e48b58d9a4c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The enzyme responsible for renal ammoniagenesis is:', '["Phosphofructokinase","Aldolase","Hexokinase","Carbonic anhydrase","Glutaminase"]'::jsonb, 'Glutaminase', 'Glutaminase in proximal tubule mitochondria deaminates glutamine to generate NH3.'),
('b4eb4e07-e6cd-50fc-99c5-6c7c4c559af4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'For each molecule of glutamine metabolized in the proximal tubule, how many HCO3- are generated?', '["5","2","4","3","1"]'::jsonb, '2', 'Glutamine metabolism yields 2 NH4+ and 2 HCO3-.'),
('73bdb1bc-66a4-5b5d-a283-05b585d38320'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Anion gap metabolic acidosis occurs in all EXCEPT:', '["Renal tubular acidosis","Lactic acidosis","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Salicylate poisoning","Methanol poisoning"]'::jsonb, 'Renal tubular acidosis', 'RTA causes hyperchloremic (normal anion gap) metabolic acidosis.'),
('184e2f71-2ba4-5960-98ec-98b6f5446416'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis is also called:', '["Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Hypochloremic acidosis","High AG acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis', 'Loss of HCO3- with retention of Cl- gives normal AG, hyperchloremic acidosis.'),
('686a2642-fe09-569b-b5a1-1110fbb3b2a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The most common cause of high anion gap metabolic acidosis is:', '["Lactic acidosis","Diarrhea","Ureterosigmoidostomy","Acetazolamide therapy","Renal tubular acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acidosis', 'Lactic acidosis from tissue hypoxia is the most common high-AG acidosis.'),
('534a4064-c2df-55c7-85f3-2c6a823acb33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Kussmaul breathing is characteristic of:', '["Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis","Normal acid-base","Respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic acidosis', 'Kussmaul breathing is deep, rapid breathing compensating for metabolic acidosis.'),
('e6af55cd-bbff-5077-b051-7ba654dde95c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In proximal renal tubular acidosis (RTA type 2), the defect is in:', '["Proximal tubule HCO3- reabsorption","Aldosterone secretion","Collecting duct H+ secretion","Distal tubule Na+ reabsorption","NH3 production"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal tubule HCO3- reabsorption', 'Type 2 RTA involves impaired proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption.'),
('ee30a3e3-9dbc-596e-8268-60bbbb74b217'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In distal renal tubular acidosis (RTA type 1), the defect is in:', '["Proximal tubule HCO3- reabsorption","Distal tubule H+ secretion","Na+ reabsorption","Aldosterone secretion","NH3 production"]'::jsonb, 'Distal tubule H+ secretion', 'Type 1 RTA is due to impaired H+ secretion in the collecting duct.'),
('cef7c87e-8072-5d66-aa91-0ec1fcf1754a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'A hallmark of distal RTA (type 1) is:', '["Hypokalemia","Urine pH >5.5 despite systemic acidosis","Hyperkalemia","Normal urine pH","Increased NH4+ excretion"]'::jsonb, 'Urine pH >5.5 despite systemic acidosis', 'Inability to acidify urine below pH 5.5 in the presence of acidosis.'),
('41f08ebf-3eaa-5bd1-b991-efc385661b67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Type 4 RTA is associated with:', '["Low aldosterone or aldosterone resistance","Proximal tubule defect mediated physiological mechanism","Glomerular disease mediated physiological mechanism","Cystic kidney disease mediated physiological mechanism","Excessive aldosterone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Low aldosterone or aldosterone resistance', 'Type 4 RTA is due to hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance, causing hyperkalemia and acidosis.'),
('26abef26-2c52-598e-854a-f0f6c1cc7b4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In RTA type 4, the typical finding is:', '["Hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperkalemia', 'Aldosterone deficiency or resistance causes K+ retention (hyperkalemia).'),
('ae8f4b38-2b67-5a67-96cc-b76576cb7d8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The normal daily net acid excretion (NAE) is approximately:', '["30 mEq","150 mEq","70 mEq","100 mEq","10 mEq"]'::jsonb, '70 mEq', 'NAE = titratable acid + NH4+ - HCO3- ~ 70 mEq/day.'),
('bfd9cdc5-32c8-5fc5-b3a7-3fbff6c01224'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The H+ concentration in the collecting duct lumen can be how many times greater than plasma?', '["100x","100,000x","1000x","10x","10,000x"]'::jsonb, '1000x', 'Collecting duct can maintain a pH gradient of 1000:1 (plasma pH 7.4 to urine pH 4.4).'),
('ec75ca3c-c10c-5e15-9b06-a7512b26f7c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In chronic metabolic acidosis, renal NH4+ excretion can increase up to:', '["2 times normal","20 times normal","5 times normal","50 times normal","10 times normal"]'::jsonb, '10 times normal', 'NH4+ excretion can increase ~10-fold in chronic acidosis.'),
('9120bd27-e935-52d2-97f7-b608cb28a1b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The bicarbonate buffer system is effective because:', '["It has no pKa close to pH mediated physiological mechanism","HCO3- is the only buffer mediated physiological mechanism","The components are regulated by lungs and kidneys","It works only in plasma mediated physiological mechanism","It works only in cells mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'The components are regulated by lungs and kidneys', 'The open system allows independent regulation of CO2 (lungs) and HCO3- (kidneys).'),
('a4e8d25a-21af-5221-976a-22230b228a4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation for bicarbonate buffer is:', '["pH = pKa + log","pH = pKa + log","pH = pKa - log","pH = pKa + log","pH = pKa - log"]'::jsonb, 'pH = pKa + log', 'pH = 6.1 + log([HCO3-]/(0.03 x PaCO2)).'),
('33341379-315b-50fe-8a1e-e0b7b361b599'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The base excess in arterial blood gases normally ranges from:', '["-10 to -5 mEq/L","-5 to -2 mEq/L","+2 to +5 mEq/L","-2 to +2 mEq/L","+5 to +10 mEq/L"]'::jsonb, '-2 to +2 mEq/L', 'Normal base excess is -2 to +2 mEq/L.'),
('819cc69d-2e45-5de1-89f8-3039e8f850f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'A negative base excess indicates:', '["Normal acid-base","Respiratory compensation","Alkalosis","Renal compensation","Acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Acidosis', 'Negative BE (base deficit) indicates metabolic acidosis.'),
('ce4839c5-fe6b-572d-a941-6e8bcb4e1e77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Winter''s formula (PaCO2 = 1.5 x [HCO3-] + 8 +/- 2) is used to assess:', '["Mixed acid-base disorders","Respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis","Anion gap","Oxygenation","Renal compensation for respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis', 'Winter''s formula predicts expected PaCO2 in pure metabolic acidosis.'),
('9a436956-de8b-54f2-ab2b-96ae134b2a9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In compensated metabolic acidosis, the pH:', '["Is completely normal","Becomes alkalotic","Remains slightly acidic","Is unpredictable","Returns to exactly 7.40"]'::jsonb, 'Remains slightly acidic', 'Complete compensation does not return pH to 7.40; it remains slightly acidic.'),
('58f4e51e-b625-5477-9a3c-93218ab7f8fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Which of the following is a cause of metabolic alkalosis?', '["Lactic acidosis","Renal failure","Vomiting","Diarrhea","Diabetic ketoacidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Vomiting', 'Vomiting causes loss of gastric HCl, leading to metabolic alkalosis.'),
('89718ce9-96c4-5213-8959-90ab37b937ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Which of the following is a cause of non-anion gap metabolic acidosis?', '["Diarrhea","Ethylene glycol poisoning","Salicylate poisoning","Diabetic ketoacidosis","Lactic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Diarrhea', 'Diarrhea causes loss of HCO3- with Cl- retention, giving normal AG acidosis.'),
('2f5f5bae-0e5c-5a21-ae0a-625ccf16e345'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Salicylate poisoning in adults typically causes:', '["Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory acidosis with metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis","No acid-base disturbance","Mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis', 'Salicylates stimulate respiratory center (resp alkalosis) and cause organic acid production (metabolic acidosis).'),
('73ff1f41-ad36-5fe6-8c70-cf488bb91c83'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In a patient with metabolic alkalosis, if the urine chloride is <10 mEq/L, this indicates:', '["Chloride-resistant alkalosis","Renal failure","Chloride-responsive alkalosis","Adrenal insufficiency","SIADH"]'::jsonb, 'Chloride-responsive alkalosis', 'Low urine chloride indicates volume contraction and responds to NaCl administration.'),
('b91b6e32-2208-5041-a319-7b2481cccc46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In a patient with metabolic alkalosis, if the urine chloride is >20 mEq/L, this indicates:', '["Chloride-responsive alkalosis","Volume depletion","Chloride-resistant alkalosis","Diuretic use","Vomiting"]'::jsonb, 'Chloride-resistant alkalosis', 'High urine chloride suggests mineralocorticoid excess (e.g., Conn syndrome).'),
('e37d3245-4412-50c3-9bb0-3544162d083d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The delta-delta ratio in high AG metabolic acidosis is used to detect:', '["Renal compensation","Simple acidosis","Concomitant metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Concomitant metabolic alkalosis', 'Delta AG / Delta HCO3- ratio helps detect mixed anion gap acidosis and metabolic alkalosis.'),
('2aa281a7-8890-52c6-b987-9e9a10b38496'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The normal adult bladder capacity is approximately:', '["100-200 mL","700-900 mL","1000-1200 mL","400-600 mL","250-300 mL"]'::jsonb, '400-600 mL', 'Normal bladder capacity is ~400-600 mL.'),
('bee5b8e7-72a8-57e8-93b1-a58def9e48b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The first sensation of bladder filling occurs at approximately:', '["100-150 mL","500-600 mL","300-400 mL","50 mL","200-250 mL"]'::jsonb, '100-150 mL', 'First sensation of filling is at ~100-150 mL.'),
('e40b6755-da5c-53c5-b50a-e66ff82bc35d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'A strong urge to void occurs at bladder volume of approximately:', '["100-150 mL","300-400 mL","200-250 mL","600-700 mL","400-500 mL"]'::jsonb, '300-400 mL', 'Urge to void becomes strong at ~300-400 mL.'),
('373eb473-048f-5204-9fa8-24885eaff4db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The detrusor muscle of the urinary bladder is composed of:', '["Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle","Striated muscle","Smooth muscle","Mixed smooth and skeletal"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle', 'The detrusor is smooth muscle innervated by parasympathetic nerves.'),
('157f11ed-631d-5ec4-ab01-06be0a1d8a49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The internal urethral sphincter is composed of:', '["Striated muscle","Elastic tissue","Smooth muscle","Skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth muscle', 'The internal sphincter is smooth muscle (bladder neck) under sympathetic control.'),
('33424f5f-2796-5127-85f1-9db59363d9d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The external urethral sphincter is composed of:', '["Elastic tissue","Smooth muscle","Skeletal (striated) muscle","Fibrous tissue","Cardiac muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Skeletal (striated) muscle', 'The external sphincter is voluntary skeletal muscle.'),
('725c421b-ab73-5f85-a97b-71ded7dfa860'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The micturition reflex center is located in the:', '["Cerebral cortex","Sacral spinal cord","Thoracic spinal cord","Medulla oblongata","Pontine micturition center"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral spinal cord', 'The sacral micturition center at S2-S4 is the spinal reflex center.'),
('838a85bb-fed4-53df-bfec-c4d586e227bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The pontine micturition center (PMC) is also known as:', '["Red nucleus","Locus coeruleus","Substantia nigra","Barrington''s nucleus","Raphe nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Barrington''s nucleus', 'Barrington''s nucleus in the pons coordinates the micturition reflex.'),
('9474c506-6e68-55e2-8f65-b4bd1968b8de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Parasympathetic innervation of the bladder arises from:', '["S2-S4","T1-T5","L4-S1","T10-L2","C8-T1"]'::jsonb, 'S2-S4', 'Parasympathetic (pelvic nerve) arises from sacral segments S2-S4.'),
('07e41c21-6d19-5dda-a028-fb8f0ba167b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Sympathetic innervation of the bladder arises from:', '["S2-S4","T1-T5","T10-L2","L4-S1","C8-T1"]'::jsonb, 'T10-L2', 'Sympathetic (hypogastric nerve) arises from T10-L2.'),
('ca4caeaa-aa62-5b21-96f9-2567562def22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Somatic innervation of the external urethral sphincter is via:', '["Phrenic nerve","Pudendal nerve","Vagus nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Pelvic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pudendal nerve', 'The pudendal nerve (S2-S4) supplies the external urethral sphincter.'),
('651ce53d-03e6-559f-a1c5-a3a25465f214'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Sympathetic stimulation of the bladder causes:', '["Both detrusor and sphincter contraction","Both detrusor and sphincter relaxation","Detrusor relaxation, sphincter contraction","No effect","Detrusor contraction, sphincter relaxation"]'::jsonb, 'Detrusor relaxation, sphincter contraction', 'Sympathetic activation promotes urine storage.'),
('f2af1dca-b781-5bcc-9616-e2035ca5af54'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Parasympathetic stimulation of the bladder causes:', '["No effect","Both contraction","Detrusor contraction and sphincter relaxation","Both relaxation","Detrusor relaxation and sphincter contraction"]'::jsonb, 'Detrusor contraction and sphincter relaxation', 'Parasympathetic (ACh, M3 receptors) contracts detrusor and relaxes sphincter.'),
('5c685b70-9f5c-5668-a860-f32e3d5436f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The neurotransmitter at the neuromuscular junction of the detrusor is:', '["Dopamine","Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Acetylcholine","Serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine', 'Parasympathetic nerves release ACh acting on M3 muscarinic receptors on detrusor.'),
('5b5da24c-440c-5bfb-96b2-9298d7edfbe1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The bladder stretches to accommodate urine without pressure rise due to:', '["Parasympathetic inhibition","Reflex inhibition","Active relaxation","Sympathetic activation","Passive properties of smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Passive properties of smooth muscle', 'Bladder compliance allows filling without significant pressure increase.'),
('c389762a-cfb0-54ee-a92e-35850dcff1f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'During bladder filling, PMC is inhibited by:', '["Cerebral cortex and periaqueductal gray","Parasympathetic discharge","Sympathetic discharge","Spinal reflexes","Sacral afferents from bladder"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebral cortex and periaqueductal gray', 'Cortical and PAG inputs inhibit PMC during filling.'),
('62a503ab-18c4-537d-a335-9a99ceeffe98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Micturition is initiated when bladder afferents signal the PMC via:', '["Pudendal nerve","Phrenic nerve","Hypogastric nerve","Pelvic nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic nerve', 'Stretch receptors in bladder wall send signals via pelvic nerve to spinal cord and PMC.'),
('0ec50e0c-0eb4-5068-bc64-6c9aa837b28a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'In infants, micturition occurs as a:', '["Sympathetic reflex","Chemical reflex","Somatic reflex","Spinal reflex without cortical control","Voluntary reflex under cortical control"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal reflex without cortical control', 'Infants have an uninhibited spinal micturition reflex until cortical maturation.'),
('491fbc24-dfcc-52d9-b29d-f34d30d32934'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia refers to:', '["Simultaneous contraction of detrusor and external sphincter","Coordination of detrusor and sphincter","Only detrusor contraction","Only sphincter relaxation","Flaccid bladder"]'::jsonb, 'Simultaneous contraction of detrusor and external sphincter', 'This is a common problem in spinal cord injury patients.'),
('7d4574ff-10f4-55af-89b2-620d595221d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'A lesion above the pontine micturition center typically causes:', '["Flaccid bladder","Automatic (reflex) bladder","Uninhibited bladder","Atonic bladder","Normal function"]'::jsonb, 'Uninhibited bladder', 'Suprapontine lesions cause loss of cortical inhibition, leading to overactive bladder.'),
('bbfa199f-7a04-5319-b0e1-f6331ea27a68'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'A complete spinal cord transection above S2 level initially causes:', '["Normal bladder","Flaccid bladder","Spastic bladder","Uninhibited bladder","Autonomic dysreflexia"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid bladder', 'Initial spinal shock causes flaccid bladder, later becoming spastic/automatic.'),
('cf83d3f5-f8be-5182-a904-6b0fa8beb371'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'After recovery from spinal shock in a lesion above S2, the bladder becomes:', '["Flaccid and atonic","Uninhibited","Obstructed","Spastic","Normal"]'::jsonb, 'Spastic', 'Over several weeks, the sacral reflex returns, producing automatic voiding.'),
('0571fc37-914a-5ab3-a76a-9af909aa3476'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'A lesion in the sacral spinal cord (S2-S4) causes:', '["Overactive bladder","Uninhibited bladder","Normal function","Flaccid bladder","Spastic bladder"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid bladder', 'Sacral lesions destroy the parasympathetic center, causing a flaccid, non-contractile bladder.'),
('1250a89f-5d24-5087-9fa8-6cf6706d9738'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Autonomic dysreflexia is a complication of spinal cord injury above:', '["T10","T12","T2","T6","C5"]'::jsonb, 'T6', 'Lesions above T6 can cause autonomic dysreflexia with severe hypertension due to unopposed sympathetic discharge.'),
('ef81bfb8-1549-5c3a-b4dd-1a6c28057f5c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The sign of autonomic dysreflexia is:', '["Hypotension and tachycardia","Hypertension and bradycardia","Hypotension and bradycardia","Flushing and sweating","Hypertension and tachycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension and bradycardia', 'Massive sympathetic reflex causes hypertension, with baroreflex-mediated bradycardia.'),
('680516bb-b58f-5db5-82e2-36c48bb8d75d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Post-void residual volume is normally:', '["0 mL","100-150 mL","150-200 mL","50-100 mL","<50 mL"]'::jsonb, '<50 mL', 'Normal post-void residual is <50 mL.'),
('20eba580-f18f-59b1-852c-2ef4f8e0e69b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'An overactive bladder is characterized by:', '["Weak detrusor contraction","Detrusor overactivity during filling","Sphincter spasm","Low bladder compliance","High post-void residual"]'::jsonb, 'Detrusor overactivity during filling', 'OAB involves involuntary detrusor contractions during filling phase.'),
('e6640df5-0766-5c08-9aac-f401f9e9317f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Stress urinary incontinence is caused by:', '["Detrusor overactivity","Overflow incontinence","Bladder outlet obstruction","Urethral sphincter weakness","Neurogenic bladder"]'::jsonb, 'Urethral sphincter weakness', 'Stress incontinence is due to poor urethral support/weakness.'),
('eefb83d4-19f4-55cb-b414-5898acbd2792'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Urge incontinence is associated with:', '["Detrusor overactivity","Urethral stricture","Neurogenic bladder","Bladder outlet obstruction","Sphincter weakness"]'::jsonb, 'Detrusor overactivity', 'Urge incontinence is due to involuntary detrusor contractions during filling.'),
('3472f1fe-0b33-5bbf-85c1-bba81a5f5782'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Overflow incontinence occurs due to:', '["Pregnancy mediated physiological mechanism","Bladder outlet obstruction or detrusor underactivity","Detrusor overactivity mediated physiological mechanism","Urinary tract infection mediated physiological mechanism","Sphincter weakness mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Bladder outlet obstruction or detrusor underactivity', 'Overflow incontinence is leakage from an overdistended, poorly emptying bladder.'),
('e4b1189c-3e79-5f3b-b49f-c680b2729026'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Erythropoietin (EPO) is produced primarily by:', '["Podocytes","Glomerular cells","Peritubular capillary endothelial cells","Interstitial fibroblasts in renal cortex","Mesangial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Interstitial fibroblasts in renal cortex', 'EPO is produced by peritubular interstitial fibroblasts in the renal cortex.'),
('fd5f1754-bde6-5723-922f-171c29fca5e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The primary stimulus for erythropoietin secretion is:', '["Tissue hypoxia","High potassium","Low sodium","Low blood pH","High blood pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue hypoxia', 'Hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF) upregulates EPO gene expression.'),
('5a4a787d-f509-5575-a717-4868ce43f390'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Erythropoietin acts on:', '["Lymphoid progenitor cells","Erythroid progenitor cells","Megakaryocytes","Myeloid progenitor cells","Neutrophil precursors"]'::jsonb, 'Erythroid progenitor cells', 'EPO promotes survival, proliferation, and differentiation of erythroid progenitors.'),
('8c42be0e-9dc4-5fcd-af20-f543f09d9cdf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Recombinant EPO is used to treat:', '["Leukemia mediated physiological mechanism","Thrombocytopenia mediated physiological mechanism","Anemia of chronic kidney disease","Polycythemia vera mediated physiological mechanism","Hemophilia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Anemia of chronic kidney disease', 'EPO deficiency in CKD causes anemia; recombinant EPO is replacement therapy.'),
('a2e61aba-8ba2-5844-a34d-51ee38f1619b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Renin is an enzyme that converts:', '["Aldosterone to angiotensin","Bradykinin to angiotensin","Angiotensin I to angiotensin II","Angiotensinogen to angiotensin I","Angiotensin II to angiotensin III"]'::jsonb, 'Angiotensinogen to angiotensin I', 'Renin cleaves angiotensinogen (from liver) to form angiotensin I.'),
('c9371f75-5201-5223-a1f5-8da0e3e8b394'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Angiotensin II (AT II) directly causes all EXCEPT:', '["Decreased thirst","Aldosterone secretion","Increased proximal tubular Na+ reabsorption","Increased ADH secretion","Vasoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased thirst', 'AT II strongly stimulates thirst, not decreases it.'),
('630236ac-c8ad-5de3-8dd1-452887da31e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Angiotensin II acts on which receptor to cause vasoconstriction?', '["AT1 receptor","AT2 receptor","Mas receptor","AT3 receptor","AT4 receptor"]'::jsonb, 'AT1 receptor', 'AT1 receptors mediate vasoconstriction, aldosterone secretion, and cell proliferation.'),
('2c2e501c-09a2-5447-a906-278ad4daa771'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'ACE inhibitors cause:', '["Increased angiotensin II and decreased bradykinin","Decreased angiotensin II and increased bradykinin","No change in either","Decreased angiotensin II and decreased bradykinin","Increased angiotensin II and increased bradykinin"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased angiotensin II and increased bradykinin', 'ACE degrades bradykinin, so ACE inhibitors increase bradykinin.'),
('94c3d7a2-84bf-56f3-b987-4934ec0a580a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Bradykinin causes:', '["Platelet aggregation mediated physiological mechanism","Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability","Sodium retention mediated physiological mechanism","Bronchoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism","Vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation and increased vascular permeability', 'Bradykinin is a vasodilator that increases capillary permeability.'),
('e2bf8322-5c4c-5abf-9947-a3c6f054341f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Kallikrein is produced in the kidney and acts on:', '["ACE","Aldosterone","Angiotensinogen","Kinogen","Renin"]'::jsonb, 'Kinogen', 'Renal kallikrein cleaves kininogen to form bradykinin.'),
('c7fca8cb-557a-51b0-847f-42d2f0d2e003'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', '1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol) is produced in the kidney by:', '["Proximal tubule cells","Podocytes","Collecting duct cells","Distal tubule cells","Mesangial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal tubule cells', 'The 1-alpha-hydroxylase enzyme in proximal tubule converts 25-OH-D3 to 1,25-(OH)2-D3.'),
('b391aa56-321a-5c7a-91bb-c08fe1f4175e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The enzyme 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the kidney is stimulated by:', '["High calcium","PTH and low phosphate","Calcitonin","High phosphate","Vitamin D excess"]'::jsonb, 'PTH and low phosphate', 'PTH stimulates and low phosphate enhances 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity.'),
('7d7d77db-726e-5455-8a8f-bf071f827bee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The major function of calcitriol is:', '["Increase calcium absorption from gut","Decrease bone resorption","Decrease calcium reabsorption in kidney","Increase phosphate excretion","Decrease calcium absorption from gut"]'::jsonb, 'Increase calcium absorption from gut', 'Calcitriol increases intestinal absorption of Ca2+ and phosphate.'),
('88267ee5-1ce3-565c-a382-8d363130f466'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Renal osteodystrophy in CKD is primarily due to:', '["Hypercalcemia","Increased calcitriol production","Alkaline phosphatase deficiency","PTH deficiency","Decreased calcitriol production and hyperphosphatemia"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased calcitriol production and hyperphosphatemia', 'In CKD, decreased 1-alpha-hydroxylase leads to low calcitriol, contributing to bone disease.'),
('ec43d252-b4e7-5e74-940d-3914f7407138'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'FGF23 (fibroblast growth factor 23) is produced by:', '["Osteocytes","Thyroid","Liver","Parathyroid","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Osteocytes', 'Osteocytes secrete FGF23 to regulate phosphate and vitamin D metabolism.'),
('67c38944-d5e9-50ef-92e3-deed3a374889'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'FGF23 acts on the kidney to:', '["Increase phosphate reabsorption and increase calcitriol","Increase calcium reabsorption","Increase potassium secretion","Decrease sodium reabsorption","Decrease phosphate reabsorption and decrease calcitriol"]'::jsonb, 'Decrease phosphate reabsorption and decrease calcitriol', 'FGF23 promotes phosphate excretion and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase.'),
('c70f75fc-b23b-5afd-b9cd-479c9cfb965c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Klotho is a co-receptor for:', '["Erythropoietin","Renin","PTH","FGF23","Calcitriol"]'::jsonb, 'FGF23', 'Klotho is an essential co-receptor for FGF23 signaling in the kidney.'),
('0b4e551a-444f-57ca-a775-51b4cdb9ea22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Prostaglandins in the kidney are synthesized primarily in:', '["Distal tubule","Proximal tubule","Afferent arteriole","Glomerulus","Renal medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Renal medulla', 'PGE2 and PGI2 are produced in the medulla and have vasodilatory effects.'),
('6d3276f5-fd2d-54d5-a993-1d9e95fa386a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Renal prostaglandins (PGE2) cause:', '["Increased water reabsorption","Vasoconstriction and decreased GFR","Increased sodium reabsorption","Increased potassium secretion","Vasodilation and increased GFR"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation and increased GFR', 'PGE2 is a vasodilator that increases renal blood flow and GFR.'),
('5851ac19-e754-536c-b99b-3f8e0e8e4d31'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'NSAIDs can cause renal impairment by:', '["Inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis","Increasing renin release","Decreasing ADH","Increasing prostaglandin synthesis","Increasing angiotensin II"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis', 'NSAIDs block COX, reducing renal prostaglandins that maintain GFR in hypovolemia.'),
('359f4569-06b1-5fc0-81c8-a92ac094fb58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Endothelin is a powerful:', '["Hormone produced by heart","Vasodilator produced by endothelium","Neurotransmitter","Vasoconstrictor produced by endothelium","Enzyme produced by kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstrictor produced by endothelium', 'Endothelin-1 from vascular endothelium is a potent vasoconstrictor.'),
('2b298a6b-e382-553d-ace1-a17412180766'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Nitric oxide (NO) in the kidney causes:', '["Vasoconstriction","Water reabsorption","Vasodilation","Sodium retention","Potassium secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation', 'NO from endothelial NOS (eNOS) causes vasodilation, important for renal blood flow.'),
('59eb7981-2910-5c22-9455-cac53eeb7c6b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Urodilatin is a member of which peptide family?', '["Kinin family","Growth factor family","Natriuretic peptide family","Angiotensin family","Endothelin family"]'::jsonb, 'Natriuretic peptide family', 'Urodilatin is a renal natriuretic peptide produced in the kidney.'),
('614961de-b528-5eb3-8479-87f3e02101f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Urodilatin is produced in:', '["Kidney","Heart","Liver","Lungs","Brain"]'::jsonb, 'Kidney', 'Urodilatin is synthesized in distal tubular cells and acts locally.'),
('b39dde71-65f9-558b-aa5a-b67f23848082'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Urodilatin''s primary effect is:', '["Antidiuresis","Calcium absorption","Vasoconstriction","Potassium retention","Natriuresis and diuresis"]'::jsonb, 'Natriuresis and diuresis', 'Urodilatin binds to NPR-A receptors, increasing cGMP and promoting Na+ and water excretion.'),
('75dea7ee-cf4e-5598-9410-d6c0df1f4e16'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Adrenomedullin is produced in the kidney and causes:', '["Vasodilation and natriuresis","Antidiuresis","Renin release","Vasoconstriction","Erythropoiesis"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation and natriuresis', 'Adrenomedullin is a vasodilator peptide with natriuretic effects.'),
('a8799f56-ef8f-51ef-8184-50477f7161be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The renal sympathetic nervous system causes all EXCEPT:', '["Vasodilation","Vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles","Increased sodium reabsorption directly","Decreased GFR","Increased renin release"]'::jsonb, 'Vasodilation', 'Renal sympathetic activation causes vasoconstriction, decreases GFR, and increases Na+ reabsorption.'),
('96505ca0-9595-5b4e-a4b0-30210c8ddf41'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Beta-1-adrenergic receptors on JG cells stimulate:', '["Calcitriol synthesis","ADH secretion","Renin release","Aldosterone secretion","Erythropoietin secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Renin release', 'Beta-1 receptors on JG cells are activated by sympathetic nerves to increase renin release.'),
('6a4db474-bb38-567a-8e8a-53563362a608'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The renal dopamine system promotes:', '["Water retention","Sodium retention","Sodium excretion","Potassium secretion","Calcium absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium excretion', 'Renal dopamine increases Na+ excretion by inhibiting Na+/K+ ATPase and NHE3.'),
('59ea468d-b163-508f-a85d-446a5d23a347'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The primary site of ammonia (NH3) production in the kidney is:', '["Glomerulus","Proximal tubule","Thick ascending limb","Collecting duct","Distal convoluted tubule"]'::jsonb, 'Proximal tubule', 'Proximal tubular cells produce NH3 from glutamine metabolism.'),
('552eee18-dfe4-54e5-a623-c035f484d418'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'The enzyme glutaminase in the kidney is induced by:', '["High sodium","Acidosis","High calcium","Alkalosis","High potassium"]'::jsonb, 'Acidosis', 'Chronic acidosis increases glutaminase expression to enhance NH3 production.'),
('efbb1467-c0c5-599c-835d-5952f2c46d42'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Which hormone increases calcium reabsorption in the kidneys?', '["PTH","ADH","Calcitonin","ANP","Aldosterone"]'::jsonb, 'PTH', 'PTH increases Ca2+ reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule.'),
('b7b8f714-4a34-5f44-b979-f35130bff2dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Which hormone decreases calcium reabsorption in the kidneys?', '["Aldosterone","ADH","Calcitonin","PTH","Calcitriol"]'::jsonb, 'Calcitonin', 'Calcitonin decreases Ca2+ reabsorption, opposing PTH effects.'),
('65471f65-dc14-5974-8556-f43bc2c1b81a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Renal and Body Fluids', 'Magnesium reabsorption primarily occurs in:', '["Distal convoluted tubule","Proximal tubule","Glomerulus","Thick ascending limb","Collecting duct"]'::jsonb, 'Thick ascending limb', 'About 60-70% of Mg2+ is reabsorbed in the TAL via paracellular route.'),
('ac6b6af3-59e6-55b6-8c68-1a1a5b6da26e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is the outermost layer covering the brain and spinal cord?', '["Ependyma","Periosteum","Arachnoid mater","Pia mater","Dura mater"]'::jsonb, 'Dura mater', 'The dura mater is the outermost, toughest layer of the meninges. It has two layers in the cranium (periosteal and meningeal) and one layer in the spinal cord.'),
('077d4f1c-3444-5e55-b826-077b4348a671'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The blood-brain barrier is primarily formed by which of the following?', '["Microglia mediated physiological mechanism","Tight junctions between endothelial cells of brain capillaries","Pericytes mediated physiological mechanism","Astrocytes mediated physiological mechanism","Oligodendrocytes mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between endothelial cells of brain capillaries', 'Tight junctions (zonulae occludentes) between cerebral capillary endothelial cells form the structural basis of the blood-brain barrier. Astrocytes contribute via foot processes but do not form the primary barrier.'),
('230bed41-5888-5a8d-9755-95def4afc123'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which structure is responsible for the production of cerebrospinal fluid?', '["Sylvian fissure","Choroid plexus","Arachnoid granulations","Foramen of Luschka","Cerebral aqueduct"]'::jsonb, 'Choroid plexus', 'The choroid plexus (found in lateral, third, and fourth ventricles) produces CSF by active secretion at a rate of ~500 mL/day. Arachnoid granulations absorb CSF.'),
('75fb54bf-8ce0-52b5-9cae-309c2a7e053b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The internal capsule is a white matter structure that contains:', '["Projection fibers","Arcuate fibers","Association fibers","Commissural fibers","Commissural and association fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Projection fibers', 'The internal capsule contains projection fibers connecting the cerebral cortex with subcortical structures (thalamus, brainstem, spinal cord). It is divided into anterior limb, genu, and posterior limb.'),
('4b25445a-debc-557e-bdff-449857af6918'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is NOT a part of the brainstem?', '["Midbrain","Medulla oblongata","Pons","Cerebellum","Medulla and pons"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellum', 'The cerebellum is a separate structure posterior to the brainstem. The brainstem consists of the medulla oblongata, pons, and midbrain.'),
('fb1b7fb6-c009-5451-b890-4ce6f9737201'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The central sulcus separates which two lobes of the brain?', '["Frontal and parietal","Parietal and occipital","Temporal and occipital","Frontal and temporal","Frontal and occipital"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal and parietal', 'The central sulcus (fissure of Rolando) separates the frontal lobe (anteriorly) from the parietal lobe (posteriorly). The precentral gyrus is motor, postcentral gyrus is sensory.'),
('64981261-45ea-5583-9e56-65804f493e72'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The insula (insula of Reil) is located deep within which fissure?', '["Sylvian fissure","Calcarine sulcus","Central sulcus","Cingulate sulcus","Parieto-occipital sulcus"]'::jsonb, 'Sylvian fissure', 'The insula lies deep within the lateral sulcus (Sylvian fissure). It is covered by the opercula of the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes.'),
('1696a2b5-947a-5a82-b0b4-f7f7b4eea8dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Cerebrospinal fluid circulates from the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle through:', '["Sylvian aqueduct","Cerebral aqueduct","Foramen of Magendie","Foramen of Luschka","Foramen of Monro"]'::jsonb, 'Foramen of Monro', 'CSF flows from lateral ventricles to foramen of Monro to third ventricle to cerebral aqueduct to fourth ventricle to foramina of Luschka and Magendie to subarachnoid space.'),
('6a415e7e-ee9d-5c7e-8b6c-57d787a60674'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The major commissure connecting the two cerebral hemispheres is the:', '["Habenular commissure","Fornix","Anterior commissure","Internal capsule","Corpus callosum"]'::jsonb, 'Corpus callosum', 'The corpus callosum is the largest commissural fiber tract, connecting corresponding regions of the two cerebral hemispheres. It is divided into rostrum, genu, body, and splenium.'),
('f4339897-073c-5665-baf6-92ad6a0fa6aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The percentage of total body oxygen consumed by the brain is approximately:', '["10%","30%","40%","5%","20%"]'::jsonb, '20%', 'The brain accounts for about 2% of body weight but consumes approximately 20% of total body oxygen and 15-20% of cardiac output.'),
('31255c73-486a-5ca3-b2f2-300ceea4fcad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which glial cell type myelinates axons in the central nervous system?', '["Ependymal cells","Schwann cells","Astrocytes","Microglia","Oligodendrocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Oligodendrocytes', 'Oligodendrocytes myelinate CNS axons (one oligodendrocyte can myelinate multiple axons). Schwann cells myelinate PNS axons.'),
('538b29f4-c3b9-507a-811f-fec013d16849'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The functional unit of the nervous system is the:', '["Neuron","Dendrite","Axon","Synapse","Neuroglia"]'::jsonb, 'Neuron', 'The neuron is the fundamental structural and functional unit of the nervous system, specialized for the transmission of electrical impulses.'),
('815ba65c-66f0-5aed-90b5-f8abe866dfc2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Grey matter in the spinal cord is organized into:', '["Lemnisei","Pedicels","Horns and columns","Capsules","Tracts and fasciculi"]'::jsonb, 'Horns and columns', 'Spinal cord grey matter is organized into dorsal (sensory), lateral, and ventral (motor) horns. These are also referred to as columns (Rexed laminae I-X).'),
('87d12528-03cc-517d-b569-2ee47c87ebdf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The subarachnoid space lies between which two layers?', '["Pia and brain tissue","Dura and bone","Arachnoid and pia","Epidural space and dura","Dura and arachnoid"]'::jsonb, 'Arachnoid and pia', 'The subarachnoid space is between the arachnoid mater (outer) and pia mater (inner). It contains CSF and major cerebral blood vessels.'),
('e7e405bb-7b44-512a-b9c1-11b152235a30'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Circle of Willis is formed by anastomosis of which arteries?', '["Internal carotid and ophthalmic arteries","Basilar and facial arteries","Internal carotid and vertebral arteries","Vertebral and maxillary arteries","External carotid and internal carotid"]'::jsonb, 'Internal carotid and vertebral arteries', 'The Circle of Willis is formed by anastomosis between branches of internal carotid arteries (anterior circulation) and vertebral arteries (posterior circulation) via posterior communicating arteries.'),
('3e5df1e2-ef95-544f-8132-0a2687dcb370'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway is primarily responsible for carrying which type of sensation?', '["Fine touch, vibration, and proprioception","Temperature only","Pain and temperature","Crude touch and pressure","Itch and tickle"]'::jsonb, 'Fine touch, vibration, and proprioception', 'The dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway carries discriminative touch, vibration, and conscious proprioception. It decussates at the medulla (internal arcuate fibers).'),
('5b8338a8-3b1a-5b4a-aef8-8336d2aaf624'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature sensation. Where does it decussate?', '["Medulla","Pons","Midbrain","Spinal cord","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord', 'The spinothalamic tract decussates within 1-2 segments of entry in the spinal cord via the anterior white commissure.'),
('6b54ab9f-3178-5ed8-b847-724ba6767910'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'All of the following sensations are carried by the spinothalamic tract EXCEPT:', '["Temperature","Crude touch","Pain","Vibration sense","Tickle"]'::jsonb, 'Vibration sense', 'Vibration sense is carried by the dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway, NOT the spinothalamic tract.'),
('4651fd88-a878-5b28-bb30-8adcc11cad87'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The third-order neuron of the dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway is located in the:', '["Somatosensory cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Inferior olivary nucleus mediated physiological mechanism","Nucleus gracilis/cuneatus mediated physiological mechanism","Dorsal root ganglion mediated physiological mechanism","Ventral posterolateral (VPL) nucleus of thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral posterolateral (VPL) nucleus of thalamus', 'First-order neurons are in dorsal root ganglia, second-order in nucleus gracilis/cuneatus, and third-order in VPL thalamus, which projects to the somatosensory cortex.'),
('305ccc0f-098e-57fa-bd8a-5b709f9806bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Its third-order neurons are located in the:', '["Ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus of thalamus","Ventral posterolateral (VPL) nucleus of thalamus","Pulvinar","Lateral geniculate body","Medial geniculate body"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral posteromedial (VPM) nucleus of thalamus', 'The trigeminothalamic pathway (trigeminal lemniscus) carries facial sensation. Third-order neurons are in the VPM nucleus.'),
('14ca061a-4f4c-5e8d-bc93-d7801f408f96'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which thalamic nucleus is the main sensory relay for the visual system?', '["Lateral geniculate body","Medial geniculate body","VPL","VPM","Pulvinar"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral geniculate body', 'The lateral geniculate body (LGB) is the thalamic relay nucleus for vision.'),
('9ef32ad3-a014-5064-be37-5b14f4f4ffb2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The medial geniculate body (MGB) is the thalamic relay for which sensory system?', '["Olfactory","Auditory","Vision","Gustatory","Somatosensory"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory', 'The MGB receives auditory information from the inferior colliculus and projects to the auditory cortex.'),
('89b375f2-191d-52ac-a638-0dbfdb7caaf9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with a lesion in the left dorsal column at the cervical level would present with:', '["Ipsilateral loss of all sensation below the lesion","Loss of pain and temperature on the left side below the lesion","Loss of fine touch and proprioception on the left side below the lesion","Loss of fine touch and proprioception on the right side below the lesion","Loss of pain and temperature on the right side below the lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of fine touch and proprioception on the left side below the lesion', 'The dorsal columns carry ipsilateral fine touch and proprioception (they decussate at the medulla). A left dorsal column lesion causes loss of these modalities on the left side below the lesion.'),
('fd129dc5-7ba2-5f02-9c88-59c9dd7192c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following tracts carries unconscious proprioception to the cerebellum?', '["Spinothalamic tract","Dorsal column","Corticospinal tract","Rubrospinal tract","Spinocerebellar tract"]'::jsonb, 'Spinocerebellar tract', 'The spinocerebellar tract carries unconscious proprioceptive information to the cerebellum.'),
('c9769598-267f-5842-8367-304b7feed86d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The dorsal spinocerebellar tract originates from which nucleus?', '["Nucleus gracilis","Clarke''s nucleus","Dorsal root ganglion","Inferior olivary nucleus","Nucleus cuneatus"]'::jsonb, 'Clarke''s nucleus', 'Clarke''s nucleus (nucleus dorsalis) at T1-L2 gives rise to the dorsal spinocerebellar tract.'),
('dabe7605-15f5-5a24-86cb-b609fd5ace9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'All of the following are functions of the thalamus EXCEPT:', '["Regulation of consciousness and alertness","Relay of sensory information to the cortex","Processing of olfactory information before relay to cortex","Involvement in motor pathways","Memory consolidation"]'::jsonb, 'Processing of olfactory information before relay to cortex', 'Olfaction is the only sensory system that does NOT relay through the thalamus.'),
('668765d2-27cb-5885-b6d0-785381edb759'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which type of sensory receptor is responsible for detecting light touch and texture?', '["Free nerve endings","Pacinian corpuscles","Muscle spindles","Meissner''s corpuscles","Ruffini endings"]'::jsonb, 'Meissner''s corpuscles', 'Meissner''s corpuscles are rapidly adapting receptors in dermal papillae, responsible for light touch and texture discrimination.'),
('e3004f55-1ed4-589b-a9a5-aa039ec56899'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Referred pain is best explained by which hypothesis?', '["Convergence-projection theory","Gate control theory","Pattern theory","Specificity theory","Central sensitization theory"]'::jsonb, 'Convergence-projection theory', 'The convergence-projection theory states that visceral and somatic afferents converge on the same second-order neurons in the spinal cord.'),
('8e00ebcb-14a7-561c-b22b-6728a0cefbf3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following best describes the sensory homunculus in the postcentral gyrus?', '["Face represented medially, leg laterally","Leg represented medially, face laterally","Hand represented medially, trunk laterally","Equal representation of all body parts","Inverted representation with face inferior"]'::jsonb, 'Leg represented medially, face laterally', 'The sensory homunculus has the leg/foot medially (paracentral lobule) and the face/lips laterally.'),
('a94f1053-01f0-5c91-9b6d-0877049ca0b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The anterolateral system (spinothalamic tract) carries all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Vibration","Crude touch","Burning pain","Sharp pain","Temperature sensation"]'::jsonb, 'Vibration', 'Vibration sense is carried by the dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway.'),
('61f4e416-56f1-56c9-a710-483817fc2487'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which neurotransmitter is released by primary afferent pain fibers (A-delta and C fibers) in the dorsal horn?', '["Serotonin","Dopamine","Acetylcholine","Glutamate","GABA"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate', 'Glutamate is the primary excitatory neurotransmitter released by nociceptive afferents at their synapse in the dorsal horn.'),
('dfdf2cd2-87aa-5a6f-8876-28cdcc62cf47'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The endogenous opioid system in pain modulation involves all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Substance P","Beta-endorphin","Endorphins","Enkephalins","Dynorphins"]'::jsonb, 'Substance P', 'Substance P is a nociceptive neurotransmitter released by C fibers, NOT an endogenous opioid.'),
('11d82022-6eeb-5589-92ab-b90bb6fe0057'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The most likely lesion is:', '["Right dorsal column at lumbar level","Left half of spinal cord at thoracic level","Right half of spinal cord at thoracic level","Anterior spinal artery occlusion","Left dorsal column at lumbar level"]'::jsonb, 'Left half of spinal cord at thoracic level', 'Left Brown-Sequard syndrome: ipsilateral loss of fine touch/proprioception and contralateral loss of pain/temperature below the lesion.'),
('264c37fb-f8c8-5368-9c25-b66acdfe3605'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The gate control theory of pain involves modulation at which level?', '["Dorsal root ganglion","Dorsal horn of spinal cord","Cerebral cortex","Peripheral nerve","Thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal horn of spinal cord', 'The gate control theory (Melzack and Wall) proposes a ''gate'' in the substantia gelatinosa (lamina II) of the dorsal horn.'),
('d66e1808-bd22-545b-a90d-5fc1e0579b77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which receptor type is responsible for the sensation of joint position and movement?', '["Ruffini endings","Pacinian corpuscles","Meissner''s corpuscles","Free nerve endings","Krause end bulbs"]'::jsonb, 'Ruffini endings', 'Ruffini endings (type II cutaneous mechanoreceptors) respond to skin stretch and contribute to proprioception.'),
('9e70e3cc-8e4f-57b9-9a1e-f3a31d7c6920'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The first-order neurons for the dorsal column pathway are located in the:', '["Dorsal root ganglion","VPL thalamus","Substantia gelatinosa","Clarke''s nucleus","Nucleus proprius"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal root ganglion', 'First-order neurons for all somatosensory pathways (except CN V for face) are in the dorsal root ganglia.'),
('abdc2940-662c-58b4-b383-e96458e18c7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The fasciculus gracilis and fasciculus cuneatus are components of which tract?', '["Spinothalamic","Spinocerebellar","Dorsal column","Corticospinal","Spinoreticular"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column', 'Fasciculus gracilis (lower body, T7 and below) and fasciculus cuneatus (upper body, T6 and above) form the dorsal columns.'),
('28ce9f2f-ac1c-57f0-a1b4-2642b438ddce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus are located in which part of the brainstem?', '["Pons","Medulla","Thalamus","Midbrain","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla', 'These nuclei are in the medulla oblongata. Their axons decussate as internal arcuate fibers to form the medial lemniscus.'),
('13742ba0-30ab-5f3e-a4b4-9453efbe990a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following sensory systems has NO representation in the thalamus?', '["Vision","Smell","Hearing","Touch","Taste"]'::jsonb, 'Smell', 'The olfactory system bypasses the thalamus, going from olfactory bulb directly to olfactory cortex.'),
('6776c265-0c4e-5567-aa58-927cda4c94ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The medial lemniscus carries sensory information to which thalamic nucleus?', '["VPM","MGB","VPL","LGB","Anterior nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'VPL', 'The medial lemniscus synapses in the VPL nucleus of the thalamus.'),
('e83dbebc-11f7-55ee-b694-053f9c0807ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'This is called:', '["Agnosia","Allodynia","Astereognosis","Ataxia","Dysdiadochokinesia"]'::jsonb, 'Astereognosis', 'Astereognosis is the inability to identify objects by touch despite intact primary sensory modalities.'),
('0f2e48f0-67c2-58c3-9a32-53cbe93c03fa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The lateral spinothalamic tract carries:', '["Fine touch and vibration","Crude touch and pressure","Pain and temperature","Unconscious proprioception","Conscious proprioception"]'::jsonb, 'Pain and temperature', 'The lateral spinothalamic tract carries pain and temperature. The anterior spinothalamic tract carries crude touch and pressure.'),
('233551d0-94b9-5a76-8c41-8fff03fae207'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which lamina of the spinal grey matter receives nociceptive input?', '["Lamina VII","Lamina II","Lamina X","Lamina IV","Lamina IX"]'::jsonb, 'Lamina II', 'Lamina II (substantia gelatinosa) is the primary site of nociceptive processing in the dorsal horn.'),
('435c9672-dbc3-5fed-abad-863bfed5e97d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The non-nociceptive (inhibitory) neurotransmitter in the descending pain modulation pathway is primarily:', '["Dopamine mediated physiological mechanism","Glutamate mediated physiological mechanism","Substance P mediated physiological mechanism","Serotonin (5-HT) and norepinephrine","Acetylcholine mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin (5-HT) and norepinephrine', 'The descending pain modulatory pathway uses serotonin and norepinephrine to inhibit nociceptive transmission.'),
('4264a102-2fb5-5c03-a734-a9742bf739e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'All of the following are true about the anterolateral system EXCEPT:', '["It is a somatotopically organized tract","It ascends in the white matter anterior to the dorsal horn","It decussates in the spinal cord","It carries pain and temperature","Its second-order neurons are in the dorsal root ganglia"]'::jsonb, 'Its second-order neurons are in the dorsal root ganglia', 'Second-order neurons of the anterolateral system are in the dorsal horn, NOT in the dorsal root ganglia (which contain first-order neurons).'),
('e7351ea1-cbc6-5e6b-8650-6470afcaa9ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Its second-order neurons are located in the:', '["Mesencephalic nucleus of V","Spinal nucleus of V","Main sensory nucleus of V","Motor nucleus of V","Nucleus ambiguous"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal nucleus of V', 'Pain and temperature from the face: first-order in trigeminal ganglion, second-order in spinal nucleus of V, then cross to VPM.'),
('adef223b-dcd7-5982-a207-5da9a20f3956'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with a lesion in the right medial lemniscus at the level of the pons would have loss of:', '["Pain and temperature on the right side","Fine touch and proprioception on the left side","Fine touch and proprioception on the right side","Fine touch on the right face","Pain and temperature on the left side"]'::jsonb, 'Fine touch and proprioception on the right side', 'The medial lemniscus has already decussated (in the medulla). A right medial lemniscus lesion causes loss on the RIGHT side.'),
('da18144d-bd0a-5a43-90e3-3b0865e53b2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which thalamic nucleus is involved in the motor loop (cerebellar and basal ganglia projections to cortex)?', '["Pulvinar","Anterior nucleus","Ventral lateral (VL) nucleus","VPL","VPM"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral lateral (VL) nucleus', 'The VL nucleus receives input from basal ganglia and cerebellum and projects to the motor cortex.'),
('7cc4bb5d-fc22-5205-ada0-16ec6a7d201b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The lateral geniculate body has how many layers?', '["8","3","6","4","2"]'::jsonb, '6', 'The LGB has 6 layers. Layers 1,4,6 receive contralateral input; layers 2,3,5 receive ipsilateral input.'),
('f4d28dcb-ad93-5ba3-9c28-9d3018b32c85'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is a rapidly adapting mechanoreceptor?', '["Muscle spindles","Pacinian corpuscles","Golgi tendon organs","Merkel discs","Ruffini endings"]'::jsonb, 'Pacinian corpuscles', 'Pacinian corpuscles are rapidly adapting (phasic) receptors. Merkel discs and Ruffini endings are slowly adapting.'),
('8d534d0d-8246-5810-8491-4351c20d77b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The ability to localize a light touch stimulus on the skin is called:', '["Stereognosis","Point localization","Two-point discrimination","Topognosis","Graphesthesia"]'::jsonb, 'Topognosis', 'Topognosis is the ability to accurately localize a tactile stimulus on the skin.'),
('5c8e4149-58db-51c7-af71-8a13e5dc94e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'This is most likely due to:', '["Syringomyelia","Thalamic stroke","Spinal cord transection","Brown-Sequard syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy', 'Stocking-and-glove distribution of sensory loss is classic for peripheral neuropathy.'),
('62a87854-3659-536f-91fb-d355c007b903'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Syringomyelia typically causes which pattern of sensory loss?', '["Alternating sensory loss on the face","Loss of pain and temperature with preserved fine touch in the upper extremities","Ipsilateral loss of all sensation","Loss of all modalities below the lesion","Loss of fine touch with preserved pain in the upper extremities"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of pain and temperature with preserved fine touch in the upper extremities', 'Syringomyelia damages crossing fibers in the anterior commissure, causing bilateral loss of pain and temperature in a ''cape'' distribution.'),
('11251b77-2445-5f03-84b6-4b6071185697'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The corticospinal tract originates primarily from which area of the cerebral cortex?', '["Somatosensory cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Prefrontal cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Parietal association cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Premotor cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Primary motor cortex (area 4) and premotor/supplementary motor area"]'::jsonb, 'Primary motor cortex (area 4) and premotor/supplementary motor area', 'Corticospinal tract originates ~30% from M1, ~30% from premotor/SMA, and ~40% from somatosensory cortex.'),
('1d549a0f-92eb-5f11-840e-950f6e26407d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The corticospinal tract decussates at which level?', '["Pons","Internal capsule","Midbrain","Spinal cord","Medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Medulla', 'About 85-90% of corticospinal fibers decussate at the caudal medulla (pyramidal decussation).'),
('0ab03b97-442b-5542-823d-285ef8f26c77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A lesion of the corticospinal tract at the level of the internal capsule would present with:', '["Ipsilateral spastic hemiplegia and hyperreflexia","Ipsilateral flaccid hemiplegia","Contralateral flaccid hemiplegia","Quadriplegia","Contralateral spastic hemiplegia and hyperreflexia"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral spastic hemiplegia and hyperreflexia', 'A corticospinal tract lesion above the decussation causes contralateral spastic hemiplegia (UMN type).'),
('3be72c5b-6cd3-5181-9341-9adb2e0f152f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Betz cells, which give rise to some corticospinal fibers, are located in which layer of the motor cortex?', '["Layer III","Layer VI","Layer I","Layer V","Layer IV"]'::jsonb, 'Layer V', 'Betz cells (giant pyramidal neurons) are located in layer V of the primary motor cortex.'),
('9b428f42-395e-5408-b9b7-93329c76c800'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is a characteristic of upper motor neuron (UMN) lesions?', '["Fasciculations","Muscle atrophy","Flaccid paralysis","Hyporeflexia","Hyperreflexia and spasticity"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperreflexia and spasticity', 'UMN lesions cause spasticity, hyperreflexia, clonus, and Babinski sign.'),
('2efd1a40-4446-55e2-9eca-30367bcd6556'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'All of the following are features of lower motor neuron paralysis EXCEPT:', '["Hypertonia","Flaccid paralysis","Fasciculations","Hyporeflexia","Muscle atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertonia', 'Hypertonia and spasticity are features of UMN lesions. LMN lesions cause flaccidity, hypotonia, and hyporeflexia.'),
('ec6f4332-c08b-5e20-b7e4-b3e372f4e9bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The rubrospinal tract originates from the red nucleus and primarily facilitates:', '["Fine motor control of facial muscles","Autonomic control","Extensor tone in the lower limbs","Flexor tone in the upper limbs","Postural reflexes"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor tone in the upper limbs', 'The rubrospinal tract facilitates flexor motor neurons, particularly in the upper limbs.'),
('f85005e4-b38c-5fc0-8897-6d18150471d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The extrapyramidal system includes all of the following tracts EXCEPT:', '["Reticulospinal tract","Corticospinal tract","Tectospinal tract","Rubrospinal tract","Vestibulospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal tract', 'Corticospinal (pyramidal) tract is NOT part of the extrapyramidal system.'),
('090931f1-2613-5da3-b440-222551204cc8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The vestibulospinal tract is primarily involved in:', '["Flexor reflex initiation","Head turning in response to visual stimuli","Postural adjustments and extensor tone","Pain modulation","Fine motor control of the hand"]'::jsonb, 'Postural adjustments and extensor tone', 'The lateral vestibulospinal tract facilitates extensor (antigravity) motor neurons.'),
('18424f76-702f-5f45-9677-7288c69ccede'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The tectospinal tract is involved in which type of reflex?', '["Crossed extensor reflex","Tonic neck reflex","Stretch reflex","Flexor withdrawal reflex","Visual and auditory reflex head turning"]'::jsonb, 'Visual and auditory reflex head turning', 'The tectospinal tract originates from superior and inferior colliculi and mediates reflexive head/eye turning.'),
('22a0656f-7457-5bcb-8344-407ca71e3bcf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The medial reticulospinal tract originates from the pontine reticular formation and has what effect?', '["Inhibits all motor activity","Facilitates extensor tone","Inhibits extensor tone","Controls fine digit movements","Facilitates flexor tone"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitates extensor tone', 'The pontine (medial) reticulospinal tract facilitates extensor/antigravity tone.'),
('8fd602e1-36c9-5ffc-9493-96fd5a9ed80b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which area of the motor cortex is responsible for programming complex sequences of movement?', '["Premotor cortex","Primary motor cortex","Prefrontal cortex","Supplementary motor area","Parietal cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Supplementary motor area', 'The SMA is involved in planning and programming complex motor sequences.'),
('3d8022aa-e3f9-515b-8ad2-ddb36f7533d0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The premotor cortex (PMC) is primarily involved in:', '["Regulation of muscle tone","Execution of fine finger movements","Sensory-guided movements and selecting motor programs","Oculomotor control","Postural reflexes"]'::jsonb, 'Sensory-guided movements and selecting motor programs', 'The premotor cortex is critical for sensory-guided movements and selecting motor programs.'),
('8e76e599-fafc-56e5-bcac-0c899714b04e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The motor homunculus in the precentral gyrus is organized such that:', '["The leg is represented medially and the face laterally","Representation is inverted with face superior","The hand is represented in the Sylvian fissure","All body parts are equally represented","The face is represented medially and the leg laterally"]'::jsonb, 'The leg is represented medially and the face laterally', 'Motor homunculus: lower limb medially (paracentral lobule), face laterally near Sylvian fissure.'),
('874da50e-bf8c-553b-ba3d-d0134f9b2fb1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with a lesion in the left cerebral peduncle (midbrain) would present with:', '["Left flaccid hemiplegia","Quadriplegia","Right spastic hemiplegia","Left spastic hemiplegia","Right flaccid hemiplegia"]'::jsonb, 'Right spastic hemiplegia', 'Corticospinal tract in cerebral peduncle is above the decussation. Left peduncle lesion causes right spastic hemiplegia.'),
('72658540-bb54-5680-ba6f-e7a0cf17beeb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which structures are affected?', '["Oculomotor nerve and corticospinal tract","Hypoglossal nerve and corticospinal tract","Oculomotor nerve and medial lemniscus","Facial nerve and corticospinal tract","Abducens nerve and corticospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve and corticospinal tract', 'Weber syndrome (PCA paramedian branches) affects CN III fibers and corticospinal tract in the midbrain.'),
('98c65710-61be-58af-8594-3eb240233c6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which cranial nerve nucleus receives bilateral corticobulbar innervation?', '["Hypoglossal nucleus","Spinal accessory nucleus","Oculomotor nucleus","Facial nerve (upper face) subnucleus","Facial nerve (lower face) subnucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (upper face) subnucleus', 'Upper face (frontalis, orbicularis oculi) receives bilateral corticobulbar innervation. Lower face receives only contralateral.'),
('46578a11-e797-5e5b-9d64-f4e687c26a08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The ''extrapyramidal system'' is traditionally defined as:', '["The corticospinal tract exclusively","The basal ganglia and its connections","The cerebellar motor pathways","All motor pathways that do NOT pass through the medullary pyramids","All motor pathways originating from the cerebral cortex"]'::jsonb, 'All motor pathways that do NOT pass through the medullary pyramids', 'Extrapyramidal = all motor pathways that do NOT pass through the medullary pyramids.'),
('c1475977-4159-5e65-8afb-f16deddc8015'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The corticospinal tract constitutes about what percentage of fibers in the cerebral peduncle?', '["60-70%","40-50%","25-30%","10-15%","80-90%"]'::jsonb, '40-50%', 'The corticospinal tract occupies the middle 3/5ths of the cerebral peduncle (~40-50% of descending fibers).'),
('bf100c2a-6c8c-551a-b2d8-d977f14381f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which tract is most important for fine, fractionated movements of the digits?', '["Corticospinal tract","Rubrospinal tract","Reticulospinal tract","Tectospinal tract","Vestibulospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Corticospinal tract', 'The corticospinal tract is essential for fine, fractionated movements of the digits via monosynaptic connections with alpha motor neurons.'),
('04ca6ff0-a82e-5a4c-a9fb-e6500f6a8852'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The anterior corticospinal tract:', '["Carries fibers from the cerebellum mediated physiological mechanism","Is primarily involved in pain modulation mediated physiological mechanism","Synapses in the dorsal horn mediated physiological mechanism","Is the largest descending tract mediated physiological mechanism","Carries mostly uncrossed fibers that decussate in the spinal cord at the level of termination"]'::jsonb, 'Carries mostly uncrossed fibers that decussate in the spinal cord at the level of termination', 'The anterior corticospinal tract contains ~10% of fibers that decussate segmentally in the spinal cord.'),
('73acf0d4-46e4-5f6f-9be0-e4502963fef5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with an UMN lesion at C5 would present with:', '["Loss of bladder control only mediated physiological mechanism","Fasciculations in the leg mediated physiological mechanism","Spastic paralysis of ipsilateral arm and leg with Babinski sign","Atrophy of the hand muscles only mediated physiological mechanism","Flaccid paralysis of the arm only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Spastic paralysis of ipsilateral arm and leg with Babinski sign', 'An UMN lesion above cervical enlargement causes ipsilateral spastic hemiparesis with hyperreflexia and Babinski.'),
('c5952480-ace4-5127-9f4c-0b198aedc650'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The primary motor cortex (area 4) is characterized by:', '["Equal development of all layers mediated physiological mechanism","A prominent layer V with giant Betz cells and absence of layer IV","A well-developed layer IV mediated physiological mechanism","A prominent layer II mediated physiological mechanism","Absence of Betz cells mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'A prominent layer V with giant Betz cells and absence of layer IV', 'Area 4 is agranular cortex: lacks distinct layer IV and has prominent layer V with Betz cells.'),
('5209f896-e862-5f1d-80d0-c33e3287519f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The supplementary motor area (Brodmann area 6, medial part) is important for:', '["Bimanual coordination and internally initiated movements","Visual processing mediated physiological mechanism","Language comprehension mediated physiological mechanism","Execution of simple movements mediated physiological mechanism","Sensory processing mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Bimanual coordination and internally initiated movements', 'The SMA is crucial for bimanual coordination and internally generated movements.'),
('84db135e-476a-5cf6-838f-dcd306692f2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Mirror neurons, which fire both when performing an action and when observing it, are found in:', '["Premotor cortex and inferior parietal lobule","Primary motor cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Somatosensory cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Occipital cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Prefrontal cortex mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Premotor cortex and inferior parietal lobule', 'Mirror neurons are found in premotor cortex and inferior parietal lobule.'),
('aab0af62-46fc-51fc-8eba-a5e8bc80f798'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The red nucleus receives input from the:', '["Basal ganglia","Cerebellum","Motor cortex","Reticular formation","Vestibular nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Motor cortex', 'The red nucleus receives input from the motor cortex (corticorubral fibers).'),
('a1e461fa-3c85-565e-acc0-7191aa48d8de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Medullary pyramid lesions (unilateral) at the level of the pyramid (above decussation) cause:', '["Ipsilateral flaccid paralysis","Quadriparesis","Ipsilateral spastic hemiplegia","Contralateral flaccid paralysis","Contralateral spastic hemiplegia"]'::jsonb, 'Contralateral spastic hemiplegia', 'The pyramids are above the decussation. Left pyramid lesion causes right spastic hemiplegia.'),
('d7a28624-086d-5ac3-a309-7472025eaa26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following tracts originates from the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF)?', '["Corticospinal","Rubrospinal","Tectospinal","Medial vestibulospinal","Lateral vestibulospinal"]'::jsonb, 'Medial vestibulospinal', 'The medial vestibulospinal tract originates from the medial vestibular nucleus and descends in the MLF.'),
('d8cd6a3a-f3fe-569f-ae1b-0180bea567ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The ''pyramidal tract'' specifically refers to:', '["The extrapyramidal tracts","Only the lateral corticospinal tract","The corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts","The rubrospinal tract","All descending motor pathways"]'::jsonb, 'The corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts', 'The pyramidal tract refers to corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts, which pass through the medullary pyramids.'),
('7ee10ebd-2f2e-5feb-b346-ca51f4c8bf1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Damage to the primary motor cortex causes:', '["Flaccid paralysis that never recovers mediated physiological mechanism","Chorea mediated physiological mechanism","Resting tremor mediated physiological mechanism","Spastic paralysis initially, with some recovery of proximal movements","Ataxia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Spastic paralysis initially, with some recovery of proximal movements', 'Acute M1 lesions cause initial flaccidity evolving to spasticity. Proximal muscles recover better than distal.'),
('b46d21f1-5159-5ded-97a9-a55d62af7492'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The corticospinal tract terminates primarily on which spinal cord laminae?', '["Laminae V-VII and lamina IX","Laminae I-II","Lamina X","Laminae I-III","Laminae III-IV"]'::jsonb, 'Laminae V-VII and lamina IX', 'Corticospinal fibers synapse on interneurons in laminae V-VII and directly on alpha motor neurons in lamina IX.'),
('a0bcc2c7-1371-5603-97ae-ec5f783bf764'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'In the internal capsule posterior limb, fibers destined for the cervical enlargement are located ________ to fibers for the lumbosacral enlargement.', '["Lateral","Inferior","Medial","Anterior","Posterior"]'::jsonb, 'Anterior', 'In the internal capsule: anterior = corticobulbar; middle = upper limb; posterior = lower limb.'),
('81c65589-61e7-57cb-a066-ebced8e08781'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The crossed corticospinal tract is also called:', '["Tectospinal tract","Lateral corticospinal tract","Anterior corticospinal tract","Extrapyramidal tract","Rubrospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral corticospinal tract', 'The lateral corticospinal tract is the crossed component (~85-90% of fibers).'),
('7b5cfb2e-5aec-5882-8d81-29b8b48e46cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Lesion of the right cerebral peduncle (crus cerebri) results in:', '["Right flaccid hemiplegia","Right spastic hemiplegia","Left flaccid hemiplegia","Ataxia","Left spastic hemiplegia"]'::jsonb, 'Left spastic hemiplegia', 'Right crus cerebri lesion (above decussation) causes left spastic hemiplegia.'),
('6ef21d6d-523c-5d4c-b24f-afd19f2dc794'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is NOT a function of the cerebellum?', '["Initiation of voluntary movements","Motor learning","Coordination of skilled movements","Balance and equilibrium","Fine motor control"]'::jsonb, 'Initiation of voluntary movements', 'The cerebellum does NOT initiate voluntary movements. It coordinates movements initiated by the motor cortex.'),
('e9fdd87b-6bd2-5ede-b0fc-b5a9d1845735'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The flocculonodular lobe of the cerebellum (archicerebellum) is primarily concerned with:', '["Motor planning","Speech articulation","Balance and equilibrium","Fine digit movements","Truncal coordination"]'::jsonb, 'Balance and equilibrium', 'The flocculonodular lobe receives vestibular input and is involved in balance, equilibrium, and eye movements.'),
('213250d4-7075-5342-8e40-423872fa1e5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The cerebellum receives input from the spinal cord (unconscious proprioception) via which peduncle?', '["Inferior and middle peduncles","Superior cerebellar peduncle","Middle cerebellar peduncle","Inferior cerebellar peduncle","Superior and middle peduncles"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior cerebellar peduncle', 'The inferior cerebellar peduncle carries afferents from the spinal cord (spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar).'),
('d8c6b6d6-8d12-5cbe-9cb9-931a9025aa62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'All of the following cerebellar afferents enter via the superior cerebellar peduncle EXCEPT:', '["Ventral spinocerebellar tract","Reticulocerebellar fibers","Trigeminocerebellar fibers","Tectocerebellar fibers","Pontocerebellar fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Pontocerebellar fibers', 'Pontocerebellar fibers enter via the middle cerebellar peduncle, NOT the superior peduncle.'),
('d013939e-77be-5716-a16a-3aa126a6d7f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The output of the cerebellar cortex is mediated by:', '["Golgi cells","Basket cells","Granule cells","Purkinje cells","Stellate cells"]'::jsonb, 'Purkinje cells', 'Purkinje cells provide the ONLY output from the cerebellar cortex. They are GABAergic (inhibitory).'),
('1599c10a-38c3-5114-86e2-43b33978b785'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which deep cerebellar nucleus is involved in the coordination of planned movements of the extremities?', '["Globose nucleus","Emboliform nucleus","Dentate nucleus","Fastigial nucleus","Nucleus interpositus"]'::jsonb, 'Dentate nucleus', 'The dentate nucleus (largest) receives input from the lateral hemisphere and coordinates limb movements.'),
('2a1da629-d867-57a7-8893-3dfe778bf407'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Cerebellar ataxia is characterized by all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Dysdiadochokinesia","Dysmetria","Intention tremor","Spasticity","Wide-based gait"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity', 'Spasticity is a feature of UMN lesions. Cerebellar lesions cause hypotonia.'),
('40c81ce4-9c6a-5ed0-a838-90e8fae351c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, This is called:', '["Dysdiadochokinesia","Ataxia","Dysmetria","Intention tremor","Adiadochokinesia"]'::jsonb, 'Dysdiadochokinesia', 'Dysdiadochokinesia is the inability to perform rapid alternating movements.'),
('b72c072f-c13a-5c47-96f1-b087e2a08dfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The basal ganglia include all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Caudate nucleus","Putamen","Substantia nigra","Red nucleus","Globus pallidus"]'::jsonb, 'Red nucleus', 'The red nucleus is in the midbrain but is NOT part of the basal ganglia.'),
('38a9b42f-1ef2-51d2-b46f-c657ed80108c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Parkinson''s disease is primarily caused by degeneration of:', '["Dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta","Subthalamic nucleus","Globus pallidus pars externa","Cholinergic neurons in the caudate","GABAergic neurons in the putamen"]'::jsonb, 'Dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta', 'Parkinson''s results from loss of dopaminergic neurons in SNc, reducing dopamine in the striatum.'),
('b3797115-605a-571b-8fde-9b089bbd7469'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The classical triad of Parkinson''s disease includes:', '["Ataxia, dysmetria, nystagmus","Chorea, athetosis, dystonia","Spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign","Hypotonia, intention tremor, dysdiadochokinesia","Bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor"]'::jsonb, 'Bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor', 'The classic triad: bradykinesia, rigidity (lead-pipe/cogwheel), and resting tremor (4-6 Hz).'),
('1dbb02e9-bcf8-5736-a20a-6ba27327e56b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Huntington disease is characterized by degeneration of:', '["GABAergic neurons in the striatum","Thalamic VPL nucleus","Substantia nigra pars compacta","Corticospinal tract","Purkinje cells in the cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'GABAergic neurons in the striatum', 'Huntington disease involves degeneration of GABAergic medium spiny neurons in the striatum.'),
('70a732af-c2e7-5158-a59b-25088f992b05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Hemiballismus is typically caused by a lesion in the:', '["Substantia nigra","Caudate nucleus","Globus pallidus","Putamen","Subthalamic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Subthalamic nucleus', 'Hemiballismus (unilateral flinging movements) is caused by a lesion of the subthalamic nucleus.'),
('62a15137-ed65-5b3e-94a0-d92e28ca0585'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which neurotransmitter does GPi use?', '["Serotonin","GABA","Glutamate","Dopamine","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'GABA', 'GPi neurons are GABAergic (inhibitory), tonically inhibiting the thalamus.'),
('d7edfca3-8e80-550b-82d3-eb083972570a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'In the direct pathway of the basal ganglia, activation of D1 receptors in the striatum leads to:', '["No effect","Decreased dopamine release","Inhibition of movement","Facilitation of movement","Increased muscle tone"]'::jsonb, 'Facilitation of movement', 'Direct pathway (D1) facilitates movement. Indirect pathway (D2) suppresses unwanted movements.'),
('bd25d893-402c-5709-ab4c-25996580dc2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'L-DOPA is used in Parkinson''s disease because it:', '["Stimulates GABA receptors","Inhibits acetylcholine release","Is a precursor of dopamine that crosses the BBB","Is an MAO inhibitor","Blocks dopamine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Is a precursor of dopamine that crosses the BBB', 'L-DOPA crosses the BBB and is converted to dopamine by AADC.'),
('faab2dba-2538-5a64-8c55-a3146a54b892'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The discrepancy signal is called:', '["Efference copy","Motor error signal","Corollary discharge","Cerebellar error","Feedback error"]'::jsonb, 'Motor error signal', 'The cerebellum acts as a comparator; Purkinje cell output represents the motor error signal.'),
('4712ddc9-e253-535b-ab70-2dbe857e4bb4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Cerebellar lesions produce intention tremors that are characterized by:', '["Fine rapid tremor","Constant tremor regardless of movement","Tremor only in the legs","Tremor at rest that disappears with movement","Tremor during voluntary movement, worse near the target"]'::jsonb, 'Tremor during voluntary movement, worse near the target', 'Intention tremor is absent at rest but appears during goal-directed movement, worsening near the target.'),
('759fc36f-b52e-5214-a0bf-9ef0259541e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The neocerebellum (cerebrocerebellum) is primarily involved in:', '["Visual tracking mediated physiological mechanism","Posture and stance mediated physiological mechanism","Autonomic control mediated physiological mechanism","Vestibular reflexes mediated physiological mechanism","Planning and coordination of limb movements"]'::jsonb, 'Planning and coordination of limb movements', 'The neocerebellum (lateral hemispheres) is involved in planning and coordination of limb movements.'),
('12a354c5-264f-5a0d-9f61-27e5900f8cff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The middle cerebellar peduncle (brachium pontis) contains predominantly:', '["Efferent fibers from cerebellum","Olivocerebellar fibers","Vestibulocerebellar fibers","Afferent fibers from the pons","Spinocerebellar fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Afferent fibers from the pons', 'The MCP contains pontocerebellar fibers from the corticopontocerebellar pathway.'),
('699daf0b-ab34-51d8-9360-952895495a7d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is true about cerebellar histology?', '["Granule cells are excitatory and use glutamate","Purkinje cells are excitatory","Purkinje cells are the most numerous cells in the cerebellum","All cerebellar afferents are GABAergic","Climbing fibers synapse on granule cells"]'::jsonb, 'Granule cells are excitatory and use glutamate', 'Granule cells are the most numerous neurons in the brain and are excitatory (glutamatergic).'),
('e2a98147-a7c2-5962-a685-793ae2a23ba1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Climbing fibers, which synapse on Purkinje cells, originate from the:', '["Inferior olivary nucleus","Reticular formation","Vestibular nuclei","Spinal cord","Pontine nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Inferior olivary nucleus', 'Climbing fibers originate from the inferior olivary nucleus.'),
('834975c9-e001-5190-921e-79434e1c6873'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with midline cerebellar vermis lesion would most likely present with:', '["Dysarthria mediated physiological mechanism","Truncal ataxia and wide-based gait","Limb ataxia mediated physiological mechanism","Nystagmus mediated physiological mechanism","Intention tremor mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Truncal ataxia and wide-based gait', 'Vermis lesions cause truncal ataxia with wide-based gait (titubation). Hemisphere lesions cause ipsilateral limb ataxia.'),
('c8c2b6f6-3ee1-598b-800c-7917fee7034c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'In the basal ganglia, the striatum receives dopaminergic input from:', '["Substantia nigra pars compacta","Ventral tegmental area (VTA) and SNc","Red nucleus","Globus pallidus","Subthalamic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral tegmental area (VTA) and SNc', 'The striatum receives dopaminergic input from both SNc (nigrostriatal) and VTA (mesolimbic).'),
('3b4a65a1-f627-5351-ad07-b8042b6a19e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Kinesigenic dyskinesias are a feature of which condition?', '["Ataxia-telangiectasia","Wilson disease","Huntington chorea","Parkinsonism","Neuroacanthocytosis"]'::jsonb, 'Huntington chorea', 'Huntington chorea features brief, irregular, involuntary movements that are worsened by purposeful movement.'),
('ae4c0fb3-426b-5b2b-8f9e-27f53dee1e7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which surgical procedure has been used for Parkinson''s disease by lesioning the GPi or VIM thalamus?', '["Rizotomy","Pallidotomy/thalamotomy","Commissurotomy","Cordotomy","Lobotomy"]'::jsonb, 'Pallidotomy/thalamotomy', 'Pallidotomy (lesioning GPi) and thalamotomy (lesioning VIM) improve Parkinson''s symptoms.'),
('dad75670-5e29-52d0-a125-11511043133a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Cerebellar mutism is a transient complication following surgery involving the:', '["Vermis","Flocculonodular lobe","Cerebellar hemispheres","Cerebellar peduncles","Dentate nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Vermis', 'Posterior fossa syndrome (cerebellar mutism) follows resection of midline cerebellar tumors.'),
('99ef4916-4875-5f11-912e-f3cb80bd6f89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Wilson disease, which causes copper accumulation, leads to which extrapyramidal manifestations?', '["Dystonia, tremor, dysarthria","Hemiballismus","Chorea and athetosis","Intention tremor and ataxia","Resting tremor and bradykinesia"]'::jsonb, 'Dystonia, tremor, dysarthria', 'Wilson disease causes copper deposition in the basal ganglia, leading to dystonia, tremor, and dysarthria.'),
('7b64243b-891d-5bcc-a1b5-74d8834d2c39'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The basal ganglia ''direct pathway'' uses which dopamine receptor in the striatum?', '["D2 receptor","D3 receptor","D1 receptor","D4 receptor","D5 receptor"]'::jsonb, 'D1 receptor', 'Direct pathway involves D1 receptors (Gs-coupled), which facilitate movement.'),
('779516ce-2c66-5c79-896d-092883e5c81d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The reticular formation in the brainstem is involved in all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Sleep-wake cycle regulation","Motor control and posture","Fine discrimination of touch","Cardiovascular and respiratory control","Pain modulation"]'::jsonb, 'Fine discrimination of touch', 'Fine discrimination of touch is mediated by the dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway, not the reticular formation.'),
('dc4b7569-bd9b-50b2-91d0-35abc9591da8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The periaqueductal gray (PAG) in the midbrain is primarily involved in:', '["Balance","Pain modulation","Motor coordination","Visual processing","Auditory processing"]'::jsonb, 'Pain modulation', 'The PAG is a key center for descending pain modulation.'),
('8f72c5f1-d121-5059-af90-0230bdbcaf07'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) in the medulla receives input from:', '["Motor fibers for swallowing mediated physiological mechanism","Vestibular afferents mediated physiological mechanism","Pain fibers from the face mediated physiological mechanism","Taste receptors (gustatory) and visceral afferents","Auditory afferents mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Taste receptors (gustatory) and visceral afferents', 'The NTS is the primary sensory nucleus for taste and visceral afferents.'),
('40f80beb-1ae4-5df9-8159-0cdc44dc7eff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The red nucleus in the midbrain gives rise to which descending tract?', '["Tectospinal tract","Corticospinal tract","Reticulospinal tract","Vestibulospinal tract","Rubrospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Rubrospinal tract', 'The red nucleus gives rise to the rubrospinal tract, which facilitates flexor tone.'),
('1fc9bd04-e189-59bb-9a5e-567e6e4f2df7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The parabrachial nucleus in the pons is involved in:', '["Auditory processing","Visual reflexes","Pain transmission","Respiratory control and taste","Motor coordination"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory control and taste', 'The parabrachial nucleus (pneumotaxic center) is involved in respiratory control and gustatory relay.'),
('942f1248-f7ef-5987-a7f7-521c13c6ec27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Decerebrate rigidity (extensor posturing) results from a lesion at which level?', '["Medulla mediated physiological mechanism","Spinal cord mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebral cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebellum mediated physiological mechanism","Between superior and inferior colliculi"]'::jsonb, 'Between superior and inferior colliculi', 'Decerebrate rigidity results from midbrain transection between superior and inferior colliculi.'),
('71a6a0f7-552c-5623-988b-35ddcd27a46f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Decorticate rigidity (flexor posturing) results from a lesion at which level?', '["Medulla","Internal capsule","Spinal cord","Cerebellum","Midbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Internal capsule', 'Decorticate posturing (flexed arms, extended legs) results from a lesion above the brainstem.'),
('8d1a164a-97c8-5f19-b4a0-993abf224ec2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The pontine micturition center (Barrington''s nucleus) is located in the:', '["Cerebellum","Spinal cord","Medulla","Pons","Midbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Pons', 'Barrington''s nucleus (PMC) in the dorsolateral pons coordinates micturition.'),
('657b56ba-0a1b-54cc-87e1-e8816bf823bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which cranial nerve motor nucleus is located in the midbrain?', '["Hypoglossal nerve mediated physiological mechanism","Facial nerve mediated physiological mechanism","Glossopharyngeal nerve mediated physiological mechanism","Vagus nerve mediated physiological mechanism","Oculomotor nerve (CN III) and trochlear nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III) and trochlear nerve', 'CN III nucleus (superior colliculus level) and CN IV nucleus (inferior colliculus level) are in the midbrain.'),
('f222b93d-2b1f-588c-b6a0-86d062911492'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The nucleus ambiguus, located in the medulla, gives rise to motor fibers for which cranial nerves?', '["CN VII","CN XII","CN III and IV","CN V and VII","CN IX, X, and XI"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX, X, and XI', 'The nucleus ambiguus provides motor innervation via CN IX, X, and the cranial root of XI.'),
('c60dc58e-338a-5c2a-9b5a-23aa8aca6d2e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The parabrachial/Kolliker-Fuse nuclei in the pons constitute which respiratory center?', '["Pneumotaxic center","Apneustic center","Ventral respiratory group","Pre-Botzinger complex","Dorsal respiratory group"]'::jsonb, 'Pneumotaxic center', 'The pneumotaxic center (parabrachial/Kolliker-Fuse) limits inspiration.'),
('f4e847dd-6db5-5bf3-baec-c6f2c1c4dbad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The locus coeruleus, a major source of norepinephrine in the brain, is located in the:', '["Hypothalamus","Medulla","Pons","Thalamus","Midbrain"]'::jsonb, 'Pons', 'The locus coeruleus is located in the rostral pons (floor of fourth ventricle).'),
('bda80882-a731-5273-8cc4-a8b419e1cad3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The raphe nuclei in the brainstem are the primary source of which neurotransmitter?', '["Serotonin","Acetylcholine","Norepinephrine","Histamine","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin', 'The raphe nuclei are the primary source of serotonin in the brain.'),
('4c677c5c-2aa4-5783-baad-806235498799'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The ventral tegmental area (VTA) is a major source of which neurotransmitter?', '["Norepinephrine","Serotonin","Acetylcholine","GABA","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine', 'The VTA gives rise to the mesolimbic and mesocortical dopaminergic pathways.'),
('274697d1-275a-59b5-aeb8-1c13807e34ef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which brainstem nucleus is the major source of cholinergic input to the thalamus and forebrain?', '["Ventral tegmental area mediated physiological mechanism","Pedunculopontine tegmental nucleus (PPT) and laterodorsal tegmental nucleus","Raphe nuclei mediated physiological mechanism","Substantia nigra mediated physiological mechanism","Locus coeruleus mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Pedunculopontine tegmental nucleus (PPT) and laterodorsal tegmental nucleus', 'PPT and LDT provide cholinergic innervation to the thalamus and forebrain, important for arousal and REM sleep.'),
('3fcafc17-f8bd-55aa-8ec7-c434cda334fc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The monosynaptic stretch reflex (e.g., patellar reflex) involves which receptors?', '["Free nerve endings","Golgi tendon organs","Pacinian corpuscles","Ruffini endings","Muscle spindles"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle spindles', 'The stretch reflex involves muscle spindles. Ia afferents synapse directly on alpha motor neurons.'),
('9baf61b8-7059-5cbb-887b-acdae309a0c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The patellar reflex (knee jerk) tests which spinal cord segments?', '["S1-S2","T10-T12","C5-C6","C7-T1","L2-L4"]'::jsonb, 'L2-L4', 'Patellar reflex tests L2-L4 (femoral nerve). Achilles reflex tests S1-S2.'),
('bd402084-86bc-5ee8-8f67-875e2ef1e550'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Golgi tendon organ reflex (inverse stretch reflex) is mediated by:', '["II afferents","III afferents","Ib afferents","IV afferents","Ia afferents"]'::jsonb, 'Ib afferents', 'Golgi tendon organs are innervated by Ib afferents, causing autogenic inhibition.'),
('5e1be6e9-626d-52b8-815a-31f78cef7fde'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'What is the correct order of events in the flexor (withdrawal) reflex?', '["Activation of flexors with no crossed effects mediated physiological mechanism","Direct inhibition of flexors bilaterally mediated physiological mechanism","Monosynaptic activation of extensor only mediated physiological mechanism","Polysynaptic activation of flexors ipsilaterally and extensors contralaterally (crossed extensor","Activation of Golgi tendon organ only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Polysynaptic activation of flexors ipsilaterally and extensors contralaterally (crossed extensor', 'Flexor reflex: ipsilateral flexors activated for withdrawal, crossed extensor reflex supports the body.'),
('f8ec21b8-b088-51f4-b960-5a018644440b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Brown-Sequard syndrome (hemisection of spinal cord) results in all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Bilateral loss of pain and temperature below lesion","Ipsilateral loss of vibration sense","Contralateral loss of pain and temperature 1-2 segments below lesion","Ipsilateral spastic paralysis below lesion","Ipsilateral loss of fine touch and proprioception below lesion"]'::jsonb, 'Bilateral loss of pain and temperature below lesion', 'Bilateral pain/temperature loss indicates anterior commissure damage, not Brown-Sequard.'),
('d04b4e81-efa6-5295-9ac7-5d2af9ec051d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'In the stretch reflex, which of the following prevents overstretching of the muscle?', '["Autogenic inhibition","Presynaptic inhibition","Recurrent inhibition","Post-tetanic potentiation","Reciprocal inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Autogenic inhibition', 'Autogenic inhibition via Golgi tendon organs prevents excessive muscle tension.'),
('81ce2afe-b21e-51c1-a3ed-df9b080c8633'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Renshaw cells in the spinal cord mediate which type of inhibition?', '["Presynaptic inhibition","Recurrent inhibition","Postsynaptic inhibition","Feed-forward inhibition","Reciprocal inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent inhibition', 'Renshaw cells are inhibitory interneurons mediating recurrent inhibition of alpha motor neurons.'),
('b790200b-7b52-5152-a9d5-f46e57e918ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'This is classic for:', '["Brown-Sequard syndrome","Posterior cord syndrome","Syringomyelia","Anterior cord syndrome","Complete cord transection"]'::jsonb, 'Brown-Sequard syndrome', 'Left cord hemisection: ipsilateral UMN signs + dorsal column loss, contralateral spinothalamic loss.'),
('979fd0b6-5bc4-5154-9793-58a874d413db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which reflex is used to clinically assess the integrity of the S1-S2 spinal segments?', '["Achilles reflex","Biceps reflex","Triceps reflex","Patellar reflex","Brachioradialis reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Achilles reflex', 'Achilles reflex tests S1-S2 via the tibial nerve.'),
('b3d81c12-35a8-5a46-b825-3ecb9d28f5ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Anterior cord syndrome (often from anterior spinal artery occlusion) causes:', '["Ipsilateral motor loss only","Bilateral loss of all modalities","Loss of all modalities below the lesion","Loss of fine touch and vibration with preservation of pain","Loss of pain and temperature with preservation of fine touch and proprioception"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of pain and temperature with preservation of fine touch and proprioception', 'Anterior spinal artery occlusion spares dorsal columns but damages spinothalamic and corticospinal tracts.'),
('72061033-ba2f-5ef6-8cd9-ddc2aaeac82b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The spinal reflex for micturition is mediated by:', '["Thoracic splanchnic nerves mediated physiological mechanism","Cervical micturition center mediated physiological mechanism","Phrenic nerve mediated physiological mechanism","Lumbar sympathetic mediated physiological mechanism","Sacral parasympathetic (S2-S4) and somatic (pudendal nerve) centers"]'::jsonb, 'Sacral parasympathetic (S2-S4) and somatic (pudendal nerve) centers', 'S2-S4 coordinates parasympathetic (detrusor contraction) and somatic (pudendal, sphincter relaxation).'),
('4c152912-8120-509a-a3f2-e0d66cee6bb8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with spinal shock (acute complete cord transection) initially presents with:', '["Spastic paralysis and hyperreflexia","Only motor deficits with preserved sensation","Flaccid paralysis, areflexia, and loss of all sensation below the lesion","Brown-Sequard pattern of deficits","Spasticity and clonus"]'::jsonb, 'Flaccid paralysis, areflexia, and loss of all sensation below the lesion', 'Spinal shock causes flaccid paralysis and areflexia. Spasticity develops after weeks.'),
('267b2cee-ed8c-575b-8c38-f23400b3b54c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Superficial reflexes (e.g., abdominal, cremasteric) are:', '["Polysynaptic cutaneous reflexes mediated by spinal cord and brainstem","Vestibular reflexes mediated physiological mechanism","Monosynaptic stretch reflexes mediated physiological mechanism","Tonic reflexes mediated physiological mechanism","Autonomic reflexes mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Polysynaptic cutaneous reflexes mediated by spinal cord and brainstem', 'Superficial reflexes are polysynaptic cutaneous reflexes, lost in UMN lesions.'),
('ca701684-6e00-53a8-ba37-61610c5582b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Babinski sign (extensor plantar response) is an UMN sign. In what direction does the great toe move?', '["Plantar flexion mediated physiological mechanism","Lateral deviation mediated physiological mechanism","Flexion of all toes mediated physiological mechanism","Dorsiflexion (extension) and fanning of toes","No movement mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsiflexion (extension) and fanning of toes', 'UMN lesion causes dorsiflexion (extension) of the great toe with fanning of other toes.'),
('f537eeb8-8a8c-5730-a528-5a60919aaec4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The spinal cord level corresponding to spinous process T11 is approximately:', '["Spinal cord segment T11","Spinal cord segment L3-L4","Spinal cord segment T6","Spinal cord segment C7","Spinal cord segment S1"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord segment L3-L4', 'T11 vertebra corresponds approximately to L3-L4 spinal cord segments.'),
('f1268df5-0b13-5baf-b5bf-0787dda9a766'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The conus medullaris is located at which vertebral level in adults?', '["L3-L4","L1-L2","S1-S2","T12-L1","L4-L5"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2', 'The spinal cord (conus medullaris) typically ends at L1-L2 in adults.'),
('ecc93c9d-6ce9-5113-b27b-6fcb12b92099'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Cauda equina syndrome typically presents with:', '["Spastic paraplegia mediated physiological mechanism","UMN signs mediated physiological mechanism","LMN signs in the lower limbs with saddle anesthesia","Babinski sign mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperreflexia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'LMN signs in the lower limbs with saddle anesthesia', 'Cauda equina syndrome causes LMN flaccid paralysis and saddle anesthesia.'),
('89c0bfa5-fcd2-5d5d-b674-02651b70af36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Hoffmann reflex (H-reflex) is the electrophysiological correlate of which reflex?', '["Cutaneous reflex","Flexor withdrawal reflex","Crossed extensor reflex","Stretch reflex","Golgi tendon reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Stretch reflex', 'The H-reflex is the electrical analog of the monosynaptic stretch reflex.'),
('6d2cd29c-d728-5ff7-ae25-324777615c86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The clasp-knife phenomenon (initial resistance that suddenly gives way) is characteristic of:', '["Dystonia","Spasticity","Rigidity","Paratonia","Chorea"]'::jsonb, 'Spasticity', 'Clasp-knife spasticity is characteristic of UMN lesions, velocity-dependent.'),
('f8dbc77e-1ae5-5d5f-a088-494e5f528f66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which spinal segment contains the phrenic nucleus (innervating the diaphragm)?', '["C3-C5","C1-C2","C6-C8","T3-T5","T1-T2"]'::jsonb, 'C3-C5', 'The phrenic nucleus is at C3-C5 (primarily C4).'),
('1893235b-0bd5-51ef-9d2b-76a5d37e4d2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Posterior cord syndrome (tabes dorsalis) is characterized by:', '["Spastic paresis","Loss of fine touch and proprioception with preserved pain and motor function","Loss of pain and temperature with preserved motion","Hemiparesis","Complete paralysis with preserved sensation"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of fine touch and proprioception with preserved pain and motor function', 'Tabes dorsalis involves dorsal column degeneration, causing loss of proprioception and fine touch.'),
('89adb70d-f534-526d-b459-33d26aa9c547'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Clonus (rhythmic involuntary muscle contraction) is typically seen in:', '["Basal ganglia lesions","LMN lesions","Peripheral neuropathy","Cerebellar lesions","UMN lesions"]'::jsonb, 'UMN lesions', 'Clonus is a feature of UMN lesions due to stretch reflex hyperexcitability.'),
('fd88aa9b-3ea0-5f5f-91a2-9211cb5e1383'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The crossed extensor reflex is a component of:', '["Stretch reflex","Tonic neck reflex","Postural reflex","Golgi tendon reflex","Flexor withdrawal reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor withdrawal reflex', 'The crossed extensor reflex occurs as part of the flexor withdrawal reflex.'),
('4ee0958c-ec4a-5d92-adb1-c801df1b0faf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'All of the following are monosynaptic reflexes EXCEPT:', '["Achilles reflex","Patellar reflex","Abdominal reflex","Triceps reflex","Biceps reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal reflex', 'The abdominal reflex is a polysynaptic superficial reflex.'),
('c925bc03-a951-535a-92af-5aab50a9e24f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Babinski sign is present in all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Post-ictal state","Corticospinal tract lesions","Normal newborns","Peripheral neuropathy with absent reflexes","Deep sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy with absent reflexes', 'Peripheral neuropathy (LMN) causes absent reflexes but NOT Babinski sign.'),
('8fce2cd0-5d1d-5717-824e-bff09bbb409f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The dermatome map shows that the C6 dermatome includes:', '["Shoulder area","Little finger","Ring finger","Thumb and index finger","Middle finger"]'::jsonb, 'Thumb and index finger', 'C6 dermatome includes thumb and index finger (radial side). C7 = middle finger, C8 = ring/little fingers.'),
('4e36a429-3920-5f2a-9496-82b2d4148275'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A lesion at spinal segment T1 would affect which of the following?', '["Diaphragm function","Paralysis of legs only","Hand muscles and Horner syndrome","Biceps reflex","Quadriceps function"]'::jsonb, 'Hand muscles and Horner syndrome', 'T1 contains ciliospinal center and contributes to brachial plexus. Lesions cause hand weakness and Horner syndrome.'),
('9aa34b1e-60ff-5fa3-be53-d739879cbd32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Lhermitte sign (electric shock sensation down the spine on neck flexion) is associated with:', '["Anterior horn cell disease","Spinal cord transection","Dorsal column pathology","Basal ganglia disease","Cerebellar degeneration"]'::jsonb, 'Dorsal column pathology', 'Lhermitte sign is due to dorsal column irritation, seen in MS, cervical spondylosis, B12 deficiency.'),
('ba82c191-0216-5fb6-a3fc-11e24dda7224'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The sympathetic nervous system originates from which spinal cord segments?', '["C1-C4","S2-S4","C5-T1","L3-S1","T1-L2"]'::jsonb, 'T1-L2', 'Sympathetic = thoracolumbar (T1-L2). Parasympathetic = craniosacral.'),
('c995993d-7b10-5fb5-9bc9-7a6fb29090b5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The primary neurotransmitter at the ganglia in both sympathetic and parasympathetic systems is:', '["Norepinephrine","Serotonin","Epinephrine","Dopamine","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine', 'All preganglionic autonomic fibers release ACh acting on nicotinic receptors at ganglia.'),
('caed7cd4-57dc-53dd-aae4-90bee0a4f8d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The postganglionic neurotransmitter for most sympathetic fibers is:', '["Acetylcholine","Norepinephrine","Dopamine","Histamine","Serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine', 'Most postganglionic sympathetic fibers release norepinephrine. Exceptions: sweat glands (ACh).'),
('28b7754b-a9a1-5bd8-9dcd-cba09a7b9421'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Sympathetic stimulation causes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Increased peristalsis and secretion in the gut","Vasoconstriction of skin blood vessels","Pupillary dilation","Increased heart rate and contractility","Bronchodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Increased peristalsis and secretion in the gut', 'Sympathetic stimulation DECREASES GI peristalsis and secretion.'),
('4f6c0a9f-4a0b-5e02-800e-ec5befd6e92e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Parasympathetic stimulation causes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Increased salivation","Pupillary constriction","Ejaculation","Decreased heart rate","Bronchoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Ejaculation', 'Ejaculation is a sympathetic function. Parasympathetic causes erection.'),
('91488333-9fdb-554c-9326-cdb5fb788c3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Alpha-1 adrenergic receptors mediate which of the following?', '["Vasodilation in skeletal muscle","Lipolysis","Vasoconstriction in skin and viscera","Increased heart rate","Bronchodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Vasoconstriction in skin and viscera', 'Alpha-1 receptors (Gq-coupled) mediate vasoconstriction.'),
('b2579130-6b03-5f21-a5d4-5a90c7156317'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Beta-1 adrenergic receptors are predominantly located in the:', '["Heart","Blood vessels","Sweat glands","Bronchial smooth muscle","Uterus"]'::jsonb, 'Heart', 'Beta-1 receptors are primarily in the heart (increased HR and contractility).'),
('12df3791-f589-511d-a20c-cacb2523ba3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is an example of a sympathetic cholinergic (non-adrenergic) fiber?', '["Postganglionic fibers to sweat glands","Preganglionic sympathetic fibers","Postganglionic fibers to the iris dilator","Postganglionic fibers to GI tract","Postganglionic fibers to blood vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Postganglionic fibers to sweat glands', 'Postganglionic sympathetic fibers to sweat glands release ACh (cholinergic sympathetic).'),
('47931608-3406-5fb2-a207-044bc5a02c67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Muscarinic receptors (M1-M5) are located:', '["At neuromuscular junctions mediated physiological mechanism","On target organs of postganglionic parasympathetic and some sympathetic (sweat) fibers","At autonomic ganglia mediated physiological mechanism","On the SA node only mediated physiological mechanism","On the adrenal medulla mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'On target organs of postganglionic parasympathetic and some sympathetic (sweat) fibers', 'Muscarinic receptors are on target organs of postganglionic parasympathetic and cholinergic sympathetic fibers.'),
('189ecfbc-9582-582d-8d08-daf1a4897562'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Nicotinic receptors are found at all of the following locations EXCEPT:', '["Sweat glands","Neuromuscular junction","Autonomic ganglia","CNS","Adrenal medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Sweat glands', 'Sweat glands have muscarinic receptors (M3), not nicotinic.'),
('bbc66c2a-583d-5c44-8aae-5f811210f9e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) results from a lesion of which system?', '["Parasympathetic cervical outflow","Sympathetic chain","Oculomotor nerve","Cranial nerve III","Cervical spinal cord only"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic chain', 'Horner syndrome is from interruption of sympathetic supply to the head and neck.'),
('62f8b588-536f-543a-9f63-4726f881ae08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The baroreceptor reflex is mediated by which cranial nerves?', '["CN IX (glossopharyngeal) and CN X","CN V and VII mediated physiological mechanism","CN X and XI mediated physiological mechanism","CN I and II mediated physiological mechanism","CN VII and VIII mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'CN IX (glossopharyngeal) and CN X', 'CN IX (carotid sinus) and CN X (aortic arch) carry baroreceptor afferents.'),
('948f724c-14a8-5caf-9573-08a58fc039ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Stimulation of the carotid sinus baroreceptors leads to:', '["Increased heart rate and blood pressure","Increased contractility only","Mydriasis","Decreased heart rate and blood pressure","Bronchoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased heart rate and blood pressure', 'Carotid sinus stretch causes reflex bradycardia and vasodilation via vagal activation and sympathetic inhibition.'),
('3da8d8a2-c26b-57ae-94fc-21b071866fbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The adrenal medulla is essentially a modified sympathetic ganglion that secretes:', '["Dopamine mediated physiological mechanism","Acetylcholine mediated physiological mechanism","Cortisol mediated physiological mechanism","Epinephrine (~80%) and norepinephrine","Histamine mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Epinephrine (~80%) and norepinephrine', 'Adrenal medulla secretes ~80% epinephrine and ~20% norepinephrine.'),
('d54f8c17-5a49-54bd-a4ef-06ae7db1ae37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Micturition is regulated by which combination of autonomic and somatic innervation?', '["Sympathetic only","Somatic only","Parasympathetic (S2-S4): detrusor contraction; Somatic (pudendal): external sphincter relaxation","Sympathetic: detrusor contraction; Parasympathetic: sphincter contraction","Parasympathetic only"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic (S2-S4): detrusor contraction; Somatic (pudendal): external sphincter relaxation', 'Parasympathetic (pelvic nerve) contracts detrusor; somatic (pudendal) relaxes external sphincter.'),
('f4a686f8-1472-51bb-9d85-c2e58a3ee0f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'During the fight-or-flight response, cardiac output increases due to:', '["M2-mediated chronotropy","Alpha-1 mediated bradycardia","Beta-2 mediated vasoconstriction","Beta-1 mediated increased heart rate and contractility","Alpha-2 mediated presynaptic inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-1 mediated increased heart rate and contractility', 'Beta-1 receptors increase heart rate (positive chronotropy) and contractility (positive inotropy).'),
('cbc66687-006c-5b5b-8771-6f7d995c1af7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The vasomotor center in the medulla oblongata is located in the:', '["Nucleus tractus solitarius","Nucleus ambiguus","Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus","Rostral ventrolateral medulla","Hypoglossal nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Rostral ventrolateral medulla', 'The RVLM contains sympathetic premotor neurons projecting to the IML.'),
('c6ce068e-6592-5ab4-ad6e-132cff7cb302'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with a complete spinal cord transection at T5 will have which effect on blood pressure?', '["Loss of supraspinal vasomotor control, causing hypotension with autonomic dysreflexia","No change in blood pressure regulation mediated physiological mechanism","Hypertension below the lesion mediated physiological mechanism","Orthostatic hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","Hypertension above the lesion only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of supraspinal vasomotor control, causing hypotension with autonomic dysreflexia', 'High cord transection causes initial hypotension; autonomic dysreflexia develops later.'),
('933726f5-decc-512c-bf87-926b3f1ddcc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Autonomic dysreflexia in patients with high spinal cord injury above T6 is triggered by:', '["Hyperventilation","Cold exposure to the face","Visual stimulation","Visceral stimulation below the lesion","Valsalva maneuver"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral stimulation below the lesion', 'Autonomic dysreflexia is triggered by noxious stimuli below the lesion, most commonly bladder distension.'),
('2db25711-14b2-52b6-903c-2f3c49d88d45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Beta-2 adrenergic agonists (e.g., salbutamol) cause:', '["Increased heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","GI contraction mediated physiological mechanism","Vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism","Bronchodilation and vasodilation in skeletal muscle","Mydriasis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Bronchodilation and vasodilation in skeletal muscle', 'Beta-2 agonists cause bronchodilation and vasodilation in skeletal muscle.'),
('d220480b-af7b-5f56-8614-6363fe78739f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Alpha-2 adrenergic receptors are primarily located:', '["On the adrenal medulla","At autonomic ganglia","Presynaptically","At the NMJ","On target organs"]'::jsonb, 'Presynaptically', 'Alpha-2 receptors are primarily presynaptic autoreceptors inhibiting norepinephrine release.'),
('be733c5d-7f06-5680-98d4-cfac3037764b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Bezold-Jarisch reflex involves:', '["Tachycardia in response to stretch of the right atrium","Pupillary light reflex","Bronchoconstriction in response to pulmonary embolism","Bradycardia and hypotension in response to chemical stimuli in the heart","Vomiting reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia and hypotension in response to chemical stimuli in the heart', 'The Bezold-Jarisch reflex causes bradycardia and hypotension in response to cardiac irritants.'),
('e88b5304-fe1e-5e1c-b7d6-3b16836e1e20'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Bainbridge reflex refers to:', '["Increased heart rate in response to increased right atrial pressure","Decreased heart rate in response to increased BP","Carotid sinus reflex","Reflex bradycardia from ocular pressure","Pulmonary stretch reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Increased heart rate in response to increased right atrial pressure', 'The Bainbridge reflex: increased right atrial pressure causes increased heart rate.'),
('006debc0-26e6-599c-9d66-26c1c3bae630'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is a function of the parasympathetic nervous system in the eye?', '["Relaxes the ciliary muscle mediated physiological mechanism","Increases intraocular pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Causes lens flattening mediated physiological mechanism","Contracts the ciliary muscle for accommodation and constricts the pupil","Dilates the pupil mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Contracts the ciliary muscle for accommodation and constricts the pupil', 'Parasympathetic (CN III) causes miosis and accommodation for near vision.'),
('fd3d193f-57f5-55ce-9cc2-34bdccd322e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Its main plexuses are:', '["Celiac and mesenteric plexus","Cardiac and pulmonary plexus","Hypogastric and pelvic plexus","Myenteric (Auerbach''s) and submucosal","Solar plexus"]'::jsonb, 'Myenteric (Auerbach''s) and submucosal', 'ENS: myenteric (motility) and submucosal (secretion/absorption) plexuses.'),
('c9a9a9eb-e36f-548b-992f-8441100891de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Sympathetic preganglionic neuron cell bodies are located in the:', '["Intermediolateral cell column (IML) at T1-L2","Ventral horn of spinal cord","Dorsal root ganglia","Sacral parasympathetic nucleus","Lateral horn of the cervical cord"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediolateral cell column (IML) at T1-L2', 'The IML (lateral horn) at T1-L2 contains sympathetic preganglionic neurons.'),
('8f9ac292-c8e0-55cb-bc56-2abe831b2934'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The white rami communicantes contain:', '["Sensory fibers only","Postganglionic sympathetic fibers","Somatic motor fibers","Preganglionic sympathetic fibers","Parasympathetic fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Preganglionic sympathetic fibers', 'White rami contain preganglionic (myelinated) sympathetic fibers from T1-L2.'),
('48c695fb-eab4-5f81-bb2c-adda992d531c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The ciliospinal reflex (pupillary dilation in response to painful neck stimulation) is a:', '["Parasympathetic reflex","Pupillary light reflex","Sympathetic reflex","Somatic reflex","Vestibular reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic reflex', 'The ciliospinal reflex is a sympathetic reflex: pain afferents to T1-T2, then sympathetic chain.'),
('1e55b87c-d00c-5907-b250-ea478b03975a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which adrenergic receptor subtype mediates the inhibition of insulin release from pancreatic beta cells?', '["Alpha-2","Beta-1","Alpha-1","Beta-3","Beta-2"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-2', 'Alpha-2 adrenergic receptors on pancreatic beta cells inhibit insulin secretion.'),
('9d3c91d7-76bc-572a-8dcd-f4d9fd874a28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The hypothalamus is the primary regulator of all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Body temperature","Autonomic activity","Endocrine function via pituitary","Thirst and hunger","Motor coordination"]'::jsonb, 'Motor coordination', 'Motor coordination is a cerebellar function.'),
('e816d0f7-763e-5a50-8ddf-e1cd208f0b88'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus is the:', '["Sexual behavior center","Master circadian pacemaker","Temperature regulation center","Thirst center","Feeding center"]'::jsonb, 'Master circadian pacemaker', 'The SCN receives retinal input via retinohypothalamic tract and generates circadian rhythms.'),
('db76236d-7d9b-509a-9971-bb0d0ee2804b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The preoptic area of the hypothalamus contains the:', '["Thirst center","Circadian pacemaker","Thermoregulatory center","Feeding center","Sexual dimorphic nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Thermoregulatory center', 'The preoptic area initiates heat loss mechanisms (sweating, vasodilation).'),
('28dd7086-f006-5f36-a11d-79188991536f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The lateral hypothalamic area contains the:', '["Satiety center","Pleasure center","Thirst center","Sleep center","Feeding center"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding center', 'Lateral hypothalamus = feeding center. Lesions cause anorexia.'),
('d62d4d99-0527-5840-a187-f581ed85be4b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The amygdala is primarily involved in:', '["Visual processing mediated physiological mechanism","Emotion processing, especially fear and aggression","Memory consolidation mediated physiological mechanism","Language comprehension mediated physiological mechanism","Motor coordination mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Emotion processing, especially fear and aggression', 'The amygdala is critical for processing emotions, particularly fear.'),
('9e674953-818e-55fd-be5f-d3db02cfcdd2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, The hippocampus is essential for:', '["Declarative (explicit) memory formation and spatial navigation","Language production mediated physiological mechanism","Emotional responses mediated physiological mechanism","Procedural memory mediated physiological mechanism","Motor learning mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Declarative (explicit) memory formation and spatial navigation', 'The hippocampus is crucial for consolidation of declarative memories and spatial navigation.'),
('487501df-a106-57aa-b864-cfb623223515'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Damage to the mammillary bodies (as in Korsakoff syndrome) results in:', '["Apraxia","Aphasia","Loss of motor function","Agnosia","Anterograde amnesia and confabulation"]'::jsonb, 'Anterograde amnesia and confabulation', 'Korsakoff syndrome (thiamine deficiency) causes anterograde amnesia and confabulation.'),
('1da58fd7-5f80-558e-8a02-0ace73dd37ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The Papez circuit (limbic system) includes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Anterior thalamic nucleus","Cingulate gyrus","Mammillary bodies","Amygdala","Hippocampus"]'::jsonb, 'Amygdala', 'The amygdala is part of the limbic system but NOT the Papez circuit.'),
('d4ff825a-3665-52b5-b5a7-be07333af44f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The orbitofrontal cortex is primarily involved in:', '["Spatial navigation mediated physiological mechanism","Visual processing mediated physiological mechanism","Motor coordination mediated physiological mechanism","Language production mediated physiological mechanism","Decision-making, reward, and impulse control"]'::jsonb, 'Decision-making, reward, and impulse control', 'Orbitofrontal cortex is involved in reward, decision-making, and impulse control.'),
('b697a2b8-4511-5b4d-8620-ea8753218d56'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The dominant hemisphere for language in ~95% of right-handed individuals is the:', '["Left hemisphere","Frontal lobe","Depends on handedness alone","Both hemispheres equally","Right hemisphere"]'::jsonb, 'Left hemisphere', 'Left hemisphere is dominant for language in 95-99% of right-handed individuals.'),
('3b2f4c75-879c-5f97-90e7-b2b7d943652a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Broca''s aphasia (expressive aphasia) is characterized by:', '["Non-fluent, effortful speech with relatively preserved comprehension","Fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension","Loss of fluency in writing only","Inability to repeat words with intact fluency","Complete loss of all language function"]'::jsonb, 'Non-fluent, effortful speech with relatively preserved comprehension', 'Broca''s aphasia: non-fluent, telegraphic speech with preserved comprehension.'),
('5da46dc4-83c1-54e6-8609-d7872d4f50cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Wernicke''s aphasia (receptive aphasia) is characterized by:', '["Difficulty naming objects only mediated physiological mechanism","Inability to repeat words mediated physiological mechanism","Loss of articulation (dysarthria) only mediated physiological mechanism","Fluent, grammatical speech that is meaningless (word salad) with poor comprehension","Non-fluent speech with good comprehension mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Fluent, grammatical speech that is meaningless (word salad) with poor comprehension', 'Wernicke''s aphasia: fluent but meaningless speech with impaired comprehension.'),
('203df32a-e38f-555e-939f-00310224cea0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The arcuate fasciculus connects which two language areas?', '["Heschl''s gyrus and Broca''s area","Broca''s area and the motor cortex","Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas","Angular gyrus and Wernicke''s area","Visual cortex and Wernicke''s area"]'::jsonb, 'Broca''s and Wernicke''s areas', 'Arcuate fasciculus connects Broca''s (inferior frontal) with Wernicke''s (superior temporal) areas.'),
('96600eec-a1d0-53bd-888b-cf260c59b8a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Conduction aphasia (from arcuate fasciculus lesion) is characterized by:', '["Fluent speech with poor comprehension mediated physiological mechanism","Non-fluent speech mediated physiological mechanism","Disproportionate difficulty with repetition with relatively fluent spontaneous speech","Pure word deafness mediated physiological mechanism","Complete language loss mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Disproportionate difficulty with repetition with relatively fluent spontaneous speech', 'Conduction aphasia: fluent speech, good comprehension, but impaired repetition.'),
('9460de83-de5b-536f-ae8e-2d3b6b4c9479'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Transcortical motor aphasia differs from Broca''s aphasia in that:', '["Writing is intact","Naming is preserved","Repetition is preserved","Comprehension is worse","Speech is more fluent"]'::jsonb, 'Repetition is preserved', 'Transcortical motor aphasia: non-fluent speech like Broca''s but repetition is preserved.'),
('8c06c704-885c-53e9-86fe-bb51228b5472'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The fornix is the major efferent pathway from the:', '["Hippocampus","Thalamus","Amygdala","Cingulate gyrus","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Hippocampus', 'The fornix is the major output tract of the hippocampus.'),
('f56f01b4-49ef-543d-ac3f-22c51ffccc76'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum) is a key structure in the:', '["Memory consolidation","Visual processing","Reward pathway","Pain modulation","Motor control circuit"]'::jsonb, 'Reward pathway', 'Nucleus accumbens is central to the mesolimbic dopamine reward pathway.'),
('0c6bfa0f-f20c-5002-a4ee-6794096df225'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Lesion of the ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus causes:', '["Polydipsia","Hypothermia","Hyperphagia and obesity","Insomnia","Anorexia and weight loss"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperphagia and obesity', 'Ventromedial nucleus = satiety center. Lesions cause hyperphagia and obesity.'),
('5fa5cb55-dd79-5e5f-8b6c-f4f0bcde977e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus receives direct visual input from the retina via the:', '["Geniculohypothalamic tract","Retinohypothalamic tract","Accessory optic tract","Optic radiation","Optic tract"]'::jsonb, 'Retinohypothalamic tract', 'The retinohypothalamic tract from melanopsin-containing ganglion cells projects to the SCN.'),
('6d33f3bc-2434-5192-88e0-bacac85518cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The paraventricular (PVN) and supraoptic (SON) nuclei of the hypothalamus produce:', '["CRH and TRH","GnRH","GHRH","ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin","Somatostatin"]'::jsonb, 'ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin', 'PVN and SON produce ADH and oxytocin, transported to posterior pituitary.'),
('722191d7-9f3d-5e8b-8f27-b23a2945d109'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The hypophysiotropic area (mediobasal hypothalamus, including arcuate nucleus) produces:', '["Thyroid hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Gonadal steroids mediated physiological mechanism","Posterior pituitary hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Releasing and inhibiting hormones that regulate the anterior pituitary","Corticosteroids mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Releasing and inhibiting hormones that regulate the anterior pituitary', 'The arcuate nucleus produces releasing/inhibiting hormones for anterior pituitary regulation.'),
('8c75da95-afea-5eb1-8e87-8e733140b620'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which lobe of the brain is most associated with personality, judgment, and planning?', '["Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe","Frontal lobe","Parietal lobe","Insula"]'::jsonb, 'Frontal lobe', 'The prefrontal cortex (frontal lobe) is critical for executive functions and personality.'),
('2584ae0a-4a4b-5f69-8aea-273663c8d676'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The angular gyrus (Brodmann area 39) is involved in:', '["Motor coordination","Taste processing","Face recognition","Reading and language processing","Sound localization"]'::jsonb, 'Reading and language processing', 'Angular gyrus is important for reading and language (alexia with agraphia when damaged).'),
('25a07ee4-686f-5920-a362-37660f189096'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Gerstmann syndrome (dominant parietal lobe lesion) includes all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Acalculia","Left-right disorientation","Agnosia for faces","Finger agnosia","Agraphia"]'::jsonb, 'Agnosia for faces', 'Gerstmann syndrome: finger agnosia, L-R disorientation, acalculia, agraphia. Prosopagnosia is occipitotemporal.'),
('829988e1-5246-57fd-817c-6cca19122759'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Hemispatial neglect is most commonly associated with lesions of the:', '["Left temporal lobe","Cerebellum","Occipital lobe","Left frontal lobe","Right parietal lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Right parietal lobe', 'Hemispatial neglect is far more common with RIGHT parietal lobe lesions.'),
('3a7c512c-c5a8-599f-80e5-92b19d097d4d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Prosopagnosia (inability to recognize faces) is associated with lesions of the:', '["Fusiform face area (FFA) in the fusiform gyrus","Angular gyrus mediated physiological mechanism","Amygdala mediated physiological mechanism","Prefrontal cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Hippocampus mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Fusiform face area (FFA) in the fusiform gyrus', 'Prosopagnosia is due to bilateral occipitotemporal (fusiform gyrus) lesions.'),
('77f9c54d-c901-527c-a8c5-bbc598095a75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Delirium is characterized by all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Usually reversible","Disturbance in sleep-wake cycle","Normal EEG with posterior dominant rhythm","Acute onset and fluctuating course","Impaired attention and consciousness"]'::jsonb, 'Normal EEG with posterior dominant rhythm', 'In delirium, EEG shows generalized slowing, not normal.'),
('e7d3a8de-edd5-5694-9534-78a234c55b4c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Alzheimer disease is associated with:', '["Senile plaques (beta-amyloid) and neurofibrillary tangles","Loss of dopaminergic neurons in SNc","Huntingtin protein aggregates","Pick bodies","Lewy bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Senile plaques (beta-amyloid) and neurofibrillary tangles', 'Alzheimer''s pathology: beta-amyloid plaques and tau-containing neurofibrillary tangles.'),
('903aaac7-6c03-56ac-ad9a-93a8b31ae562'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The nucleus basalis of Meynert, which degenerates in Alzheimer disease, is the major source of:', '["Acetylcholine to the cerebral cortex","GABA to the thalamus","Serotonin to the cortex","Dopamine to the cortex","Norepinephrine to the hippocampus"]'::jsonb, 'Acetylcholine to the cerebral cortex', 'Nucleus basalis is the primary source of cholinergic innervation to the cortex.'),
('17cf9be2-48b4-5557-8c3f-f198a64894cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'At the neuromuscular junction, acetylcholine acts on which type of receptor?', '["Beta-2 adrenergic","Nicotinic (N1, Nn) - neuronal type","Muscarinic","Alpha-1 adrenergic","Nicotinic (N2, Nm) - muscle type"]'::jsonb, 'Nicotinic (N2, Nm) - muscle type', 'At the NMJ, ACh acts on nicotinic N2 (muscle-type) ionotropic receptors.'),
('f268e085-fb45-5d38-ab4f-8d5b9f6402f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS is:', '["Aspartate","Dopamine","Glutamate","Acetylcholine","GABA"]'::jsonb, 'GABA', 'GABA is the primary inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS (~30% of all synapses).'),
('f4b7b362-88e7-5d1d-b451-0030ca0254f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The major excitatory neurotransmitter in the CNS is:', '["Glycine","Glutamate","Serotonin","GABA","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate', 'Glutamate is the primary excitatory neurotransmitter in the CNS.'),
('3fc6cf54-5810-5c1f-a26a-685e965b6457'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Glycine is an inhibitory neurotransmitter primarily found in the:', '["Thalamus","Cerebellum","Basal ganglia","Spinal cord and brainstem","Cerebral cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord and brainstem', 'Glycine is the major inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord and brainstem.'),
('57f876b5-d897-5a4d-87c9-f233e869fa22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Dopamine is synthesized from which precursor?', '["Tryptophan to 5-HTP to Serotonin","Tyrosine to L-DOPA to Dopamine","Choline to Acetylcholine","Glutamate to GABA","Histidine to Histamine"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine to L-DOPA to Dopamine', 'Dopamine synthesis: Tyrosine -> L-DOPA -> Dopamine. Tyrosine hydroxylase is rate-limiting.'),
('68cfc93e-d8cf-5f31-9f4d-20cedef30bf1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The NMDA receptor requires which co-agonist in addition to glutamate for activation?', '["Dopamine","Acetylcholine","ATP","GABA","Glycine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'NMDA receptors require both glutamate and glycine (or D-serine) as co-agonists.'),
('13c01336-25e4-50ed-9053-2166f3081ea5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following monoamine neurotransmitters is derived from tryptophan?', '["Histamine","Epinephrine","Serotonin","Norepinephrine","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin', 'Serotonin (5-HT) is derived from tryptophan.'),
('f4297342-564b-573d-91fb-f59c08976753'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Substance P is a neuropeptide involved in:', '["Motor control mediated physiological mechanism","Transmission of pain (nociception) in the dorsal horn","Descending pain inhibition mediated physiological mechanism","Sleep regulation mediated physiological mechanism","Memory formation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Transmission of pain (nociception) in the dorsal horn', 'Substance P is released by nociceptive C fibers in the dorsal horn.'),
('6f6efb19-0250-5c71-b668-a801fdd2971f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The enzyme acetylcholinesterase is responsible for:', '["Activation of muscarinic receptors","Reuptake of acetylcholine into the presynaptic terminal","Regulation of choline transport","Breakdown of acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft","Synthesis of acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Breakdown of acetylcholine in the synaptic cleft', 'Acetylcholinesterase hydrolyzes ACh in the synaptic cleft.'),
('2b82ec78-d68c-5c95-9667-cda426522d24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which neurotransmitter is released from the presynaptic terminal of the photoreceptor in the dark?', '["Dopamine","Acetylcholine","Glutamate","GABA","Serotonin"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate', 'In the dark, photoreceptors are depolarized and tonically release glutamate.'),
('ab52da50-a956-5c02-9b66-f42e167876cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The rate-limiting enzyme in catecholamine synthesis is:', '["Tyrosine hydroxylase","Monoamine oxidase","DOPA decarboxylase","PNMT","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine hydroxylase', 'Tyrosine hydroxylase is the rate-limiting step in catecholamine synthesis.'),
('68577e74-9405-5ae0-b865-edbd0b5b60df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is NOT a typical neurotransmitter found in the brain?', '["Hydrogen sulfide","Endocannabinoids","Sulfuric acid","Nitric oxide","Carbon monoxide"]'::jsonb, 'Sulfuric acid', 'Sulfuric acid is not a neurotransmitter. NO, CO, H2S are gaseous neurotransmitters.'),
('a2bc6e60-b96a-5b1f-ac79-7eee8b663192'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Histamine in the brain is produced by neurons located in the:', '["Substantia nigra mediated physiological mechanism","Locus coeruleus mediated physiological mechanism","Ventral tegmental area mediated physiological mechanism","Raphe nuclei mediated physiological mechanism","Tuberomammillary nucleus of the posterior hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Tuberomammillary nucleus of the posterior hypothalamus', 'Tuberomammillary nucleus (posterior hypothalamus) is the sole source of brain histamine.'),
('43461c83-0af8-5448-a7c1-7d298cdd376a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Cocaine exerts its effects primarily by:', '["Blocking serotonin receptors mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibiting MAO mediated physiological mechanism","Releasing monoamines from vesicles mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking the reuptake of dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin","Activating dopamine receptors mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking the reuptake of dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin', 'Cocaine inhibits DAT, NET, and SERT, increasing synaptic monoamines.'),
('cc375e1c-af17-5a77-9b6e-9cf9ebd7e68f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The enzyme monoamine oxidase (MAO) is responsible for:', '["Converting dopamine to norepinephrine","Reuptake of serotonin","Inactivation of acetylcholine","Synthesis of catecholamines","Oxidative deamination (breakdown) of monoamines"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidative deamination (breakdown) of monoamines', 'MAO oxidatively deaminates monoamines (dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin).'),
('2f9e1854-81ff-5ec5-b9ca-37026963651c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Visceral pain is typically described as:', '["Poorly localized, dull, aching, cramping, or burning","Pulsating and severe","Radiating along a specific dermatome","Electric shock-like","Sharp, well-localized, stabbing"]'::jsonb, 'Poorly localized, dull, aching, cramping, or burning', 'Visceral pain is poorly localized and often referred to somatic sites.'),
('ad240dad-628b-5745-ba98-8fb1da00233c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Cardiac ischemic pain (angina) is most commonly referred to:', '["Lower back","Right shoulder","Anterior thigh","Epigastrium","Left shoulder and medial arm"]'::jsonb, 'Left shoulder and medial arm', 'Cardiac pain is referred to left shoulder and medial arm (T1-T2).'),
('ba363245-1dd4-5797-b851-e4b717d15c53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Renal colic pain (from ureteric stone) is typically referred to the:', '["Anterior knee","Groin and inner thigh","Epigastrium","Lower back","Shoulder"]'::jsonb, 'Groin and inner thigh', 'Ureteric pain radiates from flank to groin and inner thigh (L1-L2).'),
('6d2def47-e5ef-5b88-aa7d-1ceeeeba48d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Appendicitis pain initially begins in the periumbilical region and later localizes to the right lower quadrant. This is because:', '["Early pain is from the liver mediated physiological mechanism","The pain is always in the right lower quadrant mediated physiological mechanism","Early pain is referred (visceral, T10); later pain is somatic due to peritoneal irritation","Later pain is from the small intestine mediated physiological mechanism","The appendix innervation changes over time mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Early pain is referred (visceral, T10); later pain is somatic due to peritoneal irritation', 'Early appendicitis: visceral pain referred to T10 (periumbilical). Later: parietal peritoneal irritation localizes to RLO.'),
('fffa813c-24d2-56df-88b6-d785fa09d67d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Gallbladder pain (cholecystitis) is typically referred to the:', '["Left shoulder mediated physiological mechanism","Lower back mediated physiological mechanism","Right shoulder and right scapular region","Epigastrium only mediated physiological mechanism","Groin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Right shoulder and right scapular region', 'Gallbladder pain (phrenic nerve, C3-C5) is referred to right shoulder/scapula.'),
('37980d65-999d-5398-9abe-cfa63cd77034'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Pancreatic pain is typically referred to the:', '["Left arm","Right thigh","Shoulder","Epigastrium and mid-back","Groin"]'::jsonb, 'Epigastrium and mid-back', 'Pancreatic pain (T6-T10 via splanchnic nerves) radiates to epigastrium and mid-back.'),
('582b9890-975a-5f65-8468-2f4faee673c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The convergence-projection theory of referred pain proposes that:', '["Visceral pain is carried by separate pathways that are misrouted","The cortex is unable to distinguish visceral from somatic pain","The thalamus incorrectly processes pain signals","Visceral and somatic afferents converge on the same second-order neurons in the spinothalamic tract","Pain is purely psychological"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral and somatic afferents converge on the same second-order neurons in the spinothalamic tract', 'Convergence-projection: visceral and somatic afferents converge on same spinothalamic neurons.'),
('342b4841-81bf-5cfb-b42a-eda16637e4e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is a characteristic difference between visceral and somatic pain?', '["Somatic pain produces more autonomic symptoms","Visceral pain is better localized than somatic pain","Visceral pain is often associated with autonomic symptoms","Visceral pain does not produce referred pain","Visceral pain is sharp and discrete"]'::jsonb, 'Visceral pain is often associated with autonomic symptoms', 'Visceral pain is frequently accompanied by autonomic symptoms (nausea, sweating, pallor).'),
('8dd65926-6994-5be7-9d07-d5e60a5e15c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Phrenic nerve irritation (e.g., from subphrenic abscess) causes referred pain to the:', '["Anterior chest","Shoulder","Scapular region","Neck","Lumbar area"]'::jsonb, 'Shoulder', 'Phrenic nerve (C3-C5) irritation refers pain to the shoulder tip.'),
('ad83ff98-8e37-5daa-b840-33f721f05eb7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Sensory fibers that carry visceral pain from the pelvic organs travel with which nerves?', '["Phrenic nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Greater splanchnic nerve","Vagus nerve","Lesser splanchnic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Pelvic splanchnic nerves', 'Pelvic visceral pain is carried by pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4). Vagus does not carry pain.'),
('b10bb104-b76e-5843-9ebd-a7d87a633397'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The greater splanchnic nerve (T5-T9) transmits visceral pain from which organ?', '["Lower colon","Upper abdominal viscera","Pelvic organs","Heart","Kidneys"]'::jsonb, 'Upper abdominal viscera', 'Greater splanchnic nerve carries pain from upper GI tract, liver, gallbladder, and pancreas.'),
('793a40d5-6818-53e7-b214-ed07889103a2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Referred pain from the heart (myocardial ischemia) can be felt in the jaw because:', '["Cardiac ischemia directly affects the trigeminal ganglion","Cardiac afferents enter at T1-T4 and C3-C5, converging with trigeminal and cervical afferents","Cervical radiculopathy mimics angina","Cardiac and jaw afferents converge in the trigeminal nucleus","The vagus nerve innervates both the heart and the jaw"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac afferents enter at T1-T4 and C3-C5, converging with trigeminal and cervical afferents', 'Cardiac afferents at T1-T4 and cervical levels overlap with trigeminal (jaw) dermatomes.'),
('4f18f048-1744-5db6-be66-385df9fccb81'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which nerve carries referred pain from the diaphragm to the shoulder?', '["Vagus nerve","Greater splanchnic nerve","Phrenic nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Intercostal nerves"]'::jsonb, 'Phrenic nerve', 'Phrenic nerve (C3-C5) provides sensory innervation to the central diaphragm.'),
('dfba504e-e040-56f9-bb70-ecf4dd03a293'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Referred pain from the ureter is to which dermatome?', '["T4","S2-S4","C5","L1-L2","T10"]'::jsonb, 'L1-L2', 'Ureteric pain is referred to L1-L2 dermatomes (groin, inner thigh).'),
('63d2caa7-921f-53ac-a50b-c70eaf26e1d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'True visceral pain is often accompanied by which type of motor reflex?', '["Patellar reflex","Knee jerk","Babinski sign","Ankle clonus","Abdominal guarding"]'::jsonb, 'Abdominal guarding', 'Visceral pain often causes reflex abdominal guarding via viscerosomatic reflex.'),
('cac2c52c-f245-553b-9704-cb74db72ac89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The tonic neck reflex (asymmetric) in infants involves:', '["Extension of the arm on the face side and flexion on the occiput side when head is turned (fencin","Bilateral arm flexion mediated physiological mechanism","Leg crossing mediated physiological mechanism","Bilateral arm extension mediated physiological mechanism","No response mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Extension of the arm on the face side and flexion on the occiput side when head is turned (fencin', 'Asymmetric tonic neck reflex (ATNR): arm extends on face side, flexes on occiput side.'),
('8bec4eb8-8d81-5ca3-89f7-96c8f3f2cc9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The righting reflex involves which sensory system?', '["Visual alone mediated physiological mechanism","Auditory alone mediated physiological mechanism","Tactile alone mediated physiological mechanism","Visual, vestibular, and somatosensory (proprioceptive) input","Vestibular alone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Visual, vestibular, and somatosensory (proprioceptive) input', 'Righting reflexes integrate visual, vestibular, and somatosensory input.'),
('408f5bc3-09ab-5efc-ad63-b97fdc5ef32a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The postural reflexes in the midbrain involve which descending tract?', '["Spinothalamic tract","Dorsal column","Rubrospinal tract","Reticulospinal tract","Corticospinal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Reticulospinal tract', 'Pontine reticulospinal tract facilitates antigravity extensor tone.'),
('ba5a5105-cfc5-56dc-9744-62b003f3162c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with loss of proprioception in the legs (e.g., tabes dorsalis) will demonstrate a positive:', '["Babinski sign","Clonus","Hoffmann sign","Romberg sign","Tremor"]'::jsonb, 'Romberg sign', 'Romberg sign: patient sways/falls with eyes closed due to loss of proprioception.'),
('b9a90556-128f-5d05-b7b1-fb4d85b75600'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The lateral vestibulospinal tract is important for:', '["Vision tracking mediated physiological mechanism","Posture by facilitating antigravity muscles","Auditory reflexes mediated physiological mechanism","Pain modulation mediated physiological mechanism","Fine finger movements mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Posture by facilitating antigravity muscles', 'Lateral vestibulospinal tract facilitates extensor motor neurons for posture.'),
('813545ae-7126-5d2d-9b57-a9bcaf4169ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The ''central pattern generators'' (CPGs) for locomotion are located in the:', '["Spinal cord","Basal ganglia","Midbrain locomotor region","Cerebellum","Cerebral cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal cord', 'CPGs for rhythmic locomotion are neural networks in the spinal cord.'),
('097889f4-cdae-561e-923e-120a6d63b602'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The midbrain locomotor region (MLR) is located in the:', '["Substantia nigra mediated physiological mechanism","Pedunculopontine nucleus (PPN) region","Superior colliculus mediated physiological mechanism","Red nucleus mediated physiological mechanism","Periaqueductal gray mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Pedunculopontine nucleus (PPN) region', 'The MLR is centered on the pedunculopontine nucleus (PPN) in the midbrain tegmentum.'),
('ccee63d5-49ff-575d-bf71-1a0005c9b413'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is NOT a postural reflex?', '["Righting reflex","Flexor withdrawal reflex","Vestibulospinal reflex","Tonic neck reflex","Stretch reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Flexor withdrawal reflex', 'The flexor withdrawal reflex is a protective nociceptive reflex, not a postural reflex.'),
('be0507b1-6ac2-57c4-b56d-217e0ffcd29b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) functions to:', '["Constrict the pupil in bright light mediated physiological mechanism","Accommodate for near vision mediated physiological mechanism","Maintain visual fixation during head movement by moving the eyes in the opposite direction","Dilate the pupil in the dark mediated physiological mechanism","Focus the lens mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Maintain visual fixation during head movement by moving the eyes in the opposite direction', 'VOR: eyes move opposite to head rotation to stabilize the retinal image.'),
('5d7628b1-2366-5da2-bf35-6e1767bc1a1c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with cerebellar ataxia attempting to touch the nose will show:', '["Dystonic posturing","Bradykinesia","Intention tremor and overshoot","Smooth, accurate movement","Resting tremor"]'::jsonb, 'Intention tremor and overshoot', 'Cerebellar lesions cause dysmetria (overshoot) and intention tremor on finger-to-nose.'),
('e8cc4fd4-1539-5ea4-b3cc-ef63c1108e23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The ''pull test'' is a clinical test used to assess:', '["Muscle strength mediated physiological mechanism","Sensory loss mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebellar ataxia mediated physiological mechanism","Postural instability in Parkinson''s disease","Reflex integrity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Postural instability in Parkinson''s disease', 'The pull test assesses postural instability; Parkinson''s patients show retropulsion.'),
('349f2b6c-03b0-5d7e-b5f7-f78f6c17ede7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Gait in Parkinson''s disease is characterized by all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Difficulty initiating gait","Festination","Shuffling gait with small steps","Decreased arm swing","Wide-based ataxic gait"]'::jsonb, 'Wide-based ataxic gait', 'Wide-based ataxic gait is cerebellar. Parkinsonian gait is narrow-based and shuffling.'),
('5502acbb-bbf0-502f-a913-9c900ce6b019'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A wide-based, staggering gait (drunken gait) with inability to tandem walk is typical of:', '["Cerebellar vermis lesion","Myopathy","Peripheral neuropathy","Hemiparesis","Parkinsonism"]'::jsonb, 'Cerebellar vermis lesion', 'Midline cerebellar (vermis) lesions cause truncal ataxia: wide-based, staggering gait.'),
('65aae033-4457-5d66-97c0-96a14c607a0e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Steppage gait (foot drop) is caused by lesion of which nerve?', '["Tibial nerve","Obturator nerve","Femoral nerve","Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve","Sciatic nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Sciatic nerve', 'Foot drop (steppage gait) is caused by common peroneal nerve palsy or L5 radiculopathy.'),
('0a4dd6da-aa39-549d-9944-acd082d507fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Trendelenburg gait (pelvic drop on the swing side) indicates weakness of which muscle?', '["Hamstrings","Iliopsoas","Quadriceps","Gluteus maximus","Gluteus medius and minimus"]'::jsonb, 'Gluteus medius and minimus', 'Trendelenburg gait is due to gluteus medius/minimus weakness (superior gluteal nerve, L5-S1).'),
('c162b282-8626-580f-abdd-5bfa0af6567c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Alpha waves on EEG (8-13 Hz) are characteristically seen in which state?', '["REM sleep","Awake, relaxed, eyes closed","Coma","Deep sleep","Alert, active concentration"]'::jsonb, 'Awake, relaxed, eyes closed', 'Alpha rhythm (8-13 Hz) is seen in awake, relaxed adults with eyes closed.'),
('07a49690-c28b-598f-8a2c-3923e616248f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Beta waves (13-30 Hz) on EEG are associated with:', '["Drowsiness mediated physiological mechanism","Infant sleep mediated physiological mechanism","Deep sleep mediated physiological mechanism","Active concentration, alertness, or anxious state","Coma mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Active concentration, alertness, or anxious state', 'Beta activity is seen during alert wakefulness and active thinking.'),
('efbbe5af-ba42-5efc-8c00-4510c2dfd10c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Theta waves (4-8 Hz) are normal on EEG in:', '["Children and drowsy adults","REM sleep only","Adults during deep sleep","Coma","Alert wakefulness in adults"]'::jsonb, 'Children and drowsy adults', 'Theta waves are normal in children and drowsy adults (stage N1).'),
('36180fb3-c1cc-5c3f-8980-8e9157c952f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Delta waves (0.5-4 Hz) are the predominant rhythm in:', '["REM and stage N1","Stage N3 sleep","Drowsiness","Awake state","REM sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Stage N3 sleep', 'Delta waves are characteristic of stage N3 (slow-wave, deep) sleep.'),
('9797ba67-d14c-532f-817f-33534febf9d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Sleep spindles and K-complexes are characteristic of which sleep stage?', '["Stage N3","Stage N2","Wakefulness","Stage N1","REM sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Stage N2', 'Stage N2 sleep is characterized by sleep spindles (12-14 Hz) and K-complexes.'),
('58715d0e-dd0c-5306-8561-12e78de43240'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'REM sleep is characterized by all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Skeletal muscle atonia","Low voltage, mixed-frequency EEG","Regular pulse and respiration","Dreaming is most vivid","Rapid eye movements"]'::jsonb, 'Regular pulse and respiration', 'REM sleep has irregular pulse and respiration (autonomic instability).'),
('43592d99-c965-5855-8534-b85dd55cee3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The neural center primarily responsible for generating REM sleep is located in the:', '["Cerebral cortex","Thalamus","Medulla","Pons","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Pons', 'REM-on neurons in the pons (subcoeruleus, PPT) generate REM sleep.'),
('b6bf5c7a-3850-58cb-b0a0-7ba51ea7007f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'NREM sleep is initiated by neurons in the:', '["Raphe nuclei mediated physiological mechanism","Ventrolateral preoptic (VLPO) nucleus of the hypothalamus","Pons mediated physiological mechanism","Midbrain reticular formation mediated physiological mechanism","Locus coeruleus mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ventrolateral preoptic (VLPO) nucleus of the hypothalamus', 'VLPO (ventrolateral preoptic) nucleus is the ''sleep switch'' that inhibits arousal centers.'),
('354957d4-c0ac-58e2-b90f-f3831c064562'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The ascending reticular activating system (ARAS) is essential for:', '["Motor coordination","Pain perception","Arousal and consciousness","Sleep generation","Memory consolidation"]'::jsonb, 'Arousal and consciousness', 'The ARAS maintains wakefulness and arousal. Lesions cause coma.'),
('a588415d-62c2-55eb-8c7c-9e91cc91d2e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'In a surgical context, The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) assesses:', '["Eye opening, verbal response, and motor response","Sleep quality mediated physiological mechanism","Sensory function mediated physiological mechanism","Cognitive ability mediated physiological mechanism","Memory function mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Eye opening, verbal response, and motor response', 'GCS (3-15) assesses eye opening (1-4), verbal response (1-5), and motor response (1-6).'),
('1a8ee412-affa-5864-baf5-5615ae299c50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'A patient with a GCS of 3 (no eye opening, no verbal, no motor) is:', '["Brain dead","In persistent vegetative state","Comatose with lowest possible GCS","Locked-in syndrome","In REM sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Comatose with lowest possible GCS', 'GCS 3 is the lowest possible score, indicating deep coma. Brain death requires additional criteria.'),
('51e1f13f-733f-5402-92a9-c90424d35ee5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Locked-in syndrome (pseudo-coma) results from a lesion in the:', '["Medulla","Midbrain","Ventral pons","Basal ganglia","Cerebral cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral pons', 'Locked-in syndrome from ventral pontine lesion: paralysis of all voluntary muscles except vertical eye movements, but patient is fully conscious.'),
('ffa1d854-cde7-52c0-aa61-9cdd74825e91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The persistent vegetative state is characterized by:', '["Complete consciousness with paralysis","Minimal conscious state with inconsistent responses","Wakefulness without awareness","Brain death","Complete absence of all brain function"]'::jsonb, 'Wakefulness without awareness', 'Persistent vegetative state: intact brainstem (wakefulness) but no cortical awareness.'),
('3ad4fd1a-be5c-5e96-b126-bd3db5fabbc8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Melatonin secretion from the pineal gland is regulated by:', '["Light via retinohypothalamic tract to SCN to pineal","Direct light sensitivity of the pineal gland","Body temperature","Corticosteroid levels","Food intake"]'::jsonb, 'Light via retinohypothalamic tract to SCN to pineal', 'Melatonin pathway: retina -> retinohypothalamic tract -> SCN -> PVN -> IML -> SCG -> pineal.'),
('c7b40ec1-0bd8-5217-9002-b4996fd5f2f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Narcolepsy with cataplexy is associated with deficiency of which neuropeptide?', '["Galanin mediated physiological mechanism","Neuropeptide Y mediated physiological mechanism","Orexin (hypocretin) from the lateral hypothalamus","CRH mediated physiological mechanism","Melatonin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Orexin (hypocretin) from the lateral hypothalamus', 'Narcolepsy type 1 (with cataplexy) is caused by loss of orexin/hypocretin neurons.'),
('f54b2aaf-168d-52af-8f85-af8403100bbe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'What type of aphasia is characterized by fluent speech with poor comprehension and impaired repetition?', '["Transcortical sensory aphasia","Global aphasia","Conduction aphasia","Broca''s aphasia","Wernicke''s aphasia"]'::jsonb, 'Wernicke''s aphasia', 'Wernicke''s aphasia: fluent, meaningless speech with poor comprehension and impaired repetition.'),
('f3cd0d19-beff-5590-bc3e-f3178257fa07'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'What type of aphasia is characterized by non-fluent speech with good comprehension and good repetition?', '["Broca''s aphasia","Wernicke''s aphasia","Global aphasia","Conduction aphasia","Transcortical motor aphasia"]'::jsonb, 'Transcortical motor aphasia', 'Transcortical motor aphasia: non-fluent speech, good comprehension, good repetition (around but not in Broca''s area).'),
('ae589c7a-da99-5339-ae96-497ca25b6a37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which tract carries pain and temperature from the face?', '["Medial lemniscus","Trigeminal lemniscus","Corticobulbar tract","Spinothalamic tract","Dorsal column"]'::jsonb, 'Trigeminal lemniscus', 'The trigeminal lemniscus (trigeminothalamic tract) carries pain, temperature, and touch from the face to VPM thalamus.'),
('89fac97c-9faf-501a-90aa-a67cc9ea9c8e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'In the knee jerk reflex, which muscle contracts?', '["Quadriceps femoris","Gastrocnemius","Tibialis anterior","Hamstrings","Gluteus maximus"]'::jsonb, 'Quadriceps femoris', 'The patellar reflex tests the quadriceps femoris muscle (L2-L4, femoral nerve).'),
('a68ebfa6-c006-5ae7-9eef-979d6121e43c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The corneal reflex involves which cranial nerves?', '["CN III and IV","CN VIII and VII","CN II and III","CN V (afferent) and CN VII","CN V and XII"]'::jsonb, 'CN V (afferent) and CN VII', 'Corneal reflex: afferent via ophthalmic division of CN V, efferent via CN VII (orbicularis oculi).'),
('58ee027f-f8a9-5f01-9db2-46efa8dc2363'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The jaw jerk reflex involves which cranial nerve?', '["CN X","CN V","CN XII","CN IX","CN VII"]'::jsonb, 'CN V', 'The jaw jerk is a monosynaptic stretch reflex involving the trigeminal nerve (CN V, motor division).'),
('a67b6780-05df-5b8c-8dfc-1dc6457ca835'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The pupillary light reflex involves which cranial nerves?', '["CN III and VI","CN II (afferent) and CN III","CN V and VII","CN II and IV","CN III and IV"]'::jsonb, 'CN II (afferent) and CN III', 'Pupillary light reflex: afferent via CN II (optic), efferent via CN III (oculomotor, parasympathetic).'),
('a8ecfc3b-db51-54e5-904a-78c19d425e70'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Argyll Robertson pupils (accommodation preserved, light reflex absent) are classically associated with:', '["Diabetes mellitus","Third nerve palsy","Horner syndrome","Multiple sclerosis","Neurosyphilis"]'::jsonb, 'Neurosyphilis', 'Argyll Robertson pupils (light-near dissociation) are classically seen in neurosyphilis.'),
('92520efb-814d-56f4-b9f7-c9f1cda27915'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The flexor plantar response (normal) is mediated by which spinal segment?', '["L2-L4","C5-C6","T10-T12","L4-S2","S1-S2"]'::jsonb, 'S1-S2', 'The plantar reflex is mediated by S1-S2 spinal segments via the tibial nerve.'),
('dca41e0b-0c19-52cc-b8f6-527221b043dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'In the descending pain modulation pathway, the periaqueductal gray (PAG) projects to which nucleus?', '["Red nucleus","Substantia nigra","Locus coeruleus","Raphe magnus nucleus","Ventral tegmental area"]'::jsonb, 'Raphe magnus nucleus', 'PAG activates the raphe magnus nucleus, which sends serotonergic projections to the dorsal horn to inhibit pain.'),
('5750ae3e-27c7-5b7a-beeb-c9c8b9cc3b64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following is a feature of neuropathic pain?', '["Well-localized to the site of injury","Responds well to NSAIDs","Burning, shooting, or electric shock-like pain in a dermatomal distribution","Dull, aching pain referred to a somatic site","Associated with guarding and muscle spasm"]'::jsonb, 'Burning, shooting, or electric shock-like pain in a dermatomal distribution', 'Neuropathic pain is typically burning, shooting, or electric shock-like along a nerve distribution.'),
('a73050d7-89fc-59a1-84af-5955b259995a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Allodynia is defined as:', '["Increased sensitivity to a normally painful stimulus","Pain in response to a normally non-painful stimulus","Loss of pain sensation","Referred pain to a distant site","Spontaneous pain without any stimulus"]'::jsonb, 'Pain in response to a normally non-painful stimulus', 'Allodynia = pain from a normally innocuous stimulus (e.g., light touch on sunburned skin). Hyperalgesia = increased pain from a normally painful stimulus.'),
('22f97727-5d37-58b5-952a-4d1ff104aa0b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Hyperalgesia is defined as:', '["Increased pain sensitivity to a normally painful stimulus","Pain referred to a distant site","Pain from a normally non-painful stimulus","Complete absence of pain sensation","Spontaneous pain without stimulus"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pain sensitivity to a normally painful stimulus', 'Hyperalgesia = increased pain response to a normally painful stimulus. Allodynia = pain from a normally non-painful stimulus.'),
('61e9a297-fede-587a-8715-18d4aaf497da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Primary hyperalgesia occurs at the site of tissue injury and is primarily due to:', '["Sensitization of peripheral nociceptors by inflammatory mediators","Central sensitization in the dorsal horn","Descending pain modulation failure","Thalamic plasticity","Cortical reorganization"]'::jsonb, 'Sensitization of peripheral nociceptors by inflammatory mediators', 'Primary hyperalgesia is due to peripheral sensitization (inflammatory mediators lowering nociceptor threshold). Secondary hyperalgesia involves central sensitization.'),
('acdc80bc-c474-5847-9d2e-0d9b76902e69'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The medial forebrain bundle is the major pathway connecting the:', '["Thalamus to the cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex","Raphe nuclei and spinal cord mediated physiological mechanism","Basal ganglia to the thalamus mediated physiological mechanism","Locus coeruleus to the cerebellum mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex', 'The medial forebrain bundle carries dopaminergic fibers from VTA to nucleus accumbens and prefrontal cortex (reward pathway).'),
('07e9076b-4d88-56ab-ab9a-f13090e37715'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Which of the following best describes the blood-brain barrier at the level of the circumventricular organs?', '["It is tighter than elsewhere in the brain","It is impermeable to all substances","It allows free passage of large proteins only","It is formed by oligodendrocytes","It is absent or fenestrated, allowing sampling of blood composition"]'::jsonb, 'It is absent or fenestrated, allowing sampling of blood composition', 'Circumventricular organs (e.g., area postrema, OVLT, SFO) have fenestrated capillaries and lack BBB, allowing them to monitor blood composition.'),
('a926cff0-772e-5d86-806e-1756d029cc25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The area postrema in the medulla oblongata is the:', '["Respiratory center","Pain modulation center","Vomiting center","Cardiovascular center","Sleep center"]'::jsonb, 'Vomiting center', 'The area postrema (a circumventricular organ) is the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) for vomiting, sensitive to emetic agents in the blood.'),
('de1fbc74-07e6-509c-87a8-00ea773570f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The subfornical organ (SFO) is involved in:', '["Thirst regulation","Sleep regulation","Motor coordination","Visual processing","Pain modulation"]'::jsonb, 'Thirst regulation', 'The SFO (a circumventricular organ) detects circulating angiotensin II and signals the hypothalamus to stimulate thirst and vasopressin release.'),
('ab220d7e-120f-5887-94c6-bff34b1ec638'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Diffuse axonal injury (DAI) in traumatic brain injury typically affects which structures?', '["White matter tracts (corpus callosum, internal capsule, brainstem) due to shearing forces","Spinal cord only mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebellar hemispheres only mediated physiological mechanism","Basal ganglia only mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebral cortex only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'White matter tracts (corpus callosum, internal capsule, brainstem) due to shearing forces', 'DAI results from rotational acceleration/deceleration forces, damaging white matter tracts (corpus callosum, internal capsule, brainstem).'),
('b1adfc6a-c0ee-532c-a4ed-d76afcf7589f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Wallerian degeneration refers to:', '["Retrograde degeneration of the proximal axon","Degeneration of the cell body after axonal injury","Degeneration of the distal segment of an axon after injury","Demyelination of intact axons","Synaptic degeneration"]'::jsonb, 'Degeneration of the distal segment of an axon after injury', 'Wallerian degeneration: the distal portion of the transected axon degenerates (anterograde). Chromatolysis occurs in the cell body (retrograde).'),
('ada2c8fa-8221-598b-b50c-a26847c73f24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'On diagnostic evaluation, The most common primary brain tumor in adults is:', '["Astrocytoma","Glioblastoma multiforme","Schwannoma","Meningioma","Oligodendroglioma"]'::jsonb, 'Glioblastoma multiforme', 'Glioblastoma multiforme (WHO grade IV astrocytoma) is the most common primary brain malignancy in adults.'),
('f0bfc460-ce8a-5d0e-9356-592975062292'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'The most common primary brain tumor overall (including benign) is:', '["Pituitary adenoma","Acoustic neuroma","Meningioma","Glioblastoma","Craniopharyngioma"]'::jsonb, 'Meningioma', 'Meningioma is the most common primary brain tumor overall (accounts for ~30% of all primary brain tumors).'),
('1042f778-52b0-5de0-a341-aa8559a3cd34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'CNS', 'Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) classically presents with the triad of:', '["Tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia","Seizures, spasticity, hyperreflexia","Ataxia, nystagmus, dysarthria","Headache, vomiting, papilledema","Gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, dementia"]'::jsonb, 'Gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, dementia', 'Normal pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) triad: gait apraxia/magnetic gait, urinary incontinence, and cognitive impairment (dementia).'),
('36a633c0-49f6-524c-aae1-962b5dbe2ea3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The normal refractive power of the human eye is approximately:', '["70 D","50 D","40 D","60 D","80 D"]'::jsonb, '60 D', 'The eye has ~60 diopters of refractive power (cornea ~43D, lens ~17D).'),
('fc61bc5a-b996-51c4-9f23-db75bd2dc512'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The cornea contributes what percentage of the eye''s total refractive power?', '["1/3","1/4","2/3","1/2","3/4"]'::jsonb, '2/3', 'The cornea provides about 2/3 (43/60) of the eye''s total refractive power.'),
('28e7e9c2-7344-5784-9782-e15cf0b4d627'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The crystalline lens contributes approximately how many diopters of refractive power?', '["5 D","25 D","10 D","30 D","17 D"]'::jsonb, '17 D', 'The lens provides ~17 diopters of the total 60 D.'),
('92e0be47-f6b0-5fb7-b8f6-2a8abea66214'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The image formed on the retina is:', '["Inverted and real","Upright and real","Inverted and upright depending on distance","Upright and virtual","Inverted and virtual"]'::jsonb, 'Inverted and real', 'The lens forms a real, inverted image on the retina.'),
('4a8621d4-ff35-5653-af5b-33341876e54a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The term emmetropia refers to:', '["Normal vision","Farsightedness","Astigmatism","Nearsightedness","Presbyopia"]'::jsonb, 'Normal vision', 'Emmetropia is normal vision where parallel rays focus on the retina.'),
('f4695e82-1230-5890-8b4f-ea800f07ec11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Myopia (nearsightedness) is caused by:', '["Irregular curvature of cornea","Eyeball too short or lens too flat","Loss of lens elasticity","Corneal opacity","Eyeball too long or lens too strong"]'::jsonb, 'Eyeball too long or lens too strong', 'In myopia, the focal point falls in front of the retina.'),
('6584602b-58af-58ea-8967-90c1a13d0374'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Myopia is corrected with:', '["Prism lenses","Convex (plus) lenses","Bifocal lenses","Cylindrical lenses","Concave (minus) lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Concave (minus) lenses', 'Concave lenses diverge light, moving the focal point back onto the retina.'),
('1a8b8442-71f7-5ef8-a8e1-e3ce3782f76d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Hyperopia (farsightedness) is caused by:', '["Lens opacity","Eyeball too short or lens too flat","Increased intraocular pressure","Irregular cornea","Eyeball too long"]'::jsonb, 'Eyeball too short or lens too flat', 'In hyperopia, the focal point falls behind the retina.'),
('4d78fc96-19e9-50c0-a290-701c34b6deff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Hyperopia is corrected with:', '["Prism lenses","No correction needed","Concave (minus) lenses","Cylindrical lenses","Convex (plus) lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Convex (plus) lenses', 'Convex lenses converge light, moving the focal point forward onto the retina.'),
('171c05ef-a757-5527-84ae-e24c7c8d860b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Astigmatism is caused by:', '["Loss of accommodation mediated physiological mechanism","Unequal curvature of cornea in different meridia","Lens opacity mediated physiological mechanism","Increased axial length mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased axial length mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Unequal curvature of cornea in different meridia', 'Astigmatism causes point images to appear as lines.'),
('99c6085f-570c-5687-b0af-f0edd9359ed2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Astigmatism is corrected with:', '["Spherical lenses","Prism lenses","Cylindrical lenses","Contact lenses only","Bifocal lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Cylindrical lenses', 'Cylindrical lenses compensate for the uneven corneal curvature.'),
('bcaf2b13-8b91-52ce-972d-72a0b232ede8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Presbyopia is due to:', '["Cataract formation","Shortened eyeball","Loss of elasticity of the lens with age","Increased lens power","Increased corneal curvature"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of elasticity of the lens with age', 'Presbyopia is the age-related loss of accommodation starting around age 40.'),
('efe73b77-6a52-5f32-a42e-08a80b26eb3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Presbyopia is corrected with:', '["Convex (plus) lenses for near vision","Cylindrical lenses mediated physiological mechanism","Concave lenses mediated physiological mechanism","No correction mediated physiological mechanism","Prism lenses mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Convex (plus) lenses for near vision', 'Plus lenses (reading glasses) compensate for the inability to increase lens power.'),
('e47d2f64-61c6-538e-b6d3-c19c20629915'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Accommodation is the process of:', '["Pupil constriction mediated physiological mechanism","Retinal adaptation mediated physiological mechanism","Tear production mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing the refractive power of the lens for near vision","Eye movement mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing the refractive power of the lens for near vision', 'Accommodation increases lens curvature for near objects.'),
('872d9acf-dc83-5c15-bf82-7a34f139dd7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'During accommodation, the ciliary muscle:', '["Relaxes, loosening zonular fibers","Contracts, tightening zonular fibers","Does not change","Relaxes, tightening zonular fibers","Contracts (sphincter-like), relaxing zonular fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Contracts (sphincter-like), relaxing zonular fibers', 'Ciliary muscle contraction reduces zonular tension, allowing the lens to round up.'),
('38305b93-2247-5e1b-9fe1-3c6b121adc1c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The lens is held in place by:', '["Ciliary processes","Vitreous body","Cornea","Iris","Zonular fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Zonular fibers', 'Zonular fibers attach the lens to the ciliary body.'),
('d5eb90cd-5200-5e05-b115-77c0c7ba2c06'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The near point of accommodation in a young adult (20 years) is approximately:', '["20 cm","25 cm","5 cm","10 cm","15 cm"]'::jsonb, '10 cm', 'At age 20, the near point is ~10 cm; it recedes with age.'),
('f0978e01-f479-5177-b007-14c496e8a039'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The near point of accommodation in a 60-year-old is approximately:', '["10 cm","100 cm","200 cm","25 cm","50 cm"]'::jsonb, '100 cm', 'By age 60, the near point recedes to ~100 cm due to presbyopia.'),
('466536aa-dfa0-5fcc-8caf-c6458dd88950'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The amplitude of accommodation at age 10 is approximately:', '["14 D","18 D","20 D","5 D","10 D"]'::jsonb, '14 D', 'At age 10, accommodative amplitude is ~14 D; by age 60 it drops to ~1 D.'),
('191ee5d8-c2b2-5060-b2fb-d8e77e98aa73'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'During accommodation, which of the following also occurs?', '["Lens flattening","Pupil dilation","Increased heart rate","Eye divergence","Pupil constriction"]'::jsonb, 'Pupil constriction', 'The near triad includes accommodation, pupil constriction, and convergence.'),
('f929ecb6-911e-5e3f-9055-b369aeeb597a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The pupillary light reflex involves the:', '["Optic nerve (II) and trigeminal nerve","Oculomotor nerve (III) and optic nerve","Optic nerve (II) and trochlear nerve","Optic nerve (II) and oculomotor nerve","Optic nerve (II) and facial nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Optic nerve (II) and oculomotor nerve', 'Afferent: CN II; efferent: CN III (parasympathetic to sphincter pupillae).'),
('af84a97b-3daa-51b0-b5d5-89db156500e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The sphincter pupillae muscle is innervated by:', '["Somatic fibers via CN III","Parasympathetic fibers via CN VII","Parasympathetic fibers via CN III","Sympathetic fibers via CN V","Sympathetic fibers"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic fibers via CN III', 'Parasympathetic (Edinger-Westphal nucleus) constricts the pupil.'),
('fe707d49-f2ed-571e-ac00-e15ba625f218'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The dilator pupillae muscle is innervated by:', '["Optic nerve fibers mediated physiological mechanism","Somatic fibers mediated physiological mechanism","Parasympathetic fibers mediated physiological mechanism","Sympathetic fibers from superior cervical ganglion","Facial nerve fibers mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic fibers from superior cervical ganglion', 'Sympathetic stimulation dilates the pupil.'),
('5f37aacd-3aec-5db0-8785-c1eade86f9ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The near triad includes all EXCEPT:', '["Pupil dilation","Medial rectus contraction","Pupil constriction","Lens accommodation","Convergence of eyes"]'::jsonb, 'Pupil dilation', 'The near triad: accommodation, pupil constriction (not dilation), and convergence.'),
('59ab7305-16fc-5eb6-a308-5046c96e802d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The far point of an emmetropic eye is:', '["50 cm","1 m","10 cm","25 cm","Infinity"]'::jsonb, 'Infinity', 'An emmetropic eye focuses parallel rays from infinity on the retina without accommodation.'),
('01ad43f6-5ff9-5a9d-8374-b32de8d6fc7a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'An object placed at 25 cm from an emmetropic eye requires how much accommodation?', '["1 D","4 D","6 D","8 D","2 D"]'::jsonb, '4 D', 'Accommodation = 1/distance in meters = 1/0.25 = 4 D.'),
('2bb50d5e-b71c-592b-9321-901cd89cb7d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The refractive index of the cornea is approximately:', '["1.38","1.00","1.42","1.33","1.50"]'::jsonb, '1.38', 'The cornea has a refractive index of about 1.376.'),
('d259e672-eccd-5592-b71a-8e8c533615cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The aqueous humor has a refractive index of:', '["1.38","1.50","1.00","1.33","1.42"]'::jsonb, '1.33', 'Aqueous humor refractive index is ~1.336, similar to water.'),
('219871be-0be3-5ffd-9f2b-75e8e9237596'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The vitreous humor has a refractive index of:', '["1.42","1.38","1.50","1.33","1.00"]'::jsonb, '1.33', 'Vitreous refractive index is ~1.336, close to aqueous.'),
('1704e532-c334-50e4-8ba6-3ab7e6c5dd1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The lens of the eye has a refractive index of:', '["1.40","1.33","1.38","1.35","1.42"]'::jsonb, '1.42', 'The lens has a core refractive index of ~1.42, with a gradient decreasing toward the periphery.'),
('647f0dd5-bcee-57f3-a623-cf0352aac4d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'A myope has a far point of 50 cm. What is the required correction?', '["-1 D","-3 D","-0.5 D","-5 D","-2 D"]'::jsonb, '-2 D', 'Far point = 50 cm, correction = -1/0.5 = -2 D concave lens.'),
('a318d5fe-2f01-525f-ad33-9bba3ab6f381'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'A hyperope needs +3 D for clear distance vision. What is the near point without correction assuming 4 D accommodation?', '["100 cm","25 cm","10 cm","50 cm","33 cm"]'::jsonb, '100 cm', 'The hyperope uses 3 D just for distance; remaining 1 D gives near point = 1/1 = 1 m.'),
('da6260ae-6e2c-58f9-b299-3ba77ef1f2c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The Scheiner principle (double pinhole) is used to demonstrate:', '["Depth of focus of the eye","Color vision","Dark adaptation","Pupillary light reflex","Visual acuity"]'::jsonb, 'Depth of focus of the eye', 'The Scheiner disc demonstrates the depth of focus via two pinholes.'),
('6fe7d100-4c06-50dc-bb56-92ce10bdc535'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The pinhole effect improves visual acuity by:', '["Changing lens curvature mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing light entering the eye mediated physiological mechanism","Relaxing accommodation mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulating the retina mediated physiological mechanism","Reducing the effective aperture, eliminating peripheral rays"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing the effective aperture, eliminating peripheral rays', 'A pinhole blocks peripheral rays, reducing blur circles on the retina.'),
('f2cbe45a-e087-5de4-824c-0edab7edcfee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Retinoscopy is used to:', '["Determine refractive error objectively","Measure intraocular pressure","Measure visual acuity","Test color vision","Examine the retina"]'::jsonb, 'Determine refractive error objectively', 'Retinoscopy objectively measures the refractive state of the eye.'),
('1a2782b5-6e43-5d54-8112-1128059c1be6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The Snellen chart measures:', '["Visual acuity","Intraocular pressure","Visual field","Refractive error","Color vision"]'::jsonb, 'Visual acuity', 'The Snellen chart tests distance visual acuity.'),
('b34c4f1d-ce8e-5251-8e76-8adfd92db6ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Normal visual acuity is:', '["40/20","20/20","20/200","20/10","20/40"]'::jsonb, '20/20', '20/20 means the patient sees at 20 feet what a normal eye sees at 20 feet.'),
('f659a641-7e32-5635-bce7-9bbb438803b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', '20/40 vision means:', '["The patient has 2x normal vision","The patient sees 20 lines","The patient sees at 20 feet what a normal eye sees at 40 feet","The patient sees at 40 feet what a normal eye sees at 20 feet","The patient has 0.5x normal vision"]'::jsonb, 'The patient sees at 20 feet what a normal eye sees at 40 feet', '20/40 indicates reduced acuity.'),
('9cd305ad-ee4a-5087-9782-f1af9bb4fa75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Depth perception primarily depends on:', '["Monocular cues only","Binocular disparity","Lens accommodation","Color vision","Pupil size"]'::jsonb, 'Binocular disparity', 'Binocular disparity from slightly different views in each eye creates depth perception.'),
('e18582cc-af51-5b8d-bd7b-04500d4f0964'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The radius of curvature of the anterior cornea is approximately:', '["15 mm","12 mm","8 mm","10 mm","5 mm"]'::jsonb, '8 mm', 'The anterior cornea has a radius of curvature of ~7.8 mm.'),
('4c08b049-3f2d-5bf9-93ad-d90ae2f34b41'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The anterior chamber depth is approximately:', '["2.5 mm","7 mm","3.5 mm","5 mm","1 mm"]'::jsonb, '3.5 mm', 'Anterior chamber depth is ~3.5 mm.'),
('8e3c4a40-0ba7-5029-85e1-ceaa351d0f19'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The axial length of a typical adult eye is approximately:', '["28 mm","24 mm","32 mm","16 mm","20 mm"]'::jsonb, '24 mm', 'Axial length of the adult eye is ~24 mm.'),
('13970001-691d-549e-9a3d-90da8fdde696'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The nodal point of the eye lies approximately:', '["At the retina","17 mm in front of retina","At the anterior surface of lens","At the optic disc","17 mm behind the cornea"]'::jsonb, '17 mm in front of retina', 'The nodal point is about 17 mm from the retina (1 nodal point model).'),
('255d9537-5aca-577b-b6d0-e990dbb5f850'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Red-green color blindness is most commonly due to deficiency of:', '["All three cone pigments","Green (M) cone pigment","Blue (S) cone pigment","Rod pigment","Red (L) cone pigment"]'::jsonb, 'Green (M) cone pigment', 'Deuteranopia (green deficiency) is the most common form of color blindness.'),
('d49c87af-fd7b-5eac-888b-5eb400a306c7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The cornea is kept transparent by:', '["Melanin pigment mediated physiological mechanism","Lack of blood vessels and regular arrangement of collagen","Lymphatic drainage mediated physiological mechanism","Blood vessels mediated physiological mechanism","High water content mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of blood vessels and regular arrangement of collagen', 'The cornea is avascular with regularly arranged collagen fibrils for transparency.'),
('c6246343-8037-522e-b4ef-920869cee047'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The main source of nutrients for the cornea is:', '["Blood vessels in cornea","Scleral vessels","Aqueous humor and tears","Vitreous humor","Lacrimal gland"]'::jsonb, 'Aqueous humor and tears', 'The avascular cornea receives nutrients from the aqueous humor and tears.'),
('67e2a168-f410-57e4-9bf0-26d193c4983b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The ciliary body is responsible for all EXCEPT:', '["Synthesis of hyaluronic acid","Secretion of vitreous humor","Production of aqueous humor","Contraction changes lens shape","Accommodation"]'::jsonb, 'Secretion of vitreous humor', 'The ciliary body produces aqueous humor, not vitreous humor.'),
('b4a3d65c-56d6-5653-9a9d-d8d800defc29'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The function of the iris is to:', '["Nourish the cornea mediated physiological mechanism","Focus light on retina mediated physiological mechanism","Regulate the amount of light entering the eye","Produce tears mediated physiological mechanism","Absorb light mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Regulate the amount of light entering the eye', 'The iris diaphragm controls pupil size and light entry.'),
('c866fd0c-5762-536d-afae-3133ab180a8a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Intraocular pressure is normally maintained at:', '["25-35 mmHg","10-21 mmHg","35-45 mmHg","5-10 mmHg","50-60 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '10-21 mmHg', 'Normal IOP is 10-21 mmHg; higher indicates glaucoma.'),
('66dc1ee6-6590-555b-8841-172412832fe6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Aqueous humor flows from the posterior chamber to the anterior chamber through the:', '["Scleral venous sinus","Zonular fibers","Pupil","Optic disc","Vitreous body"]'::jsonb, 'Pupil', 'Aqueous humor passes from posterior to anterior chamber through the pupil.'),
('68cf66a2-52a3-5ebe-bd3f-c5fcf988b164'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The retina is derived from:', '["Surface ectoderm","Neural crest","Mesoderm","Ectoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Ectoderm', 'The retina develops as an outpouching of the forebrain (neural tube).'),
('ee849278-9cb2-58c5-adc7-619e85e9b636'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The light-sensitive cells in the retina are:', '["Ganglion cells","Rods and cones","Bipolar cells","Horizontal cells","Amacrine cells"]'::jsonb, 'Rods and cones', 'Rods and cones are the photoreceptor cells of the retina.'),
('1bf4a28f-4bda-5e15-9907-7ca3cd832b61'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The total number of rods in the human retina is approximately:', '["120 million","200 million","60 million","20 million","6 million"]'::jsonb, '120 million', 'There are about 120 million rods in the human retina.'),
('59394ab0-a83e-53a3-b57c-c3e3d257a887'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The total number of cones in the human retina is approximately:', '["6 million","1 million","60 million","20 million","12 million"]'::jsonb, '6 million', 'There are about 6 million cones, concentrated in the fovea.'),
('f5b00ae0-fb24-50fa-89d2-1e001b660305'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The highest density of cones is found in the:', '["Fovea centralis","Macula lutea periphery","Peripheral retina","Optic disc","Ora serrata"]'::jsonb, 'Fovea centralis', 'The fovea contains the highest density of cones (only cones, no rods).'),
('973ac6f1-14ab-5dc6-8724-3f0bb3217011'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The region of the retina with the highest density of rods is:', '["Macula lutea","Peripheral retina","Fovea","Ora serrata","Optic disc"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral retina', 'Rod density peaks in a ring ~20 degrees from the fovea.'),
('72688361-f167-5935-96d1-55fcf5340755'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The optic disc is the point where:', '["Blood vessels enter only","Visual acuity is highest","The optic nerve exits the eye","Cones are concentrated","Photoreceptors are most dense"]'::jsonb, 'The optic nerve exits the eye', 'The optic disc has no photoreceptors, creating the blind spot.'),
('c0a34451-d601-5ebc-8586-19f249b40a38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The blind spot of the eye corresponds to the:', '["Central retinal artery entry","Macula lutea","Optic disc","Ora serrata","Fovea"]'::jsonb, 'Optic disc', 'No photoreceptors exist at the optic disc where the optic nerve exits.'),
('633c6f21-c6b4-5ae4-ad6f-d80b801302c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The visual pigment in rods is:', '["Melanopsin","Iodopsin","Cyanopsin","Rhodopsin","Photopsin"]'::jsonb, 'Rhodopsin', 'Rhodopsin (visual purple) is the photopigment in rods.'),
('f3e148aa-56ea-5425-a81c-5ea92d488738'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Rhodopsin is composed of:', '["Photopsin and retinal","Iodopsin and vitamin A","Opsin and retinal","Melanopsin and retinol","Opsin and 11-trans retinal"]'::jsonb, 'Opsin and retinal', 'Rhodopsin = scotopsin + 11-cis retinal (derived from vitamin A).'),
('e3feb73e-a169-5891-85b8-e10f33ffd915'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Light causes which change in retinal?', '["11-trans to 11-cis","Opsin to retinal","Retinal to retinoic acid","Retinal to retinol","11-cis retinal to 11-trans retinal"]'::jsonb, '11-cis retinal to 11-trans retinal', 'Photon absorption isomerizes 11-cis retinal to all-trans retinal.'),
('f135a21e-8c8b-561c-85d1-d72291df3e26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Phototransduction in rods involves:', '["Action potential generation","No change in membrane potential","Increased neurotransmitter release","Depolarization of the photoreceptor","Hyperpolarization of the photoreceptor"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperpolarization of the photoreceptor', 'Light hyperpolarizes rods by closing cGMP-gated Na+ channels.'),
('2f0d67a0-e075-5629-82a3-78f834e352be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'In the dark, rods are:', '["Inactive","Hyperpolarized","Depolarized","At resting potential","Bleached"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarized', 'In the dark, cGMP levels are high, keeping Na+ channels open (dark current).'),
('41b25440-484c-5b04-a9a3-43de1983f877'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The dark current in rods is carried mainly by:', '["Ca2+ influx","Na+ influx","H+ influx","K+ influx","Cl- efflux"]'::jsonb, 'Na+ influx', 'Na+ enters the rod outer segment through cGMP-gated channels in the dark.'),
('613ecac7-a555-5a7b-a1a5-35c455601af8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Light activates which enzyme in phototransduction?', '["Phospholipase C","Protein kinase A","Adenylyl cyclase","Guanylyl cyclase","Phosphodiesterase"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphodiesterase', 'Light-activated rhodopsin activates transducin, which activates PDE, breaking down cGMP.'),
('be6b08ea-42d9-5640-87c3-9a546f134a3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The second messenger in rod phototransduction is:', '["cGMP","DAG","IP3","Ca2+","cAMP"]'::jsonb, 'cGMP', 'cGMP keeps Na+ channels open in the dark; light reduces cGMP, closing channels.'),
('dd0ff769-77a8-524a-a5ab-67e0d8bc58dc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The enzyme that synthesizes cGMP in rods is:', '["Guanylyl cyclase","Transducin","Phosphodiesterase","Adenylyl cyclase","Protein kinase G"]'::jsonb, 'Guanylyl cyclase', 'Guanylyl cyclase synthesizes cGMP from GTP in the dark.'),
('6dccd44b-a301-5748-85cc-5d7d8d4fdda2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Transducin is a:', '["Structural protein","Enzyme that synthesizes cGMP","Transporter protein","G physiological pathway","Ion channel"]'::jsonb, 'G physiological pathway', 'Transducin (Gt) is a heterotrimeric G-protein that activates PDE6.'),
('2d7fe060-4cee-5d4b-acfd-a7d973c81f94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Activated rhodopsin (metarhodopsin II) catalyzes:', '["Exchange of GDP for GTP on transducin","Closure of K+ channels","Hydrolysis of cGMP","Opening of Na+ channels","Synthesis of cGMP"]'::jsonb, 'Exchange of GDP for GTP on transducin', 'Metarhodopsin II acts as a GEF for transducin.'),
('1fca5745-a228-5361-9f69-d6cc30c6ef6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The response of a rod to light is:', '["Graded depolarization","Oscillatory potential","All-or-none action potential","Graded hyperpolarization","Spontaneous firing"]'::jsonb, 'Graded hyperpolarization', 'Photoreceptors produce graded potentials, not action potentials.'),
('7a2bcb62-4178-525b-a972-488e29e23837'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Recovery of rhodopsin after bleaching requires:', '["Phosphorylation of rhodopsin mediated physiological mechanism","Synthesis of new opsin mediated physiological mechanism","Isomerization of all-trans retinal to 11-cis retinal","Degradation of retinal mediated physiological mechanism","Increase in cGMP mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Isomerization of all-trans retinal to 11-cis retinal', 'All-trans retinal must be isomerized back to 11-cis retinal.'),
('551f0046-5a3d-5c9a-b124-14cd11c8ca9e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Dark adaptation recovery is primarily limited by:', '["Rate of opsin synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Pupil dilation mediated physiological mechanism","Rate of cGMP synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Rate of retinal re-isomerization and regeneration of rhodopsin","Blood flow to retina mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Rate of retinal re-isomerization and regeneration of rhodopsin', 'Rhodopsin regeneration determines dark adaptation speed.'),
('94dac64a-be6f-5c2d-a8ff-74aa9173d697'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Vitamin A deficiency leads to:', '["Night blindness","Color blindness","Cataracts","Tunnel vision","Blind spot enlargement"]'::jsonb, 'Night blindness', 'Vitamin A is essential for retinal synthesis; deficiency impairs rod function.'),
('300af33c-e866-51fe-a257-84b253a7b67f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The cone photopigments are called:', '["Cryptochrome","Rhodopsin","Flavoprotein","Photopsins","Melanopsin"]'::jsonb, 'Photopsins', 'Three types of cone photopsins: erythrolabe (red), chlorolabe (green), cyanolabe (blue).'),
('89dcec97-8f02-56d0-910e-c6df59b82659'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Compared to rods, cones have:', '["Lower threshold, higher acuity","Higher threshold, higher acuity","Higher threshold, lower acuity","Same threshold, higher acuity","Lower threshold, lower acuity"]'::jsonb, 'Higher threshold, higher acuity', 'Cones require more light but provide high acuity vision.'),
('4fd181f9-e948-588b-af0e-1fab913630b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The fovea is specialized for:', '["Color vision only in dark","Night vision","High visual acuity","Peripheral vision","High sensitivity in dim light"]'::jsonb, 'High visual acuity', 'The fovea''s pure cone array provides the highest acuity.'),
('23e1c676-2b4a-57f6-b51c-9054e45ed224'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Rods are specialized for:', '["Vision in dim light","Color vision","High acuity","Central vision","Daylight vision"]'::jsonb, 'Vision in dim light', 'Rods are highly sensitive to low light levels (scotopic vision).'),
('ad6e389e-747f-5fa8-a20c-e322473268e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The dark adaptation curve shows:', '["Only cone adaptation","Two limbs: cone adaptation (fast) and rod adaptation","A single exponential recovery","Linear recovery","Only rod adaptation"]'::jsonb, 'Two limbs: cone adaptation (fast) and rod adaptation', 'Cones adapt in ~5 min, rods take ~20-30 min for full dark adaptation.'),
('46297987-28c1-538f-92b2-05dc5116498a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The Purkinje shift refers to:', '["Change in spectral sensitivity in dark vs light adaptation","Accommodation reflex","Pupillary light reflex","Change in color perception from green to red","Change from rod to cone vision in bright light"]'::jsonb, 'Change in spectral sensitivity in dark vs light adaptation', 'In dim light (rod vision), green light appears brightest; in bright light (cone vision), yellow appears brightest.'),
('bf716f59-7e0f-5865-a600-cb2ef5315202'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'In bright light (photopic vision), which are primarily active?', '["Rods more than cones","Rods only","Cones only","Intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells","Both rods and cones equally"]'::jsonb, 'Cones only', 'Photopic vision is mediated by cones.'),
('777bcc92-3dcf-5475-b72c-f3a5ac4a5d37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'In dim light (scotopic vision), which are primarily active?', '["Cones","Both equally","Bipolar cells","Horizontal cells","Rods"]'::jsonb, 'Rods', 'Scotopic vision is mediated by rods.'),
('2760ab13-3878-5261-b503-463b1120a21d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The visual pigment melanopsin is found in:', '["Rods mediated physiological mechanism","Cones mediated physiological mechanism","Amacrine cells mediated physiological mechanism","Bipolar cells mediated physiological mechanism","Intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells', 'Melanopsin in ipRGCs mediates the pupillary light reflex and circadian entrainment.'),
('7743264b-dca5-5fd0-bf26-8aa01e82c1df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The sensitivity of a single rod to a single photon of light is:', '["It can detect a single photon","It requires 100 photons","It requires 10 photons","It requires 1000 photons","It cannot detect single photons"]'::jsonb, 'It can detect a single photon', 'Rods are sensitive enough to respond to a single photon.'),
('31d3d177-37e4-5913-848a-0e633abc95a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The convergence ratio (rods:ganglion cells) in the peripheral retina is approximately:', '["1:1","6:1","100:1","20:1","1000:1"]'::jsonb, '100:1', 'High convergence (~100 rods per ganglion cell) increases sensitivity but reduces acuity.'),
('d907bd08-20eb-5a1d-8e01-575d43e6db35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The convergence ratio (cones:ganglion cells) in the fovea is approximately:', '["1:1","50:1","100:1","500:1","10:1"]'::jsonb, '1:1', 'Near 1:1 ratio in fovea provides high acuity (each cone has a dedicated ganglion cell).'),
('41fba260-8471-5576-8b21-7094726e75ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Bipolar cells in the retina transmit signals from:', '["Photoreceptors to horizontal cells","Photoreceptors to ganglion cells","Amacrine cells to ganglion cells","Ganglion cells to photoreceptors","Horizontal cells to amacrine cells"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptors to ganglion cells', 'Bipolar cells are interneurons connecting photoreceptors to ganglion cells.'),
('7f4d9abf-2b0f-52fd-9e0b-a023388901c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Horizontal cells in the retina mediate:', '["Signal amplification","Direct transmission to ganglion cells","Motion detection","Lateral inhibition","Color detection"]'::jsonb, 'Lateral inhibition', 'Horizontal cells create center-surround antagonistic receptive fields.'),
('c937c66d-8bed-53a5-ad35-bedbb4f437d7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'ON bipolar cells depolarize in response to:', '["Light in the center of their receptive field","Color change mediated physiological mechanism","Light in the surround mediated physiological mechanism","Darkness in the center mediated physiological mechanism","Darkness in the surround mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Light in the center of their receptive field', 'ON bipolar cells have sign-inverting glutamate receptors (mGluR6) and depolarize to light.'),
('526fa793-3e2e-58fd-a0de-7d203b1eca7e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'OFF bipolar cells depolarize in response to:', '["Light in the center","Darkness in the center","Motion","Color change","Light in the surround"]'::jsonb, 'Darkness in the center', 'OFF bipolar cells have ionotropic glutamate receptors (AMPA/kainate) and depolarize to darkness.'),
('79b7113a-dd79-5853-bb5a-edb46ed894fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Ganglion cells are the only retinal cells that produce:', '["Electrotonic conduction","None","Slow potentials","Action potentials","Graded potentials"]'::jsonb, 'Action potentials', 'Ganglion cells generate action potentials that travel via the optic nerve.'),
('33be16fd-be85-54c0-a080-8e4897f480bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The receptive field of a retinal ganglion cell is typically:', '["Circular with center-surround antagonism","Triangular","Concentric with no antagonism","Rectangular","Uniform"]'::jsonb, 'Circular with center-surround antagonism', 'Classic center-surround organization enhances edge detection and contrast.'),
('82505939-5d50-5a78-a7c7-c12805cfb9c5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Midget ganglion cells are associated with:', '["Melanopsin expression","Rod pathways in the periphery","Motion detection","Cone pathways in the fovea","Color opponency only"]'::jsonb, 'Cone pathways in the fovea', 'Midget (P) cells synapse with single cones, providing high resolution.'),
('b4286348-76f8-55e5-93bc-772dcb67959f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Parasol ganglion cells are associated with:', '["Foveal vision","Motion detection","Stereopsis","Color vision","High acuity"]'::jsonb, 'Motion detection', 'Parasol (M) cells have large receptive fields and detect motion.'),
('a4affd0a-943f-564f-8e4b-be5dab382da2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The optic nerve (CN II) is formed by axons of:', '["Photoreceptors","Ganglion cells","Horizontal cells","Bipolar cells","Amacrine cells"]'::jsonb, 'Ganglion cells', 'Retinal ganglion cell axons form the optic nerve.'),
('676bdc40-1b56-53db-af97-bd156a822bde'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The optic chiasm is where:', '["No fibers cross","Complete crossing of all fibers occurs","Fibers from temporal retinae cross","Optic nerves join the brain","Partial crossing (decussation) of fibers from nasal retinae occurs"]'::jsonb, 'Partial crossing (decussation) of fibers from nasal retinae occurs', 'Nasal retinal fibers cross; temporal fibers remain ipsilateral.'),
('90ce5dbf-0da9-5a4a-b3cb-03190c04faee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'After the optic chiasm, fibers form the:', '["Optic nerve","Optic tract","Lateral geniculate body","Visual cortex","Optic radiation"]'::jsonb, 'Optic tract', 'Optic tract runs from chiasm to lateral geniculate nucleus.'),
('df4a437c-7acb-5ab5-ba4c-c0da8ab064de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The primary visual cortex (V1) is located in the:', '["Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe","Insula","Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Occipital lobe', 'V1 (striate cortex) is in and around the calcarine sulcus in the occipital lobe.'),
('e3455100-babe-54ce-baac-c0bf80d3592b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus has how many layers?', '["10","2","4","8","6"]'::jsonb, '6', 'LGN has 6 layers: layers 1-2 magnocellular, layers 3-6 parvocellular.'),
('7696aa4b-f3b8-52cb-954d-99b3c7f811d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Magnocellular layers of LGN receive input from:', '["Midget (P) ganglion cells","Photosensitive ganglion cells","Parasol (M) ganglion cells","Rods","Bistratified ganglion cells"]'::jsonb, 'Parasol (M) ganglion cells', 'M cells project to magnocellular LGN layers (motion processing).'),
('f143e7ce-de94-5d22-b055-91f5d83dbbd6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Parvocellular layers of LGN receive input from:', '["Rod bipolar cells","Amacrine cells","Parasol (M) cells","Intrinsically photosensitive cells","Midget (P) cells"]'::jsonb, 'Midget (P) cells', 'P cells project to parvocellular LGN layers (color and detail).'),
('51c54ce4-d230-5e54-ba7c-7072df0b02a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Lesion of the right optic nerve causes:', '["Right homonymous hemianopia","Left homonymous hemianopia","Left nasal hemianopia","Complete blindness in right eye","Bitemporal hemianopia"]'::jsonb, 'Complete blindness in right eye', 'Optic nerve lesion causes monocular blindness on the ipsilateral side.'),
('8797867c-dafe-5e58-ba8e-8868660a4c76'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Lesion at the optic chiasm (e.g., pituitary tumor) causes:', '["Bitemporal hemianopia","Binasal hemianopia","Right homonymous hemianopia","Central scotoma","Left homonymous hemianopia"]'::jsonb, 'Bitemporal hemianopia', 'Crossing fibers from nasal retinae are affected, causing bitemporal field loss.'),
('2b571720-c87c-5330-8769-0f06a8fbb965'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Lesion of the right optic tract causes:', '["Left nasal hemianopia","Bitemporal hemianopia","Right homonymous hemianopia","Left homonymous hemianopia","Right eye blindness"]'::jsonb, 'Left homonymous hemianopia', 'Right optic tract lesion causes loss of left visual field in both eyes.'),
('b16fcfd3-baed-5d3a-8331-ebb2344b74f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'A lesion in the left occipital cortex (V1) causes:', '["Central scotoma mediated physiological mechanism","Left homonymous hemianopia mediated physiological mechanism","Bitemporal hemianopia mediated physiological mechanism","Right homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing","Left eye blindness mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Right homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing', 'Occipital lesions cause contralateral homonymous hemianopia, often with macular sparing.'),
('d1801096-17f5-5ab9-94c2-4427cdbc6f40'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Macular sparing in occipital lesions is attributed to:', '["Protection by the skull","Dual blood supply","Bilateral cortical representation","Larger size of macular fibers","Earlier development of macular vision"]'::jsonb, 'Dual blood supply', 'The macular area is supplied by both MCA and PCA, providing collateral circulation.'),
('1262d531-5fb8-587e-ad5b-64d2e170ae79'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The pupillary light reflex pathway involves the LGN:', '["True - LGN is essential","LGN is involved only in consensual reflex","False - LGN is not involved","LGN modifies the reflex","LGN is involved only in direct reflex"]'::jsonb, 'False - LGN is not involved', 'The pupillary light reflex bypasses LGN: retina -> pretectal nucleus -> Edinger-Westphal nucleus.'),
('d504879e-0ae4-5efb-aed9-947f367d8ca6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The direct pupillary light reflex is:', '["Dilation of the illuminated eye","Constriction of the opposite eye","No response","Constriction of both eyes equally","Constriction of the illuminated eye"]'::jsonb, 'Constriction of the illuminated eye', 'Direct reflex = constriction of the ipsilateral pupil to light.'),
('63519e6d-cf89-5cdc-8058-38d79bd734b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The consensual pupillary light reflex is:', '["Constriction of the illuminated eye","Constriction of both eyes","Dilation of the opposite eye","No response","Constriction of the opposite (non-illuminated) eye"]'::jsonb, 'Constriction of the opposite (non-illuminated) eye', 'Consensual reflex = constriction of the contralateral pupil to light.'),
('607b4c92-35ba-5799-bfc4-f7ea87f8d195'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'A lesion in the pretectal area (between superior colliculi) would affect:', '["Visual fields","Color vision","Accommodation","Visual acuity","Pupillary light reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Pupillary light reflex', 'The pretectal nucleus is a relay for the pupillary light reflex.'),
('ae96c886-2294-589e-8227-abf02273fbb6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Argyll Robertson pupil is characterized by:', '["No response to accommodation","Constricted pupil bilaterally","Light-near dissociation","Anisocoria","Fixed dilated pupil"]'::jsonb, 'Light-near dissociation', 'Argyll Robertson pupil is classically seen in neurosyphilis.'),
('daf5dfa4-2cf4-5395-9c2e-a3e359462634'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'With respect to management, The superior colliculus is involved in:', '["Color processing mediated physiological mechanism","Visual reflexes and orienting eye movements","Face recognition mediated physiological mechanism","Pattern recognition mediated physiological mechanism","Reading mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Visual reflexes and orienting eye movements', 'The superior colliculus controls saccadic eye movements and orientation.'),
('b791f15d-2f2f-5318-9cf8-589e1454cfd9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The visual association cortex (extrastriate cortex) is involved in:', '["Pupillary reflex","Basic light detection","Accommodation","Eye movement control","Higher-order processing"]'::jsonb, 'Higher-order processing', 'Areas V2-V5 process complex visual features.'),
('23d0f675-316a-5b94-a058-5c8d20171f1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Area V5 (MT) of the visual cortex is specialized for processing:', '["Color","Faces","Faces and objects","Depth perception","Motion"]'::jsonb, 'Motion', 'V5/MT (middle temporal area) is specialized for motion perception.'),
('3147095f-3dfd-5967-bf0e-38a730b95fd2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The ventral visual stream (what pathway) projects to the:', '["Temporal lobe","Frontal lobe","Parietal lobe","Occipital lobe only","Brainstem"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe', 'The ventral stream (V1 -> V2 -> V4 -> IT) processes object recognition (''what'').'),
('f456f88c-5335-5a52-be7a-dc8e4fec1d33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The dorsal visual stream (where pathway) projects to the:', '["Frontal lobe","Temporal lobe","Parietal lobe","Occipital pole","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Parietal lobe', 'The dorsal stream (V1 -> V2 -> V5/MT -> parietal) processes spatial location and motion (''where/how'').'),
('aea164d3-a3d0-55af-aee8-8d2859f621b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Prosopagnosia (face blindness) is associated with damage to:', '["V5/MT mediated physiological mechanism","Superior colliculus mediated physiological mechanism","V1 mediated physiological mechanism","Parietal lobe mediated physiological mechanism","Fusiform face area (FFA) in temporal lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Fusiform face area (FFA) in temporal lobe', 'The fusiform gyrus is specialized for face recognition.'),
('7ae06520-f64a-5fd7-82ed-a6d2b3356377'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'A scotoma is defined as:', '["Flashing lights","Blind spot in the visual field","Loss of half the visual field","Blurred vision","Tunnel vision"]'::jsonb, 'Blind spot in the visual field', 'A scotoma is an area of partial or complete loss of vision within the visual field.'),
('46b758d7-082a-5962-b325-6ed1681a9e92'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Homonymous hemianopia means:', '["Loss of upper quadrant","Loss of nasal fields only","Opposite side field loss in both eyes","Loss of temporal fields only","Same side field loss in both eyes"]'::jsonb, 'Same side field loss in both eyes', 'Homonymous = same side affected in both eyes.'),
('bd8bdc59-2fd1-5a8b-8463-9b72e9a4ddc9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Bitemporal hemianopia is most commonly caused by:', '["Pituitary tumor compressing the optic chiasm","Optic nerve glioma mediated physiological mechanism","Glaucoma mediated physiological mechanism","Stroke in occipital lobe mediated physiological mechanism","Retinal detachment mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary tumor compressing the optic chiasm', 'Pituitary tumors compress the chiasm from below, affecting crossing nasal fibers.'),
('8fb504ee-4e6b-5d41-a4a9-c20d1705d23a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil) is tested with:', '["Swinging flashlight test","Tonometry","Slit lamp","Ishihara plates","Snellen chart"]'::jsonb, 'Swinging flashlight test', 'The swinging flashlight test reveals a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD).'),
('2c1446bf-70de-5069-beee-0d4c7f33815b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'In a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD), both pupils dilate when light is swung to the:', '["Neither eye changes","No dilation occurs","Both eyes equally","Affected eye","Normal eye"]'::jsonb, 'Affected eye', 'The affected eye has reduced afferent input, so both pupils dilate when light moves to it.'),
('af2e27c8-43de-5078-9017-ec94a3a76096'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The primary blood supply to the visual cortex is from the:', '["Posterior cerebral artery","Anterior cerebral artery","Internal carotid artery","Ophthalmic artery","Middle cerebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Posterior cerebral artery', 'PCA supplies the occipital lobe, including the primary visual cortex.'),
('bb621abd-d64c-5d84-9fda-1e8525d0ede4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'A stroke in the right posterior cerebral artery would cause:', '["Left eye blindness","Left homonymous hemianopia","Right homonymous hemianopia","Right eye blindness","Bitemporal hemianopia"]'::jsonb, 'Left homonymous hemianopia', 'PCA stroke on the right affects the right occipital cortex, causing left homonymous hemianopia.'),
('a24159c4-d9ce-5098-b2d7-39035079041e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The trichromatic theory of color vision was proposed by:', '["Young and Helmholtz","Hubel and Wiesel","Rushton","Hering","Kuffler"]'::jsonb, 'Young and Helmholtz', 'The trichromatic theory states that three types of cones (L, M, S) mediate color vision.'),
('26cbc9fe-f3be-5812-a8d8-fea2051290a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The opponent-process theory of color vision was proposed by:', '["Rushton","Hering","Wald","Young","Helmholtz"]'::jsonb, 'Hering', 'Hering''s opponent theory: red-green, blue-yellow, black-white opponent pairs.'),
('39af72c3-04cb-5eb0-a99f-48a8d2823f7e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The three types of cones are sensitive to which peak wavelengths?', '["400 nm, 500 nm, 600 nm","500 nm, 550 nm, 600 nm","450 nm, 500 nm, 550 nm","450 nm, 550 nm, 650 nm","420 nm (blue), 530 nm (green), 560 nm"]'::jsonb, '420 nm (blue), 530 nm (green), 560 nm', 'S-cones peak at ~420 nm (blue), M-cones at ~530 nm (green), L-cones at ~560 nm (red).'),
('0c374399-0a75-5556-b51a-f54cf74e560a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The most common form of color blindness is:', '["Achromatopsia","Monochromacy","Protanopia","Deuteranopia","Tritanopia"]'::jsonb, 'Deuteranopia', 'Deuteranopia is the most common, affecting ~6% of males.'),
('3eff9c81-dedb-5051-a36d-a65820b69a38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Color blindness is inherited as:', '["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive","Mitochondrial","X-linked dominant"]'::jsonb, 'X-linked recessive', 'Red-green color blindness genes (OPN1LW, OPN1MW) are on the X chromosome.'),
('855bd7ed-e367-548f-9511-8824fc7cd799'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Tritanopia (blue-yellow deficiency) is caused by mutation in:', '["OPN1SW","RPE65","RHO","OPN1MW","OPN1LW"]'::jsonb, 'OPN1SW', 'The S-cone opsin gene is on chromosome 7; tritanopia is autosomal dominant.'),
('8a5bbe1d-0487-5cd3-b1f0-70a6cd73b1f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'A person with protanopia cannot distinguish:', '["All colors","Brightness levels","Red from green","Blue from yellow","Fine details"]'::jsonb, 'Red from green', 'Protanopia is red-green color blindness due to L-cone deficiency.'),
('acfc4c25-2192-5d82-93e6-fc3e4dc47244'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The Ishihara color plates test for:', '["Visual acuity","Depth perception","Color vision","Visual field","Contrast sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Color vision', 'Ishihara plates identify red-green color blindness.'),
('8aa19135-2009-549e-bcc0-27ad93272b5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The Farnsworth-Munsell 100 hue test is used to:', '["Test color discrimination","Test pupil reactivity","Test dark adaptation","Test visual acuity","Test visual fields"]'::jsonb, 'Test color discrimination', 'The FM-100 hue test assesses fine color discrimination ability.'),
('365aa062-4ab7-58a1-aa6a-fc66391cbc5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Dark adaptation refers to:', '["Pupil dilation in the dark","Decreased sensitivity in bright light","Lens changes for near vision","Increased sensitivity of the eye in dim light","Color vision changes"]'::jsonb, 'Increased sensitivity of the eye in dim light', 'Dark adaptation is the process by which the eye becomes more sensitive in low light.'),
('38420b80-10a6-5984-aab6-491fa32a4151'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The initial rapid phase of dark adaptation is due to:', '["Rod adaptation","Increased blood flow","Cone adaptation","Pupil dilation","Lens changes"]'::jsonb, 'Cone adaptation', 'Cone adaptation occurs in the first ~5 minutes, raising sensitivity ~10-fold.'),
('da19f9a5-7751-55a2-be5e-cd8863dd6813'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The second slow phase of dark adaptation is due to:', '["Horizontal cell adaptation","Pupil dilation","Rod adaptation","Cone adaptation","Bleaching of photopigments"]'::jsonb, 'Rod adaptation', 'Rod adaptation takes ~20-30 minutes and increases sensitivity ~10,000-fold.'),
('0c002ddd-5631-5970-bfe5-337b7185077e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Light adaptation involves all EXCEPT:', '["Pupil constriction","Cone dominance","Decreased retinal sensitivity","Increased rhodopsin concentration","Adaptation of retinal circuits"]'::jsonb, 'Increased rhodopsin concentration', 'Light adaptation decreases rhodopsin (bleaching), reducing sensitivity.'),
('f4deab53-4601-5955-8f0a-26c3f09865f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The pupillary light reflex arc includes the pretectal nucleus, which is located in the:', '["Medulla","Pons","Thalamus","Midbrain","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Midbrain', 'The pretectal nucleus is in the rostral midbrain.'),
('8358b1a2-7548-5269-97e2-fcb11352fd23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The Edinger-Westphal nucleus supplies:', '["Parasympathetic fibers to the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle","Motor fibers to levator palpebrae mediated physiological mechanism","Somatic fibers to extraocular muscles mediated physiological mechanism","Sensory fibers to cornea mediated physiological mechanism","Sympathetic fibers to the eye mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic fibers to the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle', 'Edinger-Westphal is the parasympathetic nucleus of CN III.'),
('83fea25a-1a4b-5b12-9546-9073a63a7782'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Anisocoria refers to:', '["Constricted pupils","Irregular pupil shape","Unequal pupil sizes","Equal pupil sizes","Dilated pupils"]'::jsonb, 'Unequal pupil sizes', 'Anisocoria is a condition of unequal pupil sizes.'),
('ca369cfe-f9bb-5ff1-9cf4-f24edd365e33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) results from damage to:', '["Trigeminal nerve","Sympathetic supply to the eye","Optic nerve","Oculomotor nerve","Parasympathetic supply to the eye"]'::jsonb, 'Sympathetic supply to the eye', 'Horner syndrome is due to interruption of the sympathetic pathway.'),
('cc3ee4f5-3ddb-5f78-9df8-ff00d578c63b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Pharmacologic dilation of the pupil (mydriasis) is achieved by:', '["Atropine","Neostigmine","Pilocarpine","Latanoprost","Timolol"]'::jsonb, 'Atropine', 'Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors on sphincter pupillae, causing dilation.'),
('c876e679-a46b-56fd-8fee-435cb6fa5b10'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Pilocarpine causes pupil constriction by:', '["Blocking muscarinic receptors","Stimulating alpha-adrenergic receptors","Stimulating muscarinic receptors on sphincter pupillae","Blocking beta-receptors","Inhibiting AChE"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating muscarinic receptors on sphincter pupillae', 'Pilocarpine is a muscarinic agonist used to treat glaucoma.'),
('183b2a8f-a6c2-57fc-ba5e-eaf6c5818f4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The ciliary muscle is innervated by:', '["Trigeminal fibers mediated physiological mechanism","Sympathetic fibers mediated physiological mechanism","Abducens fibers mediated physiological mechanism","Parasympathetic fibers via CN III","Somatic fibers mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Parasympathetic fibers via CN III', 'Parasympathetic input contracts the ciliary muscle for accommodation.'),
('5c4db89d-311c-5a7b-8b56-fc551b0f4532'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The process of emmetropization refers to:', '["Correction of refractive error with lenses","Loss of accommodation","Development of refractive error","Surgical correction of myopia","The eye''s growth to achieve emmetropia"]'::jsonb, 'The eye''s growth to achieve emmetropia', 'Emmetropization is the developmental process by which the eye achieves normal refraction.'),
('46be0c18-fcef-5ac3-b8dc-a02939bb4988'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Accommodative esotropia (crossed eyes) is associated with:', '["Presbyopia","Uncorrected myopia","Cataracts","Astigmatism","Uncorrected hyperopia"]'::jsonb, 'Uncorrected hyperopia', 'Hyperopic children accommodate excessively, causing convergence and esotropia.'),
('674e60bc-78f9-582a-8aa8-40e001f466a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Stereopsis (depth perception) requires:', '["Color vision mediated physiological mechanism","Monocular vision only mediated physiological mechanism","Accommodation mediated physiological mechanism","Binocular vision with overlapping visual fields","Pupil constriction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Binocular vision with overlapping visual fields', 'Stereopsis requires binocular disparity from overlapping fields.'),
('420b1fb7-c0d3-5be2-8b53-c27d6a6dccc9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The minimum angle of resolution (MAR) for normal human vision is approximately:', '["1 degree","1 arcminute","10 arcminutes","0.1 arcminute","5 arcminutes"]'::jsonb, '1 arcminute', 'Normal visual acuity corresponds to resolving 1 arcminute of visual angle.'),
('08a5de42-3d4a-5f08-85cb-a1d704610926'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The contrast sensitivity function measures:', '["Ability to detect differences in luminance (contrast) across spatial frequencies","Motion detection mediated physiological mechanism","Visual acuity only mediated physiological mechanism","Color discrimination mediated physiological mechanism","Depth perception mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ability to detect differences in luminance (contrast) across spatial frequencies', 'CSF measures threshold contrast for different spatial frequencies.'),
('7134201c-eebc-5ef4-aee9-58232349ee52'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Saccadic eye movements are:', '["Slow tracking movements","Smooth pursuit movements","Vestibular-induced eye movements","Rapid, ballistic eye movements that shift gaze","Convergence movements"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid, ballistic eye movements that shift gaze', 'Saccades are fast eye movements that bring objects onto the fovea.'),
('dfb721d4-dbed-59eb-99c2-e43093f3d234'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Smooth pursuit eye movements are used to:', '["Diverge for far objects","Converge on near objects","Shift gaze between objects","Maintain focus during head movement","Track a moving object smoothly"]'::jsonb, 'Track a moving object smoothly', 'Smooth pursuit stabilizes the image of a moving target on the fovea.'),
('ecd57b3b-da5d-54b0-85d9-9e51d216b060'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) functions to:', '["Shift gaze rapidly mediated physiological mechanism","Track moving objects mediated physiological mechanism","Converge the eyes mediated physiological mechanism","Stabilize gaze during head movement by moving eyes opposite to head","Accommodate the lens mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stabilize gaze during head movement by moving eyes opposite to head', 'VOR generates compensatory eye movements to maintain visual fixation during head rotation.'),
('947d66d3-f7fc-5c90-8687-835987aa04c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The optokinetic reflex involves:', '["Smooth pursuit and saccadic eye movements in response to large-field motion","Saccadic eye movements only mediated physiological mechanism","Vestibular input only mediated physiological mechanism","Accommodation mediated physiological mechanism","Pupillary response mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Smooth pursuit and saccadic eye movements in response to large-field motion', 'OKR combines smooth pursuit and saccades to track large moving fields.'),
('ed26b5de-0cdc-5122-92e2-5cb0e4fec206'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The three layers of the retina (from outer to inner) are:', '["Ganglion, photoreceptor, bipolar","Photoreceptor layer, bipolar cell layer, ganglion cell layer","Bipolar layer, ganglion layer, photoreceptor layer","Photoreceptor, ganglion, bipolar","Ganglion cell layer, bipolar layer, photoreceptor layer"]'::jsonb, 'Photoreceptor layer, bipolar cell layer, ganglion cell layer', 'Light must pass through ganglion and bipolar layers to reach photoreceptors.'),
('66201a7a-688c-5cf9-b961-d8c7f1006c2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Sound waves are transmitted from the tympanic membrane to the oval window via:', '["Cochlear aqueduct","Malleus, incus, and stapes","Round window","Auditory canal only","Eustachian tube"]'::jsonb, 'Malleus, incus, and stapes', 'The three ossicles amplify and transmit vibrations from tympanic membrane to oval window.'),
('dac048aa-e6da-5fd6-8be8-15977f84f8a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The tympanic membrane (eardrum) separates the:', '["Middle ear from inner ear","Cochlea from semicircular canals","Outer ear from middle ear","Outer ear from inner ear","Eustachian tube from pharynx"]'::jsonb, 'Outer ear from middle ear', 'The tympanic membrane divides the external acoustic meatus from the middle ear cavity.'),
('e2de8dfe-ee53-57ec-9c19-15869c5f93df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The primary function of the middle ear ossicles is:', '["To amplify sound pressure and match impedance between air and cochlear fluid","To protect the inner ear mediated physiological mechanism","To equalize pressure mediated physiological mechanism","To produce mucus mediated physiological mechanism","To detect sound direction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'To amplify sound pressure and match impedance between air and cochlear fluid', 'The ossicular chain provides ~22x impedance matching between air and fluid.'),
('44518157-d7c1-569c-952d-6fcbc5e6136a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The area ratio of the tympanic membrane to the oval window is approximately:', '["22:1","17:1","30:1","10:1","5:1"]'::jsonb, '17:1', 'The tympanic membrane is ~17x larger than the oval window, contributing to pressure amplification.'),
('5447350d-105f-52fd-9c36-868bc799bf81'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The lever action of the ossicular chain provides an amplification factor of approximately:', '["2.5x","5x","1.3x","3.5x","10x"]'::jsonb, '1.3x', 'The lever ratio of the ossicular chain provides ~1.3x force amplification.'),
('5eabebf8-aceb-57cd-846f-7903b8d54a27'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The total pressure gain provided by the middle ear is approximately:', '["5x","50x","200x","22x","100x"]'::jsonb, '22x', 'The product of area ratio (17x) and lever ratio (1.3x) gives ~22x gain.'),
('b9b98082-b829-54af-bcf7-ed60ad450cce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The stapes footplate is attached to the:', '["Round window","Cochlear apex","Tympanic membrane","Oval window","Semicircular canal"]'::jsonb, 'Oval window', 'The stapes footplate sits in the oval window, transmitting vibrations to the cochlear fluid.'),
('fe81d424-d7bf-5f31-abea-b333a6389d1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The round window functions to:', '["Allow pressure relief as the stapes pushes into the oval window","Produce perilymph mediated physiological mechanism","Connect to the eustachian tube mediated physiological mechanism","Equalize middle ear pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Receive sound from ossicles mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Allow pressure relief as the stapes pushes into the oval window', 'The round window bulges outward when the stapes pushes inward.'),
('e6ab07cb-9310-591e-a6cd-58c410242f75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The eustachian tube connects the:', '["Cochlea to the semicircular canals","Eustachian tube to the mastoid air cells","Middle ear to the nasopharynx","Outer ear to the middle ear","Inner ear to the throat"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear to the nasopharynx', 'The eustachian tube equalizes pressure between the middle ear and atmosphere.'),
('b0ef69ac-9531-5b66-9bca-248a3fceef94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The tensor tympani and stapedius muscles:', '["Dampen loud sounds","Protect from infection","Amplify sound","Equalize pressure","Move the ossicles for hearing"]'::jsonb, 'Dampen loud sounds', 'The middle ear muscles contract reflexively to loud sounds, attenuating transmission.'),
('051397f8-01f2-565a-8465-ff246a89704c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The acoustic reflex threshold is approximately:', '["70-80 dB SPL","90-100 dB SPL","130-140 dB SPL","110-120 dB SPL","40-50 dB SPL"]'::jsonb, '70-80 dB SPL', 'The stapedius reflex is triggered at ~70-80 dB above hearing threshold.'),
('d733b2c9-4ac1-5de3-927d-488b4cf29190'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The cochlea is divided into three scalae. Which are they?', '["Scala externa, scala media, scala interna","Ductus cochlearis, ductus vestibularis, ductus tympani","Scala vestibuli, scala media, scala tympani","Upper canal, middle canal, lower canal","Vestibular duct, cochlear duct, tympanic duct only"]'::jsonb, 'Scala vestibuli, scala media, scala tympani', 'The cochlea has three parallel chambers: scala vestibuli, scala media (cochlear duct), scala tympani.'),
('997e6077-3de4-5b47-b4dd-e5974bdf3bb8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The scala vestibuli and scala tympani are filled with:', '["Mucus","Endolymph","Cerebrospinal fluid","Perilymph","Blood"]'::jsonb, 'Perilymph', 'Perilymph has high Na+ (~140 mM) and low K+ (~5 mM), similar to extracellular fluid.'),
('c8be6891-ab6f-519b-8fb0-364a4d2438a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The scala media (cochlear duct) is filled with:', '["Perilymph","CSF","Endolymph","Blood","Aqueous humor"]'::jsonb, 'Endolymph', 'Endolymph has high K+ (~150 mM) and low Na+ (~1 mM), similar to intracellular fluid.'),
('faea5828-cd3f-55dd-95be-2fccdaf2b47c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The endocochlear potential (potential difference between endolymph and perilymph) is approximately:', '["-80 mV","+20 mV","+80 mV","+150 mV","0 mV"]'::jsonb, '+80 mV', 'The endocochlear potential is ~+80 mV, maintained by the stria vascularis.'),
('141bed77-6453-5b30-a046-83765f648fc6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The organ of Corti is located on the:', '["Vestibular membrane","Spiral ligament","Tectorial membrane","Reissner''s membrane","Basilar membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Basilar membrane', 'The organ of Corti (hearing organ) sits on the basilar membrane.'),
('f612f094-346a-5034-ab57-be3f51489514'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The tectorial membrane is attached to the:', '["Spiral limbus","Stria vascularis","Basilar membrane","Reissner''s membrane","Round window"]'::jsonb, 'Spiral limbus', 'The tectorial membrane projects from the spiral limbus and contacts hair cell stereocilia.'),
('13b3c05f-5e75-504e-89f1-f1047b6a286f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Inner hair cells in the cochlea number approximately:', '["12,000","3,500","20,000","500","50,000"]'::jsonb, '3,500', 'There are ~3,500 inner hair cells in each human cochlea.'),
('f885f90e-dd31-5d40-a27f-dbd5d5c2be67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Outer hair cells in the cochlea number approximately:', '["20,000","12,000","50,000","3,500","500"]'::jsonb, '12,000', 'There are ~12,000 outer hair cells in each human cochlea.'),
('e3716b96-181a-5f85-bf3e-d7254b53ec24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The primary sensory receptors for hearing are:', '["Basilar membrane fibers","Supporting cells","Outer hair cells","Both equally","Inner hair cells"]'::jsonb, 'Inner hair cells', 'Inner hair cells are the primary auditory receptors; outer hair cells amplify the response.'),
('577400c7-90a0-56e4-ae10-bf4df4d7f938'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The function of outer hair cells is:', '["Support for inner hair cells","Direct signal transduction","Electromotility","Inhibition of sound","Production of endolymph"]'::jsonb, 'Electromotility', 'Outer hair cells exhibit electromotility via prestin, amplifying sound by ~40 dB.'),
('ba899174-36e2-58c5-8408-e41d2ffed66c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The stereocilia of hair cells are embedded in the:', '["Spiral ligament","Tectorial membrane","Stria vascularis","Reissner''s membrane","Basilar membrane"]'::jsonb, 'Tectorial membrane', 'Hair cell stereocilia contact the overlying tectorial membrane.'),
('d84070ac-d41e-54f9-be07-19b64de45cea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Deflection of stereocilia toward the tallest row causes:', '["No change","Depolarization","Hyperpolarization of the hair cell","Closing of K+ channels","Inhibition of neurotransmitter release"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarization', 'Tip link tension opens mechanotransduction channels, allowing K+ influx and depolarization.'),
('9490f1e8-e284-54fa-aeb0-16bf409d7c66'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The mechanotransduction channels in hair cells are located at:', '["The cell body","The tips of the stereocilia","The nucleus","The synaptic terminal","The base of the hair cell"]'::jsonb, 'The tips of the stereocilia', 'Tip links connect stereocilia and control mechanotransduction channel opening.'),
('5dda7f6b-6ee4-5dea-aa4e-e272b1ca5907'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Hair cell depolarization causes opening of which type of calcium channel?', '["L-type Ca2+ channels at the synaptic ribbon","R-type Ca2+ channels mediated physiological mechanism","T-type Ca2+ channels mediated physiological mechanism","P/Q-type Ca2+ channels mediated physiological mechanism","N-type Ca2+ channels mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'L-type Ca2+ channels at the synaptic ribbon', 'Depolarization opens L-type Ca2+ channels, triggering glutamate release.'),
('5f7cbe6b-dd5d-5985-b6ec-f296dd0723a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The neurotransmitter released by inner hair cells is:', '["Acetylcholine","GABA","Glutamate","Dopamine","Glycine"]'::jsonb, 'Glutamate', 'Inner hair cells release glutamate onto spiral ganglion neuron dendrites.'),
('c1ae2f59-3d0c-5bb2-96ed-01cd8dc97d8f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The basilar membrane is widest and most flexible at the:', '["Apex","Helicotrema","Round window","Middle turn","Base"]'::jsonb, 'Apex', 'The basilar membrane is narrow and stiff at the base, wide and flexible at the apex.'),
('7038bbf2-32fa-5b3f-8692-b5de6a42c454'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The place theory of pitch perception states that:', '["Pitch is encoded by phase locking","Pitch is encoded by the rate of hair cell firing","Pitch is learned","Pitch is encoded by the number of active fibers","Pitch is encoded by the position of maximal basilar membrane displacement"]'::jsonb, 'Pitch is encoded by the position of maximal basilar membrane displacement', 'High frequencies peak near the base, low frequencies near the apex.'),
('6dab1545-9555-56d6-8e58-a797fbdc8b26'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The frequency range of human hearing is approximately:', '["20 - 20,000 Hz","10 - 100,000 Hz","200 - 20,000 Hz","50 - 50,000 Hz","20 - 2000 Hz"]'::jsonb, '20 - 20,000 Hz', 'Humans can hear from about 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz.'),
('56b48e55-d177-541a-bb47-c66317c7a709'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The greatest sensitivity of human hearing is in the range of:', '["100-500 Hz","10,000-20,000 Hz","20-100 Hz","500-5000 Hz","5000-10,000 Hz"]'::jsonb, '500-5000 Hz', 'Human hearing is most sensitive in the 500-5000 Hz range.'),
('a847f4bb-9968-51af-996b-23dd5894e86c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The traveling wave on the basilar membrane moves from:', '["Randomly","Apex to base","Middle to both ends","Base to apex","Both directions simultaneously"]'::jsonb, 'Base to apex', 'The traveling wave propagates from the base (stiff) to the apex (flexible).'),
('0929790f-cd7c-5f04-859e-ddac9ca7c86b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'High-frequency sounds cause maximal displacement of the basilar membrane at the:', '["Helicotrema","Middle","Apex","Round window","Base"]'::jsonb, 'Base', 'High frequencies peak at the base where the membrane is narrow and stiff.'),
('a0dac481-5804-51dc-8567-800a5b2f34c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Low-frequency sounds cause maximal displacement of the basilar membrane at the:', '["Round window","Base","Middle turn","Apex","Oval window"]'::jsonb, 'Apex', 'Low frequencies travel farther and peak at the apex.'),
('6061ef6e-81ae-588a-86a3-87b46a1d39bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The cochlear amplifier is provided by:', '["Tectorial membrane","Basilar membrane structure","Outer hair cells","Inner hair cells","Endocochlear potential"]'::jsonb, 'Outer hair cells', 'Outer hair cell electromotility amplifies the traveling wave.'),
('94b31869-2e65-5078-8772-e5493b42bd08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Otoacoustic emissions are produced by:', '["Stapedius muscle contraction","Inner hair cell activity","Endolymphatic potential changes","Outer hair cell electromotility","Cochlear blood flow"]'::jsonb, 'Outer hair cell electromotility', 'Otoacoustic emissions are sounds generated by outer hair cell motility, used in newborn hearing screening.'),
('71d006b3-33db-5569-8676-34b91af519a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The stria vascularis is responsible for:', '["Anchoring the tectorial membrane mediated physiological mechanism","Draining perilymph mediated physiological mechanism","Producing endolymph and maintaining the endocochlear potential","Transducing sound mediated physiological mechanism","Supporting the organ of Corti mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Producing endolymph and maintaining the endocochlear potential', 'The stria vascularis actively secretes K+ into the scala media, creating the endocochlear potential.'),
('72f23eb1-9725-5f79-8b2d-399e75ba9d21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Endolymph is produced by the:', '["Stria vascularis","Basilar membrane","Reissner''s membrane","Spiral ligament","Organ of Corti"]'::jsonb, 'Stria vascularis', 'The stria vascularis on the outer wall of the scala media produces endolymph.'),
('590ee029-7f50-59b5-8b59-59fcce80b630'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The spiral ganglion contains the cell bodies of:', '["Auditory nerve (CN VIII) bipolar neurons","Supporting cells","Outer hair cells","Efferent olivocochlear neurons","Inner hair cells"]'::jsonb, 'Auditory nerve (CN VIII) bipolar neurons', 'Spiral ganglion neurons are bipolar, with peripheral processes to hair cells and central axons to the cochlear nucleus.'),
('fd54e19a-8193-500e-8df8-18e7c234d135'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The olivocochlear bundle provides:', '["Efferent feedback from the brainstem to outer hair cells","Sympathetic innervation mediated physiological mechanism","Blood supply regulation mediated physiological mechanism","Afferent input to the cochlea mediated physiological mechanism","Parasympathetic innervation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Efferent feedback from the brainstem to outer hair cells', 'The olivocochlear efferents modulate outer hair cell activity, reducing cochlear gain.'),
('75759fa7-c6de-5801-aeae-1b481d83d426'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Conductive hearing loss results from:', '["Pathology of the outer or middle ear","Damage to hair cells or auditory nerve","Ototoxicity","Lesions in the auditory cortex","Lesions in the cochlear nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Pathology of the outer or middle ear', 'Conductive loss is due to impaired sound transmission through the outer/middle ear.'),
('4df7dd09-8ffd-5302-83a6-3831208cc55b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Sensorineural hearing loss results from:', '["Middle ear infection","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Ossicular chain damage","Earwax impaction","Damage to cochlea or auditory nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Damage to cochlea or auditory nerve', 'Sensorineural loss involves the cochlea or auditory nerve (CN VIII).'),
('f1b5d04a-c46b-59bb-9f24-fda10ff0c793'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Rinne test compares:', '["High frequency vs low frequency","Speech discrimination","Air conduction vs bone conduction","Left ear vs right ear hearing","Tympanic membrane mobility"]'::jsonb, 'Air conduction vs bone conduction', 'Rinne: AC > BC (normal), BC > AC (conductive loss).'),
('5cbddef0-85a6-55fa-9e12-1068c6ba02ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Weber test uses a tuning fork placed on the:', '["Mastoid process","Nasion","Tympanic membrane","Vertex of the skull","External auditory canal"]'::jsonb, 'Vertex of the skull', 'Weber test: sound lateralizes to the better ear in sensorineural loss and to the worse ear in conductive loss.'),
