('e0f71ec7-f5e1-5b71-b291-214b614825e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Auditory processing in Wernicke''s area (left temporal lobe) is critical for:', '["Sound detection","Sound localization","Pitch discrimination","Temporal coding","Speech comprehension"]'::jsonb, 'Speech comprehension', 'Wernicke''s area (BA 22) is essential for language comprehension.'),
('4ee5fbe2-ea6b-53aa-bd2c-76529af1be72'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Conduction aphasia results from damage to the:', '["Primary auditory cortex","Medial geniculate body","Wernicke''s area","Broca''s area","Arcuate fasciculus"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate fasciculus', 'Conduction aphasia: impaired repetition with fluent speech and good comprehension.'),
('114d3edb-a04b-562b-aa2f-0c6610cbac33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Amusia (tone deafness) is associated with damage to:', '["Right auditory cortex","Broca''s area","Wernicke''s area","Primary auditory cortex","Inferior colliculus"]'::jsonb, 'Right auditory cortex', 'The right hemisphere is dominant for music and prosody perception.'),
('02d3e078-74d3-543d-920b-e6d0f82bbc30'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Tinnitus (ringing in the ears) is most commonly associated with:', '["Sensorineural hearing loss and cochlear damage","Ottis media","Acoustic neuroma","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Conductive hearing loss"]'::jsonb, 'Sensorineural hearing loss and cochlear damage', 'Tinnitus is often linked to cochlear hair cell damage and aberrant neural activity.'),
('83e732d3-f05d-5f9d-a5b3-47911f62613c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Auditory neuropathy spectrum disorder involves:', '["Middle ear infection mediated physiological mechanism","Dysfunction of inner hair cells or the auditory nerve with normal outer hair cell function","Damage to ossicles mediated physiological mechanism","Central auditory processing deficit mediated physiological mechanism","Cochlear amplification loss mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Dysfunction of inner hair cells or the auditory nerve with normal outer hair cell function', 'Auditory neuropathy: normal OHC function (otoacoustic emissions present) but abnormal auditory brainstem response.'),
('b78580fe-9823-5df7-8bc3-48559828a8cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The vestibular system is located in the:', '["Middle ear mediated physiological mechanism","Cochlea mediated physiological mechanism","Brainstem mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebellum mediated physiological mechanism","Semicircular canals and otolith organs"]'::jsonb, 'Semicircular canals and otolith organs', 'The vestibular apparatus in the inner ear includes semicircular canals and otoliths.'),
('814385d9-7564-5c0b-beb4-a2d5cf03bf84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The three semicircular canals are oriented:', '["All in the same plane","Horizontally only","Vertically only","In three orthogonal planes","Random orientation"]'::jsonb, 'In three orthogonal planes', 'Three canals at right angles detect rotation in any plane.'),
('3d7e2682-0382-520f-b991-fce0fee57617'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The semicircular canals detect:', '["Linear acceleration","Pressure changes","Sound direction","Vibration","Angular (rotational) acceleration"]'::jsonb, 'Angular (rotational) acceleration', 'Semicircular canals are stimulated by angular acceleration/deceleration.'),
('91bb2eff-5473-5997-87f0-a807d71f49ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The utricle and saccule detect:', '["Rotation mediated physiological mechanism","Sound frequency mediated physiological mechanism","Linear acceleration and head tilt relative to gravity","Angular acceleration mediated physiological mechanism","Vibration mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Linear acceleration and head tilt relative to gravity', 'Otolith organs detect linear acceleration and static head position.'),
('81f1ee0a-8b69-5fd5-9c65-020693f77d21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The utricle is oriented primarily in the:', '["Diagonal plane","Vertical plane","Horizontal plane","Sagittal plane","Coronal plane"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal plane', 'The utricle''s macula lies horizontally, detecting horizontal acceleration.'),
('30f09364-4984-52dd-a527-425d06c3d08f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The saccule is oriented primarily in the:', '["Transverse plane","Oblique plane","Horizontal plane","Vertical (parasagittal) plane","Coronal plane"]'::jsonb, 'Vertical (parasagittal) plane', 'The saccular macula is vertical, detecting vertical acceleration and tilt.'),
('fe6029d2-4120-5945-a9d7-992fc7f7320d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The sensory epithelium of the semicircular canals is the:', '["Organ of Corti","Basilar papilla","Spiral organ","Crista ampullaris","Macula"]'::jsonb, 'Crista ampullaris', 'Each semicircular canal has an ampulla containing the crista ampullaris with hair cells.'),
('f4b8632a-fca4-5279-9200-8262d2318440'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The sensory epithelium of the utricle and saccule is the:', '["Spiral limbus","Stria vascularis","Organ of Corti","Crista ampullaris","Macula"]'::jsonb, 'Macula', 'The macula contains hair cells with stereocilia embedded in an otolithic membrane weighted with otoconia.'),
('de16ee49-3a6b-5750-97b9-55bb06da6cec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The cupula in the semicircular canal is a:', '["Gelatinous structure that moves with endolymph flow, deflecting hair cell stereocilia","Membrane for support mediated physiological mechanism","Fluid-filled space mediated physiological mechanism","Bony projection mediated physiological mechanism","Calcified structure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Gelatinous structure that moves with endolymph flow, deflecting hair cell stereocilia', 'The cupula is a gelatinous mass that seals the ampulla and bends with endolymph movement.'),
('aa705554-d01c-5aad-830e-c21cc9290ce7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The otoconia (otoliths) are composed of:', '["Silica","Magnesium phosphate","Calcium carbonate crystals","Protein only","Sodium chloride"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium carbonate crystals', 'Otoconia are calcium carbonate crystals that weight the otolithic membrane.'),
('15e7a709-dcdf-568e-b080-7072be07974f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Hair cells in the vestibular system have a morphology similar to:', '["Gustatory cells","Photoreceptors","Olfactory neurons","Cochlear hair cells","Pacinian corpuscles"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear hair cells', 'Vestibular hair cells have a single kinocilium and multiple stereocilia.'),
('90865748-3b39-5855-b3d0-5ec6869655be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Deflection of stereocilia toward the kinocilium causes:', '["Closing of K+ channels","Hyperpolarization","No change","Inhibition of neurotransmitter release","Depolarization"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarization', 'Deflection toward the kinocilium opens mechanotransduction channels, causing depolarization.'),
('320a4cf4-410b-513a-8632-508d01bf2644'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Deflection of stereocilia away from the kinocilium causes:', '["Depolarization","Hyperpolarization","No response","Channel opening","Increased firing"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperpolarization', 'Opposite deflection closes channels, reducing neurotransmitter release.'),
('f4576f81-68a3-5dc3-82e3-61aabf5b769c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The primary vestibular afferents are found in:', '["Trigeminal nerve","Facial nerve","Vestibular nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Cochlear nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nerve', 'The vestibular nerve carries afferents from hair cells to the vestibular nuclei.'),
('8a335016-8e68-5366-8e16-687d48e10070'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The vestibular nuclei are located in the:', '["Pons and medulla","Midbrain","Thalamus","Spinal cord","Cerebellum"]'::jsonb, 'Pons and medulla', 'The four vestibular nuclei (superior, lateral, medial, inferior) are in the pons and medulla.'),
('209f3291-d5a2-5386-90aa-6337fe067bc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The vestibular nuclei project to all EXCEPT:', '["Cerebellum","Spinal cord","Thalamus and cortex","Oculomotor nuclei","Cochlear nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Cochlear nucleus', 'Vestibular nuclei project to the cerebellum, thalamus, spinal cord, and oculomotor nuclei.'),
('8862b24d-2afe-5acb-a034-66d11117855e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) is mediated by connections between the:', '["Vestibular nuclei and spinal cord only","Vestibular nuclei and oculomotor nuclei","Vestibular nuclei and cerebellum only","Vestibular nuclei and thalamus","Vestibular nuclei and cochlear nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular nuclei and oculomotor nuclei', 'VOR produces compensatory eye movements opposite to head rotation.'),
('c5400194-2d36-525f-b6a8-521671d81385'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Nystagmus is characterized by:', '["Involuntary rhythmic eye movements with slow and fast components","Blurred vision mediated physiological mechanism","Smooth eye movement only mediated physiological mechanism","Loss of eye movement mediated physiological mechanism","Constant eye deviation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Involuntary rhythmic eye movements with slow and fast components', 'Nystagmus has a slow phase (VOR) and a fast phase (brainstem correction).'),
('5bbe7bde-6471-5ab0-b2d4-fae109486076'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Caloric testing (warm water in ear) is used to:', '["Test hearing","Test cochlear function","Test middle ear pressure","Test vestibular function","Treate vertigo"]'::jsonb, 'Test vestibular function', 'Caloric testing induces convection currents in semicircular canals, producing nystagmus.'),
('cdcc9c16-7deb-5e91-abbd-f1c5952cc99a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Warm water caloric stimulation produces nystagmus:', '["No nystagmus","Vertical nystagmus","Toward the stimulated ear","Rotary nystagmus","Away from the stimulated ear"]'::jsonb, 'Toward the stimulated ear', 'Warm water (COWS): warm = toward same side, cold = toward opposite side.'),
('d641898d-ab1a-5896-9026-b2707d0d1980'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Cold water caloric stimulation produces nystagmus:', '["No nystagmus","Toward the stimulated ear","Away from the stimulated ear","Rotary nystagmus","Vertical nystagmus"]'::jsonb, 'Away from the stimulated ear', 'COWS: Cold Opposite, Warm Same.'),
('a9291d1d-0f57-5118-a2a9-8d60fffb20be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The Romberg test assesses:', '["Hearing","Visual acuity","Pupil reactivity","Vestibular function","Coordination"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular function', 'Romberg: patient stands with feet together, eyes closed; swaying indicates proprioceptive or vestibular loss.'),
('a07da800-fb3e-50a3-866f-18e020ecb428'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV) is caused by:', '["Acoustic neuroma mediated physiological mechanism","Vestibular neuritis mediated physiological mechanism","Otodis lodged in a semicircular canal","Meniere disease mediated physiological mechanism","Cochlear damage mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Otodis lodged in a semicircular canal', 'BPPV: otoconia migrate into semicircular canals, causing positional vertigo.'),
('3034fbf2-ff1f-5326-8dd5-149d3aab29a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The Epley maneuver is used to treat:', '["Presbycusis","Acoustic neuroma","Tinnitus","Meniere disease","BPPV"]'::jsonb, 'BPPV', 'The Epley maneuver is a canalith repositioning procedure for BPPV.'),
('da34dfb7-8dd6-54d4-a64b-6082ee6a51f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Vestibular neuritis (vestibular neuronitis) is thought to be caused by:', '["Autoimmune disorder","Bacterial infection of the middle ear","Trauma","Viral inflammation of the vestibular nerve","Vascular insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Viral inflammation of the vestibular nerve', 'Vestibular neuritis is likely viral, causing acute vertigo without hearing loss.'),
('5bff27a2-2d98-51b2-b523-85cafbceaade'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The lateral vestibulospinal tract functions to:', '["Control eye movements mediated physiological mechanism","Control head movement mediated physiological mechanism","Coordinate swallowing mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibit flexor muscles mediated physiological mechanism","Excite extensor (antigravity) muscles for balance"]'::jsonb, 'Excite extensor (antigravity) muscles for balance', 'The lateral vestibulospinal tract from Deiters'' nucleus facilitates extensor tone.'),
('9ff10310-2edc-575a-af3e-8e0d267f48b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The medial vestibulospinal tract functions to:', '["Control eye movements mediated physiological mechanism","Control leg muscles mediated physiological mechanism","Control neck and upper back muscles for head stabilization","Control breathing mediated physiological mechanism","Control arm movements mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Control neck and upper back muscles for head stabilization', 'The medial vestibulospinal tract innervates cervical motor neurons.'),
('d1d58b49-badd-5a32-bfcd-71858d911e51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Motion sickness is thought to involve:', '["Conflict between visual, vestibular, and proprioceptive inputs","Vestibular hair cell loss mediated physiological mechanism","Cochlear damage mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebellar lesion mediated physiological mechanism","Optic nerve damage mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Conflict between visual, vestibular, and proprioceptive inputs', 'Sensory conflict (e.g., reading in a car) causes motion sickness.'),
('9d7eac92-02a3-534e-90d9-15d437a67264'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The flocculonodular lobe of the cerebellum is important for:', '["Hearing","Vision","Language","Vestibular function and balance","Fine motor coordination"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular function and balance', 'The flocculonodular lobe (archicerebellum) is the vestibular cerebellum.'),
('327a7f6f-c10b-5cdc-80ac-dbd8b7c243a3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The macula of the utricle detects which type of acceleration?', '["Angular acceleration","Sound vibration","Vertical linear acceleration","Horizontal linear acceleration","None"]'::jsonb, 'Horizontal linear acceleration', 'The utricle''s horizontal macula detects horizontal acceleration and tilt.'),
('3ea3cd9d-8ae6-5ddd-96a8-1749deee75f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The olfactory epithelium is located in the:', '["Trachea","Oral cavity","Paranasal sinuses","Pharynx","Nasal cavity"]'::jsonb, 'Nasal cavity', 'Olfactory epithelium is in the roof of the nasal cavity, covering ~2-4 cm2.'),
('e97f6a28-fcb7-5072-be06-d54d50f0bec0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Olfactory receptor neurons are:', '["Basal cells mediated physiological mechanism","Multipolar neurons mediated physiological mechanism","Pseudounipolar neurons mediated physiological mechanism","Supporting cells only mediated physiological mechanism","Bipolar neurons with cilia bearing odorant receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Bipolar neurons with cilia bearing odorant receptors', 'Olfactory receptor neurons are bipolar with cilia extending into the mucus layer.'),
('049fea7e-7912-55de-be05-f17d55f8f496'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Olfactory receptor neurons are unique because they:', '["Are located in the brain","Can regenerate throughout life","Are myelinated","Cannot regenerate","Respond to multiple modalities"]'::jsonb, 'Can regenerate throughout life', 'Olfactory neurons are replaced every ~30-60 days, unlike most neurons.'),
('beb4b725-c9ed-56e2-968f-c9e39f7675a6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The G-protein is:', '["Gs","Gq","Go","Gi","Golf"]'::jsonb, 'Golf', 'Golf activates adenylyl cyclase III, increasing cAMP.'),
('04b7fd6d-3d75-5f89-828d-a3e5e78f8d4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The second messenger in olfactory transduction is:', '["cAMP","IP3","Ca2+","DAG","cGMP"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP', 'cAMP opens cyclic nucleotide-gated (CNG) channels, causing Na+/Ca2+ influx and depolarization.'),
('4fded85a-99ac-5b70-a219-d4b548c03176'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Binding of an odorant to its receptor ultimately causes:', '["Depolarization of the olfactory neuron","Action potential suppression","Hyperpolarization of the olfactory neuron","Inhibition of firing","No change in membrane potential"]'::jsonb, 'Depolarization of the olfactory neuron', 'CNG channel opening causes cation influx, depolarizing the cell and generating action potentials.'),
('f097a958-0521-5109-9cd9-7b59cd8e7e38'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Adaptation in olfaction involves:', '["Receptor degradation mediated physiological mechanism","Neurotransmitter depletion mediated physiological mechanism","Synaptic depression mediated physiological mechanism","Desensitization of the receptor via phosphorylation and Ca2+/calmodulin feedback","Channel inactivation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Desensitization of the receptor via phosphorylation and Ca2+/calmodulin feedback', 'Ca2+/calmodulin-dependent kinase and receptor phosphorylation mediate adaptation.'),
('546d8b7f-c105-5cd5-959a-563a11aba63b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Each olfactory receptor neuron expresses:', '["Only one type of odorant receptor","Many different odorant receptor types","Two receptor types","Three receptor types","No specific receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Only one type of odorant receptor', 'Each neuron expresses one of ~350 functional olfactory receptor genes.'),
('05c146bb-ae41-53e9-952f-380e2b3a63e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The human genome contains approximately how many functional olfactory receptor genes?', '["50","100","350","500","1000"]'::jsonb, '350', 'Humans have ~350 functional OR genes (from ~900 total, many pseudogenes).'),
('b57d6a5a-d657-5aa6-b25e-4b4c5a59be5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Olfactory sensory neurons project to the:', '["Olfactory bulb","Hippocampus","Thalamus","Hypothalamus","Primary olfactory cortex directly"]'::jsonb, 'Olfactory bulb', 'Olfactory axons pass through the cribriform plate and synapse in the olfactory bulb.'),
('6aba8167-f300-5cec-af42-d0e8c40fbff3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Axons of olfactory receptor neurons synapse in the olfactory bulb on:', '["Pyramidal cells","Stellate cells","Periglomerular cells only","Granule cells only","Mitral cells and tufted cells"]'::jsonb, 'Mitral cells and tufted cells', 'Mitral and tufted cells are the principal output neurons of the olfactory bulb.'),
('eebfa8e6-be5e-53ad-b2a0-bb0c1c17f211'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Each glomerulus in the olfactory bulb receives input from:', '["Axons of one specific receptor type","A random mix of receptors","Only supporting cells","Basal cell axons","Axons of many different receptor types"]'::jsonb, 'Axons of one specific receptor type', 'Each glomerulus collects signals from neurons expressing the same receptor.'),
('020ddd5e-d4cf-5abb-8038-4c0424d0c0a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The olfactory tract projects to the primary olfactory cortex which includes:', '["Thalamus only mediated physiological mechanism","Primary visual cortex mediated physiological mechanism","Frontal lobe only mediated physiological mechanism","Piriform cortex, amygdala, and entorhinal cortex","Primary auditory cortex mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Piriform cortex, amygdala, and entorhinal cortex', 'Olfaction bypasses the thalamus and projects directly to the piriform cortex.'),
('5a17c18e-74ba-5831-88c6-f8757b98cb5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Olfaction is unique among sensory systems because it:', '["Has no cortical representation","Has no conscious perception","Relays in the thalamus exclusively","Is processed in the cerebellum","Bypasses the thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Bypasses the thalamus', 'Olfaction is the only sense that does not relay in the thalamus before reaching cortex.'),
('a69cfd89-483f-5002-af0f-14dbeec75d7c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Anosmia (loss of smell) can result from:', '["Vestibular damage","Retinal detachment","Cochlear damage","Head trauma","Taste bud damage"]'::jsonb, 'Head trauma', 'Trauma can tear olfactory axons passing through the cribriform plate.'),
('03621ba5-298f-5032-83cc-8902c7d4e0ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The human can distinguish approximately how many different odors?', '["1 million","10,000 or more","10-100","1 billion","100-1000"]'::jsonb, '10,000 or more', 'Humans can discriminate at least 10,000 different odorants.'),
('3db571c1-9c72-51f1-aaa4-47a76fbb6cc2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Granule cells in the olfactory bulb function as:', '["Supporting cells","Excitatory interneurons","Output neurons","Sensory receptors","Inhibitory interneurons"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibitory interneurons', 'Granule cells form reciprocal dendrodendritic synapses with mitral cells, providing lateral inhibition.'),
('f55872ea-b75f-587c-9020-713418609c9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Periglomerular cells in the olfactory bulb:', '["Are sensory receptors","Produce mucus","Project to the cortex","Support glomerular structure","Modulate input to glomeruli via inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Modulate input to glomeruli via inhibition', 'Periglomerular cells provide inhibitory modulation of glomerular activity.'),
('9ea9b335-c99e-5a11-8e40-3771778ff12f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The piriform cortex is located in the:', '["Parietal lobe","Occipital lobe","Temporal lobe","Insula","Frontal lobe"]'::jsonb, 'Temporal lobe', 'The piriform (olfactory) cortex is in the anterior temporal lobe.'),
('03f8bb60-d637-5f49-baf8-6715aec669a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Pheromone detection in humans is thought to involve the:', '["Taste buds","Facial nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Main olfactory epithelium only","Vomeronasal organ"]'::jsonb, 'Vomeronasal organ', 'The vomeronasal organ is vestigial in humans; pheromone detection may occur via the main olfactory system.'),
('0cfbf331-0b85-5f48-b7a3-277c1f47f2b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Taste buds are located primarily on:', '["Pharynx only","Soft palate only","Tongue papillae","All oral surfaces equally","Epiglottis only"]'::jsonb, 'Tongue papillae', 'Most taste buds are on the tongue, but some are on the palate, pharynx, and epiglottis.'),
('b0610fa1-77e5-5487-aa3d-3b57b5511710'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The average number of taste buds in the human mouth is approximately:', '["5,000-10,000","500","1,000-2,000","20,000-30,000","50,000-100,000"]'::jsonb, '5,000-10,000', 'Humans have about 5,000-10,000 taste buds.'),
('86aa09fc-38ca-5a27-a566-940ac4daf360'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Each taste bud contains how many taste receptor cells?', '["2000","50-100","10-20","200-300","500-1000"]'::jsonb, '50-100', 'Each taste bud contains ~50-100 taste receptor cells.'),
('cbcdd73a-d553-53df-8624-084fc705565b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The five basic taste modalities are:', '["Sweet, salty, bitter, astringent, umami","Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, spicy","Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, fatty","Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, umami","Sweet, sour, salty, metallic, umami"]'::jsonb, 'Sweet, sour, salty, bitter, umami', 'The five accepted basic tastes: sweet, sour, salty, bitter, and umami (savory).'),
('5ef2567d-23de-578a-9ec8-1a0b1e059084'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The taste receptor for umami is:', '["T1R1 + T1R3","T1R2 + T1R3","PKD2L1","ENaC","T2R family"]'::jsonb, 'T1R1 + T1R3', 'Umami (glutamate) is detected by the T1R1/T1R3 heterodimeric receptor.'),
('38d38964-2346-59cc-b1ff-83f2c78c92bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The taste receptor for sweet is:', '["T2R family","T1R1 + T1R3","T1R2 + T1R3","TRPM5","ENaC"]'::jsonb, 'T1R2 + T1R3', 'Sweet compounds are detected by the T1R2/T1R3 heterodimer.'),
('f5f2c631-a640-5c92-8b2c-dd9926689585'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Bitter taste is mediated by which receptor family?', '["ENaC","T2R family","T1R family","PKD channels","TRP channels"]'::jsonb, 'T2R family', 'T2Rs are GPCRs for bitter compounds; humans have ~25 different T2Rs.'),
('cf46c5bc-d328-5e32-a23d-a065fe4f3bef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Salty taste is primarily detected by:', '["T1R1","T1R3","ENaC","TRPM5","T2R"]'::jsonb, 'ENaC', 'Na+ ions enter via ENaC (amiloride-sensitive) on taste cell apical membranes.'),
('9505cc00-c596-517d-8d8b-5fe7ff062276'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Sour taste is detected by:', '["T1R3","ENaC","T2R","T1R1","PKD2L1"]'::jsonb, 'PKD2L1', 'Sour (acid) taste involves PKD2L1 channels that respond to intracellular acidification.'),
('91a0fe42-f0aa-5fd3-ab2e-29be12603a3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The sensory neurons for taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travel via:', '["Facial nerve (CN VII) via chorda tympani","Glossopharyngeal nerve mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoglossal nerve mediated physiological mechanism","Trigeminal nerve mediated physiological mechanism","Vagus nerve mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Facial nerve (CN VII) via chorda tympani', 'Chorda tympani (branch of CN VII) carries taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue.'),
('a5b1fde3-f536-50f4-9b24-87ead01fa8e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The sensory neurons for taste from the posterior one-third of the tongue travel via:', '["Vagus nerve","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Glossopharyngeal nerve', 'CN IX carries taste from the posterior 1/3 of the tongue (circumvallate papillae).'),
('ee3475d8-d4a9-594c-908f-30f727949680'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Taste from the epiglottis and pharynx is carried by:', '["Accessory nerve","Facial nerve","Trigeminal nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve","Vagus nerve"]'::jsonb, 'Vagus nerve', 'CN X carries taste from the epiglottis and pharyngeal region.'),
('eb84f552-fcfa-5b04-87b1-fbdf043f3352'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The primary taste cortex is located in the:', '["Insula and frontal operculum","Occipital lobe","Cingulate gyrus","Parietal lobe","Temporal pole"]'::jsonb, 'Insula and frontal operculum', 'The primary gustatory cortex is in the insula and overlying frontal operculum.'),
('3fb25186-a182-5119-9166-7112aec0dcf0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The nucleus of the solitary tract (NST) in the medulla is the first relay for:', '["Auditory information","Taste (gustatory) information","Visual information","Somatosensory information","Olfactory information"]'::jsonb, 'Taste (gustatory) information', 'Cranial nerve taste afferents synapse in the rostral NST.'),
('79fd8cbd-f86f-572b-805f-ffb4044af877'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'From the NST, taste information projects to the:', '["Thalamus","Lateral geniculate nucleus","Cerebellum","Medial geniculate nucleus","Hypothalamus only"]'::jsonb, 'Thalamus', 'Taste fibers from NST ascend to the VPM of the thalamus.'),
('10464211-ce81-5fdd-a561-57363eb8bd8d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'From the VPM thalamus, taste fibers project to the:', '["Primary auditory cortex","Prefrontal cortex","Primary visual cortex","Hippocampus","Insular gustatory cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Insular gustatory cortex', 'VPM projects to the insular gustatory cortex.'),
('c12056a1-d495-5a63-a36b-cb6e289a30d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Taste receptor cells have a lifespan of approximately:', '["1-2 days","10-14 days","1 year","30-60 days","6 months"]'::jsonb, '10-14 days', 'Taste cells are replaced every ~10-14 days.'),
('0b6b0901-4bf6-5950-a68c-5b0cf1a3b101'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The threshold for salty taste detection is approximately:', '["0.01 M NaCl","0.5 M NaCl","1.0 M NaCl","0.1 M NaCl","0.001 M NaCl"]'::jsonb, '0.01 M NaCl', 'The taste threshold for NaCl is ~0.01 M (10 mM).'),
('09ce9814-7579-5efe-8a22-ca4fd1f67fcd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Supertasters are individuals with:', '["Fewer taste buds than average mediated physiological mechanism","Loss of bitter taste mediated physiological mechanism","No ability to taste PTC mediated physiological mechanism","A higher density of fungiform papillae and taste buds","Loss of sweet taste mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'A higher density of fungiform papillae and taste buds', 'Supertasters have a higher density of fungiform papillae and greater sensitivity to PROP/PTC.'),
('0ff23b4b-0ac8-5f95-8d51-e973bb7b2681'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The PROP/PTC tasting ability is genetically determined by the:', '["TAS2R38 gene","ENaC gene","PKD2L1 gene","TAS1R2 gene","TAS1R3 gene"]'::jsonb, 'TAS2R38 gene', 'TAS2R38 polymorphisms determine sensitivity to PROP/PTC bitter compounds.'),
('49ad9322-6f02-5f7b-8936-98471670eb64'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Taste adaptation refers to:', '["Decreased sensitivity with prolonged stimulation","Increased sensitivity over time","Enhanced flavor perception","Complete loss of taste","Recovery after stimulation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased sensitivity with prolonged stimulation', 'Taste adaptation decreases perceived intensity with continuous exposure.'),
('6a09aeb2-66e5-5806-8d14-1a2b56c99a93'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Cross-adaptation in taste occurs when:', '["Taste sensitivity increases mediated physiological mechanism","All tastes become more intense mediated physiological mechanism","Taste receptors proliferate mediated physiological mechanism","One taste reduces the perceived intensity of another taste","No change occurs mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'One taste reduces the perceived intensity of another taste', 'Adaptation to one tastant can reduce sensitivity to a tastant sharing the same receptor.'),
('617c0b10-b695-5ed8-b38f-e796a82b7bf6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The trigeminal nerve (CN V) contributes to flavor perception by detecting:', '["Only touch","Texture only","Chemesthetic sensations","Taste only","Temperature only"]'::jsonb, 'Chemesthetic sensations', 'CN V detects chemical irritation (capsaicin, menthol, CO2) contributing to flavor.'),
('ecd8d494-9826-5a35-b63f-6d9019f60489'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Ageusia (loss of taste) can result from:', '["Damage to the chorda tympani or glossopharyngeal nerve","Cochlear damage mediated physiological mechanism","Olfactory nerve damage mediated physiological mechanism","Optic nerve damage mediated physiological mechanism","Vestibular damage mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Damage to the chorda tympani or glossopharyngeal nerve', 'Taste loss results from damage to gustatory nerves or taste receptor cells.'),
('270262ba-bfc8-5eb6-9470-4fe5fa304a05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Flavor perception is a combination of:', '["Olfaction (retronasal) and taste","Vision alone","Taste alone","Only somatosensation","Hearing and touch"]'::jsonb, 'Olfaction (retronasal) and taste', 'Most of what we perceive as flavor comes from retronasal olfaction.'),
('1b57be74-02a4-5d74-bc8a-07c651fa7cc1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'The fungiform papillae contain approximately how many taste buds each?', '["5-10","1-3","50-100","10-20","None"]'::jsonb, '1-3', 'Fungiform papillae have a few (1-3) taste buds each.'),
('430cb14f-a660-5bf7-9621-7e914322b55f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Special Senses', 'Circumvallate papillae are located:', '["On the soft palate","At the tip of the tongue","On the sides of the tongue","On the epiglottis","At the back of the tongue in a V-shaped row"]'::jsonb, 'At the back of the tongue in a V-shaped row', 'Circumvallate papillae form a V-shaped row at the posterior tongue.'),
('f0efa2b0-7662-538a-9217-e166a79d7337'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The hypothalamic-pituitary axis connects the hypothalamus to the pituitary via:', '["Only neural connection mediated physiological mechanism","Direct arterial connection mediated physiological mechanism","Hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal system and hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract","Lymphatic channels mediated physiological mechanism","Only venous drainage mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal system and hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract', 'The anterior pituitary receives blood via the portal system; the posterior pituitary is connected by the neural tract.'),
('22d969b1-63b6-5380-b312-f4c4d52c630f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypothalamic releasing hormones reach the anterior pituitary via:', '["Hypophyseal portal veins","Cavernous sinus","Inferior hypophyseal artery","Third ventricle","Hypothalamic-hypophyseal tract"]'::jsonb, 'Hypophyseal portal veins', 'Releasing hormones are secreted into the primary capillary plexus and travel via portal veins to the anterior pituitary.'),
('9862a083-a9ee-5a50-8a11-500fedbbe9b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Which hypothalamic hormone inhibits prolactin secretion?', '["CRH","GHRH","GnRH","TRH","Dopamine"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine', 'Dopamine from the hypothalamus tonically inhibits prolactin release from lactotrophs.'),
('28f4530d-1900-5fb5-9df4-715d790f8dcf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The posterior pituitary stores and releases:', '["FSH and LH","GH and TSH","Prolactin","ACTH","ADH and oxytocin"]'::jsonb, 'ADH and oxytocin', 'The posterior pituitary stores ADH and oxytocin synthesized in the hypothalamus.'),
('8ad6ac13-1abc-552e-bec0-868950f07975'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'ADH (vasopressin) is synthesized in the:', '["Kidney mediated physiological mechanism","Anterior pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of hypothalamus","Posterior pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Adrenal mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of hypothalamus', 'ADH is synthesized in the supraoptic (mainly) and paraventricular nuclei and transported to the posterior pituitary.'),
('3474fce5-5bbe-5806-b4b5-a16fd28057a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Oxytocin is synthesized in the:', '["Mammary gland mediated physiological mechanism","Uterus mediated physiological mechanism","Posterior pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Anterior pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei"]'::jsonb, 'Paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei', 'Oxytocin is synthesized in magnocellular neurons of these hypothalamic nuclei.'),
('77d082e9-f425-57cf-b29d-65953cb9e970'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'ADH acts on the kidney to:', '["Increase Na+ reabsorption","Increase K+ secretion","Increase water reabsorption via V2 receptors","Decrease urine osmolality","Decrease water reabsorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increase water reabsorption via V2 receptors', 'ADH binds V2 receptors on collecting duct cells, inserting AQP2 channels for water reabsorption.'),
('b6ef44a6-6107-544e-8009-f043151ab31a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Diabetes insipidus is characterized by:', '["Oliguria, edema","Hematuria, proteinuria","Polyuria, high urine specific gravity","Polyuria, polydipsia, low urine specific gravity","Glycosuria, polyphagia"]'::jsonb, 'Polyuria, polydipsia, low urine specific gravity', 'DI: inability to concentrate urine due to ADH deficiency (central) or resistance (nephrogenic).'),
('dee5d101-c758-5478-85f1-066be0d94b1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis is activated by:', '["Sexual activity","Rest","Stress","Sleep","Feeding"]'::jsonb, 'Stress', 'CRH and AVP from the hypothalamus activate the HPA axis in response to stress.'),
('27cff4e8-dc3b-5a46-bcc4-b971beaa9be9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'CRH (corticotropin-releasing hormone) is secreted by:', '["Anterior pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Paraventricular nucleus of hypothalamus","Brainstem mediated physiological mechanism","Posterior pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Adrenal cortex mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Paraventricular nucleus of hypothalamus', 'CRH from the paraventricular nucleus stimulates ACTH secretion from the anterior pituitary.'),
('8bd19fb2-64c2-54a8-a7b2-6131a2f749cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The hypothalamic-pituitary-thyroid (HPT) axis is regulated by:', '["TRH from hypothalamus stimulating TSH from pituitary","Autocrine regulation mediated physiological mechanism","Only negative feedback mediated physiological mechanism","Positive feedback only mediated physiological mechanism","Direct neural control mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'TRH from hypothalamus stimulating TSH from pituitary', 'TRH stimulates TSH; T3/T4 provide negative feedback at both levels.'),
('8eddae2a-eaae-57d4-a0d0-18cbe15f1870'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GnRH is secreted in a:', '["Steady state","Random bursts","Cyclic fashion only in females","Pulsatile fashion","Continuous fashion"]'::jsonb, 'Pulsatile fashion', 'Pulsatile GnRH maintains pituitary responsiveness; continuous GnRH causes desensitization.'),
('3ece6e8e-f184-5c98-9140-caf5190d0f91'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal (HPG) axis controls:', '["Metabolism mediated physiological mechanism","Stress response mediated physiological mechanism","Calcium balance mediated physiological mechanism","Growth mediated physiological mechanism","Reproductive function and sex hormone production"]'::jsonb, 'Reproductive function and sex hormone production', 'The HPG axis regulates gametogenesis and sex steroid production.'),
('fd80ddf8-7271-5225-8206-b00ade24cbaa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Somatostatin (GHIH) is produced by:', '["Paraventricular nucleus","Arcuate nucleus","Supraoptic nucleus","Periventricular nucleus of hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Periventricular nucleus of hypothalamus', 'Somatostatin from the periventricular nucleus inhibits GH and TSH secretion.'),
('49bdc386-3916-5afd-8182-a0d3c5fe6251'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GHRH is produced by:', '["Anterior pituitary","Periventricular nucleus","Arcuate nucleus of hypothalamus","Supraoptic nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus"]'::jsonb, 'Arcuate nucleus of hypothalamus', 'GHRH from the arcuate nucleus stimulates GH release from somatotrophs.'),
('c0efd303-dcd5-5045-b075-5a072519bbf2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The pituitary gland is located in the:', '["Sella turcica","Cavernous sinus","Third ventricle","Cribriform plate","Foramen magnum"]'::jsonb, 'Sella turcica', 'The pituitary fossa (sella turcica) houses the pituitary gland at the base of the skull.'),
('b1677b41-d61a-563f-8d04-35dcc1c1ada7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The anterior pituitary develops from:', '["Rathke''s pouch","Neuroepithelium","Mesoderm","Neural ectoderm","Endoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Rathke''s pouch', 'The anterior pituitary derives from oral ectoderm; the posterior pituitary from neural ectoderm.'),
('5b8b9e50-5465-59ed-8757-c6c7eb228397'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The posterior pituitary develops from:', '["Mesoderm","Rathke''s pouch","Stomodeum","Endoderm","Neural ectoderm"]'::jsonb, 'Neural ectoderm', 'The posterior pituitary is a neurovascular structure derived from the hypothalamus.'),
('99fa3435-b3f6-5aeb-965c-639d0f81da05'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypothalamic-releasing hormones are transported to the anterior pituitary via:', '["Lymphatics","Long portal veins","Systemic circulation","Short portal veins","Neural tract"]'::jsonb, 'Long portal veins', 'The long portal veins connect the median eminence to the anterior pituitary.'),
('8bb4e146-adaf-5d69-8ff1-2acde3a2d405'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The median eminence is part of the:', '["Hypothalamus","Third ventricle","Anterior pituitary","Posterior pituitary","Pituitary stalk"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus', 'The median eminence is the final common pathway for hypothalamic releasing hormones.'),
('c3d2a0f4-88f2-5891-92b9-384f4805f5f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Negative feedback of thyroid hormones occurs at the:', '["Thyroid gland","Peripheral tissues","Only pituitary","Pituitary and hypothalamus","Only hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary and hypothalamus', 'T3 inhibits TSH gene expression in the pituitary and TRH in the hypothalamus.'),
('d8a97d74-f3cb-5228-8db2-bf0f25cceecb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol exerts negative feedback on:', '["Only CRH","Only the adrenal","Only ACTH","Only the pituitary","CRH and ACTH secretion"]'::jsonb, 'CRH and ACTH secretion', 'Cortisol inhibits both hypothalamic CRH and pituitary ACTH secretion.'),
('b6e0513c-8020-52ae-b51d-afbb0070910e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Sex steroids (estrogen/testosterone) exert negative feedback on:', '["Only the gonads","Only GnRH","Only LH","GnRH, FSH, and LH","Only FSH"]'::jsonb, 'GnRH, FSH, and LH', 'Sex steroids suppress GnRH pulse frequency and gonadotropin secretion.'),
('e3e89b07-8673-5e61-9e4d-0119413f33c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The pituitary stalk connects:', '["Hypothalamus to pituitary","Pituitary to adrenal","Pituitary to brainstem","Hypothalamus to thalamus","Pituitary to thalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothalamus to pituitary', 'The infundibulum (pituitary stalk) carries axons to the posterior pituitary and portal vessels to the anterior pituitary.'),
('c339cb00-68b1-53be-9ec7-17a27a656534'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypothalamic thermoregulation is mediated by:', '["Brainstem mediated physiological mechanism","Preoptic/anterior hypothalamus (heat loss) and posterior hypothalamus","Only posterior hypothalamus mediated physiological mechanism","Thalamus mediated physiological mechanism","Only anterior hypothalamus mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Preoptic/anterior hypothalamus (heat loss) and posterior hypothalamus', 'The preoptic area contains thermosensitive neurons that coordinate body temperature.'),
('c79b9f91-4d3d-5e64-8999-93a869c616d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus regulates:', '["Thirst","Circadian rhythms","Emotion","Appetite","Sexual behavior"]'::jsonb, 'Circadian rhythms', 'The SCN is the master circadian pacemaker, receiving light input from the retina.'),
('0cc8d4fe-bec2-5e49-a54f-9d1d0051b20b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The lateral hypothalamus contains the:', '["Thermostat","Thirst center","Satiety center","Feeding center","Sexual center"]'::jsonb, 'Feeding center', 'The lateral hypothalamus (hunger center) stimulates appetite; the ventromedial nucleus is the satiety center.'),
('3482a33a-72d4-56c6-86f5-1d31573753c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The ventromedial hypothalamus is the:', '["Satiety center","Thirst center","Pleasure center","Anger center","Hunger center"]'::jsonb, 'Satiety center', 'Lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus cause hyperphagia and obesity.'),
('e6b59850-2405-5b72-8759-0c99041f017f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The arcuate nucleus contains neurons that produce:', '["Only dopamine mediated physiological mechanism","Only GnRH mediated physiological mechanism","Only TRH mediated physiological mechanism","NPY, AgRP, POMC, and kisspeptin","Only CRH mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'NPY, AgRP, POMC, and kisspeptin', 'The arcuate nucleus integrates energy balance and reproductive signals.'),
('4f8bcbae-9c26-583c-b3f5-bb546fb321e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Kisspeptin is produced by the arcuate nucleus and:', '["Stimulates GnRH secretion","Stimulates ACTH","Inhibits GnRH","Inhibits TSH","Stimulates GH"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates GnRH secretion', 'Kisspeptin signals via KISS1R on GnRH neurons to initiate puberty and regulate fertility.'),
('27906ab9-de1d-5bac-917b-bab27a504e62'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The anterior pituitary consists of ___ cell types:', '["4","5","7","6","3"]'::jsonb, '5', 'The five cell types correspond to the five major anterior pituitary hormones.'),
('c202a68f-4524-5410-9388-66dbb86e7b28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Somatotrophs secrete:', '["ACTH","FSH and LH","Growth hormone","TSH","Prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'Growth hormone', 'Somatotrophs constitute ~50% of anterior pituitary cells.'),
('3f49e059-a85a-5d2b-9cb0-9c63a8e343be'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Lactotrophs secrete:', '["TSH","GH","ACTH","Prolactin","FSH and LH"]'::jsonb, 'Prolactin', 'Lactotrophs increase in number during pregnancy and lactation.'),
('a007af97-4aa6-568c-9af6-c336fbcca0f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Corticotrophs secrete:', '["FSH","Prolactin","ACTH","GH","TSH"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH', 'Corticotrophs produce POMC which is cleaved to ACTH, MSH, and beta-endorphin.'),
('ec08d35f-c627-57df-bd17-914315217fba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Thyrotrophs secrete:', '["Prolactin","FSH","TSH","GH","ACTH"]'::jsonb, 'TSH', 'Thyrotrophs constitute ~5% of anterior pituitary cells.'),
('45838483-e330-5c0f-946f-323f3b8c4937'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Gonadotrophs secrete:', '["ACTH and TSH","Only LH","Only FSH","FSH and LH","GH and prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'FSH and LH', 'Gonadotrophs are unique in secreting two different hormones.'),
('cfc5bd98-130d-5531-89f4-9775d71df6dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'ACTH is derived from:', '["Pro-GHRH","Pre-proinsulin","Pro-TRH","Pro-GnRH","POMC"]'::jsonb, 'POMC', 'POMC is cleaved by prohormone convertases to yield ACTH, MSH, and beta-endorphin.'),
('b30b038c-8e3a-5e65-b3fc-bfc4b1298bea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'FSH and LH are:', '["Amine hormones","Fatty acid derivatives","Peptide hormones","Steroid hormones","Glycoprotein hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoprotein hormones', 'The glycoprotein hormones have a common alpha subunit and a unique beta subunit.'),
('c7f37569-7570-5186-8539-e5603e3fe244'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'TSH is a:', '["Glycoprotein hormone","Peptide","Steroid","Amine","Polypeptide"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoprotein hormone', 'TSH, FSH, LH, and hCG share the same alpha subunit.'),
('99ab02be-e4e3-5839-a210-bf09c3fb7257'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Growth hormone (GH) is a:', '["Peptide fragment mediated physiological mechanism","191-amino acid polypeptide hormone","Steroid mediated physiological mechanism","Glycoprotein mediated physiological mechanism","Amine mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, '191-amino acid polypeptide hormone', 'GH is a single-chain polypeptide with two disulfide bonds.'),
('41c36dc8-3ddf-5ec9-ba87-ee6743220220'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Prolactin is structurally similar to:', '["ACTH mediated physiological mechanism","Growth hormone and human placental lactogen","FSH mediated physiological mechanism","LH mediated physiological mechanism","TSH mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Growth hormone and human placental lactogen', 'GH, prolactin, and hPL belong to the same family of lactogenic hormones.'),
('0014583a-0392-5422-850d-67309e483850'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'ACTH secretion follows a:', '["Menstrual pattern","No rhythm","Ultradian rhythm","Circadian rhythm","Seasonal pattern"]'::jsonb, 'Circadian rhythm', 'ACTH and cortisol have a diurnal rhythm with highest levels in early morning.'),
('12125c29-02c7-5861-a103-f98d4f5b4f99'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The half-life of ACTH is:', '["Long","Hours","Variable","Very long","Short"]'::jsonb, 'Short', 'ACTH has a short half-life due to rapid proteolysis in plasma.'),
('8ffaf7ba-96d3-543a-8321-dd484f848743'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The half-life of GH is:', '["~2 hours","~10 seconds","~1 day","~5 minutes","~20-30 minutes"]'::jsonb, '~20-30 minutes', 'GH is cleared rapidly, mostly by the liver and kidney.'),
('f0b21905-77ca-5ca4-a386-1e6105f418c0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The half-life of TSH is:', '["~4 hours","~60 minutes","~10 minutes","~5 minutes","~1 day"]'::jsonb, '~60 minutes', 'TSH has a moderate half-life compared to other pituitary hormones.'),
('12334fd2-c4d2-5615-a3f8-190dd48744b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Prolactin secretion is increased by:', '["Low estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","TRH, suckling, stress, sleep, estrogen","Somatostatin mediated physiological mechanism","GABA mediated physiological mechanism","Dopamine mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'TRH, suckling, stress, sleep, estrogen', 'Many stimuli increase prolactin; only dopamine inhibits it tonically.'),
('dbe0b27a-357d-517a-8191-acb6d203422e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hyperprolactinemia causes:', '["Cushing syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Diabetes mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperthyroidism mediated physiological mechanism","Acromegaly mediated physiological mechanism","Galactorrhea, amenorrhea, infertility, hypogonadism"]'::jsonb, 'Galactorrhea, amenorrhea, infertility, hypogonadism', 'High prolactin suppresses GnRH, causing hypogonadotropic hypogonadism.'),
('d93db9bc-7a94-528c-bcf5-13d76cee6bec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Prolactinomas are the most common:', '["Functional pituitary tumors","ACTH-secreting tumors","Non-functional tumors","Malignant tumors","TSH-secreting tumors"]'::jsonb, 'Functional pituitary tumors', 'Prolactinomas account for ~40% of pituitary adenomas.'),
('b4aaeb12-8121-57c5-abef-bf7bc68c961a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'TSH stimulates the thyroid to produce:', '["Only T4","Only T3","T4 and T3","Thyroglobulin","Calcitonin"]'::jsonb, 'T4 and T3', 'TSH binds TSH receptors on follicular cells, stimulating all steps of thyroid hormone synthesis.'),
('bc76a2ef-1065-5891-9382-df1ae15de472'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'TSH secretion is inhibited by:', '["Cold exposure mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","TRH mediated physiological mechanism","Low T4 mediated physiological mechanism","T3 (negative feedback), somatostatin, dopamine, high cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'T3 (negative feedback), somatostatin, dopamine, high cortisol', 'Multiple factors inhibit TSH; T3 is the most important physiological inhibitor.'),
