('f4d79370-77d1-5405-a022-943fd43c028e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'ACTH has a melanocyte-stimulating effect because:', '["It shares sequence with MSH","It stimulates melanin","It increases cortisol","It binds MSH receptors","It causes tanning"]'::jsonb, 'It shares sequence with MSH', 'This explains hyperpigmentation in Addison disease (high ACTH).'),
('7f0966a1-f30b-5817-b70e-2ef1736b7e36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'FSH in males acts on:', '["Sertoli cells","Epididymis","Germ cells","Prostate","Leydig cells"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells', 'FSH is essential for the initiation and maintenance of spermatogenesis.'),
('521eaab9-f7a0-5698-85f7-cd91862167aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'LH in males acts on:', '["Epididymis","Germ cells","Prostate","Leydig cells","Sertoli cells"]'::jsonb, 'Leydig cells', 'LH binds to LHCG receptors on Leydig cells, activating steroidogenesis.'),
('3e8ac9ec-493a-5c7e-9338-b262974e39f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'FSH in females acts on:', '["Oocyte","Granulosa cells","Theca cells","Endometrium","Corpus luteum"]'::jsonb, 'Granulosa cells', 'FSH stimulates follicle growth, aromatase activity, and inhibin production.'),
('56057908-3e7f-56fd-91d5-b10489a24905'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'LH in females acts on:', '["Endometrium mediated physiological mechanism","Granulosa cells mediated physiological mechanism","Breast mediated physiological mechanism","Oocyte mediated physiological mechanism","Theca cells (produces androstenedione) and triggers ovulation"]'::jsonb, 'Theca cells (produces androstenedione) and triggers ovulation', 'LH stimulates theca interna to produce androgen substrates for estrogen synthesis.'),
('28187878-64e8-5863-b054-d6577908f888'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The LH surge is essential for:', '["Menstruation","Lactation","Endometrial proliferation","Ovulation and luteinization","Follicular growth"]'::jsonb, 'Ovulation and luteinization', 'The mid-cycle LH surge triggers oocyte maturation, ovulation, and corpus luteum formation.'),
('07c2c28f-1ef5-51a6-a00d-b1bbd69d6328'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Anterior pituitary hormone deficiencies can result from:', '["Pituitary adenoma, surgery, radiation, infarction (Sheehan), autoimmunity","Only infection mediated physiological mechanism","Only trauma mediated physiological mechanism","Only tumors mediated physiological mechanism","Only genetic mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary adenoma, surgery, radiation, infarction (Sheehan), autoimmunity', 'Sheehan syndrome is postpartum pituitary necrosis due to hemorrhage.'),
('50390d8a-3a2f-5c8e-aa9b-8f8440cb028e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Sheehan syndrome is:', '["Pituitary adenoma","Empty sella syndrome","Hypothalamic tumor","Pituitary hyperplasia","Postpartum pituitary infarction"]'::jsonb, 'Postpartum pituitary infarction', 'During pregnancy the pituitary enlarges and is vulnerable to ischemic necrosis if blood pressure drops.'),
('c0dd6e9d-7bee-5c8d-8a1c-d9bc97397c45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Empty sella syndrome refers to:', '["Pituitary atrophy mediated physiological mechanism","Pituitary hemorrhage mediated physiological mechanism","Pituitary tumor mediated physiological mechanism","Flattening of the pituitary gland due to herniation of subarachnoid space into the sella","Congenital absence of pituitary mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Flattening of the pituitary gland due to herniation of subarachnoid space into the sella', 'Empty sella is often benign but can be associated with hormonal abnormalities.'),
('ad4b8b13-fa53-58bc-9312-73fb3b280a6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Pituitary apoplexy is:', '["Empty sella mediated physiological mechanism","Pituitary hyperplasia mediated physiological mechanism","Sheehan syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Acute hemorrhage/infarction of a pituitary adenoma","Craniopharyngioma mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Acute hemorrhage/infarction of a pituitary adenoma', 'Pituitary apoplexy is a medical emergency requiring surgical decompression and steroid replacement.'),
('f3ee171f-1a74-54af-b9f7-53d00420ed2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Craniopharyngioma is a:', '["Pituitary adenoma mediated physiological mechanism","Hypothalamic glioma mediated physiological mechanism","Suprasellar tumor (Rathke pouch remnant) causing visual defects, endocrine deficits","Meningioma mediated physiological mechanism","Germinoma mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Suprasellar tumor (Rathke pouch remnant) causing visual defects, endocrine deficits', 'Craniopharyngiomas often present with bitemporal hemianopia and growth hormone deficiency.'),
('1e98e115-0ed5-5ec2-894e-f3d1c5b91b09'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Lymphocytic hypophysitis is:', '["Pituitary infarction mediated physiological mechanism","Pituitary hyperplasia mediated physiological mechanism","Pituitary tumor mediated physiological mechanism","Autoimmune inflammation of the pituitary","Pituitary infection mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune inflammation of the pituitary', 'Lymphocytic hypophysitis can cause mass effects and hormonal deficiencies.'),
('1e664d18-9858-5dc2-b4a8-6caa3c4f5035'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The pituitary gland receives its blood supply from:', '["Vertebral artery","Anterior cerebral artery","Circle of Willis","Superior and inferior hypophyseal arteries","Middle cerebral artery"]'::jsonb, 'Superior and inferior hypophyseal arteries', 'The superior hypophyseal artery supplies the anterior pituitary; the inferior supplies the posterior pituitary.'),
('92e1cb13-e8e1-51a0-bccb-addd4d8b9770'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Kallmann syndrome involves:', '["GnRH deficiency with anosmia","TSH deficiency","ADH deficiency","ACTH deficiency","GH deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'GnRH deficiency with anosmia', 'Kallmann syndrome is congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia.'),
('2d94d6b4-2749-53ff-8ba7-e70585ac8f74'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Prader-Willi syndrome (PWS) features:', '["Diabetes insipidus mediated physiological mechanism","Precocious puberty mediated physiological mechanism","Tall stature, hypergonadism mediated physiological mechanism","Cushing syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperphagia, obesity, hypotonia, intellectual disability, GH deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperphagia, obesity, hypotonia, intellectual disability, GH deficiency', 'PWS is due to loss of paternal 15q11-q13; hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and GH deficiency are common.'),
('90536581-1388-52bd-a8b1-1b353754bc4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'ACTH deficiency (secondary adrenal insufficiency) presents with:', '["Hypertension, hypokalemia","Fatigue, weight loss, hypotension, hypoglycemia","Hyperpigmentation, hyperkalemia","Moon face, striae","Galactorrhea"]'::jsonb, 'Fatigue, weight loss, hypotension, hypoglycemia', 'Without ACTH, the adrenal cortex atrophies; aldosterone is usually preserved.'),
('a809fa4e-5f31-5d9c-ab3d-fff17501a910'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'TSH deficiency (secondary hypothyroidism) presents with:', '["Thyroid nodules mediated physiological mechanism","Low T4 with inappropriately normal/low TSH","High TSH, low T4 mediated physiological mechanism","High T4, low TSH mediated physiological mechanism","Goiter, hyperthyroidism mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Low T4 with inappropriately normal/low TSH', 'Secondary hypothyroidism: TSH is not elevated despite low thyroid hormones.'),
('1845c61d-0d46-5e07-89b6-958860efc701'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Growth hormone is secreted by:', '["Gonadotrophs mediated physiological mechanism","Thyrotrophs mediated physiological mechanism","Somatotrophs of the anterior pituitary","Lactotrophs mediated physiological mechanism","Corticotrophs mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Somatotrophs of the anterior pituitary', 'Somatotrophs constitute ~50% of anterior pituitary cells and secrete GH.'),
('ed9f26e8-f8ba-5370-a71e-0ecaaee1c42c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GH secretion is stimulated by:', '["Somatostatin mediated physiological mechanism","GABA mediated physiological mechanism","GHRH (from hypothalamus) and ghrelin","Serotonin mediated physiological mechanism","Dopamine mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'GHRH (from hypothalamus) and ghrelin', 'GHRH stimulates GH release; somatostatin inhibits it. Ghrelin also stimulates GH.'),
('2f6c703c-90ac-5d6d-986a-8d90dc67e29a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GH secretion is inhibited by:', '["Low blood glucose","Exercise","Arginine","Somatostatin","GHRH"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Somatostatin (GHIH) from the periventricular nucleus inhibits GH release.'),
('c40a6b2e-643e-5f66-80d9-841e3189c0b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GH acts directly on tissues via:', '["GH receptor","Ion channel","Tyrosine kinase receptor","GPCR","Nuclear receptor"]'::jsonb, 'GH receptor', 'GH binds to a homodimeric receptor activating JAK2/STAT5 signaling.'),
('3f4f634e-31ee-5d5a-a985-59a70853e873'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'IGF-1 (somatomedin C) is produced mainly in:', '["Kidney","Bone","Muscle","Liver","Brain"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'GH stimulates the liver to produce IGF-1, which mediates many of GH''s growth-promoting effects.'),
('ad9994bc-b27f-5ff6-a396-56cfbc43e73a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Excess GH during childhood causes:', '["Precocious puberty","Acromegaly","Cushing syndrome","Gigantism","Dwarfism"]'::jsonb, 'Gigantism', 'GH excess before epiphyseal closure causes gigantism (tall stature).'),
('d82a7fa1-a9c7-564a-ba9d-e28d8a1278e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Excess GH after epiphyseal closure causes:', '["Acromegaly","Dwarfism","Gigantism","Cushing syndrome","Thyrotoxicosis"]'::jsonb, 'Acromegaly', 'Acromegaly features periosteal bone growth, organomegaly, and soft tissue enlargement.'),
('d3703ad7-efcb-53be-92ef-682e8ad67c98'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GH deficiency in children causes:', '["Cushing syndrome","Marfan syndrome","Cretinism","Achondroplasia","Pituitary dwarfism"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary dwarfism', 'GH-deficient children have short stature with normal body proportions.'),
('d9e22674-fbc8-587c-bdb0-0f281fc54473'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The most potent stimulus for GH release is:', '["Exercise","Arginine","Stress","Deep sleep","Hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Deep sleep', 'GH is secreted in pulses, with the largest peak during slow-wave sleep.'),
('48d14787-46e7-583b-bf32-ebf928b3dd75'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GH effects on metabolism include:', '["Increased lipolysis, increased protein synthesis, anti-insulin effect","Decreased lipolysis mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased amino acid uptake mediated physiological mechanism","Increased glucose uptake mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased protein synthesis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased lipolysis, increased protein synthesis, anti-insulin effect', 'GH is anabolic (protein, bone) but also diabetogenic (anti-insulin).'),
('42eb4db0-8239-5208-8b17-98334ad35533'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'IGF-1 acts via:', '["GH receptor","IGF-1 receptor","Nuclear receptor","Cytokine receptor","GPCR"]'::jsonb, 'IGF-1 receptor', 'The IGF-1 receptor has high homology with the insulin receptor.'),
('2da2713b-669c-5107-998b-6464291c9b11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'IGF-binding proteins (IGFBPs) primarily:', '["Increase IGF-1 synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Block GH receptor mediated physiological mechanism","Carry IGF-1 in circulation and regulate its bioavailability","Degrade IGF-1 mediated physiological mechanism","Promote IGF-1 excretion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Carry IGF-1 in circulation and regulate its bioavailability', 'IGFBP-3 is the major carrier; IGFBPs control IGF-1 half-life and tissue delivery.'),
('a0fd785e-7c73-5de4-9810-aa6f8c2798f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Acromegaly features include:', '["Weight loss, tachycardia mediated physiological mechanism","Short stature mediated physiological mechanism","Delayed puberty mediated physiological mechanism","Coarse facial features, large hands/feet, prognathism, organomegaly, glucose intolerance","Hypoglycemia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Coarse facial features, large hands/feet, prognathism, organomegaly, glucose intolerance', 'Acromegaly is insidious; diagnosis is often delayed by years.'),
('2a169433-2439-57c7-bd31-a03e87bbe3df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Acromegaly is most commonly caused by:', '["Ectopic GH secretion","GH-secreting pituitary adenoma","IGF-1 excess","Adrenal tumor","Hypothalamic GHRH excess"]'::jsonb, 'GH-secreting pituitary adenoma', '~95% of acromegaly cases are due to pituitary adenomas.'),
('26479a6d-0847-5782-93a4-61283e21e8d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Treatment of acromegaly includes:', '["Only radiotherapy mediated physiological mechanism","GH replacement mediated physiological mechanism","Only surgery mediated physiological mechanism","Dopamine agonists mediated physiological mechanism","Transsphenoidal surgery, somatostatin analogs (octreotide), GH receptor antagonists"]'::jsonb, 'Transsphenoidal surgery, somatostatin analogs (octreotide), GH receptor antagonists', 'Somatostatin analogs inhibit GH secretion; pegvisomant blocks GH receptor.'),
('ac8f9c5a-dcc6-5589-b2ca-dc85c9718ba7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Laron syndrome is:', '["GH receptor deficiency","IGF-1 deficiency","GH excess","Pituitary tumor","GH deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'GH receptor deficiency', 'Laron dwarfism: defective GH receptor causes short stature with high GH but low IGF-1.'),
('2f605af2-4b43-59c1-80a3-4b38d5a15a54'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GH insensitivity syndromes are treated with:', '["GHRH","GH","Somatostatin","Octreotide","Recombinant IGF-1"]'::jsonb, 'Recombinant IGF-1', 'Since GH cannot act, exogenous IGF-1 bypasses the receptor defect.'),
('3fd1ea3b-d153-5474-8476-1813af9335a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GH acts on the liver to produce:', '["ALS","IGFBP-3","IGF-1","All of the above","IGF-2"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'GH stimulates the liver to secrete IGF-1, IGFBP-3, and ALS, forming a ternary complex.'),
('a7ed95fb-afb3-5b77-8304-92e259ead2fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The GH-IGF-1 axis is important for:', '["Only metabolism mediated physiological mechanism","Linear bone growth, muscle mass, organ size, metabolism","Only muscle mediated physiological mechanism","Only brain development mediated physiological mechanism","Only bone growth mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Linear bone growth, muscle mass, organ size, metabolism', 'GH and IGF-1 are anabolic hormones with widespread effects.'),
('91c742b5-7e4f-5db4-ab42-516c92114c3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Ghrelin stimulates GH release via:', '["IGF-1 mediated physiological mechanism","Direct GH action mediated physiological mechanism","Somatostatin inhibition mediated physiological mechanism","GHS-R (growth hormone secretagogue receptor) on the pituitary and hypothalamus","GHRH receptor mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'GHS-R (growth hormone secretagogue receptor) on the pituitary and hypothalamus', 'Ghrelin is an endogenous ligand for the GHS-R and acts as a potent GH secretagogue.'),
('97176c95-7f47-5157-9307-c043e89032f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The thyroid gland produces:', '["Only T3 mediated physiological mechanism","TRH mediated physiological mechanism","Only T4 mediated physiological mechanism","TSH mediated physiological mechanism","T3 (triiodothyronine), T4 (thyroxine), and calcitonin"]'::jsonb, 'T3 (triiodothyronine), T4 (thyroxine), and calcitonin', 'The thyroid follicle produces T3/T4; parafollicular C cells produce calcitonin.'),
('59ba9760-df0a-5f80-8057-a7111412e28b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Thyroglobulin is synthesized by:', '["Pituitary thyrotrophs","Thyroid follicular cells","Liver","Parafollicular cells","Hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid follicular cells', 'Thyroglobulin is a large glycoprotein produced in the follicular cells that serves as the matrix for thyroid hormone synthesis.'),
('b14dc8fc-afcc-5810-8570-7befc659369f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Iodide transport into the thyroid follicular cell is mediated by:', '["Anion exchanger","Glucose transporter","Na+/K+ ATPase","NIS (Na+/I- symporter) on the basolateral membrane","Pendrin on the apical membrane"]'::jsonb, 'NIS (Na+/I- symporter) on the basolateral membrane', 'NIS uses the Na+ gradient to co-transport I- against its gradient into follicular cells.'),
('c2b6c95b-990d-5d00-a4fa-9ddbdc2fd3ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The rate-limiting step in thyroid hormone synthesis is:', '["Proteolysis","Iodide trapping","Peroxidase organification","Coupling reaction","Thyroglobulin synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Iodide trapping', 'Iodide uptake via NIS is the first and rate-limiting step for thyroid hormone synthesis.'),
('23127bcd-27aa-5817-bd0f-375d492565a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) catalyzes:', '["Iodination of thyroglobulin and coupling of iodotyrosines","Iodide transport mediated physiological mechanism","Deiodination mediated physiological mechanism","Thyroglobulin synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Proteolysis of thyroglobulin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Iodination of thyroglobulin and coupling of iodotyrosines', 'TPO oxidizes I- and attaches it to tyrosyl residues in thyroglobulin (organification), then couples MIT and DIT.'),
('a2a4876f-cf57-50f4-a56d-9ca5fcb65f59'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Monoidotyrosine (MIT) and diiodotyrosine (DIT) are:', '["Final hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Inactive metabolites mediated physiological mechanism","Intermediate forms in thyroid hormone synthesis","Breakdown products mediated physiological mechanism","Transport proteins mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Intermediate forms in thyroid hormone synthesis', 'MIT (one iodine) and DIT (two iodines) are coupled to form T3 (MIT+DIT) or T4 (DIT+DIT).'),
('18a2a2be-baa0-5f73-8334-7f73b689b79a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'T4 (thyroxine) contains:', '["3 iodine atoms","1 iodine atom","2 iodine atoms","5 iodine atoms","4 iodine atoms"]'::jsonb, '4 iodine atoms', 'T4 (tetraiodothyronine, thyroxine) has 4 iodines; T3 has 3.'),
('8e39643e-6717-5684-b38d-7efff4a25c04'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'T3 is more potent than T4 because:', '["It has more iodine mediated physiological mechanism","It is water-soluble mediated physiological mechanism","It is larger mediated physiological mechanism","It is more abundant mediated physiological mechanism","It binds with higher affinity to nuclear receptors"]'::jsonb, 'It binds with higher affinity to nuclear receptors', 'T3 binds ~10-15x more tightly to thyroid hormone receptors than T4.'),
('7d1d33fc-e247-510f-b875-b9fec128d04e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Most circulating T3 and T4 are bound to:', '["Transthyretin","Albumin","Lipoproteins","Free form","Thyroid-binding globulin"]'::jsonb, 'Thyroid-binding globulin', 'TBG (~70%), transthyretin (~10%), and albumin (~15%) carry thyroid hormones in blood.'),
('8f69c299-d648-5884-ad69-6e896e667016'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The free (unbound) fraction of T4 is approximately:', '["3%","0.3%","30%","10%","0.03%"]'::jsonb, '0.03%', 'Only ~0.03% of T4 is free; ~0.3% of T3 is free. Free hormones are biologically active.'),
('d4689b8d-d5ea-5e9c-9f59-4068c8c1824e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The major site of deiodination of T4 to T3 is:', '["Brain mediated physiological mechanism","Thyroid gland mediated physiological mechanism","Liver, kidney, and peripheral tissues","Pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Heart mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Liver, kidney, and peripheral tissues', 'Type 1 and type 2 deiodinases in peripheral tissues convert T4 to the more active T3.'),
('b2c51c25-accb-577c-84cd-cabee47e08bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Type 1 deiodinase (D1) produces:', '["T2 from T3","MIT from DIT","Reverse T3","rT3 from T4","T3 from T4"]'::jsonb, 'T3 from T4', 'D1 in the liver, kidney, and thyroid contributes to circulating T3.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('20411ec4-6974-5cf3-a56e-7a3ff0e46a86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Type 2 deiodinase (D2) produces:', '["rT3 from T4","MIT","DIT","T2","T3 from T4"]'::jsonb, 'T3 from T4', 'D2 is important in the brain, pituitary, brown fat, and placenta.'),
('d592c033-0082-5e06-b6b4-f5788eb59e34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Type 3 deiodinase (D3) converts T4 to:', '["MIT","T3","T2","Reverse T3","DIT"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse T3', 'D3 inactivates T4 to rT3 and T3 to T2, regulating local thyroid hormone availability.'),
('d041f9c7-e7e5-5036-a004-85ad446d77fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Thyroid hormones act via:', '["Nuclear thyroid hormone receptors","Ion channels","Cytoplasmic receptors","cGMP","Membrane GPCR"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear thyroid hormone receptors', 'T3 binds to nuclear receptors acting as transcription factors, regulating gene expression.'),
('82bdc425-72e4-5b7e-83e5-8ec3aed5007e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Physiological effects of thyroid hormones include all EXCEPT:', '["Increased protein synthesis","Increased heart rate and contractility","Decreased basal metabolic rate","Enhanced lipolysis","Increased oxygen consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased basal metabolic rate', 'Thyroid hormones increase BMR, heart rate, contractility, and metabolism.'),
('7e0a0a13-b7ff-55df-9b71-7edac7517b2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hyperthyroidism (thyrotoxicosis) features:', '["Hypotension, bradycardia","Tachycardia, weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor, exophthalmos","Bradycardia, weight gain, cold intolerance","Dry skin, hair loss","Constipation, lethargy"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia, weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor, exophthalmos', 'Hyperthyroidism has increased metabolic rate, sympathetic overactivity, and ocular signs.'),
('9ba0ea32-6e9a-5afb-8e03-90c4e7ad8aa1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypothyroidism features:', '["Fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, bradycardia","Heat intolerance mediated physiological mechanism","Diarrhea mediated physiological mechanism","Tachycardia, weight loss mediated physiological mechanism","Tremor mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, bradycardia', 'Hypothyroidism decreases metabolism, causing lethargy, weight gain, constipation, and bradycardia.'),
('18b70ed5-8f5d-508e-8e3a-60ddb5a59c65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Congenital hypothyroidism (cretinism) causes:', '["Precocious puberty mediated physiological mechanism","Tall stature mediated physiological mechanism","Advanced bone age mediated physiological mechanism","Severe mental retardation and growth failure","Hyperactivity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Severe mental retardation and growth failure', 'Lack of thyroid hormone during development impairs brain growth and skeletal maturation.'),
('f075cf88-4315-5642-83c5-c5d5b21e479e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Goiter refers to:', '["Thyroid atrophy mediated physiological mechanism","Thyroid cancer mediated physiological mechanism","Thyroid nodule mediated physiological mechanism","Enlargement of the thyroid gland","Thyroiditis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of the thyroid gland', 'Goiter can result from iodine deficiency, autoimmune disease, or nodules.'),
('453ba4b0-9c0a-5659-ab8d-9e6495bb958d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Endemic goiter is caused by:', '["Autoimmunity","Thyroiditis","Iodine deficiency","Excess iodine","TSH deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Iodine deficiency', 'Lack of dietary iodine impairs thyroid hormone synthesis, causing compensatory TSH-driven goiter.'),
('fef3e50b-2d57-5984-9fad-014a8da7c49b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Graves disease is an autoimmune cause of:', '["Goiter without hyperthyroidism","Hypothyroidism","Thyroid cancer","Hyperthyroidism","Thyroiditis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperthyroidism', 'Graves: autoantibodies (TSI) bind the TSH receptor, stimulating excess thyroid hormone.'),
('c3166fbc-3453-5704-ad42-151a5f230455'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hashimoto thyroiditis is an autoimmune cause of:', '["Hyperthyroidism","Hypothyroidism","Goiter","Graves disease","Thyroid cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Hypothyroidism', 'Hashimoto: autoimmune destruction of the thyroid with lymphocytic infiltration and fibrosis.'),
('fb29978a-6e05-56d4-9f87-7366ae0a61f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis is caused by:', '["Iodine excess","Trauma","Bacterial infection","Autoimmunity","Viral infection"]'::jsonb, 'Viral infection', 'Subacute thyroiditis: viral inflammation causes follicular destruction with release of preformed hormones.'),
('df3df8df-6058-527a-b28d-448cb2a78015'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Silent (painless) thyroiditis is:', '["Drug-induced mediated physiological mechanism","Viral infection mediated physiological mechanism","Autoimmune thyroiditis with transient thyrotoxicosis","Iodine-induced mediated physiological mechanism","Bacterial infection mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune thyroiditis with transient thyrotoxicosis', 'Silent thyroiditis resembles Hashimoto but has a triphasic course (thyrotoxic, hypothyroid, recovery).'),
('8dabcb8b-abe2-58cc-a95b-2c364463113f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Amiodarone can cause thyroid dysfunction because:', '["It contains iodine","It blocks TSH","It destroys the thyroid","It increases TBG","It inhibits TPO"]'::jsonb, 'It contains iodine', 'Amiodarone has high iodine content, leading to type 1 (hyper) or type 2 (destructive) thyroiditis.'),
('17c4d760-feb5-506e-bab2-dab3b363dbd7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Thyroid function tests include:', '["Only T3 mediated physiological mechanism","Only TBG mediated physiological mechanism","Only T4 mediated physiological mechanism","Only TSH mediated physiological mechanism","TSH (most sensitive), free T4, total T4, free T3"]'::jsonb, 'TSH (most sensitive), free T4, total T4, free T3', 'TSH is the first-line screening test; fT4 and fT3 confirm the diagnosis.'),
('5de0ef2b-29f4-5620-9817-7d7ec20a396f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'In primary hypothyroidism:', '["TSH is high, free T4 is low","TSH is low, free T4 is low","TSH is high, free T4 is high","TSH is normal","TSH is low, free T4 is high"]'::jsonb, 'TSH is high, free T4 is low', 'Primary hypothyroidism: the thyroid fails, so TSH rises from lack of negative feedback.'),
('e9122346-86f7-5fb3-a0fc-907e13faa98b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'In primary hyperthyroidism:', '["TSH low, T4 low mediated physiological mechanism","TSH is high, T4 is low mediated physiological mechanism","TSH is high mediated physiological mechanism","TSH is normal mediated physiological mechanism","TSH is low (or undetectable), free T4 and T3 are high"]'::jsonb, 'TSH is low (or undetectable), free T4 and T3 are high', 'Elevated thyroid hormones suppress TSH secretion via negative feedback.'),
('b6cc67ba-011c-51fc-8a20-40303b80c753'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Secondary hypothyroidism (pituitary) shows:', '["Low TSH, low free T4","Low TSH, high T4","Normal TSH, low T4","High TSH, low T4","High TSH, high T4"]'::jsonb, 'Low TSH, low free T4', 'In secondary hypothyroidism, the pituitary cannot secrete enough TSH.'),
('e73b144d-dd46-5902-8058-3db854bd33d6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Euthyroid sick syndrome (non-thyroidal illness) shows:', '["High TSH mediated physiological mechanism","High T4 mediated physiological mechanism","Low T3 (and often low T4) with normal TSH in severely ill patients","Normal T3 mediated physiological mechanism","High T3 mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Low T3 (and often low T4) with normal TSH in severely ill patients', 'In severe illness, peripheral T4 to T3 conversion decreases (low T3 syndrome).'),
('3bcd5a3f-cd37-5105-880b-245706421e43'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Treatment for hypothyroidism is:', '["Radioiodine","Antithyroid drugs","T3 replacement","Surgery","Levothyroxine (T4) replacement"]'::jsonb, 'Levothyroxine (T4) replacement', 'Levo-thyroxine is the standard; T3 is generated from T4 peripherally.'),
('111617e3-4f9c-5bc6-8e73-2611d1444897'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Treatment for hyperthyroidism includes:', '["Levothyroxine mediated physiological mechanism","Beta-blockers only mediated physiological mechanism","Steroids mediated physiological mechanism","Iodine supplementation mediated physiological mechanism","Antithyroid drugs (methimazole, PTU), radioiodine, or thyroidectomy"]'::jsonb, 'Antithyroid drugs (methimazole, PTU), radioiodine, or thyroidectomy', 'Methimazole and PTU inhibit TPO; radioiodine destroys thyroid tissue.'),
('32d1fc8c-392c-51f6-8920-d9d8cc375327'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Propylthiouracil (PTU) has the additional effect of:', '["Blocking TSH receptor mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibiting peripheral T4 to T3 conversion","Stimulating TSH mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking NIS mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing TBG mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting peripheral T4 to T3 conversion', 'PTU, unlike methimazole, inhibits type 1 deiodinase, rapidly lowering T3 levels.'),
('cabdc595-4be4-5dcf-9fc0-74fd5b332cd3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Radioactive iodine (I-131) treatment:', '["Is excreted unchanged mediated physiological mechanism","Is taken up by the pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulates the thyroid mediated physiological mechanism","Blocks TSH mediated physiological mechanism","Is taken up by the thyroid and destroys follicular cells"]'::jsonb, 'Is taken up by the thyroid and destroys follicular cells', 'Radioiodine is used for Graves disease and toxic nodules.'),
('63d22895-42bc-5d0e-ac4c-702571dfd8e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Thyroid storm is:', '["Life-threatening hyperthyroidism","Severe goiter","Thyroiditis","Thyroid cancer","Hypothyroid crisis"]'::jsonb, 'Life-threatening hyperthyroidism', 'Thyroid storm is triggered by stress/infection in uncontrolled hyperthyroidism.'),
('21b015a3-7aac-55cc-8f4e-41cc43d2e7cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Myxedema coma is:', '["Goiter","Thyroid cancer","Hyperthyroid crisis","Coma from hyperthyroidism","Life-threatening hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Life-threatening hypothyroidism', 'Myxedema coma requires aggressive IV levothyroxine and supportive care.'),
('249dd7b6-a0c7-567e-8460-8002f563bc7a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Thyroid hormone resistance syndrome is due to:', '["Mutation in thyroid hormone receptor beta","TSH receptor mutation mediated physiological mechanism","TPO deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Deiodinase defect mediated physiological mechanism","TBG deficiency mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Mutation in thyroid hormone receptor beta', 'Resistance to thyroid hormone causes high T4/T3 with inappropriately normal/high TSH.'),
('4b1f5ab0-caed-5129-806a-874063402da8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'TBG (thyroid-binding globulin) is increased by:', '["Glucocorticoids mediated physiological mechanism","Low estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Androgens mediated physiological mechanism","Testosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen (pregnancy, OCPs), hepatitis, genetics"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen (pregnancy, OCPs), hepatitis, genetics', 'High estrogen increases TBG synthesis, raising total T4 but free T4 remains normal.'),
('bbc62006-d26c-50d7-ad47-f104271e2611'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'TBG is decreased by:', '["Pregnancy mediated physiological mechanism","OCPs mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Hepatitis mediated physiological mechanism","Androgens, glucocorticoids, severe illness, protein loss"]'::jsonb, 'Androgens, glucocorticoids, severe illness, protein loss', 'Low TBG reduces total T4/T3; free hormones remain normal in euthyroid patients.'),
('ee728c20-ec65-5355-8134-4e9706120918'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Wolf-Chaikoff effect refers to:', '["Iodine deficiency goiter mediated physiological mechanism","Acute inhibition of thyroid organification by excess iodide","Deiodinase activation mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulation of thyroid by TSH mediated physiological mechanism","Autoimmune thyroiditis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Acute inhibition of thyroid organification by excess iodide', 'High iodide transiently inhibits TPO (protective mechanism). The thyroid usually escapes.'),
('6f648631-acc9-519d-8d55-379d63f16666'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'PTH (parathyroid hormone) is secreted by:', '["Parafollicular cells","A cells","C cells","Thyroid follicular cells","Chief cells of the parathyroid glands"]'::jsonb, 'Chief cells of the parathyroid glands', 'The four parathyroid glands contain chief cells that synthesize and secrete PTH.'),
('591ebbfa-6efd-5b0a-9935-1c479288b8f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The major regulator of PTH secretion is:', '["Calcitonin","Magnesium","Vitamin D","Plasma ionized calcium","Plasma phosphate"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma ionized calcium', 'Low Ca2+ stimulates PTH release via the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR).'),
('569a5e62-2c3f-59fe-b2aa-18c49f9c7143'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'PTH acts on bone to:', '["Decrease osteoclast activity","Decrease Ca2+ release","Increase bone formation","Increase bone resorption","Stimulate osteoblasts only"]'::jsonb, 'Increase bone resorption', 'PTH stimulates osteoclasts indirectly via osteoblasts (RANKL) to resorb bone.'),
('48fa12fa-2ebf-55a1-b1ce-a06f3f45d166'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'PTH acts on the kidney to:', '["Increase PO4- reabsorption mediated physiological mechanism","Increase Ca2+ reabsorption and decrease PO4- reabsorption","Decrease calcitriol production mediated physiological mechanism","Decrease Ca2+ reabsorption mediated physiological mechanism","Increase Na+ reabsorption mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increase Ca2+ reabsorption and decrease PO4- reabsorption', 'PTH enhances distal Ca2+ reabsorption and inhibits proximal PO4- reabsorption.'),
('39b9aced-fc76-5112-a9cd-dc922ea8ce6e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'PTH increases calcitriol production by:', '["Activating liver 25-hydroxylase","Inhibiting it","Increasing dietary absorption","Directly synthesizing vitamin D","Stimulating renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase', 'PTH upregulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the kidney, increasing calcitriol synthesis.'),
('e7171554-0a9c-518b-b746-21c78a1d38ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Vitamin D (calcitriol) is:', '["25-hydroxycholecalciferol","Calciferol","7-dehydrocholesterol","1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","Cholecalciferol"]'::jsonb, '1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol', 'Active vitamin D is 1,25-(OH)2-D3 (calcitriol) produced by renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase.'),
('8b21c948-447b-54d0-9ee9-0ac6a9d2ee45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Vitamin D is activated by:', '["Parathyroid gland","Two hydroxylations: liver (25-OH) and kidney","Skin synthesis only","Bone","Direct dietary absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Two hydroxylations: liver (25-OH) and kidney', 'Cholecalciferol from skin/diet is hydroxylated to 25-OH-D in the liver, then to 1,25-(OH)2-D in the kidney.'),
('546ea1ed-a2e8-5299-95dc-97b1e08ae1d9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The main function of calcitriol is:', '["Decrease Ca2+ absorption","Decrease Ca2+ reabsorption","Increase intestinal Ca2+ and PO4- absorption","Decrease PO4- absorption","Increase bone resorption"]'::jsonb, 'Increase intestinal Ca2+ and PO4- absorption', 'Calcitriol increases expression of TRPV6 and calbindin in enterocytes for Ca2+ absorption.'),
('53283823-1aed-52de-b657-68f3861a9581'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Vitamin D deficiency in children causes:', '["Osteomalacia","Hypercalcemia","Osteoporosis","Osteitis fibrosa cystica","Rickets"]'::jsonb, 'Rickets', 'Rickets: defective bone mineralization with growth plate changes in children.'),
('73baa39d-f5be-529a-b601-647d75a6dd80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, Vitamin D deficiency in adults causes:', '["Rickets","Osteomalacia","Osteoporosis","Osteopetrosis","Hypercalcemia"]'::jsonb, 'Osteomalacia', 'Osteomalacia: defective mineralization of osteoid in adults.'),
('a539526b-a7f2-53fe-b9d2-8a573ddbf3df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Calcitonin is secreted by:', '["Parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid","Parathyroid chief cells","Adrenal","Thyroid follicular cells","Pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid', 'Calcitonin is produced by C cells in response to hypercalcemia.'),
('12750ce4-73dd-5776-950c-ab28fa04f49e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Calcitonin acts to:', '["Lower blood Ca2+ by inhibiting bone resorption","Raise blood Ca2+","Increase vitamin D production","Increase Ca2+ absorption","Increase PTH"]'::jsonb, 'Lower blood Ca2+ by inhibiting bone resorption', 'Calcitonin inhibits osteoclast activity, decreasing bone resorption and lowering Ca2+.'),
('24ff9620-fcdd-5a10-a3ff-a3816636b01f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Primary hyperparathyroidism causes:', '["Hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatemia","Hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, increased PTH","Hypercalcemia, increased calcitonin","Normal Ca2+, high PO4-","Hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia, increased PTH', 'PTH excess increases Ca2+ reabsorption and PO4- excretion, causing high Ca2+ and low PO4-.'),
('95c9faa3-04d5-5a54-859e-0e1072500e55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Secondary hyperparathyroidism is commonly due to:', '["Low calcium intake","Thyroid disease","Chronic kidney disease","Parathyroid adenoma","Parathyroid hyperplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic kidney disease', 'CKD causes hyperphosphatemia and low calcitriol, stimulating PTH secretion.'),
('09fec78c-ddf3-5f4b-a859-57115a5fd439'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypoparathyroidism causes:', '["Hypocalcemia, hypophosphatemia","Hypercalcemia, hypophosphatemia","Normal Ca2+","Hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, low PTH","Hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hypocalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, low PTH', 'PTH deficiency reduces Ca2+ and increases PO4- levels due to loss of PTH actions.'),
('0650d7b1-66f8-5c77-b675-5bdff50757bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Pseudohypoparathyroidism is:', '["End-organ resistance to PTH","PTH antibody production","Low PTH","Secondary hyperparathyroidism","Parathyroid agenesis"]'::jsonb, 'End-organ resistance to PTH', 'Pseudohypoparathyroidism: target cells cannot respond to PTH due to defective Gs-alpha (GNAS mutation).'),
('79b3c428-2439-5e87-a58a-0692df9ddfd2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) is expressed on:', '["Only bone mediated physiological mechanism","Only thyroid mediated physiological mechanism","Only pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Parathyroid chief cells and renal tubules","Only intestine mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroid chief cells and renal tubules', 'CaSR detects plasma Ca2+ and regulates PTH secretion and renal Ca2+ excretion.'),
('cf19a28b-82e6-58e1-aa80-9645d64b81ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypercalcemia causes:', '["Hypotension mediated physiological mechanism","Tetany, muscle cramps mediated physiological mechanism","Edema mediated physiological mechanism","Bradycardia mediated physiological mechanism","Bones, stones, groans, psychiatric overtones"]'::jsonb, 'Bones, stones, groans, psychiatric overtones', 'Hypercalcemia has multi-system effects including renal, GI, and neurological.'),
('ec700bbc-2621-534a-9991-52d964dc26db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypocalcemia causes:', '["Tachycardia mediated physiological mechanism","Tetany (neuromuscular irritability), Chvostek sign, Trousseau sign, QT prolongation","Hyperreflexia mediated physiological mechanism","Hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","Constipation, renal stones mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Tetany (neuromuscular irritability), Chvostek sign, Trousseau sign, QT prolongation', 'Low Ca2+ increases neuronal excitability due to reduced membrane stabilization.'),
('8c9ebd09-5ad8-5cb3-8db6-e37893117797'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Magnesium is important for:', '["Calcium absorption","Vitamin D synthesis","Calcitonin release","Bone formation","PTH secretion and function"]'::jsonb, 'PTH secretion and function', 'Severe hypomagnesemia impairs PTH secretion and causes functional hypoparathyroidism.'),
('2525af89-572a-559c-bc80-73626aff6880'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Osteitis fibrosa cystica is associated with:', '["Severe hyperparathyroidism","Hypoparathyroidism","Paget disease","Osteoporosis","Vitamin D deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Severe hyperparathyroidism', 'Excess PTH causes high bone turnover with fibrous replacement and cysts.'),
('f5e8d599-2821-5114-a224-4148fb6ea4ec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Renal osteodystrophy in CKD involves:', '["High PTH, low calcitriol, hyperphosphatemia, bone disease","Paget disease mediated physiological mechanism","Osteoporosis mediated physiological mechanism","Low PTH, high calcitriol mediated physiological mechanism","Osteomalacia only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'High PTH, low calcitriol, hyperphosphatemia, bone disease', 'Renal failure leads to mineral and bone disorder (CKD-MBD) with mixed bone lesions.'),
('7cb57e6a-625d-5658-aa1a-fb1987c9a444'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hungry bone syndrome occurs after:', '["Pancreatectomy mediated physiological mechanism","Thyroidectomy mediated physiological mechanism","Adrenalectomy mediated physiological mechanism","Hypophysectomy mediated physiological mechanism","Parathyroidectomy for severe hyperparathyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Parathyroidectomy for severe hyperparathyroidism', 'After PTH removal, bone rapidly takes up Ca2+, causing severe postoperative hypocalcemia.'),
('ce66f1f2-cd49-574d-a7c3-1553dac1a77d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH) is due to:', '["Vitamin D excess","PTH adenoma","Inactivating CaSR mutation","Hyperthyroidism","Adrenal insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Inactivating CaSR mutation', 'FHH is benign; the CaSR mutation reduces renal Ca2+ excretion and PTH suppression.'),
('4775e4c0-641e-55c1-a81a-9ca7e642a319'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Calcium homeostasis involves:', '["Only PTH","Only vitamin D","Only calcitonin","PTH, calcitriol, and calcitonin","PTH and calcitonin only"]'::jsonb, 'PTH, calcitriol, and calcitonin', 'These three hormones regulate Ca2+ balance at bone, kidney, and intestine.'),
('8b671ac5-3568-5c7c-aa8e-7dbe5bab71d4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Ionized calcium is the:', '["Complexed form","Total calcium","Protein-bound form","Biologically active form","Inactive form"]'::jsonb, 'Biologically active form', 'Ionized Ca2+ is the physiologically important fraction, regulated by PTH and vitamin D.'),
('d3a4939e-15ab-5a47-bb00-827918cf8880'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypercalcemia of malignancy is most often due to:', '["Bone metastases","Ectopic PTH","Calcitonin deficiency","Vitamin D excess","PTHrP secretion by tumors"]'::jsonb, 'PTHrP secretion by tumors', 'PTHrP mimics PTH and causes hypercalcemia, most commonly in squamous cell carcinoma.'),
('084a0b83-47c2-5802-9ccd-37e1aee3cfd2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Phosphate homeostasis is primarily regulated by:', '["Vitamin D alone","Bone deposition","Intestinal absorption","Renal excretion","Calcitonin"]'::jsonb, 'Renal excretion', 'The kidney is the main regulator of phosphate balance via NaPi-2a/2c transporters.'),
('657893ca-6e32-5604-90dd-5a61da858c58'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'FGF23 is produced by:', '["Intestine","Osteocytes","Thyroid","Parathyroid","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Osteocytes', 'FGF23 decreases renal phosphate reabsorption and suppresses 1-alpha-hydroxylase.'),
('579f11a1-232f-597e-b7e5-d8a87a1a8463'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Klotho is a:', '["Calcium channel","PTH receptor","Phosphate transporter","Vitamin D receptor","Co physiological pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Co physiological pathway', 'Klotho converts FGFR into a high-affinity receptor for FGF23.'),
('da9ff34e-fb5a-5b5d-8c21-5b4e8841b4d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Beta cells secrete:', '["Glucagon","Somatostatin","Ghrelin","Insulin","Pancreatic polypeptide"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin', 'Beta cells (~60-70% of islet cells) secrete insulin.'),
('2ff5ea60-eb70-5964-81a2-e69c2a9a605a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Alpha cells of the pancreas secrete:', '["Ghrelin","Glucagon","Insulin","Somatostatin","PP"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon', 'Alpha cells (~20% of islet cells) secrete glucagon.'),
('07374d5b-cf4f-5869-9e1f-bfd1a44330a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Delta cells of the pancreas secrete:', '["Insulin","Ghrelin","PP","Glucagon","Somatostatin"]'::jsonb, 'Somatostatin', 'Delta cells (~5-10%) secrete somatostatin, which inhibits insulin, glucagon, and GH.'),
('6fd6670f-f02e-5b7a-99f7-bc91287f6982'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'PP cells (F cells) of the pancreas secrete:', '["Ghrelin","Pancreatic polypeptide","Glucagon","Somatostatin","Insulin"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic polypeptide', 'PP cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide, which regulates digestive functions.'),
('c7746477-5f91-5d1a-8ddd-8bf8315acd8c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin is synthesized as:', '["Preproinsulin -> proinsulin -> insulin + C-peptide","Directly as insulin","Insulin + glucagon","Proinsulin -> insulin only","Insulin chain A and B separately"]'::jsonb, 'Preproinsulin -> proinsulin -> insulin + C-peptide', 'Insulin is made as preproinsulin, cleaved to proinsulin, then to insulin and C-peptide.'),
('819639f9-faa4-523b-ba08-69cace96dcdc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The main stimulus for insulin secretion is:', '["Decreased blood glucose","Increased blood glucose","Fatty acids","GIP","Amino acids"]'::jsonb, 'Increased blood glucose', 'Glucose enters beta cells via GLUT2, is metabolized, increasing ATP/ADP ratio, closing K-ATP channels, and triggering insulin exocytosis.'),
('735ae587-3a44-5321-a3af-246d61024d71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin secretion is inhibited by:', '["High blood glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Acetylcholine mediated physiological mechanism","Low blood glucose, somatostatin, epinephrine","GLP-1 mediated physiological mechanism","Amino acids mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Low blood glucose, somatostatin, epinephrine', 'Hypoglycemia, somatostatin (delta cells), and sympathetic activation (epinephrine) inhibit insulin.'),
('6e247f17-0f01-5c30-87df-a88badff8b34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin acts via:', '["Ion channel","Tyrosine kinase receptor","JAK-STAT","Nuclear receptor","GPCR"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine kinase receptor', 'The insulin receptor is a heterotetrameric tyrosine kinase that autophosphorylates and activates IRS proteins.'),
('6bf1411a-fc58-5762-852f-3e4643451090'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin promotes:', '["Glycogenolysis","Ketogenesis","Glucose uptake","Gluconeogenesis","Lipolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose uptake', 'Insulin stimulates GLUT4 translocation to the membrane in muscle and adipose tissue for glucose uptake.'),
('7223b204-4cb7-565e-afbd-3b3e7a8b513e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin effects on the liver include:', '["Increased ketogenesis mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased lipogenesis mediated physiological mechanism","Increased gluconeogenesis mediated physiological mechanism","Increased glycogen synthesis and decreased gluconeogenesis","Increased glycogenolysis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased glycogen synthesis and decreased gluconeogenesis', 'Insulin promotes hepatic glucose storage (glycogen) and suppresses glucose production.'),
('99a23e86-ef26-5d15-ad13-eab855c35b2f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin effects on adipose tissue include:', '["Decreased glucose uptake","Decreased triglyceride storage","Increased fatty acid release","Increased lipolysis","Increased lipogenesis, decreased lipolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased lipogenesis, decreased lipolysis', 'Insulin promotes fat storage by increasing glucose uptake, lipogenesis, and inhibiting lipolysis.'),
('a1317896-26be-5a2d-8a67-3d68a4d31d0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin effects on muscle include:', '["Decreased amino acid transport","Increased glucose uptake and protein synthesis","Decreased glucose uptake","Increased glycogenolysis","Increased proteolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Increased glucose uptake and protein synthesis', 'Insulin stimulates amino acid uptake and protein synthesis in muscle.'),
('dd116657-cbf9-5db9-af42-0e12a803f3ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Type 1 diabetes mellitus is caused by:', '["Pancreatitis","Obesity","Genetic beta cell dysfunction","Insulin resistance","Autoimmune destruction of beta cells"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune destruction of beta cells', 'Type 1 DM: autoimmune attack on beta cells leads to complete insulin deficiency.'),
('c8abec85-28a8-5609-a7db-588d1463654b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Type 2 diabetes mellitus is characterized by:', '["Autoimmune beta cell destruction","Absolute insulin deficiency","Glucagon deficiency","Insulin resistance and relative insulin deficiency","Pancreatic alpha cell hyperplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin resistance and relative insulin deficiency', 'Type 2 DM: target tissues are resistant to insulin, and beta cells cannot compensate.'),
('56927400-4ab4-5c89-b8ee-219bb3137496'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is more common in:', '["Type 1 diabetes","Both types equally","Pre-diabetes","Gestational diabetes","Type 2 diabetes"]'::jsonb, 'Type 1 diabetes', 'DKA results from severe insulin deficiency, leading to lipolysis, ketogenesis, and metabolic acidosis.'),
('54e84da9-222f-50b7-98f6-381a920a4e2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) is more common in:', '["Normal weight","Children","Gestational diabetes","Type 1 diabetes","Type 2 diabetes"]'::jsonb, 'Type 2 diabetes', 'HHS features extreme hyperglycemia (>600 mg/dL), hyperosmolality, and dehydration without significant ketosis.'),
('9f24ec8a-3eef-5deb-b2a7-a929d19870b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Glucagon is secreted in response to:', '["Exercise","Food intake","Low blood glucose","High blood glucose","Insulin"]'::jsonb, 'Low blood glucose', 'Hypoglycemia stimulates alpha cell glucagon secretion via direct and paracrine (low insulin) mechanisms.'),
('5af78620-dbb9-50f7-9bd7-870a08683ae2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Glucagon acts primarily on the:', '["Brain","Adipose tissue","Liver","Muscle","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Glucagon binds to hepatic GPCRs, activating cAMP to increase glucose production.'),
('9095e87e-a796-535a-bd8a-0969f63739a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Glucagon effects include:', '["Decreased ketogenesis","Decreased blood glucose","Increased glycogen synthesis","Increased lipogenesis","Increased blood glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Increased blood glucose', 'Glucagon raises blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.'),
('552b0cae-b6c9-53ea-a6db-5fc6547a68e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Somatostatin (from delta cells) inhibits:', '["Only insulin","Insulin, glucagon, and GH","Only GH","Only glucagon","Only digestive enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin, glucagon, and GH', 'Somatostatin is a paracrine inhibitor of both insulin and glucagon, and endocrine inhibitor of GH.'),
('01002d63-bdd4-5d83-9ca5-bbf0e9e7c85d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Incretins (GLP-1, GIP) are:', '["Intestinal hormones that enhance insulin secretion after meals","Pancreatic hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Pituitary hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Adrenal hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Thyroid hormones mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal hormones that enhance insulin secretion after meals', 'GLP-1 and GIP are incretins that potentiate glucose-stimulated insulin secretion.'),
('b39ae870-f696-5aae-932d-d7276f29f712'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) is secreted by:', '["Liver","Pancreatic alpha cells","Intestinal L cells","Stomach","Pancreatic beta cells"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal L cells', 'GLP-1 is an incretin with multiple antidiabetic effects and is degraded by DPP-4.'),
('30d389c6-b65c-5be8-bd15-512c6b783215'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'DPP-4 inhibitors (gliptins) work by:', '["Increasing glucose uptake","Decreasing insulin resistance","Blocking glucagon","Stimulating insulin secretion directly","Increasing endogenous GLP-1 and GIP levels"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing endogenous GLP-1 and GIP levels', 'DPP-4 degrades incretins; its inhibition raises active GLP-1/GIP levels.'),
('8b425d0f-3145-5d18-96c1-f6d2a2cfb707'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins) work by:', '["Stimulating insulin secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking renal glucose reabsorption, causing glucosuria","Increasing insulin sensitivity mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibiting DPP-4 mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking glucagon mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking renal glucose reabsorption, causing glucosuria', 'SGLT2 in the proximal tubule reabsorbs glucose; its inhibition excretes glucose in urine.'),
('25107f40-1867-51cf-abf0-72c43f489af8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Metformin works primarily by:', '["Increasing insulin secretion","Inhibiting DPP-4","Stimulating insulin secretion","Decreasing hepatic gluconeogenesis","Blocking glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Decreasing hepatic gluconeogenesis', 'Metformin activates AMPK, reducing hepatic glucose output and improving insulin sensitivity.'),
('34e224e8-365e-525a-8cd0-41cde0fed30f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Sulfonylureas work by:', '["Increasing insulin sensitivity mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking glucagon mediated physiological mechanism","Decreasing glucose absorption mediated physiological mechanism","Closing K-ATP channels on beta cells to stimulate insulin secretion","Increasing GLP-1 mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Closing K-ATP channels on beta cells to stimulate insulin secretion', 'Sulfonylureas bind SUR1 on K-ATP channels, causing depolarization and insulin release.'),
('ae73b5ab-25da-55c8-ba37-9c1422b869e1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin resistance is characterized by:', '["Increased glucose uptake mediated physiological mechanism","Increased glucagon mediated physiological mechanism","Impaired glucose uptake in muscle/adipose and increased hepatic glucose output","Decreased hepatic glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Increased insulin secretion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired glucose uptake in muscle/adipose and increased hepatic glucose output', 'Insulin resistance is a key feature of type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome.'),
('328f535c-d8e2-5433-96b0-5e8c19768863'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'C-peptide is used to:', '["Diagnose hypoglycemia","Detect insulin antibodies","Measure insulin activity","Assess endogenous insulin secretion","Monitor glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Assess endogenous insulin secretion', 'C-peptide is co-secreted with insulin but is not degraded by the liver; it reflects beta cell function.'),
('f060ef1a-de88-57b7-b54a-926cdc767da3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'HbA1c reflects:', '["Fasting glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Insulin level mediated physiological mechanism","Postprandial glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Random glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Average blood glucose over the past 2-3 months"]'::jsonb, 'Average blood glucose over the past 2-3 months', 'HbA1c measures glycated hemoglobin; it is used for diabetes diagnosis and monitoring.'),
('b3c989a4-b60a-56f7-8828-2cd9184d14aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is:', '["Preexisting diabetes in pregnancy","Transient diabetes","Diabetes after delivery","Glucose intolerance first recognized during pregnancy","Type 1 diabetes in pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose intolerance first recognized during pregnancy', 'GDM involves placental hormones causing insulin resistance; it resolves after delivery.'),
('9ebbe468-6585-51f4-9fb7-65109c204820'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Counter-regulatory hormones that oppose insulin include:', '["Only GH mediated physiological mechanism","Glucagon, cortisol, GH, epinephrine","Only epinephrine mediated physiological mechanism","Only glucagon mediated physiological mechanism","Only cortisol mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon, cortisol, GH, epinephrine', 'Counter-regulatory hormones increase blood glucose by opposing insulin action.'),
('b5f934bc-ed7c-5dba-8d71-46a8df6ed295'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The fasting glucose threshold for diabetes diagnosis is:', '["≥110 mg/dL","≥200 mg/dL","≥126 mg/dL","≥140 mg/dL","≥100 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '≥126 mg/dL', 'ADA criteria: fasting glucose ≥126 on two separate occasions indicates diabetes.'),
('ee8bd7ed-98c3-55c1-be79-657c02974eaf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) is:', '["Fasting glucose 100-125","Hypoglycemia","Normal glucose","Diabetes","2-hour OGTT glucose 140-199 mg/dL"]'::jsonb, '2-hour OGTT glucose 140-199 mg/dL', 'IGT and IFG are prediabetic states with increased risk of progression to diabetes.'),
('a5165889-53bc-58c5-bd87-2435c2269561'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin glargine is a:', '["Long-acting insulin analog","Short-acting insulin","Intermediate insulin","Mixed insulin","Rapid-acting analog"]'::jsonb, 'Long-acting insulin analog', 'Glargine forms microprecipitates at injection site for sustained release.'),
('241ec185-2341-58da-864f-a7c0c9662e21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Insulin lispro is a:', '["Long-acting analog","Mixed insulin","Short-acting regular insulin","Rapid-acting insulin analog","Intermediate insulin"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid-acting insulin analog', 'Lispro has reversed amino acids (B28, B29) to reduce self-association and speed absorption.'),
('ac13ca05-cd3d-5da1-8503-1f977f51a3e2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'K-ATP channels in beta cells are composed of:', '["Ca2+ channels","SUR1 (sulfonylurea receptor) and Kir6.2","SUR2 and Kir6.1","Na+ channels","Voltage-gated K+ channels"]'::jsonb, 'SUR1 (sulfonylurea receptor) and Kir6.2', 'K-ATP channels couple metabolic state to membrane potential in beta cells.'),
('6ff85cc8-c2d2-5b98-92e4-7f7f341eaf40'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GLUT2 (beta cell glucose transporter) has:', '["Low Km","No glucose transport","High Km (~15-20 mM) for glucose","ATP dependence","High affinity"]'::jsonb, 'High Km (~15-20 mM) for glucose', 'GLUT2''s high Km ensures glucose uptake is proportional to blood glucose level.'),
('839dfce2-28a6-59eb-bbb4-30f4e752ef7d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'GLUT4 is expressed in:', '["Intestine and liver","Erythrocytes","Muscle and adipose tissue","Brain and kidney","Liver and pancreas"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle and adipose tissue', 'Insulin stimulates GLUT4 translocation from intracellular vesicles to the plasma membrane.'),
('e0e7e920-b907-51b1-9069-44c60f0e5bb6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Ketone bodies are produced when:', '["Protein intake is high","Glycogen stores are full","Insulin is high","Glucose is abundant","Insulin is low and glucagon is high"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin is low and glucagon is high', 'Ketogenesis provides an alternative fuel source during starvation or DKA.'),
('d7f5960c-6e75-5a44-867a-b3238255bd95'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The three ketone bodies are:', '["The three ketone bodies are correct","Glucose, fructose, galactose","FFA, glycerol, TG","Acetoacetate, beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetone","Lactate, pyruvate, alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetoacetate, beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetone', 'Acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate are the main circulating ketones; acetone is volatile.'),
('db3ccc5e-de8d-5d89-abc5-8accd2bd0fcc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'In a surgical context, The adrenal cortex secretes:', '["CRH mediated physiological mechanism","ACTH mediated physiological mechanism","Epinephrine and norepinephrine mediated physiological mechanism","Catecholamines only mediated physiological mechanism","Cortisol (glucocorticoid), aldosterone (mineralocorticoid), and androgens"]'::jsonb, 'Cortisol (glucocorticoid), aldosterone (mineralocorticoid), and androgens', 'The adrenal cortex has three zones: glomerulosa (mineralocorticoids), fasciculata (glucocorticoids), reticularis (androgens).'),
('25cfa77a-0e7e-5f25-8788-99ef28f1dfdb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'On diagnostic evaluation, The zona glomerulosa produces:', '["Epinephrine","Cortisol","Norepinephrine","DHEA","Aldosterone"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone', 'Zona glomerulosa lacks 17-alpha-hydroxylase, so it produces aldosterone.'),
('5baefc8d-a1fd-5f93-a2e5-5b925bbbd386'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'With respect to management, The zona fasciculata produces:', '["Aldosterone","Corticosterone","Cortisol","Androgens","Epinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Cortisol', 'Zona fasciculata is the main site of cortisol production.'),
('9241b3e1-be47-5f20-b668-a191032f0d08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, The zona reticularis produces:', '["Aldosterone","Norepinephrine","DHEA and DHEA-S","Epinephrine","Cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'DHEA and DHEA-S', 'Zona reticularis produces adrenal androgens, precursors for sex hormone synthesis.'),
('128fb647-6756-5e46-8098-3af45f6babf1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol synthesis from cholesterol is stimulated by:', '["ACTH","CRH","Epinephrine","Angiotensin II","ANP"]'::jsonb, 'ACTH', 'ACTH binds MC2R on adrenocortical cells activating adenylyl cyclase and steroidogenesis.'),
('098d7f7c-7c0f-5c5c-a7c6-c4dc78511b22'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The rate-limiting step in cortisol synthesis is:', '["17-alpha-hydroxylation","21-hydroxylation","11-beta-hydroxylation","Conversion to aldosterone","Cholesterol side-chain cleavage by CYP11A1"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol side-chain cleavage by CYP11A1', 'Desmolase converts cholesterol to pregnenolone, the first and rate-limiting step.'),
('faa6bc92-7cae-5f1a-a83c-51ba443d5cd6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Most circulating cortisol is bound to:', '["Albumin","Transthyretin","Sex hormone-binding globulin","Corticosteroid-binding globulin","Free form"]'::jsonb, 'Corticosteroid-binding globulin', '~90% of cortisol is bound to CBG (transcortin), ~7% to albumin, ~3% free.'),
('3377df5c-519c-5a19-bf3e-c764097b20b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol acts via:', '["JAK-STAT","Ion channel","Tyrosine kinase receptor","Membrane GPCR","Intracellular glucocorticoid receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Intracellular glucocorticoid receptor', 'Cortisol binds cytoplasmic GR, which translocates to the nucleus to regulate gene transcription.'),
('b1ccc9f5-ed0d-5773-bda1-c11ff467dd55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Metabolic effects of cortisol include:', '["Decreased blood glucose mediated physiological mechanism","Increased glucose uptake mediated physiological mechanism","Increased gluconeogenesis, proteolysis, and lipolysis","Decreased lipolysis mediated physiological mechanism","Increased protein synthesis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased gluconeogenesis, proteolysis, and lipolysis', 'Cortisol is catabolic: it increases glucose production, breaks down protein and fat.'),
('a304cac6-987c-505d-89dd-83edfad9425f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol has which effect on inflammation?', '["No effect","Increases histamine","Increases cytokine production","Pro-inflammatory","Anti-inflammatory"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-inflammatory', 'Cortisol inhibits phospholipase A2, cytokines, and immune cell function, reducing inflammation.'),
('b8fd977a-e974-5964-93ca-e29c98efe7e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol secretion follows a:', '["Circadian rhythm","No rhythm","Ultradian rhythm","Menstrual cycle pattern","Random pattern"]'::jsonb, 'Circadian rhythm', 'Cortisol peaks in early morning and declines throughout the day.'),
('faf28ede-2ae4-53cd-ba76-ccc6890ea885'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cushing syndrome (cortisol excess) features:', '["Hypoglycemia, bronzing","Osteoporosis only","Central obesity, moon face, buffalo hump, striae, hypertension, hyperglycemia","Weight loss, hypotension, hyperpigmentation","Galactorrhea"]'::jsonb, 'Central obesity, moon face, buffalo hump, striae, hypertension, hyperglycemia', 'Cushing syndrome has characteristic fat redistribution, metabolic, and catabolic effects.'),
('441a2dd6-7536-5df0-82c6-10de7f7ac048'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cushing disease (pituitary ACTHoma) is treated by:', '["Transsphenoidal adenomectomy","Glucocorticoid replacement","Radiation","Ketoconazole","Adrenalectomy"]'::jsonb, 'Transsphenoidal adenomectomy', 'Cushing disease is pituitary ACTH-secreting tumor treated by surgical removal.'),
('bdf615f4-1eae-5327-b007-e886f95f0c03'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Addison disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) features:', '["Hyperpigmentation, hypernatremia mediated physiological mechanism","Pallor, hypernatremia mediated physiological mechanism","Obesity, hirsutism mediated physiological mechanism","Hypokalemia, hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperpigmentation, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperpigmentation, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, hypotension', 'Addison: loss of cortisol (pigmentation from ACTH rise), aldosterone (Na+/K+ imbalance), and androgens.'),
('d2c35a49-c1ca-59b5-9d5b-3b529e085227'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Adrenal insufficiency is treated with:', '["ACTH mediated physiological mechanism","Glucocorticoid (hydrocortisone) and mineralocorticoid (fludrocortisone) replacement","Only mineralocorticoid mediated physiological mechanism","Only glucocorticoid mediated physiological mechanism","Epinephrine mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Glucocorticoid (hydrocortisone) and mineralocorticoid (fludrocortisone) replacement', 'Both glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid must be replaced in primary adrenal insufficiency.'),
('4cafc6f9-631e-5ff5-963f-75c26190b220'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Secondary adrenal insufficiency features:', '["Hypertension, hypokalemia","No hyperpigmentation (low ACTH), preserved aldosterone","Weight gain, striae","Hyperpigmentation, hyperkalemia","Hypoglycemia only"]'::jsonb, 'No hyperpigmentation (low ACTH), preserved aldosterone', 'Secondary (pituitary) ACTH deficiency lacks hyperpigmentation and usually spares aldosterone.'),
('e6d7f3b1-3ef1-5c72-b706-9979514f7dbd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) most commonly results from:', '["Desmolase deficiency","21-hydroxylase deficiency","3-beta-HSD deficiency","11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency","17-alpha-hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, '21-hydroxylase deficiency', '~90% of CAH is due to 21-OH deficiency, causing cortisol deficiency, aldosterone deficiency, and androgen excess.'),
('345a4ac9-8afc-525e-b303-5131b18605c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', '21-hydroxylase deficiency causes:', '["Feminization mediated physiological mechanism","Virilization (androgen excess), salt-wasting (aldosterone deficiency), cortisol deficiency","Hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","Cushing syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Precocious puberty mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Virilization (androgen excess), salt-wasting (aldosterone deficiency), cortisol deficiency', 'CAH blocks cortisol and aldosterone synthesis, shunting precursors to androgens.'),
('3bb40f61-fe58-5245-8e50-cb7ab5de0ed9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', '17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency causes:', '["Hypertension, hypogonadism (decreased sex hormones), cortisol low","Salt-wasting mediated physiological mechanism","Precocious puberty mediated physiological mechanism","Virilization mediated physiological mechanism","Cushing mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension, hypogonadism (decreased sex hormones), cortisol low', '17-OH deficiency blocks cortisol and sex hormones but increases DOC (mineralocorticoid, causing hypertension).'),
('d024a46e-3aa8-54d4-a3b5-1615f66f467e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', '11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency causes:', '["Hypogonadism","Hypertension, virilization","Cortisol excess","Feminization","Salt-wasting"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension, virilization', '11-OH deficiency increases DOC (hypertension) and androgen excess (virilization).'),
('a1919e78-20bc-5bd9-b7ed-bd706105f8d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'ACTH stimulation test (cosyntropin test) is used to:', '["Assess pituitary function","Diagnose pheochromocytoma","Diagnose adrenal insufficiency","Measure aldosterone","Diagnose Cushing syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Diagnose adrenal insufficiency', 'A normal response is cortisol >18 mcg/dL after ACTH; low response indicates adrenal insufficiency.'),
('987ecb44-170e-5996-bfa9-30b4780a6a25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Dexamethasone suppression test:', '["Tests adrenal function mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulates ACTH mediated physiological mechanism","Diagnoses diabetes mediated physiological mechanism","Suppresses ACTH in normal individuals; used to diagnose Cushing syndrome","Increases cortisol mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Suppresses ACTH in normal individuals; used to diagnose Cushing syndrome', 'Low-dose dexamethasone suppresses ACTH in normals; lack of suppression suggests Cushing.'),
('e42f3496-6eed-54da-934f-bc2bb866029d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'CRH stimulation test helps differentiate:', '["Primary vs secondary adrenal insufficiency","Pituitary (Cushing disease) vs ectopic ACTH source","Hypothalamic vs pituitary disease","Cushing vs pseudo-Cushing","Adrenal tumor vs hyperplasia"]'::jsonb, 'Pituitary (Cushing disease) vs ectopic ACTH source', 'ACTH responds to CRH in Cushing disease but not in ectopic ACTH syndrome.'),
('8fd4ab43-9a4c-5744-830f-bb1e89c14855'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Glucocorticoid withdrawal syndrome:', '["Adrenal crisis upon abrupt withdrawal after chronic steroid use","Cushing syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Increased cortisol mediated physiological mechanism","Addison disease mediated physiological mechanism","Pituitary tumor mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal crisis upon abrupt withdrawal after chronic steroid use', 'Chronic glucocorticoid use suppresses the HPA axis; taper is needed to allow recovery.'),
('70feedc7-2eca-55f1-9e9d-32414bf85991'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol increases blood glucose by:', '["Increasing glycogen synthesis mediated physiological mechanism","Decreasing glucagon mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulating gluconeogenesis, decreasing glucose uptake, increasing glycogenolysis","Increasing glucose uptake mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulating insulin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating gluconeogenesis, decreasing glucose uptake, increasing glycogenolysis', 'Cortisol''s diabetogenic effects include hepatic glucose production and peripheral insulin resistance.'),
('80589fd5-6bcd-5b2b-a83f-2d803fa8909c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol effects on bone include:', '["Decreased bone formation (inhibits osteoblasts) and increased bone resorption","Decreased osteoclast activity mediated physiological mechanism","Increased calcium absorption mediated physiological mechanism","Increased bone formation mediated physiological mechanism","Increased osteoblast activity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased bone formation (inhibits osteoblasts) and increased bone resorption', 'Chronic cortisol excess causes osteoporosis by suppressing bone formation.'),
('81d91287-b503-5e64-a7e0-d79f25d97457'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol effects on immunity include:', '["Increases cytokine release mediated physiological mechanism","Activates macrophages mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulates immune response mediated physiological mechanism","Suppresses T cells, cytokines, inflammation; increases susceptibility to infection","Increases antibody production mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Suppresses T cells, cytokines, inflammation; increases susceptibility to infection', 'Glucocorticoids are potent immunosuppressants used in autoimmune diseases and transplant.'),
('6f4a8c01-e853-53c9-b9a4-094eee2850d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Cortisol effects on the kidney include:', '["Decreases K+ secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Increases ADH mediated physiological mechanism","Increases GFR (water diuresis) and free water clearance","Increases Na+ reabsorption mediated physiological mechanism","Decreases GFR mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increases GFR (water diuresis) and free water clearance', 'Cortisol increases renal blood flow and GFR, contributing to polyuria in Cushing syndrome.'),
('452842f8-a8ad-5ab0-8f9c-7062dff76ff5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Glucocorticoids are used therapeutically for:', '["Anti-inflammatory/immunosuppressive effects","Infertility mediated physiological mechanism","Diabetes mediated physiological mechanism","Hypertension mediated physiological mechanism","Infection treatment mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-inflammatory/immunosuppressive effects', 'Synthetic glucocorticoids like prednisone are widely used for their anti-inflammatory properties.'),
('4122549d-c2ae-568e-9c12-3452be9feefa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Fludrocortisone is a:', '["Androgen","Glucocorticoid","Progestin","Synthetic mineralocorticoid","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Synthetic mineralocorticoid', 'Fludrocortisone has potent mineralocorticoid activity with minimal glucocorticoid effect.'),
('c3fd202c-5146-5d3a-8a91-aa25d422a442'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The adrenal medulla produces:', '["Epinephrine (~80%) and norepinephrine","Aldosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Cortisol mediated physiological mechanism","Androgens mediated physiological mechanism","ACTH mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Epinephrine (~80%) and norepinephrine', 'Adrenal medullary chromaffin cells secrete ~80% epinephrine and ~20% norepinephrine.'),
('f1692cef-28d5-5a34-8c56-7bead43b9cec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Catecholamines are synthesized from:', '["Serine mediated physiological mechanism","Tyrosine -> DOPA -> dopamine -> norepinephrine -> epinephrine","Histidine mediated physiological mechanism","Phenylalanine mediated physiological mechanism","Tryptophan mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine -> DOPA -> dopamine -> norepinephrine -> epinephrine', 'Catecholamine synthesis: tyrosine hydroxylase (rate-limiting) converts tyrosine to DOPA.'),
('98e4c46e-8673-5915-9679-32e380fbd0f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'In a surgical context, The rate-limiting enzyme in catecholamine synthesis is:', '["Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","DOPA decarboxylase","MAO","PNMT"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine hydroxylase', 'Tyrosine hydroxylase converts tyrosine to DOPA; it is inhibited by catecholamines (feedback).'),
('922dbda2-ad6c-5988-b4bf-bea525de53cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Phenylethanolamine N-methyltransferase (PNMT) converts:', '["Dopamine to norepinephrine","Epinephrine to metanephrine","Norepinephrine to epinephrine","DOPA to dopamine","Tyrosine to DOPA"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine to epinephrine', 'PNMT (induced by cortisol) in the adrenal medulla converts NE to E.'),
('560c082d-2bbf-55b9-a477-e89424e91067'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Catecholamines are stored in:', '["Lysosomes","Synaptic vesicles","Cytosol","Endoplasmic reticulum","Chromaffin granules"]'::jsonb, 'Chromaffin granules', 'Catecholamines are stored in secretory vesicles with ATP and chromogranin A.'),
('b6294e81-3a7b-5bdb-a16a-fe119edba9e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Catecholamines are degraded by:', '["COMT and MAO","Aldehyde dehydrogenase","XO","ACE","Cholinesterase"]'::jsonb, 'COMT and MAO', 'Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) and monoamine oxidase (MAO) break down catecholamines.'),
('d7f879fe-b671-55b6-8b0f-9cb1b33268f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'The main metabolite of norepinephrine and epinephrine is:', '["VMA","HVA","DOPA","Normetanephrine","Metanephrine"]'::jsonb, 'VMA', 'VMA is the final urinary metabolite of catecholamines, measured in pheochromocytoma diagnosis.'),
('4d1667a8-5911-556f-8a79-93f876d39979'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Epinephrine acts on adrenergic receptors:', '["Beta-1, beta-2, alpha-1, alpha-2","Only alpha-1 mediated physiological mechanism","Only beta-1 mediated physiological mechanism","Only alpha-2 mediated physiological mechanism","Only beta-2 mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-1, beta-2, alpha-1, alpha-2', 'Epinephrine is a non-selective adrenergic agonist with varied effects.'),
('1c8a1c56-8b5b-526d-9284-48bc1fe0c7da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Norepinephrine acts primarily on:', '["All receptors equally","Alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors","Beta-1","Beta-2","Dopamine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1 and alpha-2 receptors', 'Norepinephrine is mainly an alpha agonist with some beta-1 effects.'),
