('ba41e34e-cfc1-5789-bd43-310d51a4a8c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Osteopetrosis is:', '["Increased bone density due to defective osteoclast function","Bone cancer mediated physiological mechanism","Bone loss mediated physiological mechanism","Defective mineralization mediated physiological mechanism","Excess collagen mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased bone density due to defective osteoclast function', 'Osteopetrosis (marble bone disease) results from failure of osteoclast-mediated bone resorption.'),
('4185a5a1-55d5-509f-95c3-7f8739d63b6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Hypophosphatasia is:', '["Vitamin D deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","PTH excess mediated physiological mechanism","Low alkaline phosphatase activity causing defective mineralization","High ALP mediated physiological mechanism","Phosphate deficiency mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Low alkaline phosphatase activity causing defective mineralization', 'Hypophosphatasia: ALP deficiency leads to accumulation of pyrophosphate, inhibiting mineralization.'),
('016f0f05-08a1-5420-a17f-4ce4e78b4b4e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'X-linked hypophosphatemia (XLH) is caused by:', '["Calcium deficiency","PTH excess","FGF23 excess","ALP deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'FGF23 excess', 'XLH (vitamin D-resistant rickets) is due to PHEX mutation causing elevated FGF23.'),
('17ca76e5-5934-5c99-8184-b2a1967b1bc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Endocrinology', 'Bone morphogenetic proteins (BMPs) are:', '["Mineralization inhibitors mediated physiological mechanism","Bone-resorbing factors mediated physiological mechanism","Growth factors that induce bone and cartilage formation","Osteoclast activators mediated physiological mechanism","Collagen types mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Growth factors that induce bone and cartilage formation', 'BMPs (especially BMP-2, -7) are used clinically to promote bone healing.'),
('e4d50401-5db5-5fc1-a47a-9f536a634b3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The testes are located in the scrotum because spermatogenesis requires:', '["Neutral pH mediated physiological mechanism","A temperature 2-3C below core body temperature","Low CO2 mediated physiological mechanism","High pressure mediated physiological mechanism","High oxygen tension mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'A temperature 2-3C below core body temperature', 'The scrotum maintains ~33C (vs. 37C core) via the pampiniform plexus and dartos/cremaster muscles.'),
('2d4b25cb-de54-5873-8600-8dcf85cef1c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis occurs in the:', '["Epididymis","Vas deferens","Seminiferous tubules","Prostate","Seminal vesicles"]'::jsonb, 'Seminiferous tubules', 'Spermatogenesis (production of sperm) takes place in the seminiferous tubules.'),
('e9eadbbb-bd2e-556a-978e-596eb0217703'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis takes approximately:', '["90-100 days","120 days","14 days","28 days","64-74 days"]'::jsonb, '64-74 days', 'The complete spermatogenic cycle (spermatogonia to mature sperm) takes ~64-74 days in humans.'),
('f759a7ef-118d-55cb-957a-592affe3b678'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis stages in order are:', '["Primary -> secondary -> spermatogonia mediated physiological mechanism","Spermatid -> spermatocyte -> spermatogonia mediated physiological mechanism","Spermatozoon -> spermatid -> spermatocyte mediated physiological mechanism","Spermatogonia -> primary spermatocyte -> secondary spermatocyte -> spermatid -> spermatozoon","Spermatogonia -> spermatid -> spermatocyte mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Spermatogonia -> primary spermatocyte -> secondary spermatocyte -> spermatid -> spermatozoon', 'Spermatogenesis proceeds from diploid spermatogonia through meiosis to haploid spermatids, then maturation.'),
('aceee9b5-7500-565a-92d2-f42b77e46360'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogonia divide by:', '["Mitosis","Only budding","Only amitosis","Both mitosis and meiosis","Meiosis"]'::jsonb, 'Mitosis', 'Spermatogonia undergo mitotic divisions; primary spermatocytes undergo meiosis I.'),
('3d39f5c0-ee08-56b9-8306-beca2cdec089'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Primary spermatocytes are:', '["Haploid with 46","Haploid","Tetraploid","Diploid","Diploid with 23"]'::jsonb, 'Diploid', 'Primary spermatocytes have 46 chromosomes (2n, 4C DNA) and undergo meiosis I.'),
('b2a54139-f5db-5c6d-aed3-952a50f3dfde'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Secondary spermatocytes are:', '["Diploid","Triploid","Tetraploid","Haploid","Haploid with 46"]'::jsonb, 'Haploid', 'After meiosis I, secondary spermatocytes have 23 chromosomes but each chromosome has two chromatids.'),
('1aa55316-0083-5c59-8c89-db8b3471aafc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatids are:', '["Haploid (n, 23 chromosomes, 1C DNA) after meiosis II","Spermatozoa mediated physiological mechanism","Spermatogonia mediated physiological mechanism","Diploid mediated physiological mechanism","Secondary spermatocytes mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Haploid (n, 23 chromosomes, 1C DNA) after meiosis II', 'Meiosis II produces haploid spermatids with 23 single-chromatid chromosomes.'),
('67d5d427-6010-523a-aa81-498c9e84d9e4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermiogenesis is:', '["Spermiation mediated physiological mechanism","The transformation of spermatids into mature spermatozoa","Spermatogonial division mediated physiological mechanism","Meiosis II mediated physiological mechanism","Meiosis I mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'The transformation of spermatids into mature spermatozoa', 'Spermiogenesis involves acrosome formation, nuclear condensation, flagellum development, and cytoplasmic elimination.'),
('9961be11-0cd2-5942-b5e8-3962dcc034e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Sertoli cells line the:', '["Seminiferous tubules","Prostate","Epididymis","Interstitial space","Vas deferens"]'::jsonb, 'Seminiferous tubules', 'Sertoli cells (sustentacular cells) form the blood-testis barrier and support developing germ cells.'),
('17a0c94d-8972-5858-b858-6c42c8b86de7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Sertoli cells produce:', '["FSH mediated physiological mechanism","Testosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibin, ABP (androgen-binding protein), and activin","LH mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibin, ABP (androgen-binding protein), and activin', 'Sertoli cells secrete inhibin (negative feedback on FSH), ABP (binds testosterone), and activin.'),
('e8a2a55a-7293-59c4-92d0-fc868dba0695'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The blood-testis barrier is formed by:', '["Leydig cells","Tight junctions between Sertoli cells","Peritubular myoid cells","Basement membrane","Capillary endothelium"]'::jsonb, 'Tight junctions between Sertoli cells', 'Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells create the BTB, protecting germ cells from immune attack.'),
('578ae87c-a40a-5e05-9448-1c3a76af34ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The blood-testis barrier divides the seminiferous epithelium into:', '["Basal and adluminal compartments","Proximal and distal","Alpha and beta","Upper and lower","Inner and outer"]'::jsonb, 'Basal and adluminal compartments', 'Spermatogonia reside in the basal compartment; primary spermatocytes and beyond are in the adluminal compartment.'),
('f4f01dcc-f3b8-5faa-b93d-dcd16f2729fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Leydig cells (interstitial cells) produce:', '["Inhibin","Testosterone","ABP","Progesterone","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Testosterone', 'Leydig cells in the interstitium produce testosterone when stimulated by LH.'),
('3b98473d-0b85-5106-a320-338d5f1a428d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Leydig cells are located:', '["In the prostate mediated physiological mechanism","Inside seminiferous tubules mediated physiological mechanism","In the epididymis mediated physiological mechanism","In the interstitial space between seminiferous tubules","In the vas deferens mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'In the interstitial space between seminiferous tubules', 'Leydig cells (interstitial cells of Leydig) are outside the seminiferous tubules.'),
('acb220ec-828d-5c3d-bdb9-9ebfc068da6c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'LH acts on Leydig cells via:', '["GPCR (Gs/cAMP) to stimulate testosterone synthesis","JAK-STAT mediated physiological mechanism","Ion channel mediated physiological mechanism","Tyrosine kinase mediated physiological mechanism","Nuclear receptor mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'GPCR (Gs/cAMP) to stimulate testosterone synthesis', 'LH binds to the LHCG receptor (GPCR) on Leydig cells, activating cAMP and steroidogenesis.'),
('2e0f2299-990e-5930-b452-350e68d37321'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'FSH acts on:', '["Leydig cells","Epididymis","Sertoli cells","Germ cells","Prostate"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells', 'FSH binds Sertoli cell receptors, essential for initiating and maintaining spermatogenesis.'),
('2dd6de63-8fbe-564b-a34d-0dec5501f74f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Testosterone is produced from cholesterol in:', '["Germ cells","Leydig cells","Prostate","Sertoli cells","Adrenal cortex"]'::jsonb, 'Leydig cells', 'Leydig cells convert cholesterol to testosterone via desmolase, 17-hydroxylase, and 17-beta-HSD.'),
('f58cbd67-1639-5d60-aaeb-545dad8f85c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Most circulating testosterone is bound to:', '["Free form","TBG","CBG","Sex hormone-binding globulin","Albumin"]'::jsonb, 'Sex hormone-binding globulin', '~50-60% of testosterone is tightly bound to SHBG; ~40% loosely to albumin; ~2% free.'),
('9402de9b-139f-537d-aa15-deeed34ea901'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Testosterone is converted to DHT by:', '["5-alpha-reductase in target tissues","17-beta-HSD mediated physiological mechanism","3-beta-HSD mediated physiological mechanism","Aromatase mediated physiological mechanism","P450 aromatase mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, '5-alpha-reductase in target tissues', '5-alpha-reductase type 2 converts testosterone to DHT in androgen-sensitive tissues.'),
('68f518fe-1f77-5135-a02b-55cadb060786'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Testosterone is converted to estradiol by:', '["Desmolase mediated physiological mechanism","Lysyl oxidase mediated physiological mechanism","5-alpha-reductase mediated physiological mechanism","Aromatase (CYP19) in adipose and other tissues","17-beta-HSD mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Aromatase (CYP19) in adipose and other tissues', 'Aromatase converts testosterone to estradiol, important for bone health and libido.'),
('7642cf0a-437b-588f-b873-303137b76ad3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Testosterone actions include:', '["Development of male reproductive organs, secondary sex characteristics, anabolism, libido","Oogenesis mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine growth mediated physiological mechanism","Breast development mediated physiological mechanism","Menstrual cycle regulation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Development of male reproductive organs, secondary sex characteristics, anabolism, libido', 'Testosterone promotes Wolffian development, spermatogenesis, muscle mass, bone density, and sexual function.'),
('4b92f2bf-0452-5d06-b096-53747b6607c8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'DHT is essential for:', '["Wolffian duct development mediated physiological mechanism","Testicular descent mediated physiological mechanism","External genitalia development and prostate growth","Libido mediated physiological mechanism","Spermatogenesis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'External genitalia development and prostate growth', 'DHT mediates external genital virilization, prostate growth, and male pattern baldness.'),
('a6502884-5311-5562-839c-14bf34f318bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Inhibin is secreted by:', '["Leydig cells","Pituitary","Hypothalamus","Germ cells","Sertoli cells"]'::jsonb, 'Sertoli cells', 'Inhibin B from Sertoli cells negatively feeds back on the pituitary to selectively suppress FSH.'),
('f26eaac3-f1b9-5d18-80d5-6f2664e2763f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Activin is produced by:', '["Leydig cells only","Adrenal","Sertoli cells only","Hypothalamus","Gonads and pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Gonads and pituitary', 'Activin stimulates FSH synthesis and secretion, counterbalanced by inhibin and follistatin.'),
('52dba909-ad0d-5bd9-9f5b-4d00ee4dbbff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Male sex accessory glands include:', '["Seminal vesicles (+60% semen), prostate (+30%), bulbourethral glands","Epididymis mediated physiological mechanism","Only seminal vesicles mediated physiological mechanism","Only bulbourethral mediated physiological mechanism","Only prostate mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Seminal vesicles (+60% semen), prostate (+30%), bulbourethral glands', 'Seminal vesicles produce fructose-rich fluid; prostate produces citrate, PSA, acid phosphatase.'),
('a51ec41e-b468-582d-9d7c-243d9d43bc68'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Semen analysis normal parameters include:', '["Motility >80% mediated physiological mechanism","Volume >1.5 mL, sperm count >15 million/mL, motility >40%, morphology >4% normal","Count >100 million mediated physiological mechanism","No abnormal forms mediated physiological mechanism","Volume >5 mL, count >50 million mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Volume >1.5 mL, sperm count >15 million/mL, motility >40%, morphology >4% normal', 'WHO 5th edition criteria: low reference limits for semen analysis.'),
('d99052fe-e1b4-580b-aeba-b8653a3f122e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Sperm acquire motility and fertilizing capacity in the:', '["Epididymis","Seminiferous tubules","Seminal vesicles","Female reproductive tract","Vas deferens"]'::jsonb, 'Epididymis', 'Sperm mature in the epididymis, gaining motility and ability to fertilize.'),
('3e161d85-bba2-559c-9a32-33e9a961c985'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Capacitation of sperm occurs in:', '["Female genital tract","Vas deferens","Seminal vesicles","Testes","Epididymis"]'::jsonb, 'Female genital tract', 'Capacitation involves cholesterol efflux, membrane changes, and increased calcium influx.'),
('5d675769-d78e-5843-a0bd-ef4e2ecfd561'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'With respect to management, The acrosome reaction is triggered by:', '["Temperature change mediated physiological mechanism","Progesterone mediated physiological mechanism","Zona pellucida glycoprotein ZP3 binding to sperm receptors","Capacitation alone mediated physiological mechanism","pH change mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Zona pellucida glycoprotein ZP3 binding to sperm receptors', 'ZP3 binding induces the acrosome reaction, releasing hydrolytic enzymes for zona penetration.'),
('eb05b0e3-0281-5a3a-8442-87b47852fb79'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Puberty in males is initiated by:', '["Increased inhibin mediated physiological mechanism","Increased GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude","Decreased GnRH mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased testosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased FSH/LH mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude', 'Puberty begins with increased pulsatile GnRH from the hypothalamus, stimulating gonadotropins.'),
('760a8020-e897-5b85-a41a-6f3a584f4327'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Gonadarche refers to:', '["Breast development","Pubic hair","Growth spurt","Adrenal androgen production","The reactivation of the gonads at puberty"]'::jsonb, 'The reactivation of the gonads at puberty', 'Gonadarche is the activation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis at puberty.'),
('94fccd38-af8d-5e90-8edb-24a5f5210f08'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Adrenarche refers to:', '["Gonadal activation mediated physiological mechanism","Increased adrenal androgen production (DHEA-S) before gonadarche","Growth spurt mediated physiological mechanism","Breast development mediated physiological mechanism","Pubic hair mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased adrenal androgen production (DHEA-S) before gonadarche', 'Adrenarche occurs at ~6-8 years, increasing adrenal androgens, leading to early pubic hair.'),
('f1cbd548-d937-5745-84a0-de8c5d127474'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogenesis requires which hormone from the anterior pituitary?', '["TSH","LH","Prolactin","FSH","ACTH"]'::jsonb, 'FSH', 'FSH is essential for spermatogenesis; LH stimulates testosterone which also supports it.'),
('8feccc1d-b8bb-5926-9f32-09c237267c07'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Testosterone negative feedback occurs on:', '["GnRH and LH secretion","Only the adrenal","Only FSH","Only the testes","Only the prostate"]'::jsonb, 'GnRH and LH secretion', 'Testosterone feeds back to reduce GnRH pulse frequency and LH secretion.'),
('663022e7-d463-504d-aafd-6069b290a8e3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Inhibin B provides negative feedback specifically on:', '["LH secretion","Prolactin secretion","Testosterone secretion","FSH secretion","GnRH secretion"]'::jsonb, 'FSH secretion', 'Inhibin B selectively inhibits FSH without affecting LH.'),
('9e87d7c4-7d6d-5c20-a909-cb5b21878cfc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The male sexual response includes:', '["Only emission mediated physiological mechanism","Erection and emission mediated physiological mechanism","Erection (NO-mediated vasodilation), emission, ejaculation, and detumescence","Only ejaculation mediated physiological mechanism","Only erection mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Erection (NO-mediated vasodilation), emission, ejaculation, and detumescence', 'Erection is NO/cGMP-mediated; emission and ejaculation are sympathetic/somatic.'),
('1fbd5e81-b165-563e-88b8-07fc449cd8ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Andropause (late-onset hypogonadism) features:', '["Hypergonadism mediated physiological mechanism","Precocious puberty mediated physiological mechanism","Gynecomastia mediated physiological mechanism","High testosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Low testosterone, decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, fatigue, loss of muscle mass"]'::jsonb, 'Low testosterone, decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, fatigue, loss of muscle mass', 'Andropause is gradual testosterone decline with age; not all men are affected.'),
('4319e3bf-c40b-57e9-99e9-303d1c0e06df'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Gynecomastia is:', '["Breast enlargement in males due to estrogen/androgen imbalance","Pseudogynecomastia mediated physiological mechanism","Normal finding mediated physiological mechanism","Fat deposition mediated physiological mechanism","Male breast cancer mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Breast enlargement in males due to estrogen/androgen imbalance', 'Gynecomastia can be physiological (puberty, aging) or pathological (drugs, tumors).'),
('b0c32861-7e77-589a-9507-f7401edd2044'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The epididymis functions to:', '["Secrete seminal fluid","Produce testosterone","Produce sperm","Store, mature, and transport sperm","Filter blood"]'::jsonb, 'Store, mature, and transport sperm', 'Sperm spend ~12 days in the epididymis undergoing maturation.'),
('fe2de80f-867c-5ce6-ba2a-97f72e99dce2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The vas deferens transports sperm from:', '["Testes to epididymis","Epididymis to the ejaculatory duct","Prostate to urethra","Seminal vesicles to urethra","Epididymis to prostate"]'::jsonb, 'Epididymis to the ejaculatory duct', 'Vasectomy involves cutting the vas deferens to prevent sperm transport.'),
('aa1748af-f6df-513d-86e6-6c02f034973f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Cryptorchidism (undescended testis) should be treated before:', '["Age 10 mediated physiological mechanism","Puberty mediated physiological mechanism","Adulthood mediated physiological mechanism","Age 5 mediated physiological mechanism","Age 1-2 years to preserve fertility"]'::jsonb, 'Age 1-2 years to preserve fertility', 'Early orchidopexy prevents testicular damage and preserves spermatogenesis.'),
('1cd2bb45-f00a-5771-832a-069685f71647'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Varicocele is:', '["Epididymitis mediated physiological mechanism","Hydrocele mediated physiological mechanism","Inguinal hernia mediated physiological mechanism","Abnormal dilation of testicular veins","Testicular tumor mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Abnormal dilation of testicular veins', 'Varicocele causes heat-related testicular dysfunction; it is typically left-sided.'),
('934b9bf0-8f85-5ba6-ac41-da5398e0d6ba'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Sperm count is:', '["Volume of ejaculate","Percentage normal forms","Motile sperm count","Total sperm in ejaculate","Number of sperm per milliliter of semen"]'::jsonb, 'Number of sperm per milliliter of semen', 'Normal sperm count: >15 million/mL.'),
('43376891-f0fe-58e8-9f85-e8425c2247ac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Erectile physiology requires:', '["Sympathetic activation mediated physiological mechanism","Nitric oxide (NO) -> cGMP -> relaxation of corpus cavernosum smooth muscle","Decreased blood flow mediated physiological mechanism","Venous constriction mediated physiological mechanism","Adrenergic constriction mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Nitric oxide (NO) -> cGMP -> relaxation of corpus cavernosum smooth muscle', 'NO from NANC neurons and endothelium activates guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP and causing vasodilation.'),
('31825520-65a9-5519-aeb4-10721fc57bef'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil) work by:', '["Blocking Ca2+ channels","Preventing cGMP breakdown in the corpus cavernosum","Increasing NO production","Blocking alpha receptors","Stimulating guanylyl cyclase"]'::jsonb, 'Preventing cGMP breakdown in the corpus cavernosum', 'PDE5 breaks down cGMP; sildenafil inhibits PDE5, enhancing and prolonging erection.'),
('6262f2ae-278c-5393-bce7-57461c0df2a0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Descent of the testes is mediated by:', '["Estrogen","LH","Testosterone and INSL3","GnRH","FSH"]'::jsonb, 'Testosterone and INSL3', 'Testosterone drives transabdominal descent; INSL3 is important for gubernacular development.'),
('bdafda56-4964-5d5a-be6b-dca52ed551a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermatogenic arrest can be caused by:', '["Only genetic factors mediated physiological mechanism","Only infection mediated physiological mechanism","Only hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Heat, toxins, radiation, chemotherapy, infection, genetic factors","Only heat mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Heat, toxins, radiation, chemotherapy, infection, genetic factors', 'Multiple factors can disrupt spermatogenesis at various stages.'),
('aaf769f3-589f-5443-a6bb-ab0c281423d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, The pampiniform plexus of veins:', '["Carries sperm","Secretes testosterone","Drains lymph","Supplies blood to the testes","Cools testicular arterial blood"]'::jsonb, 'Cools testicular arterial blood', 'The pampiniform plexus surrounds the testicular artery, cooling incoming blood.'),
('098e744f-0f25-5892-96d9-176cf83f7b7d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The dartos and cremaster muscles:', '["Store sperm","Produce sperm","Regulate scrotal temperature by moving the testes","Form the blood-testis barrier","Secrete hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Regulate scrotal temperature by moving the testes', 'Dartos (smooth muscle) wrinkles scrotum; cremaster (skeletal) elevates testes.'),
('c6c8eb24-a8ef-5478-bea2-21b54c96b39f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The seminal vesicle contributes what to semen?', '["Prostate-specific antigen mediated physiological mechanism","Fructose (energy for sperm), prostaglandins, and fibrinogen","Citrate and acid phosphatase mediated physiological mechanism","Alkaline fluid mediated physiological mechanism","Sperm mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose (energy for sperm), prostaglandins, and fibrinogen', 'Seminal vesicle fluid constitutes ~60% of ejaculate volume.'),
('7b76c80a-7842-5432-8112-abacfb39b4a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The prostate contributes what to semen?', '["Alkaline mucus mediated physiological mechanism","Fructose mediated physiological mechanism","Testosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Sperm mediated physiological mechanism","Citrate, acid phosphatase, PSA, zinc, and proteolytic enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Citrate, acid phosphatase, PSA, zinc, and proteolytic enzymes', 'Prostatic fluid constitutes ~30% of ejaculate; PSA liquefies semen.'),
('b91bd420-174f-545b-8423-886403a0941a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The bulbourethral (Cowper) glands secrete:', '["Testosterone","PSA","Fructose","Pre-ejaculate mucus","Sperm"]'::jsonb, 'Pre-ejaculate mucus', 'Cowper gland secretion is alkaline, protecting sperm from urethral acid.'),
('92f373cb-ec56-51d7-a2f1-91a5e354eb04'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Kallmann syndrome (congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism) is associated with:', '["Normal smell","Gynecomastia","Precocious puberty","Anosmia","Hyperosmia"]'::jsonb, 'Anosmia', 'Kallmann syndrome: failure of GnRH neurons to migrate from the olfactory placode leads to hypogonadism and anosmia.'),
('118fffda-4860-528a-b132-177bd97fafee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) causes:', '["Short stature mediated physiological mechanism","Precocious puberty mediated physiological mechanism","Hypogonadism, gynecomastia, tall stature, low testosterone","Accelerated development mediated physiological mechanism","Testosterone excess mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypogonadism, gynecomastia, tall stature, low testosterone', 'Klinefelter: extra X chromosome leads to primary hypogonadism, infertility, and gynecomastia.'),
('06c989a0-73f4-5d00-af2e-c3243755dfbb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) is due to:', '["Estrogen excess","Testosterone deficiency","5-alpha-reductase deficiency","LH receptor mutation","Loss-of-function mutation in the androgen receptor"]'::jsonb, 'Loss-of-function mutation in the androgen receptor', 'Complete AIS (46,XY): female external genitalia, no pubic hair, testes present, breast development.'),
('0c1f3fb4-279d-5172-b283-3f5fa8a83666'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', '5-alpha-reductase deficiency causes:', '["Gynecomastia","Precocious puberty","Infertility with normal male phenotype","Complete feminization","Ambiguous genitalia at birth, virilization at puberty"]'::jsonb, 'Ambiguous genitalia at birth, virilization at puberty', '5-alpha-reductase deficiency: DHT is absent, so external genitalia are undervirilized at birth.'),
('39ea9d5b-6632-5562-922b-9d0783529108'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Delayed puberty in boys is:', '["No voice change","No testicular enlargement by age 14","No growth spurt","No spermarche","No facial hair by 16"]'::jsonb, 'No testicular enlargement by age 14', 'Testicular volume <4 mL by age 14 indicates delayed puberty.'),
('3578805b-5f2b-5ba9-b64b-a1d1feba6f2a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Precocious puberty in boys is:', '["Onset before age 10","Rapid puberty","Onset before age 12","No puberty","Onset of puberty before age 9"]'::jsonb, 'Onset of puberty before age 9', 'Central precocious puberty is GnRH-dependent; peripheral is independent of GnRH.'),
('124a9c1b-9a03-58c0-8324-26d5e1c9bd86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Male infertility can be due to:', '["Only hormonal defects mediated physiological mechanism","Only obstruction mediated physiological mechanism","Only infection mediated physiological mechanism","Only low count mediated physiological mechanism","Low sperm count, poor motility, abnormal morphology, obstruction, hormonal defects, varicocele"]'::jsonb, 'Low sperm count, poor motility, abnormal morphology, obstruction, hormonal defects, varicocele', 'Male factor infertility accounts for ~40% of couple infertility.'),
('7f33fabf-1cfa-5319-9296-7c1862e6651b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Thelarche is:', '["Menarche","Adrenarche","Pubic hair development","Breast development","Growth spurt"]'::jsonb, 'Breast development', 'Thelarche (breast budding) is the first sign of female puberty, usually at age 8-13.'),
('ee93a224-4e9c-551e-afc0-abc77b0109c2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Menarche is:', '["Breast development","Growth spurt","Pubic hair","The first menstrual period","Ovarian maturation"]'::jsonb, 'The first menstrual period', 'Menarche occurs around age 12-13 in most populations.'),
('3e30420d-120e-51f8-8806-bbac5188dd1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The ovarian cycle includes:', '["Periovulatory only","Only follicular phase","Menstrual, proliferative, secretory","Only luteal phase","Follicular phase, ovulation, luteal phase"]'::jsonb, 'Follicular phase, ovulation, luteal phase', 'The ovarian cycle: follicular (days 1-14), ovulation (day 14), luteal (days 14-28).'),
('094cdd4e-aedf-548b-b705-d3f8200c8d36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The menstrual cycle includes:', '["Menstrual, proliferative, and secretory phases of the endometrium","Only proliferative mediated physiological mechanism","Only bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Desquamation only mediated physiological mechanism","Only secretory mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Menstrual, proliferative, and secretory phases of the endometrium', 'The endometrial cycle: menstrual (days 1-5), proliferative (days 6-14), secretory (days 15-28).'),
('a8afd9b5-7a1c-57e0-b7ea-d3d97e11c1b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The menstrual cycle is approximately:', '["28 days","40 days","21 days","14 days","35 days"]'::jsonb, '28 days', 'The average menstrual cycle is 28 days, but normal varies from 21-35 days.'),
('54158e95-00bb-5265-9dcb-83d2d976f72d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The follicular phase is characterized by:', '["Corpus luteum formation mediated physiological mechanism","Menstruation mediated physiological mechanism","Growth and development of ovarian follicles under FSH","Luteinization mediated physiological mechanism","Progesterone dominance mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Growth and development of ovarian follicles under FSH', 'FSH recruits a cohort of follicles; one dominant follicle emerges.'),
('98091100-b877-5444-9072-7296466a873f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Ovulation occurs when:', '["Estrogen peaks mediated physiological mechanism","FSH peaks mediated physiological mechanism","Progesterone peaks mediated physiological mechanism","LH surge triggers final oocyte maturation and follicle rupture","hCG peaks mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'LH surge triggers final oocyte maturation and follicle rupture', 'The mid-cycle LH surge is essential for ovulation; it is triggered by high estradiol.'),
('5ed147a3-4f9e-5cb2-a2e4-055bc3c7906c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The LH surge is caused by:', '["Inhibin mediated physiological mechanism","Positive feedback of high estradiol from the dominant follicle","Progesterone mediated physiological mechanism","FSH mediated physiological mechanism","GnRH only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Positive feedback of high estradiol from the dominant follicle', 'When estradiol reaches ~200 pg/mL for ~36 hours, it switches from negative to positive feedback on LH.'),
('cef2075a-5bc4-536c-b4dd-26fdac657fe8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The luteal phase is characterized by:', '["Estrogen dominance mediated physiological mechanism","Ovulation mediated physiological mechanism","Follicle growth mediated physiological mechanism","Menstruation mediated physiological mechanism","Corpus luteum formation and progesterone secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Corpus luteum formation and progesterone secretion', 'After ovulation, the follicle becomes the corpus luteum, secreting progesterone.'),
('5949e28d-f7b2-5536-b8ea-43cd1cbb0c21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, The corpus luteum secretes:', '["Only estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Only progesterone mediated physiological mechanism","LH mediated physiological mechanism","Progesterone, estradiol, and inhibin A","FSH mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Progesterone, estradiol, and inhibin A', 'The corpus luteum produces progesterone (dominant), estradiol, and inhibin to support potential pregnancy.'),
('3d70c5f3-c2c9-5c4f-b1e9-dbe246076b80'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The corpus luteum regresses if no pregnancy occurs after:', '["3 days","7 days","21 days","28 days","14 days"]'::jsonb, '14 days', 'The corpus luteum has a fixed lifespan of ~14 days unless rescued by hCG.'),
('65a844bd-7d5f-59ef-a45c-7e84725f98a9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Luteolysis is caused by:', '["Low estrogen","High FSH","High LH","Lack of hCG","PGF2-alpha"]'::jsonb, 'Lack of hCG', 'Without hCG from the embryo, the corpus luteum regresses and progesterone falls.'),
('9ba036ca-83bb-5ae8-8699-5e9570a67413'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Estrogens are produced by:', '["Only corpus luteum mediated physiological mechanism","Only placenta mediated physiological mechanism","Granulosa cells (follicle) and corpus luteum","Theca interna cells mediated physiological mechanism","Only adrenal mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Granulosa cells (follicle) and corpus luteum', 'Granulosa cells aromatize androstenedione to estradiol; the corpus luteum also secretes estrogens.'),
('5fe015c6-efcd-5684-b453-057a1ef61bf5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The two-cell theory of estrogen synthesis states:', '["Both cells make the same","Only theca makes estrogen","Only granulosa makes androgens","Theca interna produces androstenedione -> granulosa cells aromatize it to estradiol","Granulosa makes androgens -> theca makes estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Theca interna produces androstenedione -> granulosa cells aromatize it to estradiol', 'Theca interna (LH) synthesizes androstenedione; granulosa cells (FSH) aromatize it to estradiol.'),
('fa0ec6ff-53e6-5629-9ba1-318d9aaa8cb1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Estradiol is the most potent:', '["Mineralocorticoid","Androgen","Natural estrogen","Progestin","Corticosteroid"]'::jsonb, 'Natural estrogen', 'Estradiol (E2) is the main and most potent ovarian estrogen; estrone (E1) is weaker.'),
('5d2a34f1-a7a6-5a29-a650-7301e4c2443b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Estrogen receptors are:', '["Nuclear receptors","Membrane GPCR","Cytoplasmic only","Tyrosine kinase","Ion channel"]'::jsonb, 'Nuclear receptors', 'Estrogen binds nuclear ERs, regulating gene transcription, plus some rapid non-genomic effects.'),
('a4b8690c-1d9c-5295-bb02-f39033225250'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Estrogen effects include:', '["Development of female secondary sex characteristics, endometrial proliferation, bone protection","Lactation mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine relaxation mediated physiological mechanism","Breast milk production mediated physiological mechanism","Endometrial secretion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Development of female secondary sex characteristics, endometrial proliferation, bone protection', 'Estrogen promotes growth of breasts, uterus, vagina; maintains bone density; and has metabolic effects.'),
('65d39a85-7bd1-5f72-8738-16ea1c2db34f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Estrogen causes:', '["Menstruation mediated physiological mechanism","Decidualization mediated physiological mechanism","Vasoconstriction mediated physiological mechanism","Proliferation of the endometrium","Secretory changes mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Proliferation of the endometrium', 'Estrogen stimulates endometrial gland and stromal cell proliferation after menstruation.'),
('f844ce58-4c16-5af5-9611-1466840b37f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Progesterone is produced mainly by:', '["Granulosa cells","Corpus luteum","Adrenal","Follicle","Theca cells"]'::jsonb, 'Corpus luteum', 'Progesterone is the main hormone of the luteal phase and pregnancy.'),
('74fe7048-7bd5-5879-a2f3-73913caa51b7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Progesterone effects include:', '["Cervical mucus thinning mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine contraction mediated physiological mechanism","Endometrial proliferation mediated physiological mechanism","Bone resorption mediated physiological mechanism","Secretory transformation of endometrium, decreased myometrial contractility, thermogenic"]'::jsonb, 'Secretory transformation of endometrium, decreased myometrial contractility, thermogenic', 'Progesterone prepares the endometrium for implantation, maintains pregnancy, and raises basal body temperature.'),
('5ca813ea-9714-56c1-9e58-bb40e9cd4e56'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Progesterone causes basal body temperature to rise by:', '["No change","0.5C","Decrease","2C","1.5C"]'::jsonb, '0.5C', 'Progesterone raises BBT by ~0.5C due to its thermogenic effect.'),
('4bed0582-e5f9-5f71-b8f0-295ee2e7b77d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Oogenesis begins:', '["At menopause","At ovulation","Before birth","After fertilization","At puberty"]'::jsonb, 'Before birth', 'Oogonia enter meiosis I in the fetal ovary and arrest at the diplotene stage of prophase I as primary oocytes.'),
('a5c81d41-370f-5c4a-9c90-8e2de73264ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Oogonia differentiate into:', '["Polar bodies","Ova","Primary oocytes","Granulosa cells","Secondary oocytes"]'::jsonb, 'Primary oocytes', 'All oogonia become primary oocytes arrested in prophase I by the time of birth.'),
('de01250f-0130-5856-a2a0-0e9a951e7287'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'At birth, the female has approximately:', '["1-2 million primary oocytes","10 million","100 million","100,000","500,000"]'::jsonb, '1-2 million primary oocytes', 'At birth, there are ~1-2 million oocytes; this declines to ~300,000-500,000 at puberty.'),
('307881ee-6b8b-5b5e-b649-179a6a5980b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'At menopause, the ovary contains:', '["Thousands of oocytes","Few to no remaining oocytes","Millions of oocytes","Full ovarian reserve","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Few to no remaining oocytes', 'Ovarian reserve depletes with age; menopause occurs when few follicles remain.'),
('782cf8b9-8db5-5a43-9647-ec98f272af3c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The first meiotic division of the oocyte is completed:', '["At ovulation","At birth","During fetal development","At fertilization","At puberty"]'::jsonb, 'At ovulation', 'The LH surge triggers completion of meiosis I, producing a secondary oocyte and first polar body.'),
('b1a0d633-8681-5e8c-b833-07890cff3d1c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The second meiotic division is completed:', '["Before ovulation","At birth","At puberty","At ovulation","At fertilization"]'::jsonb, 'At fertilization', 'The secondary oocyte arrests at metaphase II until fertilization, which triggers completion.'),
('22dedbc2-ddf2-5730-a431-0d2183f5e518'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Menstruation is caused by:', '["Decrease in GnRH mediated physiological mechanism","LH surge mediated physiological mechanism","Increase in estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Increase in FSH mediated physiological mechanism","Sharp drop in progesterone and estrogen at the end of the luteal phase"]'::jsonb, 'Sharp drop in progesterone and estrogen at the end of the luteal phase', 'Withdrawal of hormonal support causes endometrial shedding.'),
('4d6c5de7-39a5-55bc-b71e-2feb108d5552'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The thick cervical mucus during the luteal phase is due to:', '["Estrogen","Oxytocin","LH","FSH","Progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Progesterone', 'Progesterone thickens cervical mucus, forming a plug that blocks sperm entry.'),
('505209b3-3545-55d1-975f-fdba0e049164'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Thin, watery, stretchy cervical mucus at ovulation is due to:', '["FSH","GnRH","Estrogen","LH","Progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen', 'High estrogen during the follicular phase makes cervical mucus thin and elastic (spinnbarkeit).'),
('28410274-e3b7-55b7-97da-fd76214ef97c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Menopause is defined as:', '["Absence of menstruation for 3 months","High FSH","Low estrogen","Irregular cycles","12 months of amenorrhea"]'::jsonb, '12 months of amenorrhea', 'Menopause is retrospectively diagnosed after 12 months without menses.'),
('bff5a48a-fef1-5736-95d8-9f59c210cd1b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Menopause is characterized by:', '["Normal hormones","Low estradiol, high FSH and LH","High progesterone","Low LH","High estradiol, low FSH"]'::jsonb, 'Low estradiol, high FSH and LH', 'Ovarian failure removes inhibin and estradiol feedback, markedly elevating gonadotropins.'),
('41110637-8430-5a27-b75e-938609d53106'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Perimenopause refers to:', '["The transition period before menopause with irregular cycles","Delayed puberty mediated physiological mechanism","Postmenopause mediated physiological mechanism","Premature ovarian failure mediated physiological mechanism","Primary amenorrhea mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'The transition period before menopause with irregular cycles', 'Perimenopause features irregular cycles, fluctuating hormones, and vasomotor symptoms.'),
('a6b8c851-2ae1-58ca-8447-83f7e4fee4c9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Hormone replacement therapy (HRT) for menopause uses:', '["Estrogen","FSH","Only estrogen","Testosterone","Only progestin"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen', 'Unopposed estrogen in a woman with a uterus increases endometrial cancer risk; progestin is protective.'),
('ed1ef229-47da-52a6-b8fd-5f393269a746'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) features:', '["Premature ovarian failure mediated physiological mechanism","Endometriosis mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoandrogenism mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine fibroids mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperandrogenism, anovulation, polycystic ovaries, insulin resistance"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperandrogenism, anovulation, polycystic ovaries, insulin resistance', 'PCOS: elevated LH/FSH ratio, increased ovarian androgen production, and metabolic syndrome.'),
('c95ef2b5-2c1a-5509-b070-76a3f2539e9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Premature ovarian failure (POF) is:', '["Ovarian failure before age 40","Pituitary disorder","Delayed puberty","Menopause at normal age","Hypothalamic amenorrhea"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian failure before age 40', 'POF causes amenorrhea, infertility, and estrogen deficiency in women <40.'),
('6d84e2fd-be91-5ca0-a9b2-8191a300145f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Turner syndrome (45,X) causes:', '["Trisomy mediated physiological mechanism","Klinefelter syndrome mediated physiological mechanism","Ovarian dysgenesis (streak gonads), short stature, webbed neck","Tall stature mediated physiological mechanism","Normal ovarian function mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian dysgenesis (streak gonads), short stature, webbed neck', 'Turner syndrome: monosomy X leads to gonadal dysgenesis with no estrogen production.'),
('894a8d33-931e-52e3-918a-030ae6215638'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Endometriosis is associated with:', '["Ectopic endometrial tissue outside the uterus","Normal endometrium mediated physiological mechanism","Ovarian cysts mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine fibroids mediated physiological mechanism","Cervical dysplasia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ectopic endometrial tissue outside the uterus', 'Endometriosis: endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterus, causing pain and infertility.'),
('8a42fdac-a896-50d1-b7b5-8f85083ac458'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Dysmenorrhea is:', '["Heavy menstruation","Infrequent menstruation","Painful menstruation","Absent menstruation","Irregular menstruation"]'::jsonb, 'Painful menstruation', 'Primary dysmenorrhea is due to excess prostaglandin F2-alpha causing uterine ischemia.'),
('73d6e479-6114-5d2d-a3e3-e1a2b655ec43'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Amenorrhea is classified as:', '["Primary (no menses by age 15) or secondary","Absence of ovulation mediated physiological mechanism","Irregular bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Heavy bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Painful periods mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Primary (no menses by age 15) or secondary', 'Causes include hypothalamic, pituitary, ovarian, uterine, and outflow tract disorders.'),
('86051e02-b0bc-5ae4-bb8a-2586430f33f2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Hypothalamic amenorrhea is caused by:', '["Pituitary tumor mediated physiological mechanism","Stress, weight loss, excessive exercise","Thyroid disease mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine adhesions mediated physiological mechanism","Ovarian failure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stress, weight loss, excessive exercise', 'Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea is reversible with lifestyle modification.'),
('e42df904-c019-5e8b-864d-36ca52851dfd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Primary ovarian insufficiency (POI) is:', '["Hypothalamic suppression","Constitutional delay","Uterine factor","Pituitary failure","Ovarian failure before age 40"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian failure before age 40', 'POI has autoimmune, genetic, and iatrogenic causes.'),
('d6fec3ce-0d65-5a2a-adad-1dbf08784699'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Asherman syndrome causes amenorrhea due to:', '["Cervical stenosis","Pituitary failure","Intrauterine adhesions","Ovarian failure","Hypothalamic suppression"]'::jsonb, 'Intrauterine adhesions', 'Asherman syndrome is treated by hysteroscopic adhesiolysis.'),
('a282a7e6-4439-5c85-8bff-b20fdde0fe37'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Mittelschmerz is:', '["Menstrual cramps mediated physiological mechanism","Ovarian torsion mediated physiological mechanism","Implantation pain mediated physiological mechanism","Ectopic pregnancy mediated physiological mechanism","Mid-cycle pain associated with ovulation"]'::jsonb, 'Mid-cycle pain associated with ovulation', 'Mittelschmerz is a benign condition; pain resolves within hours.'),
('d7b5a5a1-b394-5f20-a3f4-1dbca4d4bcff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spinnbarkeit refers to:', '["Vaginal discharge mediated physiological mechanism","Thick cervical mucus mediated physiological mechanism","Stretchy, elastic cervical mucus at ovulation","Endometrial shedding mediated physiological mechanism","Ovarian follicular fluid mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stretchy, elastic cervical mucus at ovulation', 'Spinnbarkeit is the ability of cervical mucus to stretch; it peaks at ovulation.'),
('5562ce1d-0365-53f8-97b1-d63001f49347'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Contraceptive vaginal rings release:', '["Ethinyl estradiol and etonogestrel","Only progestin mediated physiological mechanism","Levonorgestrel mediated physiological mechanism","Only estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Copper mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ethinyl estradiol and etonogestrel', 'The vaginal ring provides continuous hormone delivery for 3 weeks.'),
('d98e9baf-4e0e-5c7d-916f-8760fd157f44'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Delayed puberty in girls is:', '["No menses by 14 mediated physiological mechanism","No growth spurt mediated physiological mechanism","No pubic hair mediated physiological mechanism","Short stature mediated physiological mechanism","No breast development by age 13"]'::jsonb, 'No breast development by age 13', 'Thelarche by age 13 is the benchmark for female puberty onset.'),
('4ff8657e-172b-5be5-8b9f-62fa4a03cd0c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Precocious puberty in girls is:', '["Growth spurt before 9","All of the above","Breast development before age 8","Pubic hair before 9","Menarche before 10"]'::jsonb, 'Breast development before age 8', 'Central precocious puberty is more common in girls than boys.'),
('1a225202-a11c-5127-bb98-6e02a7257b5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The menstrual cycle is controlled by:', '["Only estrogen","HPG axis","Only LH","Only FSH","Only progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'HPG axis', 'The HPG axis coordinates follicular development, ovulation, and endometrial changes.'),
('c6df0aca-806c-53b9-889b-a76b17c1b61b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Estrogen positive feedback requires:', '["Sustained high estradiol levels","High FSH mediated physiological mechanism","Low estradiol mediated physiological mechanism","Progesterone mediated physiological mechanism","High inhibin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Sustained high estradiol levels', 'Positive feedback triggers the LH surge leading to ovulation.'),
('d492a526-2fe3-5c47-bfb7-571637e3c75d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The dominant follicle is selected during the:', '["Luteal phase","Menstrual phase","Late luteal phase","Follicular phase","Ovulation"]'::jsonb, 'Follicular phase', 'The dominant follicle has more FSH receptors and produces more estradiol.'),
('c56922f9-4783-527f-a3de-7e3aa9b88369'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Inhibin A is produced by:', '["Granulosa cells of small follicles","Corpus luteum and dominant follicle","Hypothalamus","Pituitary","Theca cells"]'::jsonb, 'Corpus luteum and dominant follicle', 'Inhibin A is high in the luteal phase; inhibin B is high in the follicular phase.'),
('7d5a0579-c895-5e1a-bbeb-79bdd35f8b3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Inhibin B is produced by:', '["Granulosa cells of early antral follicles","Hypothalamus mediated physiological mechanism","Pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Corpus luteum mediated physiological mechanism","Theca cells mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Granulosa cells of early antral follicles', 'Inhibin B reflects ovarian reserve and is used in fertility assessment.'),
('37b6516a-f7d8-532e-93ee-5a570eb55068'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) is produced by:', '["Granulosa cells of preantral and small antral follicles","Pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Theca cells mediated physiological mechanism","Hypothalamus mediated physiological mechanism","Corpus luteum mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Granulosa cells of preantral and small antral follicles', 'AMH reflects the remaining primordial follicle pool and declines with age.'),
('5fad0f84-8c81-5215-aee1-7898a1bf9e71'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The luteal phase is fixed at:', '["21 days","3 days","7 days","28 days","14 days"]'::jsonb, '14 days', 'The luteal phase duration is relatively constant; variability in cycle length is due to the follicular phase.'),
('b920a5fd-6429-5a59-8c4f-2acf1a5364ae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Leutinizing hormone (LH) stimulates which ovarian cells?', '["Granulosa cells","Luteal cells","Stromal cells","Oocytes","Theca interna cells"]'::jsonb, 'Theca interna cells', 'LH acts on theca cells via cAMP to produce androgen precursors for estrogen synthesis.'),
('2f9e4ad7-ee96-5502-a50e-5c6c837d80f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) stimulates which ovarian cells?', '["Oocytes","Granulosa cells","Luteal cells","Stromal cells","Theca interna cells"]'::jsonb, 'Granulosa cells', 'FSH receptors are exclusively on granulosa cells.'),
('bff47396-5e5b-557e-ac1a-a63bbf42d124'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'In a surgical context, Progesterone maintains pregnancy by:', '["Suppressing uterine contractility and supporting the endometrium","Stimulating contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing cervical dilation mediated physiological mechanism","Thinning the endometrium mediated physiological mechanism","Decreasing blood flow mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Suppressing uterine contractility and supporting the endometrium', 'Progesterone inhibits myometrial contractions and maintains decidual integrity.'),
('bdf78427-9694-5cab-8276-cae31f74a64f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Estrogen during the menstrual cycle is lowest during:', '["Early follicular phase","Ovulation","Late follicular phase","Luteal phase","Mid-luteal phase"]'::jsonb, 'Early follicular phase', 'Estrogen is at its nadir during menses and rises as the follicle develops.'),
('9c294307-63b4-5757-affb-3dfa1f593728'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Progesterone is highest during the:', '["Ovulation","Mid-luteal phase","Menstruation","Early follicular phase","Follicular phase"]'::jsonb, 'Mid-luteal phase', 'Progesterone peaks during the mid-luteal phase if ovulation occurred.'),
('75eb505c-6e8b-59d4-beba-c8eae69b2bf6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Fertilization typically occurs in the:', '["Cervix","Ampulla of the fallopian tube","Uterine cavity","Ovary","Fimbriae"]'::jsonb, 'Ampulla of the fallopian tube', 'Sperm and oocyte meet in the ampulla of the uterine tube.'),
('27e4d2ea-d979-57c2-96d3-b522b2e1bcb7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The zona pellucida is:', '["The ovarian capsule mediated physiological mechanism","The uterine lining mediated physiological mechanism","A layer of granulosa cells mediated physiological mechanism","The outer layer of sperm mediated physiological mechanism","A glycoprotein layer surrounding the oocyte that mediates sperm binding"]'::jsonb, 'A glycoprotein layer surrounding the oocyte that mediates sperm binding', 'ZP1, ZP2, ZP3 are glycoproteins; ZP3 is the sperm receptor.'),
('dec926fd-ca04-557f-bfcf-be36056b81ed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The cortical reaction after fertilization:', '["Initiates labor","Causes endometrial thickening","Prevents polyspermy by modifying the zona pellucida","Triggers ovulation","Stimulates sperm motility"]'::jsonb, 'Prevents polyspermy by modifying the zona pellucida', 'Cortical granules release enzymes that harden the zona pellucida, blocking additional sperm entry.'),
('80cc9416-8b46-5ae5-8603-dd0b9e831c51'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Implantation occurs at:', '["~6-7 days after fertilization","~3 days","Immediately after fertilization","~14 days","~10 days"]'::jsonb, '~6-7 days after fertilization', 'The blastocyst implants into the endometrium ~6-7 days after fertilization.'),
('6bd215a3-4829-5dba-bc8b-94a98426af25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The trophoblast develops into:', '["Placenta","Amnion","Embryo","Yolk sac","Umbilical cord"]'::jsonb, 'Placenta', 'Trophoblast cells form the outer layer of the blastocyst and give rise to the placenta.'),
('4453510b-db9c-558b-8367-1ad3129d0cb7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The morula stage is reached when:', '["Gastrulation begins","The blastocyst forms","Implantation occurs","There are ~16-32 cells","The zygote divides"]'::jsonb, 'There are ~16-32 cells', 'The morula enters the uterus and develops into a blastocyst.'),
('b8888399-e59e-5159-b4e3-8cbf15eea295'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The blastocyst consists of:', '["Only inner cell mass mediated physiological mechanism","Inner cell mass (embryoblast) and outer trophoblast","Solid ball of cells mediated physiological mechanism","Two identical cells mediated physiological mechanism","Only trophoblast mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Inner cell mass (embryoblast) and outer trophoblast', 'The inner cell mass becomes the embryo; trophoblast becomes the placenta.'),
('84b0c8b0-d077-56bd-abd0-8350ac23d3fe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The endometrium must undergo decidualization for implantation:', '["Stromal cells enlarge and become secretory under progesterone influence","Endometrial shedding mediated physiological mechanism","Vascular regression mediated physiological mechanism","Proliferative changes mediated physiological mechanism","Glandular atrophy mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stromal cells enlarge and become secretory under progesterone influence', 'Decidualization provides a supportive environment for the implanting blastocyst.'),
('f55947b4-8c65-5a99-9611-1e270b15202a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The zona pellucida must be shed before implantation (hatching):', '["To allow the blastocyst to attach to the endometrium","To allow fertilization mediated physiological mechanism","To activate sperm mediated physiological mechanism","To prevent polyspermy mediated physiological mechanism","To trigger meiosis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'To allow the blastocyst to attach to the endometrium', 'Hatching allows the trophoblast to directly contact the endometrial epithelium.'),
('ec3c545a-01ab-5f7c-8f9b-dd03a8ec04cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Implantation is completed by:', '["Day 7","Day 14","Day 10-12 after fertilization","Day 3","Day 5"]'::jsonb, 'Day 10-12 after fertilization', 'By day 12, the implantation site is covered by regenerating endometrial epithelium.'),
('8dc2bef2-5e0e-534c-8067-5145f1f04a77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The decidual reaction involves:', '["Vascular thrombosis mediated physiological mechanism","Stromal fibrosis mediated physiological mechanism","Enlargement of endometrial stromal cells, increased vascularity, and secretion of growth factors","Endometrial atrophy mediated physiological mechanism","Glandular hyperplasia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Enlargement of endometrial stromal cells, increased vascularity, and secretion of growth factors', 'The decidua supports the embryo and regulates trophoblast invasion.'),
('863a5c86-88fc-5c9c-ba41-71fbfb6e5371'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is first detectable:', '["~8-10 days after fertilization","Immediately after fertilization","At the first missed period","At delivery","At ovulation"]'::jsonb, '~8-10 days after fertilization', 'hCG is secreted by syncytiotrophoblast and is the basis for pregnancy tests.'),
('50c28905-f943-5340-b591-2263ce7c882e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Ectopic pregnancy most commonly occurs in:', '["Fallopian tube","Cervix","Abdominal cavity","Ovary","Uterine cornua"]'::jsonb, 'Fallopian tube', 'Ectopic pregnancy is a medical emergency; risk factors include PID, smoking, and prior tubal surgery.'),
