('8a84359a-4964-56ac-9b07-aaf0985a271b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Maternal physiological changes in pregnancy include:', '["Increased insulin sensitivity mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased cardiac output mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased GFR mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased blood volume mediated physiological mechanism","Increased blood volume, cardiac output, GFR, and insulin resistance"]'::jsonb, 'Increased blood volume, cardiac output, GFR, and insulin resistance', 'Maternal adaptations support fetal growth; insulin resistance ensures glucose supply to the fetus.'),
('14442dcf-ec54-53bd-a9de-dc77fd7cdfc0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Maternal blood volume increases by:', '["10%","~40-50%","20%","5%","100%"]'::jsonb, '~40-50%', 'The disproportionate increase in plasma volume dilutes hemoglobin (physiologic anemia of pregnancy).'),
('94e7aed4-d29c-544c-ab2c-4e6d41da8c44'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Maternal cardiac output increases by:', '["10%","~30-40%","100%","50%","5%"]'::jsonb, '~30-40%', 'Cardiac output peaks at 20-24 weeks and remains elevated until delivery.'),
