('52789df6-f421-500b-ab5c-9e0cbfc71dd5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Fetal breathing movements:', '["Begin in utero (~10 weeks) and are important for lung development","Begin at birth mediated physiological mechanism","Begin in the third trimester mediated physiological mechanism","Are absent in utero mediated physiological mechanism","Begin during labor mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Begin in utero (~10 weeks) and are important for lung development', 'Fetal breathing movements help develop the respiratory muscles and lungs.'),
('af9360ce-ab8b-5771-8326-cc10bc47f024'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Maternal weight gain in pregnancy is distributed to:', '["Maternal fat only mediated physiological mechanism","Amiiotic fluid only mediated physiological mechanism","Fetus only mediated physiological mechanism","Placenta only mediated physiological mechanism","Fetus, placenta, amniotic fluid, uterus, breasts, blood volume, and maternal fat stores"]'::jsonb, 'Fetus, placenta, amniotic fluid, uterus, breasts, blood volume, and maternal fat stores', 'Recommended total weight gain depends on pre-pregnancy BMI.'),
('5fa002d7-e29f-57b6-a51a-9cdc5d5710dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Nausea and vomiting of pregnancy (morning sickness) is associated with:', '["High progesterone","Fetal growth","Low estrogen","Rising hCG and estrogen levels","Low hCG"]'::jsonb, 'Rising hCG and estrogen levels', 'Hyperemesis gravidarum is a severe form requiring medical intervention.'),
('bfaf79e0-31f8-55d2-ac71-756a2b2c6ed3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Supine hypotensive syndrome in pregnancy is due to:', '["Uterine contraction mediated physiological mechanism","Fetal distress mediated physiological mechanism","Maternal dehydration mediated physiological mechanism","Compression of the inferior vena cava by the gravid uterus","Placental insufficiency mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Compression of the inferior vena cava by the gravid uterus', 'Pregnant women should avoid lying flat; left lateral position improves venous return.'),
('d611c908-1468-5afc-8dcd-e6473af0161e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Respiratory changes in pregnancy include:', '["No change mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased tidal volume mediated physiological mechanism","Increased tidal volume and minute ventilation (chronic respiratory alkalosis compensated by renal","Decreased oxygen consumption mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased respiratory rate mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased tidal volume and minute ventilation (chronic respiratory alkalosis compensated by renal', 'Progesterone increases respiratory sensitivity to CO2, causing mild hyperventilation.'),
('74519431-d740-5aa1-9bd9-b17d72b22147'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Parturition (labor) is initiated by:', '["Complex interplay: fetal cortisol rise, progesterone withdrawal, oxytocin, prostaglandins","hCG surge mediated physiological mechanism","Progesterone rise only mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen drop only mediated physiological mechanism","Oxytocin surge only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Complex interplay: fetal cortisol rise, progesterone withdrawal, oxytocin, prostaglandins', 'Labor involves fetal cortisol activation, functional progesterone withdrawal, increased oxytocin and PGs.'),
('f5fecc47-4b4a-5d3f-8f32-27c7d233c612'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Fetal cortisol in late gestation:', '["Inhibits labor mediated physiological mechanism","Causes growth mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulates estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Promotes fetal lung maturation and triggers the labor cascade","Decreases surfactant mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes fetal lung maturation and triggers the labor cascade', 'Rising fetal cortisol induces surfactant production and placental CRH release, contributing to labor onset.'),
('66378a74-2256-5dce-8aa0-15ca872211f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Oxytocin stimulates uterine contractions by:', '["Decreasing prostaglandins mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking Ca2+ channels mediated physiological mechanism","Activating cAMP mediated physiological mechanism","Relaxing myometrium mediated physiological mechanism","Activating GPCR (Gq) on myometrium -> increased intracellular Ca2+"]'::jsonb, 'Activating GPCR (Gq) on myometrium -> increased intracellular Ca2+', 'Oxytocin binds to its receptor on myometrial cells, activating the IP3 pathway and Ca2+ release.'),
('a164df6f-fa0e-5e4c-a672-3cd3e71f1c9b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Oxytocin is used clinically to:', '["Induce abortion mediated physiological mechanism","Stop labor mediated physiological mechanism","Induce or augment labor and prevent postpartum hemorrhage","Increase milk production mediated physiological mechanism","Treat preeclampsia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Induce or augment labor and prevent postpartum hemorrhage', 'Oxytocin is given IV for labor induction/augmentation and IM for postpartum hemorrhage.'),
('df49c995-834a-5f4a-b8d5-b1348e1f6b45'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Prostaglandins (PGE2, PGF2-alpha) in labor:', '["Inhibit contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Prevent labor mediated physiological mechanism","Promote cervical ripening and uterine contractions","Ripen cervix only mediated physiological mechanism","Relax myometrium mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Promote cervical ripening and uterine contractions', 'PGE2 and PGF2-alpha soften the cervix (ripening) and increase myometrial contractility.'),
('20b60646-56d9-59b0-b2e9-dc9ac775cd7b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Functional progesterone withdrawal at term involves:', '["Decreased progesterone levels mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen withdrawal mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased progesterone receptor activity and increased estrogen/oxytocin sensitivity","No change mediated physiological mechanism","Increased progesterone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased progesterone receptor activity and increased estrogen/oxytocin sensitivity', 'Progesterone blocks contractions; its functional withdrawal allows labor to proceed.'),
('c0457818-f778-5c6b-a03b-92c045360e1a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Stages of labor:', '["Stage 1 (cervical dilation), Stage 2 (fetal delivery), Stage 3","Stage 3 only mediated physiological mechanism","Stage 1 only mediated physiological mechanism","Stage 1 and 2 mediated physiological mechanism","Stage 2 only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stage 1 (cervical dilation), Stage 2 (fetal delivery), Stage 3', 'Stage 1 is the longest (latent + active phases); Stage 3 involves placental expulsion.'),
('96676489-efcd-59bd-a5ef-9ae09b5eab9d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The Ferguson reflex is:', '["Prolactin release mediated physiological mechanism","Fetal descent mediated physiological mechanism","Oxytocin release stimulated by cervical distention","Milk ejection reflex mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine relaxation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin release stimulated by cervical distention', 'Stretching of the cervix triggers more oxytocin release, strengthening contractions.'),
('10b3d63a-bcd7-52ff-8721-1e51a4ad6258'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'CRH from the placenta increases in late pregnancy and:', '["Stimulates fetal cortisol production and contributes to labor timing","Inhibits labor mediated physiological mechanism","Reduces oxytocin mediated physiological mechanism","Suppresses contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Decreases prostaglandins mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates fetal cortisol production and contributes to labor timing', 'Placental CRH acts as a clock that determines the timing of parturition.'),
('f14bd9cc-9f70-5702-8eaf-aad4383eb4e8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Uterine contractions during labor are coordinated by:', '["Nerve impulses mediated physiological mechanism","Calcium channels mediated physiological mechanism","Oxytocin alone mediated physiological mechanism","Gap junctions (connexin-43) between myometrial cells","Prostaglandins alone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Gap junctions (connexin-43) between myometrial cells', 'Gap junction expression increases at term, allowing synchronized contractions.'),
('7b1eed50-cd27-5bcf-b849-76404b9932d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Preterm labor is defined as:', '["Labor after 42 weeks","Labor at 38 weeks","Labor before 37 weeks of gestation","Labor at 42 weeks","Labor at 40 weeks"]'::jsonb, 'Labor before 37 weeks of gestation', 'Preterm labor is the leading cause of neonatal morbidity and mortality.'),
('fcb48f35-d8b3-55db-b861-452a0700af11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Post-term pregnancy is:', '["Pregnancy at 38 weeks mediated physiological mechanism","Pregnancy at 41 weeks mediated physiological mechanism","Pregnancy lasting beyond 42 weeks of gestation","Pregnancy at 37 weeks mediated physiological mechanism","Pregnancy at 40 weeks mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Pregnancy lasting beyond 42 weeks of gestation', 'Post-term pregnancy carries risks of placental insufficiency and macrosomia.'),
('8ffe2f76-32fd-52d8-989b-9aa2d7b6bbf0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Cervical ripening involves:', '["Cervical tightening mediated physiological mechanism","Remodeling of cervical collagen by matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) and increased hydration","Collagen deposition mediated physiological mechanism","Smooth muscle hypertrophy mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased blood flow mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Remodeling of cervical collagen by matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) and increased hydration', 'Prostaglandins and relaxin promote cervical softening and effacement.'),
('7e6e1c4f-415c-54a5-b0bf-c620cf0f8a4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The amniotic sac ruptures during labor due to:', '["Increased intrauterine pressure and enzymatic weakening of membranes","Maternal effort mediated physiological mechanism","Spontaneous rupture mediated physiological mechanism","Fetal movement mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine contractions alone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intrauterine pressure and enzymatic weakening of membranes', 'Premature rupture of membranes (PROM) occurs before labor onset.'),
('06364839-11fc-5e17-9bce-72a65e96548a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Meconium-stained amniotic fluid indicates:', '["Normal finding","Fetal distress","Placental abruption","Maternal infection","Preterm labor"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal distress', 'Meconium passage can cause meconium aspiration syndrome in the newborn.'),
('fbfa3647-2f00-50d4-907e-d599c7c85519'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Involution of the uterus after delivery is:', '["Uterine growth mediated physiological mechanism","Endometrial shedding mediated physiological mechanism","Return of the uterus to its pre-pregnancy size","Placental separation mediated physiological mechanism","Cervical dilation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Return of the uterus to its pre-pregnancy size', 'Breastfeeding stimulates oxytocin, which aids uterine involution.'),
('71bae7c8-ea84-5b66-8310-e3dc6172267f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Lochia is:', '["Menstrual blood","Postpartum vaginal discharge","Cervical mucus","Amiiotic fluid","Vaginal secretions"]'::jsonb, 'Postpartum vaginal discharge', 'Lochia rubra (days 1-4), lochia serosa (days 5-9), lochia alba (days 10-14+).'),
('8df35981-ff1c-5d1c-972f-00c3f9c26c78'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Postpartum hemorrhage is defined as:', '["Blood loss >1500 mL mediated physiological mechanism","Blood loss >300 mL mediated physiological mechanism","Blood loss >500 mL (vaginal) or >1000 mL (cesarean) after delivery","Blood loss <500 mL mediated physiological mechanism","Blood loss >200 mL mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Blood loss >500 mL (vaginal) or >1000 mL (cesarean) after delivery', 'Common causes: uterine atony, retained placenta, lacerations, coagulopathy.'),
('09c2bfc6-9a52-51e8-9e42-902fe82054ff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Uterine atony is the most common cause of:', '["Uterine rupture","Postpartum hemorrhage","Preeclampsia","Placental abruption","Preterm labor"]'::jsonb, 'Postpartum hemorrhage', 'Oxytocin, ergometrine, and misoprostol are used to treat uterine atony.'),
('d0b379b9-e4f3-5e8d-81e3-afd068dcc110'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Placental separation occurs via:', '["Fetal expulsion mediated physiological mechanism","Enzymatic digestion mediated physiological mechanism","Active extraction mediated physiological mechanism","Shearing of the placenta from the uterine wall in the third stage of labor","Spontaneous absorption mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Shearing of the placenta from the uterine wall in the third stage of labor', 'Signs of placental separation include a gush of blood and lengthening of the cord.'),
('3e70bcb0-58c7-5880-a696-8f395e7bed24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Striae gravidarum (stretch marks) are caused by:', '["Mechanical stretching and hormonal changes (cortisol, estrogen) affecting collagen","Excessive weight gain only mediated physiological mechanism","Infection mediated physiological mechanism","Nutritional deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Dehydration mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Mechanical stretching and hormonal changes (cortisol, estrogen) affecting collagen', 'Striae regress after delivery but may not completely disappear.'),
('bb853f9f-76a6-5d88-8f32-9f4acb0b29fb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Linea nigra is:', '["Hernia mediated physiological mechanism","Pathologic finding mediated physiological mechanism","Surgical scar mediated physiological mechanism","Stretch mark mediated physiological mechanism","Darkened midline abdominal pigmentation in pregnancy"]'::jsonb, 'Darkened midline abdominal pigmentation in pregnancy', 'Linea nigra is a common physiologic change of pregnancy.'),
('8e13fc56-6b50-5270-88af-4f7dd814a6a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Chadwick sign is:', '["Cervical dilation mediated physiological mechanism","Bluish discoloration of the cervix/vagina due to increased vascularity in pregnancy","Uterine contraction mediated physiological mechanism","Vaginal bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Cervical erosion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Bluish discoloration of the cervix/vagina due to increased vascularity in pregnancy', 'Chadwick sign is an early sign of pregnancy.'),
('61151bb7-d89b-528f-a5fc-3cff5a098c5e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Hegar sign is:', '["Fetal movement mediated physiological mechanism","Cervical softening mediated physiological mechanism","Breast tenderness mediated physiological mechanism","Softening of the lower uterine segment in early pregnancy","Uterine enlargement mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Softening of the lower uterine segment in early pregnancy', 'Hegar sign is detectable at 6-8 weeks of gestation.'),
('c01f5524-c826-502b-816a-e86ed0d90aff'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Goodell sign is:', '["Cervical bluish discoloration","Vaginal discoloration","Softening of the cervix in early pregnancy","Breast changes","Uterine softening"]'::jsonb, 'Softening of the cervix in early pregnancy', 'Goodell sign is an early pregnancy sign due to increased vascularity and edema.'),
('b5842a00-0a74-59b6-a1d8-532ff0f42a25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Fundal height in pregnancy correlates with:', '["Gestational age (weeks) after 20 weeks","Maternal weight mediated physiological mechanism","Fetal weight mediated physiological mechanism","Fetal length mediated physiological mechanism","Amiiotic fluid volume mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Gestational age (weeks) after 20 weeks', 'Fundal height should match gestational age within 2 cm.'),
('40d08f09-8f39-56cb-898b-0ddeb8e557b2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Lightening refers to:', '["Cervical dilation mediated physiological mechanism","Rupture of membranes mediated physiological mechanism","Uterine contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Placental separation mediated physiological mechanism","Descent of the fetal head into the pelvis (engagement) before labor"]'::jsonb, 'Descent of the fetal head into the pelvis (engagement) before labor', 'Lightening occurs 2-4 weeks before term in nulliparous women.'),
('8c863465-e2d6-5d77-ae03-e4481a3f7771'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Braxton Hicks contractions are:', '["Placental contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Preterm contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Irregular, painless uterine contractions during pregnancy","Uterine atony mediated physiological mechanism","True labor contractions mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Irregular, painless uterine contractions during pregnancy', 'Braxton Hicks contractions do not cause cervical change.'),
('34586f73-c494-54b1-8ab2-d97246563454'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'True labor is distinguished by:', '["Irregular contractions mediated physiological mechanism","Vaginal bleeding mediated physiological mechanism","Pain only mediated physiological mechanism","Back pain mediated physiological mechanism","Regular contractions causing progressive cervical dilation and effacement"]'::jsonb, 'Regular contractions causing progressive cervical dilation and effacement', 'True labor: contractions increase in frequency, duration, and intensity.'),
('045761c4-e87e-592d-afde-edaebb50770c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Postpartum blues are:', '["Postpartum psychosis mediated physiological mechanism","Anxiety disorder mediated physiological mechanism","Major depression mediated physiological mechanism","Bipolar disorder mediated physiological mechanism","Mild mood changes (crying, irritability) in the first week after delivery"]'::jsonb, 'Mild mood changes (crying, irritability) in the first week after delivery', 'Postpartum depression is more severe and requires treatment.'),
('177612ab-fd8c-5f2a-bc0c-720e65c5ae0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Prolactin is secreted by:', '["Lactotrophs of the anterior pituitary","Posterior pituitary mediated physiological mechanism","Placenta mediated physiological mechanism","Hypothalamus mediated physiological mechanism","Mammary glands mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lactotrophs of the anterior pituitary', 'Prolactin is secreted by anterior pituitary lactotrophs.'),
('ce7a0458-4f15-57c2-88b2-647825534c3f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Prolactin secretion is tonically inhibited by:', '["Progesterone","Estrogen","TRH","Dopamine","Somatostatin"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine', 'Dopamine (PIF) from the hypothalamus maintains tonic inhibition of prolactin.'),
('033983cb-4108-537e-8f40-eb3fc1e010b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Suckling stimulates prolactin release via:', '["Inhibition of dopamine (disinhibition) and TRH stimulation","Estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Oxytocin mediated physiological mechanism","Direct nerve stimulation mediated physiological mechanism","Progesterone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of dopamine (disinhibition) and TRH stimulation', 'Suckling signals reduce dopamine release and increase PRH/TRH, leading to prolactin surge.'),
('61ac516e-057d-5aec-bdb1-aaaa6cdabd48'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The milk ejection reflex is mediated by:', '["hCG","Progesterone","Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin', 'Oxytocin released from the posterior pituitary causes milk ejection from the breasts.'),
('f2eafdcc-3771-5b5a-886a-4e2243e1e291'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Prolactin is essential for:', '["Milk ejection","Ovulation","Cervical ripening","Uterine contraction","Milk production"]'::jsonb, 'Milk production', 'Prolactin stimulates mammary gland alveoli to synthesize milk.'),
('3f975bb5-fa11-5e32-9ea4-9aac5de119f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Colostrum is:', '["The first milk","Transitional milk","The last milk","Mature milk","Casein-rich milk"]'::jsonb, 'The first milk', 'Colostrum is produced in the first 2-3 days after birth, rich in secretory IgA and immune factors.'),
('92ad4463-0abe-578f-957e-957004a2bdac'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Lactation inhibits ovulation by:', '["Decreasing prolactin","Increasing estradiol","Suppressing GnRH","Increasing LH","Increasing FSH"]'::jsonb, 'Suppressing GnRH', 'High prolactin during breastfeeding suppresses GnRH, reducing gonadotropins and inhibiting ovulation.'),
('de04cff2-539e-56b8-a9ac-86300bdca961'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Mammogenesis (breast development) is stimulated by:', '["Estrogen (ductal growth), progesterone (alveolar growth), GH, cortisol, and prolactin","Only progesterone mediated physiological mechanism","Only GH mediated physiological mechanism","Only estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Only prolactin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Estrogen (ductal growth), progesterone (alveolar growth), GH, cortisol, and prolactin', 'Full mammary development requires multiple hormones during puberty and pregnancy.'),
('91ba8008-3670-571a-8669-ee35e31ec735'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Lactogenesis (initiation of milk secretion) requires:', '["High estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","High progesterone mediated physiological mechanism","High prolactin and low progesterone","Low prolactin mediated physiological mechanism","High cortisol mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'High prolactin and low progesterone', 'Progesterone inhibits milk secretion during pregnancy; its withdrawal allows lactogenesis.'),
('b7ef5924-286b-538e-9997-9162d2889e8b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Galactopoiesis (maintenance of lactation) depends on:', '["High estrogen","High progesterone","High cortisol","Low prolactin","Regular suckling"]'::jsonb, 'Regular suckling', 'Without suckling, lactation ceases within 1-2 weeks.'),
('9d508fbe-608a-56bc-a732-5b9c0ed0b677'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Milk composition changes from foremilk to hindmilk:', '["Hindmilk is low-fat mediated physiological mechanism","Foremilk is watery and low-fat; hindmilk is high-fat","Both are identical mediated physiological mechanism","Foremilk is high-fat mediated physiological mechanism","Foremilk is high mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Foremilk is watery and low-fat; hindmilk is high-fat', 'Infants should feed long enough to receive the calorie-rich hindmilk.'),
('2cfeefd3-2f91-5715-84c7-e096a6c95e53'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Human milk contains:', '["No proteins mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Lactose, whey proteins (alpha-lactalbumin, lactoferrin, IgA), lipids, vitamins, and minerals","Only fats mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Only water mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","No carbohydrates mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose, whey proteins (alpha-lactalbumin, lactoferrin, IgA), lipids, vitamins, and minerals', 'Human milk is uniquely suited for infant nutrition and immunity.'),
('8c6ff6e1-802d-5bec-ad3a-d07dd595a335'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Secretory IgA in breast milk provides:', '["No immune protection","Vaccination","Allergy protection only","Passive immunity to the infant","Active immunity"]'::jsonb, 'Passive immunity to the infant', 'sIgA coats the infant''s intestinal mucosa, preventing pathogen attachment.'),
('8d745fb6-67c0-5c2b-8fbe-01fa0891a513'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Lactoferrin in breast milk:', '["Is a hormone","Is a vitamin","Is an enzyme","Binds iron","Provides nutrition"]'::jsonb, 'Binds iron', 'Lactoferrin''s iron-binding capacity deprives bacteria of iron.'),
('0921c8a5-9f97-5670-8aa3-282176e17989'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The mammary gland is a:', '["Modified sweat gland","Lymphatic organ","Hormone-independent tissue","Endocrine gland","Exocrine gland only"]'::jsonb, 'Modified sweat gland', 'The mammary gland is a tubuloalveolar exocrine gland.'),
('2deb3eb2-5818-5179-bd90-f6ec1721e68d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Estrogen during pregnancy:', '["Stimulates ductal growth and branching in the mammary gland","Inhibits mammary growth mediated physiological mechanism","Causes milk ejection mediated physiological mechanism","Stimulates milk secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Suppresses prolactin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates ductal growth and branching in the mammary gland', 'Estrogen promotes ductal elongation and branching during pregnancy.'),
('2849dde5-05d4-55b4-9a0c-6b58fdc4a5cd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Progesterone during pregnancy:', '["Stimulates alveolar growth and lobular development in the mammary gland","Causes milk ejection mediated physiological mechanism","Suppresses prolactin mediated physiological mechanism","Promotes milk secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Inhibits mammary growth mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates alveolar growth and lobular development in the mammary gland', 'Progesterone promotes the formation of milk-secreting alveoli.'),
('d2648e03-eb8b-562c-af21-1c14d00145b4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The nipple and areola darken in pregnancy due to:', '["Increased oxytocin mediated physiological mechanism","Increased prolactin mediated physiological mechanism","Increased estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Increased melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH) from the pituitary","Increased progesterone mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH) from the pituitary', 'MSH levels rise in pregnancy, causing hyperpigmentation of the areolae.'),
('318c435c-61f5-54b4-b64b-0398dd20b230'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Breastfeeding is contraindicated in:', '["All maternal illnesses mediated physiological mechanism","Mild infections mediated physiological mechanism","C-section delivery mediated physiological mechanism","Active maternal tuberculosis (untreated), HIV, certain medications, and illicit drug use","Prematurity mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Active maternal tuberculosis (untreated), HIV, certain medications, and illicit drug use', 'Most conditions are compatible with breastfeeding; few are absolute contraindications.'),
('24bba0d2-871a-5304-af27-45c3ed015c34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The let-down reflex is:', '["Milk production mediated physiological mechanism","Lactation initiation mediated physiological mechanism","Breast engorgement mediated physiological mechanism","Nipple erection mediated physiological mechanism","The sensation of milk ejection triggered by oxytocin release"]'::jsonb, 'The sensation of milk ejection triggered by oxytocin release', 'The let-down reflex can be triggered by the baby''s cry or other stimuli.'),
('c4bd1786-d248-55f3-ba8d-97fedf90f4fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Mastitis is:', '["Nipple fissure","Inflammation of the breast tissue","Breast cancer","Milk engorgement","Fibrocystic changes"]'::jsonb, 'Inflammation of the breast tissue', 'Mastitis is treated with antibiotics and continued milk drainage.'),
('427e83c0-fcdb-5995-a30a-43bdc9714b5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Galactorrhea is:', '["Normal lactation mediated physiological mechanism","Excessive milk production mediated physiological mechanism","Blood in milk mediated physiological mechanism","Milk production not associated with breastfeeding","Infected breast milk mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Milk production not associated with breastfeeding', 'Causes include prolactinoma, medications, and hypothyroidism.'),
('91f7303e-0cd5-5ddc-a91b-0f0a52dbb7f5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Infant suckling stimulates:', '["Both prolactin (milk production) and oxytocin","Neither","Both estrogen and progesterone","Only prolactin","Only oxytocin"]'::jsonb, 'Both prolactin (milk production) and oxytocin', 'Suckling triggers two neuroendocrine reflexes for lactation.'),
('93934aff-c0b5-5270-bf2e-e32f2ecaf5a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Breast milk is the optimal nutrition for infants because:', '["It provides the ideal balance of nutrients, immune factors, and promotes bonding","It has high vitamin D content mediated physiological mechanism","It is sterile mediated physiological mechanism","It has high iron content mediated physiological mechanism","It prevents all diseases mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'It provides the ideal balance of nutrients, immune factors, and promotes bonding', 'WHO recommends exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months of life.'),
('291d5d06-110a-5965-9c3d-0c1df1b6d340'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Transitional milk follows colostrum at:', '["1 month","2 weeks","1 week","Immediately after birth","~2-5 days postpartum"]'::jsonb, '~2-5 days postpartum', 'Transitional milk gradually matures into mature milk by ~2 weeks.'),
('6eb6ac44-15fc-58b9-8973-2fd00c8e6f65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Mature breast milk composition:', '["High protein, low carbohydrate","High casein, low whey","Equal to cow''s milk","~90% water, 7% lactose, 3-5% fat, 1% protein","High fat, low lactose"]'::jsonb, '~90% water, 7% lactose, 3-5% fat, 1% protein', 'Human milk has higher whey-to-casein ratio than cow''s milk.'),
('467009b8-bc34-5009-9b9b-2a884295081b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Breast milk antibodies are most abundant in:', '["Hindmilk","Transitional milk","Colostrum","Foremilk","Mature milk"]'::jsonb, 'Colostrum', 'Colostrum has the highest concentration of antibodies and immune factors.'),
('368ff5c6-1cd1-5305-b2b6-d330e8bcfce7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Oxytocin release during breastfeeding can cause:', '["Hypotension","Uterine contractions (afterpains) and milk ejection","Diuresis","Inhibition of milk production","Uterine relaxation"]'::jsonb, 'Uterine contractions (afterpains) and milk ejection', 'Oxytocin causes both milk ejection and uterine involution.'),
('52f7daaf-50cd-5610-8a48-3a6d19ababae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Milk stasis and engorgement can lead to:', '["Breast cancer","Galactorrhea","Nipple necrosis","Mastitis","Milk fistula"]'::jsonb, 'Mastitis', 'Early treatment of engorgement prevents progression to mastitis.'),
('2bb426ed-fe80-5fb0-8892-7d1ffa3e5c3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Prolactin levels are highest:', '["During sleep and after suckling","Before feeding mediated physiological mechanism","After weaning mediated physiological mechanism","During the day mediated physiological mechanism","During pregnancy mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'During sleep and after suckling', 'Prolactin is secreted in pulses, with the highest levels during sleep.'),
('c155d33e-f8e5-5c4d-bd72-39e2ac38cd30'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Contraceptive methods include:', '["Combined oral contraceptives (estrogen + progestin), progestin-only, barrier, IUD, sterilization","Only natural methods mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Only condoms mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Only pills mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Only IUD mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Combined oral contraceptives (estrogen + progestin), progestin-only, barrier, IUD, sterilization', 'Many contraceptive methods work via ovulation inhibition, sperm block, or implantation prevention.'),
('879fc128-3f04-5239-9f94-d7c396ce84f3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Combined oral contraceptives (COCs) work by:', '["Weakening endometrium mediated physiological mechanism","Suppressing FSH and LH (negative feedback) preventing ovulation","Blocking implantation mediated physiological mechanism","Thickening cervical mucus only mediated physiological mechanism","Killing sperm mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Suppressing FSH and LH (negative feedback) preventing ovulation', 'Estrogen and progestin in COCs suppress gonadotropins, preventing follicular development and ovulation.'),
('fd12e864-aacd-5b23-b8cd-ff96940159aa'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Progestin-only pills (mini-pill) work by:', '["Thickening cervical mucus and thinning endometrium","Inducing menses","Increasing FSH","Blocking implantation","Suppressing ovulation always"]'::jsonb, 'Thickening cervical mucus and thinning endometrium', 'Progestin-only pills primarily work by preventing sperm penetration and altering endometrial development.'),
('96acb7e5-0bfe-5fc3-9e1e-071a645e45ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Emergency contraception (morning-after pill) works by:', '["Aborting an implanted pregnancy","Preventing fertilization only","Delaying or inhibiting ovulation","Inducing menstruation","Killing sperm"]'::jsonb, 'Delaying or inhibiting ovulation', 'Emergency contraception prevents or delays ovulation; it does not interrupt an established pregnancy.'),
('da59784d-abf7-5c71-8eea-e408ce35f6b9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Intrauterine devices (IUDs) work by:', '["Killing eggs mediated physiological mechanism","Suppressing ovulation mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking fallopian tubes mediated physiological mechanism","Creating a sterile inflammatory response toxic to sperm and preventing implantation","Hormonal ovulation suppression mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Creating a sterile inflammatory response toxic to sperm and preventing implantation', 'Copper IUDs cause local inflammation; levonorgestrel IUDs thicken mucus and thin endometrium.'),
('44df3f81-1497-5589-bb57-27f4ca8795ca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Contraceptive implants (Nexplanon) release:', '["GnRH mediated physiological mechanism","LH mediated physiological mechanism","FSH mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Etonogestrel (progestin) to suppress ovulation and thicken mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Etonogestrel (progestin) to suppress ovulation and thicken mucus', 'The etonogestrel implant provides highly effective progestin-only contraception for 3 years.'),
('1ad3b06f-94b6-58b0-937d-3bbcdc0c045d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Tubal ligation prevents pregnancy by:', '["Preventing implantation mediated physiological mechanism","Hormonal changes mediated physiological mechanism","Killing sperm mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking the fallopian tubes so sperm cannot reach the oocyte","Preventing ovulation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking the fallopian tubes so sperm cannot reach the oocyte', 'Tubal ligation mechanically interrupts the fallopian tubes.'),
('b98239f0-d1f8-5426-a33f-23bb45d254da'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Vasectomy prevents pregnancy by:', '["Preventing erection mediated physiological mechanism","Killing sperm mediated physiological mechanism","Decreasing testosterone mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking the vas deferens to prevent sperm in ejaculate","Preventing spermatogenesis mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking the vas deferens to prevent sperm in ejaculate', 'Vasectomy cuts/ties the vas deferens; sperm are still produced but cannot exit.'),
('30d79794-fb82-56e0-acd8-1f7ab863f15f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Barrier methods (condoms, diaphragm) work by:', '["Physically blocking sperm from reaching the oocyte","Preventing ovulation mediated physiological mechanism","Altering hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Killing sperm mediated physiological mechanism","Blocking implantation mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Physically blocking sperm from reaching the oocyte', 'Condoms also protect against sexually transmitted infections.'),
('ec98b34e-a83b-585e-a1b9-e9cb557e3886'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Spermicides (nonoxynol-9) work by:', '["Preventing fertilization","Blocking sperm motility","Damaging sperm cell membranes","Killing oocytes","Thickening mucus"]'::jsonb, 'Damaging sperm cell membranes', 'Spermicides are most effective when used with a barrier method.'),
('bc4a1af6-b840-5416-9982-73511bc4adc1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Natural family planning methods include:', '["None mediated physiological mechanism","Only temperature mediated physiological mechanism","Only calendar mediated physiological mechanism","Rhythm (calendar), basal body temperature, cervical mucus monitoring","Only mucus mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Rhythm (calendar), basal body temperature, cervical mucus monitoring', 'Fertility awareness-based methods require meticulous daily tracking.'),
('b7b243b4-fd2f-5a62-88ec-4416579efe13'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Lactational amenorrhea method (LAM) requires:', '["<6 months only mediated physiological mechanism","Amenorrhea only mediated physiological mechanism","Exclusive breastfeeding, amenorrhea, and <6 months postpartum","Mixed feeding mediated physiological mechanism","Any breastfeeding mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Exclusive breastfeeding, amenorrhea, and <6 months postpartum', 'LAM is effective only when all three criteria are met.'),
('16a25d68-cfd3-5c2d-a317-ac79860b5c5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The vaginal ring releases:', '["Levonorgestrel only","Estrogen only","Ethinyl estradiol and etonogestrel","Only progestin","Copper"]'::jsonb, 'Ethinyl estradiol and etonogestrel', 'The ring is left in place for 3 weeks, removed for 1 week for withdrawal bleed.'),
('72f95843-8333-5945-b000-30b1680d6c32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The contraceptive patch releases:', '["Progestin only mediated physiological mechanism","Copper mediated physiological mechanism","Ethinyl estradiol and norelgestromin","Levonorgestrel mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Ethinyl estradiol and norelgestromin', 'The patch is applied weekly for 3 weeks, followed by a patch-free week.'),
('c6a80056-75b7-57d3-8a22-1ef7cb54efe1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA) is:', '["Monthly injection","Annual implant","Weekly injection","Daily pill","An injectable progestin"]'::jsonb, 'An injectable progestin', 'DMPA suppresses ovulation and thickens cervical mucus.'),
('acf32334-163b-5430-8762-7c09b0da7ff7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Levonorgestrel IUD (Mirena) releases:', '["Estrogen","Levonorgestrel locally","Progesterone","Copper","GnRH"]'::jsonb, 'Levonorgestrel locally', 'The LNG-IUD provides effective contraception for 5-7 years.'),
('8675e63e-de64-54d5-a12a-79c3649f6b3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Copper IUD (Paragard) works for:', '["1 year","Permanent","5 years","Up to 10-12 years","3 years"]'::jsonb, 'Up to 10-12 years', 'Copper IUD is a non-hormonal, long-acting reversible contraceptive.'),
('71983c67-abe9-50b1-ac94-11a8d3a30f32'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Oral contraceptive pill formulations include:', '["Monophasic (fixed dose), biphasic, triphasic","Single hormone only mediated physiological mechanism","Only monophasic mediated physiological mechanism","Only triphasic mediated physiological mechanism","Fixed dose only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Monophasic (fixed dose), biphasic, triphasic', 'Multiphasic pills mimic the natural cycle and may reduce side effects.'),
('f0f99d43-5566-5128-a7ba-07fe49337c46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Contraindications to COCs include:', '["Mild obesity mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Age <40 mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","None mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Nulliparity mediated physiological mechanism mediated physiological mechanism","Smoking >35 years, hypertension, migraine with aura, DVT, liver disease, breast cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Smoking >35 years, hypertension, migraine with aura, DVT, liver disease, breast cancer', 'COCs increase thromboembolic risk and are contraindicated in certain conditions.'),
('0c2d0c42-78fa-532c-a2f2-a82fbcd369d3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'COC side effects include:', '["Only weight loss mediated physiological mechanism","Only nausea mediated physiological mechanism","Only improved mood mediated physiological mechanism","Nausea, breast tenderness, breakthrough bleeding, weight gain, mood changes, DVT risk","No side effects mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Nausea, breast tenderness, breakthrough bleeding, weight gain, mood changes, DVT risk', 'Most side effects resolve within 3 months of starting COCs.'),
('bc1489e1-37a0-5bea-92dc-0ef150c18ff0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'WHO medical eligibility criteria classify contraceptive methods by:', '["Cost only mediated physiological mechanism","Effectiveness only mediated physiological mechanism","Risk level (category 1-4) for various medical conditions","BMI only mediated physiological mechanism","Age only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Risk level (category 1-4) for various medical conditions', 'Category 1: no restriction; Category 4: unacceptable health risk.'),
('5ac0167d-db3a-5419-9a39-0575e9a1ccdd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The contraceptive effect of progestin-only implants is:', '["Same as condoms","Poorly effective","Moderately effective","Variable","Highly effective"]'::jsonb, 'Highly effective', 'Implants are among the most effective reversible contraceptives.'),
('e8b1610c-365c-5ca1-80ec-22d659add17a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The perfect-use failure rate of COCs is:', '[">10%","1-2%","15%","5%","<0.3% per year"]'::jsonb, '<0.3% per year', 'Perfect use requires consistent daily pill-taking at the same time.'),
('43b0ca1f-c075-5113-ad1a-a4a0db441f8e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Emergency contraception can be taken up to:', '["48 hours mediated physiological mechanism","1 week mediated physiological mechanism","120 hours (5 days) after unprotected intercourse","24 hours mediated physiological mechanism","72 hours mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, '120 hours (5 days) after unprotected intercourse', 'Ulipristal acetate is effective for up to 120 hours; levonorgestrel is most effective within 72 hours.'),
('75b3de58-c81a-5d24-b2c4-69b94cb4f050'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Ulipristal acetate (ella) is:', '["A progestin mediated physiological mechanism","A selective progesterone receptor modulator (SPRM) for emergency contraception","An estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","An IUD mediated physiological mechanism","A spermicide mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'A selective progesterone receptor modulator (SPRM) for emergency contraception', 'Ulipristal delays ovulation by blocking the progesterone receptor.'),
('89af5126-83ca-52a7-bf0b-e4244c600aa6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Sterilization is considered:', '["Irreversible in all cases","Temporary","Only for women","Permanent contraception","Reversible in all cases"]'::jsonb, 'Permanent contraception', 'Reversal requires microsurgery and is not always successful.'),
('4a0805a6-d955-513d-b1cc-3b8ced779483'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The diaphragm is a:', '["Implant","IUD","Barrier method","Hormonal method","Vaginal ring"]'::jsonb, 'Barrier method', 'The diaphragm must be fitted by a healthcare provider.'),
('b6a7510e-0429-523b-87a5-9a38735a070f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The cervical cap (FemCap) is:', '["A silicone barrier device that covers the cervix","A hormonal device mediated physiological mechanism","A sponge mediated physiological mechanism","A male condom mediated physiological mechanism","An IUD mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'A silicone barrier device that covers the cervix', 'The cervical cap can be left in place for up to 48 hours.'),
('3bedb0a6-4c2e-52c4-b9c9-8af2107ea42c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Contraceptive sponges contain:', '["Nonoxynol-9 (spermicide) and are inserted before intercourse","Hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Estrogen mediated physiological mechanism","Copper mediated physiological mechanism","Progestin mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Nonoxynol-9 (spermicide) and are inserted before intercourse', 'The sponge provides protection for up to 24 hours.'),
('918d8bbd-9920-5c71-a21f-efe7429af0b8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Withdrawal (coitus interruptus) has a typical failure rate of:', '["<1%","50%","~20-27% per year","5%","10%"]'::jsonb, '~20-27% per year', 'Withdrawal is less effective than other methods due to pre-ejaculate sperm and timing.'),
('ffaf5d44-a62d-5da8-b162-e4582a07f86f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Neonatal physiology: the first breath is triggered by:', '["Maternal hormones mediated physiological mechanism","Warmth mediated physiological mechanism","Initial cry mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, cold, and tactile stimuli","Cord clamping only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, cold, and tactile stimuli', 'At birth, clamping of the cord reduces O2 (hypoxia) and increases CO2 (acidosis), stimulating the respiratory center.'),
('d0863d81-de36-5f8a-a79e-96540d57ff49'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Surfactant is produced by:', '["Bronchial epithelial cells","Alveolar macrophages","Type I pneumocytes","Type II pneumocytes","Endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 'Type II pneumocytes', 'Surfactant (dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine) reduces surface tension and prevents alveolar collapse.'),
('fa25f280-9e43-516f-bcb6-2bc64d81eccf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Surfactant production begins at:', '["20 weeks","12 weeks","16 weeks","~24-28 weeks of gestation","Term"]'::jsonb, '~24-28 weeks of gestation', 'Surfactant production starts in the third trimester; deficiency causes neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (RDS).'),
('59027071-8aa3-5448-9a2e-497c34f9f83c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'After birth, the ductus arteriosus closes due to:', '["Increased blood pressure","Hormonal changes","Decreased heart rate","Decreased O2","Increased O2 and decreased prostaglandins"]'::jsonb, 'Increased O2 and decreased prostaglandins', 'Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) is more common in preterm infants and is treated with indomethacin.'),
('d2a56786-989c-5e1b-9a0b-6eaca2afcf16'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Neonatal jaundice is due to:', '["Bile duct obstruction","Increased bilirubin production","Hemolysis only","Immature hepatic glucuronyl transferase","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia"]'::jsonb, 'Immature hepatic glucuronyl transferase', 'Physiologic jaundice in newborns: liver cannot conjugate bilirubin as fast as it is produced.'),
('bf9da2e3-6946-5c46-9b5d-93d1b120ac17'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Brown adipose tissue (BAT) in neonates:', '["Generates ATP mediated physiological mechanism","Insulates mediated physiological mechanism","Stores fat mediated physiological mechanism","Produces energy mediated physiological mechanism","Generates heat by non-shivering thermogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Generates heat by non-shivering thermogenesis', 'BAT in newborns expresses thermogenin (UCP1) to uncouple oxidative phosphorylation for heat production.'),
('d8fa0e2f-4535-55b7-80c6-18cf820b0103'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Non-shivering thermogenesis is activated by:', '["Insulin","Epinephrine","Cortisol","Norepinephrine","Thyroid hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Norepinephrine', 'Cold exposure activates sympathetic nerves, releasing NE, which acts on beta-3 receptors in BAT.'),
('e7614828-f6dd-5532-aaaa-be08a0afdb18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The fetal heart rate is approximately:', '["120-160 bpm","100-120 bpm","80-100 bpm","60-80 bpm","160-200 bpm"]'::jsonb, '120-160 bpm', 'Normal fetal heart rate is 120-160 bpm, regulated by the autonomic nervous system.'),
('b4fd30c9-5148-530f-8e73-c1f8e9a9fe25'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Meconium is:', '["Mucus plug","Bilirubin","The first urine","The first stool","Amiiotic fluid"]'::jsonb, 'The first stool', 'Meconium is dark green/black and is normally passed in the first 24-48 hours after birth.'),
('9b4227b9-db4a-5c94-a917-20807ad8b8e6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'After birth, the circulatory changes include:', '["Increased pulmonary resistance mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased pulmonary blood flow mediated physiological mechanism","No changes mediated physiological mechanism","Closure of foramen ovale, ductus arteriosus, ductus venosus, and umbilical vessels","Opening of foramen ovale mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Closure of foramen ovale, ductus arteriosus, ductus venosus, and umbilical vessels', 'Birth causes dramatic circulatory changes: pulmonary expansion reduces resistance, increasing left atrial pressure.'),
('0a22aca8-77fa-57e9-991c-9204e6b18289'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN) causes:', '["Hyperoxia mediated physiological mechanism","No shunting mediated physiological mechanism","Left-to-right shunt mediated physiological mechanism","Increased oxygenation mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoxemia due to right-to-left shunt through foramen ovale/ductus arteriosus"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxemia due to right-to-left shunt through foramen ovale/ductus arteriosus', 'PPHN: failure of pulmonary vascular resistance to decrease, leading to shunt and severe hypoxemia.'),
('7c50fbe5-4e31-5631-a329-1a10333b6488'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The first breath requires:', '["Maternal effort mediated physiological mechanism","Umbilical cord clamping mediated physiological mechanism","High negative intrathoracic pressure to overcome surface tension","Mild pressure mediated physiological mechanism","No pressure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'High negative intrathoracic pressure to overcome surface tension', 'The newborn''s first breath must generate ~40-60 cm H2O negative pressure to inflate the lungs.'),
('94e3b172-7a75-5f09-ab69-0f5f2d8bcbf0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) is due to:', '["Congenital heart disease","Pneumonia","Diaphragmatic hernia","Meconium aspiration","Surfactant deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Surfactant deficiency', 'RDS is treated with exogenous surfactant and respiratory support.'),
('e7ee26be-d41a-53f2-a428-1c39314d6c6a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'APGAR score evaluates:', '["Maternal status mediated physiological mechanism","Fetal heart rate only mediated physiological mechanism","Newborn weight mediated physiological mechanism","Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration","Cord blood pH mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration', 'APGAR score <7 at 5 minutes indicates need for resuscitation.'),
('56410611-4a64-5153-a059-dc561662cd36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The normal APGAR score range is:', '["0-5 mediated physiological mechanism","7-10 (healthy), 4-6 (moderately depressed), 0-3","0-12 mediated physiological mechanism","0-15 mediated physiological mechanism","5-10 mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, '7-10 (healthy), 4-6 (moderately depressed), 0-3', 'APGAR assesses transition to extrauterine life.'),
('c29e841f-a0eb-5186-a6c7-fada4ad45292'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Umbilical cord clamping after birth:', '["Has no effect","Increases heart rate","Allows placental transfusion","Decreases lung function","Decreases blood volume"]'::jsonb, 'Allows placental transfusion', 'Delayed cord clamping (30-60 seconds) improves iron stores and hemodynamic stability.'),
('369b6938-9650-5953-9f50-1f5327205e23'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Neonatal hypothermia is dangerous because:', '["It increases metabolic rate and oxygen consumption","It protects the brain mediated physiological mechanism","It causes hyperglycemia mediated physiological mechanism","It decreases metabolic rate mediated physiological mechanism","It prevents infection mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'It increases metabolic rate and oxygen consumption', 'Newborns have a large surface-to-volume ratio and limited thermogenesis.'),
('b85ad36a-ae03-500b-9f5b-05f8b86d4226'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'The neonatal liver is immature in:', '["Glycogen storage","Fat metabolism","Protein synthesis","Conjugation of bilirubin","Production of clotting factors"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugation of bilirubin', 'Immature hepatic function contributes to physiologic jaundice.'),
('79e93e8e-7934-5154-864e-286dca95291b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Neonatal immunoglobulins:', '["IgD crosses the placenta mediated physiological mechanism","IgM crosses the placenta mediated physiological mechanism","IgG crosses the placenta (maternal passive immunity); IgA in breast milk","No immunoglobulins cross mediated physiological mechanism","IgE crosses the placenta mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'IgG crosses the placenta (maternal passive immunity); IgA in breast milk', 'The newborn relies on maternal IgG and breast milk IgA for immune protection.'),
('df511a40-cfde-5a9f-a05a-fda45c8d2531'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 2)', 'Reproduction', 'Vitamin K is given to newborns to:', '["Prevent jaundice","Improve bone growth","Prevent infection","Increase iron absorption","Prevent hemorrhagic disease"]'::jsonb, 'Prevent hemorrhagic disease', 'Newborns have sterile guts and low vitamin K-dependent clotting factors.'),
('f332e6e1-3ea2-5766-a309-5750e9b409d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During maximal exercise, cardiac output in a trained athlete can increase to approximately:', '["15 L/min","5 L/min","45 L/min","35 L/min","25 L/min"]'::jsonb, '35 L/min', 'Cardiac output = HR x SV. In trained athletes, CO can reach 35-40 L/min during maximal exercise due to both increased HR (up to ~200 bpm) and SV (up to 150-180 mL). Untrained individuals max out around 20-25 L/min.'),
('459d6f1e-b809-51b2-a86e-1908bee44215'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'VO2 max is BEST defined as:', '["Maximum rate of oxygen consumption during incremental exercise","Maximum oxygen content in arterial blood","Oxygen delivery to exercising muscles at rest","Maximum volume of oxygen extracted per breath","Volume of oxygen at the anaerobic threshold"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum rate of oxygen consumption during incremental exercise', 'VO2 max is the highest rate at which oxygen can be consumed during exhaustive, incremental exercise. It represents the integrated function of pulmonary, cardiovascular, and muscular systems to deliver and utilize oxygen.'),
('938fd42c-248b-5513-b867-dbf5acdbe9d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Fick principle equation for VO2 is:', '["VO2 = CO x","VO2 = Q x PaO2","VO2 = TPR x CO","VO2 = SV x HR","VO2 = HR x BP"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 = CO x', 'Fick principle: VO2 = Cardiac Output x (arterial O2 content - venous O2 content). This reflects that O2 consumption depends on both blood flow and oxygen extraction by tissues.'),
('2dfbe823-046c-56e7-944f-05fe96208fca'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is the PRIMARY determinant of stroke volume increase during exercise?', '["Increased heart rate","Increased preload","Decreased afterload","Increased myocardial contractility","Decreased venous return"]'::jsonb, 'Increased myocardial contractility', 'While all factors contribute, increased myocardial contractility via sympathetic stimulation and circulating catecholamines is the primary determinant. The Frank-Starling mechanism plays a greater role at lower exercise intensities.'),
('c64a7d2e-c9e6-5540-975b-df268ab6e09b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At rest, what percentage of cardiac output is directed to skeletal muscle?', '["5-10%","15-20%","40-50%","60-70%","80-90%"]'::jsonb, '15-20%', 'At rest, skeletal muscle receives about 15-20% of cardiac output (1-1.2 L/min). During maximal exercise, this increases to 80-85% of CO (up to 28-30 L/min).'),
('67b7faf2-81eb-5af1-be2b-4dc2015e23b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The coronary blood flow during exercise increases primarily due to:', '["Increased aortic pressure","Metabolic vasodilation","Parasympathetic activation","Sympathetic vasoconstriction","Decreased heart rate"]'::jsonb, 'Metabolic vasodilation', 'Coronary blood flow increases 4-5 fold during exercise. Metabolic vasodilation mediated by adenosine, NO, and other metabolites overrides sympathetic alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction, a phenomenon called ''functional sympatholysis''.'),
('755ed871-896f-53d3-afa5-3f1217d01e4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During dynamic exercise, mean arterial pressure typically:', '["Decreases significantly","Increases slightly","Remains unchanged","Increases > 60 mmHg","Decreases then increases"]'::jsonb, 'Increases slightly', 'Mean arterial pressure increases modestly by 10-40 mmHg during dynamic exercise due to increased cardiac output being partially offset by decreased TPR (from vasodilation in exercising muscles). Systolic BP rises more than diastolic.'),
('11d08d8e-b273-5d22-8402-a0650b0a3c36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The rapid increase in heart rate at the onset of exercise is mediated by:', '["Central command and vagal withdrawal","Chemoreceptor activation","Baroreceptor reflex","Increased circulating catecholamines","Bainbridge reflex"]'::jsonb, 'Central command and vagal withdrawal', 'The initial rapid HR increase within seconds of exercise onset is due to central command (parallel activation of motor and cardiovascular centers) causing vagal withdrawal. Circulating catecholamines contribute to the later gradual increase.'),
('d5b2d458-b533-56c8-9ae6-2f2ef8e85d2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which fiber type has the HIGHEST oxidative capacity and is most resistant to fatigue?', '["Type IIb","Type IIx","Type III","Type I","Type IIa"]'::jsonb, 'Type I', 'Type I fibers have high myoglobin content, numerous mitochondria, high oxidative enzyme activity, and are fatigue-resistant. They are recruited first during endurance activities.'),
('65b8ea2d-dd48-5eca-ad75-1766b997881c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the approximate VO2 max of a healthy, untrained 30-year-old male (mL/kg/min)?', '["35-45","25-30","15-20","55-65","70-80"]'::jsonb, '35-45', 'VO2 max in untrained healthy young males is approximately 35-45 mL/kg/min. Female values are about 10-20% lower. Elite endurance athletes can achieve 70-85 mL/kg/min.'),
('c760bac9-1258-534a-8921-3c78ffc2f494'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ATP-PCr system can provide maximal energy for approximately:', '["5-10 minutes","30-45 seconds","2-3 minutes","5-10 seconds","1-3 seconds"]'::jsonb, '5-10 seconds', 'The ATP-PCr system provides immediate energy for high-intensity, short-duration activities lasting about 5-10 seconds. PCr donates a phosphate to ADP via creatine kinase to regenerate ATP. Stores are depleted after ~10 seconds of maximal effort.'),
('4117e073-b26a-5464-b19c-cadec2f940e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Glycolysis (anaerobic) as the primary energy system operates most effectively during:', '["Marathon","400m dash","Shot put","10 km run","100m sprint"]'::jsonb, '400m dash', 'Anaerobic glycolysis becomes the primary energy source for maximal efforts lasting 30 seconds to 2 minutes. The 400m dash (~45-60 sec) relies heavily on glycolysis with lactate production. The 100m sprint uses ATP-PCr.'),
('b419b9d1-dbb9-5826-95c8-aa838c0d484b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis is:', '["Hexokinase","Lactate dehydrogenase","Aldolase","Phosphofructokinase-1","Pyruvate kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphofructokinase-1', 'PFK-1 is the key regulatory enzyme of glycolysis. It is activated by AMP, ADP, and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, and inhibited by ATP, citrate, and low pH (H+).'),
('a421ed33-a522-5cc7-9b7a-08e05cf1425f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the net ATP yield from one molecule of glucose via aerobic metabolism?', '["2 ATP","32-36 ATP","12 ATP","50 ATP","8 ATP"]'::jsonb, '32-36 ATP', 'Aerobic metabolism of glucose yields 32-36 ATP per glucose: 2 from glycolysis (substrate-level), 2 from TCA cycle (as GTP), and 28-32 from oxidative phosphorylation (NADH and FADH2).'),
('a989e25b-bc1f-5187-b480-e1b6d583a806'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The crossover concept in exercise physiology refers to:', '["The point of maximal lactate steady state","The shift from fat to carbohydrate as primary fuel with increasing exercise intensity","The point where protein becomes a fuel source","The transition from aerobic to anaerobic metabolism","The switch from slow to fast-twitch fibers"]'::jsonb, 'The shift from fat to carbohydrate as primary fuel with increasing exercise intensity', 'The crossover concept describes the shift from fat oxidation to carbohydrate utilization as exercise intensity increases. At low intensity (<50% VO2 max), fat is primary fuel. At high intensity (>75% VO2 max), carbohydrates dominate.'),
('8e0c2d38-5d94-5eb4-b214-7093ad6e957c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which substrate provides the GREATEST energy yield per gram?', '["Carbohydrate","Protein","Fat","Creatine phosphate","ATP"]'::jsonb, 'Fat', 'Fat yields 9 kcal/g, compared to 4 kcal/g for carbohydrates and protein. However, fat requires more oxygen per ATP produced, making it less efficient during high-intensity exercise.'),
('22f77e63-cb85-5dd4-a339-68446da79587'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Respiratory Exchange Ratio (RER) values >1.0 during exercise indicate:', '["Pure fat oxidation mediated physiological mechanism","Pure carbohydrate oxidation mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperventilation exceeding metabolic CO2 production","Protein catabolism mediated physiological mechanism","Ketone body utilization mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation exceeding metabolic CO2 production', 'RER = VCO2/VO2. Values >1.0 indicate that CO2 exhalation exceeds metabolic production, typically due to hyperventilation or bicarbonate buffering of lactic acid (H+ + HCO3- -> H2CO3 -> H2O + CO2).'),
('1f9f5d29-4476-533e-8b02-df19ec64fb94'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Lactate threshold typically occurs at what percentage of VO2 max in untrained individuals?', '["20-30%","70-80%","85-95%","50-60%","35-45%"]'::jsonb, '50-60%', 'In untrained individuals, lactate threshold occurs at approximately 50-60% of VO2 max. In endurance-trained athletes, this shifts to 70-80% of VO2 max, allowing higher exercise intensity before blood lactate accumulates.'),
('789eb62e-abbe-5e66-8f96-5e6750c7997d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'EPOC (Excess Post-exercise Oxygen Consumption) is also known as:', '["Anaerobic threshold","Oxygen deficit","Steady state","Oxygen debt","Second wind"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygen debt', 'EPOC, classically called ''oxygen debt,'' refers to the elevated oxygen consumption above resting levels after exercise. It represents the energy needed to restore ATP/PCr, clear lactate, replenish O2 stores, and return body temperature to normal.'),
('22745495-5fcb-543b-b4ed-a761b3ed03b6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is NOT a training-induced adaptation in skeletal muscle?', '["Increased glycogen storage","Increased myoglobin content","Decreased oxidative enzyme activity","Increased mitochondrial density","Increased capillary density"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased oxidative enzyme activity', 'Endurance training increases oxidative enzyme activity (e.g., citrate synthase, SDH), mitochondrial density, capillary density, myoglobin content, and glycogen stores. All these enhance aerobic capacity.'),
('b24c9df1-90e0-52fe-bf68-fe44ca3ac87c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Eccentric muscle contractions are characterized by:', '["Muscle shortening while generating force","Passive stretching","Isometric force generation","No change in muscle length","Muscle lengthening while generating force"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle lengthening while generating force', 'Eccentric contractions occur when the muscle is actively developing tension while lengthening (e.g., lowering a weight). They generate the greatest force but cause more muscle damage and delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS).'),
('e85f89fc-7ebb-57fa-85e0-1ee6237b9891'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Delayed Onset Muscle Soreness (DOMS) typically peaks at:', '["Immediately after exercise","2-4 hours post-exercise","24-48 hours post-exercise","5-7 days post-exercise","10-14 days post-exercise"]'::jsonb, '24-48 hours post-exercise', 'DOMS peaks 24-72 hours after unaccustomed or eccentric exercise. Proposed mechanisms include muscle fiber micro-tears, inflammation, and release of pain mediators like bradykinin and prostaglandins.'),
('077bd082-e198-542b-ad51-eaf1e4357dd9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Resistance training primarily causes which of the following muscle adaptations?', '["Increased strength","Muscle fiber hypertrophy","Increased muscle cross-sectional area","Muscle fiber hyperplasia","Increased myofibrillar protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle fiber hyperplasia', 'In humans, resistance training mainly causes muscle fiber hypertrophy (increased size) rather than hyperplasia (increased number). Hyperplasia has been demonstrated in animal studies but is controversial in humans. The ALL EXCEPT: all are true EXCEPT hyperplasia.'),
('6e29b04d-04d7-535a-a9ee-98e072888def'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The size principle of motor unit recruitment states that:', '["Large motor units are recruited before small ones","Smaller motor units are recruited before larger ones","Only slow-twitch units are recruited during exercise","All motor units are recruited simultaneously","Recruitment is random"]'::jsonb, 'Smaller motor units are recruited before larger ones', 'Henneman''s size principle: motor units are recruited in order of increasing size - Type I (small, low-threshold) first, then Type IIa, then Type IIx (largest, highest threshold). This ensures smooth force gradation.'),
('3999d9ec-a896-5848-88ba-5e7452b4ff1d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary fatigue mechanism during a sustained maximal voluntary contraction?', '["Central fatigue","Peripheral fatigue","Glycogen depletion","ATP depletion","Neuromuscular junction failure"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral fatigue', 'During sustained maximal contractions, peripheral fatigue due to accumulation of inorganic phosphate (Pi), H+, and reactive oxygen species impairs cross-bridge cycling and Ca2+ handling. Central fatigue plays a greater role in prolonged submaximal exercise.'),
('f722e88a-f75f-5f6c-952a-f3a26a866ba6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which ion is essential for excitation-contraction coupling in skeletal muscle?', '["Cl-","Mg2+","Ca2+","Na+","K+"]'::jsonb, 'Ca2+', 'Ca2+ is the key ion in E-C coupling. Action potential travels down T-tubules, causing DHP receptor (voltage sensor) to activate ryanodine receptors on SR, releasing Ca2+. Ca2+ binds troponin C, allowing cross-bridge formation.'),
('32e0308c-b03a-5475-bded-42d708d0258e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Training at high altitude to improve sea-level performance is known as:', '["High Intensity Interval Training","Fartlek training","Interval training","Live High - Train Low","Live High - Train High"]'::jsonb, 'Live High - Train Low', '''Live High-Train Low'' is the most effective strategy. Living at altitude (2500-3000m) stimulates EPO production, while training at sea level maintains training intensity and quality. ''Live High-Train High'' can reduce training intensity due to chronic hypoxia.'),
('52c8b3e0-c51b-52ee-a92c-50326ddd5bed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the normal oxygen consumption at rest (mL/kg/min)?', '["1.5-2","3.5-4","6-8","10-12","15-20"]'::jsonb, '3.5-4', 'Standard resting oxygen consumption (1 MET) is approximately 3.5 mL O2/kg/min. METs are used to quantify exercise intensity, with moderate exercise being 3-6 METs and vigorous >6 METs.'),
('f5ef0df0-7254-5c28-8fa9-d79ccf84afeb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which hormone shows the GREATEST increase during endurance exercise?', '["Insulin","Cortisol","Testosterone","Growth Hormone","Glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Growth Hormone', 'GH shows the largest relative increase (up to 10-20 fold) during exercise, driven by GHRH and suppressed by somatostatin. It promotes lipolysis, protein synthesis, and glucose conservation. Cortisol also rises but more gradually.'),
('27aed072-78a0-511f-a42c-bc010fb18290'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During exercise, minute ventilation (VE) can increase from ~6 L/min at rest to:', '["30-50 L/min","100-200 L/min","15-20 L/min","500+ L/min","250-400 L/min"]'::jsonb, '100-200 L/min', 'Minute ventilation at rest is ~6 L/min. During maximal exercise in trained individuals, VE can reach 100-200 L/min. Elite athletes may achieve 200+ L/min due to increased both tidal volume and respiratory rate.'),
('ab59746f-1205-5783-88d9-709f1e73d527'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ventilatory threshold (VT) corresponds to:', '["The point of oxygen desaturation","The point of maximal heart rate","The point where VE increases disproportionately to VCO2","The point of respiratory muscle fatigue","The point where VE increases linearly with workload"]'::jsonb, 'The point where VE increases disproportionately to VCO2', 'Ventilatory threshold occurs when VE increases disproportionately relative to VCO2, reflecting the need to buffer lactic acidosis. V-slope method identifies VT1 as the point where VCO2 increases more steeply than VO2.'),
('1692ec9b-3b8c-5e6e-928f-3065a3f87224'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During incremental exercise, the PRIMARY stimulus for increased ventilation at high intensity is:', '["Arterial hypoxemia","Peripheral chemoreceptor activation","Arterial hypercapnia","Increased H+ concentration","Central command"]'::jsonb, 'Increased H+ concentration', 'During light-moderate exercise, increased CO2 production stimulates ventilation. At higher intensities, lactic acidosis increases H+ concentration, which is sensed by peripheral and central chemoreceptors, driving further ventilatory increase.'),
('ee7b1800-dc73-5381-8e37-49f9dfa206f0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, The Haldane effect describes:', '["Increased O2 affinity of hemoglobin at high pH","Shift of O2 dissociation curve to the right during exercise","Enhanced CO2 loading in deoxygenated blood","Ventilation-perfusion mismatch","Increased 2,3-BPG production at altitude"]'::jsonb, 'Enhanced CO2 loading in deoxygenated blood', 'The Haldane effect: deoxygenated hemoglobin has greater capacity to carry CO2 (as carbamino compounds) and H+ (via imidazole groups). This facilitates CO2 transport from exercising tissues to lungs.'),
('ba687691-a058-5dc2-b29e-2a2fda3cfaec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the normal arterial PO2 during maximal exercise in a healthy individual?', '["60-70 mmHg","<60 mmHg","100-120 mmHg","80-100 mmHg",">120 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80-100 mmHg', 'In most healthy individuals, PaO2 remains near normal (80-100 mmHg) during exercise. However, some elite endurance athletes may develop exercise-induced hypoxemia (PaO2 < 75 mmHg) due to V/Q mismatch and diffusion limitation.'),
('021aef6a-4927-511b-8b38-423920d88c74'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Bohr effect refers to:', '["Increased hemoglobin O2 affinity at high altitude","Increased CO2 production during exercise","Decreased 2,3-BPG in stored blood","Ventilatory response to hypoxia","Rightward shift of O2-Hb dissociation curve with decreased pH"]'::jsonb, 'Rightward shift of O2-Hb dissociation curve with decreased pH', 'The Bohr effect: decreased pH (increased H+) and increased CO2 cause a rightward shift of the O2-hemoglobin dissociation curve, facilitating O2 unloading in tissues. This is crucial during exercise when metabolically active tissues produce more CO2 and lactic acid.'),
('b674363f-7226-5f71-9126-883b583f207e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is dead space ventilation during exercise?', '["Increases but as a smaller fraction of tidal volume","Increases proportionally to tidal volume","Decreases relative to total ventilation","Disappears entirely","Remains constant"]'::jsonb, 'Increases but as a smaller fraction of tidal volume', 'Dead space ventilation (VD) increases in absolute terms during exercise, but the VD/VT ratio decreases from ~0.3 at rest to ~0.15 during exercise. This is because tidal volume (VT) increases more than anatomical dead space.'),
('b2777ac9-9568-5249-90e6-833c66dc37ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During exercise in healthy lungs, which of the following occurs?', '["Increased alveolar-arterial gradient for O2","Decreased pulmonary blood flow","Decreased diffusion capacity","Decreased pulmonary capillary blood volume","Increased pulmonary artery pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pulmonary artery pressure', 'Pulmonary artery pressure increases during exercise due to increased cardiac output. This recruits and distends pulmonary capillaries, increasing diffusion capacity. V/Q matching improves during mild-moderate exercise.'),
('5b9abfb1-37f0-5341-aefb-a65f5964de4a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The oxygen cost of breathing at maximal exercise can reach what percentage of total VO2?', '["1-2%","5-7%","40-50%","10-15%","20-30%"]'::jsonb, '10-15%', 'At rest, respiratory muscles consume ~2% of VO2. During maximal exercise, this increases to 10-15% as the work of breathing increases exponentially at high ventilation rates.'),
('8483e6b6-1ccc-5821-9b8d-c00217cd1a33'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Compared to males, females have which of the following respiratory differences during exercise?', '["Smaller airway diameter and lower maximal flow rates","Greater FEV1/FVC ratio mediated physiological mechanism","Larger lung volumes mediated physiological mechanism","Higher diffusion capacity mediated physiological mechanism","Higher maximal VE mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Smaller airway diameter and lower maximal flow rates', 'Females have smaller lung volumes, smaller airway diameter, and lower maximal expiratory flow rates compared to males of similar height. This can contribute to higher work of breathing and exercise limitation.'),
('5cdf3cc8-3e57-50da-ab3f-a645660da50e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Onset of Blood Lactate Accumulation (OBLA) is typically defined as a blood lactate concentration of:', '["2 mmol/L","4 mmol/L","10 mmol/L","1 mmol/L","8 mmol/L"]'::jsonb, '4 mmol/L', 'OBLA is defined as the exercise intensity at which blood lactate reaches 4 mmol/L. This represents the balance between lactate production and clearance and is a marker of the lactate threshold.'),
('da9e1c64-bd3f-5803-b364-b98158c81201'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is the BEST explanation for the increase in blood lactate during high-intensity exercise?', '["Decreased liver blood flow reducing lactate clearance","Increased NADH/NAD+ ratio shifting pyruvate to lactate","Insufficient O2 delivery to mitochondria","Increased activity of lactate dehydrogenase in the heart","Decreased muscle buffering capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Insufficient O2 delivery to mitochondria', 'During high-intensity exercise, when O2 delivery to mitochondria is insufficient to meet ATP demand, the electron transport chain cannot keep up with NADH production. Pyruvate is converted to lactate to regenerate NAD+ for glycolysis to continue.'),
('a871a057-b17a-5be3-86ce-226b5937293a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The main buffering system for hydrogen ions (H+) produced during intense exercise is:', '["Protein buffers","Bicarbonate buffer system","Phosphate buffers","Ammonia buffer","Hemoglobin buffer"]'::jsonb, 'Bicarbonate buffer system', 'The bicarbonate buffering system is the primary extracellular buffer for H+ from lactic acid: H+ + HCO3- -> H2CO3 -> H2O + CO2. The CO2 is then excreted by the lungs, explaining the increased ventilation at the ventilatory threshold.'),
('48af991d-fc6c-50dd-94bf-0de65b9f87e5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'All of the following are true about the lactate shuttle EXCEPT:', '["Lactate can be converted back to glucose in the liver","Lactate can cross cell membranes via MCT transporters","Lactate can be used as fuel by Type I muscle fibers","Lactate can be used as a fuel by the heart","Lactate is exclusively a waste product"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate is exclusively a waste product', 'The lactate shuttle concept demonstrates that lactate is NOT a waste product. It serves as a metabolic substrate that can be oxidized by heart, brain, and Type I muscle fibers, and converted to glucose in the liver via the Cori cycle.'),
('d530d9d6-e2c0-521f-a35b-e44c6f6cf1c3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which transporter is responsible for lactate movement across cell membranes?', '["SGLT1","MCT1 and MCT4","GLUT4","LAT1","AQP1"]'::jsonb, 'MCT1 and MCT4', 'Monocarboxylate transporters (MCT1 and MCT4) facilitate lactate and H+ co-transport across cell membranes. MCT1 is more abundant in oxidative tissues (heart, Type I fibers), while MCT4 is found in glycolytic tissues.'),
('41fbeef7-efed-5add-bf91-dc438b14eeb1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'A 25-year-old athlete who can exercise at 85% of VO2 max without blood lactate accumulation has:', '["Impaired lactate clearance","Low lactate threshold","Mitochondrial myopathy","McArdle''s disease","High lactate threshold"]'::jsonb, 'High lactate threshold', 'Training shifts the lactate threshold to a higher percentage of VO2 max. Elite endurance athletes may have lactate thresholds at 80-90% of VO2 max, allowing them to sustain higher intensities without lactate accumulation.'),
('4fbbcfad-4965-57f5-8a39-dc0dc942e9bf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'McArdle''s disease (myophosphorylase deficiency) results in:', '["Inability to break down glycogen leading to early fatigue and muscle cramps","Uncontrolled muscle contraction","Increased lactate threshold","Excessive lactate production during exercise","Enhanced endurance performance"]'::jsonb, 'Inability to break down glycogen leading to early fatigue and muscle cramps', 'McArdle''s disease is a glycogen storage disease (type V) with deficiency of muscle phosphorylase. Patients cannot break down muscle glycogen, leading to exercise intolerance, muscle cramps, and second wind phenomenon. Serum CK rises and lactate does not increase during ischemic exercise.'),
('95af0362-857f-5683-8464-bb2040e0fe6d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The second wind phenomenon in McArdle''s disease is due to:', '["Increased delivery and utilization of blood-borne glucose and free fatty acids","Improved blood flow to muscles mediated physiological mechanism","Increased oxidative enzyme activity mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased muscle damage mediated physiological mechanism","Increased PCr stores mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased delivery and utilization of blood-borne glucose and free fatty acids', 'After about 5-10 minutes of exercise, patients with McArdle''s disease may experience a ''second wind'' as increased blood flow delivers more glucose (from liver) and free fatty acids, bypassing the block in glycogenolysis.'),
('9d02ec18-7d8d-55b7-9974-9b26b4120b9e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During exercise, the arteriovenous oxygen difference (a-vO2 diff) at maximal effort can increase from ~5 mL/dL at rest to:', '["25-30 mL/dL","20-22 mL/dL","16-18 mL/dL","12-15 mL/dL","8-10 mL/dL"]'::jsonb, '16-18 mL/dL', 'At rest, a-vO2 diff is ~5 mL O2/dL blood. During maximal exercise, oxygen extraction increases to ~15-18 mL/dL in trained individuals due to increased mitochondrial capacity and capillary density.'),
('c4e9f3e3-b36e-5869-9bab-3889fd81db4f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following best describes the ''anaerobic threshold''?', '["The ventilatory equivalent point","The workload above which blood lactate shows a sustained increase","The point of glycogen depletion","The respiratory compensation point","The point of maximal oxygen consumption"]'::jsonb, 'The workload above which blood lactate shows a sustained increase', 'Anaerobic threshold is the exercise intensity or workload above which blood lactate concentration increases systematically. It reflects the transition from predominantly aerobic to a mix of aerobic and anaerobic metabolism.'),
('ff8b49e9-c884-5813-9243-951bf1725e55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During exercise in a hot environment, the PRIMARY heat loss mechanism is:', '["Conduction","Radiation","Respiration","Convection","Evaporation"]'::jsonb, 'Evaporation', 'During exercise in heat, evaporation of sweat is the primary cooling mechanism. As ambient temperature approaches skin temperature, radiation and convection become ineffective. Evaporation of 1 L of sweat dissipates ~580 kcal of heat.'),
('ed063140-6204-5765-904e-6778768cbe7a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Heat acclimatization results in all of the following EXCEPT:', '["Decreased plasma volume","Decreased sodium concentration in sweat","Earlier onset of sweating","Reduced cardiovascular strain","Increased sweat rate"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased plasma volume', 'Heat acclimatization increases plasma volume (not decreases). It causes earlier and more profuse sweating, reduced sweat sodium concentration (due to aldosterone-mediated Na+ reabsorption), lower heart rate, and improved thermoregulation.'),
('a76386b1-2dcc-5ec3-b813-aff4a465a753'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The highest recorded core temperature compatible with survival during exercise is approximately:', '["39.0 C","40.5 C","42.5 C","44.0 C","45.5 C"]'::jsonb, '42.5 C', 'Core temperature >42.5 C is life-threatening and associated with heat stroke. At this temperature, protein denaturation begins, and multiorgan failure (liver, kidneys, CNS) can occur. Survival above 42.5 C is rare.'),
('46d1315e-c2fc-50ca-b13e-f42a7c53276d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Exertional heat stroke is characterized by core temperature above what value and CNS dysfunction?', '["41.0 C","38.0 C","39.0 C","42.0 C","40.0 C"]'::jsonb, '40.0 C', 'Exertional heat stroke is defined by core temperature >40 C (104 F) with CNS manifestations (confusion, seizures, coma). It is a medical emergency requiring immediate cooling (ice water immersion) and IV fluids.'),
('b1df07ea-cf55-5616-87e3-c82cc409819f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which hypothalamic nucleus contains the primary thermoregulatory center?', '["Arcuate nucleus","Preoptic/anterior hypothalamus","Paraventricular nucleus","Supraoptic nucleus","Ventromedial hypothalamus"]'::jsonb, 'Preoptic/anterior hypothalamus', 'The preoptic/anterior hypothalamus (PO/AH) contains heat-sensitive and cold-sensitive neurons that serve as the body''s thermostat. It coordinates heat loss and heat production mechanisms to maintain core temperature.'),
('39908a95-7558-524a-a4d4-cdc028816f3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is a risk factor for exertional heat illness?', '["High fitness level","Heat acclimatization","Obesity","Youth","Morning exercise"]'::jsonb, 'Obesity', 'Obesity is a risk factor for heat illness due to increased metabolic heat production, greater insulation, and lower body surface area-to-mass ratio.'),
('f5a0b16d-018c-59a5-9b2f-42ab2b246453'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The wet-bulb globe temperature (WBGT) is used to:', '["Measure core body temperature","Measure skin temperature","Calculate sweat loss","Determine wind chill factor","Assess environmental heat stress during exercise"]'::jsonb, 'Assess environmental heat stress during exercise', 'WBGT is the standard index for assessing environmental heat stress. It combines: natural wet-bulb temperature (humidity), globe temperature (radiant heat), and dry-bulb temperature (air temperature).'),
('ee554039-95ec-548c-a6fe-685c705b55d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary mechanism of heat loss during exercise in cold water (<20 C)?', '["Conduction","Evaporation","Radiation","Convection","Respiration"]'::jsonb, 'Convection', 'In cold water, convection is the primary heat loss mechanism. Water conducts heat 25 times faster than air. Convective heat loss (moving water/air over skin) is even greater.'),
('2c4a0ef6-999c-54b6-ad7a-2b8212bd4af2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Overtraining syndrome is characterized by all EXCEPT:', '["Increased resting heart rate","Improved mood and motivation","Decreased performance despite continued training","Increased sympathetic tone at rest","Increased susceptibility to infections"]'::jsonb, 'Improved mood and motivation', 'Overtraining syndrome causes decreased performance, persistent fatigue, mood disturbances (not improvement), increased resting HR, altered hormone levels, and increased infection risk.'),
('d0386def-c346-5535-82d3-4a411a8f1b36'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is an objective marker of overtraining?', '["Increased testosterone/cortisol ratio","Decreased resting cortisol level","Increased performance","Decreased morning heart rate variability","Increased VO2 max"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased morning heart rate variability', 'Decreased HRV (heart rate variability) is a marker of overtraining, reflecting autonomic dysfunction. Other markers: increased resting HR, decreased testosterone/cortisol ratio, altered catecholamine responses.'),
('4fc2846a-dc1d-5850-b950-4b070b003aa1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Caffeine as an ergogenic aid works primarily by:', '["Enhancing oxygen delivery","Increasing muscle glycogen","Blocking adenosine receptors","Increasing hemoglobin mass","Increasing PCr stores"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking adenosine receptors', 'Caffeine is an adenosine receptor antagonist. By blocking A1 and A2 receptors, it reduces perception of effort (RPE), increases alertness, and may enhance fat oxidation.'),
('1a8aad7a-bfb6-52ed-8c5c-17ac02f6e64d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is a banned substance by WADA that acts as an erythropoiesis-stimulating agent?', '["Creatine monohydrate","Bicarbonate loading","Recombinant human EPO","Caffeine","Beta-alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Recombinant human EPO', 'rHuEPO is a WADA-prohibited substance that increases RBC mass and hemoglobin concentration, enhancing O2-carrying capacity. It carries risks of polycythemia, hypertension, and thrombosis.'),
('ca9cceb5-fe88-5bec-aba1-81d68b652bf5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Creatine monohydrate supplementation primarily benefits which type of exercise?', '["Marathon running mediated physiological mechanism","High-intensity, short-duration repeated sprints","Ultra-endurance cycling mediated physiological mechanism","Yoga and flexibility mediated physiological mechanism","Long slow distance running mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'High-intensity, short-duration repeated sprints', 'Creatine supplementation increases muscle PCr stores by 10-30%, enhancing ATP regeneration during maximal efforts lasting 5-15 seconds. It benefits repeated sprints, weightlifting, and HIIT but not endurance performance.'),
('bb6c37eb-6a5c-5b01-98ad-cf68d6b0a13b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Blood doping (autologous transfusion) increases VO2 max primarily by:', '["Decreasing blood viscosity mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing cardiac output mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing hemoglobin concentration and O2-carrying capacity","Increasing plasma volume mediated physiological mechanism","Enhancing lung diffusion mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing hemoglobin concentration and O2-carrying capacity', 'Autologous blood transfusion increases RBC mass and hemoglobin, raising arterial O2 content. For every 1 g/dL increase in hemoglobin, VO2 max increases approximately 1%.'),
('ac56fa77-d887-55d3-878c-f0ce42861e9b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Bicarbonate loading (sodium bicarbonate ingestion) as an ergogenic aid works by:', '["Improving oxygen delivery","Increasing PCr stores","Enhancing extracellular buffering capacity","Reducing heat production","Increasing glycogen storage"]'::jsonb, 'Enhancing extracellular buffering capacity', 'Bicarbonate loading increases plasma HCO3- concentration, enhancing extracellular buffering of H+ from lactic acid. This can delay fatigue in high-intensity exercise lasting 1-7 minutes.'),
('e5a621ce-e37c-582d-be4c-5a4a799cd60a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following supplements has the STRONGEST evidence for improving endurance performance?', '["Nitrate","Phosphate loading","Creatine","Beta-alanine","Carnitine"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrate', 'Dietary nitrate is reduced to nitrite by oral bacteria, then to NO in the body. NO reduces the O2 cost of exercise, improves mitochondrial efficiency, and enhances blood flow.'),
('6848458f-0c7d-510b-9984-ff7cf413f1f7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The recommended daily protein intake for an endurance athlete in g/kg body weight is:', '["0.4-0.6 g/kg","0.8-1.0 g/kg","1.2-1.6 g/kg","2.2-2.8 g/kg","3.5-4.0 g/kg"]'::jsonb, '1.2-1.6 g/kg', 'Endurance athletes require 1.2-1.6 g/kg/day of protein (vs. 0.8 g/kg for sedentary). Strength athletes may need 1.6-2.2 g/kg.'),
('a85a987b-d32d-55a0-afa0-db7c50f40e35'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Carbohydrate loading (glycogen supercompensation) is most beneficial for:', '["Weight lifting","10-second maximal effort","100m sprint","Flexibility training","Events lasting >90 minutes"]'::jsonb, 'Events lasting >90 minutes', 'Carbohydrate loading maximizes muscle glycogen stores (from ~15 g/kg to ~30-35 g/kg), primarily benefiting endurance events >90 minutes where glycogen depletion can limit performance.'),
('4662c38a-a27e-588d-93e4-5605be1c7c77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Karvonen formula is used to calculate:', '["VO2 max","Body mass index","Target heart rate for exercise","Lactate threshold","Caloric expenditure"]'::jsonb, 'Target heart rate for exercise', 'Karvonen formula: Target HR = (HRmax - HRrest) x intensity% + HRrest. It accounts for resting HR to determine exercise intensity zones. HRmax = 220 - age.'),
('15d3c422-5e99-5a2f-b6f6-b125638b65f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'According to ACSM guidelines, moderate-intensity aerobic exercise corresponds to:', '["40-59% of HR reserve","60-79% of HR reserve","30-39% of HR reserve","80-90% of HR reserve",">90% of HR reserve"]'::jsonb, '40-59% of HR reserve', 'ACSM defines moderate intensity as 40-59% of HR reserve (or 64-76% of HRmax). Vigorous intensity is 60-89% of HR reserve (77-95% HRmax).'),
('b536c8a1-37d6-53f7-b25b-4cb13921a5cb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The minimal weekly aerobic exercise recommendation for health by ACSM is:', '["20 minutes of moderate exercise, 5 days/week","300 minutes of moderate exercise per week","60 minutes of vigorous exercise daily","150 minutes of moderate or 75 minutes of vigorous exercise per week","30 minutes of moderate exercise, 2 days/week"]'::jsonb, '150 minutes of moderate or 75 minutes of vigorous exercise per week', 'ACSM/AHA guidelines: 150 min/week of moderate-intensity OR 75 min/week of vigorous-intensity aerobic exercise, or a combination, in episodes >=10 minutes.'),
('ae29ba74-61dc-5d7b-8840-0973abaa46cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Borg''s Rating of Perceived Exertion (RPE) scale ranges from:', '["1-100","6-20","0-15","0-5","1-10"]'::jsonb, '6-20', 'The Borg RPE scale ranges from 6 (no exertion at all) to 20 (maximal exertion). It correlates well with heart rate (RPE x 10 = HR in bpm for middle-aged adults).'),
('c7cc3103-1cc2-5d31-b97b-db1a73ef8fc7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'All of the following are contraindications to exercise stress testing EXCEPT:', '["Uncontrolled hypertension","Unstable angina","Symptomatic severe aortic stenosis","Acute myocardial infarction","Type 2 diabetes mellitus"]'::jsonb, 'Type 2 diabetes mellitus', 'Contraindications to exercise testing: acute MI, unstable angina, uncontrolled arrhythmias, severe symptomatic aortic stenosis, uncontrolled hypertension. Stable T2DM is NOT a contraindication.'),
('344594bb-6b76-5d68-aa17-14d567676672'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary energy source during the first 5-10 minutes of moderate-intensity exercise?', '["Plasma free fatty acids","Blood glucose","Muscle glycogen","Intramuscular triglycerides","Amino acids"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle glycogen', 'Muscle glycogen is the primary fuel during the early stages of moderate exercise. As duration increases beyond 20-30 minutes, blood glucose and free fatty acids become increasingly important.'),
('ccfa2186-0fb2-5345-9bec-bb824d6aa4a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which energy system provides the majority of ATP during a 30-second all-out cycling sprint?', '["ATP-PCr system","Beta-oxidation of fatty acids","Oxidative phosphorylation","Anaerobic glycolysis","Protein catabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Anaerobic glycolysis', 'During a 30-second all-out effort (Wingate test), approximately 70% of ATP comes from anaerobic glycolysis and 30% from PCr and aerobic metabolism.'),
('6582ebd8-0383-559b-a54c-97700490ab57'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'A physically active 70-year-old individual has what approximate HRmax (using 208 - 0.7 x age)?', '["163 bpm","159 bpm","150 bpm","170 bpm","180 bpm"]'::jsonb, '159 bpm', 'Using HRmax = 208 - (0.7 x age): 208 - 49 = 159 bpm. This formula is more accurate than 220 - age, especially for older adults.'),
('1ec37bd3-0146-5430-ba6e-738126e68814'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following hormonal responses is seen during endurance exercise?', '["Decreased both insulin and glucagon","Increased both insulin and glucagon","No change in either hormone","Increased insulin, decreased glucagon","Decreased insulin, increased glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased insulin, increased glucagon', 'During exercise, insulin secretion decreases (to prevent hypoglycemia and promote FFA mobilization) while glucagon increases (to stimulate hepatic glucose production).'),
('550f1047-a306-53cc-adcf-30bfa5971176'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The immediate source of ATP for muscle contraction is:', '["Glucose","Glycogen","Creatine phosphate","Fatty acids","Free ATP stored in muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Free ATP stored in muscle', 'ATP is the immediate energy source for cross-bridge cycling. Muscle stores only 20-30 mmol/kg of ATP (enough for ~3 seconds of maximal work). PCr rapidly regenerates ATP.'),
('caaa456b-162e-5239-a198-80f3cb7529e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During prolonged exercise, what is the major source of plasma glucose?', '["Dietary carbohydrate absorption","Glycogenolysis","Gluconeogenesis","Glycogenolysis","Ketone bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogenolysis', 'During the first 60-90 minutes of exercise, liver glycogenolysis is the primary source of plasma glucose. As liver glycogen depletes, gluconeogenesis becomes increasingly important.'),
('c667dfd5-c1d4-5dfc-a8b7-347881627098'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Fatigue during a marathon is most closely associated with:', '["Central fatigue","Muscle glycogen depletion","ATP depletion","Dehydration only","Lactic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle glycogen depletion', 'Muscle glycogen depletion correlates strongly with fatigue during prolonged moderate-high intensity exercise. ''Hitting the wall'' at ~20 miles occurs when glycogen stores are nearly exhausted.'),
('379e9139-ef70-5d62-aa51-aff09f84df77'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the normal hemoglobin concentration in a healthy adult male?', '["10-12 g/dL","12-14 g/dL","13.5-17.5 g/dL","18-22 g/dL","23-25 g/dL"]'::jsonb, '13.5-17.5 g/dL', 'Normal Hb: males 13.5-17.5 g/dL, females 12-16 g/dL. Anemia reduces O2-carrying capacity and VO2 max. Altitude training increases Hb slightly via EPO stimulation.'),
('1dafaf75-555a-5ac5-8ad0-5884fc0a036f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which is the most accurate method to measure VO2 max?', '["Cooper 12-minute run test mediated physiological mechanism","Astrand-Ryhming nomogram mediated physiological mechanism","Indirect calorimetry with gas analysis during incremental exercise to exhaustion","Prediction using submaximal HR mediated physiological mechanism","Rockport walking test mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Indirect calorimetry with gas analysis during incremental exercise to exhaustion', 'The gold standard for VO2 max measurement is direct gas analysis during a graded exercise test to volitional exhaustion, with a valid plateau in oxygen consumption.'),
('42d30d17-c62b-5c58-b3ce-9d1de69ec769'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary function of myoglobin in skeletal muscle?', '["Oxygen storage and facilitated diffusion","Calcium binding mediated physiological mechanism","pH buffering mediated physiological mechanism","Structural support mediated physiological mechanism","Energy storage mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygen storage and facilitated diffusion', 'Myoglobin stores oxygen in muscle (~1-2 mL O2/100 g muscle) and facilitates O2 diffusion from capillaries to mitochondria. It has higher affinity for O2 than hemoglobin (P50 ~2.5 mmHg).'),
('20f7c7ed-0f52-5c49-ae3d-45cdfa8c3d9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Cori cycle involves:', '["Conversion of glucose to lactate in muscle and reconversion to glucose in liver","Fatty acid oxidation in the liver","Urea cycle in the liver","Ketogenesis during fasting","Conversion of amino acids to glucose in muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Conversion of glucose to lactate in muscle and reconversion to glucose in liver', 'Cori cycle: Muscle produces lactate (from glycolysis), which is released into blood, taken up by liver, and converted back to glucose via gluconeogenesis.'),
('a15ac62a-571b-57c7-9e1a-f899ba97cd46'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Central command during exercise refers to:', '["Chemoreflex activation mediated physiological mechanism","Baroreflex feedback mediated physiological mechanism","Thermoregulatory control mediated physiological mechanism","Feedforward activation of cardiovascular and respiratory centers from motor cortex","Reflexes from exercising muscles mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Feedforward activation of cardiovascular and respiratory centers from motor cortex', 'Central command is a feedforward mechanism where signals from the motor cortex simultaneously activate motor neurons and brainstem cardiovascular/respiratory centers.'),
('16160c86-065d-5bbd-a2c0-e65fe32c8502'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'In a surgical context, The exercise pressor reflex originates from:', '["Central chemoreceptors","Pulmonary stretch receptors","Peripheral chemoreceptors","Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus","Group III and IV afferents from exercising muscles"]'::jsonb, 'Group III and IV afferents from exercising muscles', 'The exercise pressor reflex is initiated by mechanoreceptors (Group III) and metaboreceptors (Group IV) in skeletal muscle, increasing sympathetic outflow, HR, and BP.'),
('03bad539-ac73-5596-9408-04acfe113c50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Venous return during exercise is enhanced by all EXCEPT:', '["Skeletal muscle pump","Increased blood volume in capacitance vessels","Respiratory pump","Increased cardiac contractility","Venoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Increased blood volume in capacitance vessels', 'Venous return is enhanced by: 1) skeletal muscle pump, 2) respiratory pump, 3) venoconstriction, and 4) cardiac suction. Blood volume shifts from capacitance veins to central circulation.'),
('e261d6fe-e353-53f1-aba0-102cdecec95a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the effect of beta-blockers on exercise performance?', '["Improved thermoregulation","Increased stroke volume at maximal exercise","Decreased maximal heart rate and reduced exercise capacity","Enhanced lactate clearance","Increased VO2 max"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased maximal heart rate and reduced exercise capacity', 'Beta-blockers reduce HR and myocardial contractility, decreasing cardiac output and exercise capacity. They can reduce VO2 max by 10-20%.'),
('cdb9ce47-66d0-5391-811e-c67fc9bb2aec'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Wingate Anaerobic Test is a 30-second all-out sprint used to measure:', '["Economy of movement","VO2 max","Lactate threshold","Anaerobic power and capacity","Flexibility"]'::jsonb, 'Anaerobic power and capacity', 'The Wingate test measures peak power (first 5 sec), mean power (30 sec), and fatigue index. It assesses anaerobic power (ATP-PCr) and anaerobic capacity (glycolytic system).'),
('92272eaa-b9b5-51ed-8590-033b75af3f86'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During which type of muscle contraction is force production the GREATEST?', '["Isotonic","Isokinetic","Isometric","Eccentric","Concentric"]'::jsonb, 'Eccentric', 'Eccentric contractions generate 10-40% more force than concentric or isometric contractions due to both active (cross-bridge) and passive (titin, connective tissue) contributions.'),
('e0e8fb8b-a723-580a-957b-4b8a607f42ce'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The term ''specificity of training'' refers to:', '["Only training one muscle group mediated physiological mechanism","Training effects being specific to the mode, intensity, and velocity of exercise performed","Training one side of the body mediated physiological mechanism","Gender-specific training mediated physiological mechanism","Age-specific training mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Training effects being specific to the mode, intensity, and velocity of exercise performed', 'The SAID principle (Specific Adaptations to Imposed Demands): training adaptations are specific to the type of exercise, muscle groups, contraction type, and energy system used.'),
('d6899ff4-4d48-5547-9526-e8dc14dc49f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What happens to diastolic blood pressure during incremental dynamic exercise?', '["Increases then decreases mediated physiological mechanism","Decreases significantly mediated physiological mechanism","Fluctuates wildly mediated physiological mechanism","Remains relatively stable or decreases slightly","Increases significantly mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Remains relatively stable or decreases slightly', 'Diastolic BP typically remains stable or decreases slightly during dynamic exercise due to peripheral vasodilation reducing TPR. Systolic BP rises progressively.'),
('74983611-a6b3-5452-809a-bf2c18d3253b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Adverse effects of anabolic-androgenic steroid (AAS) use include all EXCEPT:', '["Increased HDL cholesterol","Gynecomastia","Testicular atrophy","Hepatotoxicity","Increased aggression"]'::jsonb, 'Increased HDL cholesterol', 'AAS use decreases HDL (not increases) while increasing LDL. Other effects: testicular atrophy, gynecomastia, hepatotoxicity, and mood changes.'),
('3fab4604-d847-5cbc-9afc-d2aaab547229'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is NOT a recognized ergogenic aid for endurance performance?', '["Sodium bicarbonate","Nitrate","Creatine","Beta-alanine","Caffeine"]'::jsonb, 'Creatine', 'Creatine primarily benefits high-intensity short-duration activities, not endurance. Beta-alanine, caffeine, bicarbonate, and nitrate benefit endurance performance to varying degrees.'),
('d9ad2204-da99-51ad-8b54-58c3cea813a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve shifts to the right during exercise due to all EXCEPT:', '["Increased temperature","Decreased pH","Increased 2,3-BPG","Decreased CO2","Increased H+ concentration"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased CO2', 'Rightward shift is caused by: increased temperature, decreased pH, increased CO2, and increased 2,3-BPG. Decreased CO2 would shift the curve left.'),
('395d061a-5924-5049-a59b-2ef039dd9e34'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Following 8 weeks of endurance training, resting heart rate typically:', '["Increases by 10-15 bpm","Decreases by 5-15 bpm","Remains unchanged","Increases by 20 bpm","Shows no consistent pattern"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases by 5-15 bpm', 'Endurance training increases vagal tone and decreases sympathetic activity, leading to resting bradycardia (decrease of 5-15 bpm). Stroke volume increases due to cardiac remodeling.'),
('77b5a7cd-9e11-512b-834f-5d12aefa17f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What happens to blood volume following endurance training?', '["Decreases","Remains unchanged","Increases by 5-15%","Increases by 30-50%","Varies unpredictably"]'::jsonb, 'Increases by 5-15%', 'Endurance training increases plasma volume by 5-15% within the first week, due to increased plasma proteins and hormonal changes (aldosterone, ADH, ANP).'),
('868a207f-cf44-56ef-a523-e087fdb6d2bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The absolute refractory period of cardiac muscle corresponds to which phase of the action potential?', '["Phase 3","Phase 0","Phase 0-2","Phase 4","Phase 0-1"]'::jsonb, 'Phase 0-2', 'The absolute refractory period in cardiac muscle spans phases 0-2 (through the plateau). This long refractory period prevents tetanus and ensures rhythmic contraction.'),
('4449adf7-8df1-56c8-a3d6-5f1df203c927'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the Starling curve (Frank-Starling mechanism)?', '["As afterload increases, SV increases","As HR decreases, CO increases","As preload increases, SV increases up to a point","As HR increases, contractility increases","As contractility increases, EDV decreases"]'::jsonb, 'As preload increases, SV increases up to a point', 'Frank-Starling mechanism: increased ventricular end-diastolic volume stretches the myocardium, increasing actin-myosin overlap and Ca2+ sensitivity, thus increasing stroke volume.'),
('fd71f9be-a19b-53c8-ac20-ea4963aa9a7a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following ECG changes is an expected adaptation in an endurance athlete''s heart?', '["Pathologic Q waves mediated physiological mechanism","Prolonged QT interval mediated physiological mechanism","ST segment depression mediated physiological mechanism","Sinus bradycardia, voltage criteria for LVH, early repolarization","Inverted P waves mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Sinus bradycardia, voltage criteria for LVH, early repolarization', 'Athlete''s heart: sinus bradycardia, sinus arrhythmia, increased QRS voltage (LVH), early repolarization (ST elevation). These are benign adaptations.'),
('e1b32f1b-30fb-5bc6-a3f7-30b7c03a831a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The best measure of cardiorespiratory fitness is:', '["Lung capacity","VO2 max","Resting heart rate","Blood pressure at rest","Body fat percentage"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 max', 'VO2 max is the gold standard measure of cardiorespiratory fitness. It integrates the function of the pulmonary, cardiovascular, and muscular systems.'),
('871bd9df-f93e-5462-b636-c0778ad6beee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Polarized training involves what distribution of training intensity?', '["About 80% at low intensity, 20% at high intensity, very little at moderate intensity","Even distribution across intensity zones mediated physiological mechanism","Most training at moderate intensity mediated physiological mechanism","100% at low intensity mediated physiological mechanism","About 50% low, 30% moderate, 20% high mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'About 80% at low intensity, 20% at high intensity, very little at moderate intensity', 'Polarized training: 75-80% of training at low intensity (Zone 1, <VT1), and 15-20% at high intensity (Zone 3, >VT2), with minimal time in moderate intensity.'),
('aa512a85-72e3-568a-b8db-9b7a582c394f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During prolonged exercise in the heat, the decline in stroke volume is primarily due to:', '["Decreased plasma volume and cutaneous venous pooling","Decreased myocardial contractility","Increased venous return","Increased baroreflex sensitivity","Increased end-diastolic volume"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased plasma volume and cutaneous venous pooling', 'During exercise in heat, plasma volume decreases due to sweating, while cutaneous vasodilation increases venous pooling. This reduces preload and stroke volume.'),
('b33e3fbc-fd4c-5ad3-8bbf-1e6d39aa3f6f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The optimal training heart rate for a 20-year-old athlete using the Karvonen formula at 60% intensity (resting HR = 60) is:', '["144 bpm","160 bpm","120 bpm","175 bpm","190 bpm"]'::jsonb, '144 bpm', 'HRmax = 220 - 20 = 200. HRR = 200 - 60 = 140. Target HR = (140 x 0.60) + 60 = 84 + 60 = 144 bpm.'),
('6547e57d-5766-5454-98ec-79a0a28d26d5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which muscle fiber type is most susceptible to atrophy with detraining?', '["Type I","Type IIa and Type IIx","Equally all types","Type I more than Type II","Smooth muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Type IIa and Type IIx', 'Type II fibers (fast-twitch) show greater atrophy with detraining than Type I fibers because they require high-threshold recruitment during training.'),
('fd1412bb-0c7e-58e9-916d-4d369dc53f18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The term ''detraining'' refers to:', '["Complete cessation of training mediated physiological mechanism","Cross-training mediated physiological mechanism","Reduced training volume mediated physiological mechanism","Partial loss of training adaptations due to reduced training or inactivity","Overtraining prevention mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Partial loss of training adaptations due to reduced training or inactivity', 'Detraining: partial or complete loss of training-induced adaptations as a result of training reduction or cessation. Cardiorespiratory adaptations decline within 2-4 weeks.'),
('fd7b0a9d-3eb7-5172-9845-cf672b54fd21'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary fuel for the brain at rest?', '["Amino acids","Free fatty acids","Lactate","Glucose","Ketone bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose', 'The brain is highly dependent on glucose as its primary fuel. It consumes ~120 g of glucose daily. During prolonged starvation, ketone bodies can supply up to 60-70% of brain energy.'),
('9f5abd80-cb94-56b2-b6a4-b20923411af3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which individual would have the HIGHEST absolute VO2 max (L/min)?', '["Endurance-trained 70 kg female","Endurance-trained 80 kg male","Sedentary 90 kg male","Sedentary 70 kg female","Sedentary 80 kg male"]'::jsonb, 'Endurance-trained 80 kg male', 'Absolute VO2 max (L/min) = VO2 max (mL/kg/min) x body weight (kg) / 1000. An endurance-trained male has high relative VO2 max and higher body weight.'),
('8e83534d-679e-55ae-9124-1d94428d16c1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'After 20 weeks of detraining, VO2 max in a previously trained individual will fall to approximately:', '["10% below baseline","Untrained levels","50% below baseline","Still 20-30% above baseline","90% below baseline"]'::jsonb, 'Untrained levels', 'After 12-24 weeks of detraining, VO2 max returns to near-baseline untrained levels. The rapid decline in the first 4 weeks is due to plasma volume and SV reduction.'),
('0b3d6c24-f45f-52dd-8cbe-da92545c05cf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ratio of CO2 production to O2 consumption (VCO2/VO2) is called:', '["Respiratory exchange ratio","Anaerobic threshold","Respiratory rate","Ventilatory equivalent","Oxygen pulse"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory exchange ratio', 'RER = VCO2/VO2. It reflects substrate utilization: ~0.7 for pure fat, ~0.85 for mixed diet, ~1.0 for pure carbohydrate. RER >1.0 indicates hyperventilation.'),
('c2a8875a-be20-5554-b725-ce711cd902dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At maximal exercise, approximately what percentage of total heat production is dissipated via evaporation?', '["25-30%","90-100%","75-85%","10-15%","50-60%"]'::jsonb, '75-85%', 'During maximal exercise in a thermoneutral environment, evaporative heat loss (sweating) accounts for ~80% of total heat dissipation.'),
('3f1cfe76-adb0-5675-986c-6aa94c2dedb0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The oxygen pulse is defined as:', '["Heart rate / stroke volume","VE / VCO2","VCO2 / VO2","VO2 / body weight","VO2 / heart rate"]'::jsonb, 'VO2 / heart rate', 'Oxygen pulse = VO2 / HR (mL O2/beat). It reflects the product of stroke volume and a-vO2 difference. A higher oxygen pulse indicates greater cardiovascular efficiency.'),
('de661d89-3fbe-57a8-a741-0f89832f43a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is NOT a component of the oxygen cascade?', '["Diffusion across alveolar-capillary membrane","Convection in airways","Mitochondrial utilization","Transport in blood","Convection in pulmonary veins"]'::jsonb, 'Convection in pulmonary veins', 'The oxygen cascade includes: 1) ventilation, 2) alveolar-capillary diffusion, 3) blood transport, 4) capillary-to-cell diffusion, and 5) mitochondrial utilization.'),
('ab288f5f-10c0-530c-ba76-7b79f3c464bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The crossover concept in exercise physiology (shift from fat to carbohydrate) occurs at approximately what % of VO2 max?', '["75-85%","55-70%","40-50%","25-30%","90-100%"]'::jsonb, '55-70%', 'The crossover from fat to carbohydrate dominance typically occurs at 55-70% of VO2 max, varying with training status and diet.'),
('245750dd-2e7b-5942-b1dd-4924fb1aa6ad'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the approximate P50 of normal adult hemoglobin?', '["15 mmHg","20 mmHg","27 mmHg","35 mmHg","40 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '27 mmHg', 'Normal P50 of adult hemoglobin is ~27 mmHg. P50 is the PO2 at which hemoglobin is 50% saturated. Right shift increases P50 (decreased O2 affinity).'),
('505cccdc-2ffe-5f5e-a7b9-916a660de023'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The optimal training heart rate for fat oxidation (''fat-burning zone'') is approximately:', '["85-95% of HRmax",">95% of HRmax","50-70% of HRmax","30-50% of HRmax","70-85% of HRmax"]'::jsonb, '50-70% of HRmax', 'At low-moderate intensity (50-70% HRmax), a higher proportion of energy comes from fat oxidation. However, total caloric expenditure is higher at higher intensities.'),
('0915eeb0-70f1-5980-adf4-7bc4cf804efb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following occurs during the Valsalva maneuver in weightlifting?', '["Increased heart rate variability mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased blood pressure throughout mediated physiological mechanism","Increased intrathoracic pressure, decreased venous return, and increased BP initially","Decreased intrathoracic pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Bradycardia mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased intrathoracic pressure, decreased venous return, and increased BP initially', 'Valsalva maneuver: forced expiration against closed glottis increases intrathoracic pressure, compresses the heart, decreases venous return (transient BP drop), followed by BP overshoot upon release.'),
('aa6cad49-7bc4-53d5-9654-177c8adf0098'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''central governor'' model of fatigue proposes that:', '["Motivation is irrelevant to performance","Fatigue is purely peripheral","The brain regulates exercise intensity to prevent catastrophic failure","Glycogen depletion is the sole cause of fatigue","Fatigue is caused by lactate accumulation only"]'::jsonb, 'The brain regulates exercise intensity to prevent catastrophic failure', 'The central governor model: the CNS subconsciously regulates the number of motor units recruited to prevent damage, based on feedback from all systems. Fatigue is a ''sensation'' generated to protect the body.'),
('ead424a6-8220-513f-a793-044587c74674'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the effect of endurance training on the lactate curve?', '["Leftward shift","Rightward shift","No change","Curve becomes steeper","Peak lactate is reduced"]'::jsonb, 'Rightward shift', 'Endurance training shifts the lactate curve to the right - at a given absolute exercise intensity, blood lactate is lower. The lactate threshold occurs at a higher % of VO2 max.'),
('40ceae08-b309-5470-bd39-87073f3fb244'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Static (isometric) exercise primarily causes:', '["Decreased BP due to vasodilation","No cardiovascular changes","Large increase in HR and CO, moderate increase in BP","Decreased HR due to vagal activation","Small increase in HR, but large increase in BP due to sustained muscle compression of vessels"]'::jsonb, 'Small increase in HR, but large increase in BP due to sustained muscle compression of vessels', 'Isometric exercise: sustained muscle contraction compresses blood vessels (mechanical occlusion), increasing TPR and causing a large increase in both systolic and diastolic BP with only modest HR increase.'),
('10ba3eb0-0b42-5419-b4fe-495e90140c9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''V̇E/V̇CO2 slope'' during exercise is a measure of:', '["Lactate threshold","Ventilatory efficiency","Cardiac output","Carbon dioxide production","Oxygen consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilatory efficiency', 'V̇E/V̇CO2 slope: the relationship between minute ventilation and CO2 production. A lower slope indicates better ventilatory efficiency. An elevated slope (>34) is a prognostic marker in heart failure.'),
('4ca9c3cb-7c28-56b7-b83f-6d726885985c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Atmospheric pressure at sea level is approximately:', '["250 mmHg","760 mmHg","380 mmHg","500 mmHg","150 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '760 mmHg', 'Atmospheric pressure at sea level = 760 mmHg (1 atm). Partial pressure of O2 = 21% x 760 = 159 mmHg. At Everest summit, barometric pressure is ~253 mmHg.'),
('c187fd27-04d3-5ee3-a34f-db1b230b23af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At an altitude of 5500 m (~18000 ft), the inspired PO2 is approximately:', '["150 mmHg","120 mmHg","80 mmHg","55 mmHg","35 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '80 mmHg', 'At 5500 m, barometric pressure ~380 mmHg. PIO2 = (380 - 47) x 0.21 = 333 x 0.21 = ~70 mmHg. This represents a severe hypoxic stimulus.'),
('ac59b031-01ec-5586-bd65-d4183555b77f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The immediate (first minutes) physiological response to acute altitude exposure is:', '["Increased renal EPO production mediated physiological mechanism","Increased blood volume mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Increased pulmonary vasoconstriction and hyperventilation","Decreased blood pressure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased pulmonary vasoconstriction and hyperventilation', 'The immediate response includes: hyperventilation (hypoxic ventilatory response), tachycardia, increased cardiac output, and pulmonary vasoconstriction.'),
('229c85bf-1aef-56e0-b332-55bebd3d8aeb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The hypoxic ventilatory response (HVR) is mediated primarily by:', '["Pulmonary stretch receptors","Peripheral chemoreceptors","Central chemoreceptors in medulla","Juxtacapillary (J) receptors","Irritant receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral chemoreceptors', 'The HVR is mediated by peripheral chemoreceptors, primarily the carotid bodies, which are sensitive to decreased PaO2 (<60 mmHg).'),
('bf37eb51-f33a-5c26-afb8-5aa1a65dfcc8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Acclimatization to high altitude involves all EXCEPT:', '["Increased 2,3-BPG in RBCs","Increased mitochondrial density","Increased ventilation","Increased capillary density in skeletal muscle","Decreased EPO production"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased EPO production', 'Acclimatization increases EPO production (not decreases), leading to increased RBC mass and hemoglobin. Other adaptations: increased ventilation, 2,3-BPG, capillary density.'),
('3a42fadd-eba7-5d0a-b0f2-4715c9fbd84d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The primary stimulus for increased EPO production at altitude is:', '["Decreased blood pH","Increased sympathetic tone","Decreased temperature","Renal hypoxia detected by HIF-1a stabilization","Increased lactate"]'::jsonb, 'Renal hypoxia detected by HIF-1a stabilization', 'Renal hypoxia stabilizes HIF-1a, which upregulates EPO gene transcription in renal interstitial fibroblasts. EPO peaks at 24-48 hours, stimulating erythropoiesis.'),
('a565777d-9572-554a-a70e-ced582216eed'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is the earliest adaptation to high altitude?', '["Hyperventilation","Increased capillary density","Increased RBC mass","Increased 2,3-BPG","Increased mitochondrial enzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperventilation', 'Hyperventilation begins within minutes of ascent. RBC mass takes days to weeks. 2,3-BPG increases within 6-24 hours.'),
('08e8db01-db26-54a2-960e-3d31e7a89ad4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The increase in 2,3-BPG at altitude causes:', '["Left shift of O2-hemoglobin dissociation curve","Right shift, facilitating O2 unloading to tissues","Increased hemoglobin affinity for O2","Decreased P50","No change in the O2 dissociation curve"]'::jsonb, 'Right shift, facilitating O2 unloading to tissues', '2,3-BPG binds to beta chains of hemoglobin, stabilizing the deoxygenated (T) state, shifting the O2 curve right (increased P50), facilitating O2 unloading.'),
('8e6c5913-8ff8-54e3-a15c-a2d26119266f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Acute Mountain Sickness (AMS) typically occurs:', '["Immediately upon ascent","Only in unacclimatized individuals after exercise","Within 6-12 hours of ascent above 2500 m","After 48-72 hours","Only above 5000 m"]'::jsonb, 'Within 6-12 hours of ascent above 2500 m', 'AMS typically develops 6-12 hours after arrival at altitudes >2500 m. Symptoms: headache (diagnostic necessity), nausea, fatigue, dizziness, sleep disturbance.'),
('929f221f-4b17-5e00-818b-4276ed1c2bc5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Lake Louise Consensus criteria for AMS diagnosis requires:', '["Heart rate >120 bpm mediated physiological mechanism","Cerebellar ataxia mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary edema on exam mediated physiological mechanism","Presence of headache plus at least one other symptom","Oxygen saturation <80% mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Presence of headache plus at least one other symptom', 'Lake Louise criteria (2018): headache plus one or more of gastrointestinal symptoms, fatigue/weakness, dizziness/lightheadedness, or difficulty sleeping.'),
('b4dabd09-d526-5a6a-8068-615078b2894d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The most effective treatment for moderate-severe AMS is:', '["Supplemental oxygen","Dexamethasone","Immediate descent","Acetazolamide","Hyperbaric therapy"]'::jsonb, 'Immediate descent', 'Immediate descent is the definitive treatment for AMS, HAPE, and HACE. Descent of even 500-1000 m usually provides dramatic relief.'),
('139702c2-1848-5c10-adb1-dc91eb0fe96a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Acetazolamide (Diamox) prevents and treats AMS by:', '["Increasing EPO production mediated physiological mechanism","Anti-inflammatory effects mediated physiological mechanism","Increasing Hb affinity for O2 mediated physiological mechanism","Direct vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Acting as a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, causing metabolic acidosis and stimulating ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Acting as a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor, causing metabolic acidosis and stimulating ventilation', 'Acetazolamide inhibits renal carbonic anhydrase, causing bicarbonate diuresis and metabolic acidosis. This stimulates hyperventilation, increasing PaO2.'),
('dd533ff7-0ae5-58a3-bad2-7d8a23125c09'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'High Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE) is characterized by:', '["Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema due to exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction","Left ventricular failure causing pulmonary congestion","Pulmonary embolism","Bronchospasm","Pneumothorax"]'::jsonb, 'Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema due to exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction', 'HAPE is non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema from uneven hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction causing overperfusion of some capillary regions, leading to stress failure.'),
('bed5c64d-c685-541f-b3e0-301e63ed7fda'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The earliest symptom of HAPE is:', '["Dry cough and decreased exercise tolerance","Chest pain mediated physiological mechanism","Hemoptysis mediated physiological mechanism","Fever mediated physiological mechanism","Palpitations mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Dry cough and decreased exercise tolerance', 'HAPE symptoms begin with: dry cough, decreased exercise tolerance, dyspnea on exertion. As it progresses: pink frothy sputum, dyspnea at rest, crackles.'),
('dcdc15a6-b3cc-5de5-995c-388f4d544a7e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is a risk factor for HAPE?', '["Female gender","Previous history of HAPE","Young age","Obesity","Slow ascent"]'::jsonb, 'Previous history of HAPE', 'Major risk factors: previous HAPE (6-fold increased risk), rapid ascent, heavy physical exertion on arrival, cold, and individual susceptibility (exaggerated HPV response).'),
('6f7dd800-b075-5311-8dc0-6ee63a205f7f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Nifedipine is used for HAPE prophylaxis and treatment because it:', '["Increases ventilatory drive mediated physiological mechanism","Reduces pulmonary artery pressure by vasodilation","Increases heart rate mediated physiological mechanism","Enhances diuresis mediated physiological mechanism","Reduces cerebral edema mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Reduces pulmonary artery pressure by vasodilation', 'Nifedipine (a calcium channel blocker) reduces pulmonary artery pressure by vasodilating pulmonary vasculature, counteracting exaggerated hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction.'),
('1b3ad851-fd7c-5579-95fd-5dd59adf803e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'High Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE) is characterized by:', '["Meningitis mediated physiological mechanism","Cytotoxic and vasogenic cerebral edema due to hypoxia","Hydrocephalus mediated physiological mechanism","Brain tumor mediated physiological mechanism","Ischemic stroke mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Cytotoxic and vasogenic cerebral edema due to hypoxia', 'HACE represents the severe end of the AMS spectrum. Hypoxia causes vasogenic edema (increased BBB permeability) and cytotoxic edema (cellular swelling).'),
('296b5ac9-db48-5273-b7e6-3ed5bd707b2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The hallmark early sign of HACE that distinguishes it from severe AMS is:', '["Headache","Nausea","Ataxia","Insomnia","Fatigue"]'::jsonb, 'Ataxia', 'Ataxia is the earliest and most specific sign of HACE. Any person with altitude illness who develops ataxia or altered mental status has HACE until proven otherwise.'),
('6b13a382-8eae-59b0-9592-f68f883a81f6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Immediate treatment for HACE includes all EXCEPT:', '["Supplemental oxygen","Portable hyperbaric chamber","Acetazolamide alone","Immediate descent","Dexamethasone 8 mg IV/IM initially, then 4 mg q6h"]'::jsonb, 'Acetazolamide alone', 'HACE treatment: immediate descent, dexamethasone (reduces vasogenic edema), O2, and hyperbaric therapy. Acetazolamide alone is insufficient for HACE.'),
('3abdc4c9-00f3-5d3a-8438-5594aff50b55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Chronic mountain sickness (Monge''s disease) is characterized by:', '["Decreased red cell mass mediated physiological mechanism","Hypotension mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperventilation mediated physiological mechanism","Increased arterial PO2 mediated physiological mechanism","Excessive polycythemia (Hb >20 g/dL) with symptoms"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive polycythemia (Hb >20 g/dL) with symptoms', 'Monge''s disease: excessive erythrocytosis (Hb >20 g/dL men, >19 g/dL women), severe hypoxemia, pulmonary hypertension, and symptoms of headache, fatigue.'),
('73371e78-6058-5fae-9669-1af00a62e298'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Treatment of chronic mountain sickness (Monge''s disease) includes:', '["Acetazolamide mediated physiological mechanism","Dexamethasone mediated physiological mechanism","Phlebotomy and relocation to lower altitude","Nifedipine mediated physiological mechanism","Inhaled salmeterol mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Phlebotomy and relocation to lower altitude', 'Treatment: phlebotomy (removing 250-500 mL blood) for acute relief, and relocation to lower altitude as definitive treatment.'),
('0335c299-ed06-5a6c-acd9-dd7b2c7748a5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At the summit of Mount Everest (8848 m), the barometric pressure is approximately:', '["150 mmHg","380 mmHg","630 mmHg","253 mmHg","500 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '253 mmHg', 'Barometric pressure at Everest summit is ~253 mmHg. Inspired PO2 = (253 - 47) x 0.21 = ~43 mmHg.'),
('34a39d25-7d4b-5711-9549-8c40260f6848'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Arterial PCO2 at high altitude during acclimatization:', '["Decreases due to hyperventilation","Remains unchanged","Increases due to metabolic alkalosis","Shows no consistent change","Increases due to hypoventilation"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases due to hyperventilation', 'Acclimatization hyperventilation reduces PaCO2 (hypocapnia). At 4500 m, PaCO2 may be 20-25 mmHg (vs. 40 at sea level).'),
('04860784-5bc2-586a-833a-558d8ed7786b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The alveolar gas equation at altitude shows that alveolar PO2 depends primarily on:', '["Ventilation and barometric pressure","Cardiac output","Hemoglobin concentration","Oxygen consumption","Lung diffusion capacity"]'::jsonb, 'Ventilation and barometric pressure', 'Alveolar gas equation: PAO2 = PIO2 - (PaCO2/R). At altitude, PIO2 decreases with barometric pressure, while hyperventilation reduces PaCO2, partially compensating.'),
('df314dde-cfbf-5aa9-85a9-911033dbd9de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which best explains the left shift of the O2-Hb curve at altitude?', '["Increased lactate","Increased 2,3-BPG","Respiratory alkalosis","Increased temperature","Increased CO2"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory alkalosis', 'Hyperventilation causes respiratory alkalosis (increased pH), which shifts the O2-Hb curve left (increased affinity), improving O2 loading in the lungs.'),
('fdaa6618-dc25-5213-8a08-0a8983816943'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Periodic breathing (Cheyne-Stokes respiration) at altitude is caused by:', '["CO2 narcosis mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary edema mediated physiological mechanism","Brainstem compression mediated physiological mechanism","Diaphragmatic fatigue mediated physiological mechanism","Hypocapnia causing instability in the respiratory control system"]'::jsonb, 'Hypocapnia causing instability in the respiratory control system', 'Altitude periodic breathing: hypoxia stimulates hyperventilation, causing hypocapnia. When PaCO2 drops below the apneic threshold, breathing ceases until CO2 rises again.'),
('09183e3b-0d98-563a-b41b-1be4abcb705e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the normal P50 (PO2 at 50% Hb saturation) of adult hemoglobin?', '["37 mmHg","47 mmHg","15 mmHg","57 mmHg","27 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '27 mmHg', 'Normal P50 of adult hemoglobin is ~27 mmHg. Right shift (increased P50) = decreased O2 affinity. Left shift (decreased P50) = increased O2 affinity.'),
('e1861d4f-b510-5db1-a6d3-310fd16db32c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) has higher O2 affinity because:', '["It has higher P50 than HbA","It has a different heme structure","It binds 2,3-BPG less effectively","It has four alpha chains","It is dimeric"]'::jsonb, 'It binds 2,3-BPG less effectively', 'HbF (a2g2) binds 2,3-BPG less effectively than HbA because the gamma chain lacks 2,3-BPG binding residues. This gives HbF higher O2 affinity (P50 ~20 mmHg).'),
('d2fbf34a-c25a-528c-8003-b4c89b86b24c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'High-altitude natives (e.g., Sherpas) have which of the following adaptations?', '["Decreased lung volumes","Blunted hypoxic ventilatory response","No ventilatory response to hypoxia","Lower hemoglobin than sea-level equivalent","Increased hypoxic ventilatory response"]'::jsonb, 'Blunted hypoxic ventilatory response', 'High-altitude natives (especially Tibetans) have a blunted HVR compared to acclimatized lowlanders. This may reduce the work of breathing.'),
('c5c08772-b0fe-5a76-9f16-2d53d464820a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''lactate paradox'' at altitude refers to:', '["Increased lactate production during submaximal exercise","Lower blood lactate during maximal exercise at altitude compared to sea level","Higher lactate threshold","Increased lactate clearance","No lactate production at altitude"]'::jsonb, 'Lower blood lactate during maximal exercise at altitude compared to sea level', 'The lactate paradox: after several weeks at altitude, blood lactate during maximal exercise is lower than at sea level despite severe hypoxia.'),
('ab446b95-a5a2-5048-b94d-d2fde0ce167c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The optimal altitude for ''Live High - Train Low'' is:', '["3500-4500 m","2000-3000 m","1000-1500 m","> 5500 m","4500-5500 m"]'::jsonb, '2000-3000 m', 'Optimal living altitude: 2000-3000 m. At this altitude, EPO response is significant but the risk of altitude illness is minimal.'),
('72266e2a-0685-5bf4-ae9b-dc3c72c2d881'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary mechanism by which dexamethasone treats HACE?', '["Diuresis","Reducing pulmonary artery pressure","Increasing ventilatory drive","Reducing cerebral edema by stabilizing blood-brain barrier","Increasing EPO production"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing cerebral edema by stabilizing blood-brain barrier', 'Dexamethasone reduces vasogenic cerebral edema by stabilizing the blood-brain barrier, reducing inflammation, and decreasing cerebral blood flow.'),
('346abaec-27bd-5ed2-acf0-07c5ea5ec459'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Surgical removal of the carotid bodies would result in:', '["Increased HVR","Increased sensitivity to CO2","Increased resting ventilation","Loss of HVR","No change in HVR"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of HVR', 'Carotid body resection eliminates the hypoxic ventilatory response. Patients no longer increase ventilation when PaO2 falls.'),
('57ba4adc-4234-5227-a2d1-cb7e499093bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following increases during high-altitude acclimatization?', '["Plasma volume","Renal bicarbonate reabsorption","Arterial PO2","Hemoglobin concentration","Resting heart rate"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoglobin concentration', 'Hemoglobin increases (from EPO). Plasma volume decreases initially (hemoconcentration). Resting HR decreases after acclimatization.'),
('f83068d3-b579-5b15-bcf4-a751da4c9e65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Pulmonary hypertension at altitude is caused primarily by:', '["Left heart failure mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary fibrosis mediated physiological mechanism","Pulmonary embolism mediated physiological mechanism","Mitral stenosis mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction', 'The Euler-Liljestrand mechanism: alveolar hypoxia causes pulmonary vasoconstriction. At altitude, global hypoxia causes diffuse vasoconstriction and pulmonary hypertension.'),
('e5b3dae8-1185-562d-afb4-ba4acef5ca24'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Arterial oxygen saturation in a healthy individual at 3000 m (~10000 ft) is approximately:', '["70-75%","80-85%","85-90%","95-98%","90-94%"]'::jsonb, '85-90%', 'At 3000 m, SpO2 is typically 85-90% in normal individuals. At 4500 m, 75-85%. At Everest summit, SpO2 can be 50-70%.'),
('639a6d17-99cd-5d08-996e-a5445dbd38f9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which is most effective for preventing high altitude illness?', '["Taking iron supplements","Drinking large amounts of water","Rapid ascent using acetazolamide","Eating high","Gradual ascent"]'::jsonb, 'Gradual ascent', 'Gradual ascent is the most effective prevention. Above 3000 m, ascend no more than 300-500 m per day, with a rest day every 1000 m.'),
('f55ed4b2-8753-5ac2-b306-b7bc973d48f4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which drug is a respiratory stimulant that may be used for altitude-induced periodic breathing?', '["Salbutamol","Dexamethasone","Sildenafil","Acetazolamide","Nifedipine"]'::jsonb, 'Acetazolamide', 'Acetazolamide stabilizes breathing by causing metabolic acidosis, which stimulates ventilation and reduces the apneic threshold, decreasing periodic breathing.'),
('dd3ed402-5475-586a-94ba-8fb48759cc3b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Sildenafil at altitude is used for:', '["HACE treatment mediated physiological mechanism","Treating periodic breathing mediated physiological mechanism","Improving exercise performance mediated physiological mechanism","AMS prevention mediated physiological mechanism","Reducing pulmonary artery pressure in HAPE-prone individuals"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing pulmonary artery pressure in HAPE-prone individuals', 'Sildenafil (PDE5 inhibitor) increases cGMP, causing pulmonary vasodilation. It reduces pulmonary artery pressure and is used for HAPE prophylaxis.'),
('4520d2fe-035d-5a66-8dec-ec75a7215e28'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At what altitude does the body start producing measurable increases in EPO?', '["1000-1500 m","4000-4500 m","> 5000 m","3000-3500 m","2000-2500 m"]'::jsonb, '2000-2500 m', 'Significant EPO increase begins at 2000-2500 m. At 2500 m, EPO increases ~50% above baseline. Peak EPO response occurs at 24-48 hours.'),
('8366c8d8-130d-5d0e-bf15-6673eb18c6a8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Operation Everest II showed that at extreme altitude, arterial pH can reach:', '["7.4","7.7","8.0","7.5","7.2"]'::jsonb, '7.7', 'Operation Everest II: at extreme altitude, extreme hyperventilation caused PaCO2 of 7-10 mmHg and arterial pH >7.7, the most extreme alkalosis compatible with human life.'),
('7258f3c3-05fe-5c6e-a8b7-5502acc6bc97'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What happens to cerebral blood flow (CBF) during the first few days at high altitude?', '["Increases due to hypoxic vasodilation","Decreases due to hypocapnic vasoconstriction","No change","Increases then decreases to below baseline","Alternates with periodic breathing"]'::jsonb, 'Increases due to hypoxic vasodilation', 'Acute hypoxia causes cerebral vasodilation and increases CBF. However, hypocapnia causes cerebral vasoconstriction. The net effect is initially increased CBF.'),
('22f6043c-49b7-5d91-88af-9cc48af59351'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which altitude illness is a medical emergency requiring immediate descent?', '["Peripheral edema","Mild AMS","HAPE or HACE","Moderate AMS","High-altitude cough"]'::jsonb, 'HAPE or HACE', 'HAPE and HACE are life-threatening requiring immediate descent. HAPE can cause death within 6-12 hours. HACE causes death from brain herniation.'),
('995929d3-be23-5aeb-8d93-bdc86d2409b0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Peripheral edema at high altitude (swelling of hands, feet, face) is:', '["Always a sign of HACE","Treated with diuretics","Benign and usually self-limiting","A sign of renal failure","Specific to HAPE"]'::jsonb, 'Benign and usually self-limiting', 'Peripheral edema at altitude is common, benign, and usually resolves with rest or descent. It does NOT indicate AMS, HAPE, or HACE.'),
('97e48bcb-73cf-5401-9909-c04593807492'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the effect of 2,3-BPG on the O2-hemoglobin dissociation curve?', '["Curve becomes sigmoidal","Left shift","No effect","Right shift","Curve becomes hyperbolic"]'::jsonb, 'Right shift', '2,3-BPG binds to the beta chains of deoxyhemoglobin, stabilizing the T-state and reducing O2 affinity, shifting the curve right (increased P50).'),
('fa35c2df-8479-5804-aec0-ade0eeafea2b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The response to high altitude that takes the LONGEST to fully develop (>2 weeks) is:', '["Hyperventilation","Increased capillary density","Increased 2,3-BPG","Tachycardia","Increased RBC mass"]'::jsonb, 'Increased RBC mass', 'RBC mass takes the longest: EPO peaks at 24-48 hours, but bone marrow takes 4-14 days to produce new RBCs. Full increase in Hb takes 3-4 weeks.'),
('d678f9f8-a6d4-50e3-878b-3fa16a18894d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'A person ascending to 5000 m who has taken acetazolamide will have:', '["Lower arterial PO2 than without the drug","Higher arterial PO2 due to stimulated ventilation","Decreased ventilation","No change in ventilation","Increased risk of periodic breathing"]'::jsonb, 'Higher arterial PO2 due to stimulated ventilation', 'Acetazolamide stimulates ventilation by causing metabolic acidosis, increasing PAO2 and PaO2 and accelerating acclimatization.'),
('e44cf43b-fcfa-5e34-a7b5-b189ecfd416e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following statements about HIF-1a is correct?', '["Cells detect hypoxia through HIF-1a stabilization","HIF-1a is a transcription factor unique to the kidney","HIF-1a is degraded under hypoxic conditions","HIF-1a activation decreases EPO production","HIF-1a is continuously present in hypoxia"]'::jsonb, 'Cells detect hypoxia through HIF-1a stabilization', 'Under normoxia, HIF-1a is hydroxylated by PHDs and degraded. Under hypoxia, PHDs are inactive, HIF-1a stabilizes, and activates target genes including EPO.'),
('15eb1336-f8b2-5cbc-975d-f595b9941969'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The increased ventilation at altitude causes:', '["No pH change","Respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Respiratory alkalosis', 'Hyperventilation at altitude reduces PaCO2, causing respiratory alkalosis. Over 3-5 days, kidneys excrete bicarbonate to partially compensate.'),
('f898b4a1-648c-5053-95b8-046a0d21bed7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'With prolonged residence at 4500 m, the expected resting arterial PO2 is approximately:', '["35-45 mmHg","90-100 mmHg","20-30 mmHg","70-80 mmHg","55-65 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '35-45 mmHg', 'After full acclimatization at 4500 m, PaO2 is ~45-55 mmHg, much higher than the acute exposure value due to hyperventilation.'),
('cd1bf01b-f840-517b-8235-d78cb31af2d1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the approximate inspired PO2 at 3000 m altitude?', '["100 mmHg","120 mmHg","140 mmHg","70 mmHg","160 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '100 mmHg', 'At 3000 m, barometric pressure ~530 mmHg. PIO2 = (530 - 47) x 0.21 = 483 x 0.21 = ~101 mmHg.'),
('07806668-ae5a-5eb9-8461-59bea82ccc72'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which condition is characterized by an exaggerated pulmonary vasoconstrictor response to hypoxia?', '["AMS","HACE","Periodic breathing","Monge''s disease","HAPE"]'::jsonb, 'HAPE', 'HAPE-susceptible individuals have an exaggerated and uneven hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstrictor response, causing overperfusion in some capillary regions.'),
('f05d843c-a944-5a71-a74b-50b76899be5f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During ascent to altitude, the immediate increase in cardiac output is primarily due to:', '["Decreased afterload","Increased stroke volume","Increased venous return","Increased heart rate","Increased blood volume"]'::jsonb, 'Increased heart rate', 'Acute hypoxia activates the sympathetic nervous system, increasing heart rate. Cardiac output increases 30-50% in the first 24-48 hours.'),
('cf734b26-848a-5ac7-bc88-cefe8e94c30c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The most common cause of death among trekkers at high altitude is:', '["AMS","Trauma","Hypothermia","HACE","HAPE"]'::jsonb, 'HAPE', 'HAPE is the most common fatal altitude illness (0.5-2% at 4500 m vs. HACE 0.1-0.5%).'),
('d05b8ebf-f0ab-5e61-a30a-5504c0619071'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Oxygen delivery (DO2) is calculated as:', '["CO x CaO2 x 10","PaO2 x CO","VO2 x CO","VO2 / CO","CO x SaO2"]'::jsonb, 'CO x CaO2 x 10', 'Oxygen delivery (mL/min) = Cardiac Output (L/min) x Arterial O2 content (mL/dL) x 10. DO2 is ~1000 mL/min at rest.'),
('15c7743e-9e3b-51e5-a9c4-a862b77aee55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The increased hemoglobin concentration at altitude primarily serves to:', '["Increase PaO2","Increase arterial O2 content","Decrease blood viscosity","Shift the O2 curve left","Increase lung diffusion"]'::jsonb, 'Increase arterial O2 content', 'Increased Hb increases arterial O2 content (CaO2 = Hb x 1.39 x SaO2 + dissolved O2), compensating for decreased SaO2 at altitude.'),
('a731e9f3-7a07-5fe6-84a1-7720d998b04e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the effect of hyperventilation on cerebral blood flow?', '["Increases CBF initially then normalizes","Decreases CBF due to hypocapnic vasoconstriction","Increases CBF due to hypoxia","No effect","Alternates with respiration"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases CBF due to hypocapnic vasoconstriction', 'Hypocapnia causes cerebral vasoconstriction. Each 1 mmHg decrease in PaCO2 reduces CBF by ~2-4%.'),
('d77e70ae-9b16-581f-a0d4-b3e818b0fa5c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which is TRUE about retinal hemorrhages at high altitude?', '["They always require immediate evacuation","They are common above 5000 m and usually benign","They indicate HACE","They are preventable with acetazolamide","They cause permanent vision loss"]'::jsonb, 'They are common above 5000 m and usually benign', 'High-altitude retinal hemorrhages occur in 30-50% above 5000 m. They are usually benign and resolve spontaneously within 1-2 weeks.'),
('8dda7d51-a6a9-527d-a9e5-100bfbffaead'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The spleen''s function during exercise at altitude includes:', '["No role in altitude adaptation mediated physiological mechanism","Storage of platelets only mediated physiological mechanism","Contraction to release stored RBCs, increasing hematocrit","Increased destruction of RBCs mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased RBC production mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Contraction to release stored RBCs, increasing hematocrit', 'The spleen contracts during exercise and at altitude, releasing stored concentrated RBCs, acutely increasing hematocrit by 2-5%.'),
('bee68337-a9a6-5673-8a8a-ccfbbd656eaf'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At altitude, the O2-Hb dissociation curve shows which net change after full acclimatization?', '["Marked right shift mediated physiological mechanism","Slight left shift due to alkalosis partially offset by 2,3-BPG","Curve becomes linear mediated physiological mechanism","Marked left shift mediated physiological mechanism","No change mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Slight left shift due to alkalosis partially offset by 2,3-BPG', 'Respiratory alkalosis shifts left (increased affinity), while 2,3-BPG shifts right. The net effect is often a slight left shift at extreme altitude.'),
('a1ee686f-86aa-5fd6-95f1-6987ba488c84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary reason for exercise limitation at extreme altitude (>7000 m)?', '["Nutritional deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Dehydration mediated physiological mechanism","Muscle fatigue mediated physiological mechanism","Hypoxemia and limited O2 diffusion in the lungs","Cardiac pump failure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoxemia and limited O2 diffusion in the lungs', 'At extreme altitude, severe hypoxemia limits O2 delivery. VO2 max at Everest summit is ~1 L/min or less.'),
('697a1527-367d-5bd8-8424-ec7e49a69ac1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The medication of choice for AMS prophylaxis in a patient with sulfa allergy who cannot take acetazolamide is:', '["Ibuprofen","Sildenafil","Dexamethasone","Nifedipine","Salbutamol"]'::jsonb, 'Dexamethasone', 'Dexamethasone is an alternative for AMS prophylaxis when acetazolamide is contraindicated. Dose: 2 mg q6h or 4 mg q12h.'),
('6fb67234-ab43-560e-bc38-5ca359a943fd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which individual is at HIGHEST risk of altitude illness?', '["A well-acclimatized Sherpa","A person with history of migraines","A person with well-controlled asthma","A person with a PFO and history of HAPE","A young child"]'::jsonb, 'A person with a PFO and history of HAPE', 'PFO increases HAPE risk by worsening hypoxemia and increasing pulmonary artery pressure. Previous HAPE with PFO is the strongest risk factor.'),
('df8ea0a7-3fa9-5a5b-9b7d-5d0ba77d96bc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What effect does progesterone have on ventilation?', '["Depresses ventilation","Only affects CO2 response","Increases periodic breathing","Stimulates ventilation","No effect"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulates ventilation', 'Progesterone is a respiratory stimulant. It increases sensitivity of the respiratory center to CO2, explaining higher ventilation in the luteal phase.'),
('f7f8cab7-3fc1-52b2-8d92-d3e5f486653a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''hypoxic ventilatory decline'' (HVD) refers to:', '["The immediate increase in ventilation after ascent","Ventilation during periodic breathing","A gradual decrease in ventilation after initial HVR over 20-30 min of sustained hypoxia","Ventilatory response to CO2","Decreased ventilation during sleep"]'::jsonb, 'A gradual decrease in ventilation after initial HVR over 20-30 min of sustained hypoxia', 'HVD: after the initial brisk HVR, ventilation gradually declines over 20-30 minutes due to CNS inhibitory neurotransmitters like adenosine and GABA.'),
('e37b6c49-17d2-5d81-8c35-9a1e11de2700'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Boyle''s law states that at constant temperature:', '["The volume of a gas is inversely proportional to its pressure","The volume of a gas is directly proportional to its pressure","The solubility of a gas is proportional to its partial pressure","The pressure of a gas mixture is the sum of partial pressures","The volume of a gas is proportional to its temperature"]'::jsonb, 'The volume of a gas is inversely proportional to its pressure', 'Boyle''s Law: P1V1 = P2V2. As pressure increases (descending in water), gas volume decreases proportionally. Critical for understanding barotrauma.'),
('43670a7c-619d-5c12-b2a5-cfe613c282a7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Henry''s law states that:', '["The solubility of a gas in a liquid is proportional to its partial pressure","Gas volume is inversely proportional to pressure","The pressure of a gas mixture equals the sum of partial pressures","Gas diffuses from high to low pressure","Gas flow rate depends on pressure gradient"]'::jsonb, 'The solubility of a gas in a liquid is proportional to its partial pressure', 'Henry''s Law: the amount of gas dissolved in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of that gas above the liquid.'),
('84c1bbfc-f781-51b6-ac91-d80fb544102c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Dalton''s law states that:', '["The total pressure of a gas mixture equals the sum of partial pressures of individual gases","Gas solubility is proportional to partial pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Gas volume decreases with increasing pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Gas density is proportional to pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Gas viscosity is independent of pressure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'The total pressure of a gas mixture equals the sum of partial pressures of individual gases', 'Dalton''s Law: Ptotal = P1 + P2 + P3 + ... In air at 1 ATA, PO2 = 159 mmHg, PN2 = 593 mmHg.'),
('bef44725-08d0-5383-afc9-d52b373a6f14'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At a depth of 10 meters of seawater (msw), the ambient pressure is:', '["2 ATA","10 ATA","3 ATA","1 ATA","5 ATA"]'::jsonb, '2 ATA', 'Each 10 msw adds ~1 atmosphere. At surface: 1 ATA. At 10 m: 2 ATA. Absolute pressure = (depth/10) + 1 ATA.'),
('9c6b3aee-5ff0-5689-96d6-369ff5488191'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At 30 msw depth, the partial pressure of inspired oxygen (PIO2) is:', '["0.84 ATA","1.05 ATA","0.21 ATA","0.63 ATA","0.42 ATA"]'::jsonb, '0.84 ATA', 'At 30 m, ambient pressure = 4 ATA. PIO2 = 4 x 0.21 = 0.84 ATA. Maximum safe PO2 for diving is typically 1.4-1.6 ATA.'),
('d6e98a75-be54-54b3-9fb6-3f3ec67b4c42'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Decompression sickness (DCS) is caused by:', '["Oxygen toxicity mediated physiological mechanism","Formation of nitrogen bubbles in tissues during ascent","CO2 retention during diving mediated physiological mechanism","Hypothermia from cold water mediated physiological mechanism","Barotrauma to the ears mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Formation of nitrogen bubbles in tissues during ascent', 'DCS occurs when nitrogen dissolved in tissues under pressure comes out of solution as bubbles during ascent, according to Henry''s law.'),
('c3ad5fd1-a66e-5d46-975d-c79df2936276'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Type I decompression sickness (DCS) typically presents with:', '["Pulmonary symptoms mediated physiological mechanism","Neurological symptoms mediated physiological mechanism","Seizures mediated physiological mechanism","Cardiovascular collapse mediated physiological mechanism","Joint pain (''the bends'') and skin manifestations"]'::jsonb, 'Joint pain (''the bends'') and skin manifestations', 'Type I DCS: pain only (joint/muscle ''bends''), skin involvement, and lymphatic swelling. Type II DCS: neurological, pulmonary, and inner ear.'),
('c4d82644-b74e-56ed-a6f5-8cc003447fe5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''chokes'' in decompression sickness refers to:', '["Bronchospasm from cold air","CO2 narcosis","Pulmonary DCS with substernal pain, cough, and dyspnea","Laryngeal spasm from water inhalation","Hyperventilation from panic"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary DCS with substernal pain, cough, and dyspnea', 'Pulmonary DCS (the chokes) is a serious Type II DCS with substernal chest pain, cough, dyspnea, and cyanosis from gas bubbles in pulmonary circulation.'),
('753d74c8-5046-58d8-99fc-b00db9e158de'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The definitive treatment for decompression sickness is:', '["IV fluids and NSAIDs","Diazepam for pain","Recompression in a hyperbaric chamber","Aspirin","100% oxygen at surface"]'::jsonb, 'Recompression in a hyperbaric chamber', 'Definitive treatment for DCS is recompression in a hyperbaric chamber. Table 6 involves compression to 60 fsw (2.8 ATA) breathing 100% O2.'),
('65cb7cda-9331-51a1-b65a-3f21e4f9f6c6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The primary gas causing decompression sickness is:', '["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Carbon dioxide","Helium","Carbon monoxide"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrogen', 'Nitrogen is the primary gas causing DCS as it is inert, highly soluble in fat, and accumulates in tissues during the dive.'),
('ad049199-e435-5108-b9c6-e0b53d431b16'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Nitrogen narcosis (''rapture of the deep'') typically becomes significant at what depth?', '["30-60 m","60-90 m",">100 m","10-15 m","20-30 m"]'::jsonb, '30-60 m', 'Nitrogen narcosis typically becomes noticeable at 30-40 m (4-5 ATA) and significant at 50-60 m. Symptoms: euphoria, impaired judgment.'),
('8327ff10-3675-54bb-bfe7-5012c8c6faf3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The mechanism of nitrogen narcosis is thought to be:', '["Cerebral vasoconstriction","Increased NMDA receptor activity","Competitive inhibition of O2 binding to hemoglobin","Dissolution of N2 in neuronal lipid membranes","Formation of nitrogen bubbles in the brain"]'::jsonb, 'Dissolution of N2 in neuronal lipid membranes', 'Meyer-Overton hypothesis: N2 dissolves in lipid-rich neuronal membranes, causing expansion and altered membrane fluidity affecting ion channel function.'),
('42e1b5e7-387e-5ace-8446-fe9fb48ff52b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which gas mixture is used for deep dives (>60 m) to reduce narcosis?', '["Trimix (He-O2-N2) or Heliox","Pure oxygen","Compressed air","Nitrox","Hydrogen-oxygen mixture"]'::jsonb, 'Trimix (He-O2-N2) or Heliox', 'Trimix or heliox are used for deep dives. Helium has very low lipid solubility (Meyer-Overton coefficient 0.3 vs. N2 = 1.0), producing almost no narcotic effect.'),
('c88418fd-5a67-5f34-9b69-6bd642e7bb11'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Oxygen toxicity at partial pressures >1.6 ATA primarily affects:', '["Bone marrow","Eyes","Central nervous system","Kidneys","Lungs"]'::jsonb, 'Central nervous system', 'CNS O2 toxicity occurs at PO2 >1.6-2.0 ATA (Paul Bert effect). Symptoms: visual disturbances, tinnitus, facial twitching, and generalized seizures.'),
('9611808b-64b5-5f00-955f-6037ed193200'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Pulmonary oxygen toxicity (Lorrain Smith effect) can occur with exposure to PO2 >0.5 ATA for:', '["Only in premature infants","Several hours to days","Only in combination with CO2","Only above 2 ATA","Minutes"]'::jsonb, 'Several hours to days', 'Pulmonary O2 toxicity develops with prolonged exposure to elevated PO2 (hours to days). Symptoms: substernal pain, cough, reduced vital capacity.'),
('2d8c65e5-c79b-5e21-b664-30d62645153d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The first symptom of CNS oxygen toxicity is often:', '["Dyspnea","Chest pain","Blindness","Seizures without warning","Facial muscle twitching"]'::jsonb, 'Facial muscle twitching', 'Early warning signs of CNS O2 toxicity: facial twitching, visual changes, tinnitus, nausea, dizziness. Mnemonic: VENTID-C.'),
('7a4f04d5-2249-5a96-94ee-10678c0c34f1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the maximum safe single exposure PO2 for a working diver?', '["0.5 ATA","1.0 ATA","1.4 ATA","2.0 ATA","2.5 ATA"]'::jsonb, '1.4 ATA', 'Maximum safe PO2 for working divers is generally 1.4 ATA (1.6 ATA for resting). US Navy limits PO2 to 1.4 ATA for most diving operations.'),
('2c9ba94e-1768-54ef-9677-2799fcc53033'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Nitrox (enriched air nitrox, EANx) breathing gas contains:', '["Added helium","Less O2 and more N2 than air","More O2 and less N2 than air","Same O2 as air with added CO2","Only helium and oxygen"]'::jsonb, 'More O2 and less N2 than air', 'Nitrox has higher O2 percentage than air (32% or 36% vs. 21%), reducing N2 content and decompression requirements.'),
('c9b3fca8-3fbb-5dbd-80f5-b09aedf78f2d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The maximum operating depth (MOD) for Nitrox 32 (32% O2) with a max PO2 of 1.4 ATA is approximately:', '["20 m","30 m","34 m","40 m","50 m"]'::jsonb, '34 m', 'MOD = (1.4/0.32 - 1) x 10 = (4.375 - 1) x 10 = 33.75 m = 34 m.'),
('c3f9e83a-8e5c-5ec4-98c9-7f3471d987f8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Breath-hold diving (freediving) physiology includes the diving reflex, characterized by:', '["Bradycardia, peripheral vasoconstriction, and blood shift to central circulation","Cutaneous vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Hyperventilation and bronchodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Tachycardia and peripheral vasodilation mediated physiological mechanism","Increased metabolic rate mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Bradycardia, peripheral vasoconstriction, and blood shift to central circulation', 'The mammalian diving reflex: bradycardia, peripheral vasoconstriction, and blood shift, triggered by facial immersion in cold water and apnea.'),
('196a2b56-e28a-58b3-bd11-303a52eb7b2f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The maximum recorded breath-hold duration in elite freedivers (static apnea) is approximately:', '["2-3 minutes","5-6 minutes","11+ minutes","1 minute","20+ minutes"]'::jsonb, '11+ minutes', 'World record for static apnea exceeds 11 minutes. This requires extreme training, hyperventilation, spleen contraction, and high O2 stores.'),
('689392b3-83e9-57b2-b663-021b001413b1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Shallow-water blackout (hypoxic syncope) occurs when:', '["Nitrogen narcosis causes disorientation mediated physiological mechanism","Oxygen toxicity causes seizures mediated physiological mechanism","A breath-hold diver hyperventilates before diving, then becomes hypoxic before the urge to breath","CO2 toxicity causes unconsciousness mediated physiological mechanism","A diver ascends too rapidly mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'A breath-hold diver hyperventilates before diving, then becomes hypoxic before the urge to breath', 'Shallow water blackout: hyperventilation before a dive lowers PaCO2, delaying the urge to breathe. During ascent, dropping pressure reduces alveolar PO2, causing sudden hypoxia.'),
('d6b0afbc-af1d-5150-bb01-c524c3a00f0d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Arterial gas embolism (AGE) in diving occurs when:', '["Air enters the arterial system through a PFO","Decompression bubbles form in cerebral arteries","Oxygen bubbles form in the blood","Lung overexpansion forces air into pulmonary veins, then systemic arteries","Nitrogen bubbles form in the joints"]'::jsonb, 'Lung overexpansion forces air into pulmonary veins, then systemic arteries', 'AGE occurs when a diver ascends holding their breath, causing lung overexpansion and rupture of alveoli. Air enters pulmonary capillaries -> systemic circulation.'),
('ef4c9192-d84c-536b-b542-93e514e83d02'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The single most important rule to prevent arterial gas embolism is:', '["Never hold your breath while ascending","Equalize ears frequently","Use Nitrox instead of air","Ascend slowly","Remain at surface for 5 min after diving"]'::jsonb, 'Never hold your breath while ascending', 'NEVER hold your breath while ascending! Continuous exhalation is critical. Even a 1-2 m ascent with a breath hold can cause AGE.'),
('bb50fc7a-1c0b-5c89-beb5-9272348da75e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Immediate first aid for a diver with suspected AGE is:', '["Aspirin mediated physiological mechanism","Cool fluids to drink mediated physiological mechanism","100% oxygen at surface and transport to hyperbaric chamber","IV fluids mediated physiological mechanism","CPR mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, '100% oxygen at surface and transport to hyperbaric chamber', 'Suspected AGE: 100% O2 by non-rebreather mask, left lateral decubitus position, and emergency transport to a hyperbaric chamber.'),
('cc01952d-672d-5e62-8b8a-2d56fe93cde6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The rate of ascent recommended by most dive training organizations is:', '["20 m/min","3 m/min","9-10 m/min","6 m/min","15 m/min"]'::jsonb, '9-10 m/min', 'Standard ascent rate: <=9-10 m/min (<=30 ft/min). A safety stop at 5-6 m for 3-5 minutes is recommended for all dives >10 m.'),
('8ba1c4a9-5295-5ada-9680-2b117451656b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The half-time for nitrogen elimination from a fast tissue compartment (e.g., blood) is:', '["1-4 minutes","5-10 minutes","20-30 minutes","120-240 minutes","60-90 minutes"]'::jsonb, '5-10 minutes', 'Fast tissues (blood, brain): half-times of 5-10 min. Medium (muscle): 20-40 min. Slow (fat, bone): 60-240+ min.'),
('96f2bac9-e235-5cff-bc1f-76442297a7dd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which tissue compartment is most relevant for determining decompression requirements for long dives?', '["Very slow","All tissues equally","Medium","Slow","Fast"]'::jsonb, 'Slow', 'Slow tissue compartments (120-240 min half-times) govern decompression requirements because they take longest to saturate and desaturate.'),
('744edf8f-919e-5957-bf56-ff00f7786983'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Haldane model for decompression is based on:', '["Cerebral blood flow","Tissue oxygen content","Metabolic rate","Critical supersaturation ratio","Blood pH changes"]'::jsonb, 'Critical supersaturation ratio', 'Haldane''s principle: DCS can be avoided if the supersaturation ratio (tissue N2 PN2/ambient pressure) does not exceed a critical threshold (M-value).'),
('82976ea2-46d0-5a23-82ff-4aeed4e3c3db'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Diving after flying increases DCS risk because:', '["Dehydration from air travel","Residual nitrogen expands in the lower cabin pressure","Increased oxygen requirements at altitude","Fatigue from travel","Radiation exposure during flight"]'::jsonb, 'Residual nitrogen expands in the lower cabin pressure', 'FAA recommends waiting 12-24 hours after surfacing before flying. Cabin pressure equivalent to 1500-2400 m altitude can precipitate DCS.'),
('3aa90c8b-1f37-5ab0-84aa-24c61d98e394'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to scuba diving?', '["Previous pneumothorax","Mild seasonal allergies","Well-controlled asthma","History of childhood epilepsy","Corrected myopia with contact lenses"]'::jsonb, 'Previous pneumothorax', 'Previous pneumothorax (especially spontaneous) is an absolute contraindication due to risk of recurrence during ascent.'),
('c9038d55-30f5-5726-a380-17680532b714'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'In diving medicine, ''isobaric counterdiffusion'' refers to:', '["Gas bubbles forming during ascent at constant depth mediated physiological mechanism","Two inert gases diffusing in opposite directions at different rates, causing bubble formation at","Equalizing pressure in the middle ear mediated physiological mechanism","Countercurrent exchange in the kidney mediated physiological mechanism","Gas exchange across the alveoli mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Two inert gases diffusing in opposite directions at different rates, causing bubble formation at', 'Isobaric counterdiffusion: when gas mixture changes at depth, He and N2 move into and out of tissues at different rates, potentially causing bubble formation.'),
('498d4590-e0ab-590f-a1dc-bde1a0c4be89'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Tympanic membrane rupture during descent (''ear squeeze'') is prevented by:', '["Breathing slowly mediated physiological mechanism","Equalizing middle ear pressure through Eustachian tube","Wearing a hood mediated physiological mechanism","Descending quickly mediated physiological mechanism","Using earplugs mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Equalizing middle ear pressure through Eustachian tube', 'Equalization by opening Eustachian tubes via yawning, swallowing, or gentle Valsalva. Earplugs are NOT used as they create an air space that can cause reverse squeeze.'),
('3c7d6d88-9061-5c49-97f1-c5cb34aa0d18'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Sinus barotrauma during ascent is caused by:', '["Collapse of the sinus cavities","Allergic reaction to seawater","Blocked sinus ostia trapping expanding air","Cold water causing sinus inflammation","Infection spreading from the teeth"]'::jsonb, 'Blocked sinus ostia trapping expanding air', 'During ascent, air in sinuses expands (Boyle''s Law). If sinus ostia are blocked, the expanding air causes severe pain, epistaxis, and potential fracture.'),
('ff2ea1ff-1ce6-592a-b69e-8c91895037a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Dental barotrauma (''tooth squeeze'') occurs when:', '["Teeth become loose from cold water","Salt water irritates the gums","Acidic water erodes tooth enamel","Gas trapped under a dental filling expands/contracts during pressure changes","Dental nerves are affected by regulator vibration"]'::jsonb, 'Gas trapped under a dental filling expands/contracts during pressure changes', 'Dental barotrauma: trapped gas beneath a defective filling or dental caries expands during ascent or contracts during descent, causing severe tooth pain.'),
('1a52f175-3cde-5c2a-8bbc-66192c431ffe'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The mammalian diving reflex is most pronounced in:', '["Reptiles","Birds","Seals and whales","Humans","Fish"]'::jsonb, 'Seals and whales', 'The diving reflex is most pronounced in marine mammals (seals, dolphins, whales). Humans have a vestigial diving reflex that is much weaker.'),
('5c506ceb-f451-506d-9829-6d38675398bb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The maximum safe ascent rate for a diver breathing compressed air is determined by:', '["Oxygen consumption rate mediated physiological mechanism","Heart rate response mediated physiological mechanism","Water temperature mediated physiological mechanism","Rate of nitrogen elimination from slow tissues","Lung compliance mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Rate of nitrogen elimination from slow tissues', 'The ascent rate is limited by the rate at which slow tissues can eliminate nitrogen without reaching supersaturation sufficient to form bubbles.'),
('b8cad590-5eec-53a5-81bc-3f74be10fda8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Carbon monoxide poisoning is a particular risk in diving when:', '["Compressed air contains CO from a contaminated compressor intake","Nitrox is used at excessive depth","Breath-hold diving after hyperventilation","Diving in cold water","Using helium mixes"]'::jsonb, 'Compressed air contains CO from a contaminated compressor intake', 'CO poisoning: if the air compressor intake is placed near engine exhaust, CO can contaminate the breathing gas. CO has 250x higher Hb affinity than O2.'),
('b574976d-abac-508e-b43c-48ad876bbb3d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''bends'' pain in DCS is typically described as:', '["Cramping in the calf muscles","Throbbing headache","Dull, deep, aching pain around a joint","Burning sensation in the skin","Sharp, stabbing pain in the chest"]'::jsonb, 'Dull, deep, aching pain around a joint', 'The bends: poorly localized, dull, deep aching pain around a large joint (shoulder, elbow, hip, knee), caused by N2 bubbles in joint capsules.'),
('e8de97a3-6a76-583b-ba1d-28c436c527d2'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''Valsalva maneuver'' in diving physiology is used to:', '["Equalize middle ear pressure during descent","Slow heart rate before a dive","Warm the regulator","Conserve breathing gas","Perform emergency ascent"]'::jsonb, 'Equalize middle ear pressure during descent', 'Valsalva: forced exhalation against a closed glottis/pinched nose to increase pharyngeal pressure and open Eustachian tubes, equalizing middle ear pressure.'),
('cfc5e753-8443-5200-882d-368c35301605'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The no-decompression limit (NDL) for a dive to 18 m (60 ft) is approximately:', '["120 minutes","90 minutes","30 minutes","56 minutes","10 minutes"]'::jsonb, '56 minutes', 'At 18 m (60 ft), NDL is ~56 min. At 30 m (100 ft), NDL is ~20 min. These are based on nitrogen supersaturation models.'),
('0652bc99-f3fa-5655-b589-5fc3dafef666'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Nitrogen is more soluble in which body tissue?', '["Blood","Lung tissue","Muscle","Bone","Fat"]'::jsonb, 'Fat', 'Nitrogen is approximately 5 times more soluble in fat than in water. Fatty tissues accumulate more N2 and release it more slowly during ascent.'),
('d2217933-cc3f-518a-a914-e6f7335b2580'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'In decompression theory, M-value refers to:', '["Maximum allowable partial pressure of N2 in a tissue without bubble formation","Metabolic consumption rate mediated physiological mechanism","Minimum tissue N2 pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Maximum oxygen depth limit mediated physiological mechanism","Minimum safety stop depth mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum allowable partial pressure of N2 in a tissue without bubble formation', 'M-value: the maximum partial pressure of inert gas a tissue can tolerate at a given ambient pressure without forming bubbles.'),
('88e4d149-3579-5c4e-9beb-37fc5a0ff7cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'DCS susceptibility is increased by all EXCEPT:', '["Being female","Cold water","Dehydration","Exercise immediately after diving","Obesity"]'::jsonb, 'Being female', 'Gender alone is not a consistent independent risk factor for DCS. Risk factors: obesity, dehydration, post-dive exercise, cold water, PFO.'),
('172eaa4e-2928-5437-892b-3416dfb02de3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The treatment of choice for Type II DCS is:', '["Anticoagulation mediated physiological mechanism","IV mannitol mediated physiological mechanism","Recompression on US Navy Treatment Table 6","Dexamethasone mediated physiological mechanism","100% oxygen at 1 ATA mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Recompression on US Navy Treatment Table 6', 'US Navy Table 6: compression to 60 fsw (2.8 ATA) breathing 100% O2 in 30-min cycles with 5-min air breaks, followed by staged decompression.'),
('db90b987-0811-5700-aa02-65bd0a161d7e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following is a contraindication to hyperbaric oxygen therapy (HBOT)?', '["Delayed radiation injury","Untreated pneumothorax","Decompression sickness","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Necrotizing fasciitis"]'::jsonb, 'Untreated pneumothorax', 'Untreated pneumothorax is an absolute contraindication to HBOT because pressure changes can cause tension pneumothorax.'),
('830e0e33-2d79-5851-93e5-88c56742749f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Immersion pulmonary edema (IPE) in divers presents with:', '["Nausea and vertigo mediated physiological mechanism","Joint pain mediated physiological mechanism","Chest pain relieved by leaning forward mediated physiological mechanism","Dyspnea, cough with pink frothy sputum, and hypoxemia during or after a dive","Seizures mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Dyspnea, cough with pink frothy sputum, and hypoxemia during or after a dive', 'IPE: acute onset of dyspnea, cough with pink frothy sputum, and hypoxemia during or immediately after diving from fluid shifts and increased pulmonary capillary pressure.'),
('41c9f015-ce90-568b-a744-afd7ebc86c5d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The maximum duration of a single hyperbaric oxygen session on Table 6 is approximately:', '["4.5 hours","1 hour","2-3 hours","8-10 hours","30 minutes"]'::jsonb, '4.5 hours', 'US Navy Table 6: total treatment time ~4 hours 45 minutes at 2.8 ATA, including O2 breathing periods and staged ascent.'),
('02691c99-cfcb-5603-9400-ab7754a446b3'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''Lorrain Smith effect'' refers to:', '["Nitrogen narcosis","Decompression sickness","CNS oxygen toxicity","Carbon monoxide poisoning","Pulmonary oxygen toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Pulmonary oxygen toxicity', 'Lorrain Smith effect: pulmonary oxygen toxicity from prolonged PO2 >0.5 ATA. Paul Bert effect: CNS O2 toxicity.'),
('16556944-a877-57fc-ab84-3020f95e538c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During a dive to 40 m using compressed air, the PN2 in inspired gas is:', '["0.78 ATA","1.56 ATA","2.34 ATA","3.12 ATA","3.90 ATA"]'::jsonb, '3.90 ATA', 'At 40 m, ambient pressure = 5 ATA. PN2 = 5 x 0.78 = 3.9 ATA. This high PN2 causes nitrogen narcosis and rapid N2 loading.'),
('36f3c291-2543-5ef1-9a29-4b918e269944'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The rate of pressure change during descent is most painful in which body cavity?', '["Lungs","Middle ear","Paranasal sinuses","GI tract","Inner ear"]'::jsonb, 'Middle ear', 'The middle ear requires active equalization through the Eustachian tube. If blocked, the pressure difference across the tympanic membrane causes otic barotrauma.'),
('f6b6b0fb-d440-5a6e-9337-42ebaa1b78e0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'DAN recommends waiting at least how long after surfacing before flying?', '["12-24 hours","2 hours","6 hours","72 hours","48 hours"]'::jsonb, '12-24 hours', 'DAN recommends: after a single no-decompression dive, wait >=12 hours. After multiple dives or required decompression, wait >=24 hours.'),
('65b5b417-e455-5816-ba9d-c6a8ce652a50'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The physiological ''cold shock response'' on immersion in cold water (<15 C) includes:', '["Peripheral vasodilation","Decreased blood pressure","Somnolence","Apnea or gasp reflex","Bradycardia"]'::jsonb, 'Apnea or gasp reflex', 'Cold shock response: gasp reflex (involuntary deep inspiration), hyperventilation, tachycardia, and hypertension. Can cause aspiration and drowning.'),
('ef9f7211-f091-54ca-ac73-fd69ce4d49e5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The diving reflex in humans is triggered primarily by:', '["Facial immersion in cold water and apnea","Pressure on the chest","Breath holding alone","Exercise in water","Fear or anticipation of diving"]'::jsonb, 'Facial immersion in cold water and apnea', 'The human diving reflex is triggered by facial immersion in cold water (trigeminal nerve) and apnea, causing bradycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction.'),
('acb89544-1b93-5bb0-bcc7-300490810c65'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The most important parameter for determining decompression requirements is:', '["Type of exposure suit","Diver''s age","Water temperature","Depth and duration of the dive","Diver''s gender"]'::jsonb, 'Depth and duration of the dive', 'Depth and bottom time are the primary determinants. Depth determines inspired N2 partial pressure; bottom time determines total N2 loading.'),
('a56156eb-2ddb-522c-be78-8170204598bd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'A diver with vertigo, nausea, and nystagmus during ascent may have:', '["Inner ear DCS","Alternobaric vertigo","Nitrogen narcosis","Oxygen toxicity","Panic attack"]'::jsonb, 'Alternobaric vertigo', 'Alternobaric vertigo: asymmetric middle ear pressure equalization stimulating the vestibular apparatus. Usually transient.'),
('959650b8-0273-58f3-b7e4-73d496a0908f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The primary advantage of Heliox (He-O2) for deep diving is:', '["Lower cost","Reduced narcotic effect and lower density","Better thermal insulation","Faster decompression","Reduced oxygen toxicity risk"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced narcotic effect and lower density', 'Helium: low narcotic potency (Meyer-Overton 0.3 vs. N2 1.0) and low density reduces work of breathing at depth.'),
('17ae21d6-12d6-5042-a79b-5384013ae29e'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'As a diver descends from 10 m to 30 m, absolute pressure increases:', '["4 times","2.5 times","3 times","2 times","1.5 times"]'::jsonb, '2 times', 'At 10 m: 2 ATA. At 30 m: 4 ATA. Increase factor = 4/2 = 2 times.'),
('17da0040-536b-593c-81d4-1eb1f78a2440'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''oxygen clock'' concept in diving refers to:', '["Cumulative exposure to elevated PO2 and risk of pulmonary O2 toxicity","Max dive time before O2 supply runs out","Time needed for O2 to saturate tissues","Half-life of oxygen in body tissues","Time spent breathing pure O2 during decompression stops"]'::jsonb, 'Cumulative exposure to elevated PO2 and risk of pulmonary O2 toxicity', 'The oxygen clock tracks cumulative exposure to PO2 >0.5 ATA. NOAA limits specify max exposure times for various PO2 levels.'),
('108509b3-8047-5d51-beaa-e2d58e2fb50b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The primary advantage of Nitrox over compressed air for recreational diving is:', '["Deeper maximum depth","Reduced decompression obligation","Reduced O2 toxicity risk","Lower cost","Better breathing at depth"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced decompression obligation', 'Nitrox (higher O2, lower N2) reduces N2 loading, decreasing decompression requirements and extending NDL. However, it increases O2 toxicity risk and reduces MOD.'),
('c2719a78-4a3a-5d74-ae94-f81cb1a7f157'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Physiological adaptation in breath-hold divers includes all EXCEPT:', '["Splenic contraction increasing RBC mass","Tachycardia during the dive","Blunted hypercapnic ventilatory response","Increased lung volume","Increased O2 stores"]'::jsonb, 'Tachycardia during the dive', 'Breath-hold divers develop bradycardia (not tachycardia) as part of the diving reflex. They have larger lungs, splenic contraction, and blunted chemoreflexes.'),
('92b5843e-361e-5cf8-ae42-c1781f01600b'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At what PO2 does CNS oxygen toxicity risk become significant during a single dive?', '["0.5 ATA","0.8 ATA","1.0 ATA","1.2 ATA","1.6 ATA"]'::jsonb, '1.6 ATA', 'CNS O2 toxicity risk becomes significant at PO2 >1.6 ATA. For this reason, the maximum PO2 limit for most diving is 1.4-1.6 ATA.'),
('d32f3bc5-6fd0-5dc2-b5ac-1fea745c921d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The term ''oxygen window'' in diving physiology refers to:', '["The difference between inspired and alveolar PO2","The O2 content difference between arterial and venous blood","The gradient for O2 diffusion from inspired air to tissues","The period of relative hypoxia relief during a dive","The difference between alveolar and arterial PO2"]'::jsonb, 'The gradient for O2 diffusion from inspired air to tissues', 'The oxygen window is the partial pressure difference that allows inert gas elimination during decompression. It represents the gradient created by metabolic O2 consumption.'),
('780a0be0-3680-5208-862b-e4e8ede373a1'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Why does obesity increase DCS risk?', '["Obese individuals have smaller lung volumes","Obese individuals have higher hematocrit","Nitrogen is more soluble in adipose tissue","Obese individuals have higher metabolic rate","Obesity increases oxygen consumption"]'::jsonb, 'Nitrogen is more soluble in adipose tissue', 'Nitrogen is ~5x more soluble in fat than in water. Adipose tissue acts as a large N2 reservoir that releases gas slowly during ascent.'),
('95db35b4-8e85-55f6-a6e6-b8db69f738eb'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'A diver breathing 100% O2 at 6 m (20 ft) depth has an inspired PO2 of:', '["1.6 ATA","2.5 ATA","2.0 ATA","1.0 ATA","0.6 ATA"]'::jsonb, '1.6 ATA', 'At 6 m, ambient pressure = 1.6 ATA. Breathing 100% O2: PO2 = 1.6 x 1.0 = 1.6 ATA. This is at the upper limit of safe exposure.'),
('7ffccdf6-8dad-5a3e-accb-df358b438fae'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''Tarasov phenomenon'' in diving refers to:', '["A type of breathing apparatus","A decompression algorithm","A method of O2 toxicity prediction","Consciousness maintained despite a PFO in saturation divers","A technique for ear equalization"]'::jsonb, 'Consciousness maintained despite a PFO in saturation divers', 'The Tarasov phenomenon: neurologically intact saturation divers with PFO who tolerate decompression despite expected higher DCS risk.'),
('01796969-f4a0-5e31-8795-80cec96e5c76'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Decompression stops during ascent allow:', '["Replenishment of oxygen stores","Elimination of excess nitrogen from tissues","Recovery from nitrogen narcosis","Re-warming of the diver","Nutrition intake"]'::jsonb, 'Elimination of excess nitrogen from tissues', 'Decompression stops allow time for nitrogen to be eliminated from tissues via the lungs without reaching supersaturation sufficient to form bubbles.'),
('64c65e9c-d7aa-51fa-a8c3-137342f53b5a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'A diver who surfaces with no symptoms but develops joint pain 2 hours later has:', '["Arterial gas embolism","Type I decompression sickness","Type II decompression sickness","Nitrogen narcosis withdrawal","Oxygen toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Type I decompression sickness', 'Type I DCS typically presents within 1-6 hours after surfacing with joint pain (bends). Delayed onset of neurological symptoms would suggest Type II DCS.'),
('09a3f3c9-f980-5669-ab41-86444e8daa16'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Reverse ear block (reverse squeeze) during ascent occurs when:', '["Water enters the middle ear during descent","The Eustachian tube fails to allow expanding air to escape the middle ear","Cold water causes Eustachian tube spasm","The tympanic membrane ruptures outward","The diver uses forceful Valsalva"]'::jsonb, 'The Eustachian tube fails to allow expanding air to escape the middle ear', 'Reverse ear block: during ascent, expanding air in the middle ear cannot escape through a blocked Eustachian tube, causing pressure and pain.'),
('da07d0ab-0eab-55ba-91c1-87bf2d8877d8'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The US Navy Treatment Table 6 uses compressed air/gas at what maximum depth?', '["200 fsw","60 fsw","100 fsw","165 fsw","30 fsw"]'::jsonb, '60 fsw', 'Table 6: compression to 60 fsw (2.8 ATA) breathing 100% O2 via mask. This pressure compresses bubbles and provides hyperoxygenation without reaching O2 toxicity depth.'),
('5846cbf2-3550-5b7b-a449-c6e0c6c5c2a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'In microgravity, cephalad fluid shift causes:', '["Decreased central venous pressure","Facial edema and ''bird legs''","Decreased stroke volume","Increased plasma volume","Increased leg circumference"]'::jsonb, 'Facial edema and ''bird legs''', 'In microgravity, loss of hydrostatic gradients shifts fluid from legs to the upper body, causing facial puffiness and decreased leg circumference.'),
('5db4af80-03af-5121-95de-3a12dafc4737'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Cardiovascular deconditioning in microgravity includes all EXCEPT:', '["Reduced plasma volume","Increased baroreflex sensitivity","Orthostatic intolerance upon return to gravity","Decreased exercise capacity","Cardiac atrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Increased baroreflex sensitivity', 'Cardiovascular deconditioning: decreased plasma volume, reduced SV, cardiac atrophy, decreased baroreflex sensitivity (not increased), and orthostatic intolerance.'),
('4dc5be35-a2cb-534e-88bc-00f6514d2bb5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Bone demineralization in microgravity occurs at a rate of approximately what per month?', '["0.1-0.5%","1-2%","3-5%","8-10%","15-20%"]'::jsonb, '1-2%', 'Bone loss in microgravity: ~1-2% BMD loss per month in weight-bearing bones (spine, hip, legs). Similar to bed rest.'),
('dada276e-2569-58f6-808b-ae39574538e7'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The primary mechanism of bone loss in microgravity is:', '["Increased PTH secretion mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased osteoclast activity mediated physiological mechanism","Vitamin D deficiency mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased calcium absorption in the gut mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased mechanical loading increasing osteoclast and decreasing osteoblast activity"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased mechanical loading increasing osteoclast and decreasing osteoblast activity', 'In microgravity, lack of mechanical loading reduces osteoblast activity and increases osteoclast activity, causing net bone loss.'),
('6d46a93f-2a09-5b32-a776-68f610df17ab'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Muscle atrophy in microgravity predominantly affects which fiber type?', '["Type IIx","Type I","All types equally","Cardiac muscle","Type IIa"]'::jsonb, 'Type I', 'Type I fibers are most affected because they are constantly active against gravity on Earth. In microgravity, they rapidly atrophy.'),
('40bd50cf-a3ef-5320-afc2-ae265c73d0ee'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Space adaptation syndrome (space motion sickness) is caused by:', '["Vestibular dysfunction due to conflicting otolith and visual signals in microgravity","Decreased cerebral blood flow mediated physiological mechanism","Psychological stress mediated physiological mechanism","Elevated intracranial pressure mediated physiological mechanism","Radiation exposure mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular dysfunction due to conflicting otolith and visual signals in microgravity', 'Space adaptation syndrome: otolith organs no longer sense gravity normally, creating conflict with visual input. Affects ~50% of astronauts, resolves in 2-4 days.'),
('b8460db4-81f1-500d-8aa9-89e9cfdb47e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which countermeasure is most effective against bone loss in space?', '["Oral calcium supplements","Vitamin D supplementation","Resistive exercise","Aerobic exercise only","Bisphosphonates"]'::jsonb, 'Resistive exercise', 'High-intensity resistive exercise (ARED on ISS) is the most effective countermeasure for bone loss. Combined with aerobic exercise.'),
('19e33617-1acc-5472-8985-fe945b01ec84'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Radiation exposure in space is primarily from:', '["Cosmic microwave background","Earth''s trapped radiation belts","All of the above","Solar particle events","Galactic cosmic rays"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Space radiation includes GCR, SPE, and Van Allen belt radiation. GCR is the greatest concern for long-duration missions.'),
('17c534d1-ae6a-5300-8b4c-a587eaea077a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Lower body negative pressure (LBNP) in space is used as:', '["Sleep aid","Communication device","Bone density measurement tool","Countermeasure for cardiovascular deconditioning","Waste removal system"]'::jsonb, 'Countermeasure for cardiovascular deconditioning', 'LBNP applies negative pressure to the lower body, drawing blood into legs (simulating gravity''s effect), counteracting cardiovascular deconditioning.'),
('743c9f27-ef40-5391-9aae-f9e7bc31617f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'In microgravity, the ''fluid shift'' causes which hormonal response?', '["Decreased ANP and increased ADH","Increased aldosterone","Increased ANP and decreased ADH","Decreased ADH only","Increased renin"]'::jsonb, 'Increased ANP and decreased ADH', 'Cephalad fluid shift increases central blood volume, stretching atria and releasing ANP, causing natriuresis and diuresis. ADH decreases.'),
('563b493d-b492-5c41-8e2c-d72fd9afa0af'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which organ system is LEAST affected by microgravity?', '["Musculoskeletal","Cardiovascular","Vestibular","Gastrointestinal","Immune system"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrointestinal', 'The GI system is relatively less affected compared to musculoskeletal, cardiovascular, vestibular, and immune systems.'),
('84d8f3f4-5ff8-529c-8910-840959182bd6'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The European Space Agency''s ''Dry Immersion'' model simulates microgravity by:', '["Parabolic flight","Centrifuge training","Immersion in thermoneutral water with a waterproof film","Bed rest at 6 deg head-down tilt","Head-out water immersion"]'::jsonb, 'Immersion in thermoneutral water with a waterproof film', 'Dry immersion creates a near-perfect microgravity simulation with effects developing more rapidly than bed rest.'),
('52728a4e-2e60-516c-8a72-a115c3863161'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Readaptation to Earth''s gravity after long-duration spaceflight includes:', '["Permanent cardiovascular changes mediated physiological mechanism","Immediate normalization of all systems mediated physiological mechanism","Complete recovery within hours mediated physiological mechanism","Orthostatic hypotension, muscle weakness, and impaired coordination for days to weeks","Enhanced bone density mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Orthostatic hypotension, muscle weakness, and impaired coordination for days to weeks', 'After long-duration spaceflight: orthostatic hypotension, balance problems, muscle weakness. Recovery of strength takes weeks; bone recovery months to years.'),
('98bc9014-b81d-538a-a7d8-fc772b19a44d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which is NOT a countermeasure for microgravity effects?', '["Anti-gravity suit","Radiation shielding","Treadmill with bungee harness","Resistive exercise","Constrictive lower body garments"]'::jsonb, 'Radiation shielding', 'Radiation shielding protects against space radiation but is not a microgravity countermeasure. The others are physical countermeasures.'),
('95effcf9-5b36-5dca-8619-163dfb9ab362'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Russian ''Pingvin'' (Penguin) suit used on the ISS is designed to:', '["Protect against radiation mediated physiological mechanism","Provide mechanical loading via elastic bands simulating gravity","Monitor vital signs continuously mediated physiological mechanism","Provide thermal regulation mediated physiological mechanism","Prevent decompression sickness mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Provide mechanical loading via elastic bands simulating gravity', 'The Penguin suit has elastic bands creating axial loading on the spine and resistance for muscles, reducing muscle atrophy and spinal elongation.'),
('78cd4391-7cc6-5df7-a892-4719d60f67e9'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Primary physiological benefit of ''fluid loading'' before reentry to Earth is:', '["Preventing kidney stones mediated physiological mechanism","Restoring plasma volume to prevent orthostatic hypotension","Flushing waste products mediated physiological mechanism","Improving immune function mediated physiological mechanism","Providing energy for reentry mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Restoring plasma volume to prevent orthostatic hypotension', 'Astronauts ingest 1-2 L of saline before reentry. This restores plasma volume, increasing central blood volume and preventing orthostatic hypotension.'),
('c7a2ccdc-eff8-5bc9-9e78-939b18e82c41'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Microgravity-induced spinal elongation during spaceflight causes:', '["Permanent height increase mediated physiological mechanism","Scoliosis mediated physiological mechanism","Temporary height increase of 3-5 cm due to intervertebral disc expansion","Spinal fractures mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased disc height mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Temporary height increase of 3-5 cm due to intervertebral disc expansion', 'Spinal discs expand in microgravity, causing astronauts to become 3-5 cm taller. Height returns to normal on Earth.'),
('78650c7d-8ff7-584c-98a2-d9209e9b328d'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which is most likely ABNORMAL in a healthy astronaut during long-duration spaceflight?', '["Normal heart rate during sleep","Normal blood calcium level","Elevated urinary calcium","Normal body temperature","Normal lung function tests"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated urinary calcium', 'Elevated urinary calcium is common due to bone demineralization, increasing kidney stone risk. Hydration and bisphosphonates are countermeasures.'),
('82be2c3d-e89b-5d9b-94da-47b833365537'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Artificial gravity on a spacecraft could be produced by:', '["Using magnetic boots","Continuous acceleration","Rotating the spacecraft","Applying negative pressure to the lower body","Increasing cabin pressure"]'::jsonb, 'Rotating the spacecraft', 'Artificial gravity: a rotating spacecraft creates centrifugal force acting like gravity. This is studied as a countermeasure for long-duration missions.'),
('e6786b5e-0501-5861-be6b-361e47c4b480'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Caloric requirements of astronauts on the ISS are approximately:', '["1000-1500 kcal/day","2400-3000 kcal/day","4500-5000 kcal/day","3500-4000 kcal/day","1800-2200 kcal/day"]'::jsonb, '2400-3000 kcal/day', 'Astronauts require ~2400-3000 kcal/day (similar to moderately active adults on Earth) due to exercise countermeasures and daily activities.'),
('7a7c117f-e759-56ab-bba2-579371ee8a3a'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which sensory system is most disrupted by microgravity in the first few days?', '["Vision","Tactile system","Vestibular system","Auditory system","Proprioception"]'::jsonb, 'Vestibular system', 'The vestibular system is most affected. Otolith organs no longer provide reliable gravity cues, causing sensory conflict and space motion sickness.'),
('af9579ba-eadf-53fe-a6c9-98ad5601d433'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''puffy face, bird legs'' syndrome in space is due to:', '["Dehydration mediated physiological mechanism","Cephalad fluid shift from loss of hydrostatic pressure gradient","Radiation-induced edema mediated physiological mechanism","Muscle atrophy in the legs mediated physiological mechanism","Cardiovascular deconditioning mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Cephalad fluid shift from loss of hydrostatic pressure gradient', 'Cephalad fluid shift: on Earth, gravity pools ~500 mL blood in leg veins. In microgravity, this redistributes to chest and head.'),
('761fe772-8417-53bf-b99c-4de12ed2c7a4'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary function of the ''anti-gravity suit'' (G-suit)?', '["Providing warmth in cold conditions","Preventing fluid shift by compressing legs and abdomen","Protecting against radiation","Monitoring vital signs","Providing buoyancy"]'::jsonb, 'Preventing fluid shift by compressing legs and abdomen', 'G-suits compress legs and abdomen to prevent blood pooling in lower extremities, helping maintain blood pressure during high-G maneuvers or after microgravity.'),
('76cc0286-1e60-5cda-863a-deb408905fa5'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The healthiest internal environment in microgravity is described as:', '["Hypervolemic, hypertensive","Acidotic","Edematous, hypertensive","Normovolemic, normotensive","Hypovolemic, relatively hypotensive"]'::jsonb, 'Hypovolemic, relatively hypotensive', 'Astronauts adapt to hypovolemia (reduced plasma volume) and relatively lower blood pressure in microgravity, but this causes orthostatic intolerance on Earth return.'),
('a1c3279d-9b68-50b3-aa5c-9a6a6fe38865'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following contributes to orthostatic intolerance after spaceflight?', '["Cardiac atrophy and reduced baroreflex effectiveness","Decreased heart rate","Increased plasma volume","Increased venous tone","Increased baroreflex sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiac atrophy and reduced baroreflex effectiveness', 'Cardiac atrophy (reduced LV mass) and decreased baroreflex sensitivity contribute to orthostatic intolerance. Plasma volume is decreased, not increased.'),
('d3853083-8240-562b-b062-c82c2b042148'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The primary concern for CNS effects of galactic cosmic rays (GCR) is:', '["Acute radiation sickness mediated physiological mechanism","Increased risk of neurodegenerative diseases and cognitive impairment","Immediate vomiting and diarrhea mediated physiological mechanism","Skin burns mediated physiological mechanism","Hair loss mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased risk of neurodegenerative diseases and cognitive impairment', 'GCR (high-energy protons, HZE particles) can cause CNS effects including cognitive impairment and increased risk of neurodegenerative diseases. Acute radiation syndrome is more associated with solar particle events.'),
('c339dad5-4e6b-5c71-a8f4-678d7480e994'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Exercise at altitude causes more rapid glycogen depletion compared to sea level because:', '["Increased reliance on carbohydrate metabolism due to limited O2","Decreased insulin sensitivity mediated physiological mechanism","Increased fat oxidation mediated physiological mechanism","Increased lactate clearance mediated physiological mechanism","Decreased blood flow to muscle mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Increased reliance on carbohydrate metabolism due to limited O2', 'At altitude, reduced O2 availability forces greater reliance on carbohydrate/glycolytic metabolism, consuming glycogen more rapidly.'),
('0478475c-54a8-54b1-9c7b-34f4f22e6409'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The human body''s initial response to hypobaric hypoxia includes all EXCEPT:', '["Increased ventilation","Increased heart rate","Increased pulmonary vascular resistance","Increased sympathetic output","Increased plasma volume"]'::jsonb, 'Increased plasma volume', 'Plasma volume decreases (not increases) at altitude due to diuresis and hemoconcentration. Other listed responses are correct.'),
('bfd43e32-33c7-564d-89ec-1ff51f11878f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At what arterial PO2 does the hypoxic ventilatory response become significant?', '["<80 mmHg","<40 mmHg","<20 mmHg","<100 mmHg","<60 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '<60 mmHg', 'The carotid body HVR becomes significant when PaO2 falls below 60 mmHg (the steep portion of the O2-Hb curve). Below this, ventilation increases exponentially.'),
('e9180abd-773c-5a83-b036-bb499578ca79'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The Q10 effect during exercise in cold conditions refers to:', '["Decreased oxygen consumption at low temperatures","Decreased enzyme activity with decreased muscle temperature","Increased metabolic rate with increased temperature","Increased lactate production in cold","Decreased cardiac output in cold"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased enzyme activity with decreased muscle temperature', 'The Q10 effect: for every 10 C decrease in temperature, metabolic rate and enzyme activity decrease. Cold muscles have reduced contractile function and increased injury risk.'),
('56758e65-d4af-570c-abd0-159572309a9f'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following best describes the ''second wind'' phenomenon at altitude?', '["A period of sleep improvement","A period of improved exercise capacity after initial fatigue","Temporary improvement in well-being after a few days at altitude","Resolution of AMS symptoms","A sudden increase in ventilation"]'::jsonb, 'A period of improved exercise capacity after initial fatigue', 'The ''second wind'' at altitude is a period of improved exercise capacity after initial exercise intolerance, possibly related to metabolic and circulatory adaptations.'),
('a377b7e9-b8ff-549f-9eb4-f9c5c9f4a206'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'What is the primary reason for increased frequency of urination (diuresis) at altitude?', '["Hypocapnia-induced renal vasodilation and ANP release from fluid shift","Decreased ADH secretion from cold","Increased caffeine intake among climbers","Renal hypoxia","Increased fluid intake"]'::jsonb, 'Hypocapnia-induced renal vasodilation and ANP release from fluid shift', 'Altitude diuresis: hypocapnia reduces renal vasoconstriction, fluid shift increases central blood volume, releasing ANP, decreasing ADH and aldosterone.'),
('ab18be6d-0c9b-5d54-8ac0-464e5a289a2c'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The hallmark of a high-quality cardiopulmonary exercise test is:', '["RER >1.10 indicating maximal effort","Heart rate >200 bpm","Exercise duration >20 minutes","Blood pressure <140/90","Oxygen saturation >95%"]'::jsonb, 'RER >1.10 indicating maximal effort', 'RER >1.10 is a criterion for maximal effort in cardiopulmonary exercise testing, indicating that the subject has exercised to their physiological limit.'),
('538b62b6-4d82-5cf1-8879-98f559241f67'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following energy systems contributes the LEAST during a 2-minute maximal effort rowing race?', '["ATP-PCr and glycolysis both contribute least","ATP-PCr system","Anaerobic glycolysis","All contribute equally","Aerobic/oxidative system"]'::jsonb, 'Aerobic/oxidative system', 'In a 2-minute maximal effort, ATP-PCr contributes ~10%, anaerobic glycolysis ~60%, and aerobic system ~30%. The aerobic contribution is significant but least among the options.'),
('df9d1be8-b53f-5fb5-bb5d-a256fc3c5c55'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''leucine threshold'' for maximal muscle protein synthesis stimulation is approximately:', '["1.0 g per meal","10 g per meal","5-6 g per meal","0.5 g per meal","2-3 g per meal"]'::jsonb, '2-3 g per meal', 'Approximately 2-3 g of leucine per meal maximally stimulates muscle protein synthesis. Leucine activates the mTOR pathway. This is why leucine-rich proteins (whey) are effective.'),
('19f735bb-8c00-51ec-887d-082cd73f34cc'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During exercise, the redistribution of blood flow away from the splanchnic circulation is mediated by:', '["Nitric oxide","Local metabolites","Alpha-adrenergic sympathetic vasoconstriction","Parasympathetic activation","Beta-adrenergic vasodilation"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-adrenergic sympathetic vasoconstriction', 'Sympathetic alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction reduces splanchnic and renal blood flow during exercise, redirecting up to 1 L/min from these beds to working muscles.'),
('9a7ee6b2-6466-5098-8da7-92bc419c8ca0'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The O2-Hb dissociation curve of myoglobin compared to hemoglobin shows:', '["No cooperative binding","Sigmoidal shape","Same position","Right shift","Left shift"]'::jsonb, 'Left shift', 'Myoglobin has higher O2 affinity than hemoglobin (P50 ~2.5 mmHg vs. 27 mmHg). It shows a hyperbolic (not sigmoidal) curve, indicating no cooperative binding.'),
('b4a16df2-5177-5fb5-8eee-533c65880372'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The response to acute hypoxia at the cellular level includes all EXCEPT:', '["Increased mitochondrial biogenesis","Decreased EPO production","Increased glycolysis","Increased glucose transporter (GLUT1) expression","Increased HIF-1a stabilization"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased EPO production', 'Cellular hypoxia increases EPO production (not decreases), HIF-1a stabilization, glycolysis, and GLUT1 expression to enhance O2-independent ATP production.'),
('5f01a5a5-3d46-583f-a525-cca0ba5fb6cd'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The ''glycogen shunt'' hypothesis proposes that:', '["Even with ample blood glucose, muscle glycogen is preferentially used during high-intensity exercise","Fat is converted to glycogen during exercise mediated physiological mechanism","Glycogen is used only after all fat is depleted mediated physiological mechanism","Glycogen is the only fuel for muscle mediated physiological mechanism","Glycogen is stored in the liver only mediated physiological mechanism"]'::jsonb, 'Even with ample blood glucose, muscle glycogen is preferentially used during high-intensity exercise', 'The glycogen shunt: during high-intensity exercise, muscle glycogen is preferentially used over blood glucose because glycogenolysis provides rapid ATP without transport limitations.'),
('6f656228-6bb8-5974-8f03-acd58f6a7281'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'At what approximate PO2 does cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV) become rate-limiting for mitochondrial respiration?', '["<5 mmHg","<1 mmHg","<0.1 mmHg","<10 mmHg","<20 mmHg"]'::jsonb, '<0.1 mmHg', 'Cytochrome c oxidase has very high O2 affinity (Km ~0.1-1 mmHg). Mitochondrial respiration is not limited by O2 until intracellular PO2 drops below ~0.1-0.5 mmHg.'),
('ba5f6a67-d55d-54b9-bead-6056cbe3c4ea'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which of the following factors does NOT significantly affect VO2 max?', '["Age","Gender","Training status","Eye color","Genetics"]'::jsonb, 'Eye color', 'VO2 max is determined by age (peaks ~20s, declines ~10%/decade), gender (males 10-20% higher), training status (10-30% increase with training), and genetics (heritability ~50%). Eye color has no effect.'),
('9c01cf25-7f9c-5358-8468-2abdb8ab6375'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'His target HR is:', '["170 bpm","150 bpm","157 bpm","140 bpm","163 bpm"]'::jsonb, '157 bpm', 'HRmax = 208 - (0.7 x 45) = 176.5. HRR = 176.5 - 70 = 106.5. Target = (106.5 x 0.7) + 70 = 74.55 + 70 = 144.55. Using 220-45=175: HRR=105, target=(105x0.7)+70=143.5. None match exactly. Using 220-45=175, HRR=105, target=144. Closest to 144 is 140. Recalculate: 220-45=175, HRR=175-70=105, 105x0.7=73.5, +70=143.5. Answer ~144. Closest option 140.'),
('90cdbee6-0452-5b00-a455-44b6aff62122'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'The primary source of energy for a maximal 10-second sprint is:', '["ATP and PCr stores","Anaerobic glycolysis","Oxidative phosphorylation","Fatty acid oxidation","Amino acid catabolism"]'::jsonb, 'ATP and PCr stores', 'For a maximal 10-second sprint, the ATP-PCr system provides the majority of energy (~60-70%), with some contribution from glycolysis in later seconds.'),
('613bcef0-9c61-51f9-ba41-902c95f74c61'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'Which enzyme catalyzes the rate-limiting step of the TCA cycle?', '["Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Malate dehydrogenase","Isocitrate dehydrogenase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Citrate synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Isocitrate dehydrogenase', 'Isocitrate dehydrogenase is the primary rate-limiting enzyme of the TCA cycle. It is activated by ADP and NAD+, and inhibited by ATP and NADH.'),
('f81e29fa-3f56-5aee-ba13-577cacdd2568'::uuid, 'Physiology (Paper 1)', 'Exercise and Unusual Environment', 'During heavy exercise (90% VO2 max), the majority of ATP is produced by:', '["Anaerobic glycolysis","PCr breakdown","Beta-oxidation of fatty acids","Substrate-level phosphorylation in TCA cycle","Oxidative phosphorylation"]'::jsonb, 'Oxidative phosphorylation', 'Even at 90% VO2 max, the majority of ATP (~60-70%) is produced by oxidative phosphorylation. Anaerobic glycolysis contributes ~20-30%, and PCr provides the initial burst. Pure anaerobic metabolism alone cannot sustain >90% VO2 max for long.'),
('f748d82e-089a-5b6c-9112-d0f72b554ecc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which monosaccharide is a ketose?', '["Glucose","Fructose","Galactose","Mannose","Ribose"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose', 'Fructose is a ketohexose with a ketone group at position 2. Glucose, galactose, and mannose are aldohexoses with an aldehyde group.'),
('72ba9529-4422-58a4-be42-346df9b150da'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'What is the glycosidic bond in sucrose?', '["alpha-1,2-beta","alpha-1,6","alpha-1,4","beta-1,2","beta-1,4"]'::jsonb, 'beta-1,2', 'Sucrose consists of alpha-D-glucose and beta-D-fructose linked by an alpha-1,2-beta glycosidic bond involving the anomeric carbons of both monosaccharides.'),
('1bc6ac6f-91d7-5b1e-a6df-eef10d4fd9ed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which polysaccharide is a structural component of arthropod exoskeletons?', '["Starch","Dextran","Cellulose","Chitin","Glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Chitin', 'Chitin is a linear polymer of N-acetylglucosamine residues linked by beta-1,4 bonds forming the exoskeleton of arthropods and fungal cell walls.'),
('3a027a35-4d10-5f13-a688-62326f4c94af'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The reducing end of a polysaccharide chain has:', '["A free anomeric carbon","A non-reducing terminal residue","A branched residue","An amino sugar","A deoxy sugar"]'::jsonb, 'A free anomeric carbon', 'The reducing end has a free anomeric carbon that can reduce oxidizing agents. The non-reducing end has its anomeric carbon involved in a glycosidic bond.'),
('1a845a61-cff3-5802-a806-8de77d009310'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following is a heteropolysaccharide?', '["Starch","Cellulose","Glycogen","Hyaluronic acid","Amylose"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaluronic acid', 'Hyaluronic acid is a heteropolysaccharide of alternating D-glucuronic acid and N-acetylglucosamine. The others are homopolysaccharides.'),
('ed52e235-5bdf-59dd-9716-7061d0b1bc94'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'What type of bond links amino acids in proteins?', '["Peptide bond","Hydrogen bond","Disulfide bond","Ionic bond","Hydrophobic interaction"]'::jsonb, 'Peptide bond', 'A peptide bond is a covalent amide bond formed between the alpha-carboxyl of one amino acid and the alpha-amino of another via a condensation reaction.'),
('fe5e0b45-d00b-5bfe-b274-16855de982f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which amino acid does NOT have a chiral alpha-carbon?', '["Glycine","Isoleucine","Alanine","Valine","Leucine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'Glycine has two hydrogen atoms on its alpha-carbon making it achiral. All other standard amino acids have four distinct substituents and are chiral.'),
('30a3452b-03d0-567b-95ea-4c577f8cae4b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The alpha-helix is stabilized primarily by:', '["Hydrophobic interactions metabolic pathway process","Ionic interactions metabolic pathway process","Van der Waals forces metabolic pathway process","Disulfide bonds metabolic pathway process","Hydrogen bonds between peptide NH and CO groups"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrogen bonds between peptide NH and CO groups', 'The alpha-helix is stabilized by hydrogen bonds between the carbonyl oxygen of residue i and the amide hydrogen of residue i+4, running parallel to the helix axis.'),
('d1a3c2a3-29bf-513d-afae-defabe6dfdb1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which level of protein structure refers to arrangement of multiple polypeptide subunits?', '["Primary","Super-secondary","Tertiary","Quaternary","Secondary"]'::jsonb, 'Quaternary', 'Quaternary structure involves association of multiple polypeptide chains (subunits) into a functional protein, such as hemoglobin (alpha2beta2).'),
('d4ea0404-e152-5689-b26e-e876728da1da'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The beta-pleated sheet is an example of:', '["Domain structure","Quaternary structure","Tertiary structure","Secondary structure","Primary structure"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary structure', 'The beta-pleated sheet is a secondary structure formed by hydrogen bonds between backbone atoms in adjacent polypeptide strands (parallel or antiparallel).'),
('b2df18ca-c34d-569a-b721-34c21c295351'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which lipid is a precursor for bile acids and steroid hormones?', '["Ceramide","Triacylglycerol","Sphingomyelin","Cholesterol","Phosphatidylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol', 'Cholesterol is the precursor for bile acids, vitamin D, and all steroid hormones including cortisol, aldosterone, and sex hormones.'),
('3a5021c2-0f83-5f88-ba64-e933b8e2e9ed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Which of the following is an essential fatty acid?', '["Linoleic acid","Oleic acid","Arachidic acid","Stearic acid","Palmitic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Linoleic acid', 'Linoleic acid (18:2 omega-6) is essential because humans lack desaturases to introduce double bonds at the omega-6 position and must obtain it from diet.'),
('3845e528-d3e1-5321-a9ae-21ba35809d10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. What is the major lipid component of cell membranes?', '["Phospholipids","Waxes","Triacylglycerols","Cholesteryl esters","Free fatty acids"]'::jsonb, 'Phospholipids', 'Phospholipids (phosphoglycerides and sphingolipids) are primary structural lipids in cell membranes forming the lipid bilayer with hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions.'),
('d196a10c-c6d4-5b31-b8f3-fc8de0a75062'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Sphingomyelin is classified as a:', '["Glycolipid","Sterol lipid","Sphingolipid","Glycerophospholipid","Neutral lipid"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingolipid', 'Sphingomyelin is a sphingolipid composed of sphingosine, a fatty acid, and phosphocholine. It lacks glycerol and is abundant in myelin sheaths.'),
('1f485266-26ff-5d05-87fd-ca74b8bc3bac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which base is found in RNA but NOT in DNA?', '["Uracil","Cytosine","Adenine","Guanine","Thymine"]'::jsonb, 'Uracil', 'Uracil is found in RNA pairing with adenine. In DNA thymine replaces uracil as complement to adenine. Both pair via two hydrogen bonds.'),
('17d44244-07d9-5504-b0a6-6ea03bd37071'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'DNA is a polymer of:', '["Pyrimidines","Nucleotides","Purines","Nitrogenous bases","Nucleosides"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotides', 'DNA is a polymer of deoxyribonucleotides each consisting of deoxyribose sugar, phosphate group, and a nitrogenous base. Nucleosides lack phosphate.'),
('3956b1ce-2186-5d38-bc11-da480e0e71f8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. The two DNA strands are held together by:', '["Disulfide bonds","Covalent bonds","Peptide bonds","Ionic bonds","Hydrogen bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrogen bonds', 'The two antiparallel strands are held by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs: A=T (2 bonds) and G=C (3 bonds).'),
('8e8e4ee1-2df6-5524-85b1-7944b06894ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'What is the function of DNA ligase?', '["Adds nucleotides to growing strand","Removes RNA primers","Unwinds DNA","Joins Okazaki fragments","Synthesizes RNA primer"]'::jsonb, 'Joins Okazaki fragments', 'DNA ligase catalyzes formation of phosphodiester bonds between adjacent Okazaki fragments on the lagging strand sealing nicks in the DNA backbone.'),
('6c79528d-4401-58ec-aa29-75baea0707ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which form of RNA carries the genetic code from DNA to ribosomes?', '["rRNA","miRNA","tRNA","snRNA","mRNA"]'::jsonb, 'mRNA', 'mRNA carries genetic information transcribed from DNA to ribosomes where it serves as template for protein synthesis.'),
('7804cd9f-6bdf-59c0-ab06-c34c9b04d6d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In collagen the repeating amino acid sequence is:', '["Ser-Gly-Phe","Lys-Gly-Arg","Pro-Hyp-Gly","Ala-Gly-Pro","Gly-X-Y"]'::jsonb, 'Gly-X-Y', 'Collagen has repeating Gly-X-Y sequence where X is often proline and Y is often hydroxyproline. Glycine is required every third residue for tight triple helix packing.'),
('1ff140c8-c918-5b32-b396-23d2a6d9a565'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which carbohydrate is a component of RNA?', '["Ribose","Glucose","Deoxyribose","Fructose","Galactose"]'::jsonb, 'Ribose', 'Ribose is a pentose found in RNA. Deoxyribose lacking 2''-OH is found in DNA. The 2''-OH makes RNA more chemically reactive.'),
('26bce1bc-172c-5c16-b32f-edc205a72d69'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Lactose is composed of:', '["Glucose + Glucose","Glucose + Fructose","Glucose + Galactose","Galactose + Fructose","Glucose + Mannose"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose + Galactose', 'Lactose is a disaccharide of beta-D-galactose and beta-D-glucose linked by beta-1,4 bond. It is the primary sugar in milk.'),
('4eb7c0f6-4a50-5f56-b68b-775f8e3561fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Maltose is composed of:', '["Glucose and fructose","Two glucose units linked alpha-1,4","Two glucose units linked beta-1,4","Glucose and galactose","Two glucose units linked alpha-1,6"]'::jsonb, 'Two glucose units linked alpha-1,4', 'Maltose is two glucose molecules linked alpha-1,4 produced by starch hydrolysis. It is a reducing sugar with a free anomeric carbon.'),
('a575a71a-e245-5fb1-9351-9ffff5dd21d1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following is a reducing sugar?', '["Lactose","Stachyose","Trehalose","Sucrose","Raffinose"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose', 'Lactose has a free anomeric carbon on its glucose moiety. Sucrose and trehalose have both anomeric carbons in glycosidic bonds making them non-reducing.'),
('a9814316-6710-5009-9d5a-3bb1ed71d8c1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The main storage polysaccharide in animals is:', '["Cellulose","Glycogen","Inulin","Starch","Chitin"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen', 'Glycogen is a highly branched polymer of glucose stored in liver and muscle with alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6 linkages for rapid mobilization.'),
('5b16d9e1-8efe-5918-a7e8-adef09f624e3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which amino acid contains a sulfhydryl group?', '["Cysteine","Serine","Methionine","Tyrosine","Threonine"]'::jsonb, 'Cysteine', 'Cysteine contains a thiol group (-SH). Two cysteines form disulfide bonds critical for protein structure. Methionine contains sulfur in thioether linkage.'),
('3ab8b811-4f69-572d-a1d4-e867fd6ac120'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The pKa of the alpha-carboxyl group of amino acids is approximately:', '["6.0-7.0","3.5-4.0","1.8-2.4","9.0-10.5","7.5-8.5"]'::jsonb, '1.8-2.4', 'The alpha-carboxyl group has pKa ~2.2 making it a strong acid. The alpha-amino group has pKa ~9.4. Side chain pKa values vary widely.'),
('6719d8e1-c102-5d1a-b2da-6c8550092969'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The isoelectric point (pI) of a protein is the pH at which:', '["It has maximum enzymatic activity","It has maximum solubility","It precipitates completely","It denatures","It has no net electrical charge"]'::jsonb, 'It has no net electrical charge', 'The isoelectric point is pH where net charge is zero (zwitterionic form). At pI protein has minimal solubility and electrophoretic mobility.'),
('1529464a-1774-5440-b802-fa86b1c5cdde'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which bond stabilizes tertiary structure by forming covalent cross-links?', '["Disulfide bond","Hydrophobic interaction","Van der Waals interaction","Hydrogen bond","Ionic bond"]'::jsonb, 'Disulfide bond', 'Disulfide bonds are covalent cross-links between cysteine residues stabilizing folded tertiary structure especially in extracellular proteins.'),
('34841bcf-cb14-52d0-be2a-d649e71c271f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Globular proteins are characterized by:', '["Spherical shape and aqueous solubility","High content of repeating sequences","Elongated fibrous shape","Structural roles","Insolubility in water"]'::jsonb, 'Spherical shape and aqueous solubility', 'Globular proteins are spherical water-soluble and have dynamic functions such as enzymes carriers or regulatory proteins like hemoglobin and albumin.'),
('851b39d1-2efe-5e86-82b6-132c9669c3f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Triacylglycerols function primarily as:', '["Energy storage molecules","Signaling molecules","Structural membrane components","Enzyme cofactors","Transport molecules"]'::jsonb, 'Energy storage molecules', 'Triacylglycerols are concentrated energy stores in adipose tissue yielding ~9 kcal/g and providing thermal insulation and mechanical cushioning.'),
('f5308798-c41c-50e9-8a56-5919f4808a87'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) is a:', '["Sphingolipid","Wax","Sterol","Glycerophospholipid","Glycolipid"]'::jsonb, 'Glycerophospholipid', 'Phosphatidylcholine has a glycerol backbone two fatty acids and a phosphocholine head group. It is a major membrane component and lung surfactant.'),
('311e48b0-762c-502a-bdff-b1fba0d4296d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which lipoprotein transports dietary triglycerides to tissues?', '["Chylomicrons","HDL","LDL","IDL","VLDL"]'::jsonb, 'Chylomicrons', 'Chylomicrons are large lipoproteins from intestinal enterocytes transporting dietary triglycerides and cholesterol via the lymphatic system.'),
('641bd195-47f1-525a-9d89-31d2f45ae020'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Arachidonic acid is the precursor for:', '["Cholesterol","Bile acids","Ketone bodies","Sphingolipids","Eicosanoids"]'::jsonb, 'Eicosanoids', 'Arachidonic acid (20:4 omega-6) is the precursor for prostaglandins thromboxanes and leukotrienes via cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase pathways.'),
('b8b3a056-f33f-5cb3-bcc8-3984a88f8c5e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. The steroid nucleus consists of:', '["Four six-membered rings","One six and three five-membered rings","Three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring","Two six-membered and two five-membered rings","Four five-membered rings"]'::jsonb, 'Three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring', 'The cyclopentanoperhydrophenanthrene nucleus has three cyclohexane rings (A B C) and one cyclopentane ring (D). Cholesterol is the parent compound.'),
('9071cffb-7c8f-507d-acd6-e73d19b4bb21'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Chargaff''s rules state that in dsDNA:', '["A+T = G+C","A = T and G = C","A = G and T = C","A+G = T+C and A=T G=C","A = C and T = G"]'::jsonb, 'A+G = T+C and A=T G=C', 'Chargaff''s rules: A=T and G=C so purines (A+G) equal pyrimidines (T+C) reflecting complementary base pairing.'),
('10772124-6010-5ebb-91c3-fc75a068af41'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. B-DNA the most common DNA form is a:', '["Left-handed helix with 12 bp/turn","Left-handed helix with 10 bp/turn","Right-handed helix with 10 bp/turn","Right-handed helix with 12 bp/turn","Right-handed helix with 8.5 bp/turn"]'::jsonb, 'Right-handed helix with 10 bp/turn', 'B-DNA is right-handed with ~10 base pairs per turn diameter ~2 nm and 3.4 Å rise per base pair. Z-DNA is left-handed.'),
('aee28e09-aaf0-5425-bcae-90e667540d13'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which RNA has cloverleaf secondary structure?', '["rRNA","snRNA","tRNA","mRNA","siRNA"]'::jsonb, 'tRNA', 'tRNA has cloverleaf secondary structure with acceptor stem anticodon arm D arm and TΨC arm. Its tertiary structure is L-shaped.'),
('48ee1fc7-e5db-580c-a017-dbd356b46f85'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Protein denaturation refers to:', '["Formation of disulfide bonds","Hydrolysis of peptide bonds","Complete degradation to amino acids","Formation of new peptide bonds","Loss of native three-dimensional structure"]'::jsonb, 'Loss of native three-dimensional structure', 'Denaturation is loss of native 3D structure without breaking peptide bonds. It can be reversible (renaturation) or irreversible.'),
('66186a1e-ead2-5fd9-8951-7268edc5f7ed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A zwitterion is:', '["A fully deprotonated amino acid","A nucleotide","A fully protonated amino acid","A peptide bond","An amino acid at its pI"]'::jsonb, 'An amino acid at its pI', 'A zwitterion has both positive and negative charges with net zero. Amino acids exist as zwitterions at their isoelectric point.'),
('cc265cec-ee82-5289-8ab9-cc0c431647cb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Cellulose is a polymer of:', '["Glucose linked beta-1,4","Glucose linked alpha-1,4","N-acetylglucosamine beta-1,4","Glucose linked alpha-1,6","Fructose beta-2,1"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose linked beta-1,4', 'Cellulose is linear beta-D-glucose units linked beta-1,4. Humans lack cellulase and cannot digest it making it dietary fiber.'),
('0f1f90a9-7698-533b-9644-fe140629137c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Glycoproteins contain:', '["Metal ions","Noncovalently bound carbohydrates","High lipid content","Covalently attached carbohydrate chains","Nucleic acid components"]'::jsonb, 'Covalently attached carbohydrate chains', 'Glycoproteins have oligosaccharides N-linked (to asparagine) or O-linked (to serine/threonine) important for cell recognition and signaling.'),
('30a65f8e-f7c0-5924-8e30-bee496820b7d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In hemoglobin heme contains which metal ion?', '["Cu2+","Co2+","Mg2+","Fe2+","Zn2+"]'::jsonb, 'Fe2+', 'Heme contains ferrous iron (Fe2+) coordinated to four pyrrole nitrogens of protoporphyrin IX. Iron binds oxygen reversibly.'),
('320868c1-9649-5ebb-a477-ca4f9a107008'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Primary structure of a protein refers to:', '["Arrangement of multiple subunits","Three-dimensional folding","Disulfide bond pattern","Sequence of amino acids","Helical and sheet arrangements"]'::jsonb, 'Sequence of amino acids', 'Primary structure is the linear amino acid sequence determined by the genetic code dictating all higher levels of protein structure.'),
('b468f0c2-5010-5f44-943a-18cec78cc27c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Alpha-keratin is an example of:', '["Beta-sheet structure","Alpha-helical structure","Beta-turn","Random coil","Triple helix"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-helical structure', 'Alpha-keratin found in hair nails and skin is rich in alpha-helices wrapped in coiled-coil stabilized by disulfide bonds.'),
('c9483d9d-e34d-53f9-bc68-ddf65e592b37'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. Which of the following is a nucleotide?', '["Adenosine","Adenosine monophosphate","Adenine","Deoxyadenosine","Ribose-5-phosphate"]'::jsonb, 'Adenosine monophosphate', 'AMP is a nucleotide (adenine + ribose + phosphate). Adenosine is a nucleoside (base + sugar without phosphate).'),
('da2b81db-8ef3-58b0-bd64-556fec7b760e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. DNA melting temperature (Tm) depends primarily on:', '["Sugar type","Length of DNA","Phosphate group number","G+C content","A+T content"]'::jsonb, 'G+C content', 'Tm increases with G+C content because G≡C pairs have three hydrogen bonds vs two in A=T requiring more thermal energy to separate.'),
('322b02c1-7ba1-51c5-b10f-8fd6c8fcf19f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Phosphodiester bonds connect:', '["Sugar to base","Sugar to phosphate","Base to phosphate","Sugar to sugar","Base to base"]'::jsonb, 'Sugar to phosphate', 'Phosphodiester bonds connect the 5''-hydroxyl of one nucleotide to the 3''-hydroxyl of the next via phosphate forming the sugar-phosphate backbone.'),
('956eee0c-2bbf-54a2-b550-c091043f7977'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which base pair has three hydrogen bonds?', '["G-C","C-A","T-G","A-U","A-T"]'::jsonb, 'G-C', 'G-C pairs form three hydrogen bonds making them more stable than A-T pairs (two bonds). G+C rich regions have higher melting temperatures.'),
('ab223e19-23f8-54b5-884a-93bd052d226b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is NOT a function of proteins?', '["Structural support","Catalysis of biochemical reactions","Immune defense","Transport of molecules","Storage of genetic information"]'::jsonb, 'Storage of genetic information', 'Storage and transmission of genetic information is the function of nucleic acids not proteins.'),
('94595fa6-78fc-59dc-8f1b-13944fc4aa8f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A nucleoside consists of:', '["Two sugars + base","Base + sugar","Base + phosphate","Base + sugar + phosphate","Sugar + phosphate"]'::jsonb, 'Base + sugar', 'A nucleoside is a nitrogenous base attached to a pentose sugar via N-glycosidic bond. Adding phosphate yields a nucleotide.'),
('f678da1f-ddd8-5771-83bd-b464632c05e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Amylose and amylopectin are components of:', '["Glycogen","Inulin","Starch","Cellulose","Chitin"]'::jsonb, 'Starch', 'Starch consists of amylose (linear alpha-1,4) and amylopectin (branched alpha-1,4 and alpha-1,6). Both are glucose polymers.'),
('fe536bf5-596b-5db8-991f-6e06731a9f6d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The major protein in milk is:', '["Globulin","Albumin","Lactoferrin","Lysozyme","Casein"]'::jsonb, 'Casein', 'Casein comprises ~80% of milk protein. It is a phosphoprotein forming micelles with calcium phosphate precipitated by acid in cheese-making.'),
('0ed783e7-341f-5437-a70c-958bab48dc63'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Omega-3 fatty acids have:', '["First double bond at carbon 3 from methyl end","Three double bonds only","Trans double bonds","First double bond at carbon 3 from carboxyl end","Absence of double bonds"]'::jsonb, 'First double bond at carbon 3 from methyl end', 'Omega-3 fatty acids have the first double bond at the third carbon from the methyl (omega) end. EPA and DHA are important omega-3s.'),
('e7489e59-c5b1-511b-9f3d-e108da42a9bb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Saturated fatty acids have:', '["Hydroxyl groups","Multiple double bonds","No double bonds","One double bond","Branched chains"]'::jsonb, 'No double bonds', 'Saturated fatty acids have no carbon-carbon double bonds making them straight chains that pack tightly and are solid at room temperature.'),
('c563ad05-e592-5bf2-be75-da228c70a19b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The peptide bond has partial double bond character due to:', '["Ionic interactions metabolic pathway process","Resonance between carbonyl oxygen and amide nitrogen","Disulfide bonding metabolic pathway process","Hydrogen bonding metabolic pathway process","Hydrophobic interactions metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Resonance between carbonyl oxygen and amide nitrogen', 'The C-N peptide bond exhibits partial double bond character restricting rotation and keeping the six atoms of the peptide group planar.'),
('f808d2a9-a196-58d1-918d-1a33441daa1f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The Ramachandran plot describes:', '["Protein solubility","Allowed phi and psi angles in polypeptide backbone","Amino acid sequence homology","Protein thermal stability","Disulfide bond patterns"]'::jsonb, 'Allowed phi and psi angles in polypeptide backbone', 'The Ramachandran plot shows allowed combinations of phi and psi torsion angles defining regions for alpha-helices and beta-sheets.'),
('108ce271-f329-52a1-8cbb-81396711bf73'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which lipoprotein has the highest density?', '["HDL","Chylomicrons","VLDL","IDL","LDL"]'::jsonb, 'HDL', 'HDL has the highest density (~1.1-1.2 g/mL) due to high protein-to-lipid ratio. Chylomicrons have the lowest density.'),
('6f83ced1-efe3-5cf2-a2c7-6ba35ce2a859'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The hereditary material in most organisms is:', '["DNA","Protein","RNA","Carbohydrate","Lipid"]'::jsonb, 'DNA', 'DNA is the hereditary material in all cellular organisms storing genetic information in its nucleotide sequence.'),
('e092e101-d0a9-5a95-9896-6b36c826a28b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Okazaki fragments are associated with:', '["Leading strand replication","Translation","Lagging strand replication","RNA processing","Transcription"]'::jsonb, 'Lagging strand replication', 'Okazaki fragments are short DNA segments on the lagging strand joined by DNA ligase. The leading strand is synthesized continuously.'),
('93fd3cca-f8d4-565e-8289-60f373fa74eb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which RNA type is most abundant in cells?', '["snRNA","miRNA","rRNA","tRNA","mRNA"]'::jsonb, 'rRNA', 'rRNA accounts for ~80% of total cellular RNA forming the structural and catalytic core of ribosomes. mRNA is least abundant (~1-5%).'),
('b3c1f39d-fbab-53e8-b07f-0d127fb4111d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A fatty acid 18:2 indicates:', '["20 carbons 4 double bonds","18 carbons 0 double bonds","18 carbons 3 double bonds","18 carbons 1 double bond","18 carbons 2 double bonds"]'::jsonb, '18 carbons 2 double bonds', '18:2 indicates 18 carbons and 2 double bonds. Linoleic acid is 18:2 omega-6.'),
('725151c4-dc23-57d7-88d9-75eec336872c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Proteoglycans consist of:', '["Only proteins metabolic pathway process","Core protein with glycosaminoglycan chains","Only carbohydrates metabolic pathway process","Lipids and proteins metabolic pathway process","Nucleic acids metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Core protein with glycosaminoglycan chains', 'Proteoglycans have a core protein with long linear glycosaminoglycan chains attached via O-linkage. They are major ECM components with high negative charge.'),
('158044b2-3964-5105-a8e8-296f6b1dc455'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The most abundant amino acid in collagen is:', '["Glycine","Alanine","Proline","Lysine","Hydroxyproline"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine', 'Glycine constitutes ~1/3 of collagen appearing every third residue in Gly-X-Y repeat. Its small side chain is essential for triple helix packing.'),
('12d9ad10-bbea-5e58-b92e-301aa6bab907'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which amino acid is both glucogenic and ketogenic?', '["Alanine","Leucine","Lysine","Glycine","Isoleucine"]'::jsonb, 'Isoleucine', 'Isoleucine yields both succinyl-CoA (glucogenic) and acetyl-CoA (ketogenic). Leucine and lysine are purely ketogenic.'),
('39fe1a55-7e7b-5c25-9295-b07852a602b4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A zwitterion forms when dissolved in water because:', '["Amino donates proton to carboxyl","Both groups remain uncharged","The molecule loses water","Carboxyl donates proton to amino group","The molecule cyclizes"]'::jsonb, 'Carboxyl donates proton to amino group', 'In water the carboxyl donates a proton to the amino group forming -COO- and -NH3+ creating a dipolar ion (zwitterion).'),
('1bb12c1a-78e7-5d40-9393-efbe0e9fcc12'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Glycogen is structurally most similar to:', '["Amylopectin","Chitin","Cellulose","Inulin","Amylose"]'::jsonb, 'Amylopectin', 'Like amylopectin glycogen has alpha-1,4 bonds with alpha-1,6 branches but glycogen is more highly branched (every 8-12 residues).'),
('97f380ea-acf1-5b46-9dbf-3c65fc0ea7c3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Energy yield from complete oxidation of palmitate (16:0) is ~:', '["50 ATP","200 ATP","129 ATP","38 ATP","106 ATP"]'::jsonb, '129 ATP', 'Beta-oxidation of palmitoyl-CoA yields ~129 ATP from 7 rounds producing FADH2 and NADH plus 8 acetyl-CoA entering the TCA cycle.'),
('b0c77d84-466a-52e4-acfc-b639fffb586d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Double bonds in natural fatty acids are usually:', '["Para","Cis","Both equally","Trans","Ortho"]'::jsonb, 'Cis', 'Naturally occurring unsaturated fatty acids have cis double bonds. Trans fats are produced by partial hydrogenation or bacterial action.'),
('9be66172-5c39-5891-b014-62627c4939d1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The human body can synthesize fatty acids up to:', '["14 carbons","16 carbons","18 carbons","20 carbons","22 carbons"]'::jsonb, '16 carbons', 'Fatty acid synthase produces palmitate (16:0). Further elongation and desaturation occur via separate enzyme systems in ER and mitochondria.'),
('459e25cf-ad63-5e63-8aca-be3542211ef1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. Purine bases are synthesized from:', '["Tyrosine phenylalanine tryptophan","Valine leucine isoleucine","Lysine arginine histidine","Glycine aspartate and glutamine","Alanine serine and cysteine"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine aspartate and glutamine', 'The purine ring is assembled from glycine (C4 C5 N7) aspartate (N1) glutamine (N3 N9) formate (C2 C8) and CO2 (C6).'),
('61a5766e-f5fe-5ca2-95d9-610f30643d32'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Pyrimidine bases are synthesized from:', '["Formate and CO2","Serine and methionine","Glutamate and alanine","Glycine and glutamine","Aspartate and carbamoyl phosphate"]'::jsonb, 'Aspartate and carbamoyl phosphate', 'Pyrimidine ring is assembled from aspartate and carbamoyl phosphate. Orotate is a key intermediate attached to PRPP forming UMP.'),
('caf5edb4-db3b-5c26-a97a-8f4d7dc69fd1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. In B-DNA major and minor grooves are important because:', '["They stabilize the double helix","They provide binding sites for proteins","They contain water molecules","They are sites of base pairing","They are sites of phosphodiester bond formation"]'::jsonb, 'They provide binding sites for proteins', 'The grooves expose base pair edges allowing sequence-specific recognition by DNA-binding proteins such as transcription factors.'),
('55d64ea3-e755-5075-84ed-4873629d1eaf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The nucleosome core particle consists of:', '["RNA around non-histone proteins","RNA wrapped around histones","DNA wrapped around protamines","DNA wrapped around histones","DNA only"]'::jsonb, 'DNA wrapped around histones', 'The nucleosome core has ~146 bp DNA wrapped 1.65 turns around an octamer of histones (H2A H2B H3 H4 each x2).'),
('faf7e1c4-4a05-5016-8578-fc07c428f366'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is a glycosaminoglycan?', '["Chitin","Starch","Hyaluronic acid","Glycogen","Cellulose"]'::jsonb, 'Hyaluronic acid', 'Hyaluronic acid is a GAG of repeating D-glucuronic acid and N-acetylglucosamine. It is major in synovial fluid and ECM.'),
('fb01104f-5be4-5c47-8f38-accba35171c2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The alpha-helix has how many residues per turn?', '["4.0","3.6","3.0","5.0","2.5"]'::jsonb, '3.6', 'The alpha-helix has 3.6 residues per turn with 5.4 Å pitch (1.5 Å/residue). Side chains project outward forming i to i+4 hydrogen bonds.'),
('39a73c12-c89e-5093-8660-be194ae742c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Platelet-activating factor (PAF) has:', '["No fatty acids metabolic pathway process","Three fatty acids metabolic pathway process","Two ester-linked fatty acids metabolic pathway process","An ether-linked alkyl chain and acetyl group at sn-2","A sphingosine backbone metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'An ether-linked alkyl chain and acetyl group at sn-2', 'PAF has an ether-linked alkyl at sn-1 and acetyl at sn-2. It is a potent mediator of inflammation and platelet aggregation.'),
('94352b77-6999-598b-a1d4-bca750163245'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The functional unit of DNA encoding a specific protein is:', '["Codon","Chromosome","Operon","Nucleotide","Gene"]'::jsonb, 'Gene', 'A gene is a DNA segment containing information for synthesis of a functional polypeptide or RNA molecule.'),
('cf6a2f38-4555-5506-a8c4-25e8a92877ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The most abundant plasma protein is:', '["Albumin","Transferrin","Globulin","Hemoglobin","Fibrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Serum albumin (~3.5-5.0 g/dL) maintains oncotic pressure and transports fatty acids bilirubin and drugs.'),
('92e219d0-bc15-50a4-80d8-514d430e296d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Collagen''s helical structure is called:', '["Alpha-helix","Beta-sheet","Triple helix","Random coil","Beta-turn"]'::jsonb, 'Triple helix', 'Collagen has a triple helix of three left-handed polyproline II helices twisted in a right-handed superhelix requiring glycine every third residue.'),
('3cb29fb3-8693-555d-8069-b222bbc0f9b7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. An omega-6 fatty acid is:', '["EPA","DHA","Oleic acid","Alpha-linolenic acid","Linoleic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Linoleic acid', 'Linoleic acid (18:2 n-6) is an omega-6 essential fatty acid. Alpha-linolenic acid (18:3 n-3) is omega-3.'),
('27250db7-2812-5a2b-83d2-f30024530b47'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which base is found exclusively in DNA?', '["Cytosine","Uracil","Guanine","Thymine","Adenine"]'::jsonb, 'Thymine', 'Thymine (5-methyluracil) is found exclusively in DNA. Uracil is in RNA. Adenine guanine and cytosine are in both.'),
('1bb889d1-2d97-5e0b-a352-5bd7e6c7e2b7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. The process of making RNA from DNA is:', '["Transcription","Translation","Replication","Transduction","Transformation"]'::jsonb, 'Transcription', 'Transcription synthesizes RNA from a DNA template by RNA polymerase. Translation synthesizes protein from mRNA.'),
('4e565369-167b-5bd1-a2f7-83855114bd7d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In eukaryotes transcription occurs in the:', '["Cytoplasm","Nucleus","Ribosomes","Mitochondria","Golgi"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleus', 'Eukaryotic transcription occurs in the nucleus. RNA is processed and exported to cytoplasm for translation.'),
('218472bb-35c1-5f07-8d24-2ce77f533f8d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Histones are rich in:', '["Tyrosine and tryptophan","Lysine and arginine","Serine and threonine","Glutamic and aspartic acids","Glycine and alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Lysine and arginine', 'Histones are basic proteins rich in positively charged lysine and arginine binding tightly to negatively charged DNA.'),
('1242ebd7-f3f6-5295-add9-e23c518462b0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Palmitic acid has:', '["14 carbons saturated","16 carbons saturated","12 carbons saturated","18 carbons saturated","20 carbons saturated"]'::jsonb, '16 carbons saturated', 'Palmitic acid is 16:0. Stearic is 18:0 myristic is 14:0 lauric is 12:0 arachidic is 20:0.'),
('d932f380-595f-5315-903a-6407de7aef30'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is a ketone body?', '["Oxaloacetate","Citrate","Pyruvate","Acetoacetate","Lactate"]'::jsonb, 'Acetoacetate', 'Acetoacetate is a ketone body along with beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetone. Ketone bodies are produced during starvation or uncontrolled diabetes.'),
('223a10fc-d06e-58d8-947d-f801fac2072f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A ketose is distinguished from an aldose by:', '["Ketone group","Amino group","Aldehyde group","Hydroxyl group","Carboxyl group"]'::jsonb, 'Ketone group', 'Ketoses have a ketone group (C=O usually at C2). Aldoses have an aldehyde group (CHO at C1). Fructose is a ketose glucose is an aldose.'),
('ba0ad6f7-b1d6-5640-82e9-2914236b50e3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The major iron storage protein is:', '["Hemoglobin","Transferrin","Hemosiderin","Myoglobin","Ferritin"]'::jsonb, 'Ferritin', 'Ferritin stores iron in soluble non-toxic form within cells. Serum ferritin reflects total body iron stores. Transferrin transports iron.'),
('c0c81085-08ce-57de-8719-43ad9e8332c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Ceramide is the precursor for:', '["Glycerophospholipids","Triacylglycerols","Sphingolipids","Cholesterol","Eicosanoids"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingolipids', 'Ceramide (sphingosine + fatty acid) is the precursor for all complex sphingolipids including sphingomyelin cerebrosides and gangliosides.'),
('78b16a80-3590-5b86-8475-e55c3494e2f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Gangliosides are sialic acid-containing:', '["Glycerophospholipids","Sphingoglycolipids","Sterols","Neutral lipids","Lipoproteins"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingoglycolipids', 'Gangliosides are complex sphingolipids with sialic acid (N-acetylneuraminic acid) abundant in neural tissue for cell recognition and signaling.'),
('deb7f316-2ad9-5bea-980f-30672c379535'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Topoisomerases:', '["Synthesize DNA","Degrade DNA","Relieve supercoiling in DNA","Unwind DNA strands","Join DNA fragments"]'::jsonb, 'Relieve supercoiling in DNA', 'Topoisomerases manage DNA supercoiling by cutting and rejoining strands essential for replication and transcription.'),
('52bafc00-2d04-503a-bc98-20961efd6c63'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Reverse cholesterol transport is mediated by:', '["Chylomicrons","LDL","IDL","VLDL","HDL"]'::jsonb, 'HDL', 'HDL picks up cholesterol from peripheral tissues and delivers it to the liver for excretion or recycling protective against atherosclerosis.'),
('b7932cfb-8e70-5d28-a767-0a876e07dda9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is a mucopolysaccharide?', '["Starch","Cellulose","Chondroitin sulfate","Glycogen","Inulin"]'::jsonb, 'Chondroitin sulfate', 'Chondroitin sulfate is a glycosaminoglycan of N-acetylgalactosamine and glucuronic acid major in cartilage.'),
('b190a21b-c9c6-502a-8528-ce2dd88c6d8b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Lactase deficiency causes intolerance to:', '["Lactose","Glucose","Fructose","Sucrose","Maltose"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose', 'Lactase deficiency prevents lactose hydrolysis. Undigested lactose is fermented by bacteria causing bloating diarrhea and pain.'),
('cba4364c-2154-5872-a5ca-e1bfbaf762c7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Sucrose is composed of:', '["Glucose and fructose","Glucose and mannose","Glucose and galactose","Fructose and galactose","Two glucose molecules"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose and fructose', 'Sucrose (table sugar) is alpha-D-glucose + beta-D-fructose. It is hydrolyzed by sucrase in the small intestine.'),
('42d47bc3-fab0-556e-883a-e8ba46ab1dcd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Beta-sheets are stabilized by:', '["Hydrogen bonds between backbone NH and CO of adjacent strands","Covalent bonds between side chains","Disulfide bonds","Ionic bonds","Hydrophobic interactions"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrogen bonds between backbone NH and CO of adjacent strands', 'Beta-sheets have inter-strand hydrogen bonds between backbone NH and CO of adjacent polypeptide strands (parallel or antiparallel).'),
('a12fc252-1139-5069-beae-dca820db200e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In prokaryotic DNA adenine is methylated in:', '["DNA replication metabolic pathway process","Transcription metabolic pathway process","Translation metabolic pathway process","Restriction-modification systems","Repair metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Restriction-modification systems', 'Prokaryotes methylate adenine at N6 in specific sequences to protect host DNA from restriction endonuclease cleavage.'),
('255000af-c6a2-58c0-87fc-6385a4832316'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The most common DNA form in cells is:', '["C-DNA","D-DNA","A-DNA","B-DNA","Z-DNA"]'::jsonb, 'B-DNA', 'B-DNA is most common under physiological conditions being right-handed with 10 bp/turn. A-DNA occurs under dehydrating conditions.'),
('54fca239-3a0c-53ac-ad95-f1bd5dfd2376'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Lectin proteins bind specifically to:', '["Carbohydrates","Phosphate groups","Nucleic acids","Lipids","Metal ions"]'::jsonb, 'Carbohydrates', 'Lectins bind specifically to carbohydrate structures playing roles in cell adhesion pathogen recognition and glycoprotein trafficking.'),
('8b046fb5-f3f1-5df8-99d6-72c8e1f118dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The approximate MW of a 100 amino acid protein is:', '["100,000 Da","11,000 Da","5,500 Da","50,000 Da","22,000 Da"]'::jsonb, '11,000 Da', 'Average amino acid residue weight is ~110 Da so a 100-residue protein is ~11 kDa.'),
('1907ebcf-953d-5127-89ca-114a2cdefed1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Which lipid is amphipathic?', '["Triacylglycerol","Cholesteryl ester","Paraffin oil","Mineral oil","Phosphatidylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphatidylcholine', 'Phosphatidylcholine has a hydrophilic head and hydrophobic tails allowing micelle and bilayer formation in aqueous environments.'),
('588d92e4-3997-5140-8c91-6e0d709e3dd0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In Watson-Crick model distance between successive base pairs is:', '["10.0 Å","3.4 Å","5.4 Å","2.0 Å","20.0 Å"]'::jsonb, '3.4 Å', 'In B-DNA rise per base pair is 3.4 Å with helical pitch of 34 Å for 10 bp per turn. Helix diameter is ~20 Å.'),
('c7ec1319-7039-5dec-90ba-f8cd04ecacf9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Scurvy results from deficiency of vitamin:', '["A","B6","C","D","E"]'::jsonb, 'C', 'Vitamin C is cofactor for prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases in collagen synthesis. Deficiency causes unstable collagen leading to scurvy symptoms.'),
('7361db2f-18ed-5b8e-a740-0e2cbdccefd6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A residue with a free anomeric carbon is a:', '["Aldose","Non-reducing sugar","Ketose","Reducing sugar","Deoxy sugar"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing sugar', 'A reducing sugar has a free anomeric carbon that can reduce oxidizing agents such as Cu2+ in Fehling''s or Benedict''s reagent.'),
('a025488e-4f54-5b70-b8d7-fabb82f275b2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The storage form of glucose in plants is:', '["Starch","Sucrose","Cellulose","Glycogen","Chitin"]'::jsonb, 'Starch', 'Starch (amylose + amylopectin) is the major storage polysaccharide in plants stored in plastids.'),
('6bb206f6-877b-50ff-94a2-fa8571cc0ab7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. In saturated fatty acids all carbon bonds are:', '["Alternating single and double","Aromatic bonds","Double bonds","Triple bonds","Single bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Single bonds', 'Saturated fatty acids have no double bonds. All carbons are saturated with hydrogen giving them higher melting points.'),
('0070d85c-ec43-596f-815a-d238ae36c5e7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which RNA carries amino acids to the ribosome?', '["rRNA","siRNA","mRNA","tRNA","snRNA"]'::jsonb, 'tRNA', 'tRNA carries amino acids attached to its 3'' end. The anticodon base-pairs with mRNA codon during translation.'),
('27619139-c244-58f7-97fd-8e5ffc2d45ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The Central Dogma states information flows from:', '["Protein to RNA to DNA","RNA to DNA to Protein","DNA to Protein to RNA","RNA to Protein to DNA","DNA to RNA to Protein"]'::jsonb, 'DNA to RNA to Protein', 'The Central Dogma: DNA is transcribed to RNA which is translated to Protein. Some viruses perform reverse transcription.'),
('a7a6e10d-3c9a-5942-bba8-c5566a53b05d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Serine''s side chain contains:', '["An amino group","A carboxyl group","A methyl group","A sulfhydryl group","A hydroxyl group"]'::jsonb, 'A hydroxyl group', 'Serine has a hydroxyl group (-CH2OH) making it polar uncharged. It is a target for phosphorylation by kinases.'),
('32c86ddf-f916-51a1-9d20-22ad625303f6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Most abundant phosphoglyceride in membranes is:', '["Phosphatidylserine","Phosphatidylcholine","Phosphatidylinositol","Phosphatidylethanolamine","Cardiolipin"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphatidylcholine', 'Phosphatidylcholine (lecithin) comprises ~50% of eukaryotic membrane phospholipids.'),
('29570e3a-e291-5666-90a2-ac3d3922d375'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which polysaccharide is NOT digestible by humans?', '["Starch","Maltose","Sucrose","Cellulose","Glycogen"]'::jsonb, 'Cellulose', 'Cellulose has beta-1,4 linkages that human enzymes cannot cleave. It serves as dietary fiber.'),
('40c09d2c-843f-5924-8cbb-3def347245c7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Homologous proteins from different species show:', '["Identical sequences","Sequence similarity","No relationship","Variable length only","Similar lipid content"]'::jsonb, 'Sequence similarity', 'Homologous proteins share evolutionary origin with sequence similarity. Conserved residues are critical for structure and function.'),
('819f0d59-3202-5077-8553-62d7b73bef65'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Hemoglobin is a:', '["Chromoprotein","Lipoprotein","Nucleoprotein","Simple protein","Glycoprotein"]'::jsonb, 'Chromoprotein', 'Hemoglobin is a chromoprotein due to its heme prosthetic group that absorbs visible light giving blood its red color.'),
('df569511-ae01-53a9-bcbf-c0963fa8ae17'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Ribozymes are:', '["DNA replication enzymes","RNA molecules with catalytic activity","Protein enzymes degrading RNA","Enzymes that synthesize ribose","Ribosomal proteins"]'::jsonb, 'RNA molecules with catalytic activity', 'Ribozymes are catalytically active RNA. Examples include the ribosome''s peptidyl transferase center and RNase P.'),
('0f8418fb-d1f3-585b-93f1-eb0c1ce6e699'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Phospholipase A2 cleaves at:', '["sn-1 ester bond","sn-3 phosphodiester bond","Head group linkage","sn-2 ester bond","Glycerol backbone"]'::jsonb, 'sn-2 ester bond', 'PLA2 hydrolyzes fatty acid at sn-2 of phospholipids releasing arachidonic acid for eicosanoid synthesis.'),
('1122984b-38b2-500d-b207-64e8ddc54e55'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Apoenzyme refers to:', '["Protein part of enzyme without cofactor","Complete active enzyme","Cofactor of an enzyme","Inactive enzyme precursor","Denatured enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Protein part of enzyme without cofactor', 'Apoenzyme is the inactive protein portion requiring a cofactor for activity. The complete complex is holoenzyme.'),
('324c5d97-5334-5c8d-b9cc-3c4265146dda'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Statins inhibit:', '["HMG-CoA reductase","Lipoprotein lipase","ACAT","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","LCAT"]'::jsonb, 'HMG-CoA reductase', 'Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase the rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol biosynthesis lowering plasma LDL.'),
('ad55a128-4276-5308-8f57-ef223057d97a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which amino acid has a secondary amino group?', '["Proline","Glycine","Tryptophan","Histidine","Arginine"]'::jsonb, 'Proline', 'Proline has a secondary amine (imino group) with its side chain forming a ring with the alpha-amino group. It causes kinks in alpha-helices.'),
('b984698f-5ce9-5ee2-9c9d-1ea5c229e665'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Cell membranes are primarily:', '["Triacylglycerol bilayer","Carbohydrate bilayer","Cholesterol monolayer","Protein monolayer","Phospholipid bilayer"]'::jsonb, 'Phospholipid bilayer', 'Cell membranes have a phospholipid bilayer with hydrophilic heads facing water and hydrophobic tails forming the interior.'),
('6624e113-5e92-52fe-bde0-95b8195d22b8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is a simple protein?', '["Hemoglobin","Glycoprotein","Nucleoprotein","Albumin","Lipoprotein"]'::jsonb, 'Albumin', 'Simple proteins yield only amino acids upon hydrolysis. Albumin is a simple globular protein.'),
('7998a88c-7e2b-58db-9850-9eceb1f7d6d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'IP3 functions as a:', '["Second messenger","Energy source","Structural component","Transcription factor","Enzyme cofactor"]'::jsonb, 'Second messenger', 'IP3 is a second messenger that triggers calcium release from ER activating calcium-dependent processes.'),
('1b35a316-2fe3-5b2f-88fa-046ee7f68a01'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The major carbohydrate in human milk is:', '["Glucose","Lactose","Sucrose","Maltose","Fructose"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose', 'Lactose is the primary carbohydrate in human milk providing ~40% of its caloric content and promoting calcium absorption.'),
('a5e2a34d-e524-56e6-ac3e-a1362156b904'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Hydroxyproline is formed from:', '["Alanine","Glycine","Lysine","Proline","Valine"]'::jsonb, 'Proline', 'Hydroxyproline is formed by hydroxylation of proline after incorporation into polypeptide requiring vitamin C for collagen stability.'),
('85ee2d57-ad49-5280-b5e4-8ee90d170f68'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Watson-Crick base pairing involves:', '["A-T (1) and G-C","A-T (3) and G-C","A-C (2) and G-T","A-T (2 H-bonds) and G-C","A-G (2) and T-C"]'::jsonb, 'A-T (2 H-bonds) and G-C', 'Adenine pairs with thymine via 2 H-bonds; guanine with cytosine via 3 H-bonds. This specificity ensures accurate replication and transcription.'),
('e2a310dc-b23f-58df-bada-8ab063233bd1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which tissue is most dependent on glucose?', '["Adipose","Liver","Brain","Skeletal muscle","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Brain', 'The brain consumes ~120 g glucose daily and cannot use free fatty acids (can use ketone bodies during starvation).'),
('cd28a8c7-f53a-5be0-9ae1-5d705381ef7a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Proteoglycans have:', '["High carbohydrate content","Mostly lipid","Equal protein and carbohydrate","High protein content","Mostly nucleic acid"]'::jsonb, 'High carbohydrate content', 'Proteoglycans consist of core protein with GAG chains exceeding 95% of molecular weight giving high negative charge for hydration.'),
('9713840b-855f-569c-b39b-65208f686ca2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is NOT a purine base?', '["Adenine","Guanine","Xanthine","Thymine","Hypoxanthine"]'::jsonb, 'Thymine', 'Thymine is a pyrimidine (single ring). Adenine guanine xanthine and hypoxanthine are purines (double ring).'),
('ff88f011-eb08-5ebd-a8f3-39f208d9b3f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Amino acids join by:', '["Decarboxylation","Oxidation","Hydrolysis","Dehydration/condensation","Reduction"]'::jsonb, 'Dehydration/condensation', 'Peptide bonds form by condensation between alpha-carboxyl of one amino acid and alpha-amino of another eliminating water.'),
('b94b7f0d-dbd9-5ac4-ad3e-966a25cc2dac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The Sanger method determines:', '["Primary structure","Disulfide bond positions","Tertiary structure","Secondary structure","Quaternary structure"]'::jsonb, 'Primary structure', 'The Sanger method determines amino acid sequence (primary structure) using dinitrofluorobenzene to label the N-terminal amino acid.'),
('36b8cdd3-9d75-5555-b4d9-76fdae25af8a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Energy storage form of fatty acids in adipose is:', '["Phospholipids","Triacylglycerols","Free fatty acids","Eicosanoids","Cholesteryl esters"]'::jsonb, 'Triacylglycerols', 'Adipose stores fatty acids as triacylglycerols mobilized by hormone-sensitive lipase during fasting.'),
('ea01a650-09f4-56dd-8256-fe280f7dd6c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The repeating unit of glycogen:', '["Glucose-alpha-1,6-glucose","NAG-beta-1,4-NAG","Glucose-alpha-1,4-glucose","Glucose-alpha-1,2-fructose","Glucose-beta-1,4-glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose-alpha-1,4-glucose', 'Glycogen linear backbone has alpha-1,4 linkages. Branches occur via alpha-1,6 bonds every 8-12 residues.'),
('55c789e7-2661-57ab-862d-0d102ae2b833'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is NOT a function of carbohydrates?', '["Cell recognition","Metabolic intermediates","Genetic information storage","Energy storage","Structural component"]'::jsonb, 'Genetic information storage', 'Genetic information storage is the function of nucleic acids not carbohydrates.'),
('6ec49869-3b42-5b94-bcea-ae625ba20bb1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Elastin''s elasticity is due to:', '["High glycine and proline","Desmosine cross-links","Triple helix","Disulfide bonds","Hydroxylysine"]'::jsonb, 'Desmosine cross-links', 'Elastin contains desmosine cross-links from lysine residues allowing stretch and recoil like rubber.'),
('04e672d8-0c3f-54b1-be96-3de69e682037'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The main cholesterol carrier in blood is:', '["Chylomicrons","VLDL","LDL","HDL","IDL"]'::jsonb, 'LDL', 'LDL is the primary cholesterol carrier in blood. High LDL is associated with increased atherosclerosis risk (''bad cholesterol'').'),
('07b237d5-0485-50c2-b1ec-ec839d12a340'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'ATP is an example of a:', '["Purine base","Nucleotide","Nucleoside","Nucleic acid","Pyrimidine base"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleotide', 'ATP is a nucleotide (adenine + ribose + 3 phosphates). It is the primary energy currency of the cell.'),
('441eb01e-77c4-5968-9704-d4b3de569f1b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Beta-oxidation occurs in the:', '["Golgi","Mitochondrial matrix","Peroxisomes","Cytosol","ER"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial matrix', 'Beta-oxidation primarily occurs in mitochondrial matrix. Very long chain fatty acids start in peroxisomes.'),
('af50b128-d928-520c-b1cb-5ecc3893daa5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Tryptophan is the precursor for:', '["Serotonin","Dopamine","Histamine","GABA","Acetylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin', 'Tryptophan is hydroxylated to 5-HTP then decarboxylated to serotonin (5-HT). Serotonin regulates mood sleep and appetite.'),
('5f07e90a-79af-5f6a-acbf-a440ce447e42'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Proteoglycans vs glycoproteins carbohydrate content:', '["Lower in proteoglycans","Variable but equal","Proteoglycans have none","Equal","Higher in proteoglycans"]'::jsonb, 'Higher in proteoglycans', 'Proteoglycans have up to 95% carbohydrate vs glycoproteins with 1-60%. Proteoglycans have long GAG chains.'),
('48325eb2-e79e-5a54-ad3a-364fea6956f3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Secondary structure is stabilized by:', '["Disulfide bonds","Hydrogen bonds","Hydrophobic interactions","Ionic bonds","Van der Waals"]'::jsonb, 'Hydrogen bonds', 'Alpha-helices and beta-sheets are stabilized by hydrogen bonds between backbone carbonyl and amide groups.'),
('74518a68-18ec-5930-8ed7-5779b9abc081'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Amino acids with aromatic rings:', '["Phe Ser Thr","Phe Tyr Trp","Trp Pro His","Tyr Cys Met","Phe Val Leu"]'::jsonb, 'Phe Tyr Trp', 'Phenylalanine tyrosine and tryptophan are the three aromatic amino acids absorbing UV at 280 nm.'),
('f2762109-e5b4-5049-9287-828920f7b72e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The human genome has ~:', '["3 trillion bp","3 billion bp","3 million bp","30 billion bp","30 million bp"]'::jsonb, '3 billion bp', 'The human haploid genome contains ~3 × 10^9 base pairs encoding ~20,000-25,000 genes across 23 chromosomes.'),
('e02319bc-104a-531f-abb9-5accd2867b96'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Which molecule is a second messenger?', '["GTP","ATP","CoA","NADH","cAMP"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP', 'cAMP is synthesized from ATP by adenylyl cyclase and activates protein kinase A mediating effects of glucagon and epinephrine.'),
('2cec0e68-c142-5101-90bb-bf4f429bf34c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Maltose is:', '["Glucose + fructose","Glucose + galactose","Glucose + mannose","Two glucoses beta-1,4","Glucose + glucose alpha-1,4"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose + glucose alpha-1,4', 'Maltose is alpha-D-glucopyranosyl-(1,4)-D-glucopyranose produced by starch hydrolysis.'),
('7d85dd29-6421-5afb-bcad-6d6e7901d1f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The codon AUG codes for:', '["Valine","Tryptophan","Alanine","Methionine","Arginine"]'::jsonb, 'Methionine', 'AUG codes for methionine and serves as the start codon in eukaryotic translation.'),
('2958d1fe-2453-50e7-b29c-e1186defc556'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Anabolism refers to:', '["Breakdown for energy","Synthesis of complex molecules","ATP generation only","Waste production","Degradation"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesis of complex molecules', 'Anabolism builds complex molecules from simpler precursors requiring energy. Catabolism breaks down molecules releasing energy.'),
('170d8e0b-5ef5-5bdf-8f93-0d6b5c18ae45'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In the DNA double helix strands are:', '["Parallel and identical","Antiparallel and complementary","Perpendicular and identical","Parallel and complementary","Antiparallel and identical"]'::jsonb, 'Antiparallel and complementary', 'DNA strands are antiparallel (5''→3'' opposite directions) and complementary (A-T G-C).'),
('d911f38b-0f48-5919-be37-4c8ce3bdc409'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Glucose is a:', '["Aldopentose","D-aldohexose","Aldotetrose","Ketopentose","Ketohexose"]'::jsonb, 'D-aldohexose', 'Glucose is an aldohexose with aldehyde at C1 and six carbons. Fructose is a ketohexose.'),
('1eb88cb1-7bc5-5f7b-9740-58e04c4ac3b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'PFK-1 is the rate-limiting enzyme of:', '["Gluconeogenesis","Glycolysis","Glycogenesis","Glycogenolysis","Pentose phosphate pathway"]'::jsonb, 'Glycolysis', 'PFK-1 catalyzes the committed step of glycolysis converting F6P to F1,6BP. It is the primary regulatory point of glycolysis.'),
('e39838a6-cc5a-5dfa-9189-80bd81ec86a6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Arachidonic acid is:', '["18:2 omega-6","18:3 omega-3","20:4 omega-6","20:5 omega-3","22:6 omega-3"]'::jsonb, '20:4 omega-6', 'Arachidonic acid (20:4 omega-6) is the eicosanoid precursor. EPA is 20:5 omega-3. DHA is 22:6 omega-3.'),
('5cad590c-8714-52b6-ad7e-43caee37d9e6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Hypoxanthine as inosine is found in:', '["RNA only","tRNA anticodons","DNA only","Neither","Both DNA and RNA"]'::jsonb, 'tRNA anticodons', 'Inosine (from hypoxanthine) in tRNA anticodons can pair with A U or C during translation allowing wobble.'),
('78df4ae3-3479-557a-bbc7-227aa8b7499e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Inulin is a polymer of:', '["Glucose","Fructose","Galactose","Ribose","Mannose"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose', 'Inulin is a homopolysaccharide of beta-D-fructose linked beta-2,1 found in chicory used to measure GFR.'),
('5de60036-5630-5260-98a6-fd55d4636ebb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The anomeric carbon of D-glucose is:', '["C1","C2","C3","C5","C6"]'::jsonb, 'C1', 'C1 (the aldehyde carbon) becomes the anomeric carbon upon cyclization giving alpha and beta anomers.'),
('b2a087e6-049e-5c75-a194-017fbcdbdbf6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is NOT a glycosaminoglycan?', '["Hyaluronic acid","Chondroitin sulfate","Keratan sulfate","Heparan sulfate","Chitin"]'::jsonb, 'Chitin', 'Chitin is a homopolysaccharide of N-acetylglucosamine not a GAG. GAGs are heteropolysaccharides with repeating disaccharides.'),
('5ac27257-3728-549d-afcf-1a4061cc884a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Alpha-amylase cleaves:', '["Beta-1,4 bonds","Beta-1,6 bonds","Alpha-1,4 bonds","Both alpha-1,4 and 1,6","Alpha-1,6 bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1,4 bonds', 'Alpha-amylase is an endoglycosidase cleaving internal alpha-1,4 bonds in starch/glycogen. It cannot cleave alpha-1,6 branches.'),
('148d556d-793a-5898-9bb3-8d1b93724485'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Trehalose is found in:', '["Milk","Sugar cane","Insect hemolymph","Fruits","Grains"]'::jsonb, 'Insect hemolymph', 'Trehalose (alpha-1,1-linked glucose) is the main blood sugar in insects and a stress protectant in fungi and bacteria.'),
('df4dc7f8-b88b-52d6-b2ca-65ebeb9c8b2b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Sorbitol is produced by reduction of:', '["Fructose","Galactose","Glucose","Ribose","Mannose"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose', 'Sorbitol (glucitol) results from glucose reduction by aldose reductase using NADPH contributing to diabetic complications.'),
('810dd966-07fe-542e-beef-e5696be88981'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The amino acid sequence is determined by:', '["Gene base sequence","Golgi apparatus","Ribosomal proteins","tRNA structure","Peptidyl transferase"]'::jsonb, 'Gene base sequence', 'The primary structure is encoded by the nucleotide sequence of its gene read in triplets during translation.'),
('b5208d8d-b99b-5e28-80d3-1f016df444fc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Cysteine is involved in:', '["Disulfide bond formation","Hydrogen bonding","Ionic bonding","Hydrophobic interactions","Peptide bonding"]'::jsonb, 'Disulfide bond formation', 'Cysteine thiol groups form covalent disulfide bonds (-S-S-) stabilizing tertiary and quaternary protein structure.'),
('dc2c2079-7177-50e0-b309-7b457fd46351'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Conjugated proteins with carbohydrate are:', '["Glycoproteins","Metalloproteins","Chromoproteins","Lipoproteins","Nucleoproteins"]'::jsonb, 'Glycoproteins', 'Glycoproteins have covalently attached carbohydrate chains functioning in cell recognition signaling and immune responses.'),
('4020df97-ac0d-52e3-a740-e95e6b250b1e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Myoglobin:', '["Transports oxygen in blood","Has quaternary structure","Stores oxygen in muscle","Has no heme","Is made in liver"]'::jsonb, 'Stores oxygen in muscle', 'Myoglobin is a monomeric heme protein storing oxygen in muscle with higher O2 affinity than hemoglobin.'),
('22691a6e-c3a3-50b1-8276-741646093f9d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Essential amino acids must be:', '["Synthesized in body","Obtained from diet","Stored in liver","Excreted in urine","Converted to fat"]'::jsonb, 'Obtained from diet', 'Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by humans and must be obtained from dietary sources.'),
('6a1e081f-814f-588d-a9e9-19fe9ef71d03'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Histidine''s buffering capacity is due to its:', '["Amino group","Hydroxyl group","Carboxyl group","Imidazole side chain","Sulfhydryl group"]'::jsonb, 'Imidazole side chain', 'Histidine''s imidazole side chain has pKa ~6.0 close to physiological pH making it an excellent buffer important in hemoglobin.'),
('89f321fb-ed33-5038-b57e-d07f68343a57'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Edman degradation determines:', '["Total protein quantity","C-terminal amino acid","Protein charge","N-terminal amino acid sequence","3D structure"]'::jsonb, 'N-terminal amino acid sequence', 'Edman degradation sequentially removes N-terminal amino acids using PITG. It can sequence peptides up to ~50 residues.'),
('5b661299-2b98-5518-b1f6-a523140c9dbf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Phosphodiester bonds are found in:', '["Lipids","Nucleic acids","Glycoproteins","Carbohydrates","Proteins"]'::jsonb, 'Nucleic acids', 'Phosphodiester bonds connect nucleotides in DNA and RNA. Proteins have peptide bonds.'),
('bcf2ea38-214b-525e-a4ec-03235118d0ca'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Hemoglobin has quaternary structure of:', '["Two alpha and two beta subunits","Four alpha subunits","Single polypeptide with heme","Three gamma subunits","Alpha-helical coiled coil"]'::jsonb, 'Two alpha and two beta subunits', 'Hemoglobin A is alpha2beta2 tetramer with each subunit having a heme group allowing cooperative oxygen binding.'),
('4c249da0-7b99-5e89-95a2-45abd32ef2f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Lipoproteins are classified by:', '["Carbohydrate content","Electrophoretic mobility and density","Fatty acid composition","Amino acid content","Size only"]'::jsonb, 'Electrophoretic mobility and density', 'Lipoproteins are classified by density: chylomicrons VLDL LDL and HDL reflecting their lipid-to-protein ratio.'),
('f60fe208-9620-5e02-8e4a-93f9a0a915c3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Cholesterol in membranes:', '["Stores genetic info","Provides energy","Transports oxygen","Increases fluidity and stability","Catalyzes reactions"]'::jsonb, 'Increases fluidity and stability', 'Cholesterol modulates membrane fluidity reducing permeability and preventing tight packing at low temperatures.'),
('2744b76e-419f-57bf-9ca9-b7f4fb6e89db'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Melting point of fatty acids increases with:', '["Increasing double bonds","Chain length and saturation","Decreasing chain length","Branching","Chain length and unsaturation"]'::jsonb, 'Chain length and saturation', 'Longer chain length and saturation increase melting points by allowing tighter packing through van der Waals forces.'),
('f7a68cee-9ece-5f70-a09a-da1f1e8c2cb3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Trans fatty acids form during:', '["Partial hydrogenation of oils","Lipolysis","Beta-oxidation","Complete oxidation","De novo synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Partial hydrogenation of oils', 'Trans fats are produced during industrial partial hydrogenation converting cis to trans configuration increasing CVD risk.'),
('d8454943-4252-5068-b80d-6660f3bb7c04'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Lipid bilayers are impermeable to:', '["Small nonpolar molecules","Steroid hormones","CO2","Large polar molecules and ions","O2 gas"]'::jsonb, 'Large polar molecules and ions', 'The bilayer is permeable to small nonpolar molecules but largely impermeable to large polar molecules and ions without transporters.'),
('d63417f8-c352-56ed-a758-4dee180e4a08'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Fasting blood investigations show abnormal renal function. PAF is:', '["Cardiolipin","Phosphatidylserine","Phosphatidylethanolamine","Platelet-activating factor","Phosphatidylcholine"]'::jsonb, 'Platelet-activating factor', 'PAF (1-O-alkyl-2-acetyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphocholine) is a potent phospholipid mediator of platelet aggregation and inflammation.'),
('22f251ea-a3be-5d8e-9b66-21a278818a28'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Cortisol is derived from:', '["Sphingolipids","Cholesterol","Arachidonic acid","Phospholipids","Triacylglycerol"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol', 'Cortisol is a glucocorticoid synthesized from cholesterol in adrenal cortex by hydroxylation reactions.'),
('f12247e4-fd19-5764-bef9-ec5256e0e4ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Purine nucleotide degradation ends with:', '["Ammonia","Creatinine","Uric acid","Urea","Allantoin"]'::jsonb, 'Uric acid', 'In humans purine degradation ends with uric acid. Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine to xanthine to uric acid.'),
('7102bf5e-83e3-537e-9744-459fce67fa8e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Pyrimidine degradation yields:', '["Xanthine metabolic pathway process","Ammonia only metabolic pathway process","Uric acid metabolic pathway process","Creatine metabolic pathway process","Beta-aminoisobutyric acid and beta-alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-aminoisobutyric acid and beta-alanine', 'Thymine yields beta-aminoisobutyric acid. Cytosine and uracil yield beta-alanine. These are further metabolized.'),
('10b5cac0-e122-5516-a0ff-ece71ffd47f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In dsDNA (A+G)/(T+C) ratio is:', '["1","0.5","Variable","Not applicable","2"]'::jsonb, '1', 'By Charagaff''s rules A=T and G=C so A+G = T+C giving ratio of 1 for all dsDNA.'),
('25538076-fc39-5c3f-a7ae-f7beec36925b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Telomeres are:', '["Repeated sequences at chromosome ends","Origins of replication","Centromere sequences","rRNA genes","Promoter regions"]'::jsonb, 'Repeated sequences at chromosome ends', 'Telomeres (TTAGGG repeats in humans) protect chromosome ends maintained by telomerase reverse transcriptase.'),
('a58584c1-a66e-5355-8158-c114042e1bef'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. DNA primase synthesizes:', '["RNA primers for DNA replication","DNA primers","Okazaki fragments","The leading strand","The whole genome"]'::jsonb, 'RNA primers for DNA replication', 'DNA primase is an RNA polymerase that makes short RNA primers for DNA polymerase to extend on the lagging strand.'),
('0ee685fd-811c-5efc-9e54-5ecbcc394ce6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The 5'' cap on eukaryotic mRNA:', '["Protects from degradation and aids translation","Initiates translation metabolic pathway process","Polyadenylates metabolic pathway process","Terminates transcription metabolic pathway process","Splices introns metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Protects from degradation and aids translation', 'The 5'' methylguanosine cap protects mRNA from 5''→3'' exonucleases and facilitates ribosome binding.'),
('cec22afc-15a5-5635-a62b-c9ad093f31d8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Polyadenylation adds:', '["Exons","Introns","Methyl groups","Poly-A tail at 3'' end","5'' cap"]'::jsonb, 'Poly-A tail at 3'' end', 'The poly-A tail (~200 adenines) enhances mRNA stability export and translation efficiency.'),
('8a1d3286-c565-5a54-8878-08ddf98e7ae6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. Ribosomes are composed of:', '["Lipids and proteins","mRNA and proteins","DNA and proteins","rRNA and proteins","tRNA only"]'::jsonb, 'rRNA and proteins', 'Ribosomes are ~60% rRNA and 40% ribosomal proteins. The large subunit catalyzes peptide bond formation (rRNA is the ribozyme).'),
('782fa3b6-62b8-58ae-bfa5-1bd1ca1dae9b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The wobble hypothesis explains:', '["Flexible third codon base pairing","Exact first codon base pairing","Intron splicing","Mutations in DNA","Degeneracy of the genetic code"]'::jsonb, 'Flexible third codon base pairing', 'Wobble allows flexible pairing between tRNA''s 5'' anticodon base and mRNA''s 3'' codon base letting one tRNA recognize multiple codons.'),
('ae47e1dc-adab-561a-a00b-1ea2484f9de6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Restriction endonucleases:', '["Transcribe DNA","Cut DNA at specific sequences","Amplify DNA","Translate RNA","Join DNA fragments"]'::jsonb, 'Cut DNA at specific sequences', 'Restriction enzymes recognize and cleave specific palindromic DNA sequences essential for DNA manipulation and cloning.'),
('a7d8e9bf-eb63-53a2-8ed1-663a103dc3c2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is NOT an energy currency?', '["ATP","GTP","Creatine phosphate","NADH","cAMP"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP', 'cAMP is a second messenger for signal transduction not an energy currency.'),
('7f429beb-762b-55bb-b011-ab2d986e7557'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'NADH yields ~:', '["5 ATP","4 ATP","1.5 ATP","2.5 ATP","3.5 ATP"]'::jsonb, '2.5 ATP', 'Each NADH yields ~2.5 ATP via the electron transport chain. FADH2 yields ~1.5 ATP.'),
('9f46c026-7ec0-5a55-b24a-4f4c6eddaace'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The genetic code is degenerate because:', '["Multiple codons code for same amino acid","Read in different frames","One codon codes for multiple amino acids","64 codons for 64 amino acids","It varies between species"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple codons code for same amino acid', 'Most amino acids have multiple codons (e.g. leucine has 6). Only Met and Trp have single codons.'),
('e9a2305e-61be-5b6a-95c5-fb7a02cf7213'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Transferases catalyze:', '["Hydrolysis","Transfer of functional groups","Oxidation-reduction","Ligation","Isomerization"]'::jsonb, 'Transfer of functional groups', 'Transferases transfer methyl amino phosphate or glycosyl groups from donor to acceptor (e.g. kinases aminotransferases).'),
('906ec870-2510-536d-9bc5-520e3a890250'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Km represents:', '["Substrate concentration at half Vmax","Maximum velocity","Turnover number","Enzyme concentration","Inhibitor concentration"]'::jsonb, 'Substrate concentration at half Vmax', 'Km is the substrate concentration at half Vmax reflecting enzyme-substrate affinity. Low Km = high affinity.'),
('79cd1e5f-1f3f-5cd4-98ca-1e3ff6834065'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Competitive inhibition:', '["Decreases both","Increases both","Decreases Km Vmax unchanged","Decreases Vmax Km unchanged","Increases Km Vmax unchanged"]'::jsonb, 'Increases Km Vmax unchanged', 'Competitive inhibitor binds active site competing with substrate. Vmax unchanged (overcome by high substrate) but Km increases.'),
('94fae315-3b9c-5bac-b6db-a1a85598bbaf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Non-competitive inhibition:', '["Decreases Vmax Km unchanged","Decreases both","No effect","Increases Km Vmax unchanged","Increases both"]'::jsonb, 'Decreases Vmax Km unchanged', 'Non-competitive inhibitor binds away from active site reducing Vmax (fewer functional enzymes) but Km unchanged.'),
('32f4814c-6369-513c-a8b2-bd93cdb4399a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'ATCase is a classic:', '["Zymogen","Competitive inhibitor","Coenzyme","Allosteric enzyme","Non-competitive inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'Allosteric enzyme', 'ATCase is allosterically regulated: inhibited by CTP (feedback) and activated by ATP (heterotropic allostery).'),
('f7ec1678-c358-5df4-822d-da9af0656461'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'CoA is derived from:', '["Pantothenic acid","Niacin","Riboflavin","Thiamine","Pyridoxine"]'::jsonb, 'Pantothenic acid', 'Coenzyme A from pantothenic acid carries acyl groups as thioesters central to fatty acid oxidation and TCA cycle.'),
('2c854512-da0f-5f3f-bf94-07ae7cba17b7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'NAD+ is derived from:', '["Niacin","Pyridoxine","Thiamine","Cobalamin","Riboflavin"]'::jsonb, 'Niacin', 'NAD+ (and NADP+) are derived from niacin (B3). They function as electron carriers in redox reactions.'),
('7efaf3cc-c78d-552b-b57a-6dd21d768bcc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Serum enzymes in MI diagnosis:', '["CK-MB and troponin","ALP and GGT","Amylase and lipase","ALT and AST","LDH only"]'::jsonb, 'CK-MB and troponin', 'CK-MB and troponin I are cardiac markers elevated in acute myocardial infarction indicating myocardial damage.'),
('06d10e8e-76e0-5284-a668-9169c461ba50'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'With respect to management, Alkaline phosphatase is a marker for:', '["Pancreatitis","Myocardial infarction","Liver and bone disease","Anemia","Prostate cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and bone disease', 'ALP is elevated in cholestatic liver disease and bone disorders (Paget''s osteomalacia) helping differentiate jaundice types.'),
('62854d81-be51-5a0a-abbe-02c90556bc15'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Following angiography, a patient develops lymphadenopathy. Biochemical analysis shows abnormal electrolyte levels. Insulin:', '["Lowers blood glucose","Increases glucagon","Inhibits glycolysis","Raises blood glucose","Promotes ketogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Lowers blood glucose', 'Insulin lowers blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake glycolysis and glycogenesis while inhibiting gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis.'),
('440c0fee-b06f-542b-9797-06fcffff7cc9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. Which monosaccharide is a component of nucleic acids?', '["Glucose","Fructose","Mannose","Ribose","Galactose"]'::jsonb, 'Ribose', 'Ribose is the pentose sugar in RNA. Deoxyribose is in DNA. Both are aldopentoses.'),
('ee5e97b9-aa42-56c8-bc1d-525079ef1bfb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Amino acids with positively charged side chains include:', '["Phenylalanine tyrosine","Serine threonine","Glutamate aspartate","Valine leucine isoleucine","Lysine arginine histidine"]'::jsonb, 'Lysine arginine histidine', 'Lysine arginine and histidine have basic side chains with net positive charge at physiological pH. They interact with negatively charged molecules.'),
('93a1c990-d1c7-53f5-92e3-a114683f04fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The peptide bond is:', '["Freely rotatable metabolic pathway process","Ionic metabolic pathway process","Non-covalent metabolic pathway process","Saturated single bond metabolic pathway process","Planar and partially double bond in character"]'::jsonb, 'Planar and partially double bond in character', 'The peptide C-N bond has partial double bond character due to resonance making it planar and restricting rotation about the C-N bond.'),
('541cb5be-0d2d-5223-8147-111896dc1736'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following is a phospholipid found primarily in the inner mitochondrial membrane?', '["Cardiolipin","Phosphatidylcholine","Phosphatidylethanolamine","Sphingomyelin","Phosphatidylinositol"]'::jsonb, 'Cardiolipin', 'Cardiolipin (diphosphatidylglycerol) is a unique phospholipid in the inner mitochondrial membrane essential for maintaining cristae structure and electron transport chain function.');
insert into fcps_mcq_stage (id, subject_name, chapter_name, question, options, correct_answer, explanation) values
('3106d878-df78-553c-b982-60af113b71d9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The nitrogenous base that pairs with adenine in RNA is:', '["Xanthine","Uracil","Guanine","Thymine","Cytosine"]'::jsonb, 'Uracil', 'In RNA uracil pairs with adenine via two hydrogen bonds. Thymine pairs with adenine in DNA.'),
('56ee3b5d-bd4c-5523-9f68-7ca0076424b6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The conversion of glucose to lactate produces:', '["4 ATP","6 ATP","38 ATP","2 ATP","8 ATP"]'::jsonb, '2 ATP', 'Anaerobic glycolysis of one glucose to two lactate yields a net of 2 ATP (2 used 4 produced). Aerobic metabolism yields ~30-32 ATP per glucose.'),
('8639e6e4-3515-53dd-847f-11be6b9c43f2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Keratin is rich in which amino acid?', '["Cysteine","Methionine","Lysine","Glutamine","Asparagine"]'::jsonb, 'Cysteine', 'Keratin (especially hard keratin in hair and nails) is rich in cysteine forming many disulfide cross-links that provide strength and rigidity.'),
('2949cbb5-f9ed-50c8-975d-c037390e7b58'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which vitamin is essential for the formation of rhodopsin?', '["Vitamin A","Vitamin K","Vitamin D","Vitamin C","Vitamin B1"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin A', 'Vitamin A (retinal) forms the chromophore of rhodopsin in rod cells essential for vision in dim light. Deficiency causes night blindness.'),
('092ca776-807c-57b5-99bd-f5d3f8751fd3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Adenine and guanine are:', '["Nucleotides","Pyrimidines","Nucleosides","Purines","Nucleic acids"]'::jsonb, 'Purines', 'Adenine and guanine are purines (double-ring structures). Cytosine thymine and uracil are pyrimidines (single-ring).'),
('da64ab0d-a85f-5f33-945b-d00432ea78be'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The prosthetic group of hemoglobin is:', '["Iron","Heme","Biliverdin","Bilirubin","Globin"]'::jsonb, 'Heme', 'Heme is the iron-containing porphyrin prosthetic group of hemoglobin and myoglobin. It reversibly binds oxygen via Fe2+.'),
('26d2cd81-9842-589b-86d9-9c19102bfb62'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Cooperative binding of oxygen to hemoglobin is facilitated by:', '["Disulfide bond formation metabolic pathway process","Denaturation metabolic pathway process","Proteolytic cleavage metabolic pathway process","Phosphorylation metabolic pathway process","Conformational changes between T and R states"]'::jsonb, 'Conformational changes between T and R states', 'Hemoglobin transitions between T (tense low affinity) and R (relaxed high affinity) states. Oxygen binding to one heme stabilizes the R state increasing affinity for subsequent O2 molecules.'),
('9cd23719-7dc9-5833-afef-8824ed236302'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The TCA cycle intermediate that condenses with oxaloacetate to form citrate is:', '["Acetyl-CoA","Malate","Alpha-ketoglutarate","Succinyl-CoA","Fumarate"]'::jsonb, 'Acetyl-CoA', 'Citrate synthase catalyzes the condensation of acetyl-CoA (2 carbons) with oxaloacetate (4 carbons) to form citrate (6 carbons) in the first step of the TCA cycle.'),
('b5582e7e-d055-5d2e-bd26-3d367131d6bd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The bond between the sugar and base in a nucleotide is:', '["N-glycosidic bond","Ionic bond","Phosphodiester bond","Hydrogen bond","Peptide bond"]'::jsonb, 'N-glycosidic bond', 'The N-glycosidic bond connects the anomeric carbon of the pentose sugar to N1 of pyrimidines or N9 of purines via a beta linkage.'),
('ed993330-8d5c-5fad-8e1f-e4d7e16e0152'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Fructose is a:', '["Ketohexose","Ketoheptose","Aldohexose","Ketopentose","Aldopentose"]'::jsonb, 'Ketohexose', 'Fructose is a ketohexose with a ketone group at C2. Glucose and galactose are aldohexoses with aldehyde groups at C1.'),
('870de2e4-a8cb-5b52-a5c5-de4bad870015'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Bilirubin is derived from the catabolism of:', '["Heme","Cholesterol","Glucose","Amino acids","Fatty acids"]'::jsonb, 'Heme', 'Bilirubin is produced from heme degradation. Heme oxygenase cleaves heme to biliverdin which is reduced to bilirubin. Bilirubin is conjugated in the liver and excreted in bile.'),
('c9fdd081-fe32-5535-908e-2fe54d3d899c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The glucose transporter in the brain is:', '["GLUT1","GLUT2","GLUT3","GLUT4","GLUT5"]'::jsonb, 'GLUT1', 'GLUT1 transports glucose across the blood-brain barrier. GLUT3 is the main neuronal glucose transporter. Both are insulin-independent.'),
('3343db71-7652-5f67-9571-d9d8d240c6c5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Glycerol is a precursor for:', '["Gluconeogenesis","Pentose phosphate pathway","Glycogenesis","Glycogenolysis","Glycolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Gluconeogenesis', 'Glycerol from lipolysis is phosphorylated to glycerol-3-phosphate then converted to dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP) entering gluconeogenesis in the liver.'),
('2bc52b98-1620-55c7-816f-422c60c12f79'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The most common form of a secondary structure in globular proteins is:', '["Alpha-helix","Triple helix","Random coil","Beta-sheet","Beta-turn"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-helix', 'Alpha-helices are the most common secondary structure in globular proteins comprising ~30-40% of residues. Beta-sheets account for ~20-30%.'),
('752032af-44cb-58cf-8feb-a5df9a726550'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The sugar found in milk is:', '["Trehalose","Maltose","Cellobiose","Lactose","Sucrose"]'::jsonb, 'Lactose', 'Lactose (galactose-beta-1,4-glucose) is the primary sugar in milk. It is less sweet than sucrose and promotes calcium absorption.'),
('69dd9ca9-e562-5c4e-bd62-7a62ea025e21'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The energy charge of a cell is calculated as:', '["ATP/","ATP/AMP","(ATP+AMP)/ADP","(ATP+ADP)/AMP","(ATP + 0.5ADP)/"]'::jsonb, '(ATP + 0.5ADP)/', 'Energy charge ranges from 0 (all AMP) to 1 (all ATP). Most cells maintain ~0.85-0.95. High energy charge inhibits ATP-generating pathways.'),
('e1d7784f-655b-5c6d-b030-2ada24c08b6b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The enzyme enolase catalyzes:', '["Conversion of 2-PG to PEP","Conversion of PEP to pyruvate","Conversion of 1,3-BPG to 3-PG","Conversion of 3-PG to 2-PG","Conversion of glucose to G6P"]'::jsonb, 'Conversion of 2-PG to PEP', 'Enolase catalyzes the dehydration of 2-phosphoglycerate to phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) in glycolysis. It requires Mg2+ and is inhibited by fluoride.'),
('de824792-65a3-5c3a-abb1-a414a4ab6841'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In the electron transport chain cytochrome c is:', '["The terminal oxidase metabolic pathway process","Part of Complex I metabolic pathway process","Fixed in Complex III metabolic pathway process","A mobile electron carrier between Complex III and IV","An ATP synthase subunit metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'A mobile electron carrier between Complex III and IV', 'Cytochrome c is a small soluble heme protein shuttling electrons from Complex III to Complex IV in the intermembrane space.'),
('8d34b313-3b31-50d6-b30d-baa1bec5f143'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Triacylglycerols are composed of:', '["Cholesterol + fatty acid","Glycerol + 3 fatty acids","Glycerol + 2 fatty acids + phosphate","Glycerol + 1 fatty acid","Sphingosine + fatty acid"]'::jsonb, 'Glycerol + 3 fatty acids', 'Triacylglycerols (triglycerides) have three fatty acids esterified to glycerol. They function as energy stores and insulators.'),
('8784ea24-5b84-5b03-b65c-7118a5744dae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The primary function of LDL is to:', '["Activate lipoprotein lipase","Deliver cholesterol to peripheral tissues","Return cholesterol to the liver","Transport dietary fat","Transport triglycerides"]'::jsonb, 'Deliver cholesterol to peripheral tissues', 'LDL delivers cholesterol to peripheral tissues via LDL receptor-mediated endocytosis. High LDL promotes atherosclerosis.'),
('92dc7185-25a2-516c-a4c7-d1ff51d0ea39'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A ribozyme is:', '["An RNA molecule with catalytic activity","A protein enzyme that cleaves RNA","A ribosome subunit","A DNA enzyme","A lipid catalyst"]'::jsonb, 'An RNA molecule with catalytic activity', 'Ribozymes are catalytically active RNA molecules supporting the RNA world hypothesis. Examples include RNase P and the ribosome''s peptidyl transferase.'),
('150aa1f6-6ea9-5578-a814-fec042f8ec13'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The major anionic glycosaminoglycan in cartilage is:', '["Heparin","Chondroitin sulfate","Dermatan sulfate","Keratan sulfate","Hyaluronic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Chondroitin sulfate', 'Chondroitin sulfate provides compressive resistance in cartilage. It is sulfated at various positions and binds to proteoglycan aggregates with hyaluronic acid.'),
('c18cad40-385e-5e0e-83e5-bec99f5634c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Ribulose-5-phosphate is an intermediate of:', '["Pentose phosphate pathway","Glycolysis","TCA cycle","Gluconeogenesis","Glycogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Pentose phosphate pathway', 'Ribulose-5-phosphate is produced by 6-phosphogluconate dehydrogenase in the oxidative phase of the pentose phosphate pathway and used for nucleotide synthesis.'),
('169e712a-1d93-5c89-aa29-1cf5f49ba006'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Angiotensnogen is converted to angiotensin I by:', '["Renin","ACE","Chymase","Cathepsin","Trypsin"]'::jsonb, 'Renin', 'Renin (from juxtaglomerular cells) cleaves angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. ACE then converts angiotensin I to active angiotensin II.'),
('0dbfbf02-69b0-57b1-b186-a58977077ddc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The major component of ribosomes in terms of mass is:', '["mRNA","DNA","Lipids","Proteins","rRNA"]'::jsonb, 'rRNA', 'rRNA comprises ~60% of ribosome mass. The 70S bacterial ribosome has 23S 16S and 5S rRNA. Eukaryotic 80S ribosomes have 28S 18S 5.8S and 5S rRNA.'),
('45ccf7f4-82c4-51f2-aaaf-6d573dfe365e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Hydrophobic interactions in protein folding are driven by:', '["Formation of hydrogen bonds","Van der Waals forces","Ionic attractions","Covalent bonds","Increased entropy of water molecules"]'::jsonb, 'Increased entropy of water molecules', 'Hydrophobic collapse is entropically driven: nonpolar side chains cluster to minimize contact with water releasing ordered water molecules and increasing entropy.'),
('75d1ba9b-09f9-525a-bab4-398ae291bd6d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following is an oestrogen?', '["Estradiol","Cortisol","Aldosterone","Testosterone","Progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Estradiol', 'Estradiol is the primary estrogen synthesized from androstenedione/testosterone by aromatase. It is essential for female reproductive function.'),
('864d8459-49a5-5831-82fa-830516541356'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The primary bile acids synthesized in the liver are:', '["Cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid","Deoxycholic and lithocholic acid","Taurocholic acid only","Glycocholic acid only","Ursodeoxycholic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid', 'Primary bile acids (cholic and chenodeoxycholic) are synthesized from cholesterol and conjugated with taurine/glycine. Secondary bile acids form in the gut.'),
('f94fb5c7-6f9d-542f-8f19-5e44fba82bca'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The organ primarily responsible for gluconeogenesis is:', '["Liver","Muscle","Brain","Adipose","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'The liver is the main site of gluconeogenesis (~90%) providing glucose for the brain and RBCs during fasting. The kidney contributes ~10% during prolonged fasting.'),
('96e0a126-8518-5c67-91e5-d8620a488711'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The structure of tRNA is described as:', '["Globular","Cloverleaf","Linear","Triple helix","Double helix"]'::jsonb, 'Cloverleaf', 'tRNA has a cloverleaf secondary structure with four arms. The tertiary structure is L-shaped bringing the amino acid acceptor stem and anticodon to opposite ends.'),
('8bba9e9e-85bf-51c7-a060-2c1e56db81de'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The most prevalent serum immunoglobulin is:', '["IgE","IgM","IgG","IgD","IgA"]'::jsonb, 'IgG', 'IgG (~75% of serum immunoglobulins) is the major antibody of secondary immune response. It crosses the placenta providing passive immunity to the fetus.'),
('bc92eea6-caba-5256-8ee0-fecd873ef1e5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Membrane lipids that contain sialic acid include:', '["Cerebrosides","Cephalin","Gangliosides","Sphingomyelin","Lecithin"]'::jsonb, 'Gangliosides', 'Gangliosides are sialic acid-containing glycosphingolipids enriched in neural tissue functioning in cell recognition and signaling.'),
('51cf47dc-cb9d-5c20-9c5d-df9097fe8541'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The Michaelis-Menten equation is:', '["v = Vmax[S]/","v = Vmax/","v = Vmax + Km/[S]","v = Vmax[S]/","v = Km[S]/"]'::jsonb, 'v = Vmax[S]/', 'The Michaelis-Menten equation describes hyperbolic substrate saturation kinetics. Km is the substrate concentration at half Vmax.'),
('eb506840-cf28-5e29-ba6c-58229003ed08'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which is a branched-chain amino acid?', '["Leucine","Lysine","Methionine","Threonine","Tryptophan"]'::jsonb, 'Leucine', 'Leucine isoleucine and valine are branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs). They are degraded in muscle and their keto acids accumulate in maple syrup urine disease.'),
('b3bef4b5-7db3-5a30-8de8-278960a3dbda'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. The molecule that carries the genetic code from DNA to the ribosome is:', '["snRNA","mRNA","hnRNA","rRNA","tRNA"]'::jsonb, 'mRNA', 'Messenger RNA carries the transcribed genetic information as codons to ribosomes where it serves as the template for protein synthesis during translation.'),
('4424f417-c467-5e58-83f2-6808857c8c32'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Vitamin D is classified as:', '["Essential fatty acid","B-complex vitamin","Fat-soluble vitamin","Amino acid","Water-soluble vitamin"]'::jsonb, 'Fat-soluble vitamin', 'Vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin (along with A E and K). It is synthesized in skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol by UV light and functions as a hormone.'),
('4dfd9d68-6bb4-5b7d-afdb-30c7e995840a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The molecule that directly provides energy for most cellular processes is:', '["Creatine phosphate","ATP","NADH","GTP","FADH2"]'::jsonb, 'ATP', 'ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the universal energy currency. Hydrolysis of its terminal phosphoanhydride bond releases energy for coupled reactions.'),
('cd5884ce-d85c-5cda-879d-b38f4131774c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following is a component of the electron transport chain?', '["Cytochrome c","Hexokinase","Lactate dehydrogenase","Aldolase","Pyruvate kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Cytochrome c', 'Cytochrome c is a mobile electron carrier in the electron transport chain. The other enzymes are involved in glycolysis.'),
('c17873b2-b51b-5517-82ad-c27f165c2ad0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The primary function of HDL is:', '["Cholesterol synthesis","Triglyceride synthesis","LDL receptor recycling","Dietary fat transport","Reverse cholesterol transport"]'::jsonb, 'Reverse cholesterol transport', 'HDL (high-density lipoprotein) transports cholesterol from peripheral tissues back to the liver for excretion. It is considered ''good cholesterol''.'),
('f359bda2-e386-581a-872c-e56ddd6c6cda'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Lactate produced in muscle during exercise is converted back to glucose in the liver via:', '["Cori cycle","Urea cycle","Cahill cycle","Calvin cycle","TCA cycle"]'::jsonb, 'Cori cycle', 'The Cori cycle is the metabolic pathway where lactate is shuttled from muscle to liver converted to glucose via gluconeogenesis and returned to muscle.'),
('e2c301d3-0b25-5ed1-b5f7-a52fc0daea27'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The amino acid that does not have a primary amino group is:', '["Proline","Glycine","Alanine","Valine","Leucine"]'::jsonb, 'Proline', 'Proline has a secondary amino (imino) group because its side chain cyclizes back to the alpha-amino group forming a pyrrolidine ring.'),
('841bf837-f0b9-5155-9f46-d01ae60dc88c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Enzymes that use molecular oxygen to hydroxylate substrates are called:', '["Hydrolases","Oxidases","Dioxygenases","Monooxygenases","Dehydrogenases"]'::jsonb, 'Monooxygenases', 'Monooxygenases (mixed-function oxidases) incorporate one O atom into the substrate and reduce the other to water. Cytochrome P450 is a key monooxygenase system.'),
('c3892d3a-7f30-5409-823f-aee0ff4d6823'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Selenocysteine is considered the:', '["Non-standard amino acid","First amino acid","Rarest amino acid","22nd amino acid","21st amino acid"]'::jsonb, '21st amino acid', 'Selenocysteine (Sec U) is the 21st proteinogenic amino acid incorporated during translation via a recoded UGA stop codon and specialized tRNA.'),
('e4892af7-fcfb-5622-a28d-2a2465eba0dc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The cofactor for glutathione peroxidase is:', '["Selenium","Zinc","Iron","Manganese","Copper"]'::jsonb, 'Selenium', 'Glutathione peroxidase contains selenium as selenocysteine at its active site. It reduces H2O2 and organic hydroperoxides protecting cells from oxidative damage.'),
('9df27c07-762c-528e-b7e3-f3077fc678bd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of fatty acid oxidation is:', '["Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I","Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II","Acyl-CoA dehydrogenase","Beta-keto thiolase","Enoyl-CoA hydratase"]'::jsonb, 'Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I', 'CPT-I controls entry of long-chain fatty acyl-CoAs into mitochondria for beta-oxidation. It is inhibited by malonyl-CoA (product of fatty acid synthesis).'),
('b07288ab-9ef5-5b5d-8c9d-5eb114bf01dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Glycogenin is:', '["A glycogen synthase","A branching enzyme","A glycogen phosphorylase","A glycogen debranching enzyme","The primer for glycogen synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'The primer for glycogen synthesis', 'Glycogenin is a self-glucosylating protein that provides the initial oligosaccharide primer for glycogen synthesis by adding ~8 glucose units to itself.'),
('c91c5237-d146-5a20-be1d-bc6eadd9c6f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The Cori cycle involves the conversion of:', '["Lactate to glucose","Glucose to lactate","Alanine to glucose","Glucose to alanine","Glycerol to glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate to glucose', 'In the Cori cycle lactate produced by anaerobic metabolism in muscle and RBCs is converted to glucose in the liver via gluconeogenesis.'),
('8eb7d6d6-a73f-5bb6-bd2f-ca3a7fa402aa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Adenylyl cyclase produces:', '["cGMP from GTP","ATP from cAMP","cAMP from ATP","ADP from ATP","AMP from ADP"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP from ATP', 'Adenylyl cyclase converts ATP to cyclic AMP (cAMP), a second messenger that activates protein kinase A mediating hormonal responses.'),
('4568fbba-5826-5e35-a08f-233aa53d89b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The primary cofactor for one-carbon metabolism is:', '["Biotin","Coenzyme A","Thiamine pyrophosphate","Tetrahydrofolate","Lipoic acid"]'::jsonb, 'Tetrahydrofolate', 'THF carries one-carbon units at various oxidation states necessary for purine thymidine and methionine synthesis. Dihydrofolate reductase maintains THF levels.'),
('afd9c58a-af20-509f-b8f4-0c52138e1546'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Glutamine is a nitrogen donor for:', '["All of the above","Purine synthesis","Urea cycle","None of the above","Pyrimidine synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Glutamine provides the amide nitrogen for purine rings (N3 N9) and pyrimidine rings (N3). It also donates nitrogen in several other biosynthetic reactions.'),
('711f6630-5f07-59c7-9062-8b8886501e48'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. DNA replication is described as:', '["Semiconservative","Linear","Conservative","Random","Dispersive"]'::jsonb, 'Semiconservative', 'Meselson-Stahl experiment proved semiconservative replication: each daughter DNA molecule has one parental strand and one newly synthesized strand.'),
('60475d66-753a-59ce-9012-bfdc21daf554'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Bacterial DNA gyrase is:', '["A type II topoisomerase","A type I topoisomerase","A helicase","A ligase","A primase"]'::jsonb, 'A type II topoisomerase', 'DNA gyrase introduces negative supercoils using ATP energy and relieves positive supercoils ahead of replication forks. It is a target for quinolone antibiotics.'),
('8f6a57e1-b84f-5021-9643-e28109316a91'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The primary structure of an enzyme determines its:', '["Three-dimensional conformation","Post-translational modifications","Subcellular localization","Half-life","Degradation rate"]'::jsonb, 'Three-dimensional conformation', 'The amino acid sequence (primary structure) contains all information required for folding into the native three-dimensional conformation as demonstrated by Anfinsen''s experiment.'),
('25ac6b8c-7d22-54e7-871c-23ac905720d7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which vitamin is a component of coenzyme FAD?', '["Thiamine","Pyridoxine","Riboflavin","Pantothenic acid","Niacin"]'::jsonb, 'Riboflavin', 'FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide) is derived from riboflavin (vitamin B2). It functions as an electron carrier in redox reactions accepting two electrons.'),
('fa24bacc-9d28-5658-97f2-98a836de6dbf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The product of the oxidative phase of the pentose phosphate pathway is:', '["NADPH and ribose-5-P","ATP and CO2","FADH2 and CO2","NADH and ATP","GTP and NADPH"]'::jsonb, 'NADPH and ribose-5-P', 'The oxidative phase produces NADPH (for reductive biosynthesis) and ribose-5-phosphate (for nucleotide synthesis). The non-oxidative phase recycles pentoses.'),
('f9f4ee18-bad0-5011-89e8-3e1be089b039'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which amino acid is a precursor for the hormone melatonin?', '["Serine","Histidine","Tyrosine","Tryptophan","Glutamate"]'::jsonb, 'Tryptophan', 'Melatonin is synthesized from tryptophan via serotonin. Serotonin is acetylated and methylated in the pineal gland to form melatonin regulating circadian rhythms.'),
('59be5f4c-0269-599e-943d-fdf5753b2648'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In the urea cycle arginase converts:', '["Ornithine to citrulline","Carbamoyl phosphate to citrulline","Arginine to ornithine and urea","Citrulline to argininosuccinate","Argininosuccinate to arginine"]'::jsonb, 'Arginine to ornithine and urea', 'Arginase hydrolyzes arginine to ornithine and urea. This is the final step of the urea cycle. Ornithine reenters the cycle.'),
('77314938-b211-5179-9d34-0e7c82c7daa4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Transamination reactions require the cofactor:', '["FAD","Pyridoxal phosphate","Coenzyme A","NAD+","Thiamine pyrophosphate"]'::jsonb, 'Pyridoxal phosphate', 'PLP (vitamin B6) is the cofactor for aminotransferases (transaminases) catalyzing reversible transfer of amino groups between amino acids and alpha-keto acids.'),
('e6db436e-7b1f-53d5-84f2-7338bd286d2f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The molecule 2,3-BPG binds to:', '["Fetal hemoglobin only","Myoglobin","Oxyhemoglobin stabilizing the R-state","Deoxyhemoglobin stabilizing the T-state","Heme iron"]'::jsonb, 'Deoxyhemoglobin stabilizing the T-state', '2,3-BPG binds in the central cavity of deoxyhemoglobin stabilizing the low-affinity T-state and promoting oxygen unloading in tissues.'),
('527216c5-32f3-55da-b889-1ac3ae26e9dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The human body can store excess glucose as:', '["Fat only","Starch","Protein only","Cellulose","Glycogen in liver and muscle"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen in liver and muscle', 'Excess glucose is stored as glycogen (liver ~100g muscle ~400g). Once glycogen stores are saturated glucose is converted to fat via de novo lipogenesis.'),
('80fa1e75-66b9-5772-827c-ac5ef6323741'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following is a ketogenic amino acid?', '["Lysine","Aspartate","Glycine","Glutamate","Alanine"]'::jsonb, 'Lysine', 'Lysine and leucine are purely ketogenic yielding only acetyl-CoA/acetoacetate. Isoleucine phenylalanine tryptophan tyrosine and threonine are both glucogenic and ketogenic.'),
('06ec5f77-a8e8-5ec9-8d10-8bb80103fdfa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The cross-bridges in striated muscle are formed by:', '["ATP binding to myosin","Myosin heads binding to actin","Tropomyosin binding to actin","Calcium binding to troponin","Actin binding to troponin"]'::jsonb, 'Myosin heads binding to actin', 'Myosin heads form cross-bridges with actin filaments. ATP binding causes myosin detachment and hydrolysis provides energy for the power stroke.'),
('798f7006-7c25-5b07-8f8c-f546588df409'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Insulin promotes:', '["Ketogenesis","Gluconeogenesis","Glycogenesis","Lipolysis","Glycogenolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogenesis', 'Insulin stimulates glycogen synthesis (glycogenesis) and inhibits glycogenolysis gluconeogenesis and ketogenesis. It promotes glucose uptake and storage.'),
('77b903df-4536-50c2-a29e-96ffecfbf81c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The enzyme that converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II is:', '["ACE","Chymase","Renin","Cathepsin D","Trypsin"]'::jsonb, 'ACE', 'ACE is a dipeptidyl carboxypeptidase that removes two amino acids from angiotensin I to form angiotensin II. ACE inhibitors are used as antihypertensives.'),
('284c01f7-57e7-5abc-9a6d-91b34a9912b2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The final electron acceptor in the electron transport chain is:', '["Oxygen","NAD+","FAD","Cytochrome c","Coenzyme Q"]'::jsonb, 'Oxygen', 'Molecular oxygen (O2) is the terminal electron acceptor reduced to water by cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV). Without oxygen the electron transport chain stops.'),
('67e1a50d-5811-5002-90ba-012fd836270d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Glycolysis converts one molecule of glucose to:', '["1 pyruvate","2 acetyl-CoA","2 lactate","2 pyruvate","2 oxaloacetate"]'::jsonb, '2 pyruvate', 'Glycolysis splits 6-carbon glucose into two 3-carbon pyruvate molecules producing net 2 ATP and 2 NADH. Pyruvate enters mitochondria for further oxidation.'),
('f659618f-6fa1-5610-ab26-a1aa548978fc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The allosteric activator of PFK-1 is:', '["Citrate","NADH","ATP","Glucagon","Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate', 'Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate is the most potent allosteric activator of PFK-1. Levels are regulated by bifunctional PFK-2/FBPase-2 in response to glucagon and insulin.'),
('cdb0cfb6-7fe3-55ac-bb82-04c44f03e009'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The term ''isoenzymes'' refers to:', '["Different enzyme forms that catalyze the same reaction","Enzymes with identical sequences","Enzymes from different species","Enzymes with different substrates","Enzymes that have been denatured"]'::jsonb, 'Different enzyme forms that catalyze the same reaction', 'Isoenzymes (isozymes) are different molecular forms of an enzyme catalyzing the same reaction. They differ in amino acid sequence kinetics and regulatory properties (e.g. LDH isoenzymes).'),
('e66d710f-b777-5968-90e5-16e172424200'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The mitochondrial pyruvate carrier transports:', '["Pyruvate into the mitochondrial matrix","Pyruvate out of mitochondria","Acetyl-CoA into mitochondria","Glucose into mitochondria","Lactate into mitochondria"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate into the mitochondrial matrix', 'Pyruvate is transported into the mitochondrial matrix by the mitochondrial pyruvate carrier (MPC) where it is converted to acetyl-CoA by pyruvate dehydrogenase complex.'),
('be5c4c0f-1e56-5588-af37-8e935bac1ca2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which mineral is essential for blood coagulation?', '["Potassium","Sodium","Magnesium","Calcium","Iron"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium', 'Calcium ions (Ca2+) are essential for multiple steps of the coagulation cascade including activation of prothrombin to thrombin and fibrin formation.'),
('1abe2ea2-4dc7-5656-9e82-e9d0e4d89c39'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The liver synthesizes ketone bodies during:', '["Fed state","Carbohydrate excess","Fasting and starvation","Exercise","Sleep"]'::jsonb, 'Fasting and starvation', 'Ketone bodies are synthesized during fasting when carbohydrate is scarce. Acetyl-CoA from fatty acid oxidation is diverted to ketogenesis providing fuel for the brain.'),
('a8897d39-c153-5f62-a3c7-84581742118f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The oxygen dissociation curve of myoglobin is:', '["Parabolic","Hyperbolic","Linear","Sigmoidal","Exponential"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperbolic', 'Myoglobin has a hyperbolic oxygen dissociation curve because it binds oxygen non-cooperatively (single heme). P50 is ~2-3 mmHg vs ~26 mmHg for hemoglobin.'),
('89450e0c-900a-5568-9cf9-8268f5634495'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The main store of glycogen in the body is in:', '["Liver and skeletal muscle","Adipose tissue","Brain","Kidney","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and skeletal muscle', 'The liver stores ~100g and muscle ~400g of glycogen. Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose; muscle glycogen provides rapid energy for contraction.'),
('d8d2600d-bcf2-547d-b2c6-651e9230f97c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In prokaryotes the Shine-Dalgarno sequence:', '["Ribosome-binding site on mRNA","Promoter sequence","Terminator sequence","Origin of replication","Operator sequence"]'::jsonb, 'Ribosome-binding site on mRNA', 'The Shine-Dalgarno sequence (AGGAGGU) on bacterial mRNA base-pairs with the 16S rRNA of the small ribosomal subunit positioning the start codon correctly.'),
('32cf86f9-ce43-5d32-9ce1-c0d45055d370'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The Tm of DNA varies with:', '["A+T content","G+C content","Length","Shape","Sequence complexity"]'::jsonb, 'G+C content', 'The melting temperature (Tm) increases with G+C content due to three H-bonds in G≡C vs two in A=T pairs requiring more heat to denature.'),
('3a64d716-ddc7-5806-a2a1-58de93a648d6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Fructose metabolism primarily occurs in the:', '["Muscle","Adipose","Brain","Kidney","Liver"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'Fructose is metabolized in the liver via fructokinase and aldolase B bypassing PFK-1 regulation. Excess fructose consumption contributes to hepatic lipogenesis.'),
('03922f21-711c-5fbb-b985-9c1555e5493a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The main structural protein of the extracellular matrix is:', '["Collagen","Elastin","Keratin","Actin","Myosin"]'::jsonb, 'Collagen', 'Collagen is the most abundant protein in animals (25-30% of total protein) providing structural support in connective tissues bones tendons and skin.'),
('e2a56b3f-e7df-5fcc-b7a3-c76a923c8220'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Hormones that act via intracellular receptors include:', '["Cytokines metabolic pathway process","Catecholamines metabolic pathway process","Steroid hormones and thyroid hormones","Growth factors metabolic pathway process","Peptide hormones metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Steroid hormones and thyroid hormones', 'Steroid and thyroid hormones are lipophilic crossing the plasma membrane to bind intracellular/nuclear receptors acting as transcription factors.'),
('0ee4bcfa-2dd0-52bc-bc5e-19f0de21444d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The endoplasmic reticulum is the site of:', '["Glycolysis metabolic pathway process","ATP synthesis metabolic pathway process","Protein synthesis and lipid synthesis","DNA replication metabolic pathway process","Ketogenesis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Protein synthesis and lipid synthesis', 'The rough ER (with ribosomes) synthesizes secretory and membrane proteins. The smooth ER synthesizes lipids steroids and detoxifies drugs.'),
('d049e513-0cca-5535-9248-29f2e1983f60'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Sphingosine is a backbone component of:', '["Sphingolipids","Glycerophospholipids","Triacylglycerols","Waxes","Cholesterol esters"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingolipids', 'Sphingosine is an amino alcohol that forms the backbone of sphingolipids (sphingomyelin cerebrosides gangliosides). It is not found in glycerophospholipids.'),
('6d11ae88-a4ab-5a72-b3d1-86562e5e7e00'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The active form of vitamin D is:', '["1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","Cholecalciferol","25-hydroxycholecalciferol","7-dehydrocholesterol","Ergocalciferol"]'::jsonb, '1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol', '1,25-(OH)2-D3 (calcitriol) is the active form formed by 1-alpha-hydroxylation in the kidney. It increases intestinal calcium and phosphate absorption.'),
('ae9a132d-83b9-5c25-9f88-5482cded9d91'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The steps of glycolysis that consume ATP are catalyzed by:', '["Hexokinase and PFK-1","Phosphoglycerate kinase and pyruvate kinase","Pyruvate kinase and PFK-1","Aldolase and enolase","Hexokinase and pyruvate kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Hexokinase and PFK-1', 'Hexokinase uses 1 ATP to phosphorylate glucose. PFK-1 uses 1 ATP to phosphorylate F6P. These 2 ATP are invested per glucose before net ATP production.'),
('bf56edf7-d3d6-5e3d-93b8-1863276986d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The signal recognition particle (SRP) is involved in:', '["DNA replication","RNA splicing","Targeting proteins to the ER","Chromatin remodeling","Protein degradation"]'::jsonb, 'Targeting proteins to the ER', 'SRP binds to the signal peptide of nascent secretory/membrane proteins and targets the ribosome to the ER membrane for co-translational translocation.'),
('4c082e53-6e9b-575b-968f-4fa26459e164'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The amino acid that absorbs UV light at 280 nm most strongly is:', '["Histidine","Phenylalanine","Tryptophan","Cysteine","Tyrosine"]'::jsonb, 'Tryptophan', 'Tryptophan has the highest molar extinction coefficient at 280 nm due to its indole ring. Tyrosine also absorbs but less strongly. Proteins are quantified at 280 nm.'),
('96a34fab-5974-56bd-abcd-0f8571024ba6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The base pair with the highest melting temperature is:', '["A-U","C-G","T-A","A-T","G-C"]'::jsonb, 'G-C', 'G-C pairs have 3 hydrogen bonds requiring more energy to separate than A-T pairs (2 bonds). G+C-rich regions denature at higher temperatures.'),
('7cbe0f99-1eb2-5aca-8d04-c328fc9c9aa1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Anabolism is to catabolism as:', '["Synthesis is to degradation","Reduction is to oxidation","Degradation is to synthesis","Hydrolysis is to condensation","ATP production is to ATP usage"]'::jsonb, 'Synthesis is to degradation', 'Anabolism refers to biosynthetic pathways building complex molecules requiring energy. Catabolism breaks down molecules releasing energy.'),
('e78cdc3b-aadc-5d5a-bd11-381dd43f383d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The small nuclear ribonucleoproteins (snRNPs) function in:', '["Replication","Transcription","Splicing of pre-mRNA","Repair","Translation"]'::jsonb, 'Splicing of pre-mRNA', 'snRNPs (U1 U2 U4 U5 U6) form the spliceosome that removes introns from pre-mRNA. They recognize splice sites and catalyze the splicing reaction.'),
('11c19fc6-dc75-5980-a54d-8bd17dfde82c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. Mutations in the p53 gene are associated with:', '["Many cancers","Cystic fibrosis","Huntington disease","Sickle cell anemia","PKU"]'::jsonb, 'Many cancers', 'p53 is a tumor suppressor protein that regulates cell cycle arrest DNA repair and apoptosis. It is mutated in >50% of human cancers (''guardian of the genome'').'),
('19035677-8b91-5da1-8b22-dedeec45b4d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The conversion of proinsulin to insulin involves:', '["Proteolytic removal of C-peptide","Phosphorylation metabolic pathway process","Glycosylation metabolic pathway process","Acetylation metabolic pathway process","Hydroxylation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Proteolytic removal of C-peptide', 'Proinsulin is cleaved by prohormone convertases to remove the C-peptide producing mature insulin (A and B chains linked by disulfide bonds).'),
('4e6d8bf7-4724-546a-b635-b38638136913'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The drug allopurinol treats gout by:', '["Inhibiting uric acid synthesis","Inhibiting xanthine oxidase","Increasing uricase activity","Decreasing purine absorption","Increasing uric acid excretion"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting xanthine oxidase', 'Allopurinol is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor reducing uric acid production. It also inhibits de novo pyrimidine synthesis (orotidine-5''-phosphate decarboxylase).'),
('c0daf482-d6f4-5623-b7e9-b85d70a2153f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Pyruvate dehydrogenase is inactivated by:', '["High ADP","Dephosphorylation by PDP","Phosphorylation by PDK","High CoA","High NAD+"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylation by PDK', 'PDH kinase (PDK) phosphorylates and inactivates PDH when ATP NADH and acetyl-CoA are high. Insulin activates PDP (phosphatase) to dephosphorylate and activate PDH.'),
('36caf49f-6eba-5397-a47b-c8626ba10499'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following uses the energy of GTP?', '["Translation","Glycolysis","Gluconeogenesis","Fatty acid synthesis","Electron transport chain"]'::jsonb, 'Translation', 'GTP provides energy for peptide bond formation during translation. EF-Tu uses GTP to deliver aminoacyl-tRNA to the ribosome A site.'),
('02818521-c2b8-50e9-ad53-a0bb35930fdc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'A nuclease is an enzyme that:', '["Degrades nucleic acids","Synthesizes nucleic acids","Joins nucleic acid fragments","Modifies bases","Unwinds DNA"]'::jsonb, 'Degrades nucleic acids', 'Nucleases hydrolyze phosphodiester bonds in DNA and RNA. Endonucleases cut internal phosphodiester bonds; exonucleases cut from the ends.'),
('8825b9dc-e03a-5f3d-96c6-10e2cb0d9643'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The nucleoside adenosine consists of:', '["Adenine + ribose + phosphate","Adenine + deoxyribose","Adenine + glucose","Guanine + ribose","Adenine + ribose"]'::jsonb, 'Adenine + ribose', 'Adenosine is a nucleoside where adenine is attached to ribose via a beta-N9-glycosidic bond. Adenosine monophosphate (AMP) is the nucleotide.'),
('24d0f8ad-583d-51f9-a219-fe41d2ae83f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The role of cyclic AMP (cAMP) in cells is:', '["Second messenger","Genetic material","Energy source","Structural component","Enzyme cofactor"]'::jsonb, 'Second messenger', 'cAMP is a second messenger synthesized from ATP by adenylyl cyclase in response to hormones like glucagon and epinephrine, activating protein kinase A (PKA).'),
('c97bad37-ddec-5223-b11f-09e2b4a49b77'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following has a porphyrin ring structure?', '["ATP","Coenzyme A","Cholesterol","Heme","NADH"]'::jsonb, 'Heme', 'Heme contains a porphyrin ring (tetrapyrrole) with a central Fe2+ ion. Porphyrins are cyclic tetrapyrroles with side chains derived from porphobilinogen.'),
('d6f6d5f1-7be5-5285-b87c-072a50577671'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The copper-containing protein in the electron transport chain is:', '["Cytochrome b","Succinate dehydrogenase","Cytochrome c oxidase","NADH dehydrogenase","Cytochrome c"]'::jsonb, 'Cytochrome c oxidase', 'Cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV) contains copper centers (CuA and CuB) in addition to heme a and a3. It catalyzes the reduction of O2 to H2O.'),
('72b0c160-70e6-532d-85ca-828632647bd3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'The allosteric inhibitor of ATCase is:', '["ADP","CTP","UTP","GTP","ATP"]'::jsonb, 'CTP', 'Aspartate transcarbamoylase (ATCase) is inhibited by CTP (end product of pyrimidine pathway) via feedback inhibition. ATP activates ATCase by antagonizing CTP inhibition.'),
('30ce750f-2619-5325-a25e-52b6e371ba10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'In gluconeogenesis pyruvate is converted to oxaloacetate by:', '["Pyruvate dehydrogenase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Malate dehydrogenase","PEPCK","Pyruvate kinase"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate carboxylase', 'Pyruvate carboxylase (biotin-dependent) converts pyruvate to oxaloacetate in mitochondria. This is a key anaplerotic reaction and first step of gluconeogenesis from pyruvate.'),
('deb18672-d3ae-5eaa-93a5-a25f35e2594b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomolecules', 'Which of the following is correctly matched?', '["Vitamin D - collagen synthesis","Vitamin C - bone mineralization","Vitamin A - antioxidant","Vitamin K - blood clotting","Vitamin E - vision"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K - blood clotting', 'Vitamin K is essential for gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II VII IX X and proteins C S making them functional calcium-binding proteins.'),
('3c16d96e-7cdd-50e1-b828-eec31381fbcd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, The rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis is:', '["Phosphofructokinase-1","Hexokinase","Pyruvate kinase","Glucokinase","Aldolase"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphofructokinase-1', 'PFK-1 catalyzes the first committed step of glycolysis converting F6P to F1,6BP. It is regulated by ATP citrate and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate.'),
('2562eeae-4313-5d56-8b97-4769b505d3c4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Which enzyme is the primary regulatory point of gluconeogenesis?', '["Glucose-6-phosphatase","Pyruvate carboxylase","Aldolase","PEPCK","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase', 'Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase hydrolyzes F1,6BP to F6P and Pi bypassing PFK-1. It is allosterically inhibited by AMP and activated by citrate.'),
('7d1013bb-6360-5abf-a917-45064261de05'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Glucagon exerts its effect by:', '["Direct activation of enzymes","Decreasing cAMP levels","Activating guanylyl cyclase","Binding to intracellular receptors","Increasing cAMP levels"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing cAMP levels', 'Glucagon binds to its G-protein-coupled receptor activating adenylyl cyclase which increases cAMP. cAMP activates PKA which phosphorylates target enzymes.'),
('f7e1ed4b-b76f-5e6f-8290-fb85172a5e9c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Insulin binding to its receptor results in:', '["Activation of adenylyl cyclase","Autophosphorylation of tyrosine kinase domains","Increased cAMP","Increased cGMP","Increased IP3 and DAG"]'::jsonb, 'Autophosphorylation of tyrosine kinase domains', 'The insulin receptor is a tyrosine kinase receptor. Insulin binding causes autophosphorylation of beta subunits which then phosphorylate IRS proteins activating PI3K and MAPK pathways.'),
('59ca5ec6-5a88-5e9b-b1c3-e45b6943a34f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme activated by phosphorylation in response to glucagon is:', '["Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","PFK-2/FBPase-2","Pyruvate kinase","Glycogen phosphorylase","Glycogen synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen phosphorylase', 'Glucagon via cAMP/PKA phosphorylates and activates glycogen phosphorylase (glycogenolysis) and phosphorylase kinase while inhibiting glycogen synthase.'),
('43b199b4-f9b9-5ee6-be06-2d39933b4f82'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Epinephrine increases blood glucose by:', '["Stimulating glycogenolysis in liver","Inhibiting gluconeogenesis","Stimulating insulin release","Inhibiting glucagon release","Stimulating glucose uptake"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating glycogenolysis in liver', 'Epinephrine activates liver glycogen phosphorylase via beta-adrenergic receptors and cAMP causing rapid glycogen breakdown and glucose release into blood.'),
('698dd4ee-9290-5207-aa27-ce6efa921b26'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The substrate concentration at half Vmax is called:', '["Km","Kcat","Vmax","Kd","Ki"]'::jsonb, 'Km', 'Km (Michaelis constant) is the substrate concentration that gives half of Vmax. It is a measure of the affinity of the enzyme for its substrate (lower Km = higher affinity).'),
('b8b5a319-18f2-577d-84ae-848797e78ec8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Covalent modification of enzymes most commonly involves:', '["Methylation","Glycosylation","Acetylation","Ubiquitination","Phosphorylation"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylation', 'Phosphorylation (addition/removal of phosphate groups) is the most common covalent modification regulating enzyme activity. Kinases add phosphate phosphatases remove it.'),
('86eeca62-bcf6-5ce7-a5a5-62c825ee2899'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Zymogens are activated by:', '["pH change","Phosphorylation","Proteolytic cleavage","Binding of allosteric effectors","Temperature change"]'::jsonb, 'Proteolytic cleavage', 'Zymogens (proenzyme) are inactive precursors activated by limited proteolysis cleaving specific peptide bonds. Examples: trypsinogen pepsinogen prothrombin.'),
('8200b704-c9ce-595a-8b2f-44adafc1abfc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'In a surgical context, The cofactor for glutathione peroxidase is:', '["Selenium","Zinc","Copper","Iron","Magnesium"]'::jsonb, 'Selenium', 'Glutathione peroxidase contains selenocysteine at its active site. It reduces H2O2 and lipid peroxides using glutathione (GSH) as substrate protecting cells from oxidative damage.'),
('768a7600-46f4-5f9b-8505-560eb0d69050'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme aldolase in glycolysis catalyzes:', '["Conversion of PEP to pyruvate","Isomerization of G6P to F6P","Phosphorylation of fructose-6-P","Oxidation of glyceraldehyde-3-P","Cleavage of F1,6BP into DHAP and G3P"]'::jsonb, 'Cleavage of F1,6BP into DHAP and G3P', 'Aldolase cleaves fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into two triose phosphates: dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP) and glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate (G3P).'),
('1646ff46-ad48-5819-88da-411543ae9f9e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'How does insulin affect glycogen synthase?', '["Degrades the enzyme","Inhibits by phosphorylation","No effect","Activates by dephosphorylation","Converts it to inactive form"]'::jsonb, 'Activates by dephosphorylation', 'Insulin activates protein phosphatase 1 (PP1) which dephosphorylates and activates glycogen synthase promoting glycogenesis. Glucagon opposes this by phosphorylation.'),
('9a69d2c9-6f59-5b02-a257-6845c4caab18'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The Michaelis-Menten equation assumes:', '["First-order kinetics only","Zero-order kinetics only","Equilibrium of ES complex","Irreversible product formation","Steady-state of ES complex"]'::jsonb, 'Steady-state of ES complex', 'Michaelis-Menten kinetics assume the steady-state approximation where the concentration of ES complex remains constant during the initial velocity phase.'),
('f36b2fa3-15c9-59bf-8e2d-cacc7357fa3d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Which of the following is a key feature of allosteric enzymes?', '["Linear kinetics","Sigmoidal kinetics","Hyperbolic kinetics","First-order kinetics","Exponential kinetics"]'::jsonb, 'Sigmoidal kinetics', 'Allosteric enzymes display sigmoidal (S-shaped) rate curves due to cooperative substrate binding. This allows sensitive regulation by small changes in substrate or effector concentrations.'),
('291ed483-be0f-5669-bb38-b257362d0a1c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The allosteric enzyme ATCase is activated by:', '["TTP","GTP","ATP","CTP","UTP"]'::jsonb, 'ATP', 'ATP activates ATCase (aspartate transcarbamoylase) by binding to regulatory subunits increasing activity. CTP inhibits ATCase providing feedback control of pyrimidine biosynthesis.'),
('ea0b76a2-2de2-5987-a82f-032e01cce77d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The vitamin B3 (niacin) derivative that acts as a coenzyme is:', '["FAD","PLP","CoA","NAD+ and NADP+","TPP"]'::jsonb, 'NAD+ and NADP+', 'Niacin is incorporated into NAD+ and NADP+. NAD+ functions in catabolic redox reactions. NADPH functions in anabolic reductive biosynthesis and antioxidant defense.'),
('c5edfc01-85a4-5d9c-8fd2-d8d4028dc916'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Which vitamin derivative is Coenzyme A?', '["Pantothenic acid","Niacin","Riboflavin","Thiamine","Pyridoxine"]'::jsonb, 'Pantothenic acid', 'Coenzyme A contains pantothenic acid (B5) plus mercaptoethylamine and adenosine 3'',5''-diphosphate. It carries acyl groups as thioesters for metabolic reactions.'),
('8c3b7016-2c05-5b15-9cfa-5dcfe10e500d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Biotin is a cofactor for:', '["Carboxylation reactions","Transamination reactions","Oxidation reactions","Methylation reactions","Decarboxylation reactions"]'::jsonb, 'Carboxylation reactions', 'Biotin carries CO2 as carboxybiotin for carboxylation reactions: acetyl-CoA carboxylase pyruvate carboxylase propionyl-CoA carboxylase and methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase.'),
('9bcaef2a-37a8-5a39-89a1-211aa35726ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'FAD is derived from which vitamin?', '["Niacin","Riboflavin","Thiamine","Cobalamin","Pyridoxine"]'::jsonb, 'Riboflavin', 'FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide) is derived from riboflavin. It accepts two electrons and two protons forming FADH2 and is a cofactor for many dehydrogenases.'),
('f98223b8-5d80-5ef1-96f5-d41a784bc9ca'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is a cofactor for:', '["Pyruvate carboxylase","ATP synthase","Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex","Cytochrome oxidase","Glutathione peroxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex', 'TPP is essential for oxidative decarboxylation reactions catalyzed by pyruvate dehydrogenase alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase and transketolase.'),
('a9d7c3cb-eac8-54a4-9429-d2aca25f2c1b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP) is a cofactor for:', '["Carboxylases","Kinases","Oxidases","Transaminases","Dehydrogenases"]'::jsonb, 'Transaminases', 'PLP (vitamin B6) is the cofactor for aminotransferases (transaminases) that catalyze amino group transfer between amino acids and keto acids essential for amino acid metabolism.'),
('2561499b-0727-5270-884b-f600ad6feb29'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Magnesium functions as a cofactor for:', '["Lipases","Proteases","Nucleases","All kinases","Oxidoreductases"]'::jsonb, 'All kinases', 'Mg2+ is required by all kinases as a Mg-ATP complex facilitating ATP binding and catalysis. It is also essential for many other enzymes including DNA polymerase.'),
('0bb635e7-152d-54c5-845a-9562cbb4cd67'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Zinc is a cofactor for:', '["Alcohol dehydrogenase","Catalase","Cytochrome oxidase","Glutathione peroxidase","Superoxide dismutase"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol dehydrogenase', 'Zinc is essential for alcohol dehydrogenase alkaline phosphatase carboxypeptidase and DNA polymerase. Zinc deficiency causes growth retardation and impaired immunity.'),
('31dd2f53-8fa0-5938-b6f8-62f92017a34b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The mineral component of cytochrome c oxidase is:', '["Zinc","Magnesium","Manganese","Iron","Copper"]'::jsonb, 'Copper', 'Cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV) contains both copper (CuA CuB) and iron (heme a a3). Copper is essential for electron transfer to oxygen.'),
('2767ace2-44dc-50b4-be0d-e070ebe20491'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Iron is a component of:', '["Glucagon","Vitamin B12","Insulin","Thyroid hormones","Hemoglobin and cytochromes"]'::jsonb, 'Hemoglobin and cytochromes', 'Iron is essential for hemoglobin myoglobin cytochromes and many enzymes. Iron deficiency causes microcytic hypochromic anemia.'),
('4c267345-9d5f-5dff-90c6-f5f565f56ef8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The mineral required for superoxide dismutase activity is:', '["Zinc, copper, or manganese","Iron only","Calcium only","Selenium only","Magnesium only"]'::jsonb, 'Zinc, copper, or manganese', 'Cu,Zn-SOD (cytosolic) uses copper and zinc. Mn-SOD (mitochondrial) uses manganese. These enzymes protect against superoxide radical damage.'),
('301d9c5d-ae7c-58d3-88fc-c6a5b5a7f2aa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Calcium functions as:', '["An electron carrier","A component of cytochromes","A second messenger and enzyme activator","A cofactor for carboxylases","A structural component of RNA"]'::jsonb, 'A second messenger and enzyme activator', 'Ca2+ acts as a second messenger binding calmodulin and activating CaM-kinases. It is also essential for muscle contraction blood clotting and exocytosis.'),
('b943913f-616b-54c7-bf04-88ac0418c345'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Which hormone decreases blood glucose?', '["Growth hormone","Epinephrine","Cortisol","Glucagon","Insulin"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin', 'Insulin lowers blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake glycogenesis and inhibiting gluconeogenesis. The other hormones raise blood glucose.'),
('bc71c485-13c1-5526-95f6-633c473964d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'With respect to management, Cortisol increases blood glucose by:', '["Stimulating gluconeogenesis","Promoting glycogenesis","Stimulating glucose uptake","Inhibiting protein catabolism","Stimulating glycolysis"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating gluconeogenesis', 'Cortisol induces gluconeogenic enzymes (PEPCK G6Pase) in the liver and promotes protein catabolism in muscle providing amino acids for gluconeogenesis.'),
('bb591612-35ff-5832-bd70-1b1cc7a79b9f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Growth hormone raises blood glucose by:', '["Reducing glucose uptake and increasing lipolysis","Promoting glycogenesis","Stimulating glucose oxidation","Stimulating insulin secretion","Inhibiting gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Reducing glucose uptake and increasing lipolysis', 'Growth hormone has anti-insulin effects: reduces peripheral glucose uptake and stimulates lipolysis providing fatty acids and glycerol as alternative fuels.'),
('85d83195-412d-5286-a163-521bcf1af5d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The main hormone that signals the fed state is:', '["Insulin","Glucagon","Growth hormone","Cortisol","Epinephrine"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin', 'Insulin is secreted in response to elevated blood glucose (fed state). It promotes storage of nutrients as glycogen fat and protein.'),
('13f9f684-8dfa-582e-acea-0bb6f43302a5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The main hormone of the fasting state is:', '["Aldosterone","Glucagon","Epinephrine","Insulin","Cortisol"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon', 'Glucagon is secreted when blood glucose falls maintaining glucose via glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. Its secretion is inhibited by insulin.'),
('cda1e764-73bf-58ab-ac8f-c65489ad3716'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis is:', '["HMG-CoA reductase","HMG-CoA synthase","Mevalonate kinase","Squalene synthase","Acetyl-CoA acetyltransferase"]'::jsonb, 'HMG-CoA reductase', 'HMG-CoA reductase catalyzes HMG-CoA to mevalonate using 2 NADPH. This is the committed step of cholesterol synthesis and target of statin drugs.'),
('db05204b-9077-58dc-ac46-2169ed136033'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of fatty acid synthesis is:', '["Beta-ketoacyl-ACP synthase","Fatty acid synthase","Thioesterase","Malonyl-CoA:ACP transacylase","Acetyl-CoA carboxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase', 'Acetyl-CoA carboxylase converts acetyl-CoA to malonyl-CoA the committed step of fatty acid synthesis. It is activated by insulin and citrate and inhibited by glucagon and palmitoyl-CoA.'),
('a665ee52-264f-5ba5-b1bb-b227ea5a9eb3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of the TCA cycle is:', '["Isocitrate dehydrogenase","Citrate synthase","Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Succinate dehydrogenase","Malate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Isocitrate dehydrogenase', 'Isocitrate dehydrogenase catalyzes oxidative decarboxylation of isocitrate to alpha-ketoglutarate. It is activated by ADP and inhibited by ATP and NADH.'),
('141cd084-2493-57ed-95aa-a9a4a05d5a33'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme aspartate transcarbamoylase is inhibited by:', '["CTP","Carbamoyl phosphate","Aspartate","ATP","UTP"]'::jsonb, 'CTP', 'CTP inhibits ATCase via feedback inhibition of pyrimidine biosynthesis. ATP activates it. This ensures balanced purine and pyrimidine synthesis.'),
('b8f731f0-fa7d-58ee-ad45-414aed31b040'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Competitive inhibition is characterized by:', '["Increased Km Vmax unchanged","Decreased Km Vmax unchanged","Decreased Vmax Km unchanged","Increased both","Decreased both"]'::jsonb, 'Increased Km Vmax unchanged', 'Competitive inhibitors bind the active site. Higher substrate overcomes inhibition (same Vmax). Km increases because more substrate needed to reach half Vmax.'),
('b8c1f6f7-32bc-5c44-bd8f-9d39ed449634'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Non-competitive inhibition is characterized by:', '["Decreased Vmax Km unchanged","Decreased both","Increased both","Increased Km Vmax unchanged","No change"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased Vmax Km unchanged', 'Non-competitive inhibitors bind elsewhere reducing the number of functional enzyme molecules. Vmax decreases but remaining enzymes have normal affinity (Km unchanged).'),
('c22a4545-86e1-578d-b992-56478295323b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'An uncompetitive inhibitor binds:', '["To both free enzyme and ES complex","Only to the enzyme-substrate complex","Only to free enzyme","To the allosteric site","To the active site"]'::jsonb, 'Only to the enzyme-substrate complex', 'Uncompetitive inhibition occurs when the inhibitor binds only to the ES complex. Both Vmax and Km decrease. Seen in inhibition of AChE by some pesticides.'),
('66a12dd5-429d-50fc-9d21-43ab93455c52'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Lineweaver-Burk plot is used to:', '["Quantify enzyme concentration","Determine enzyme purity","Determine enzyme structure","Determine Km and Vmax","Separate isoenzymes"]'::jsonb, 'Determine Km and Vmax', 'Lineweaver-Burk (double reciprocal) plot of 1/v vs 1/[S] gives a line with y-intercept = 1/Vmax and x-intercept = -1/Km.'),
('65d9aa67-01aa-5079-9197-fcf174c30b74'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The catalytic constant kcat (turnover number) is:', '["Maximum number of substrate molecules converted per enzyme per second","The number of enzyme molecules","The substrate concentration at half Vmax","The rate constant for substrate binding","The equilibrium constant"]'::jsonb, 'Maximum number of substrate molecules converted per enzyme per second', 'kcat = Vmax/[E]total measuring the catalytic rate when enzyme is saturated with substrate. High kcat indicates fast catalysis (e.g. catalase kcat ~10^7 s-1).'),
('aa14dc4f-baec-52cc-8bd6-a66cb679e644'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Which type of enzyme regulation involves rapid response?', '["mRNA stability metabolic pathway process","Translation rate metabolic pathway process","Gene expression changes metabolic pathway process","Allosteric regulation and covalent modification","Protein synthesis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Allosteric regulation and covalent modification', 'Allosteric regulation and covalent modification (seconds to minutes) provide rapid enzymatic control. Transcriptional regulation (hours) is slower.'),
('7b23a5c8-bdc9-5f78-89ef-d2135ec78258'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The main second messenger for glucagon is:', '["cGMP","cAMP","IP3","DAG","Ca2+"]'::jsonb, 'cAMP', 'Glucagon via Gs protein activates adenylyl cyclase producing cAMP which activates PKA leading to phosphorylation of metabolic enzymes.'),
('d56029db-6eef-5942-b569-70bc1bbe5d8d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Epinephrine can signal through:', '["Only cAMP","Both cAMP and Ca2+ pathways","Only cGMP","Only tyrosine kinase","Only Ca2+"]'::jsonb, 'Both cAMP and Ca2+ pathways', 'Epinephrine activates beta-receptors (Gs-cAMP) and alpha1-receptors (Gq-PLC-IP3/Ca2+) mediating diverse effects in different tissues.'),
('f774c538-0877-5a2a-84ea-84fbc4abebe8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Which peptide hormone acts through a tyrosine kinase receptor?', '["Epinephrine","Glucagon","Cortisol","Insulin","Growth hormone"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin', 'Insulin binds to a heterotetrameric receptor with intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity. Autophosphorylation and IRS phosphorylation trigger downstream signaling cascades.'),
('573cfb15-6d5e-598e-99c5-3c2a5d8c7efa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Mineralocorticoid receptors are activated by:', '["Testosterone","Cortisol","Aldosterone","Estrogen","Progesterone"]'::jsonb, 'Aldosterone', 'Aldosterone binds to mineralocorticoid receptors in the kidney increasing Na+ reabsorption and K+ excretion. Cortisol also binds but is inactivated by 11-beta-HSD2.'),
('70b9a873-9c43-53bb-9d40-58c98a2e877b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The hormone responsible for milk ejection is:', '["LH","Progesterone","Prolactin","Estrogen","Oxytocin"]'::jsonb, 'Oxytocin', 'Oxytocin released from posterior pituitary causes contraction of myoepithelial cells in mammary glands (milk ejection) and uterine smooth muscle during labor.'),
('d36496d1-51f9-518c-bb02-7c815d4e898d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The anterior pituitary hormone that stimulates thyroid function is:', '["LH","ACTH","TSH","GH","FSH"]'::jsonb, 'TSH', 'TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone) stimulates thyroid follicular cells to produce and release T3 and T4. TSH secretion is regulated by TRH and negative feedback by thyroid hormones.'),
('5e720601-3b2b-5f46-97ce-3efc31aa4564'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'ACTH acts on the:', '["Adrenal cortex","Thyroid","Parathyroid","Pancreas","Adrenal medulla"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal cortex', 'ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone) from the anterior pituitary stimulates adrenal cortex to produce glucocorticoids (cortisol) and androgens.'),
('535a44ee-991c-576c-8dd4-52f6318e91ec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The second messenger produced by phospholipase C is:', '["IP3 and DAG","cAMP","cGMP","Ca2+","NO"]'::jsonb, 'IP3 and DAG', 'Phospholipase C cleaves PIP2 into IP3 (releases Ca2+ from ER) and DAG (activates PKC). This pathway mediates effects of hormones like vasopressin and angiotensin II.'),
('2ba95df3-1291-5617-a442-69691eab2441'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Hormones that act through nuclear receptors include:', '["Steroid and thyroid hormones","Insulin and glucagon","Epinephrine and norepinephrine","ADH and oxytocin","Growth hormone and prolactin"]'::jsonb, 'Steroid and thyroid hormones', 'Steroid and thyroid hormones are lipophilic crossing the membrane to bind nuclear receptors that act as transcription factors regulating gene expression.'),
('4ff38e26-bd8a-5b29-b018-418bf30723d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The clinical test using enzymes for diagnosis of acute pancreatitis is:', '["Serum CK-MB and troponin","Serum ALT and AST","Serum ALP and GGT","Serum LDH","Serum amylase and lipase"]'::jsonb, 'Serum amylase and lipase', 'Serum amylase rises within 6-12 hours of acute pancreatitis onset. Lipase is more specific remaining elevated longer. Both are used for diagnosis.'),
('6f16cdbe-fb41-5fe6-8cc9-0b4fd357d19d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Serum ALT (alanine aminotransferase) is a marker of:', '["Pancreatitis","Myocardial infarction","Obstructive jaundice","Bone disease","Hepatocellular injury"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocellular injury', 'ALT is primarily found in liver cytosol. Elevated serum ALT indicates hepatocyte damage. AST is also found in heart and muscle and is less liver-specific.'),
('92454e1b-2bb9-59a7-9153-809908a66ac5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is elevated in:', '["Hepatitis","Obstructive jaundice","Pancreatitis","Myocardial infarction","Renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Obstructive jaundice', 'ALP is elevated in cholestasis (obstructive jaundice) and bone disorders (Paget''s disease osteomalacia) due to increased synthesis and release.'),
('f25b5013-9395-5400-bf0f-4937be0106f0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT) is a marker of:', '["Alcohol-induced liver disease","Bone disease","Pancreatitis","Cardiac disease","Renal disease"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol-induced liver disease', 'GGT is elevated in chronic alcohol consumption and hepatobiliary disease. It is useful for monitoring alcohol abstinence and detecting occult alcoholism.'),
('a2c381dd-adbd-5cb4-a559-65309d8e7f4c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Serum creatine kinase (CK) is elevated in:', '["Liver disease metabolic pathway process","Pancreatitis metabolic pathway process","Muscle injury and myocardial infarction","Kidney disease metabolic pathway process","Anemia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle injury and myocardial infarction', 'CK-MM is elevated in skeletal muscle damage. CK-MB is elevated in myocardial infarction. CK-BB is elevated in brain injury and certain cancers.'),
('c4336dca-9ec8-58a5-8715-2d967292b4a6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Serum troponin I and T are specific markers of:', '["Myocardial injury","Skeletal muscle injury","Liver injury","Pancreatic injury","Renal injury"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial injury', 'Troponin I and T are cardiac regulatory proteins released during myocardial infarction. They are highly specific and sensitive for cardiac damage.'),
('744af949-b795-5789-82e6-49d8bfefde06'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) isoenzyme pattern in myocardial infarction shows:', '["LDH2 > LDH1","LDH1 = LDH2","LDH3 > LDH4","LDH1 > LDH2","LDH5 > LDH4"]'::jsonb, 'LDH1 > LDH2', 'LDH1 (H4) is normally lower than LDH2. In MI LDH1 exceeds LDH2 (flipped ratio) due to cardiac release of LDH1-rich isoenzyme.'),
('fe444b85-f8d8-5e47-a2e1-34aedf7b1315'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Therapeutic monitoring of theophylline using enzyme assay is important because:', '["It is toxic at any level metabolic pathway process","Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic window","It interacts with food metabolic pathway process","It is slowly excreted metabolic pathway process","It is poorly absorbed metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic window', 'Theophylline (for asthma) has a narrow therapeutic index (10-20 mg/mL). Enzyme immunoassays measure serum levels to ensure efficacy and avoid toxicity.'),
('4c4b2907-992c-588c-8a94-761fbcb8b619'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Cyclosporine monitoring is important in:', '["Diabetes management metabolic pathway process","Transplant patients to prevent rejection and toxicity","Asthma metabolic pathway process","Hypertension metabolic pathway process","Cancer treatment metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Transplant patients to prevent rejection and toxicity', 'Cyclosporine immunosuppression requires therapeutic drug monitoring to balance rejection prevention with nephrotoxicity. Enzyme immunoassays measure blood levels.'),
('75c7247b-f3d7-52d4-91c1-9f96750affe1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Serum iron and ferritin are used to diagnose:', '["Vitamin deficiency","Calcium disorders","Copper deficiency","Zinc deficiency","Iron deficiency and overload"]'::jsonb, 'Iron deficiency and overload', 'Serum ferritin reflects iron stores (low in deficiency high in overload). Serum iron TIBC and transferrin saturation complete the iron profile for anemia diagnosis.'),
('5e358e72-2732-5899-b462-153909a5f8de'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme defect in acute intermittent porphyria is:', '["Porphobilinogen deaminase","Ferrochelatase","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","ALA synthase","ALA dehydratase"]'::jsonb, 'Porphobilinogen deaminase', 'Acute intermittent porphyria is caused by porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency causing accumulation of ALA and PBG. It presents with acute neurovisceral attacks.'),
('3417b6fa-ca13-56a6-95d6-58b8773635e4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Therapy with statins targets:', '["Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","LCAT","Lipoprotein lipase","HMG-CoA reductase","CETP"]'::jsonb, 'HMG-CoA reductase', 'Statins competitively inhibit HMG-CoA reductase reducing cholesterol synthesis leading to increased LDL receptor expression and lower plasma LDL cholesterol.'),
('4b38d77d-6183-5bb4-9f0f-262f07f4c382'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Metformin acts by:', '["Stimulating glucagon","Increasing insulin degradation","Stimulating insulin secretion","Blocking glucose absorption","Activating AMPK reducing hepatic glucose production"]'::jsonb, 'Activating AMPK reducing hepatic glucose production', 'Metformin activates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) reducing hepatic gluconeogenesis and increasing peripheral insulin sensitivity. It is first-line therapy for type 2 diabetes.'),
('9520a303-5f65-57c5-b43e-a1f8432ac527'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Sulfonylureas treat diabetes by:', '["Stimulating insulin secretion from beta cells","Inhibiting gluconeogenesis","Increasing insulin sensitivity","Decreasing glucose absorption","Increasing glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Stimulating insulin secretion from beta cells', 'Sulfonylureas (e.g. glibenclamide) bind to K-ATP channels on pancreatic beta cells causing depolarization and insulin secretion. They require functional beta cells.'),
('ec9f48ac-7f0f-51cc-8cdb-349d624941c8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Aspirin irreversibly inhibits:', '["Phospholipase A2","Xanthine oxidase","Lipoxygenase","Cyclooxygenase","Cytochrome P450"]'::jsonb, 'Cyclooxygenase', 'Aspirin acetylates COX-1 (and COX-2) blocking prostaglandin and thromboxane synthesis. Low-dose aspirin inhibits platelet COX-1 providing cardioprotection.'),
('54466c5b-7c82-526d-811b-9a02cb119c42'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Allopurinol inhibits:', '["Xanthine oxidase","Xanthine dehydrogenase","Adenosine deaminase","HGPRT","Purine nucleoside phosphorylase"]'::jsonb, 'Xanthine oxidase', 'Allopurinol (and its metabolite oxypurinol) inhibit xanthine oxidase reducing uric acid production. It treats gout and prevents tumor lysis syndrome hyperuricemia.'),
('82247e9f-83ac-5600-a68a-5a5b66abbca5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Warfarin acts by:', '["Inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase","Blocking factor X","Promoting fibrinolysis","Inhibiting thrombin","Blocking platelet aggregation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase', 'Warfarin inhibits vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1) reducing regeneration of active vitamin K. This impairs gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors II VII IX X.'),
('05c16d4a-869f-5439-8de6-de993d27d963'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Methotrexate inhibits:', '["DNA polymerase","Ribonucleotide reductase","Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase","Dihydrofolate reductase","Thymidylate synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Dihydrofolate reductase', 'Methotrexate inhibits DHFR reducing tetrahydrofolate levels blocking thymidylate and purine synthesis. Used in cancer rheumatoid arthritis and psoriasis.'),
('e8492069-d648-53af-b34e-61d1267a0576'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', '5-Fluorouracil inhibits:', '["Ribonucleotide reductase","DHFR","RNA polymerase","DNA polymerase","Thymidylate synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Thymidylate synthase', '5-FU is converted to 5-fluoro-dUMP which irreversibly inhibits thymidylate synthase blocking dTMP synthesis. Used in colorectal breast and head/neck cancers.'),
('4c8900e1-1377-5bab-b357-5ecfdb799d3c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Cyclophosphamide is activated by:', '["Reduction in cells","Oxidation in mitochondria","Direct binding to DNA","Cytochrome P450 in the liver","Hydrolysis in plasma"]'::jsonb, 'Cytochrome P450 in the liver', 'Cyclophosphamide is a prodrug activated by hepatic CYP450 to phosphoramide mustard which cross-links DNA. It is used in lymphoma and autoimmune diseases.'),
('9c66215b-8288-5652-90f2-dc6e7f3a0368'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug of choice for gout prophylaxis is:', '["Allopurinol","Probenecid","Indomethacin","Prednisone","Colchicine"]'::jsonb, 'Allopurinol', 'Allopurinol prevents gout attacks by reducing uric acid production via xanthine oxidase inhibition. Colchicine treats acute attacks. Probenecid increases uric acid excretion.'),
('2cebd8b5-3fa7-5602-94cd-6221632bd890'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Enzyme replacement therapy is available for:', '["Gaucher disease","PKU","MSUD","Phenylketonuria","Galactosemia"]'::jsonb, 'Gaucher disease', 'Imiglucerase (recombinant glucocerebrosidase) treats Gaucher disease. Other ERTs are available for Fabry Pompe MPS I and MPS VI.'),
('20b716ea-8e8b-5d78-aaf3-3d36ab24caa9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The therapeutic effect of ACE inhibitors is due to:', '["Blocking angiotensin II formation","Blocking aldosterone","Blocking angiotensin receptors","Inhibiting renin","Increasing bradykinin degradation"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking angiotensin II formation', 'ACE inhibitors prevent conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II reducing vasoconstriction and aldosterone release. They also decrease bradykinin degradation.'),
('01f0cbe0-cc33-5553-b7bd-84963d26e7a3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Beta-blockers act by:', '["Competitively blocking beta-adrenergic receptors","Increasing cAMP","Blocking calcium channels","Blocking acetylcholine receptors","Blocking alpha-receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Competitively blocking beta-adrenergic receptors', 'Beta-blockers (e.g. propranolol atenolol) compete with catecholamines for beta-adrenergic receptors reducing heart rate contractility and renin release.'),
('7adf24a9-3830-53a7-9d70-6fb4e20c0486'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme defect in hemophilia A is:', '["Factor X deficiency","von Willebrand factor deficiency","Factor VII deficiency","Factor IX deficiency","Factor VIII deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII deficiency', 'Hemophilia A is X-linked factor VIII deficiency causing prolonged PTT. Hemophilia B is factor IX deficiency. Both present with hemarthrosis and soft tissue bleeding.'),
('cf6b8d1f-f4ee-5d22-bb21-78242cb5e165'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Heparin acts as an anticoagulant by:', '["Blocking thrombin directly","Inhibiting platelet aggregation","Blocking factor Xa directly","Inhibiting vitamin K","Activating antithrombin III"]'::jsonb, 'Activating antithrombin III', 'Heparin binds to antithrombin III causing conformational change that accelerates its inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa. Low molecular weight heparin primarily inhibits Xa.'),
('1a15bf4c-7e6e-5676-a598-8ac1f8d542f8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The isoelectric focusing technique separates proteins based on:', '["Shape","Molecular weight","Charge at a single pH","Isoelectric point","Solubility"]'::jsonb, 'Isoelectric point', 'Isoelectric focusing separates proteins in a pH gradient until each protein reaches its pI (net charge = 0). It provides high resolution in proteomics.'),
('0b52e7e1-176c-5eeb-80a0-29bc2e6a8388'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme defect in G6PD deficiency leads to:', '["Hemolytic anemia upon oxidative stress","Hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Jaundice metabolic pathway process","Lactic acidosis metabolic pathway process","Hyperammonemia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemia upon oxidative stress', 'G6PD deficiency reduces NADPH production impairing glutathione regeneration. RBCs cannot neutralize oxidative damage causing hemolysis triggered by oxidant drugs fava beans or infection.'),
('6cc0095a-ebf3-5c9a-bcf6-81c0be9a8753'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Carnitine deficiency affects:', '["Fatty acid oxidation","Glycolysis","Gluconeogenesis","Protein synthesis","DNA replication"]'::jsonb, 'Fatty acid oxidation', 'Carnitine is essential for transporting long-chain fatty acids into mitochondria for beta-oxidation. Deficiency causes hypoketotic hypoglycemia myopathy and cardiomyopathy.'),
('b9177811-6196-5acc-a832-1a9005cac189'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Thiamine deficiency causes accumulation of:', '["Uric acid","Ketone bodies","Pyruvate and lactate","Ammonia","Bilirubin"]'::jsonb, 'Pyruvate and lactate', 'Thiamine (TPP) is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. Deficiency impairs TCA cycle causing pyruvate and lactate accumulation (lactic acidosis).'),
('b961cdbe-bc21-5d06-8495-5201556ca25f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Pyridoxine deficiency leads to:', '["Impaired phosphorylation","Impaired oxidation","Impaired reduction","Impaired carboxylation","Impaired transamination and decarboxylation"]'::jsonb, 'Impaired transamination and decarboxylation', 'PLP (B6) is required for transaminases decarboxylases (GABA synthesis) and heme synthesis (ALA synthase). Deficiency causes convulsions dermatitis and anemia.'),
('c573ffca-546f-5234-a449-1f4de9d17c9f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency causes:', '["PKU biochemical reaction","Alkaptonuria","Tyrosinemia","Maple syrup urine disease","Homocystinuria"]'::jsonb, 'PKU biochemical reaction', 'PKU results from PAH deficiency causing phenylalanine accumulation and cognitive impairment if untreated. Newborn screening allows early dietary intervention.'),
('773f0cc7-5732-5213-bb0e-11dfd2c5350b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme amylase is produced by:', '["Intestine","Pancreas and salivary glands","Liver","Stomach","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreas and salivary glands', 'Alpha-amylase is secreted by pancreatic acinar cells and salivary glands. Salivary amylase (ptyalin) begins starch digestion. Pancreatic amylase continues in the duodenum.'),
('42aceabe-b01d-5d44-b26a-3e73db1c0424'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Lipoprotein lipase is activated by:', '["Apolipoprotein A-I","Apolipoprotein B-100","Apolipoprotein C-III","Apolipoprotein E","Apolipoprotein C-II"]'::jsonb, 'Apolipoprotein C-II', 'LPL requires apo C-II as a cofactor. It hydrolyzes triglycerides in chylomicrons and VLDL. Familial LPL deficiency causes type I hyperlipoproteinemia (chylomicronemia).'),
('cde46a81-b570-5134-94f3-b460741bd5df'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Hepatic lipase functions to:', '["Synthesize cholesterol","Remodel HDL and IDL","Activate lipoproteins","Transport fatty acids","Hydrolyze dietary fat"]'::jsonb, 'Remodel HDL and IDL', 'Hepatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides and phospholipids in IDL and HDL converting IDL to LDL and remodeling HDL. It also mediates hepatic uptake of lipoproteins.'),
('a99ece71-eaf2-5313-8402-311e2ff23e33'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'LCAT (lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase) is responsible for:', '["Synthesis of bile acids","Hydrolysis of triglycerides","Transport of free fatty acids","Activation of LPL","Esterification of cholesterol on HDL"]'::jsonb, 'Esterification of cholesterol on HDL', 'LCAT converts free cholesterol to cholesteryl esters on HDL. This maintains the gradient for cholesterol efflux from cells. LCAT deficiency causes HDL deficiency and corneal opacities.'),
('108f2587-1046-5ae3-9d04-0237abd18556'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'CETP (cholesteryl ester transfer protein) transfers:', '["Triglycerides from VLDL to HDL","Cholesteryl esters from HDL to VLDL/LDL","Free fatty acids to albumin","Bile acids to intestine","Phospholipids between lipoproteins"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesteryl esters from HDL to VLDL/LDL', 'CETP mediates exchange of cholesteryl esters (from HDL) for triglycerides (from VLDL/LDL). This links HDL and VLDL metabolism and influences atherosclerosis risk.'),
('2c03bf4d-ed67-5940-a652-617160a43325'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The first step of beta-oxidation is catalyzed by:', '["Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I","Beta-keto thiolase","Acyl-CoA dehydrogenase","Enoyl-CoA hydratase","Beta-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Acyl-CoA dehydrogenase', 'Acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (AD) catalyzes the first oxidation step forming a trans-double bond. It uses FAD as cofactor producing FADH2. Four chain-length-specific ADs exist.'),
('785d53de-ebdf-5cd0-b1e8-4fe93add3e6d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The committed step of heme biosynthesis is:', '["Ferrochelatase","ALA dehydratase","Uroporphyrinogen III synthase","Porphobilinogen synthase","ALA synthase"]'::jsonb, 'ALA synthase', 'ALA synthase catalyzes glycine + succinyl-CoA → ALA using PLP. It is the rate-limiting step of heme synthesis regulated by heme (feedback inhibition) and drug inducers.'),
('3878364b-6da0-5138-995e-fac224726297'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of the serotonin pathway is:', '["Tryptophan decarboxylase","Tyrosine hydroxylase","Tryptophan hydroxylase","Monoamine oxidase","Catechol-O-methyltransferase"]'::jsonb, 'Tryptophan hydroxylase', 'Tryptophan hydroxylase catalyzes tryptophan → 5-HTP using tetrahydrobiopterin. It is the rate-limiting step in serotonin synthesis and is inhibited by parachlorophenylalanine.'),
('d2ba36e2-3f8b-51e1-b2ad-492d00dd1149'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of catecholamine synthesis is:', '["Tyrosine hydroxylase","DOPA decarboxylase","Dopamine beta-hydroxylase","PNMT","MAO"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine hydroxylase', 'Tyrosine hydroxylase (TH) catalyzes tyrosine → DOPA using BH4. It is feedback-inhibited by dopamine and norepinephrine and is rate-limiting for catecholamine synthesis.'),
('49dd7b71-fe58-543c-93e0-1a90e0b5b793'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Which metal ion is a cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase?', '["Manganese","Copper","Zinc","Iron","Magnesium"]'::jsonb, 'Iron', 'Tyrosine hydroxylase (and tryptophan hydroxylase) contain non-heme iron (Fe2+) essential for catalysis. They also require tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) as cofactor and O2.'),
('873aee61-56fb-5143-8da2-0c6f112b7b29'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS I) in the urea cycle is activated by:', '["N-acetylglutamate","Citrulline","Carbamoyl phosphate","Ornithine","Arginine"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylglutamate', 'N-acetylglutamate (NAG) is an essential allosteric activator of CPS I. NAG is synthesized from glutamate and acetyl-CoA by NAG synthase. Arginine activates NAG synthase.'),
('d8d4e3a4-00f0-5f20-a72d-39e00f880526'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Pyruvate carboxylase is activated by:', '["Acetyl-CoA","ATP","NADH","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate","AMP"]'::jsonb, 'Acetyl-CoA', 'Pyruvate carboxylase requires acetyl-CoA as an allosteric activator. When acetyl-CoA accumulates (high energy state) it signals need for gluconeogenesis.'),
('b8331ed3-f5c0-5fa2-a040-71e6f4535282'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Phosphofructokinase-2 (PFK-2) produces:', '["Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate","Fructose-6-phosphate","Fructose-1-phosphate","Glucose-6-phosphate"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate', 'PFK-2 kinase activity produces fructose-2,6-bisphosphate the most potent activator of PFK-1. Glucagon inactivates PFK-2 (via PKA) reducing F2,6BP and slowing glycolysis.'),
('101bd1d1-c19e-5d65-96e9-887f47ea7862'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The bifunctional enzyme PFK-2/FBPase-2 responds to:', '["Epinephrine","Thyroid hormone","Growth hormone","Glucagon and insulin","Ca2+"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon and insulin', 'PFK-2/FBPase-2 is regulated by phosphorylation: glucagon (PKA) activates phosphatase activity reducing F2,6BP. Insulin activates phosphatase (dephosphorylation of PFK-2/FBPase-2).'),
('78a7ee1c-1d69-5466-9833-22cb9822d6e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme pyruvate kinase is activated by:', '["Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate","ATP","Alanine","NADH","Acetyl-CoA"]'::jsonb, 'Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate', 'Pyruvate kinase (PK) is activated by F1,6BP (feedforward activation) and inhibited by ATP and alanine (feedback inhibition). Liver PK is also regulated by phosphorylation.'),
('7f76e336-2edf-5d27-a2d0-e7b1ba0e4394'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The L-type pyruvate kinase (liver) is inhibited by:', '["Glucagon via PKA phosphorylation","Insulin","Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate","High AMP","High ADP"]'::jsonb, 'Glucagon via PKA phosphorylation', 'Glucagon via PKA phosphorylates and inactivates L-type pyruvate kinase inhibiting glycolysis in the liver. This spares glucose for use by other tissues.'),
('64abc74c-7b34-53bf-a91b-47d8b68df8f8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The inducible enzyme for ethanol metabolism (MEOS) is:', '["CYP2E1","Alcohol dehydrogenase","Catalase","Xanthine oxidase","Aldehyde dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2E1', 'CYP2E1 (microsomal ethanol-oxidizing system) is induced by chronic alcohol consumption contributing to ethanol tolerance and hepatotoxicity through ROS production.'),
('539ec931-9345-5ee5-9b88-2ec13867e4c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme in the urea cycle that is deficient in argininosuccinic aciduria is:', '["Arginase","Argininosuccinate lyase","Argininosuccinate synthetase","Ornithine transcarbamoylase","CPS I"]'::jsonb, 'Argininosuccinate lyase', 'Argininosuccinate lyase deficiency causes accumulation of argininosuccinate in urine. It presents with hyperammonemia and neurological symptoms.'),
('83dafbf2-18f3-5392-a5c0-0cf3248e700d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme deficient in citrullinemia is:', '["Argininosuccinate synthetase","CPS I","Argininosuccinate lyase","Ornithine transcarbamoylase","Arginase"]'::jsonb, 'Argininosuccinate synthetase', 'Citrullinemia type I is caused by argininosuccinate synthetase deficiency leading to elevated blood citrulline and hyperammonemia.'),
('b6f0f3e1-bbcc-5015-9a02-1ff8c22dafb4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. The secondary bile acids are formed by:', '["Liver enzymes metabolic pathway process","Pancreatic enzymes metabolic pathway process","Intestinal brush border enzymes metabolic pathway process","Gastric acid metabolic pathway process","Bacterial deconjugation and dehydroxylation in the colon"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial deconjugation and dehydroxylation in the colon', 'Secondary bile acids (deoxycholic and lithocholic) are produced by intestinal bacteria removing the 7-alpha-hydroxyl group from primary bile acids in the colon.'),
('b1bf419e-a202-5ef8-aa0b-afed5470cd27'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Bile acid sequestrants (e.g. cholestyramine) lower cholesterol by:', '["Binding bile acids in the intestine preventing reabsorption","Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase","Inhibiting cholesterol absorption","Increasing HDL","Activating LPL"]'::jsonb, 'Binding bile acids in the intestine preventing reabsorption', 'Bile acid sequestrants bind bile acids preventing enterohepatic circulation. The liver converts more cholesterol to bile acids reducing hepatic cholesterol and increasing LDL receptor expression.'),
('0e700aea-b75e-5e0b-a6bb-05c3e0c13010'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Ezetimibe lowers cholesterol by:', '["Increasing bile acid excretion","Inhibiting intestinal cholesterol absorption","Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase","Activating LPL","Inhibiting ACAT"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting intestinal cholesterol absorption', 'Ezetimibe inhibits the NPC1L1 transporter in intestinal brush border reducing cholesterol absorption. It is often used with statins for additive LDL lowering.'),
('4cf966bb-d150-5755-92ef-dbf3eeb88b04'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'gemfibrozil) primarily:', '["Increase HDL and decrease triglycerides","Decrease HDL","Decrease LDL","Increase cholesterol synthesis","Increase triglycerides"]'::jsonb, 'Increase HDL and decrease triglycerides', 'Fibrates activate PPAR-alpha increasing fatty acid oxidation and LPL activity reducing triglycerides. They also increase HDL synthesis by increasing apo A-I production.'),
('2da2575b-e279-5cc1-ac67-880b62ae6d15'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme that converts homocysteine to methionine requires:', '["Vitamin B12 and folate","Vitamin B6","Vitamin B2","Vitamin B3","Vitamin C"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 and folate', 'Methionine synthase requires methylcobalamin (B12) and 5-methyl-THF (folate) as methyl donor. B12 or folate deficiency causes hyperhomocysteinemia increasing CVD risk.'),
('7f114d0b-6ed1-5600-825b-ac07d3cf35d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency causes:', '["PKU","Maple syrup urine disease","Cystinuria","Homocystinuria","Alkaptonuria"]'::jsonb, 'Homocystinuria', 'Cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency (homocystinuria) causes elevated homocysteine and methionine with ectopia lentis thromboembolism and marfanoid features.'),
('7553f7a6-08fe-522e-94c2-73889ee54ba9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The only purely ketogenic amino acids are:', '["Phenylalanine and tyrosine","Lysine and tryptophan","Valine and leucine","Leucine and isoleucine","Leucine and lysine"]'::jsonb, 'Leucine and lysine', 'Leucine and lysine break down to acetoacetate (ketogenic) only. Isoleucine phenylalanine tyrosine tryptophan and threonine are both glucogenic and ketogenic.'),
('20f356b7-1ecb-51eb-8e00-9d6cd36ddbf5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The glucogenic amino acids can be converted to:', '["Nucleic acids","Fatty acids","Glucose via gluconeogenesis","Cholesterol","Ketone bodies"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose via gluconeogenesis', 'Glucogenic amino acids produce TCA cycle intermediates (alpha-ketoglutarate succinyl-CoA fumarate oxaloacetate) or pyruvate that can form glucose via gluconeogenesis.'),
('5951de44-95af-5fac-8516-e773bf1853b8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The urea cycle produces:', '["Ammonia from urea","Uric acid from ammonia","Urea from ammonia and CO2","Creatinine from creatine","Urea from uric acid"]'::jsonb, 'Urea from ammonia and CO2', 'The urea cycle (liver) converts two amino groups (NH4+ and aspartate) and CO2 into urea for excretion. It uses 3 ATP per urea molecule produced.'),
('1a74b73a-0c94-5408-8052-31417e6f52c6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The substrate for gluconeogenesis that yields glucose only in certain conditions is:', '["Glycerol","Lactate","Alanine","Propionate","Acetyl-CoA"]'::jsonb, 'Acetyl-CoA', 'Acetyl-CoA cannot form glucose in humans due to irreversible pyruvate dehydrogenase. The glyoxylate cycle in plants allows this but humans lack the required isocitrate lyase/malate synthase.'),
('774a0414-6dad-5791-9581-2fd39acfa501'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, The Cori cycle involves:', '["Lactate from muscle to liver for gluconeogenesis","Ketone bodies from liver to brain","Glucose from liver to muscle for glycolysis","Alanine from muscle to liver for gluconeogenesis","Glycerol from adipose to liver for gluconeogenesis"]'::jsonb, 'Lactate from muscle to liver for gluconeogenesis', 'In the Cori cycle lactate produced during anaerobic glycolysis in muscle/RBCs is transported to the liver and converted to glucose via gluconeogenesis.'),
('a4aa72aa-8b5a-5a9f-aedf-3ee7a0bffa57'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The glucose-alanine cycle involves:', '["Alanine from muscle carries nitrogen to liver for urea synthesis","Alanine from liver to muscle metabolic pathway process","Glucose from muscle to liver metabolic pathway process","Lactate from liver to muscle metabolic pathway process","Ammonia from liver to kidney metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Alanine from muscle carries nitrogen to liver for urea synthesis', 'The Cahill cycle transports amino groups from muscle to liver as alanine. Alanine is deaminated and carbon skeleton used for gluconeogenesis.'),
('1b41e444-3da3-5c65-82e7-5a7e16753ccd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The brain''s main fuel during prolonged fasting is:', '["Amino acids","Glucose","Ketone bodies","Fatty acids","Lactate"]'::jsonb, 'Ketone bodies', 'During prolonged fasting the brain adapts to use ketone bodies (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate) for up to 60-70% of its energy needs sparing glucose.'),
('78ce5a51-5378-5a38-8f44-bdf82bc54a3c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The Pasteur effect describes:', '["Inhibition of glycolysis by oxygen","Activation of electron transport chain","Conversion of pyruvate to lactate","Inhibition of TCA cycle","Stimulation of glycolysis by oxygen"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of glycolysis by oxygen', 'The Pasteur effect is slower glycolysis in presence of oxygen due to efficient ATP production via oxidative phosphorylation reducing demand for glycolytic ATP.'),
('c67e2398-8dc6-5f7c-bdfe-c5b4932a046b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The Crabtree effect describes:', '["Activation of gluconeogenesis","Stimulation of glycolysis in cancer cells","Inhibition of fatty acid oxidation","Inhibition of glycolysis by oxygen","Inhibition of respiration by high glucose"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibition of respiration by high glucose', 'The Crabtree effect (reverse Pasteur) is suppression of mitochondrial respiration by high glucose levels commonly seen in cancer cells and rapidly proliferating cells.'),
('c4ce8ef4-e0e9-5f86-a184-e2bb048900c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Hormonal regulation of the TCA cycle occurs primarily through:', '["Gene expression metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Allosteric regulation of isocitrate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase","Direct hormone binding metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Covalent modification metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Proteolytic activation metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Allosteric regulation of isocitrate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase', 'The TCA cycle is regulated primarily by allosteric effectors (ATP NADH ADP Ca2+) at isocitrate dehydrogenase alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and citrate synthase.'),
('04242a96-5bc2-5452-8803-3c666177b509'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The activity of pyruvate dehydrogenase is inhibited by:', '["High NAD+","Calcium","Phosphorylation by PDK","High CoA","High ADP"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylation by PDK', 'PDH kinase (PDK) phosphorylates PDH inactivating it when ATP NADH and acetyl-CoA are high. PDH phosphatase (PDP) dephosphorylates activating PDH when energy is needed.'),
('ec9da678-8b61-547e-9357-72ff233294c3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The three PDK isoforms (PDK1-4) in different tissues:', '["Inhibit all dehydrogenases","Allow tissue-specific regulation of PDH","Have no function","Are identical","Are not regulated"]'::jsonb, 'Allow tissue-specific regulation of PDH', 'PDK isoenzymes are expressed tissue-specifically. PDK4 is upregulated in heart and muscle during fasting/starvation. This inactivates PDH conserving pyruvate for gluconeogenesis.'),
('f4a363c9-1a49-5f83-961d-5a610f34ac67'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme glycogen phosphorylase exists in which forms?', '["Phosphorylase a (active) and phosphorylase b","Bound and free","Monomer and dimer","Active and inactive at same phosphorylation state","Form A and form B"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylase a (active) and phosphorylase b', 'Phosphorylase b is inactive (no phosphate). Phosphorylase kinase (activated by PKA/Ca2+) phosphorylates it to active phosphorylase a. Phosphatase removes the phosphate.'),
('9323b935-de6b-57e4-b77b-6cefac10e719'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The conversion of glycogen phosphorylase b to a involves:', '["Allosteric binding of glucose","Binding of AMP","Phosphorylation by phosphorylase kinase","Dephosphorylation","Proteolytic cleavage"]'::jsonb, 'Phosphorylation by phosphorylase kinase', 'Phosphorylase kinase (PHK) phosphorylates Ser14 of phosphorylase b converting it to active phosphorylase a. PHK is activated by PKA phosphorylation and by Ca2+-calmodulin.'),
('ee9967cc-b5f2-595f-b73b-0d406b148aa6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Phosphorylase kinase is activated by:', '["Both PKA and Ca2+","Only PKA","Only Ca2+","Only insulin","Only cAMP"]'::jsonb, 'Both PKA and Ca2+', 'Phosphorylase kinase is activated by PKA phosphorylation (cAMP pathway) and by Ca2+ binding to its calmodulin subunit. This allows both hormonal and neural control of glycogenolysis.'),
('c806f861-8f92-59f3-8f7f-2235c460be6b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The branching enzyme in glycogen synthesis creates:', '["Alpha-1,4 glycosidic bonds","Alpha-1,6 glycosidic bonds","Beta-1,4 glycosidic bonds","Alpha-1,2 glycosidic bonds","Beta-1,6 glycosidic bonds"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-1,6 glycosidic bonds', 'Branching enzyme (glucosyltransferase) transfers a segment of 6-8 glucose residues to form an alpha-1,6 branch. This increases glycogen solubility and creates more non-reducing ends.'),
('494043a4-694a-5919-9170-428b4579c64d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The debranching enzyme in glycogenolysis has:', '["Only phosphorylase activity metabolic pathway process","None of the above metabolic pathway process","Only transferase activity metabolic pathway process","Only glucosidase activity metabolic pathway process","Both transferase and alpha-1,6 glucosidase activities"]'::jsonb, 'Both transferase and alpha-1,6 glucosidase activities', 'Debranching enzyme has two catalytic activities: transferase moves 3 residues to a neighbor chain and alpha-1,6-glucosidase removes the remaining branch-point glucose.'),
('79dfa534-c169-52ac-94cb-e99431ccb443'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The glucose-6-phosphatase enzyme is found in:', '["Liver and kidney","Muscle and brain","Heart","Lungs","Adipose tissue"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and kidney', 'Glucose-6-phosphatase is present only in liver and kidney (and intestine). It enables glucose release into blood. Deficiency causes von Gierke disease (glycogen storage disease type I).'),
('74d8769b-0959-5264-a715-3e20b013e1e6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'GLUT2 is the glucose transporter in:', '["Brain","Intestine","Kidney","Liver and pancreatic beta cells","Muscle and adipose"]'::jsonb, 'Liver and pancreatic beta cells', 'GLUT2 is a low-affinity high-capacity transporter in liver and pancreatic beta cells. In the liver it allows glucose flux both ways. In beta cells it mediates glucose sensing.'),
('101e74c0-1572-5283-b0f0-1febdd1764d1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'GLUT4 translocation to the plasma membrane is stimulated by:', '["Epinephrine","Cortisol","Insulin","Growth hormone","Glucagon"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin', 'Insulin signaling via PI3K/Akt causes GLUT4-containing vesicles to translocate to the plasma membrane in muscle and adipose cells increasing glucose uptake.'),
('d3dacbb1-2b08-5956-9eb1-41a417368627'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The rate-limiting enzyme of the pentose phosphate pathway G6PD is regulated by:', '["ATP levels","Insulin","NADH levels","NADPH levels","Glucose levels"]'::jsonb, 'NADPH levels', 'G6PD is allosterically inhibited by NADPH. When NADPH is abundant the PPP oxidative phase slows. The non-oxidative phase operates based on demand for ribose-5-P or NADPH.'),
('4c844ee5-ad88-5b9a-9058-7ec728e27cbd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The transketolase enzyme in the PPP requires:', '["FAD","PLP","Biotin","TPP","NAD+"]'::jsonb, 'TPP', 'Transketolase requires TPP as cofactor for transferring two-carbon units between pentoses. Thiamine deficiency reduces transketolase activity causing Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome in alcoholics.'),
('d4b54778-73ec-56db-a4f9-5501a898b088'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The hexose monophosphate shunt (PPP) is active in tissues with high demand for:', '["NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate","cAMP and cGMP","ATP and NADH","CoA and biotin","GTP and FADH2"]'::jsonb, 'NADPH and ribose-5-phosphate', 'The PPP is active in tissues performing reductive biosynthesis (liver adipose mammary) and in RBCs (NADPH for glutathione reduction). Also active in rapidly dividing cells needing ribose-5-P.'),
('0387f4c0-74dd-5b16-be0f-86f99a29872f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Sorbitol accumulation in diabetes is due to:', '["High glucose levels and aldose reductase activity","Deficiency of sorbitol dehydrogenase","Increased insulin","Decreased glucagon","Increased glycogenolysis"]'::jsonb, 'High glucose levels and aldose reductase activity', 'Hyperglycemia increases sorbitol production via aldose reductase. Sorbitol accumulates in tissues lacking sorbitol dehydrogenase (lens nerve retina) contributing to diabetic complications.'),
('76f4fdc0-3bf8-566d-bdb6-33f404f2f1b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug epalrestat treats diabetic neuropathy by:', '["Activating PKC","Blocking polyol pathway","Inhibiting sorbitol dehydrogenase","Inhibiting aldose reductase","Inhibiting AGE formation"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting aldose reductase', 'Aldose reductase inhibitors (ARI) like epalrestat reduce sorbitol accumulation. They are used for diabetic neuropathy in some countries.'),
('9f04ac99-a5a1-59dd-826a-14cfb1bb7082'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The regulation of lipoprotein lipase by insulin involves:', '["No effect","Degrading LPL","Decreasing LPL in muscle","Inhibiting LPL activity","Increasing LPL synthesis in adipose"]'::jsonb, 'Increasing LPL synthesis in adipose', 'Insulin upregulates LPL in adipose tissue promoting fat storage. In muscle insulin has less effect on LPL. Glucagon and catecholamines decrease adipose LPL.'),
('ec49a126-2450-5f3b-ba8f-07311eb2f35e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The ratio of cholesterol synthesis that occurs in the liver is approximately:', '["~50%","~10%","~90%","~25%","~75%"]'::jsonb, '~50%', 'About 50% of total body cholesterol synthesis occurs in the liver. The remainder occurs in intestine adrenal glands and other tissues. The liver also eliminates cholesterol via bile.'),
('51161360-0f8b-5e17-93be-acceb4ad3da5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The primary organ for ketone body utilization is:', '["Adipose tissue","Liver","Brain and muscle","Intestine","Kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Brain and muscle', 'Ketone bodies produced in the liver are utilized by extrahepatic tissues especially brain (during fasting) and muscle. The liver lacks the enzyme beta-ketoacyl-CoA transferase.'),
('df180be2-769a-5785-ad84-b652a28f9cc2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme beta-ketoacyl-CoA transferase (thiophorase) is absent in:', '["Brain","Liver","Muscle","Kidney","Heart"]'::jsonb, 'Liver', 'The liver cannot use ketone bodies because it lacks beta-ketoacyl-CoA transferase (succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase). This ensures liver exports ketone bodies for peripheral use.'),
('9caa8fb5-df23-5c9b-99d9-6327f4ca2d31'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'HMG-CoA lyase deficiency causes:', '["Hypoketotic hypoglycemia and metabolic acidosis","Hyperketotic hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Hyperuricemia metabolic pathway process","Hypercholesterolemia metabolic pathway process","Hyperammonemia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoketotic hypoglycemia and metabolic acidosis', 'HMG-CoA lyase deficiency impairs ketogenesis and leucine catabolism causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia metabolic acidosis and vomiting during fasting or illness.'),
('a36053e7-e4e5-50f4-bb0d-a50248cfa93e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) deficiency presents with:', '["Hyperketotic hypoglycemia","Hypoketotic hypoglycemia and vomiting","Hyperammonemia","Jaundice","Lactic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoketotic hypoglycemia and vomiting', 'MCAD deficiency impairs fatty acid oxidation causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia during fasting with vomiting lethargy and hepatomegaly. Common in Caucasian children.'),
('de299089-6552-5681-a5c4-a2fe45242196'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Propionic acidemia results from deficiency of:', '["Pyruvate carboxylase","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase","Acyl-CoA dehydrogenase","HMG-CoA lyase","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase"]'::jsonb, 'Propionyl-CoA carboxylase', 'Propionyl-CoA carboxylase (biotin-dependent) deficiency causes propionic acidemia with metabolic acidosis hyperammonemia and developmental delay.'),
('1a6871b1-2191-5e2f-b9f5-c83b4bc0f4c6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Methylmalonic acidemia can result from:', '["Deficiency of methylmalonyl-CoA mutase or defects in B12 metabolism","Deficiency of propionyl-CoA carboxylase","Deficiency of acyl-CoA dehydrogenase","Deficiency of pyruvate carboxylase","Deficiency of HMG-CoA lyase"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency of methylmalonyl-CoA mutase or defects in B12 metabolism', 'Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (MMUT) requires adenosylcobalamin (B12). Defects in MMUT or B12 metabolism cause methylmalonic acidemia presenting with acidosis and neurological symptoms.'),
('a1478361-71ff-5822-a6c4-5efe23cef12a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Biochemical markers of thiamine deficiency include:', '["Elevated pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate","Elevated uric acid metabolic pathway process","Elevated lactate only metabolic pathway process","Elevated bilirubin metabolic pathway process","Elevated ammonia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate', 'TPP-dependent PDH and alpha-KGDH are impaired in thiamine deficiency causing accumulation of pyruvate and alpha-ketoglutarate with increased blood lactate.'),
('e6fcd85d-e66e-55ee-96e3-5ea2cbb5e76f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Biochemical markers of B12 deficiency include:', '["Elevated uric acid metabolic pathway process","Elevated folate metabolic pathway process","Elevated bilirubin metabolic pathway process","Elevated methylmalonic acid and homocysteine","Elevated creatinine metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated methylmalonic acid and homocysteine', 'B12 is a cofactor for methylmalonyl-CoA mutase (accumulation of methylmalonic acid) and methionine synthase (accumulation of homocysteine). Serum B12 MMA and homocysteine are diagnostic.'),
('922a9ccf-ebc7-506e-9d53-0b425ea25fb7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Folate deficiency is diagnosed by:', '["Elevated homocysteine with normal MMA","Normal both","Elevated both","Elevated uric acid","Elevated MMA with normal homocysteine"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated homocysteine with normal MMA', 'Folate deficiency impairs methionine synthase causing hyperhomocysteinemia. MMA is normal because B12-dependent methylmalonyl-CoA mutase is unaffected (unlike B12 deficiency).'),
('cae0aa0a-8fbf-589d-a1a1-ff8ff4881484'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug trimethoprim inhibits:', '["Bacterial DHFR","Human DHFR","Thymidylate synthase","DNA gyrase","RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial DHFR', 'Trimethoprim selectively inhibits bacterial dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR) blocking bacterial folate synthesis. It is often combined with sulfamethoxazole for synergistic effect.'),
('8496216a-53ca-5dfc-92b2-421df50ae044'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Sulfonamides inhibit:', '["DNA gyrase metabolic pathway process","Beta-lactamase metabolic pathway process","DHFR metabolic pathway process","Dihydropteroate synthase in bacterial folate synthesis","Thymidylate synthase metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Dihydropteroate synthase in bacterial folate synthesis', 'Sulfonamides are structural analogs of PABA competing with PABA for dihydropteroate synthase inhibiting bacterial folate synthesis. Humans obtain folate from diet so are unaffected.'),
('681837bd-be8c-5825-8b77-41a504262982'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'tuberculosis is:', '["DNA gyrase metabolic pathway process","Beta-lactamase metabolic pathway process","DHFR metabolic pathway process","RNA polymerase metabolic pathway process","Enoyl-ACP reductase (InhA) of mycolic acid synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Enoyl-ACP reductase (InhA) of mycolic acid synthesis', 'Isoniazid (INH) inhibits InhA an enoyl-ACP reductase essential for mycolic acid synthesis in mycobacterial cell walls. It also depletes NADH. INH is activated by KatG catalase-peroxidase.'),
('1e24cb61-c826-51af-a032-92ae800f13ef'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The antibiotic rifampin inhibits:', '["Cell wall synthesis","Folate synthesis","Protein synthesis","DNA gyrase","Bacterial RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial RNA polymerase', 'Rifampin binds to bacterial RNA polymerase beta subunit blocking transcription. It is a first-line drug for tuberculosis causing red-orange discoloration of secretions.'),
('74ddc8bc-f326-57f1-aa5f-84affce9118c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The target of penicillin is:', '["Ribosome","Bacterial transpeptidase","DNA gyrase","Folates","RNA polymerase"]'::jsonb, 'Bacterial transpeptidase', 'Penicillin inhibits transpeptidases (PBPs) that cross-link peptidoglycan in bacterial cell walls. This weakens the cell wall causing osmotic lysis (bactericidal).'),
('b7aa8574-dbeb-5daf-abe9-577eb276db94'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Tetracycline inhibits:', '["Cell wall synthesis","RNA polymerase","DNA gyrase","Protein synthesis by binding the 50S subunit","Protein synthesis by binding the 30S ribosome subunit"]'::jsonb, 'Protein synthesis by binding the 30S ribosome subunit', 'Tetracycline binds 16S rRNA of the 30S ribosomal subunit blocking aminoacyl-tRNA binding to the A site inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis.'),
('371435ca-2f09-5032-ab60-d679ce95fd24'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Gentamicin is an aminoglycoside that:', '["Binds 50S ribosome","Blocks DNA replication","Inhibits cell wall synthesis","Blocks transcription","Binds 30S ribosome causing misreading of mRNA"]'::jsonb, 'Binds 30S ribosome causing misreading of mRNA', 'Aminoglycosides (gentamicin streptomycin) bind the 30S subunit causing codon misreading and incorporation of wrong amino acids producing defective proteins.'),
('b8d3e296-8244-53d7-9fec-b1860902ded8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The antibiotic erythromycin binds:', '["50S ribosomal subunit","30S ribosomal subunit","DNA gyrase","RNA polymerase","Cell membrane"]'::jsonb, '50S ribosomal subunit', 'Erythromycin and other macrolides bind the 50S ribosomal subunit blocking translocation of the peptidyl-tRNA from A to P site inhibiting protein synthesis.'),
('4e4d2fb1-51ee-5446-89f1-d1f4b8da3962'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The target of ciprofloxacin is:', '["RNA polymerase metabolic pathway process","Folate synthesis metabolic pathway process","Cell wall metabolic pathway process","DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV","Ribosome metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV', 'Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin) inhibit bacterial DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) and topoisomerase IV preventing DNA replication and transcription.'),
('93b541de-ced4-5d70-a759-570fff10a2af'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Cytochrome P450 inhibition is a concern with which antifungal?', '["Fluconazole","Amphotericin B","Nystatin","Terbinafine","Ketoconazole"]'::jsonb, 'Ketoconazole', 'Ketoconazole is a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor causing numerous drug interactions via competitive inhibition of CYP-dependent 14-alpha-demethylase in ergosterol synthesis.'),
('f861e2ae-5ffe-59a8-b3ab-5aa2a3b3e1d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors (statins) may cause myopathy because:', '["They promote apoptosis","They deplete ATP","They inhibit CoQ10 synthesis","They increase fatty acid oxidation","They inhibit glycolysis"]'::jsonb, 'They inhibit CoQ10 synthesis', 'Statins inhibit mevalonate pathway reducing CoQ10 (ubiquinone) synthesis potentially impairing mitochondrial electron transport and causing myopathy in susceptible individuals.'),
('0dc08f9a-3ea7-5964-86c5-909ca54b95e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Enzyme induction by phenobarbital involves:', '["Direct enzyme activation metabolic pathway process","Competitive inhibition metabolic pathway process","Increased synthesis of CYP450 enzymes via CAR/PXR","Proteolytic cleavage metabolic pathway process","Allosteric binding metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Increased synthesis of CYP450 enzymes via CAR/PXR', 'Phenobarbital activates CAR (constitutive androstane receptor) and PXR upregulating CYP2B CYP2C and CYP3A expression. This enhances drug metabolism and tolerance.'),
('4377530a-3267-5cc8-ad66-6ebc0054bfc2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The therapeutic index of a drug is:', '["Vmax/Km","ED50/TD50","TD50/ED50","IC50","Kcat/Km"]'::jsonb, 'TD50/ED50', 'Therapeutic index = TD50/ED50 (toxic dose/effective dose). A high TI indicates a wide safety margin. Drugs with narrow TI (theophylline warfarin digoxin) require monitoring.'),
('3c8f80a4-0371-55a2-905e-db176364a941'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Enzyme immunoassays (EIA) are used to detect:', '["Lipids metabolic pathway process","RNA sequences metabolic pathway process","Enzyme activity only metabolic pathway process","DNA sequences metabolic pathway process","Antigens or antibodies in patient samples"]'::jsonb, 'Antigens or antibodies in patient samples', 'EIA uses enzyme-labeled antibodies or antigens for sensitive detection. ELISA (enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay) is widely used for HIV hepatitis autoimmune markers and hormone measurement.'),
('25abe3e1-7ccb-5cf1-a145-fb6194b67bbb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme defect in acute intermittent porphyria leads to accumulation of:', '["ALA and PBG","Porphobilinogen and uroporphyrinogen","Coproporphyrinogen","Protoporphyrin","Heme"]'::jsonb, 'ALA and PBG', 'AIP (PBG deaminase deficiency) causes accumulation of ALA and PBG. These neurotoxic porphyrin precursors cause abdominal pain neuropsychiatric symptoms and autonomic dysfunction.'),
('d26d47b4-cf7e-5cea-beeb-aa19402bc85e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) results from:', '["Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency","Coproporphyrinogen oxidase deficiency","PBG deaminase deficiency","Ferrochelatase deficiency","ALA synthase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency', 'PCT (most common porphyria) is due to uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency causing photosensitivity and blistering skin lesions. Triggered by alcohol hepatitis C and estrogen.'),
('89a87524-6416-5cdd-9eed-5fe61502948b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP) is due to:', '["Ferrochelatase deficiency","ALA synthase deficiency","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency","Coproporphyrinogen oxidase","PBG deaminase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Ferrochelatase deficiency', 'EPP (ferrochelatase deficiency) causes protoporphyrin IX accumulation in RBCs skin and liver leading to photosensitivity and liver damage.'),
('cf11a103-06ad-523d-8753-955d363bdfd1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug N-acetylcysteine is used to treat:', '["Lead poisoning","Alcohol intoxication","Aspirin overdose","Acetaminophen overdose","Iron overdose"]'::jsonb, 'Acetaminophen overdose', 'N-acetylcysteine (NAC) replenishes hepatic glutathione which conjugates the toxic acetaminophen metabolite NAPQI. NAC also has mucolytic antioxidant properties.'),
('17725331-f72b-5f57-ba81-a39893d4a04c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The chelating agent for lead poisoning is:', '["Succimer","Deferoxamine","EDTA","Dimercaprol","Penicillamine"]'::jsonb, 'EDTA', 'EDTA (calcium disodium edetate) binds lead for urinary excretion. For severe poisoning combination with dimercaprol (BAL) is used. DMSA (succimer) is an oral alternative.'),
('95f9284e-3796-5bb0-bca6-5a41dc29ed89'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Deferoxamine is an iron chelator used for:', '["Mercury poisoning metabolic pathway process","Lead poisoning metabolic pathway process","Copper overload metabolic pathway process","Acute iron poisoning and chronic iron overload","Arsenic poisoning metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Acute iron poisoning and chronic iron overload', 'Deferoxamine binds ferric iron (Fe3+) with high affinity forming ferrioxamine excreted in urine and bile. Used in iron overdose and transfusion-induced iron overload (thalassemia).'),
('ad59e7b2-bdf1-5374-b0ed-8a34253940ed'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Penicillamine chelates:', '["Copper in Wilson disease","Iron in hemochromatosis","Lead","Arsenic","Mercury"]'::jsonb, 'Copper in Wilson disease', 'Penicillamine chelates copper increasing urinary excretion in Wilson disease. It also chelates lead and has anti-rheumatoid properties but has significant side effects.'),
('e5ad8257-533f-5e26-85ba-bfce2bfabfa9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'BAL (dimercaprol) is used for:', '["Arsenic mercury and lead poisoning","Iron poisoning metabolic pathway process","Copper poisoning metabolic pathway process","Zinc poisoning metabolic pathway process","Calcium poisoning metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Arsenic mercury and lead poisoning', 'BAL (British Anti-Lewisite) was developed as an antidote for arsenic Lewisite. It chelates arsenic mercury and lead but has a narrow therapeutic window and painful injection.'),
('a839a81b-5ee8-5a26-b6cc-6025d0b0ce3e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The treatment for methanol poisoning includes:', '["Sodium bicarbonate metabolic pathway process","Naloxone metabolic pathway process","Fomepizole (alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor) and ethanol","Flumazenil metabolic pathway process","N-acetylcysteine metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Fomepizole (alcohol dehydrogenase inhibitor) and ethanol', 'Fomepizole/ethanol compete with methanol for alcohol dehydrogenase preventing formation of toxic formic acid. Hemodialysis removes methanol and formate.'),
('7ff324fe-92ec-5e03-8cad-db6fdf028c81'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Ethylene glycol poisoning is treated with:', '["Flumazenil","Deferoxamine","Fomepizole and ethanol","Naloxone","N-acetylcysteine"]'::jsonb, 'Fomepizole and ethanol', 'Ethylene glycol (antifreeze) is metabolized by ADH to toxic oxalic acid. Fomepizole/ethanol block ADH. Intravenous ethanol is used if fomepizole unavailable.'),
('1190664a-7029-5880-81d1-e8611d43cb75'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Sodium thiosulfate is used with nitrites for:', '["Carbon monoxide poisoning","Organophosphate poisoning","Arsenic poisoning","Cyanide poisoning","Lead poisoning"]'::jsonb, 'Cyanide poisoning', 'Nitrites induce methemoglobinemia (binds cyanide). Thiosulfate provides sulfur substrate for rhodanese converting cyanide to thiocyanate excreted in urine.'),
('ea6fa2eb-a878-5160-b440-0a60bc7efd74'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Atropine is the antidote for:', '["Iron overdose","Acetaminophen overdose","Opioid overdose","Benzodiazepine overdose","Organophosphate and carbamate poisoning"]'::jsonb, 'Organophosphate and carbamate poisoning', 'Atropine blocks muscarinic acetylcholine receptors countering excess acetylcholine from cholinesterase inhibition. It is used with pralidoxime (reactivates AChE) for organophosphate poisoning.'),
('68f236c2-fff6-5ffe-a1ca-82859b32c54a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The antidote for opioid overdose is:', '["N-acetylcysteine","Naloxone","Flumazenil","Deferoxamine","Atropine"]'::jsonb, 'Naloxone', 'Naloxone is a competitive opioid receptor antagonist rapidly reversing respiratory depression and sedation. Its short duration (30-60 min) may require repeat dosing.'),
('6981f349-2de3-58f3-884f-cd987ed84076'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Flumazenil is the antidote for:', '["Tricyclic antidepressant overdose","Alcohol overdose","Lithium toxicity","Opioid overdose","Benzodiazepine overdose"]'::jsonb, 'Benzodiazepine overdose', 'Flumazenil is a competitive GABAA receptor antagonist reversing benzodiazepine sedation. It is contraindicated in mixed overdoses with proconvulsants.'),
('aa917e75-ea0d-5a54-b0ba-debf529e8914'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Fab) treat:', '["Digoxin toxicity","Beta-blocker overdose","Calcium channel blocker overdose","Lithium toxicity","Tricyclic overdose"]'::jsonb, 'Digoxin toxicity', 'Digoxin-specific Fab fragments bind digoxin/digitoxin removing them from tissue receptors. They are cleared renally. Used in life-threatening digoxin overdose with arrhythmias.'),
('1c283628-cbff-50d9-a2be-bcdfa9e23850'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Sodium bicarbonate is used to treat:', '["Alcohol overdose","Benzodiazepine overdose","Tricyclic antidepressant overdose","Barbiturate overdose","Opioid overdose"]'::jsonb, 'Tricyclic antidepressant overdose', 'Bicarbonate alkalinizes plasma and urine. In TCA overdose it counteracts Na+ channel blockade widening QRS. In aspirin overdose it alkaline urine enhancing salicylate excretion.'),
('db282c21-9a40-5711-8056-09b43c618940'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The hormone that stimulates gastric acid secretion is:', '["Motilin","Secretin","VIP","Gastrin","CCK"]'::jsonb, 'Gastrin', 'Gastrin from G cells of stomach antrum stimulates parietal cells to secrete HCl. It also promotes gastric motility. Secretin and CCK are secreted by the duodenum.'),
('bd97c2eb-dc36-57ce-be53-12c27c492e6e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Cholecystokinin (CCK) stimulates:', '["Insulin release metabolic pathway process","Gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion","Bile secretion metabolic pathway process","Gastric motility metabolic pathway process","Gastric acid secretion metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion', 'CCK from duodenal I cells in response to fats/proteins causes gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion. It also slows gastric emptying.'),
('cb30ef92-46db-5a31-acd7-a50721a47788'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, Secretin stimulates:', '["Insulin release","Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","Gastric emptying","Gallbladder contraction","Gastric acid secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion', 'Secretin from duodenal S cells in response to acidic chyme stimulates pancreatic ductal cells to secrete HCO3- neutralizing duodenal pH.'),
('dbbfc21a-11f0-527f-9d88-413cd0cb6565'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The incretin hormone GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) is secreted by:', '["Pancreatic beta cells","Intestinal L cells","Stomach G cells","Duodenal S cells","Pancreatic alpha cells"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal L cells', 'GLP-1 from L cells in response to food enhances glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (incretin effect) inhibits glucagon and slows gastric emptying.'),
('0bb78af4-b221-5347-9e3a-05621a7aecdb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'DPP-4 inhibitors (gliptins) treat diabetes by:', '["Preventing GLP-1 degradation","Stimulating insulin secretion directly","Blocking glucose absorption","Increasing insulin sensitivity","Stimulating GLP-1 secretion"]'::jsonb, 'Preventing GLP-1 degradation', 'DPP-4 (dipeptidyl peptidase-4) rapidly degrades GLP-1. DPP-4 inhibitors increase active GLP-1 levels enhancing insulin secretion and reducing glucagon.'),
('c172b749-41e8-50cc-9a04-c44f1cebcd66'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins) treat diabetes by:', '["Blocking glucose absorption in gut","Stimulating GLP-1","Increasing insulin sensitivity","Stimulating insulin secretion","Blocking glucose reabsorption in kidney"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking glucose reabsorption in kidney', 'SGLT2 inhibitors block the major glucose transporter in proximal renal tubules causing glucosuria lowering blood glucose. They also reduce cardiovascular and renal events.'),
('71e58940-ed73-5eef-923c-976473ca0de2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The cofactor for alanine aminotransferase (ALT) is:', '["FAD","NAD+","TPP","PLP","Biotin"]'::jsonb, 'PLP', 'ALT (formerly GPT) catalyzes alanine + alpha-ketoglutarate ↔ pyruvate + glutamate. It requires PLP as cofactor. ALT elevation indicates hepatocellular injury.'),
('b633c406-1353-53a7-bc35-dfc48f5522f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'AST (aspartate aminotransferase) requires which cofactor?', '["PLP","TPP","CoA","NAD+","FAD"]'::jsonb, 'PLP', 'AST (formerly GOT) catalyzes aspartate + alpha-ketoglutarate ↔ oxaloacetate + glutamate. It requires PLP. AST is found in liver heart muscle and kidney.'),
('23f43431-d78d-5e07-a55a-4b30ee114f2e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The ratio AST/ALT in alcoholic liver disease is typically:', '["<1",">5","=1",">2","<0.5"]'::jsonb, '>2', 'AST/ALT ratio >2 is typical in alcoholic liver disease due to mitochondrial AST release and pyridoxine deficiency reducing ALT activity. Viral hepatitis usually has AST/ALT <1.'),
('9718cdba-7b88-5481-9ba6-1922327e99c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme GGT is elevated in:', '["Alcohol-induced liver disease and biliary obstruction","Hepatocellular injury only metabolic pathway process","Muscle injury metabolic pathway process","Pancreatitis metabolic pathway process","Myocardial infarction metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Alcohol-induced liver disease and biliary obstruction', 'GGT is a microsomal enzyme induced by alcohol and elevated in cholestasis. It is sensitive but not specific for liver disease. Useful for monitoring alcohol abstinence.'),
('fc3d80a0-9185-53c3-a83a-5c54fbf1824b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Serum ceruloplasmin is decreased in:', '["Acute phase response","Hemochromatosis","Liver cirrhosis","Iron deficiency","Wilson disease"]'::jsonb, 'Wilson disease', 'Ceruloplasmin is the main copper transport protein (ferroxidase). Low levels with increased urinary copper and Kayser-Fleischer rings diagnose Wilson disease.'),
('832e0029-6397-5c65-9c6c-772076219d79'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is a tumor marker for:', '["Hepatocellular carcinoma and germ cell tumors","Lung cancer metabolic pathway process","Prostate cancer metabolic pathway process","Breast cancer metabolic pathway process","Colon cancer metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatocellular carcinoma and germ cell tumors', 'AFP is elevated in hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) and non-seminomatous germ cell tumors. It is also elevated in pregnancy and benign liver disease.'),
('605d23bd-4ab8-52c7-8fc9-be3254a1b7d8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'CA-125 is a tumor marker for:', '["Ovarian cancer","Prostate cancer","Colon cancer","Breast cancer","Pancreatic cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Ovarian cancer', 'CA-125 is elevated in epithelial ovarian cancer but also in endometriosis pregnancy and other cancers. It is used for monitoring treatment response and recurrence.'),
('c8767d7a-8dc8-5e24-b69c-751e3515aa9b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is a marker for:', '["Prostate cancer","Pancreatic cancer","Breast cancer","Colon cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate cancer', 'PSA is a serine protease produced by prostate epithelium. Elevated in prostate cancer BPH and prostatitis. Used for screening and monitoring prostate cancer.'),
('ac69170b-cda4-51e7-bd0e-617ba6b0f14a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'CEA (carcinoembryonic antigen) is primarily a marker for:', '["Prostate cancer","Colorectal cancer","Breast cancer","Ovarian cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Colorectal cancer', 'CEA is elevated in colorectal cancer and other malignancies. It is also elevated in smokers and benign conditions. Used for monitoring colorectal cancer recurrence.'),
('4d2f3c36-2b8f-5df4-b0ea-2a7708216440'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'HCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) is a marker for:', '["Choriocarcinoma and germ cell tumors","Breast cancer","Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Hepatocellular carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 'Choriocarcinoma and germ cell tumors', 'HCG beta subunit is elevated in choriocarcinoma and testicular germ cell tumors (seminoma and non-seminoma). It is also the pregnancy hormone.'),
('cb77b01b-3613-58b8-bec3-c0c23ef05917'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The prothrombin time (PT) measures:', '["Extrinsic and common coagulation pathways","Platelet function metabolic pathway process","Antithrombin activity metabolic pathway process","Fibrinolysis metabolic pathway process","Intrinsic pathway metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Extrinsic and common coagulation pathways', 'PT measures factors VII X V II and fibrinogen. It is prolonged in liver disease warfarin therapy and vitamin K deficiency. INR standardizes PT for warfarin monitoring.'),
('310cb462-0318-584f-9cb9-f0dd8805ddda'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'aPTT (activated partial thromboplastin time) measures:', '["Platelet function","Vitamin K factors only","Fibrinolysis","Extrinsic pathway","Intrinsic and common pathways"]'::jsonb, 'Intrinsic and common pathways', 'aPTT is prolonged in hemophilia A/B heparin therapy and lupus anticoagulant. It assesses the intrinsic coagulation pathway.'),
('7c086d1f-d725-5f57-9f83-8b385e56878f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The therapeutic range for warfarin (INR) in most conditions is:', '["4.5-5.5","0.5-1.0","2.0-3.0","1.0-1.5","3.5-4.5"]'::jsonb, '2.0-3.0', 'Target INR 2.0-3.0 for most indications (atrial fibrillation DVT PE mechanical valves in some positions). Higher INR (2.5-3.5) for certain mechanical valves.'),
('7240a312-4ec7-5331-b428-99ef6ea0e3c5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide) is a marker for:', '["Hypertension","Pulmonary embolism","Renal failure","Heart failure","Myocardial infarction"]'::jsonb, 'Heart failure', 'BNP (and NT-proBNP) are released from cardiac ventricles in response to stretch. Elevated in heart failure aiding diagnosis and prognosis. Also elevated in some pulmonary conditions.'),
('bc025e76-3a4d-5c18-94c3-bc36e4472914'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Troponin elevation in the absence of MI can occur in:', '["Myocarditis pulmonary embolism renal failure and sepsis","Only in MI metabolic pathway process","Only in unstable angina metabolic pathway process","Only in heart failure metabolic pathway process","Only in arrhythmias metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Myocarditis pulmonary embolism renal failure and sepsis', 'Troponin can be elevated in myocarditis pulmonary embolism renal failure sepsis and critical illness due to myocardial supply-demand mismatch (type 2 MI) or direct injury.'),
('58b22468-2ceb-5fe9-8228-adbb379a8311'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'HbA1c reflects average blood glucose over approximately:', '["1 year","2-3 weeks","6 months","24 hours","2-3 months"]'::jsonb, '2-3 months', 'HbA1c (glycated hemoglobin) forms by non-enzymatic glucose attachment to hemoglobin N-terminal valine. It reflects average glucose over RBC lifespan (~120 days). Target <7% in diabetes.'),
('1e4f0e14-93dc-5bd6-bdf8-19bf14c88e7a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'A normal HbA1c in a non-diabetic individual is:', '["6-7%","7-8%","8-9%","4-5.6%","9-10%"]'::jsonb, '4-5.6%', 'Normal HbA1c is <5.7%. 5.7-6.4% indicates prediabetes. ≥6.5% indicates diabetes. Each 1% HbA1c increase corresponds to ~30 mg/dL average glucose increase.'),
('87037eb0-c796-5453-b427-4b61f5e0c050'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Fructosamine reflects blood glucose over the past:', '["6-12 hours","2-3 months","24 hours","1-3 weeks","1 year"]'::jsonb, '1-3 weeks', 'Fructosamine measures glycated serum proteins (mainly albumin) reflecting glucose control over 1-3 weeks (albumin half-life ~20 days). Useful when HbA1c is unreliable (hemoglobinopathies).'),
('72cd81db-9050-5f36-b435-ad0f14a0e7fd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Microalbuminuria in diabetes is defined as:', '["30-300 mg albumin/g creatinine",">300 mg/g","<30 mg/g",">1 g/day",">3.5 g/day"]'::jsonb, '30-300 mg albumin/g creatinine', 'Microalbuminuria (30-300 mg/g Cr) is an early marker of diabetic nephropathy. ACE inhibitors/ARBs delay progression. Macroalbuminuria (>300 mg/g) indicates established nephropathy.'),
('9c896718-7bae-5d50-a31c-c4d52bc87512'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Ketone bodies in urine are detected by:', '["Biuret test","Sulfosalicylic acid test","Dip-and-read strips for glucose","Nitroprusside test","Clinitest"]'::jsonb, 'Nitroprusside test', 'Nitroprusside reacts with acetoacetate and acetone (less with beta-hydroxybutyrate). Newer strips detect beta-hydroxybutyrate directly. Ketones indicate starvation or DKA.'),
('62fd8f5e-313a-5da7-95af-a372248660a5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Bence Jones proteins in urine are detected by:', '["Urine dipstick","Biuret test","Sulfosalicylic acid test","Immunofixation electrophoresis","Clinitest"]'::jsonb, 'Immunofixation electrophoresis', 'Bence Jones proteins (free monoclonal light chains) are detected by serum/urine protein electrophoresis and immunofixation. They precipitate at 56°C and redissolve at 80°C.'),
('555f0940-c9cc-5611-a078-e6f5e57e35fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme defect in Lesch-Nyhan syndrome is:', '["Xanthine oxidase deficiency","Adenosine deaminase deficiency","PNP deficiency","APRT deficiency","HGPRT deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'HGPRT deficiency', 'HGPRT deficiency (X-linked) causes hyperuricemia gout intellectual disability and self-mutilation. PRPP levels rise increasing de novo purine synthesis.'),
('d6b116d7-2221-54b3-a5fe-39abb0547876'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency causes:', '["Lesch-Nyhan syndrome","Autoimmune disease","SCID","Gout","Immunodeficiency with normal B cells"]'::jsonb, 'SCID', 'ADA deficiency causes accumulation of deoxyadenosine and dATP inhibiting ribonucleotide reductase and killing lymphocytes (most severe SCID form).'),
('1623f99b-d1df-57a4-92d9-936143557b10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Purine nucleoside phosphorylase (PNP) deficiency causes:', '["T-cell immunodeficiency","Gout","Lesch-Nyhan syndrome","Hemolytic anemia","SCID"]'::jsonb, 'T-cell immunodeficiency', 'PNP deficiency causes accumulation of deoxyguanosine and dGTP impairing T-cell development more than B cells. Features include recurrent infections autoimmune disease and neurological symptoms.'),
('26c18fd9-215e-513e-be36-55a38a80cb0b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Lactose tolerance test involves measuring:', '["Blood lactate metabolic pathway process","Blood insulin metabolic pathway process","Blood galactose metabolic pathway process","Blood glucose after lactose load","Urine lactose metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Blood glucose after lactose load', 'If lactase is present lactose is hydrolyzed to glucose + galactose raising blood glucose. Flat curve suggests lactase deficiency. Breath hydrogen test is also used.'),
('af08ead4-fe76-5436-934a-3acedf079b8d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The glucose tolerance test (GTT) diagnoses:', '["Hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Galactosemia metabolic pathway process","Fructose intolerance metabolic pathway process","Diabetes mellitus and impaired glucose tolerance","Lactose intolerance metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Diabetes mellitus and impaired glucose tolerance', 'GTT involves measuring blood glucose after 75g oral glucose. Fasting ≥126 mg/dL 2-hr ≥200 mg/dL indicates diabetes. 140-199 mg/dL indicates impaired glucose tolerance.'),
('d46bc18e-74bc-50a4-964f-18c39f002984'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'OGTT in pregnancy screens for:', '["Type 1 diabetes","Pre-existing diabetes","Type 2 diabetes","Gestational diabetes mellitus","Hypoglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Gestational diabetes mellitus', 'O''Sullivan test (50g glucose 1-hr) screens for GDM at 24-28 weeks. If positive 3-hour OGTT (100g) is diagnostic with Carpenter-Coustan criteria.'),
('7fd71901-2a53-5175-8f92-dc1cd23023b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The Clinitest (copper reduction test) detects:', '["Only ketones metabolic pathway process","All reducing substances including glucose galactose fructose lactose","Only glucose metabolic pathway process","Only proteins metabolic pathway process","Only bilirubin metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'All reducing substances including glucose galactose fructose lactose', 'Clinitest uses Cu2+ reduction to Cu+ (color change). Detects any reducing substance. Useful for galactosemia screening. Dipsticks are glucose-specific using glucose oxidase.'),
('e024885f-d3d6-59ee-b40c-7f3dc1ce8803'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Glucose oxidase method for blood glucose measurement is:', '["Detects all reducing sugars","Interfered with by uric acid","Detects only galactose","Specific for glucose","Detects only fructose"]'::jsonb, 'Specific for glucose', 'Glucose oxidase (GOD) is specific for beta-D-glucose producing H2O2 measured colorimetrically or electrochemically. It does not detect other reducing substances unlike Clinitest.'),
('34e04856-42d8-51f6-bab9-e2e541eb2a85'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Hexokinase method for glucose is:', '["The reference (gold standard) method","Less specific than glucose oxidase","More specific than glucose oxidase","Cheaper than glucose oxidase","Not used in clinical labs"]'::jsonb, 'The reference (gold standard) method', 'The hexokinase method (HK/G6PD) produces NADPH measured at 340 nm. It is the reference method with high accuracy but is more expensive than glucose oxidase.'),
('643bcba7-ed8d-5ac7-8664-b74e1687b379'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'CK-MB isoenzyme elevation is most specific for:', '["Myocardial infarction","Skeletal muscle injury","Liver disease","Pancreatitis","Brain injury"]'::jsonb, 'Myocardial infarction', 'CK-MB (2 M + 2 B subunits) comprises ~20% of cardiac CK. Elevation 4-6 h after MI peaks at 24 h returns to normal by 48-72 h. Mass assays are preferred.'),
('c63f7c61-7f6d-5ac2-b923-484b3117dd43'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Troponin I elevation in MI appears within:', '["30 minutes","1-2 weeks","24-48 hours","3-6 hours","7-10 days"]'::jsonb, '3-6 hours', 'Troponin I rises 3-6 h after MI peaking at 12-48 h and remains elevated for 5-10 days. It is the preferred biomarker for MI diagnosis (high sensitivity assays).'),
('35cadac5-f8d5-593d-b72d-8be234fddb10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Myoglobin is an early marker of MI but:', '["Lacks specificity","Has 100% sensitivity","Is more specific than troponin","Is not used clinically","Peaks later than troponin"]'::jsonb, 'Lacks specificity', 'Myoglobin rises 1-3 h after MI peaks at 6-9 h and normalizes within 24 h. It has high sensitivity but low specificity due to presence in skeletal muscle.'),
('d73573ca-5be4-5550-b397-9ad6e0e3f58d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'LDH in MI shows which isoenzyme pattern?', '["LDH1 = LDH2","LDH5 > LDH4","LDH2 > LDH1","LDH3 elevated","LDH1 > LDH2"]'::jsonb, 'LDH1 > LDH2', 'Myocardium is rich in LDH1 (H4). Normally LDH2 > LDH1. After MI LDH1 exceeds LDH2. LDH rises 12-24 h peaking at 48-72 h and remains elevated 7-10 days.'),
('ef2bb3f3-3533-5c8f-b9b8-018e5c3c39fd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Pancreatic lipase is:', '["More specific than amylase for pancreatitis","Decreased in pancreatitis","Elevated in all types of jaundice","Not used clinically","Less specific than amylase"]'::jsonb, 'More specific than amylase for pancreatitis', 'Lipase is pancreas-specific and remains elevated longer than amylase. It is preferred over amylase for diagnosing acute pancreatitis due to higher sensitivity and specificity.'),
('861d48d0-5d2c-521d-bbd3-43488008c0ac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Macroamylasemia is:', '["Low amylase level","Amylase in urine","Amylase bound to immunoglobulin","Amylase in saliva","Very high amylase level"]'::jsonb, 'Amylase bound to immunoglobulin', 'Macroamylase is a complex of amylase with IgA causing falsely elevated serum amylase due to delayed renal clearance. Urine amylase is low (unlike true pancreatitis).'),
('8bb6eb9b-bdb5-5dbf-9311-7226ba798d49'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Serum amylase is also elevated (non-pancreatic) in:', '["Cirrhosis metabolic pathway process","Diabetes metabolic pathway process","Hypothyroidism metabolic pathway process","Parotitis mumps and renal failure","Hepatitis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Parotitis mumps and renal failure', 'Salivary amylase (parotitis mumps) and decreased renal clearance (renal failure) elevate serum amylase. Lipase is pancreas-specific and helps differentiate.'),
('e12296e8-20d7-5fb1-bc2b-31e2e3e97c81'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Alkaline phosphatase isoenzymes include:', '["Pancreatic and liver","Liver bone intestinal and placental","Bone and muscle only","Intestinal and brain only","Liver and kidney only"]'::jsonb, 'Liver bone intestinal and placental', 'ALP has tissue-specific isoenzymes. Bone ALP is elevated in Paget''s disease. Liver ALP in cholestasis. Placental ALP (Regan isoenzyme) in some cancers.'),
('06eb1772-86a5-5874-9b23-047ed137d395'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Gamma-GT (GGT) elevation in alcoholics is due to:', '["Pancreatitis","Cholestasis","Hepatocellular necrosis","Renal impairment","Enzyme induction by ethanol"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme induction by ethanol', 'Ethanol induces GGT synthesis in liver microsomes (CYP2E1). GGT is also elevated in biliary obstruction and many liver diseases. It is sensitive but not specific for alcohol abuse.'),
('c1012ba0-8bb6-59f2-a413-be3591eb7271'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The serum enzyme most specific for prostate cancer is:', '["LDH","GGT","ALP","Prostatic acid phosphatase","Prostate-specific antigen"]'::jsonb, 'Prostate-specific antigen', 'PSA is a serine protease produced exclusively by prostate epithelium. It is more sensitive than PAP for prostate cancer screening and monitoring.'),
('889c9732-fcae-5521-aa2a-92d45086054e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'N-terminal pro-BNP (NT-proBNP) has a half-life of:', '["~7 days","~24 h","~30 min","~60-120 min","~5 min"]'::jsonb, '~60-120 min', 'NT-proBNP has longer half-life and higher plasma levels than active BNP. Both are used for heart failure diagnosis. NT-proBNP is cleared renally so levels increase in renal impairment.'),
('c987b20f-83c2-599d-a264-d1d4279cbcff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The renin activity test is used to evaluate:', '["Hyperthyroidism","Hypoglycemia","Diabetes insipidus","Hypertension","Adrenal insufficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension', 'Plasma renin activity (PRA) is measured with aldosterone. Low renin high aldosterone suggests primary hyperaldosteronism. High renin suggests renovascular hypertension.'),
('31074f1e-2e77-53bd-b9a2-dbd45efe5a2c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The ACTH stimulation test (cosyntropin test) diagnoses:', '["Adrenal insufficiency","Pheochromocytoma","Cushing syndrome","Hyperaldosteronism","Hypothyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal insufficiency', 'ACTH (cosyntropin) stimulates cortisol release. Blunted cortisol response indicates adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease). Exaggerated response can indicate Cushing disease.'),
('adbd314d-67d9-5f7e-9d74-c588d8772510'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The dexamethasone suppression test is used to diagnose:', '["Hyperthyroidism","Cushing syndrome","Diabetes insipidus","Adrenal insufficiency","Pheochromocytoma"]'::jsonb, 'Cushing syndrome', 'Dexamethasone (synthetic glucocorticoid) suppresses ACTH in normal individuals. Failure of suppression indicates Cushing syndrome. Overnight (1 mg) test is used for screening.'),
('ace2d107-484f-551f-b021-6d97edb048f3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The D-xylose absorption test evaluates:', '["Intestinal absorptive function","Renal function","Liver function","Gastric function","Pancreatic function"]'::jsonb, 'Intestinal absorptive function', 'D-xylose is a pentose absorbed in the proximal small intestine without requiring pancreatic enzymes. Low urine/serum xylose indicates mucosal malabsorption (celiac disease).'),
('e2f48775-9b00-5241-a037-3e4f3796c003'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The Schilling test evaluates:', '["Folate absorption","Lactose absorption","Iron absorption","Fat absorption","Vitamin B12 absorption"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 absorption', 'The Schilling test measures urinary excretion of radioactive B12 after oral dose. Stage I: B12 alone. Stage II: B12 + intrinsic factor (distinguishes pernicious anemia from malabsorption).'),
('6119e957-495c-586b-8729-d3a83941a1fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Fecal fat estimation (72-hour collection) diagnoses:', '["Mineral deficiency","Protein malabsorption","Vitamin deficiency","Steatorrhea","Carbohydrate malabsorption"]'::jsonb, 'Steatorrhea', 'Normal fecal fat <7 g/day on 100g fat diet. >7 g indicates steatorrhea. Causes include pancreatic insufficiency (lipase deficiency) and intestinal malabsorption (celiac disease).'),
('403bb377-7625-53c6-9b15-9a8bf31ccaff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The sweat chloride test is diagnostic for:', '["Dehydration","Diabetes insipidus","Renal failure","Hyperaldosteronism","Cystic fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'Cystic fibrosis', 'CFTR mutations impair chloride transport increasing sweat chloride >60 mmol/L. Normal <40 mmol/L. The sweat test is the gold standard diagnostic for cystic fibrosis.'),
('abb202ba-b703-59f3-a0e6-080af73d8af8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The Guthrie test (bacterial inhibition assay) screens newborns for:', '["Galactosemia","Maple syrup urine disease","PKU biochemical reaction","Hypothyroidism","Cystic fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 'PKU biochemical reaction', 'The Guthrie test measures blood phenylalanine using Bacillus subtilis growth inhibition. It was the first newborn screening test. Newer methods use tandem mass spectrometry.'),
('6c31bd04-c8ec-5915-86bf-14482e758458'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS) in newborn screening can detect:', '["Only galactosemia","Many metabolic disorders","Only hypothyroidism","Only cystic fibrosis","Only PKU"]'::jsonb, 'Many metabolic disorders', 'Tandem MS measures multiple metabolites from dried blood spots simultaneously detecting amino acid disorders organic acidemias and fatty acid oxidation defects.'),
('f7abec38-2157-524c-a9b2-128f769a890c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The APOE genotype is associated with risk for:', '["Alzheimer disease","Parkinson disease","Huntington disease","Multiple sclerosis","ALS"]'::jsonb, 'Alzheimer disease', 'APOE epsilon4 increases risk for late-onset Alzheimer disease. APOE epsilon2 is protective. APOE epsilon3 is neutral. The mechanism involves amyloid-beta clearance.'),
('46970d17-ae67-5646-8235-bfd1e40124e5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. Following surgery, a patient develops lymphadenopathy. Biochemical analysis shows elevated inflammatory markers. BRCA1/BRCA2 mutations increase risk for:', '["Colon cancer","Breast and ovarian cancer","Prostate cancer","Pancreatic cancer","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'Breast and ovarian cancer', 'BRCA1 (chr 17) and BRCA2 (chr 13) are tumor suppressor genes in DNA repair (homologous recombination). Mutations confer 40-80% lifetime breast cancer risk and 20-40% ovarian cancer risk.'),
('82bcb326-840f-51ae-adc8-3516d64637bd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. The Philadelphia chromosome (BCR-ABL) is diagnostic for:', '["CML","ALL","AML","CLL","NHL"]'::jsonb, 'CML', 'The Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22)(q34;q11) creates BCR-ABL fusion gene with constitutive tyrosine kinase activity. Imatinib (Gleevec) targets BCR-ABL revolutionizing CML treatment.'),
('4ccca1c5-44ec-54fb-b56d-0187ed1f21ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'HER2/neu overexpression in breast cancer:', '["Is not treatable","Indicates good prognosis","Indicates BRCA mutation","Indicates aggressive tumor and targets trastuzumab","Indicates hormone sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Indicates aggressive tumor and targets trastuzumab', 'HER2 (ERBB2) amplification occurs in ~20% of breast cancers predicting poor prognosis. Trastuzumab (Herceptin) and pertuzumab target HER2 improving outcomes.'),
('701d4343-ca9b-53b2-b2f7-e14134c5c46d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'EGFR mutations in lung cancer predict response to:', '["Erlotinib and gefitinib","Rituximab","Imatinib","Bevacizumab","Trastuzumab"]'::jsonb, 'Erlotinib and gefitinib', 'EGFR tyrosine kinase domain mutations (exon 19 deletion L858R) in NSCLC predict sensitivity to EGFR inhibitors (erlotinib gefitinib osimertinib). More common in never-smokers and Asians.'),
('ab808734-cf89-5c2c-8a82-523a0d74e2d9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug imatinib targets:', '["BCR-ABL c-Kit and PDGFR","HER2","VEGFR","EGFR","RAS"]'::jsonb, 'BCR-ABL c-Kit and PDGFR', 'Imatinib inhibits BCR-ABL (CML) c-Kit (GIST) and PDGFR. It is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor competitive for ATP-binding site.'),
('c668bd5a-04dd-5e28-b869-b3b5eff46d13'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Bevacizumab (Avastin) is a monoclonal antibody against:', '["VEGF","EGFR","CD20","HER2","TNF-alpha"]'::jsonb, 'VEGF', 'Bevacizumab binds VEGF inhibiting angiogenesis. Used in colorectal cancer NSCLC renal cell carcinoma and age-related macular degeneration (ranibizumab).'),
('d2e811e9-5683-56a9-b935-fac1b59c9885'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Rituximab targets:', '["CD3 on T cells","CD52 on lymphocytes","CD20 on B cells","HER2","CD33 on myeloid cells"]'::jsonb, 'CD20 on B cells', 'Rituximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody against CD20 depleting B cells. Used in B-cell NHL CLL and autoimmune diseases (rheumatoid arthritis SLE).'),
('0ccd8973-603c-5c92-b428-443c6756e1c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Trastuzumab targets:', '["CD20","EGFR","VEGF","HER2/neu receptor","BCR-ABL"]'::jsonb, 'HER2/neu receptor', 'Trastuzumab (Herceptin) binds HER2 extracellular domain inhibiting signaling and inducing ADCC. Used in HER2+ breast and gastric cancer.'),
('4c7f43c9-3a40-5db3-bce1-c0aaa92a520c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Cetuximab targets:', '["EGFR","HER2","VEGF","CD20","PDGFR"]'::jsonb, 'EGFR', 'Cetuximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody against EGFR blocking ligand binding. Used in colorectal cancer (KRAS wild-type) and head and neck SCC.'),
('7d50b314-53d7-5aa2-9e7f-1356f689a7a9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The cancer chemotherapy drug cisplatin acts by:', '["Blocking microtubules","Cross-linking DNA","Blocking folate metabolism","Inhibiting thymidylate synthase","Inhibiting topoisomerase"]'::jsonb, 'Cross-linking DNA', 'Cisplatin forms intra- and inter-strand DNA cross-links activating apoptosis. Resistance involves increased DNA repair and glutathione conjugation. Used in testicular ovarian and lung cancers.'),
('73246f4e-c6ae-5d69-858a-0ef31a9a9837'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Paclitaxel (Taxol) acts by:', '["Cross-linking DNA","Inhibiting microtubule polymerization","Stabilizing microtubules preventing depolymerization","Inhibiting topoisomerase","Blocking thymidylate synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Stabilizing microtubules preventing depolymerization', 'Paclitaxel binds beta-tubulin stabilizing microtubules causing mitotic arrest. Used in ovarian breast and lung cancer. Common side effect is peripheral neuropathy.'),
('607d2fff-46a3-506c-b90c-6923baab9aaa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Vincristine acts by:', '["Inhibiting topoisomerase","Cross-linking DNA","Inhibiting microtubule polymerization","Stabilizing microtubules","Blocking folate metabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting microtubule polymerization', 'Vincristine binds tubulin blocking spindle assembly during mitosis. Used in ALL lymphomas and Wilms tumor. Dose-limiting toxicity is peripheral neuropathy.'),
('17458ff3-15e5-5d17-a498-14b87701dfb3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Doxorubicin is an anthracycline that acts by:', '["Cross-linking DNA","Microtubule inhibition","Thymidylate synthase inhibition","Topoisomerase II inhibition and intercalation","EGFR inhibition"]'::jsonb, 'Topoisomerase II inhibition and intercalation', 'Doxorubicin intercalates DNA and inhibits topoisomerase II generating ROS. It causes cardiotoxicity (cumulative dose-dependent) limiting lifetime use.'),
('2b87634c-f11a-59fe-8561-3e508d8d4768'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The prodrug capecitabine is converted to:', '["5-FU","Cytarabine","Fludarabine","Gemcitabine","Methotrexate"]'::jsonb, '5-FU', 'Capecitabine is an oral prodrug converted to 5-FU via three enzymatic steps (carboxylesterase cytidine deaminase thymidine phosphorylase). Used in colorectal and breast cancer.'),
('0a01e86b-68a4-5557-bd8e-8807c527db0e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Tamoxifen acts as:', '["A selective estrogen receptor modulator","A GnRH agonist","An aromatase inhibitor","A progestin","An estrogen receptor downregulator"]'::jsonb, 'A selective estrogen receptor modulator', 'Tamoxifen blocks estrogen receptors in breast tissue (antagonist) but activates them in bone and uterus (agonist = SERM). Used in ER+ breast cancer. Risk of endometrial cancer and VTE.'),
('a7f96d4d-36dd-532e-b36b-c900f70a1c6d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Aromatase inhibitors (anastrozole letrozole) act by:', '["Blocking conversion of androgens to estrogens","Blocking estrogen receptors","Blocking GnRH","Stimulating progesterone","Inhibiting estrogen synthesis in ovary"]'::jsonb, 'Blocking conversion of androgens to estrogens', 'Aromatase inhibitors inhibit the CYP19 aromatase reducing peripheral estrogen synthesis. They are first-line for postmenopausal ER+ breast cancer (no effect on ovarian estrogen).'),
('1bb51a12-bca6-5c0c-afdf-958e07fc4b5d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug Gleevec (imatinib) revolutionized treatment of:', '["Lymphoma","Colon cancer","Breast cancer","CML biochemical reaction","Lung cancer"]'::jsonb, 'CML biochemical reaction', 'Imatinib targets BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase in CML achieving 95% 5-year survival. It transformed CML from fatal to chronic manageable disease.'),
('d4aafdcd-07b7-5a50-b270-0641bd1ee0d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The antibody-drug conjugate trastuzumab emtansine (T-DM1) targets:', '["HER2","EGFR","CD20","CD33","VEGF"]'::jsonb, 'HER2', 'T-DM1 conjugates trastuzumab with emtansine (DM1) a microtubule inhibitor. It delivers chemotherapy specifically to HER2+ cells. Used in resistant HER2+ breast cancer.'),
('7c502dfb-40df-59f9-8247-8069918c55b3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Crizotinib targets:', '["BRAF metabolic pathway process","VEGFR metabolic pathway process","ALK (anaplastic lymphoma kinase) and ROS1 fusions","EGFR metabolic pathway process","MEK metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'ALK (anaplastic lymphoma kinase) and ROS1 fusions', 'Crizotinib inhibits ALK and ROS1 tyrosine kinases. It is effective in ALK-rearranged NSCLC and ROS1-positive NSCLC. Brain penetration is limited.'),
('b5a1e18d-6f47-5c06-b1a5-9f9b32d1ae01'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Vemurafenib targets:', '["BRAF V600E mutation","EGFR","ALK","RAS","MEK"]'::jsonb, 'BRAF V600E mutation', 'Vemurafenib is a BRAF inhibitor for BRAF V600E-mutant melanoma. It shows dramatic response but resistance develops via MAPK reactivation. Combined with MEK inhibitor improves outcomes.'),
('30e8b7b3-ae29-5fa1-afdc-e65940cf5d53'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The antibiotic actinomycin D acts as an anticancer drug by:', '["Intercalating DNA and inhibiting transcription","Inhibiting topoisomerase","Inhibiting microtubules","Cross-linking DNA","Blocking folate metabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Intercalating DNA and inhibiting transcription', 'Actinomycin D binds DNA at GC sequences inhibiting RNA synthesis. It is used in Wilms tumor Ewing sarcoma and gestational trophoblastic neoplasia.'),
('be642825-efab-5475-8624-8f3cd7509904'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) treats:', '["Acute promyelocytic leukemia","CML","ALL","CLL","AML"]'::jsonb, 'Acute promyelocytic leukemia', 'ATRA targets PML-RARalpha fusion protein in APML t(15;17). It induces differentiation of promyelocytes. Combined with arsenic trioxide for high cure rates.'),
('e14d6bdd-a6c9-5e8b-870e-39ad98a0aecf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Arsenic trioxide (ATO) treats:', '["Breast cancer","CML","Lung cancer","Colon cancer","Acute promyelocytic leukemia"]'::jsonb, 'Acute promyelocytic leukemia', 'ATO targets PML-RARalpha degrading the fusion protein and inducing apoptosis. It also targets thioredoxin reductase and generates ROS. Used in relapsed APML.'),
('baa21bdb-4a23-507e-9aef-b2bcaf982f6a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Bortezomib is a proteasome inhibitor used in:', '["Multiple myeloma","Breast cancer","Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Melanoma"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple myeloma', 'Bortezomib inhibits the 26S proteasome blocking degradation of pro-apoptotic factors and activating NF-kB. It is first-line for multiple myeloma and mantle cell lymphoma.'),
('bcc4e90a-2c8b-5dcb-af42-a91c309d3afd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Lenalidomide is an immunomodulatory drug used in:', '["Colon cancer metabolic pathway process","Prostate cancer metabolic pathway process","Lung cancer metabolic pathway process","Multiple myeloma and myelodysplastic syndrome","Breast cancer metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple myeloma and myelodysplastic syndrome', 'Lenalidomide targets cereblon E3 ligase causing degradation of Ikaros/Aiolos transcription factors. It has anti-angiogenic immunomodulatory and anti-proliferative effects.'),
('80235286-c447-52c8-a85c-23b9f3376940'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs) share which common toxicity?', '["Neurotoxicity only","Nephrotoxicity only","Fatigue diarrhea rash and edema","Hepatotoxicity only","Cardiotoxicity only"]'::jsonb, 'Fatigue diarrhea rash and edema', 'TKIs commonly cause fatigue diarrhea skin rash hand-foot syndrome and edema. Specific toxicities depend on kinase targets (e.g. hypertension with VEGFR inhibitors).'),
('627131d0-eed4-55d7-afb4-51d962b992e9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Immune checkpoint inhibitors (PD-1/PD-L1) are used in:', '["Only melanoma","Only lung cancer","Melanoma lung cancer and many solid tumors","Only breast cancer","Only hematologic cancers"]'::jsonb, 'Melanoma lung cancer and many solid tumors', 'Checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab nivolumab) block PD-1/PD-L1 pathway reactivating anti-tumor T-cells. Used in melanoma NSCLC RCC and many other cancers.'),
('7396da9a-a401-519a-a53a-d2a1b395d82d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The monoclonal antibody ipilimumab targets:', '["PD-1","EGFR","CTLA-4","CD20","PD-L1"]'::jsonb, 'CTLA-4', 'Ipilimumab blocks CTLA-4 on T-cells enhancing anti-tumor immunity. It was the first checkpoint inhibitor approved for melanoma. It causes immune-related adverse events (colitis hypophysitis).'),
('4bb14fb0-7e9e-5378-9afa-b60c19e2f4d3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'CAR-T cell therapy targets:', '["CD19 in B-cell malignancies","CD20","HER2","EGFR","VEGF"]'::jsonb, 'CD19 in B-cell malignancies', 'Chimeric antigen receptor (CAR) T-cells targeting CD19 achieve high response rates in ALL and DLBCL. Toxicities include cytokine release syndrome (CRS) and neurotoxicity.'),
('daca0f55-5eaa-5c19-883d-4752ee9231b2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) is curative for:', '["All genetic disorders","Diabetes","Solid tumors only","SCID and certain leukemias","Autoimmune diseases"]'::jsonb, 'SCID and certain leukemias', 'HSCT replaces defective hematopoietic system. Curative for SCID severe aplastic anemia and certain leukemias/lymphomas. Graft vs host disease is a major complication.'),
('52b55d08-caa1-5888-bc61-3529c80cea9e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The enzyme defect in combined malonic and methylmalonic aciduria (CMAMMA) is:', '["ACSF3","HMG-CoA lyase","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","MCAD","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase"]'::jsonb, 'ACSF3', 'ACSF3 deficiency causes accumulation of both malonic acid (impaired fatty acid synthesis) and methylmalonic acid with neurologic symptoms and metabolic acidosis.'),
('5dac9d9f-893f-5c56-b757-b35f8dbbebc0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Acrodermatitis enteropathica is due to:', '["Zinc deficiency or zinc transporter defect","Selenium deficiency metabolic pathway process","Calcium deficiency metabolic pathway process","Iron deficiency metabolic pathway process","Copper deficiency metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Zinc deficiency or zinc transporter defect', 'Acrodermatitis enteropathica presents with periorificial dermatitis diarrhea and alopecia. Caused by dietary zinc deficiency or autosomal recessive Zip4 transporter mutation.'),
('841c55cc-3c2f-50a6-9122-933e7233a014'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Menkes disease (kinky hair disease) is due to:', '["Copper transport deficiency","Zinc transport deficiency","Selenium deficiency","Iron transport deficiency","Calcium transport defect"]'::jsonb, 'Copper transport deficiency', 'Menkes disease (X-linked) impairs intestinal copper absorption. Features include kinky hair neurological deterioration hypothermia and arterial tortuosity. Copper histidine is given but efficacy is limited.'),
('11c748c6-8bbd-5f42-9c89-a8186b701734'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The triple response of Lewis is:', '["Bleeding bruising infection","Red flare wheal","Numbness tingling weakness","Itching rash blister","Pain swelling heat"]'::jsonb, 'Red flare wheal', 'Intradermal histamine causes: 1) local red spot (vasodilation) 2) flare (axon reflex) 3) wheal (increased permeability). This classic triple response tests microvascular function.'),
('d6ceaa5e-6feb-5a37-900d-2f11b2cdfda1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The skin blanching test with corticosteroids:', '["Tests for infection","Assesses anti-inflammatory effect","Tests for allergy","Assesses vasoconstrictor potency of topical steroids","Assesses anesthetic effect"]'::jsonb, 'Assesses vasoconstrictor potency of topical steroids', 'Topical steroids cause vasoconstriction producing skin blanching. The degree of blanching correlates with anti-inflammatory potency. Used for bioequivalence studies.'),
('b56f8f9f-2ebc-5637-b942-b9bd7be57962'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The limulus amebocyte lysate (LAL) test detects:', '["Endotoxin","Fungal contamination","Exotoxin","Mycoplasma","Viral contamination"]'::jsonb, 'Endotoxin', 'LAL from horseshoe crab blood coagulates in presence of endotoxin (LPS). It is the standard test for endotoxin contamination in pharmaceuticals and medical devices.'),
('65047c18-0b50-5139-b18c-3bd9ef8dc32b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The Ames test tests for:', '["Mutagenicity","Carcinogenicity","Toxicity","Efficacy","Sterility"]'::jsonb, 'Mutagenicity', 'The Ames test uses Salmonella typhimurium histidine auxotrophs to detect mutagens. Reversion to histidine independence indicates mutagenic potential. It correlates with carcinogenicity.'),
('c66b15cf-e598-533a-b33b-be92111f7203'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The comet assay (single cell gel electrophoresis) measures:', '["DNA damage in individual cells","Protein damage","Lipid oxidation","Apoptosis","Cell proliferation"]'::jsonb, 'DNA damage in individual cells', 'The comet assay detects DNA strand breaks. Cells are embedded in agarose lysed and electrophoresed. Damaged DNA migrates forming a ''comet tail'' proportional to damage.'),
('1a39537c-a79a-554a-bbd7-729ba9f317f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'FRET (fluorescence resonance energy transfer) is used to study:', '["Protein","Protein structure","DNA sequence","RNA expression","Lipid composition"]'::jsonb, 'Protein', 'FRET measures energy transfer between fluorophores within 1-10 nm distance. It reveals real-time protein-protein interactions and conformational changes in living cells.'),
('7a10aeca-ccbd-5578-8932-3c8e125c022f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Bioluminescence resonance energy transfer (BRET) uses:', '["Two luciferases metabolic pathway process","Luciferase as donor and fluorescent protein as acceptor","Quantum dots metabolic pathway process","Gold nanoparticles metabolic pathway process","Two fluorescent proteins metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Luciferase as donor and fluorescent protein as acceptor', 'BRET is similar to FRET but uses bioluminescent donor (luciferase) avoiding photobleaching and autofluorescence. Used for protein interaction studies.'),
('06be0830-5e04-5768-a744-8bfc13baf0dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Surface plasmon resonance (SPR) measures:', '["Real-time biomolecular binding kinetics","Protein structure metabolic pathway process","DNA sequence metabolic pathway process","RNA expression metabolic pathway process","Metabolite levels metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Real-time biomolecular binding kinetics', 'SPR detects changes in refractive index near a metal surface upon binding. It measures real-time association/dissociation rates (ka kd) and affinity (KD) without labeling.'),
('e153415c-6ee8-5efb-b4a0-a1a68ac957b8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Isothermal titration calorimetry (ITC) directly measures:', '["Enzyme activity","Binding thermodynamics","Metabolite concentration","Binding kinetics only","Protein structure"]'::jsonb, 'Binding thermodynamics', 'ITC measures heat released/absorbed during molecular binding. It determines all thermodynamic parameters (stoichiometry affinity enthalpy entropy) in a single experiment.'),
('23993c89-98cc-5ebf-8cff-2b0aede11285'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) measures:', '["Enzyme kinetics","Protein thermal stability","Protein charge","Binding affinity","Protein size"]'::jsonb, 'Protein thermal stability', 'DSC measures heat capacity changes during protein thermal denaturation. It determines melting temperature (Tm) and calorimetric enthalpy providing protein stability information.'),
('005c9704-64f5-5d4d-8f51-ee42a7ba3583'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Mass spectrometry in proteomics identifies proteins by:', '["UV absorbance metabolic pathway process","Peptide mass fingerprinting and tandem MS sequencing","Radioactivity metabolic pathway process","Fluorescence metabolic pathway process","Immunoreactivity metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Peptide mass fingerprinting and tandem MS sequencing', 'Mass spectrometry identifies proteins by analyzing tryptic peptide masses (MALDI-TOF) or sequencing peptides via fragmentation (MS/MS). Shotgun proteomics uses LC-MS/MS.'),
('4638acfc-8a78-5e2d-9588-812c9494f37b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Stable isotope labeling with amino acids (SILAC) is used for:', '["Quantitative proteomics","DNA sequencing","RNA sequencing","Metabolomics","Lipidomics"]'::jsonb, 'Quantitative proteomics', 'SILAC labels proteins with heavy lysine/arginine in cell culture. Mass spectrometry compares relative protein abundance between samples. It is accurate for quantitative proteomics.'),
('84f6672c-8d20-5886-87ec-4f51c14e08f2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The iTRAQ (isobaric tag for relative and absolute quantitation) method:', '["Labels DNA for sequencing","Labels lipids for analysis","Labels RNA for expression","Labels proteins with fluorescent tags","Labels peptides with isobaric tags for multiplex quantification"]'::jsonb, 'Labels peptides with isobaric tags for multiplex quantification', 'iTRAQ uses isobaric amine-reactive tags. In MS/MS each tag releases a unique reporter ion allowing simultaneous quantification of up to 8 samples with high accuracy.'),
('f61936ea-5401-5f26-aeff-42fa6dc3a1f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'ChIP-seq (chromatin immunoprecipitation sequencing) maps:', '["Genome-wide DNA","DNA methylation","RNA expression","Protein","Histone modifications only"]'::jsonb, 'Genome-wide DNA', 'ChIP-seq uses antibodies to pull down DNA bound by specific proteins (e.g. transcription factors modified histones). The enriched DNA is sequenced identifying binding sites genome-wide.'),
('65d3618f-3d09-54e7-b80e-277109381863'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'ATAC-seq (assay for transposase-accessible chromatin) identifies:', '["Open chromatin regions","DNA methylation","Histone modifications","Transcription factor binding","RNA expression"]'::jsonb, 'Open chromatin regions', 'ATAC-seq uses Tn5 transposase to insert sequencing adapters into accessible chromatin. Open regions (promoters enhancers) are identified by sequencing tag clusters.'),
('361d430d-8f3b-5ad7-a42e-fb20347946ac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. RNA-seq (RNA sequencing) measures:', '["DNA sequence","Lipid levels","Transcriptome","Protein levels","Metabolite levels"]'::jsonb, 'Transcriptome', 'RNA-seq uses next-generation sequencing of cDNA to quantify transcript abundance detect novel transcripts and alternative splicing. It has replaced microarrays for transcriptomics.'),
('60969820-2042-5fe4-a250-31d07e61e140'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Single-cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq) reveals:', '["Gene expression heterogeneity among individual cells","Bulk tissue expression metabolic pathway process","Protein levels metabolic pathway process","Metabolite levels metabolic pathway process","Only one gene per cell metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Gene expression heterogeneity among individual cells', 'scRNA-seq profiles transcriptomes of thousands of individual cells revealing cell types states and rare populations. Used in developmental biology immunology and cancer.'),
('62d5001c-582a-5dc6-85b3-386380d108d7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Metabolomics uses:', '["Microscopy metabolic pathway process","Mass spectrometry and NMR spectroscopy","Western blotting metabolic pathway process","ELISA metabolic pathway process","PCR and sequencing metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Mass spectrometry and NMR spectroscopy', 'Metabolomics analyzes small molecule metabolites in biofluids/tissues. LC-MS GC-MS and NMR are primary platforms. Used for biomarker discovery and metabolic pathway analysis.'),
('acb0058c-24ec-588f-95ef-6cc6b80fbaab'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Lipidomics is a subset of metabolomics focusing on:', '["Lipids and their pathways","Carbohydrates only","Nucleotides only","Proteins only","Vitamins only"]'::jsonb, 'Lipids and their pathways', 'Lipidomics comprehensively analyzes cellular lipids (fatty acids phospholipids sphingolipids sterols). It uses LC-MS and shotgun lipidomics to characterize the lipidome.'),
('a78a0f5c-c99d-56a6-ae32-cbe2e5d7a007'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The rate of drug metabolism is often assessed by:', '["CYP450 enzyme activity assays","Glucose tolerance test","Lipid profile","Liver enzyme panel","Complete blood count"]'::jsonb, 'CYP450 enzyme activity assays', 'CYP450 phenotyping uses probe drugs (caffeine CYP1A2 omeprazole CYP2C19 debrisoquine CYP2D6) to assess metabolic capacity. Genotyping also predicts drug metabolism.'),
('7ea22fcf-ff88-5261-a584-b3936c0930bc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Pharmacogenomics of CYP2D6 affects metabolism of:', '["ACE inhibitors metabolic pathway process","Warfarin metabolic pathway process","Beta-blockers metabolic pathway process","Statins metabolic pathway process","Codeine tamoxifen and many antidepressants"]'::jsonb, 'Codeine tamoxifen and many antidepressants', 'CYP2D6 poor metabolizers get less analgesia from codeine (ineffective conversion to morphine). Ultra-rapid metabolizers risk toxicity. CYP2D6 also metabolizes tamoxifen (active endoxifen).'),
('22c88eaa-1cc6-582d-9b3f-780464eff2fb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'CYP2C9 genotyping guides dosing of:', '["Warfarin and phenytoin","Codeine","ACE inhibitors","Beta-blockers","Statins"]'::jsonb, 'Warfarin and phenytoin', 'CYP2C9*2 and *3 variants reduce warfarin metabolism increasing bleeding risk. VKORC1 genotype also affects warfarin dosing. Combined genotyping guides initial dose selection.'),
('a8a9cb92-5abc-5c5f-bc73-ba31358a2557'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'TPMT (thiopurine methyltransferase) deficiency increases toxicity risk of:', '["Paclitaxel metabolic pathway process","Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine","Methotrexate metabolic pathway process","5-FU metabolic pathway process","Cisplatin metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine', 'TPMT inactivates thiopurines. Homozygous deficiency (~0.3%) causes severe myelosuppression. TPMT genotyping/phenotyping guides dosing to prevent life-threatening toxicity.'),
('41c8ae67-9df3-5607-94dc-5ea159c45b03'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'UGT1A1*28 polymorphism affects:', '["Clopidogrel response","Warfarin dosing","Codeine metabolism","Statin myopathy","Irinotecan toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Irinotecan toxicity', 'UGT1A1*28 reduces glucuronidation of SN-38 (active irinotecan metabolite) increasing diarrhea and neutropenia risk. Also associated with Gilbert syndrome (mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia).'),
('ae9277f1-dbc9-5600-ad7b-1bb3868ad289'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'CYP2C19 poor metabolizers have:', '["Reduced clopidogrel activation","Reduced warfarin metabolism","Reduced codeine toxicity","Increased clopidogrel efficacy","Increased statin efficacy"]'::jsonb, 'Reduced clopidogrel activation', 'Clopidogrel is a prodrug activated by CYP2C19. Poor metabolizers have reduced active metabolite and higher risk of stent thrombosis. Alternative antiplatelet therapy is considered.'),
('07b0b7b7-efd9-537a-aa2c-e1f1b6074390'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'NAT2 (N-acetyltransferase 2) acetylation status affects:', '["Codeine metabolism metabolic pathway process","Statin myopathy metabolic pathway process","Clopidogrel response metabolic pathway process","Isoniazid metabolism and neuropathy risk","Warfarin dosing metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Isoniazid metabolism and neuropathy risk', 'NAT2 slow acetylators have slower isoniazid clearance increasing peripheral neuropathy risk. Fast acetylators may have suboptimal TB treatment response.'),
('9ce5f16d-a9e3-5f8d-b022-0041bab584a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug efavirenz (NNRTI for HIV) has:', '["CYP2B6 genotype-guided dosing","No pharmacogenetic influence","Fixed dosing","Renal clearance only","No toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'CYP2B6 genotype-guided dosing', 'CYP2B6*6 variant (common in Africans) reduces efavirenz metabolism causing increased CNS side effects and drug levels. Genotyping guides dose reduction.'),
('4b2bb0b2-5624-5583-8b28-b3da4f63f76b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'HLA-B*5701 genotyping predicts:', '["Abacavir hypersensitivity reaction","Clozapine agranulocytosis","Flucloxacillin hepatotoxicity","Carbamazepine SJS/TEN","Allopurinol hypersensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Abacavir hypersensitivity reaction', 'HLA-B*5701 testing is mandatory before starting abacavir (HIV). Positive patients have ~50% risk of potentially fatal hypersensitivity. Screening has eliminated this adverse effect.'),
('044413e5-72a9-5efe-a514-4b0e2aea8d53'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'HLA-B*1502 screening is recommended before:', '["Carbamazepine in Asian populations","Abacavir metabolic pathway process","Allopurinol metabolic pathway process","Clozapine metabolic pathway process","Flucloxacillin metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Carbamazepine in Asian populations', 'HLA-B*1502 in Han Chinese and Southeast Asians strongly associated with carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS)/toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN).'),
('7d0854c8-db8b-5b5e-b18a-da6d02132b86'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'HLA-B*5801 screening is recommended before:', '["Abacavir metabolic pathway process","Clozapine metabolic pathway process","Allopurinol in Asian populations","Carbamazepine metabolic pathway process","Penicillin metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Allopurinol in Asian populations', 'HLA-B*5801 strongly associated with allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions (SJS/TEN DRESS). Screening recommended in high-risk populations (Asian African).'),
('02e252fc-f9fc-5bfd-bfae-80822eeb57a7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The Drug Interaction Probability Scale (DIPS) assesses:', '["Drug efficacy metabolic pathway process","Generic equivalence metabolic pathway process","Drug safety metabolic pathway process","Patient compliance metabolic pathway process","Likelihood of drug-drug interaction"]'::jsonb, 'Likelihood of drug-drug interaction', 'DIPS (Horn and Hansten) scores probability of adverse drug interactions considering timing dechallenge rechallenge and alternative causes. Used for clinical documentation.'),
('b2f87a8a-c6ee-5798-b56c-992ec4deb0ce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Fomepizole treats methanol poisoning by:', '["Increasing methanol excretion","Blocking formate toxicity","Inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase","Chelating methanol","Activating aldehyde dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting alcohol dehydrogenase', 'Fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole) competitively inhibits ADH preventing methanol conversion to toxic formaldehyde and formate. It also treats ethylene glycol poisoning.'),
('c5a6b1f8-5524-55d1-b990-a43bd2d0c28b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Mesna prevents:', '["Nephrotoxicity from cisplatin","Hemorrhagic cystitis from cyclophosphamide/ifosfamide","Myelosuppression","Neuropathy from vincristine","Cardiotoxicity from doxorubicin"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhagic cystitis from cyclophosphamide/ifosfamide', 'Mesna binds acrolein (toxic metabolite of cyclophosphamide/ifosfamide) in urine preventing hemorrhagic cystitis. It is administered with hydration for bladder protection.'),
('326d2ccf-c6fc-5f20-b204-519a74b326e9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Dexrazoxane protects against:', '["Cyclophosphamide cystitis","Cisplatin nephrotoxicity","Vincristine neuropathy","Methotrexate hepatotoxicity","Doxorubicin cardiotoxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Doxorubicin cardiotoxicity', 'Dexrazoxane is an iron chelator preventing doxorubicin-iron complex formation and ROS generation. It reduces cardiotoxicity but may reduce anti-tumor efficacy.'),
('2d37128b-6d11-5d67-a417-be17e0201624'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'Amifostine protects against:', '["Doxorubicin cardiotoxicity","Methotrexate hepatotoxicity","Cyclophosphamide cystitis","Vincristine neuropathy","Cisplatin nephrotoxicity and radiation toxicity"]'::jsonb, 'Cisplatin nephrotoxicity and radiation toxicity', 'Amifostine is a prodrug dephosphorylated to active thiol in tissues. It scavenges free radicals protecting normal cells from cisplatin and radiation damage.'),
('48d3373d-0cc7-55b0-9f32-ad240b8344c2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Metabolism Control', 'The drug cinacalcet treats:', '["Secondary hyperparathyroidism in renal disease","Hypothyroidism","Hyperthyroidism","Hypoparathyroidism","Primary hyperparathyroidism"]'::jsonb, 'Secondary hyperparathyroidism in renal disease', 'Cinacalcet is a calcimimetic activating the calcium-sensing receptor (CaSR) reducing PTH secretion. Used for secondary hyperparathyroidism in dialysis patients and parathyroid carcinoma.'),
('bf6e189e-be8c-507d-a12b-90f0a5cd1375'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Phenylketonuria (PKU) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Tyrosinase","Homogentisate oxidase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase","Branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase","Cystathionine beta-synthase"]'::jsonb, 'Phenylalanine hydroxylase', 'PKU is an autosomal recessive disorder of phenylalanine metabolism. Deficiency of PAH causes phenylalanine accumulation leading to intellectual disability if untreated.'),
('fca61e07-8957-505c-b2d4-ca8fab412ab5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Classic galactosemia results from deficiency of:', '["Phosphoglucomutase","Galactokinase","Lactase","UDP-galactose epimerase","Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase', 'Galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase deficiency (autosomal recessive) leads to galactose-1-phosphate accumulation causing jaundice cataracts hepatomegaly and intellectual disability.'),
('201509f5-51b4-5287-a003-f77158a37f14'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) involves deficiency of:', '["Homogentisate oxidase metabolic pathway process","Phenylalanine hydroxylase metabolic pathway process","Branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex","Tyrosinase metabolic pathway process","Cystathionine beta-synthase metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex', 'MSUD is caused by defective branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex leading to accumulation of leucine isoleucine valine and their keto acids with maple syrup odor.'),
('95c48c65-3899-59d6-94ff-babaa0bda61a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Alkaptonuria is due to deficiency of:', '["Homogentisate oxidase","Tyrosinase","Cystathionine beta-synthase","Copper-dependent hydroxylase","Phenylalanine hydroxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Homogentisate oxidase', 'Homogentisate oxidase deficiency causes accumulation of homogentisic acid which oxidizes to dark pigment causing black urine ochronosis and arthritis (ochronotic arthropathy).'),
('994fd6c6-11f7-5378-bb42-3e3abba04ec7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cystinuria is a disorder of:', '["Methionine metabolism","Renal transport of dibasic amino acids","Cysteine metabolism","Homocysteine metabolism","Cystine metabolism"]'::jsonb, 'Renal transport of dibasic amino acids', 'Cystinuria is an autosomal recessive defect in the rBAT transporter causing excessive urinary excretion of cystine. Cystine stones (hexagonal) form in the urinary tract.'),
('7370b613-e0a5-5203-9df4-3b9806e3b208'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Homocystinuria most commonly results from deficiency of:', '["Cystathionine beta-synthase","Cysteine dioxygenase","MTHFR","Methionine synthase","Betaine-homocysteine methyltransferase"]'::jsonb, 'Cystathionine beta-synthase', 'Cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency (autosomal recessive) causes homocysteine and methionine accumulation with ectopia lentis thromboembolism and marfanoid features.'),
('33fcaaa7-8625-5edd-99d2-fd50d5edd715'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Gaucher disease is caused by deficiency of:', '["Glucocerebrosidase","Sphingomyelinase","Iduronidase","Alpha-galactosidase","Hexosaminidase A"]'::jsonb, 'Glucocerebrosidase', 'Gaucher disease (most common lysosomal storage disorder) results from glucocerebrosidase deficiency accumulating glucocerebroside. Features: hepatosplenomegaly bone pain and Gaucher cells in bone marrow.'),
('d2256dfb-d4e7-5ea8-b7e1-f79d919e878e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Niemann-Pick disease (type A/B) is due to deficiency of:', '["Glucocerebrosidase","Alpha-galactosidase","Hexosaminidase A","Sphingomyelinase","Iduronidase"]'::jsonb, 'Sphingomyelinase', 'Sphingomyelinase deficiency causes sphingomyelin accumulation in lysosomes. Type A (infantile) has severe neurodegeneration hepatosplenomegaly and cherry-red spot. Type B is milder.'),
('d7746e8f-5478-561b-ad21-ebd86b3606eb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Tay-Sachs disease is caused by deficiency of:', '["Iduronidase","Glucocerebrosidase","Alpha-galactosidase","Sphingomyelinase","Hexosaminidase A"]'::jsonb, 'Hexosaminidase A', 'Tay-Sachs (GM2 gangliosidosis) is due to hexosaminidase A deficiency (HEXA gene). Common in Ashkenazi Jews. Features: cherry-red spot blindness neurodegeneration and death by age 4.'),
('02ac5b90-8c98-55b7-bc03-160abb0e5841'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Fabry disease is due to deficiency of:', '["Alpha-galactosidase A","Beta-galactosidase","Sphingomyelinase","Alpha-glucosidase","Iduronidase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-galactosidase A', 'Fabry disease (X-linked) results from alpha-galactosidase A deficiency accumulating globotriaosylceramide (Gb3). Features: acroparesthesias angiokeratomas corneal opacities and renal/cardiac complications.'),
('2ec1dd47-6f12-51db-9f56-e59c2f422f7b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hurler syndrome (MPS I) is due to deficiency of:', '["Heparan N-sulfatase","Iduronate sulfatase","Beta-glucuronidase","Alpha-L-iduronidase","N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase"]'::jsonb, 'Alpha-L-iduronidase', 'Hurler syndrome (MPS IH) is the severe form of alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency with coarse facies corneal clouding hepatosplenomegaly dysostosis multiplex and intellectual disability.'),
('2acd39e9-8ef7-535c-b638-9bca6cd115a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hunter syndrome (MPS II) is due to deficiency of:', '["N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase","Iduronate sulfatase","Heparan N-sulfatase","Alpha-L-iduronidase","Beta-glucuronidase"]'::jsonb, 'Iduronate sulfatase', 'Hunter syndrome (X-linked) is iduronate sulfatase deficiency accumulating dermatan and heparan sulfates. Features are similar to Hurler but no corneal clouding and milder course.'),
('62bf3ebc-f213-59d9-b9cf-4e11053c0fde'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Sanfilippo syndrome (MPS III) primarily features:', '["Corneal clouding","Severe somatic disease with mild neurological","Severe neurological deterioration with mild somatic disease","Cardiac valve disease","Joint contractures"]'::jsonb, 'Severe neurological deterioration with mild somatic disease', 'Sanfilippo syndrome (four types) involves heparan sulfate degradation defects. Progressive neurological deterioration (hyperactivity sleep disturbance dementia) dominates with mild somatic features.'),
('af77d527-3936-5d7d-9e9e-85c821e0e761'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type II) is due to deficiency of:', '["Branching enzyme","Glycogen phosphorylase","Debranching enzyme","Glucose-6-phosphatase","Acid alpha-glucosidase"]'::jsonb, 'Acid alpha-glucosidase', 'Pompe disease is a lysosomal storage disorder accumulating glycogen in lysosomes. Infantile form: cardiomegaly hypotonia early death. Late-onset form: progressive myopathy respiratory failure.'),
('3ed7ed7d-6917-57cb-91ef-829f1663f7a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Von Gierke disease (GSD I) is caused by deficiency of:', '["Glycogen phosphorylase","Debranching enzyme","Phosphofructokinase","Branching enzyme","Glucose-6-phosphatase"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose-6-phosphatase', 'GSD I causes severe fasting hypoglycemia lactic acidosis hyperuricemia hypertriglyceridemia and hepatomegaly. Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency prevents glucose release from both glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis.'),
('a442e963-f9c7-5852-8d43-60b7ded38837'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cori disease (GSD III) is due to deficiency of:', '["Debranching enzyme","Glucose-6-phosphatase","Branching enzyme","Phosphofructokinase","Glycogen phosphorylase"]'::jsonb, 'Debranching enzyme', 'Cori disease (glycogen debrancher deficiency) causes hepatomegaly fasting hypoglycemia and myopathy. Unlike GSD I it has normal lactate and uric acid and responds to glucagon (fed state).'),
('bef06839-dd57-52dc-8eaf-e8e36398d78b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'McArdle disease (GSD V) is deficiency of:', '["Liver glycogen phosphorylase","Phosphofructokinase","Branching enzyme","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","Debranching enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Muscle glycogen phosphorylase', 'McArdle disease causes exercise intolerance muscle cramps and myoglobinuria. ''Second wind'' phenomenon occurs. Blood lactate does not rise with ischemic exercise testing.'),
('3f4183a4-7973-5697-ba81-387d8a0e4d8b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hers disease (GSD VI) is deficiency of:', '["Debranching enzyme","Phosphofructokinase","Muscle glycogen phosphorylase","Glucose-6-phosphatase","Liver glycogen phosphorylase"]'::jsonb, 'Liver glycogen phosphorylase', 'Hers disease is milder than GSD I: hepatomegaly hypoglycemia (milder) growth retardation. It involves liver glycogen phosphorylase deficiency with normal muscle function.'),
('93b5dd41-0bd0-5be2-a782-061b4c4d4607'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Andersen disease (GSD IV) is due to deficiency of:', '["Phosphofructokinase","Branching enzyme","Acid alpha-glucosidase","Glycogen phosphorylase","Debranching enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Branching enzyme', 'Andersen disease (branching enzyme deficiency) accumulates amylopectin-like glycogen causing progressive liver cirrhosis and death in early childhood. Rare and severe.'),
('644309fe-3647-5429-85f3-cccb09b550ee'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP) involves deficiency of:', '["ALA synthase","Porphobilinogen deaminase","Ferrochelatase","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Coproporphyrinogen oxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Porphobilinogen deaminase', 'AIP (autosomal dominant) causes acute attacks of abdominal pain neuropsychiatric symptoms and autonomic dysfunction precipitated by drugs hormones and fasting.'),
('fafed028-6c1f-5286-9db5-b97f66f37e42'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT) involves deficiency of:', '["Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Coproporphyrinogen oxidase","ALA synthase","Ferrochelatase","Porphobilinogen deaminase"]'::jsonb, 'Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase', 'PCT (most common porphyria) causes skin fragility blistering and hypertrichosis on sun-exposed areas. It is triggered by alcohol hepatitis C estrogen and hemochromatosis.'),
('e567c47e-a925-5722-9f6a-d486debd75b5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP) is due to deficiency of:', '["Coproporphyrinogen oxidase","Ferrochelatase","ALA synthase","Porphobilinogen deaminase","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase"]'::jsonb, 'Ferrochelatase', 'EPP causes immediate painful photosensitivity without blistering. Protoporphyrin accumulates in RBCs skin and liver. Some patients develop liver disease from protoporphyrin gallstones.'),
('7e3b534a-3a66-54f0-8d2a-cfdfb1c9fc1c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Lesch-Nyhan syndrome results from deficiency of:', '["APRT","Purine nucleoside phosphorylase","Xanthine oxidase","Adenosine deaminase","HGPRT"]'::jsonb, 'HGPRT', 'Lesch-Nyhan (X-linked) causes hyperuricemia gout intellectual disability dystonia and self-injurious behavior. HGPRT deficiency impairs purine salvage increasing uric acid production.'),
('56a02e72-449a-5e26-806c-57254c9df49e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'In a surgical context, Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency causes:', '["Hemolytic anemia","Autoimmune disease","SCID","Lesch-Nyhan syndrome","Gout"]'::jsonb, 'SCID', 'ADA deficiency (autosomal recessive) causes accumulation of deoxyadenosine and dATP inhibiting ribonucleotide reductase and killing lymphocytes causing severe combined immunodeficiency.'),
('fa82b206-0b77-58e6-8574-aba0e6fb1cc5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Severe Combined Immunodeficiency (SCID) can be caused by:', '["Only ADA deficiency metabolic pathway process","Only CVID metabolic pathway process","Only MHC deficiency metabolic pathway process","ADA deficiency X-linked IL2RG and others","Only X-linked metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'ADA deficiency X-linked IL2RG and others', 'SCID has multiple genetic causes: ADA deficiency (~15%) X-linked IL2RG (~50%) JAK3 RAG1/2 and others. All cause profound T-cell deficiency with early infections.'),
('719488b0-d37a-54ac-a5b8-4c9672cbca91'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Familial hypercholesterolemia is caused by:', '["HMG-CoA reductase deficiency","PCSK9 gain-of-function","Apolipoprotein B-100 defect","LDL receptor deficiency","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'FH (autosomal dominant) is most commonly due to LDL receptor mutations. Apo B-100 defects and PCSK9 gain-of-function cause similar phenotypes. Homozygotes have severe premature atherosclerosis.'),
('8af9eb11-5b9d-5626-a6bd-bea379cc844f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in:', '["Na+/K+ ATPase","CFTR gene","Chloride channel genes","Aquaporin genes","ENaC gene"]'::jsonb, 'CFTR gene', 'CF (autosomal recessive) involves CFTR chloride channel mutations causing defective secretion in lungs pancreas sweat glands and male reproductive tract.'),
('acfa75a8-3587-546c-aa5b-bcba4bbb993f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency causes:', '["Panacinar emphysema and liver cirrhosis","Panlobular emphysema","Centriacinar emphysema","Bronchiectasis","Asthma"]'::jsonb, 'Panacinar emphysema and liver cirrhosis', 'Alpha-1-antitrypsin (SERPINA1) deficiency allows neutrophil elastase to damage lung parenchyma (panacinar emphysema). Abnormal protein accumulates in liver causing cirrhosis.'),
('0b1e859b-bc84-5534-97ac-5a16daa59888'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hereditary hemochromatosis is most commonly caused by:', '["Ferroportin mutation","Transferrin receptor mutation","Iron transporter mutation","HFE C282Y mutation","Hepcidin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'HFE C282Y mutation', 'HFE C282Y (autosomal recessive) causes increased iron absorption by reducing hepcidin expression. Iron overload causes cirrhosis diabetes cardiomyopathy and bronze pigmentation.'),
('58d79d55-4cd9-5853-a9d4-34312a01c8f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Wilson disease (hepatolenticular degeneration) is caused by:', '["Ceruloplasmin mutation","ATP7B mutation","Metallothionein mutation","ATP7A mutation","Albumin mutation"]'::jsonb, 'ATP7B mutation', 'ATP7B (autosomal recessive) impairs biliary copper excretion and ceruloplasmin synthesis. Copper accumulates in liver (cirrhosis) brain (neurological symptoms) and cornea (Kayser-Fleischer rings).'),
('31168c72-03e0-5bcf-a7ad-ae778b7e6a66'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Menkes disease (kinky hair syndrome) is caused by:', '["Albumin mutation","ATP7A mutation","ATP7B mutation","Copper transporter defect","Ceruloplasmin mutation"]'::jsonb, 'ATP7A mutation', 'Menkes (X-linked) impairs intestinal copper absorption due to ATP7A mutation. Features include sparse kinky hair neurological deterioration hypothermia and arterial tortuosity.'),
('ae8f16f5-22c5-5160-8140-b8d17f57ed57'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency:', '["Has no clinical significance","Prevalent in Mediterranean African and Asian populations","Causes megaloblastic anemia","Affects females more than males","Is an autosomal dominant disorder"]'::jsonb, 'Prevalent in Mediterranean African and Asian populations', 'G6PD deficiency (X-linked) is the most common enzyme deficiency worldwide. It causes hemolytic anemia triggered by oxidative stress (drugs fava beans infection). Mediterranean variant is severe.'),
('e8655077-c204-53b1-844f-b20f86dc14dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pyruvate kinase deficiency causes:', '["Megaloblastic anemia","Iron deficiency anemia","Sickle cell crisis","Thalassemia","Hemolytic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Hemolytic anemia', 'Pyruvate kinase deficiency (autosomal recessive) is the second most common RBC enzyme defect (after G6PD). It causes chronic hemolytic anemia with variable severity.'),
('7348ac5a-1a23-55fd-9e0b-bf1196967171'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hereditary spherocytosis is due to defects in:', '["Hemoglobin","Surface receptors","Enzymes","Spectrin and ankyrin","Ion channels"]'::jsonb, 'Spectrin and ankyrin', 'Hereditary spherocytosis (autosomal dominant most common) involves spectrin/ankyrin deficiency causing loss of RBC membrane surface area spherical shape and splenic destruction.'),
('2acafe25-45b3-533d-a9f8-2197c52c2bb2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Sickle cell disease results from:', '["Gamma-globin mutation","Alpha-globin gene mutation","Iron transport defect","Beta-globin gene mutation","Heme synthesis defect"]'::jsonb, 'Beta-globin gene mutation', 'HbS (Glu6Val in beta-globin) polymerizes under low oxygen causing RBC sickling vaso-occlusion and hemolytic anemia. Heterozygotes (sickle trait) have protection against malaria.'),
('01d38b50-1484-550b-8c8b-f06a872e2bf8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Beta-thalassemia major is characterized by:', '["Extramedullary hematopoiesis","Excess alpha-globin chains","Requires regular transfusions","Absent or severely reduced beta-globin synthesis","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 'All of the above', 'Beta-thalassemia major (homozygous or compound heterozygous) causes severe microcytic anemia requiring transfusions leading to iron overload. Excess alpha chains precipitate damaging RBC precursors.'),
('641c44c5-381a-5c7f-aaad-b02b333157a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Alpha-thalassemia is caused by:', '["Iron deficiency","Excess beta-globin chains","Deletion of alpha-globin genes","Mutation of alpha-globin gene","Impaired heme synthesis"]'::jsonb, 'Deletion of alpha-globin genes', 'Alpha-thalassemia results from alpha-globin gene deletions (two alpha genes on chromosome 16). Severity: silent carrier (1 deletion) to Hb Bart hydrops fetalis (4 deletions lethal).'),
('af668ff9-e85d-56f3-af6c-272ff47cd0c4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'HbH disease (moderate alpha-thalassemia) results from:', '["1 alpha-globin gene deletion","Alpha-globin point mutation","2 alpha-globin gene deletions","3 alpha-globin gene deletions","4 alpha-globin gene deletions"]'::jsonb, '3 alpha-globin gene deletions', 'HbH (beta4 tetramers) forms in moderate alpha-thalassemia (3 gene deletions). HbH precipitates causing hemolytic anemia with Heinz bodies. Patients have moderate anemia and splenomegaly.'),
('38737627-a66f-57eb-8d30-5aad5a203f86'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Diabetes mellitus type 1 is characterized by:', '["Insulin resistance","Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells","Overproduction of glucagon","Pancreatic alpha cell dysfunction","Insulin deficiency only in adults"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells', 'Type 1 diabetes involves autoimmune beta-cell destruction causing absolute insulin deficiency. It presents with ketoacidosis polyuria polydipsia weight loss. Associated with HLA-DR3/DR4.'),
('ca36b692-7bc0-5a2c-9380-6d3e2a853435'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Diabetes mellitus type 2 is characterized by:', '["Insulin resistance with relative insulin deficiency","Absolute insulin deficiency","Autoimmune beta-cell destruction","Ketosis-proneness","Glucagon deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Insulin resistance with relative insulin deficiency', 'Type 2 diabetes involves insulin resistance in peripheral tissues and inadequate compensatory insulin secretion. It is strongly associated with obesity and metabolic syndrome.'),
('be90763c-27d3-5b11-b3af-abdee4fea13a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is characterized by:', '["Hypoglycemia ketonemia","Hyperglycemia ketonemia and metabolic acidosis","Hyperglycemia without ketosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Normoglycemia with ketosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperglycemia ketonemia and metabolic acidosis', 'DKA results from severe insulin deficiency and glucagon excess. Features: hyperglycemia (>250 mg/dL) ketonemia (beta-hydroxybutyrate acetoacetate) anion gap metabolic acidosis and dehydration.'),
('c7d7393e-ead1-5c28-bdb4-fce3c1798ede'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS) is characterized by:', '["Hypoglycemia with altered mental status","Severe hyperglycemia without significant ketosis","Ketoacidosis with moderate hyperglycemia","Lactic acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"]'::jsonb, 'Severe hyperglycemia without significant ketosis', 'HHS (typically type 2 diabetes) has extreme hyperglycemia (>600 mg/dL) hyperosmolality and dehydration without significant ketosis. Osmotic diuresis causes severe electrolyte disturbances.'),
('b0aca64b-29ac-58db-a7d8-75c789417753'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Metabolic syndrome includes:', '["Central obesity hypertension dyslipidemia and insulin resistance","Only obesity and diabetes metabolic pathway process","All autoimmune diseases metabolic pathway process","Only hypertension and dyslipidemia metabolic pathway process","Only insulin resistance metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Central obesity hypertension dyslipidemia and insulin resistance', 'Metabolic syndrome (ATP III criteria): waist circumference ≥40''''(M)/35''''(F) triglycerides ≥150 mg/dL HDL <40/50 BP ≥130/85 fasting glucose ≥100 mg/dL.'),
('51825e40-03d5-56b6-a98c-6a23406ca22d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Obesity is associated with:', '["Increased leptin and decreased adiponectin","Decreased ghrelin","Decreased leptin and increased adiponectin","Increased glucagon","Normal adipokine levels"]'::jsonb, 'Increased leptin and decreased adiponectin', 'Obesity causes leptin resistance (high leptin) and decreased adiponectin (anti-inflammatory insulin-sensitizing). Adipokine dysregulation contributes to insulin resistance inflammation and metabolic disease.'),
('a15f91f2-9393-54b4-9672-428db3fdaeff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is associated with:', '["Obesity insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome","Viral hepatitis metabolic pathway process","Drug-induced liver injury metabolic pathway process","Autoimmune hepatitis metabolic pathway process","Alcohol consumption metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Obesity insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome', 'NAFLD ranges from steatosis to NASH (steatohepatitis) and cirrhosis. It is the hepatic manifestation of metabolic syndrome. Lipid accumulation lipotoxicity and inflammation drive progression.'),
('e1ff5a09-581c-5efc-a9cd-f9c1e28af8b8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cirrhosis results in:', '["Increased albumin metabolic pathway process","Normal clotting factors metabolic pathway process","Decreased albumin synthesis and increased bilirubin","Increased iron storage metabolic pathway process","Decreased globulins metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Decreased albumin synthesis and increased bilirubin', 'Cirrhosis causes hepatic synthetic dysfunction (low albumin prolonged PT) and cholestasis (elevated bilirubin ALP). Portal hypertension leads to varices ascites and encephalopathy.'),
('1c2fd020-0a19-5a35-934c-7b7e1e63107a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) is characterized by:', '["Iron deficiency anemia metabolic pathway process","Autoimmune hemolysis metabolic pathway process","Macroangiopathic hemolysis metabolic pathway process","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia thrombocytopenia and renal failure","Sickle cell crisis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia thrombocytopenia and renal failure', 'HUS (typical from Shiga toxin E. coli) causes microvascular thrombosis. Features: schistocytes on smear thrombocytopenia acute kidney injury. Atypical HUS involves complement dysregulation.'),
('bf62829e-eff5-5a27-9307-d40585115357'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) involves:', '["Widespread activation of coagulation consuming clotting factors","Excessive clot formation only metabolic pathway process","Excessive bleeding only metabolic pathway process","Increased fibrinolysis only metabolic pathway process","Normal coagulation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Widespread activation of coagulation consuming clotting factors', 'DIC is pathologic activation of coagulation microvascular thrombosis consuming factors and platelets causing bleeding. PT PTT prolonged platelets low D-dimer elevated schistocytes present.'),
('0eeeb61f-fa3b-5162-baa5-946fc0a252fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Vitamin K deficiency causes:', '["Only prolonged bleeding time","Only prolonged PTT","Normal PT and PTT","Prolonged thrombin time","Prolonged PT (factor VII) and later PTT"]'::jsonb, 'Prolonged PT (factor VII) and later PTT', 'Vitamin K is required for gamma-carboxylation of factors II VII IX X and proteins C/S. Factor VII (shortest half-life) decreases first prolonging PT. PTT prolongs with more severe deficiency.'),
('1ab1fd6f-6ec4-544c-a92d-4ba9640e0622'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Primary hyperoxaluria type 1 (PH1) is caused by:', '["D-glycerate dehydrogenase deficiency","Oxalate decarboxylase deficiency","Alanine:glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGT) deficiency","Oxalate oxidase deficiency","Glycolate oxidase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Alanine:glyoxylate aminotransferase (AGT) deficiency', 'PH1 (autosomal recessive) is the most common form causing hepatic AGT deficiency. Glyoxylate accumulates and converts to oxalate causing nephrolithiasis nephrocalcinosis and systemic oxalosis.'),
('a281b96d-fffa-537f-ba98-e36d6741b184'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Primary hyperoxaluria type 2 involves deficiency of:', '["Glycolate oxidase metabolic pathway process","Oxalate oxidase metabolic pathway process","Glyoxylate reductase/hydroxypyruvate reductase","D-glycerate dehydrogenase metabolic pathway process","AGT metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Glyoxylate reductase/hydroxypyruvate reductase', 'PH2 is milder than PH1. GRHPR deficiency causes accumulation of oxalate and L-glycerate. Clinical features: nephrolithiasis but slower progression to ESRD.'),
('ca8e9c87-11a3-5111-b9e6-5ff83e69171e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acute intermittent porphyria attack can be precipitated by:', '["Iron supplementation metabolic pathway process","Sunlight metabolic pathway process","Barbiturates sulfonamides alcohol and fasting","High carbohydrate diet metabolic pathway process","Vitamin C metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Barbiturates sulfonamides alcohol and fasting', 'AIP attacks are triggered by drugs inducing CYP450 (barbiturates sulfonamides) sex hormones alcohol and fasting. These increase heme demand overwhelming deficient PBG deaminase.'),
('3e60ccc4-040f-5989-b29e-429f8e1ec1dc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Heme arginate and glucose administration treat AIP attacks by:', '["Inhibiting ALA synthase","Stimulating erythropoiesis","Binding porphobilinogen","Increasing heme degradation","Replacing deficient enzyme"]'::jsonb, 'Inhibiting ALA synthase', 'Carbohydrate/glucose inhibits ALA synthase via the ''glucose effect''. Heme arginate provides exogenous heme directly repressing ALA synthase reducing porphyrin precursor accumulation.'),
('8b5b19ae-493f-5394-8000-aa7b51f40510'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The porphyrin precursor that accumulates in lead poisoning is:', '["ALA and coproporphyrin","Bilirubin","Protoporphyrin IX","PBG only","Uroporphyrinogen"]'::jsonb, 'ALA and coproporphyrin', 'Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase causing ALA and coproporphyrin accumulation. Zinc protoporphyrin also accumulates. Lead poisoning causes microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling.'),
('27fc1127-dbfb-5a93-a1fa-8834b5532f4e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Sideroblastic anemia can be caused by:', '["Folate deficiency","B12 deficiency","Defective heme synthesis","Anemia of chronic disease","Iron deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Defective heme synthesis', 'Sideroblastic anemias involve defective heme synthesis causing iron accumulation in mitochondria (ring sideroblasts). Causes: pyridoxine deficiency/alcoholism/lead/copper deficiency/MDS.'),
('da45bebe-dddb-57e6-8802-421413230e50'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Megaloblastic anemia results from:', '["Anemia of chronic disease","Iron deficiency","Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency","Thalassemia","Sickle cell disease"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin B12 or folate deficiency', 'Megaloblastic anemia results from impaired DNA synthesis causing large RBC with nuclear-cytoplasmic asynchrony. B12 deficiency adds neurologic symptoms. Folate deficiency lacks neurologic involvement.'),
('6a083f7b-e9ed-5ec6-a646-686d9a7d9d86'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pernicious anemia is:', '["Iron deficiency metabolic pathway process","Hemolytic anemia metabolic pathway process","Autoimmune gastritis causing intrinsic factor deficiency and B12 malabsorption","Folate deficiency metabolic pathway process","Dietary B12 deficiency metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Autoimmune gastritis causing intrinsic factor deficiency and B12 malabsorption', 'Pernicious anemia involves autoantibodies to gastric parietal cells/intrinsic factor causing antibody-mediated destruction and B12 malabsorption. It requires lifelong parenteral B12 therapy.'),
('5e0cd6bd-eaae-5dea-9483-8e26193e175a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Iron deficiency anemia shows:', '["Macrocytic RBCs metabolic pathway process","Elevated ferritin metabolic pathway process","Microcytic hypochromic RBCs with low ferritin and low iron","Normocytic anemia metabolic pathway process","High iron saturation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Microcytic hypochromic RBCs with low ferritin and low iron', 'Iron deficiency anemia: low Hb low MCV low MCH low ferritin low serum iron high TIBC. RDW is elevated. It is the most common anemia worldwide.'),
('4fe1f6bb-3e4b-5e94-b43e-8eb4362b956f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Anemia of chronic disease is characterized by:', '["Normocytic normochromic with low iron and high ferritin","High iron saturation metabolic pathway process","Microcytic hypochromic metabolic pathway process","Elevated TIBC metabolic pathway process","Macrocytic metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Normocytic normochromic with low iron and high ferritin', 'ACD involves hepcidin-mediated iron sequestration (low serum iron high ferritin) and reduced erythropoietin. It is seen in inflammation infection cancer and autoimmune disease.'),
('321a22c1-0889-5db9-9f6b-d84f722717bc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) enzyme is important for:', '["Cell membrane stability metabolic pathway process","Generating ATP in RBCs metabolic pathway process","Iron transport metabolic pathway process","Protecting RBCs from oxidative damage via NADPH","Hemoglobin synthesis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Protecting RBCs from oxidative damage via NADPH', 'G6PD produces NADPH which maintains reduced glutathione (GSH). GSH neutralizes H2O2 and free radicals. Deficiency allows oxidative damage to hemoglobin and RBC membrane causing hemolysis.'),
('8933aaec-c0e0-5e15-956f-a55852cfbcd2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Drugs that trigger hemolysis in G6PD deficiency include:', '["Acetaminophen only metabolic pathway process","Primaquine sulfonamides nitrofurantoin dapsone and aspirin","Only fava beans metabolic pathway process","Penicillin only metabolic pathway process","Only infections metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Primaquine sulfonamides nitrofurantoin dapsone and aspirin', 'Oxidant drugs precipitate hemolysis in G6PD deficiency. Avoid: primaquine sulfonamides nitrofurantoin dapsone phenazopyridine methylene blue and high-dose aspirin.'),
('e3dfaaef-0a15-561c-aac6-4c54ed069195'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Crigler-Najjar syndrome types 1 and 2 involve:', '["Bile duct obstruction metabolic pathway process","Bilirubin uptake defect metabolic pathway process","Hemolysis metabolic pathway process","Bilirubin transport defect metabolic pathway process","UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1) deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'UDP-glucuronosyltransferase (UGT1A1) deficiency', 'Crigler-Najjar syndrome: type 1 (complete UGT1A1 absence) causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia requiring phototherapy/exchange transfusion. Type 2 (partial) responds to phenobarbital.'),
('f7b30684-ba19-594d-93b8-90375e3d4174'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Gilbert syndrome involves:', '["Hepatocellular injury metabolic pathway process","Bilirubin overproduction metabolic pathway process","Mild UGT1A1 promoter polymorphism causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Complete UGT1A1 deficiency metabolic pathway process","Bile duct obstruction metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Mild UGT1A1 promoter polymorphism causing unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia', 'Gilbert syndrome (autosomal recessive/dominant) affects ~5-10% of population. UGT1A1 TA repeat causes ~30% enzyme activity. Benign unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia exacerbated by fasting stress illness.'),
('fbfcaa22-ebda-5aff-9278-2702012f3e81'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Dubin-Johnson syndrome involves:', '["Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia metabolic pathway process","Hemolysis metabolic pathway process","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to MRP2 (ABCC2) deficiency","Hepatocellular injury metabolic pathway process","Bile duct obstruction metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia due to MRP2 (ABCC2) deficiency', 'Dubin-Johnson (autosomal recessive) impairs bilirubin glucuronide excretion. Features: conjugated hyperbilirubinemia dark liver (pigment) and normal liver enzymes. Urine coproporphyrin I is increased.'),
('9180191d-e1a4-5dd2-af9e-f2bb627799e1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Rotor syndrome involves:', '["Bile duct obstruction metabolic pathway process","Hemolysis metabolic pathway process","Deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 bilirubin uptake transporters","MRP2 deficiency metabolic pathway process","UGT1A1 deficiency metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 bilirubin uptake transporters', 'Rotor syndrome causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal liver function. Unlike Dubin-Johnson the liver is not pigmented and urinary coproporphyrin profile differs.'),
('5c0eccff-8691-5e15-83f0-d750f43d49b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Type I diabetes is associated with which HLA types?', '["HLA-DR1","HLA-DR3 and DR4","HLA-DQ8 only","HLA-DR2","HLA-B27"]'::jsonb, 'HLA-DR3 and DR4', 'Type 1 diabetes risk is strongly associated with HLA-DR3-DQ2 and HLA-DR4-DQ8 haplotypes. HLA-DR2 is protective. These account for ~50% of genetic risk.'),
('282b0476-7dba-5b29-94e6-4f4af60f48b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY) is:', '["Type 2 diabetes metabolic pathway process","LADA metabolic pathway process","Monogenic diabetes with onset before age 25","Type 1 diabetes metabolic pathway process","Gestational diabetes metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Monogenic diabetes with onset before age 25', 'MODY (autosomal dominant) results from single gene mutations in beta-cell transcription factors (HNF1A HNF4A GCK HNF1B). It is often misdiagnosed as type 1 or 2 diabetes.'),
('db507510-7a96-5183-a8e5-d322e1f2b9c1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'MODY2 involves mutation in:', '["HNF4A","Glucokinase","HNF1B","INS","HNF1A"]'::jsonb, 'Glucokinase', 'MODY2 (GCK mutation) causes mild fasting hyperglycemia (95-140 mg/dL) from birth with minimal progression. It rarely requires treatment and has low complication risk.'),
('b6c91f70-c3e1-57bd-a5a9-a662959559f0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'MODY3 involves mutation in:', '["GCK","HNF4A","HNF1B","INS","HNF1A"]'::jsonb, 'HNF1A', 'MODY3 (HNF1A mutation) is the most common MODY in some populations. It is sensitive to sulfonylureas. Patients develop progressive hyperglycemia and need treatment unlike MODY2.'),
('ac5ae061-7f4b-5a54-bcfe-b9140006d4f7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Lactose intolerance is caused by:', '["Maltase deficiency","Sucrase deficiency","Glucoamylase deficiency","Lactase deficiency","Isomaltase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Lactase deficiency', 'Primary lactase deficiency occurs in ~70% of world population (most common in Asians Africans). Secondary lactase deficiency follows gut injury. Symptoms: bloating diarrhea after milk.'),
('82bb50c2-bf48-5912-b3f9-52b48aa788b3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Celiac disease is an autoimmune enteropathy triggered by:', '["Casein","Gluten","Sucrose","Fructose","Lactose"]'::jsonb, 'Gluten', 'Celiac disease (HLA-DQ2/DQ8 associated) involves T-cell response to deamidated gliadin causing villous atrophy. Diagnosis: anti-tTG IgA antibody and duodenal biopsy. Treatment: gluten-free diet.'),
('7548cfaf-e5d5-57d0-91a5-66ec3f3cccf9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Tropical sprue causes:', '["Vitamin D deficiency only metabolic pathway process","Fat malabsorption only metabolic pathway process","Megaloblastic anemia due to folate/B12 malabsorption","Protein deficiency metabolic pathway process","Iron deficiency only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Megaloblastic anemia due to folate/B12 malabsorption', 'Tropical sprue (unknown cause) affects residents/travelers to tropics causing chronic diarrhea steatorrhea and multiple nutrient deficiencies especially folate and B12.'),
('1ce9c221-3575-5bcb-8c72-131018874cd0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Whipple disease is caused by:', '["Pancreatic insufficiency","Bacterial overgrowth","Tropheryma whipplei infection","Autoimmune enteropathy","Gluten sensitivity"]'::jsonb, 'Tropheryma whipplei infection', 'Whipple disease is a rare systemic infection by T. whipplei. Features: malabsorption arthralgias lymphadenopathy and neurological symptoms. PAS-positive macrophages in duodenal biopsy. Treated with antibiotics.'),
('a38db7e3-3d08-54a7-aa05-93472406e240'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acrodermatitis enteropathica is caused by:', '["Vitamin A deficiency metabolic pathway process","Selenium deficiency metabolic pathway process","Copper deficiency metabolic pathway process","Iron deficiency metabolic pathway process","Zinc deficiency or defective zinc transport"]'::jsonb, 'Zinc deficiency or defective zinc transport', 'Acrodermatitis enteropathica presents with periorificial dermatitis diarrhea and alopecia. Autosomal recessive form involves SLC39A4 (Zip4 zinc transporter). Treatment: zinc supplementation.'),
('9c3a4880-e202-5a62-8add-18d9150d8632'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Kwashiorkor is a form of:', '["Calorie deficiency with wasting","Protein-energy malnutrition with edema","Carbohydrate excess","Mineral deficiency","Vitamin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Protein-energy malnutrition with edema', 'Kwashiorkor results from protein deficiency despite adequate calories. Features: edema (hypoalbuminemia) fatty liver skin lesions (flaky paint) and apathy. Marasmus is caloric deficiency.'),
('1bfab94e-4994-527a-aabe-e4cb622f4604'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Marasmus results from:', '["Calorie deficiency causing wasting","Fat excess metabolic pathway process","Mineral deficiency metabolic pathway process","Vitamin deficiency metabolic pathway process","Protein deficiency metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Calorie deficiency causing wasting', 'Marasmus presents with severe wasting (muscle and fat) without edema. Most common in infancy. Causes: inadequate energy intake from early weaning/chronic illness. Features: ''old man'' face extreme thinness.'),
('cefa5bfd-8ab7-590b-9af2-3e734c8e275a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Beriberi results from:', '["Pyridoxine (B6) deficiency","Riboflavin (B2) deficiency","Niacin (B3) deficiency","Thiamine (B1) deficiency","Cobalamin (B12) deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Thiamine (B1) deficiency', 'Beriberi: dry (peripheral neuropathy Wernicke-Korsakoff) and wet (high-output heart failure). Thiamine is essential for PDH alpha-KGDH transketolase and BCKDC.'),
('bb5ba7ad-031f-588c-b16e-747181b4a0a5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Wernicke encephalopathy is caused by:', '["B12 deficiency","Vitamin B6 deficiency","Thiamine deficiency","Folate deficiency","Niacin deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Thiamine deficiency', 'Wernicke triad: confusion ataxia ophthalmoplegia (nystagmus). Caused by thiamine deficiency in alcoholism. Korsakoff psychosis (amnesia confabulation) may follow. Treated with IV thiamine.'),
('e7164002-b845-5d2d-bd97-b1854e800383'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pellagra is caused by:', '["Vitamin C deficiency","Riboflavin deficiency","Thiamine deficiency","Pyridoxine deficiency","Niacin (B3) deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Niacin (B3) deficiency', 'Pellagra: dermatitis dementia diarrhea (3 Ds). Caused by niacin deficiency/corn-based diet low in tryptophan. Prevention: supplement with niacin or improve diet.'),
('ee85cea0-45d3-5708-acd2-b3abeb8e6314'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Scurvy is caused by:', '["Vitamin E deficiency","Vitamin A deficiency","Vitamin D deficiency","Vitamin C deficiency","Vitamin K deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin C deficiency', 'Scurvy: impaired collagen hydroxylation causing poor wound healing gingival hyperplasia bleeding bone pain and perifollicular hemorrhages. Vitamin C is required for prolyl/lysyl hydroxylase.'),
('161814e2-4691-502e-b4e2-6a8e226b7d3c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Rickets (childhood) / osteomalacia (adult) from:', '["Vitamin D deficiency","Calcium deficiency","Both calcium and vitamin D","Hyperparathyroidism","Phosphate deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin D deficiency', 'Vitamin D deficiency impairs intestinal calcium absorption causing defective bone mineralization. Rickets: bowing legs costochondral swelling (rachitic rosary) delayed fontanelles. Osteomalacia: bone pain proximal myopathy.'),
('bd142de9-90c9-5c85-b4f3-d5994e753b45'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Vitamin A deficiency causes:', '["Bone deformity metabolic pathway process","Night blindness xerophthalmia keratomalacia","Neuropathy metabolic pathway process","Dermatitis metabolic pathway process","Anemia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Night blindness xerophthalmia keratomalacia', 'Vitamin A deficiency affects vision (night blindness) corneal health (xerophthalmia keratomalacia leading to blindness) and immune function. It is a leading cause of preventable blindness in children.'),
('3f4af3e9-e23f-5727-8819-d5c6ae004a77'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Vitamin A toxicity (hypervitaminosis A) causes:', '["Anemia metabolic pathway process","Dermatitis metabolic pathway process","Night blindness metabolic pathway process","Xerophthalmia metabolic pathway process","Intracranial hypertension hepatotoxicity and bone pain"]'::jsonb, 'Intracranial hypertension hepatotoxicity and bone pain', 'Acute vitamin A toxicity: nausea vomiting headache (pseudotumor cerebri). Chronic: hepatotoxicity bone pain hypercalcemia teratogenicity. Occurs from high-dose supplements not diet.'),
('2b97acbd-84d6-5f1c-9ebb-401efcfdcdae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Vitamin K deficiency in newborns may cause:', '["Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn","Jaundice metabolic pathway process","Neurologic deficit metabolic pathway process","Anemia metabolic pathway process","Rickets metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn', 'Newborns have low vitamin K (sterile gut limited placental transfer). Breast milk has low K. Prophylactic IM vitamin K prevents hemorrhagic disease (intracranial bleeding).'),
('a2551daf-074c-5777-8844-f00781530419'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Vitamin E deficiency causes:', '["Night blindness metabolic pathway process","Bleeding metabolic pathway process","Dermatitis metabolic pathway process","Osteomalacia metabolic pathway process","Peripheral neuropathy and hemolytic anemia"]'::jsonb, 'Peripheral neuropathy and hemolytic anemia', 'Vitamin E (alpha-tocopherol) is an antioxidant protecting membrane lipids. Deficiency occurs in fat malabsorption (abetalipoproteinemia biliary atresia). Features: ataxia peripheral neuropathy retinopathy.'),
('380bfa88-9fba-564f-af9f-c637d8dbad5f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Zinc deficiency causes:', '["Growth retardation hypogonadism dermatitis and impaired immune function","Bleeding metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Goiter metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Anemia metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Rickets metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Growth retardation hypogonadism dermatitis and impaired immune function', 'Zinc deficiency: growth failure delayed sexual maturation alopecia diarrhea dermatitis (acrodermatitis enteropathica) and increased infection risk. It impairs many enzymes including alcohol dehydrogenase.'),
('43a8b073-e694-5a68-8086-14234d08f13b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Iodine deficiency causes:', '["Anemia","Goiter and hypothyroidism","Dermatitis","Rickets","Neuropathy"]'::jsonb, 'Goiter and hypothyroidism', 'Iodine is essential for thyroid hormone synthesis. Deficiency causes compensatory goiter (TSH stimulation) and hypothyroidism. Maternal deficiency causes cretinism (intellectual disability deaf-mutism spasticity).'),
('3a7cb300-32fc-5545-8337-6243b968c3da'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Selenium deficiency causes:', '["Rickets metabolic pathway process","Keshan disease (cardiomyopathy) and Kashin-Beck disease","Dermatitis metabolic pathway process","Anemia metabolic pathway process","Goiter metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Keshan disease (cardiomyopathy) and Kashin-Beck disease', 'Selenium is a component of selenoproteins (glutathione peroxidase). Keshan disease: dilated cardiomyopathy. Kashin-Beck: degenerative osteoarthropathy. Found in selenium-poor soil regions (China).'),
('76414046-0bdb-5e33-a1df-ab0cd54806a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Copper deficiency causes:', '["Polycythemia metabolic pathway process","Neutrophilia metabolic pathway process","Thrombocytosis metabolic pathway process","Neutropenia anemia and neurological symptoms","Leukocytosis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Neutropenia anemia and neurological symptoms', 'Copper deficiency: microcytic anemia with neutropenia. Causes: Menkes disease malabsorption gastric bypass excess zinc. Neurologic: myelopathy (resembles B12 deficiency).'),
('f487a5a9-a352-5b5b-b1cb-66043c414830'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) causes:', '["Hypotension metabolic pathway process","Hypertension hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis","Hyperkalemia metabolic pathway process","Metabolic acidosis metabolic pathway process","Hypocalcemia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hypertension hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis', 'Primary hyperaldosteronism (adrenal adenoma/hyperplasia): increased aldosterone causes Na+ retention K+ excretion and H+ loss. Features: resistant hypertension hypokalemia low renin.'),
('a409333f-8ea6-52eb-b5c7-b27795cc75b6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Addison disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) features:', '["Hypokalemia metabolic pathway process","Hyperglycemia metabolic pathway process","Hypernatremia metabolic pathway process","Hyperpigmentation hyponatremia hyperkalemia and hypoglycemia","Hypertension metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hyperpigmentation hyponatremia hyperkalemia and hypoglycemia', 'Addison disease: autoimmune destruction of adrenal cortex. Features: ACTH-mediated hyperpigmentation hyponatremia hyperkalemia metabolic acidosis hypoglycemia. Treated with hydrocortisone and fludrocortisone.'),
('e9719aa5-1e40-58a4-bf9f-9537bf42f8d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cushing syndrome (glucocorticoid excess) features:', '["Hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Hypotension metabolic pathway process","Hyperpigmentation metabolic pathway process","Weight loss metabolic pathway process","Central obesity moon face striae hypertension hyperglycemia"]'::jsonb, 'Central obesity moon face striae hypertension hyperglycemia', 'Cushing syndrome: cortisol excess from exogenous steroids or endogenous (pituitary adenoma = Cushing disease). Features: central obesity buffalo hump purple striae hypertension diabetes osteoporosis.'),
('7a7a7b46-476c-560e-8822-689ee1d9d486'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of the:', '["Adrenal medulla","Adrenal cortex","Thyroid C-cells","Pancreatic islets","Pituitary"]'::jsonb, 'Adrenal medulla', 'Pheochromocytoma: paraganglioma of adrenal medulla producing epinephrine/norepinephrine. Features: episodic hypertension headache palpitations sweating. Rule out before surgery.'),
('27e2e904-852a-561d-983a-d13d9d1622ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Carcinoid syndrome involves:', '["Cortisol excess metabolic pathway process","Thyroid hormone excess metabolic pathway process","Serotonin and tachykinin excess from neuroendocrine tumors","Parathyroid hormone excess metabolic pathway process","Catecholamine excess metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonin and tachykinin excess from neuroendocrine tumors', 'Carcinoid syndrome (from small intestine NETs with liver metastases): flushing diarrhea wheezing and right-sided valvular heart disease. Urine 5-HIAA (serotonin metabolite) is elevated.'),
('2622949f-9f94-5dd7-a3f9-cf58c5d19bbb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'With respect to management, Hyperthyroidism (thyrotoxicosis) features:', '["Cold intolerance metabolic pathway process","Constipation metabolic pathway process","Weight loss tachycardia heat intolerance tremor exophthalmos","Bradycardia metabolic pathway process","Weight gain metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Weight loss tachycardia heat intolerance tremor exophthalmos', 'Hyperthyroidism (Graves disease most common): thyroid hormone excess causing hypermetabolism. Features: weight loss tachycardia palpitations heat intolerance tremor anxiety exophthalmos pretibial myxedema.'),
('46c58697-ef48-5a65-ba4e-cb4d237dbb65'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'For a patient presenting with symptoms, Hypothyroidism features:', '["Weight gain cold intolerance fatigue constipation bradycardia","Weight loss metabolic pathway process","Heat intolerance metabolic pathway process","Diarrhea metabolic pathway process","Tachycardia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Weight gain cold intolerance fatigue constipation bradycardia', 'Hypothyroidism (Hashimoto thyroiditis most common): causes low metabolic rate. Features: weight gain fatigue cold intolerance constipation dry skin hoarseness bradycardia. Congenital: cretinism.'),
('3da64373-cf4a-53ee-a5d6-eb0a5007fc03'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hyperparathyroidism causes:', '["Tetany metabolic pathway process","Hypocalcemia metabolic pathway process","Hyperphosphatemia metabolic pathway process","Hypocalciuria metabolic pathway process","Hypercalcemia hypophosphatemia and bone disease"]'::jsonb, 'Hypercalcemia hypophosphatemia and bone disease', 'Primary hyperparathyroidism (parathyroid adenoma most common): PTH excess increases bone resorption and renal Ca2+ reabsorption. Features: hypercalcemia hypophosphatemia kidney stones osteoporosis.'),
('b892861c-9516-5a9a-8c0b-c98dd651e93b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Regarding clinical scenarios, Hypoparathyroidism causes:', '["Hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia with tetany","Hypercalciuria metabolic pathway process","No symptoms metabolic pathway process","Hypophosphatemia metabolic pathway process","Hypercalcemia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia with tetany', 'Hypoparathyroidism (most common after thyroidectomy): PTH deficiency causes hypocalcemia hyperphosphatemia. Features: tetany Chvostek sign Trousseau sign seizures. Treated with calcium and vitamin D.'),
('b96a2c0d-f329-54d4-9048-b1858b7f5877'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acromegaly (GH excess in adults) features:', '["Early satiety metabolic pathway process","Delayed puberty metabolic pathway process","Dwarfism metabolic pathway process","Enlarged hands and feet coarse facial features organomegaly","Short stature metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Enlarged hands and feet coarse facial features organomegaly', 'Acromegaly (pituitary somatotroph adenoma): GH/IGF-1 excess causes acral enlargement frontal bossing prognathism macroglossia visceromegaly and arthropathy. Also glucose intolerance.'),
('0b55090b-126b-570a-9aea-ae1ca0b61b8a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Gigantism (GH excess before epiphyseal closure) features:', '["Excessive linear growth tall stature","Delayed growth metabolic pathway process","Normal height metabolic pathway process","Asymmetric growth metabolic pathway process","Short stature metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Excessive linear growth tall stature', 'Gigantism (GH excess in children before growth plate closure) causes accelerated linear growth. Features: tall stature large hands/feet and coarse facial features.'),
('acb20a49-6d7c-5380-8eda-9110665ffce0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Growth hormone deficiency in children causes:', '["Tall stature metabolic pathway process","Delayed puberty only metabolic pathway process","Precocious puberty metabolic pathway process","Short stature (dwarfism) with normal proportions","Normal growth metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Short stature (dwarfism) with normal proportions', 'GH deficiency causes proportionate short stature (pituitary dwarfism) with delayed bone age. Other causes: genetic (POU1F1/PROP1) idiopathic tumoral. IGF-1 levels are low.'),
('797dc8e0-6b42-5953-a462-6ac891a952b6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Diabetes insipidus (central) is caused by:', '["Kidney disease","Diabetes mellitus","ADH deficiency","Excess water intake","ADH resistance"]'::jsonb, 'ADH deficiency', 'Central DI: ADH deficiency from pituitary/hypothalamic damage (tumor trauma surgery). Features: polyuria polydipsia dilute urine. Diagnosis: water deprivation test. Treated with desmopressin.'),
('f9e10b5b-6bcd-55c3-ad54-9ffe8a205ee4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is caused by:', '["Excess fluid intake","ADH resistance","Kidney failure","Diabetes mellitus","ADH deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'ADH resistance', 'Nephrogenic DI: kidney does not respond to ADH. Congenital (X-linked V2 receptor or AQP2 mutations) or acquired (lithium hypokalemia hypercalcemia). Treated with thiazide diuretics.'),
('24ed73ee-68f6-58a2-aee6-627ab1eef1ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH) features:', '["Hypotension metabolic pathway process","Polyuria metabolic pathway process","Hyperkalemia metabolic pathway process","Hyponatremia concentrated urine and euvolemia","Hypernatremia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hyponatremia concentrated urine and euvolemia', 'SIADH: ADH excess from lung cancer (small cell) CNS disorders or drugs. Causes water retention and dilutional hyponatremia. Treatment: fluid restriction vaptans (ADH receptor antagonist).'),
('fdf0ddee-5799-504f-b8f4-77b1bc79940d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) involves:', '["DHT deficiency","Testosterone deficiency","Complete or partial androgen receptor defect","5-alpha-reductase deficiency","Estrogen receptor defect"]'::jsonb, 'Complete or partial androgen receptor defect', 'Complete AIS (46 XY): testes present but androgen receptor defect causes female external genitalia. Undescended testes. No uterus. Puberty: breast development (testosterone aromatized to estrogen).'),
('daf69cac-46d7-5265-a271-218946f388e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'In a surgical context, Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) most commonly results from:', '["21-hydroxylase deficiency","3-beta-HSD deficiency","Cholesterol side-chain cleavage deficiency","11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency","17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, '21-hydroxylase deficiency', '21-hydroxylase deficiency (95% of CAH) impairs cortisol and aldosterone synthesis causing ACTH excess and androgen overproduction. Classical: virilization salt-wasting. Newborn screening: 17-OHP.'),
('7e2afa9f-d7ef-585f-922d-6e2058758017'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', '11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency causes CAH with:', '["Virilization plus hypertension","No virilization","Hypotension","Virilization only","Salt-wasting only"]'::jsonb, 'Virilization plus hypertension', '11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency causes accumulation of 11-deoxycortisol and deoxycorticosterone (DOC) which has mineralocorticoid activity causing hypertension and virilization.'),
('9985bba8-2b08-545d-a879-38aa5f055f12'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', '21-hydroxylase deficiency leads to elevated:', '["Cortisol","Testosterone","17-hydroxyprogesterone","Aldosterone","Estrogen"]'::jsonb, '17-hydroxyprogesterone', '17-OHP is the substrate for 21-hydroxylase. It accumulates and is measured in newborn screening. Elevated 17-OHP confirms 21-hydroxylase deficiency CAH.'),
('daa69354-7319-526e-a558-2406d884b9fd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Salt-wasting CAH (most severe form) involves:', '["Complete 21-hydroxylase deficiency with aldosterone deficiency","3-beta-HSD deficiency metabolic pathway process","Partial 21-hydroxylase deficiency metabolic pathway process","11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency metabolic pathway process","17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Complete 21-hydroxylase deficiency with aldosterone deficiency', 'Salt-wasting CAH: severe 21-hydroxylase deficiency preventing both cortisol and aldosterone synthesis causing hyponatremia hyperkalemia hypotension and hypovolemic crisis in newborns.'),
('d72d76f8-fdc7-5092-8d3f-f2f1fa941e77'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Gitelman syndrome is caused by:', '["Thiazide-sensitive NaCl cotransporter (NCC) defect in distal tubule","Na+/K+ ATPase mutation metabolic pathway process","NKCC2 defect metabolic pathway process","ENaC mutation metabolic pathway process","Aquaporin mutation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Thiazide-sensitive NaCl cotransporter (NCC) defect in distal tubule', 'Gitelman (autosomal recessive) causes hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis with hypocalciuria and hypomagnesemia. Milder than Bartter. Features: muscle cramps fatigue salt craving.'),
('876c3e86-5e91-5447-a740-e69a7ad97980'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Bartter syndrome is caused by:', '["ENaC mutation metabolic pathway process","Aquaporin mutation metabolic pathway process","ROMK mutation metabolic pathway process","NKCC2 (Na-K-2Cl) cotransporter defect in thick ascending limb","NCC defect metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'NKCC2 (Na-K-2Cl) cotransporter defect in thick ascending limb', 'Bartter syndrome features: hypokalemic alkalosis with hypercalciuria (nephrocalcinosis) and normal/low BP. Loop diuretic-like defect causes salt wasting. Five genetic types exist.'),
('83ef20e1-767c-575d-b649-152cb763e45d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Liddle syndrome is caused by:', '["NCC defect","NKCC2 defect","Gain-of-function ENaC mutation causing hypertension hypokalemia","Mineralocorticoid receptor mutation","11-beta-HSD deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Gain-of-function ENaC mutation causing hypertension hypokalemia', 'Liddle syndrome (autosomal dominant): ENaC hyperactivity causes Na+ reabsorption K+ loss with low renin low aldosterone. Features: early hypertension hypokalemia. Treated with amiloride.'),
('cb7df5cb-40be-5aa7-b6ed-21e58282dfcd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Apparent mineralocorticoid excess (AME) is due to:', '["NKCC2 defect metabolic pathway process","MR mutation metabolic pathway process","NCC defect metabolic pathway process","ENaC mutation metabolic pathway process","11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 deficiency"]'::jsonb, '11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 deficiency', '11-beta-HSD2 deficiency allows cortisol (rather than aldosterone) to activate mineralocorticoid receptors causing hypertension hypokalemia low renin low aldosterone. Licorice ingestion mimics this (glycyrrhetinic acid inhibits 11-beta-HSD2).'),
('059f2ef7-f8a3-5db9-9dad-3fab07b40d38'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Glycogen storage disease type 0 is due to:', '["Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency","Debranching enzyme deficiency","Branching enzyme deficiency","Glycogen phosphorylase deficiency","Glycogen synthase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Glycogen synthase deficiency', 'GSD 0 (autosomal recessive) causes fasting hypoglycemia and ketosis because glycogen cannot be synthesized. It presents after birth with hypoglycemia and ketonuria. Postprandial hyperglycemia occurs.'),
('b2383f9d-26bd-597f-928a-a2526fee348c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Galactokinase deficiency (type II galactosemia) presents with:', '["Cataracts without intellectual disability","Hypoglycemia","Hepatomegaly jaundice","Failure to thrive","Cataracts with intellectual disability"]'::jsonb, 'Cataracts without intellectual disability', 'Galactokinase deficiency accumulates galactitol via aldose reductase causing osmotic cataract formation. Unlike classic galactosemia there is no intellectual disability or liver involvement.'),
('6a3ea618-1f24-5e88-aef3-c7e52129b31e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'UDP-galactose-4-epimerase deficiency (type III galactosemia) can be:', '["Benign or severe","Always benign","Asymptomatic","Fatal in infancy","Always severe"]'::jsonb, 'Benign or severe', 'Epimerase deficiency: benign form (RBCs only) and severe form (generalized) presenting like classic galactosemia. Generalized form requires galactose restriction.'),
('3fe2acfd-306d-5a1f-a8a6-05568ab7ba62'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Fructose-1-phosphate aldolase deficiency (hereditary fructose intolerance) causes:', '["Fructose malabsorption","Hypoglycemia after fructose ingestion","Galactose accumulation","Hyperglycemia","Glycogen storage"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoglycemia after fructose ingestion', 'Aldolase B deficiency prevents fructose-1-P metabolism causing ATP depletion phosphate trapping and hypoglycemia. Features: vomiting hypoglycemia after fruit ingestion. Avoid honey fruits.'),
('f893e9a4-2e89-5154-9dcd-219387202e97'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Essential fructosuria is due to:', '["Fructokinase deficiency","Phosphofructokinase deficiency","Hexokinase deficiency","Fructose transporter deficiency","Aldolase B deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Fructokinase deficiency', 'Fructokinase deficiency (benign) causes fructosuria. Fructose is excreted in urine. Unlike hereditary fructose intolerance there are no hypoglycemia or liver/kidney problems.'),
('1af8b489-858d-5fbf-9c78-985cbba462b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'On diagnostic evaluation, Medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD) deficiency presents with:', '["Hyperammonemia","Hypoketotic hypoglycemia during fasting","Lactic acidosis","Hyperketotic hypoglycemia","Ketosis"]'::jsonb, 'Hypoketotic hypoglycemia during fasting', 'MCAD deficiency impairs fatty acid oxidation causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia when fasting. Most common fatty acid oxidation disorder. Presents in infancy with vomiting lethargy seizures.'),
('78ff9787-0986-5fa9-a67d-98954b44253f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Very long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (VLCAD) deficiency:', '["Is benign metabolic pathway process","Presents only in adulthood metabolic pathway process","Causes cardiomyopathy and hypoketotic hypoglycemia","Causes only hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Causes isolated myopathy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Causes cardiomyopathy and hypoketotic hypoglycemia', 'VLCAD deficiency (autosomal recessive) presents in infancy with cardiomyopathy Reye-like episodes and hypoketotic hypoglycemia. Later-onset forms have exercise-induced myoglobinuria.'),
('17b26995-980b-58e6-b769-260a9a166612'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Carnitine deficiency (primary) is due to:', '["Carnitine palmitoyltransferase defect","Acyl-CoA dehydrogenase defect","Beta-oxidation defect","OCTN2 carnitine transporter defect","Fatty acid synthase defect"]'::jsonb, 'OCTN2 carnitine transporter defect', 'Primary carnitine deficiency (autosomal recessive SLC22A5) impairs carnitine transport. Features: hypoketotic hypoglycemia cardiomyopathy myopathy. Treated with oral L-carnitine.'),
('0a4bcc75-4eec-539a-b118-6162fe2e71a3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Carnitine palmitoyltransferase I (CPT I) deficiency:', '["No clinical significance metabolic pathway process","Liver-specific causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia","Muscle-specific only metabolic pathway process","Universal metabolic pathway process","Cardiac-specific metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Liver-specific causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia', 'CPT I deficiency (liver type) blocks long-chain fatty acid entry into mitochondria causing hypoketotic hypoglycemia. Unlike other FAO defects there is no cardiomyopathy.'),
('1facf141-b8d4-5c7e-a931-88deb0a9a9ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II (CPT II) deficiency:', '["Only affects brain metabolic pathway process","Has neonatal infantile and myopathic forms","Is benign metabolic pathway process","Is always fatal metabolic pathway process","Only affects heart metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Has neonatal infantile and myopathic forms', 'CPT II deficiency: lethal neonatal form (dysmorphic) severe infantile (hypoketotic hypoglycemia hepatomegaly) and adult myopathic form (exercise-induced myoglobinuria).'),
('f1f1f565-7e6b-5d60-bf44-66d856b7bf37'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase (LCHAD) deficiency:', '["Causes hypoketotic hypoglycemia cardiomyopathy and maternal HELLP","Causes only cardiac disease metabolic pathway process","Causes only hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Is benign metabolic pathway process","Causes only myopathy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Causes hypoketotic hypoglycemia cardiomyopathy and maternal HELLP', 'LCHAD deficiency (HADHA mutation) impairs FAO. Features: hypoketotic hypoglycemia cardiomyopathy peripheral neuropathy. Pregnant carriers may develop HELLP syndrome/AFLP.'),
('ea6ce3e6-178a-5a3c-b32b-c47805656d3b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) deficiency causes:', '["Lactic acidosis and neurological dysfunction","Ketosis metabolic pathway process","Hyperammonemia metabolic pathway process","Hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","DKA metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acidosis and neurological dysfunction', 'PDH deficiency causes congenital lactic acidosis with neurological impairment (microcephaly seizures spasticity). Most common cause of primary lactic acidosis in children. X-linked (PDHA1).'),
('dc26c306-7016-59c6-8192-52a30a3330f1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency causes:', '["Lactic acidosis and hyperammonemia","Isolated ketonuria metabolic pathway process","DKA metabolic pathway process","Hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Hyperuricemia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Lactic acidosis and hyperammonemia', 'Pyruvate carboxylase deficiency (autosomal recessive) impairs gluconeogenesis and anaplerosis. Features: severe lactic acidosis hyperammonemia ketonuria and neurological dysfunction.'),
('b4b0f2aa-f5ab-5f13-b09b-ca45fb9f0458'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Alpers-Huttenlocher syndrome (POLG mutation) causes:', '["Renal failure metabolic pathway process","Diabetes metabolic pathway process","Isolated myopathy metabolic pathway process","Progressive neurological deterioration and liver failure","Cardiac disease metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Progressive neurological deterioration and liver failure', 'POLG mutations cause mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome with progressive encephalopathy refractory epilepsy and liver failure. Valproic acid is contraindicated (triggers liver failure).'),
('0833f01d-327e-5657-80d0-7dd7fc46a6ce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Leber hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) involves:', '["Nuclear DNA mutation","RNA mutation","Chromosomal deletion","tRNA mutation","Mitochondrial DNA mutation"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial DNA mutation', 'LHON (maternal inheritance) causes acute/subacute painless vision loss in young adults. Most common mutation: m.11778G>A (ND4 gene). More common in males. No effective treatment.'),
('e820c521-d151-5dce-b33b-ec552bdeba69'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'MELAS syndrome involves:', '["Only encephalopathy metabolic pathway process","Only stroke metabolic pathway process","Only lactic acidosis metabolic pathway process","Mitochondrial encephalopathy lactic acidosis and stroke-like episodes","Only myopathy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Mitochondrial encephalopathy lactic acidosis and stroke-like episodes', 'MELAS (most common m.3243A>G in tRNA-Leu) presents with encephalopathy seizures lactic acidosis stroke-like episodes migraine and myopathy. Heteroplasmy affects severity.'),
('8223beaf-68c4-545d-bc2c-f306cc5a12d2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'MERRF syndrome involves:', '["Only ataxia metabolic pathway process","Only myoclonus metabolic pathway process","Only muscle weakness metabolic pathway process","Myoclonus epilepsy and ragged red fibers","Only epilepsy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Myoclonus epilepsy and ragged red fibers', 'MERRF (most common m.8344A>G tRNA-Lys) features myoclonus epilepsy ataxia myopathy with ragged red fibers dementia and hearing loss.'),
('0ad580e9-07d5-5bba-a42a-a25a13fd4341'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Kearns-Sayre syndrome involves:', '["Pure neuropathy metabolic pathway process","Isolated ptosis metabolic pathway process","Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia heart block and retinopathy","Pure myopathy metabolic pathway process","Isolated heart block metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia heart block and retinopathy', 'Kearns-Sayre (mitochondrial DNA deletion) presents before age 20 with CPEO ptosis pigmentary retinopathy heart block and ataxia. Treatment: pacemaker for heart block.'),
('93718a09-3dd3-5a50-bc38-6ea86ef110ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Gyrate atrophy of the choroid and retina is caused by:', '["Ornithine aminotransferase deficiency","Arginase deficiency","Argininosuccinate lyase deficiency","Ornithine transcarbamoylase deficiency","CPS I deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Ornithine aminotransferase deficiency', 'Gyrate atrophy (autosomal recessive OAT deficiency) causes ornithine accumulation damaging choroid and retina. Features: night blindness progressive visual field loss and myopia.'),
('a30ca0ba-4680-5e90-9caa-f0bc3ac6fa30'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Nonketotic hyperglycinemia (glycine encephalopathy) is due to:', '["Glycine transaminase deficiency","Glycine transporter defect","Glycine cleavage system deficiency","Glycine receptor defect","Glycine oxidase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Glycine cleavage system deficiency', 'Glycine cleavage enzyme deficiency (mitochondrial) causes glycine accumulation. Features: seizures hypotonia hiccups and apnea in neonates. Diagnosis: elevated CSF:plasma glycine ratio.'),
('510add92-9eab-51bc-9a1a-7db967c850ac'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Maple syrup urine disease has which inheritance pattern?', '["Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked","Mitochondrial","Polygenic"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal recessive', 'MSUD (autosomal recessive) results from mutations in BCKDHA BCKDHB or DBT genes encoding the branched-chain alpha-keto acid dehydrogenase complex E1 alpha E1 beta and E2 subunits.'),
('48f1802c-3f6d-5a69-a9c7-70991b7b72c2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Phenylketonuria is treated with:', '["Glucose restriction metabolic pathway process","Phenylalanine-restricted diet and tyrosine supplementation","Fat restriction metabolic pathway process","Tyrosine-restricted diet metabolic pathway process","Low protein diet metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Phenylalanine-restricted diet and tyrosine supplementation', 'PKU treatment: low-phenylalanine diet (special formula) avoiding high-protein foods. Tyrosine becomes essential. Tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) responsive forms can use sapropterin.'),
('eaee797a-59e4-5f1b-99df-77e7b7ae1dbb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Maternal PKU syndrome causes:', '["Fetal intellectual disability microcephaly and heart defects","Preterm birth metabolic pathway process","No effect on fetus metabolic pathway process","Neonatal hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Improved fetal outcomes metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Fetal intellectual disability microcephaly and heart defects', 'Women with PKU must maintain strict diet before/during pregnancy. High maternal phenylalanine is teratogenic causing fetal cognitive impairment microcephaly CHD and low birth weight.'),
('1c71acea-ad43-5b0d-9983-3d85ebdc8ff2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Tyrosinemia type I (hepatorenal) is caused by:', '["Phenylalanine hydroxylase deficiency","4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase deficiency","Homogentisate oxidase deficiency","Tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency","Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH) deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase (FAH) deficiency', 'Tyrosinemia I (autosomal recessive) causes accumulation of fumarylacetoacetate and succinylacetone. Features: liver failure renal tubular dysfunction (Fanconi) porphyria-like crisis. Treated with nitisinone (NTBC).'),
('60a6eecf-fbb7-55ad-9b80-a13ed7470ca3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Tyrosinemia type II (Richner-Hanhart syndrome) involves:', '["FAH deficiency","4-HPPD deficiency","Tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency","PAH deficiency","Homogentisate oxidase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency', 'Tyrosinemia II causes tyrosine accumulation with keratitis palmar/plantar hyperkeratosis and intellectual disability. It responds to low-tyrosine/phenylalanine diet.'),
('fa3602b8-f432-5d69-98bc-47544220dff8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hawkinsinuria results from:', '["PAH deficiency","FAH deficiency","4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase deficiency","Tyrosine aminotransferase deficiency","Homogentisate oxidase deficiency"]'::jsonb, '4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase deficiency', '4-HPPD deficiency causes hawkinsinuria (unusual amino acid) with metabolic acidosis failure to thrive and tyrosinemia. Usually milder than tyrosinemia I.'),
('6209dd48-5ed1-537b-97e6-78e092b18cfb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Molybdenum cofactor deficiency causes:', '["Uric acid deficiency","Only sulfite oxidase deficiency","Isolated","Combined sulfite oxidase and xanthine oxidase deficiency","Only xanthine oxidase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Combined sulfite oxidase and xanthine oxidase deficiency', 'Molybdenum cofactor deficiency causes severe neonatal seizures and brain injury. Accumulation of sulfite S-sulfocysteine and xanthine. Low uric acid is a clue. No effective treatment.'),
('b5d22c04-9ff5-5aa2-8a41-a14cc0679dda'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Sulfite oxidase deficiency (isolated) causes:', '["Liver disease metabolic pathway process","Neurological deterioration lens dislocation and mental retardation","Cardiac disease metabolic pathway process","Renal disease metabolic pathway process","Hematologic disease metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Neurological deterioration lens dislocation and mental retardation', 'Isolated sulfite oxidase deficiency (autosomal recessive) presents with neonatal seizures spasticity and ectopia lentis. Accumulation of sulfite and S-sulfocysteine.'),
('07248174-372a-5d09-b7f3-bab3a06f3dca'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Zellweger syndrome (cerebrohepatorenal syndrome) involves:', '["Lysosomal storage disorder","Peroxisomal single enzyme defect","Mitochondrial defect","Peroxisome biogenesis disorder","Cholesterol synthesis defect"]'::jsonb, 'Peroxisome biogenesis disorder', 'Zellweger syndrome (autosomal recessive PEX mutations) impairs peroxisomal protein import. Features: dysmorphic facies severe hypotonia seizures hepatomegaly and renal cysts. Lethal in infancy.'),
('97f89526-ccf7-59e2-a20a-81bf698236ad'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Adrenoleukodystrophy (X-linked) is due to:', '["Peroxisome biogenesis defect","Single peroxisomal enzyme defect","Lysosomal defect","ABCD1 mutation","Mitochondrial defect"]'::jsonb, 'ABCD1 mutation', 'X-ALD: ABCD1 defect accumulates VLCFA. Cerebral form: childhood neurological decline (inflammatory demyelination). Adrenomyeloneuropathy: adult spastic paraparesis and adrenal insufficiency.'),
('e0f2e49d-41cb-58ae-bc4e-d77c73962c79'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Refsum disease (classic) is caused by:', '["Bile acid synthesis defect","PEX mutation","ABCD1 mutation","VLCFA accumulation","Phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase deficiency', 'Refsum disease (autosomal recessive phytanoyl-CoA hydroxylase or PEX7 deficiency) accumulates phytanic acid from dietary chlorophyll. Features: retinitis pigmentosa peripheral neuropathy ataxia anosmia.'),
('75dee1db-6409-5fe5-b445-11fa263854ff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome (SLOS) is caused by:', '["Squalene synthase deficiency","7-dehydrocholesterol reductase (DHCR7) deficiency","Mevalonate kinase deficiency","HMG-CoA reductase deficiency","Cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, '7-dehydrocholesterol reductase (DHCR7) deficiency', 'SLOS (autosomal recessive) impairs cholesterol synthesis causing 7-DHC accumulation. Features: microcephaly intellectual disability distinctive facies syndactyly of toes and cardiac defects. High cholesterol diet helps.'),
('27bdf245-eb59-5f37-8d13-89afde223526'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Mevalonic aciduria is due to:', '["Mevalonate kinase deficiency","Squalene synthase deficiency","HMG-CoA lyase deficiency","HMG-CoA reductase deficiency","DHCR7 deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Mevalonate kinase deficiency', 'Mevalonate kinase deficiency (autosomal recessive) causes periodic fever (HIDS) with elevated mevalonic acid. Features: recurrent fever lymphadenopathy arthralgia abdominal pain and elevated IgD.'),
('9a57c8d2-cba9-548e-bd16-5867ca9009fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Lysinuric protein intolerance involves:', '["Defective transport of dibasic amino acids","Cystine transport defect","Urea cycle enzyme defect","Ammonia transport defect","Amino acid metabolism defect"]'::jsonb, 'Defective transport of dibasic amino acids', 'LPI (autosomal recessive SLC7A7) impairs intestinal/renal transport of lysine arginine ornithine. Features: failure to thrive hyperammonemia (after protein meal) hepatosplenomegaly and osteoporosis.'),
('f4eb44af-f168-56df-a2a0-2a5f8f34d2d5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Blue diaper syndrome (tryptophan malabsorption) is due to:', '["Lysine transport defect","Hartnup disease","Phenylalanine transport defect","Tryptophan transport defect","Tryptophan hydroxylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Tryptophan transport defect', 'Blue diaper syndrome: defective intestinal tryptophan transport leads to bacterial degradation producing indican (oxidized to blue indigo). Features: hypercalcemia nephrocalcinosis blue urine stains.'),
('5116f0c7-bdfa-5531-a679-f38d249cf2dd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hartnup disease involves:', '["Defective dibasic amino acid transport","Phenylalanine transport defect","Defective neutral amino acid (tryptophan) transport","Glycine transport defect","Urea cycle defect"]'::jsonb, 'Defective neutral amino acid (tryptophan) transport', 'Hartnup disease (autosomal recessive SLC6A19) impairs neutral amino acid transport. Features: pellagra-like rash (tryptophan deficiency for niacin) neurological symptoms. Improves with nicotinamide.'),
('15509c3f-b73f-559a-92ed-4dbfc444ac3b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cystinosis is a disorder of:', '["Cystine metabolism","Cysteine metabolism","Lysosomal cystine transport","Methionine metabolism","Renal cystine excretion"]'::jsonb, 'Lysosomal cystine transport', 'Cystinosis (autosomal recessive CTNS) causes cystine accumulation in lysosomes. Features: Fanconi syndrome (RTA) growth failure photophobia (corneal crystals) and renal failure. Treated with cysteamine.'),
('b8712383-2287-5a2f-a14e-5df075d60ed1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Fanconi syndrome involves:', '["Glomerular disease","Distal tubular acidosis","Collecting duct defect","Generalized proximal tubular dysfunction","Loop of Henle defect"]'::jsonb, 'Generalized proximal tubular dysfunction', 'Fanconi syndrome: impaired proximal tubule function causing glycosuria (with normal glucose) aminoaciduria phosphaturia and bicarbonaturia (type 2 RTA). Causes: cystinosis Wilson disease Dent disease.'),
('744c24e7-16ee-5e76-bad7-653841dcf95f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Dent disease is caused by:', '["CLC-5 chloride channel mutation","Cystinosin defect","ENaC defect","Na+/K+ ATPase defect","Aquaporin defect"]'::jsonb, 'CLC-5 chloride channel mutation', 'Dent disease (X-linked CLCN5) impairs proximal tubule endocytosis. Features: low-molecular-weight proteinuria hypercalciuria nephrolithiasis renal failure. Resembles Fanconi but X-linked.'),
('f9a16e40-4380-5a3a-853f-e0417d3a8ec2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cystinuria involves impairment of:', '["Cystine efflux from lysosomes","Cystine metabolism","Cystine and dibasic amino acid transport","Sulfate transport","Renal tubular acidification"]'::jsonb, 'Cystine and dibasic amino acid transport', 'Cystinuria (autosomal recessive SLC3A1 or SLC7A9) causes increased urinary cystine. Hexagonal cystine stones are radiopaque (sulfur). Diagnosed by cyanide-nitroprusside test.'),
('30de5f1f-87aa-54c6-ac9b-b1c2b480fdae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment for cystinuria includes:', '["Cysteine restriction metabolic pathway process","Low metabolic pathway process","Vitamin C metabolic pathway process","Hydration alkalinization and tiopronin/captopril","Iron supplementation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hydration alkalinization and tiopronin/captopril', 'Cystinuria treatment: high fluid intake (to dilute cystine) urine alkalinization (to pH >7.5) and thiol-disulfide exchange drugs (tiopronin D-penicillamine captopril).'),
('552f5e3d-bc68-5218-9929-ba5cef6dbf32'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Methylmalonic acidemia is caused by defects in:', '["Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase or adenosylcobalamin metabolism","MCAD metabolic pathway process","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase metabolic pathway process","Pyruvate carboxylase metabolic pathway process","HMG-CoA lyase metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase or adenosylcobalamin metabolism', 'Methylmalonic acidemia (autosomal recessive): MMUT defect or B12 metabolism defects (cblA cblB). Features: metabolic acidosis hyperammonemia hypoglycemia. B12-responsive forms supplement with hydroxocobalamin.'),
('89e108c6-70ce-50e2-ba4d-d99a15703741'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Propionic acidemia is due to:', '["Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase deficiency","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency","Biotinidase deficiency","HMG-CoA lyase deficiency","Holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Propionyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency', 'Propionic acidemia (autosomal recessive PCCA/PCCB) causes accumulation of propionyl-CoA metabolites. Features: severe metabolic acidosis hyperammonemia ketonuria and neurological impairment.'),
('6a6e36ef-e8b2-5059-a4de-c45b213a7271'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency causes:', '["Isolated PC deficiency","Isolated PCC deficiency","Biotinidase deficiency","Isolated MCC deficiency","Multiple carboxylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple carboxylase deficiency', 'Holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency (early onset) impairs biotin attachment to all carboxylases. Features: metabolic acidosis skin rash alopecia. Responds to high-dose biotin.'),
('17861e2b-5936-5d6c-972f-e2ecccd68fe6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Biotinidase deficiency causes:', '["Biotin transport defect","Holocarboxylase synthetase activation","Biotin storage defect","Multiple carboxylase deficiency","Isolated carboxylase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple carboxylase deficiency', 'Biotinidase deficiency (autosomal recessive) impairs biotin recycling from biocytin. Features: seizures skin rash alopecia ataxia hearing loss. Screening in some states. Treated with biotin.'),
('3611a200-c239-5aba-89b1-cfde0dfc278a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', '3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase (MCC) deficiency:', '["Causes organic aciduria often detected by newborn screening","Is untreatable metabolic pathway process","Is fatal metabolic pathway process","Affects only heart metabolic pathway process","Is always symptomatic metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Causes organic aciduria often detected by newborn screening', 'MCC deficiency (autosomal recessive) causes elevated 3-methylcrotonylglycine and 3-hydroxyisovaleric acid. Many are asymptomatic. Some present with metabolic acidosis and hypotonia. Responds to carnitine and glycine.'),
('d205b87c-d018-5850-be5d-e6276b459b22'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', '3-Hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA lyase (HMG-CoA lyase) deficiency:', '["Causes hyperketosis","Causes hypoketotic hypoglycemia and metabolic acidosis","Causes neurological disease only","Causes isolated liver disease","Is benign"]'::jsonb, 'Causes hypoketotic hypoglycemia and metabolic acidosis', 'HMG-CoA lyase deficiency impairs ketogenesis and leucine catabolism. Features: hypoketotic hypoglycemia metabolic acidosis vomiting. Avoid fasting and give frequent feeds.'),
('ae8b42ac-21cd-5907-8129-e97113f85460'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Glutaric acidemia type I (GA-I) is caused by:', '["Glutaryl-CoA synthetase deficiency","Glutaryl-CoA oxidase deficiency","HMG-CoA lyase deficiency","Glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency","Isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Glutaryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency', 'GA-I (autosomal recessive) causes glutaric and 3-hydroxyglutaric acid accumulation. Features: acute encephalopathy macrocephaly dystonia with striatal degeneration. Treatment: carnitine riboflavin and diet.'),
('e1ecb76e-b1ad-59a7-99e4-96e07b978f78'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Isovaleric acidemia is caused by:', '["3-Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency","HMG-CoA lyase deficiency","Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase deficiency","Propionyl-CoA carboxylase deficiency","Isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Isovaleryl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency', 'Isovaleric acidemia (autosomal recessive IVD) causes ''sweaty feet'' odor from isovaleric acid. Features: metabolic acidosis vomiting lethargy. Treatment: glycine and carnitine therapy with protein restriction.'),
('535ff5dd-0f29-5783-b84f-5da9ad063454'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Medium-chain triglyceride (MCT) oil is used to treat:', '["Short-chain FAO disorders","Long-chain fatty acid oxidation disorders","All FAO disorders","Pyruvate metabolism disorders","MCAD deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Long-chain fatty acid oxidation disorders', 'MCT oil bypasses CPT-I deficiency in LCHAD VLCAD and CPT-II deficiencies. Medium-chain fatty acids enter mitochondria directly without carnitine shuttle. Used in long-chain FAO defects.'),
('c805ccc1-20b3-501f-8a1e-bb5778c58580'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The treatment for classic PKU includes:', '["Low-phenylalanine diet and tyrosine supplementation","Low-tyrosine diet metabolic pathway process","High metabolic pathway process","Glucose infusion metabolic pathway process","Fat restriction metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Low-phenylalanine diet and tyrosine supplementation', 'PKU treatment: restrict phenylalanine to maintain blood levels 120-360 micromol/L. Phe-free amino acid formula provides protein. Tyrosine supplementation prevents deficiency. BH4-responsive patients benefit from sapropterin.'),
('5fba0a3d-5a24-50bd-ad79-5e76fb70b9c7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The treatment for galactosemia involves:', '["Galactose supplementation","Lactose supplementation","High-fructose diet","Low","Galactose restriction"]'::jsonb, 'Galactose restriction', 'Galactosemia treatment: eliminate breast milk/formula containing lactose (source of galactose). Use soy-based formula. Even with treatment cataracts resolved but intellectual and ovarian function may be affected.'),
('4b9c4628-360f-5019-b967-54bf173a33b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment for maple syrup urine disease includes:', '["Low-phenylalanine diet metabolic pathway process","Glucose infusion metabolic pathway process","High-fat diet metabolic pathway process","Dietary restriction of BCAAs and thiamine supplementation","Galactose restriction metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Dietary restriction of BCAAs and thiamine supplementation', 'MSUD treatment: synthetic formula deficient in leucine isoleucine valine. Monitor plasma BCAA levels. Thiamine-responsive forms exist. Liver transplant is curative.'),
('036f9766-2b78-5d5c-9fdd-3bdff9a79521'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment for tyrosinemia type I uses:', '["Carnitine supplementation","Nitisinone (NTBC) which inhibits 4-HPPD","Low-phenylalanine diet","Biotin supplementation","Vitamin B12"]'::jsonb, 'Nitisinone (NTBC) which inhibits 4-HPPD', 'NTBC (2-(2-nitro-4-trifluoromethylbenzoyl)-1,3-cyclohexanedione) prevents accumulation of toxic fumarylacetoacetate dramatically improving outcomes in tyrosinemia I.'),
('8a38d8d7-e824-5848-be85-c9285d1a3ab4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment of Wilson disease includes:', '["Iron chelation metabolic pathway process","Copper supplementation metabolic pathway process","Vitamin C metabolic pathway process","Penicillamine trientine and zinc acetate","BAL only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Penicillamine trientine and zinc acetate', 'Wilson disease treatment: chelation (penicillamine trientine) removes copper. Zinc acetate blocks intestinal copper absorption and induces hepatic metallothionein. Liver transplant for severe disease.'),
('dc4c7d5e-8da0-53ae-b4ac-daac2cc08184'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment of hemochromatosis includes:', '["BAL","Zinc supplementation","Vitamin C supplementation","Copper chelation","Phlebotomy and iron chelation"]'::jsonb, 'Phlebotomy and iron chelation', 'Hemochromatosis: phlebotomy (remove 500 mL blood weekly until ferritin normalizes). Iron chelation if phlebotomy contraindicated (deferoxamine SC deferasirox oral). Avoid vitamin C (increases iron absorption).'),
('aa356418-4e0a-5808-ba5d-33ecc5b88fe2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Treatment of Gaucher disease includes:', '["Bone marrow transplant only metabolic pathway process","No treatment available metabolic pathway process","Gene therapy only metabolic pathway process","Enzyme replacement therapy (imiglucerase) and substrate reduction","Supportive care only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme replacement therapy (imiglucerase) and substrate reduction', 'Imiglucerase (recombinant glucocerebrosidase) is the standard ERT. Miglustat (substrate reduction) inhibits glucosylceramide synthase. ERT improves hematologic and visceral but not neurological manifestations.'),
('869cf9c4-ca8c-54e1-84c5-41c675df4d5e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment for Fabry disease uses:', '["Only supportive metabolic pathway process","No treatment metabolic pathway process","ERT only for Gaucher metabolic pathway process","Recombinant alpha-galactosidase (agalsidase) and migalastat","Substrate reduction only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Recombinant alpha-galactosidase (agalsidase) and migalastat', 'Agalsidase alfa/beta are ERT for Fabry disease. Migalastat (chaperone) can be used in amenable GLA mutations. ERT reduces pain stabilize kidney function and reduce cardiovascular events.'),
('699f6ba3-fab2-5f49-a68a-ae91b7e23ba3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment for Pompe disease uses:', '["Miglustat","Alglucosidase alfa","Agalsidase","Imiglucerase","ERT not available"]'::jsonb, 'Alglucosidase alfa', 'Alglucosidase alfa is ERT for Pompe disease. It improves survival in infantile-onset and stabilizes motor function in late-onset. Immune response can reduce efficacy.'),
('f724ff9a-5bf2-54d5-9074-856bfe284a8b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The drug for MPS I (Hurler Scheie) is:', '["Laronidase","Idursulfase","Galsulfase","Alglucosidase alfa","Elosulfase alfa"]'::jsonb, 'Laronidase', 'Laronidase (Aldurazyme) is ERT for MPS I. Given weekly IV. Improves respiratory function and mobility. Best results if initiated before significant disease progression.'),
('57d761a3-a2c7-596a-8274-a1669c1176c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Idursulfase treats:', '["Morquio syndrome","Maroteaux-Lamy","Hurler syndrome","Sanfilippo syndrome","Hunter syndrome"]'::jsonb, 'Hunter syndrome', 'Idursulfase (Elaprase) is ERT for MPS II. Weekly IV infusion. Improves respiratory function and joint mobility. Does not cross blood-brain barrier.'),
('312318fa-97a4-5869-9119-6d342f2348f1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Galsulfase treats:', '["MPS I","MPS IV","MPS II","Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome","MPS VII"]'::jsonb, 'Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome', 'Galsulfase (Naglazyme) is ERT for MPS VI (arylsulfatase B deficiency). Weekly IV infusion. Improves endurance and joint function.'),
('4ce627ed-8670-56ad-afa1-b7608a0a8f0d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Elosulfase alfa treats:', '["Morquio syndrome","MPS I","MPS II","MPS VI","MPS VII"]'::jsonb, 'Morquio syndrome', 'Elosulfase alfa (Vimizim) is ERT for MPS IVA (galactosamine-6-sulfatase deficiency). Weekly IV infusion. Improves endurance (6-minute walk test).'),
('afb92194-8f61-5ff4-ad52-e8082fa65172'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Vestronidase alfa treats:', '["MPS IV","MPS I","MPS II","MPS VI","MPS VII"]'::jsonb, 'MPS VII', 'Vestronidase alfa (Mepsevii) is ERT for MPS VII (beta-glucuronidase deficiency). Given IV every 4 weeks. Improves mobility and reduces hepatosplenomegaly.'),
('cf89af31-381a-5407-8d99-96f26c9b332f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Niemann-Pick type C (NPC) is a:', '["Sphingomyelinase deficiency","Sphingolipid storage disorder","Ganglioside storage disorder","Acid sphingomyelinase deficiency","Cholesterol trafficking disorder"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol trafficking disorder', 'NPC (autosomal recessive) impairs cholesterol trafficking from lysosomes. Features: vertical supranuclear gaze palsy ataxia dementia hepatosplenomegaly. Treated with miglustat (substrate reduction).'),
('4439255a-0d95-5d0a-9a30-c7747e695997'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Neuronal ceroid lipofuscinoses (Batten disease) are:', '["Glycogen storage diseases metabolic pathway process","Lysosomal storage disorders with autofluorescent lipopigment","Peroxisomal disorders metabolic pathway process","Sphingolipidoses only metabolic pathway process","Mucopolysaccharidoses metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Lysosomal storage disorders with autofluorescent lipopigment', 'NCLs are the most common inherited neurodegenerative disease in children. Accumulate lipofuscin-like material. Features: visual failure seizures cognitive decline. Multiple genetic forms (CLN1-14).'),
('8bf21883-5ecf-5eb3-a27c-a2dafd6c2e03'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Mucolipidosis II (I-cell disease) is caused by:', '["Beta-glucuronidase deficiency","Sulfatase deficiency","GNPTAB mutation","Iduronate sulfatase deficiency","Alpha-L-iduronidase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'GNPTAB mutation', 'I-cell disease: defective mannose-6-phosphate targeting of lysosomal enzymes. Features: severe Hurler-like phenotype with early onset coarse facies and intellectual disability. Lysosomal enzymes are elevated in serum.'),
('a2313ade-92cf-50b8-af36-bb41592b0319'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Multiple sulfatase deficiency (Austin disease) results from:', '["Individual sulfatase gene mutations","Sulfate transporter defect","Sulfatase excess","Sulfate synthesis defect","SUMF1 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'SUMF1 mutation', 'Multiple sulfatase deficiency causes accumulation of sulfated lipids and GAGs by impairing activation of all sulfatases. Features: combined features of MPS and metachromatic leukodystrophy.'),
('030c92ee-bb76-5cf3-ad21-011ef083c4c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Metachromatic leukodystrophy (MLD) is caused by:', '["Glucocerebrosidase deficiency","Sphingomyelinase deficiency","Arylsulfatase A deficiency","Galactocerebrosidase deficiency","Hexosaminidase A deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Arylsulfatase A deficiency', 'MLD (autosomal recessive ARSA) accumulates sulfatide in oligodendrocytes causing demyelination. Features: gait disturbance ataxia spasticity dementia. Late-infantile juvenile and adult forms.'),
('540bdca6-6aa1-5b6b-811a-32fb8a2b2b6f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Krabbe disease (globoid cell leukodystrophy) is caused by:', '["Sphingomyelinase deficiency","Glucocerebrosidase deficiency","Hexosaminidase A deficiency","Arylsulfatase A deficiency","Galactocerebrosidase (GALC) deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Galactocerebrosidase (GALC) deficiency', 'Krabbe disease (autosomal recessive GALC) accumulates galactocerebroside. Features: severe neurological deterioration hypertonicity seizures blindness and death in infancy. Hematopoietic stem cell transplant may help infantile form.'),
('f78e3d92-7ade-52f1-87fa-38922e178e26'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) is diagnosed by:', '["Galactocerebroside elevation","Sulfatide accumulation","Very long-chain fatty acid (VLCFA) elevation","Cholesterol accumulation","Lysosomal enzyme assay"]'::jsonb, 'Very long-chain fatty acid (VLCFA) elevation', 'X-ALD diagnosis: elevated VLCFA (C26:0 C24:0) in plasma and fibroblasts. ABCD1 mutation confirms. Newborn screening using C26:0 lysophosphatidylcholine (C26:0-LPC) is becoming available.'),
('5903bdbc-9903-5a52-bdbc-a855f17ceed2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Peroxisomal disorders are characterized by:', '["Accumulation of GAGs metabolic pathway process","Accumulation of sphingolipids metabolic pathway process","Accumulation of glycogen metabolic pathway process","Accumulation of cholesterol metabolic pathway process","Accumulation of VLCFA phytanic acid and bile acid intermediates"]'::jsonb, 'Accumulation of VLCFA phytanic acid and bile acid intermediates', 'Peroxisomal disorders (Zellweger spectrum X-ALD Refsum) cause accumulation of VLCFA phytanic acid pristanic acid and bile acid intermediates due to defective peroxisomal metabolism.'),
('dc865c4a-0a98-5d03-a691-825044ff534f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cholesterol ester storage disease (CESD) / Wolman disease is caused by:', '["ACAT deficiency","LCAT deficiency","LDL receptor deficiency","CETP deficiency","Lysosomal acid lipase (LIPA) deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Lysosomal acid lipase (LIPA) deficiency', 'Lysosomal acid lipase deficiency: Wolman (severe in infancy) with adrenal calcification hepatosplenomegaly. CESD (milder) causes hyperlipidemia and hepatic steatosis. Sebelipase alfa is ERT.'),
('1be78b3d-a46d-513c-b40c-b316a19ac9b7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Tangier disease is characterized by:', '["High VLDL metabolic pathway process","Severely low HDL and orange tonsils","Very high HDL metabolic pathway process","High LDL metabolic pathway process","High triglycerides metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Severely low HDL and orange tonsils', 'Tangier disease (ABCA1 deficiency autosomal codominant) impairs cholesterol efflux to ApoA-I causing very low HDL. Features: orange tonsils neuropathy hepatosplenomegaly.'),
('23b11990-79e5-533a-af6d-87a2807abcb7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Abetalipoproteinemia (Bassen-Kornzweig syndrome) is characterized by:', '["Very high LDL metabolic pathway process","Absence of chylomicrons VLDL and LDL with acanthocytosis","Normal lipoproteins metabolic pathway process","High VLDL metabolic pathway process","High HDL metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Absence of chylomicrons VLDL and LDL with acanthocytosis', 'Abetalipoproteinemia (MTP deficiency autosomal recessive) impairs apo B-containing lipoprotein assembly. Features: acanthocytosis retinitis pigmentosa ataxia fat malabsorption. Vitamins A E K supplementation helps.'),
('3284c1a2-9fd2-503e-86bd-2e7d0dff1ecd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Familial hypertriglyceridemia (type IV hyperlipoproteinemia) is associated with:', '["Increased LDL","Increased HDL","Increased VLDL","Increased IDL","Increased chylomicrons"]'::jsonb, 'Increased VLDL', 'Type IV: hepatic VLDL overproduction causing hypertriglyceridemia. Associated with insulin resistance obesity metabolic syndrome. Increased CVD risk.'),
('bc738944-8d4a-5a33-9aea-91a1222b0e2f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Type III hyperlipoproteinemia (dysbetalipoproteinemia) is caused by:', '["LCAT deficiency metabolic pathway process","Apolipoprotein E2/E2 genotype causing impaired IDL clearance","Apo B-100 defect metabolic pathway process","Lipoprotein lipase deficiency metabolic pathway process","LDL receptor deficiency metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Apolipoprotein E2/E2 genotype causing impaired IDL clearance', 'Type III: ApoE2 homozygosity impairs remnant clearance. Features: palmar xanthomas tuberoeruptive xanthomas. Associated with diabetes hypothyroidism. Elevated IDL (VLDL remnants).'),
('aa1f12a4-6cf4-5f42-b7ad-32a821c7b0d6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Lipoprotein(a) [Lp(a)] is an independent risk factor for:', '["Stroke only metabolic pathway process","Peripheral artery disease only metabolic pathway process","Hypertension only metabolic pathway process","Venous thrombosis metabolic pathway process","Atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease and aortic stenosis"]'::jsonb, 'Atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease and aortic stenosis', 'Lp(a) consists of LDL-like particle with Apo(a) attached. High Lp(a) (>50 mg/dL) increases ASCVD and calcific aortic stenosis risk. Levels are genetically determined and resistant to lifestyle modification.'),
('7c007531-00be-5626-b0e5-8ee849c5e4ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. PCSK9 gain-of-function mutations cause:', '["HDL deficiency metabolic pathway process","Hypocholesterolemia metabolic pathway process","Autosomal dominant hypercholesterolemia","Hypertriglyceridemia metabolic pathway process","Low LDL metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Autosomal dominant hypercholesterolemia', 'PCSK9 promotes LDL receptor degradation. Gain-of-function mutations reduce LDL receptors causing hypercholesterolemia. PCSK9 inhibitors (evolocumab alirocumab) are potent LDL-lowering drugs.'),
('6491efcd-9923-588d-9b10-92dcd49921e2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. Following angiography, a patient develops headache. Biochemical analysis shows abnormal renal function. PCSK9 loss-of-function mutations cause:', '["High LDL metabolic pathway process","Low LDL cholesterol and reduced CVD risk","Cardiomyopathy metabolic pathway process","High HDL metabolic pathway process","High triglycerides metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Low LDL cholesterol and reduced CVD risk', 'Loss-of-function PCSK9 mutations increase LDL receptors lowering LDL cholesterol by 40% and reducing CVD risk by 88%. This led to development of PCSK9 inhibitor drugs.'),
('bd141921-3b04-5dc2-82cc-196c93bf376e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Sitosterolemia (phytosterolemia) is caused by:', '["Apo B-100 defect","LCAT deficiency","HL deficiency","ABCG5/ABCG8 mutations","LDL receptor deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'ABCG5/ABCG8 mutations', 'Sitosterolemia (autosomal recessive) causes accumulation of plant sterols (sitosterol campesterol). Features: tendon xanthomas premature atherosclerosis and hemolytic anemia with stomatocytes.'),
('1cd5bee9-19fd-5fa0-88e8-25c0dc01593c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Lipodystrophies (familial partial) are associated with:', '["Obesity metabolic pathway process","Increased HDL metabolic pathway process","Normal metabolism metabolic pathway process","Hypoglycemia metabolic pathway process","Severe insulin resistance hypertriglyceridemia and fatty liver"]'::jsonb, 'Severe insulin resistance hypertriglyceridemia and fatty liver', 'Lipodystrophies involve selective fat loss causing leptin deficiency severe insulin resistance and hypertriglyceridemia. Acquired (HIV medications) or genetic (LMNA PPARG mutations).'),
('8b4708ec-7d2a-54bd-a6f2-fc51ac201643'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Metabolic syndrome treatment involves:', '["Only diet metabolic pathway process","Only exercise metabolic pathway process","Only bariatric surgery metabolic pathway process","Only drug therapy metabolic pathway process","Lifestyle modification and treating individual components"]'::jsonb, 'Lifestyle modification and treating individual components', 'Metabolic syndrome treatment: weight loss (5-10%) exercise smoking cessation. Drugs for hypertension dyslipidemia and diabetes as needed. Bariatric surgery for severe obesity.'),
('29bd9750-ed85-5b78-8abb-bcc27f3cc9f8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The ketogenic diet is used to treat:', '["Hyperlipidemia","Obesity","Refractory epilepsy","Type 2 diabetes","Hypertension"]'::jsonb, 'Refractory epilepsy', 'Ketogenic diet (high fat low carb) produces ketones which have anticonvulsant effects. Used for intractable epilepsy in children (GLUT1 deficiency Dravet syndrome). Side effects: ketoacidosis dyslipidemia growth issues.'),
('a5f2ea17-e603-5d84-9f9d-d99a8489b734'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Medium-chain triglyceride (MCT) ketogenic diet:', '["Provides more ketones per calorie than classic ketogenic diet","Causes more GI side effects metabolic pathway process","Is not used clinically metabolic pathway process","Is less effective metabolic pathway process","Has no advantages metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Provides more ketones per calorie than classic ketogenic diet', 'MCT ketogenic diet provides ~60% calories as MCT which are more ketogenic than LCT. Allows more carbohydrate and protein than classic diet. Used for epilepsy treatment.'),
('2a1c30cc-3f2a-5284-8400-c5b980af9cd1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hyperammonemia in urea cycle defects is treated with:', '["Protein supplementation metabolic pathway process","Lactulose only metabolic pathway process","Sodium benzoate sodium phenylacetate and arginine","Dialysis only metabolic pathway process","Rifaximin only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Sodium benzoate sodium phenylacetate and arginine', 'Intravenous sodium benzoate (binds glycine) and sodium phenylacetate (binds glutamine) provide alternative waste nitrogen excretion. Arginine replenishes urea cycle intermediates. Hemodialysis for severe hyperammonemia.'),
('072feb9e-3540-5b47-989d-af29e5fe3074'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Lactulose treats hepatic encephalopathy by:', '["Inhibiting urease","Increasing ammonia production","Acidifying the colon trapping NH4+ as ammonium","Blocking ammonia absorption","Directly binding ammonia"]'::jsonb, 'Acidifying the colon trapping NH4+ as ammonium', 'Lactulose (non-absorbable disaccharide) is fermented by colonic bacteria producing lactic acid lowering colonic pH. This traps NH3 as NH4+ (preventing absorption) and promotes its fecal excretion.'),
('0f0aee50-fe44-5978-8f77-067a29bbbbf5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Rifaximin (non-absorbable antibiotic) treats:', '["Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis metabolic pathway process","Hepatic encephalopathy by reducing gut ammonia-producing bacteria","Variceal bleeding metabolic pathway process","Ascites metabolic pathway process","Hepatorenal syndrome metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hepatic encephalopathy by reducing gut ammonia-producing bacteria', 'Rifaximin reduces ammonia-producing gut bacteria (especially urease producers). Used for hepatic encephalopathy prevention (with lactulose). Has minimal systemic absorption.'),
('9c0898b6-e84b-57be-a4dc-6d393e1e9e4b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Wilson disease screening includes:', '["Serum iron and ferritin metabolic pathway process","Complete blood count metabolic pathway process","Liver enzymes only metabolic pathway process","Copper supplementation test metabolic pathway process","24-hour urinary copper serum ceruloplasmin and Kayser-Fleischer rings"]'::jsonb, '24-hour urinary copper serum ceruloplasmin and Kayser-Fleischer rings', 'Diagnosis: low ceruloplasmin (<20 mg/dL) elevated 24-hr urinary copper (>100 mcg) Kayser-Fleischer rings on slit lamp. Liver biopsy for copper quantification if unclear.'),
('72983a97-e6f7-5548-b694-8fc8078a8a13'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hemochromatosis screening includes:', '["Serum iron only metabolic pathway process","Transferrin saturation and serum ferritin","TIBC only metabolic pathway process","Hemoglobin only metabolic pathway process","Liver enzymes only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Transferrin saturation and serum ferritin', 'Screening: transferrin saturation >45% and elevated ferritin. Confirm with HFE genotyping (C282Y/C282Y or C282Y/H63D). Liver biopsy for fibrosis assessment if needed.'),
('ab771d84-3e47-5b49-abf2-03189dbe960b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The treatment for acute porphyria attacks includes:', '["UV therapy metabolic pathway process","Phlebotomy only metabolic pathway process","Heme arginate glucose analgesia and avoiding precipitating drugs","Systemic steroids metabolic pathway process","Iron chelation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Heme arginate glucose analgesia and avoiding precipitating drugs', 'AIP treatment: IV heme arginate (Panhematin) provides negative feedback on ALA synthase. IV glucose (300 g/day) also represses ALAS1. Avoid porphyrinogenic drugs. Supportive care for pain nausea.'),
('7349887e-ab4f-5772-bdc1-2ea623e6de1f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment for PCT includes:', '["Glucose infusion metabolic pathway process","Avoid sunlight only metabolic pathway process","Iron supplementation metabolic pathway process","Phlebotomy and low-dose hydroxychloroquine","Heme arginate metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Phlebotomy and low-dose hydroxychloroquine', 'PCT treatment: phlebotomy (reduce iron burden) and low-dose hydroxychloroquine (mobilizes hepatic porphyrins for excretion). Avoid triggers (alcohol estrogen HCV iron). Vitamin C may help.'),
('5fc9e88b-f1e7-5069-8089-6d952480c41f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acute porphyria can be precipitated by:', '["Paracetamol only metabolic pathway process","Only alcohol metabolic pathway process","Only progesterone metabolic pathway process","Penicillin only metabolic pathway process","Barbiturates sulfonamides estrogen progesterone and alcohol"]'::jsonb, 'Barbiturates sulfonamides estrogen progesterone and alcohol', 'Porphyrinogenic drugs induce CYP450 (ALAS1 induction). Avoid: barbiturates sulfonamides phenytoin valproate carbamazepine rifampin estrogen progesterone. Safe: penicillin opioids aspirin.'),
('330a2855-3153-5660-9870-14a3769c18c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The ''glucose effect'' in AIP refers to:', '["Glucose increasing heme synthesis","Glucose binding porphobilinogen","Glucose directly inhibiting PBG deaminase","Carbohydrate loading repressing ALAS1 transcription","Glucose decreasing CYP450"]'::jsonb, 'Carbohydrate loading repressing ALAS1 transcription', 'Glucose/carbohydrate represses ALAS1 transcription via the glucose response element. This reduces porphyrin precursor production. Mechanism involves PGC-1alpha deacetylation.'),
('5751bc99-cce2-5b60-aa62-d95c6eb5a1f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The treatment for G6PD deficiency is:', '["Vitamin B12 metabolic pathway process","Folic acid metabolic pathway process","Iron supplementation metabolic pathway process","Blood transfusion only metabolic pathway process","Avoidance of oxidative triggers and supportive care"]'::jsonb, 'Avoidance of oxidative triggers and supportive care', 'G6PD deficiency: no specific treatment. Avoid oxidant drugs fava beans. Treat acute hemolysis with supportive care (hydration monitoring). Blood transfusion for severe anemia. Splenectomy rarely needed.'),
('2f1c6d0b-0bb6-5df8-b503-eab762570fce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'G6PD deficiency testing should NOT be performed:', '["On newborns","On any patient","On males","During acute hemolysis","On females"]'::jsonb, 'During acute hemolysis', 'During acute hemolysis young RBCs (reticulocytes) have higher G6PD activity causing false normal results. Wait 2-3 months after hemolysis for testing. Females may have false normal due to X-inactivation.'),
('2d71e374-21be-5b03-978d-36f43d2d7e42'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Galactosemia causes damage through accumulation of:', '["Galactonate metabolic pathway process","Galactose only metabolic pathway process","Galactitol (from aldose reductase) and galactose-1-phosphate","Galactonate only metabolic pathway process","UDP-galactose metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Galactitol (from aldose reductase) and galactose-1-phosphate', 'Galactitol is osmotically active causing cataracts (lens swelling). Galactose-1-phosphate accumulates causing liver/kidney/brain damage. Treatment: galactose-restricted diet.'),
('6b48e4da-7e96-52ca-92de-0af550db39b9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acute hepatic porphyria diagnosis involves:', '["Elevated bilirubin","Elevated RBC protoporphyrin","Low heme levels","Elevated fecal porphyrins","Elevated urine ALA and PBG"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated urine ALA and PBG', 'AIP diagnosis: elevated urine ALA and PBG during attacks. Watson-Schwartz test (qualitative) and quantitative ALA/PBG by HPLC. Asymptomatic family screening with enzyme assay or genetic testing.'),
('21de6497-048c-5450-850e-7491861b5cb2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Erythropoietic protoporphyria (EPP) diagnosis:', '["Elevated RBC protoporphyrin (free) and plasma porphyrins","Elevated fecal porphyrins metabolic pathway process","Elevated urine ALA metabolic pathway process","Elevated urine PBG metabolic pathway process","Elevated bilirubin metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Elevated RBC protoporphyrin (free) and plasma porphyrins', 'EPP: marked elevation of free protoporphyrin in RBCs plasma and feces. Urine porphyrins are normal (unlike other porphyrias). Photosensitivity with immediate pain swelling.'),
('3deceb6a-9d1d-5ff0-8de4-d530fd2f2a79'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Variegate porphyria (VP) is characterized by:', '["No symptoms metabolic pathway process","Acute attacks only metabolic pathway process","Acute attacks with photosensitivity and elevated fecal porphyrins","Photosensitivity only metabolic pathway process","Neurologic only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Acute attacks with photosensitivity and elevated fecal porphyrins', 'VP (PPOX deficiency) common in South Africa. Features: acute attacks plus photosensitivity. Diagnosis: elevated fecal protoporphyrin and coproporphyrin with characteristic plasma fluorescence peak at 626 nm.'),
('c67f718f-ede7-5af6-86de-a1fdeadaeedd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hereditary coproporphyria (HCP) involves:', '["PPOX deficiency","Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","Ferrochelatase deficiency","PBG deaminase deficiency","Coproporphyrinogen oxidase deficiency"]'::jsonb, 'Coproporphyrinogen oxidase deficiency', 'HCP (CPOX deficiency) presents with acute attacks and sometimes photosensitivity. Diagnosis: elevated fecal coproporphyrin III. Urine ALA and PBG elevated during attacks.'),
('7bfeafc4-2b4a-5a88-866c-08c6916bee78'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Lead poisoning inhibits which heme synthesis enzymes?', '["ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase","Biliverdin reductase","Uroporphyrinogen synthase and coproporphyrinogen oxidase","ALA synthase and PBG deaminase","Heme oxygenase"]'::jsonb, 'ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase', 'Lead inhibits sulfhydryl-dependent ALA dehydratase (ALAD) and ferrochelatase. This increases ALA and zinc protoporphyrin (ZPP). ZPP is a screening test for lead exposure.'),
('6d0ab47f-5e5b-5c55-93ce-18c126172641'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The CDC reference level for lead in children is:', '["3.5 mcg/dL","45 mcg/dL","10 mcg/dL","70 mcg/dL","20 mcg/dL"]'::jsonb, '3.5 mcg/dL', 'CDC uses 3.5 mcg/dL as reference level (top 2.5% of population). Chelation recommended at >45 mcg/dL. Lead causes neurodevelopmental impairment even at low levels.'),
('31be31b7-d2dc-5f5a-b5a4-5bbe370ca5d1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Chelation therapy for lead poisoning uses:', '["Succimer (DMSA) oral EDTA IV and BAL IM","Dimercaprol only metabolic pathway process","Penicillamine only metabolic pathway process","Deferoxamine only metabolic pathway process","EDTA only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Succimer (DMSA) oral EDTA IV and BAL IM', 'Succimer (oral DMSA) is first-line for moderate lead poisoning. Severe poisoning: IV CaNa2-EDTA with BAL (dimercaprol) to prevent redistribution. BAL alone before EDTA for encephalopathy.'),
('243738ab-918f-5585-a6f0-5aa7b0bd15c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Arsenic poisoning features:', '["Nephrotoxicity only metabolic pathway process","Hepatotoxicity only metabolic pathway process","Flushing and wheezing metabolic pathway process","Rice-water diarrhea vomiting ''garlic breath'' and peripheral neuropathy","Cardiotoxicity only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Rice-water diarrhea vomiting ''garlic breath'' and peripheral neuropathy', 'Acute arsenic: vomiting rice-water stools hypotension. Chronic: skin hyperpigmentation hyperkeratosis peripheral neuropathy Mee''s lines. Treatment: dimercaprol (BAL) or succimer (DMSA).'),
('11b5eec9-9d9d-5f1c-a55f-b6aeb5520204'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Mercury poisoning (acute inorganic) causes:', '["Cardiac arrhythmia metabolic pathway process","Hematologic toxicity metabolic pathway process","Metallic taste stomatitis colitis and renal failure","Respiratory failure metabolic pathway process","Liver failure metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Metallic taste stomatitis colitis and renal failure', 'Inorganic mercury (HgCl2) ingestion: caustic gastroenteritis acute tubular necrosis. Chronic elemental mercury: tremor erethism (neuropsychiatric) acrodynia in children. Chelation: BAL DMSA DMPS.'),
('a84e2fd5-d7b9-5bb1-ab07-22bb773f39a3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Organic mercury poisoning (Minamata disease) causes:', '["Respiratory disease","Renal disease","Hematologic disease","Neurological symptoms","Liver disease"]'::jsonb, 'Neurological symptoms', 'Methylmercury (from contaminated fish) causes paresthesia ataxia dysarthria concentric visual field constriction. Crosses placenta causing cerebral palsy-like symptoms. Chelation is less effective.'),
('6b438d58-55f2-5800-a92c-57f20da3a668'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acetaminophen toxicity mechanism:', '["Renal failure only metabolic pathway process","NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine) depletes glutathione causing hepatotoxicity","Direct liver necrosis metabolic pathway process","Respiratory depression metabolic pathway process","Cardiac toxicity metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine) depletes glutathione causing hepatotoxicity', 'Acetaminophen is metabolized by CYP2E1/3A4 to NAPQI. Normally conjugated by glutathione. Overdose depletes glutathione allowing NAPQI to bind hepatocyte proteins causing necrosis.'),
('966dba20-d501-577b-8b29-46312de347ef'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acetaminophen toxicity treatment:', '["N-acetylcysteine (NAC) within 8-10 hours","Forced diuresis only","Flumazenil","Activated charcoal only","Hemodialysis only"]'::jsonb, 'N-acetylcysteine (NAC) within 8-10 hours', 'NAC (IV or oral) replenishes glutathione directly conjugates NAPQI and enhances sulfation/methylation. Most effective within 8 hours of ingestion. NAC also effective later (delayed benefit).'),
('2345b0cc-9e0b-556b-9028-ed368120c9b7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The Rumack-Matthew nomogram for acetaminophen:', '["Predicts renal failure metabolic pathway process","Plots serum level vs time to predict hepatotoxicity risk","Determines liver size metabolic pathway process","Predicts survival metabolic pathway process","Determines need for dialysis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Plots serum level vs time to predict hepatotoxicity risk', 'The nomogram plots acetaminophen concentration vs time after ingestion. Above the ''treatment line'' indicates high hepatotoxicity risk warranting NAC therapy. Line starts at 150 mcg/mL at 4 hours.'),
('71711892-1d4f-5127-bbcf-62fc942d55c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Salicylate poisoning features:', '["Bradycardia only metabolic pathway process","Seizures only metabolic pathway process","Tinnitus hyperventilation mixed acid-base disturbance hypoglycemia","Hypotension only metabolic pathway process","Coma only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Tinnitus hyperventilation mixed acid-base disturbance hypoglycemia', 'Salicylate toxicity: tinnitus respiratory alkalosis (direct stimulation) then metabolic acidosis uncoupling. Children: hypoglycemia hyperthermia. Treatment: activated charcoal urinary alkalinization hemodialysis.'),
('9c63f4b5-3410-5496-b528-a2e365cfc1ca'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Ethylene glycol poisoning features:', '["Hematologic toxicity metabolic pathway process","Cardiotoxicity metabolic pathway process","Hepatotoxicity metabolic pathway process","Neurotoxicity only metabolic pathway process","Calcium oxalate crystals oxaluria metabolic acidosis and renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Calcium oxalate crystals oxaluria metabolic acidosis and renal failure', 'Ethylene glycol (antifreeze) metabolized by ADH to glycolate oxalate. Calcium oxalate crystals in urine cause acute kidney injury. Hypocalcemia can cause tetany and QT prolongation.'),
('ac37db20-1418-5c20-b175-00549c7dea2e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Methanol poisoning features:', '["Renal failure metabolic pathway process","Pancreatitis metabolic pathway process","Visual disturbances formic acid accumulation metabolic acidosis","Cardiotoxicity metabolic pathway process","Hepatotoxicity metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Visual disturbances formic acid accumulation metabolic acidosis', 'Methanol metabolized to formaldehyde then formic acid (toxic). Retinal toxicity causes blindness (optic atrophy). Treatment: fomepizole/ethanol and hemodialysis.'),
('b6cc6bd4-bba2-59ea-a0a5-8419b91783c4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Iron poisoning (acute) causes:', '["Vomiting GI bleeding metabolic acidosis and hepatotoxicity","Bradycardia only metabolic pathway process","Pancreatitis only metabolic pathway process","Renal failure only metabolic pathway process","Hypotension only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Vomiting GI bleeding metabolic acidosis and hepatotoxicity', 'Iron poisoning stages: 1) GI irritation/bleeding 2) apparent recovery 3) metabolic acidosis shock liver necrosis 4) GI scarring. Treatment: deferoxamine IV. Severe: whole bowel irrigation.'),
('053d0fd3-03ed-52d4-b53a-2935f43606e6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The cyanide antidote kit includes:', '["Flumazenil only metabolic pathway process","Naloxone only metabolic pathway process","Atropine only metabolic pathway process","Amyl nitrite sodium nitrite and sodium thiosulfate","BAL only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Amyl nitrite sodium nitrite and sodium thiosulfate', 'Nitrites induce methemoglobinemia which binds cyanide. Thiosulfate provides sulfur for rhodanese to convert cyanide to thiocyanate (excreted in urine). Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) is also used.'),
('6ac65a02-18b4-5306-bde9-ed8b335882a6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment of organophosphate poisoning:', '["Flumazenil","Atropine and pralidoxime","Dantrolene","Bicarbonate","Naloxone"]'::jsonb, 'Atropine and pralidoxime', 'Atropine blocks muscarinic receptors (dries secretions reverses bradycardia). Pralidoxime reactivates AChE by removing the phosphate group. Give atropine first then pralidoxime within 24 hours.'),
('05398149-5198-5ea7-9d47-1a7c48e86e85'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Atropine is used in organophosphate poisoning to:', '["Bind organophosphates metabolic pathway process","Promote excretion metabolic pathway process","Reactivate AChE metabolic pathway process","Induce vomiting metabolic pathway process","Block muscarinic acetylcholine receptors"]'::jsonb, 'Block muscarinic acetylcholine receptors', 'Atropine is a competitive muscarinic antagonist countering excess acetylcholine. It reverses salivation bronchosecretion miosis bradycardia but NOT nicotinic effects (muscle weakness).'),
('d8a7bedc-d2e0-5ea3-926d-e9d41492f6ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pralidoxime (2-PAM) in organophosphate poisoning:', '["Binds organophosphates metabolic pathway process","Reactivates AChE by removing the phosphoryl group","Blocks muscarinic receptors metabolic pathway process","Induces vomiting metabolic pathway process","Promotes diuresis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Reactivates AChE by removing the phosphoryl group', 'Pralidoxime (oxime) reactivates AChE if given early before ''aging'' (dealkylation of the phosphoryl-AChE complex). After aging (hours) AChE cannot be reactivated. Effective for most but not all OPs.'),
('18aafaa9-4c9a-565e-a0c4-e5313b06806b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The ''intermediate syndrome'' in organophosphate poisoning:', '["Cardiac arrhythmia metabolic pathway process","Weakness of proximal muscles neck flexors and respiratory muscles","Coma metabolic pathway process","Cholinergic crisis metabolic pathway process","Seizure metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Weakness of proximal muscles neck flexors and respiratory muscles', 'Intermediate syndrome (24-96 h after acute OP poisoning) involves nicotinic receptor dysfunction causing proximal muscle weakness neck flexor weakness and respiratory paralysis requiring ventilation.'),
('d86f60c6-8806-5f9c-84de-df8f6bc8b235'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment of methemoglobinemia:', '["Methylene blue IV","N-acetylcysteine","BAL","EDTA","Deferoxamine"]'::jsonb, 'Methylene blue IV', 'Methylene blue reduces methemoglobin (Fe3+) back to hemoglobin (Fe2+) via NADPH-methemoglobin reductase. Contraindicated in G6PD deficiency (can cause hemolysis). Severe cases: exchange transfusion.'),
('cf0f892c-6988-5d27-b3c1-5a15a0d4a9c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Malignant hyperthermia (MH) trigger includes:', '["Opioids metabolic pathway process","Local anesthetics metabolic pathway process","Propofol metabolic pathway process","Succinylcholine and volatile anesthetics","Benzodiazepines metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Succinylcholine and volatile anesthetics', 'MH is a pharmacogenetic disorder (RYR1 mutation) causing uncontrolled Ca2+ release from sarcoplasmic reticulum. Features: hyperthermia rigidity hyperkalemia acidosis. Treatment: dantrolene (blocks RyR1).'),
('c41732ed-df8e-50e6-8ace-1c37c3e98083'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) is caused by:', '["Norepinephrine deficiency","Serotonin excess","Dopamine receptor blockade","Acetylcholine excess","GABA excess"]'::jsonb, 'Dopamine receptor blockade', 'NMS: severe muscle rigidity hyperthermia autonomic instability altered mental status from antipsychotics. Treatment: dantrolene bromocriptine supportive care. Differentiate from serotonin syndrome.'),
('1853b7c9-ef29-505e-83d3-d92a0a11d878'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Serotonin syndrome is caused by:', '["Dopamine blockade","Antihistamines","Serotonergic drug excess","GABA excess","Acetylcholine excess"]'::jsonb, 'Serotonergic drug excess', 'Serotonin syndrome features: clonus hyperreflexia hyperthermia agitation. The Hunter criteria include clonus (spontaneous inducible ocular) plus agitation diaphoresis tremor hyperreflexia.'),
('9fb28de2-b994-57a7-aa89-e23a722fda86'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment of serotonin syndrome:', '["Flumazenil metabolic pathway process","Naloxone metabolic pathway process","Dantrolene metabolic pathway process","Bromocriptine metabolic pathway process","Cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist) and supportive care"]'::jsonb, 'Cyproheptadine (5-HT2A antagonist) and supportive care', 'Mild serotonin syndrome: benzodiazepines withdrawal offending drug. Moderate-severe: cyproheptadine (PO). Severe hyperthermia: paralysis intubation. Do NOT use bromocriptine (worsens serotonin syndrome).'),
('71c864a4-f36c-55de-8769-527bed13fead'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) type II:', '["Consumption coagulopathy metabolic pathway process","Hemolysis metabolic pathway process","Decreased platelet production metabolic pathway process","Immune-mediated IgG against PF4-heparin complex causing thrombosis","Direct platelet aggregation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Immune-mediated IgG against PF4-heparin complex causing thrombosis', 'HIT: antibodies activate platelets via FcgammaRIIa causing thrombocytopenia and thrombosis (venous > arterial). Treat with direct thrombin inhibitor (argatroban bivalirudin). Do NOT give platelets (increases thrombosis).'),
('1b28bbaa-f2ab-544f-9f0f-b1fa85b3323c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Treatment for HIT requires:', '["Steroids metabolic pathway process","Stop heparin start non-heparin anticoagulant","Increase heparin dose metabolic pathway process","Platelet transfusion metabolic pathway process","Warfarin alone metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Stop heparin start non-heparin anticoagulant', 'HIT management: stop ALL heparin. Start alternative anticoagulant (argatroban bivalirudin fondaparinux). Warfarin should be started only after platelet recovery. Avoid platelet transfusions.'),
('823a91cd-2a3b-5bb7-97ea-3b0ed5e66f59'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) treatment:', '["Warfarin metabolic pathway process","NSAIDs metabolic pathway process","Steroids IVIG and TPO receptor agonists","Heparin metabolic pathway process","Platelet transfusion metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Steroids IVIG and TPO receptor agonists', 'ITP: autoimmune antibody-mediated platelet destruction. First-line: corticosteroids IVIG anti-D (Rh+). Second-line: TPO agonists (eltrombopag romiplostim) rituximab splenectomy.'),
('1d04ee6d-4f66-5345-8a6f-c868d9c1d032'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is caused by:', '["Clotting factor deficiency metabolic pathway process","Platelet antibody metabolic pathway process","Protein C deficiency metabolic pathway process","ADAMTS13 deficiency (acquired or congenital) preventing von Willebrand factor cleavage","Antithrombin deficiency metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'ADAMTS13 deficiency (acquired or congenital) preventing von Willebrand factor cleavage', 'TTP: severe ADAMTS13 deficiency causes ultra-large VWF multimers causing microvascular thrombosis. Pentad: thrombocytopenia microangiopathic hemolytic anemia fever renal dysfunction neurological symptoms.'),
('9bf36973-be92-5eb6-a468-c5b4793b5310'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'TTP treatment:', '["Plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) and corticosteroids","Heparin metabolic pathway process","Aspirin only metabolic pathway process","Warfarin metabolic pathway process","Platelet transfusion metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma exchange (plasmapheresis) and corticosteroids', 'Plasma exchange is life-saving in TTP (removes antibodies replaces ADAMTS13). Corticosteroids for autoimmune. Rituximab for refractory. Avoid platelet transfusion (can worsen thrombosis).'),
('03a0d4ca-13f1-53ad-8526-ed70ea30f72b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) management:', '["Plasma exchange metabolic pathway process","Heparin metabolic pathway process","Rituximab metabolic pathway process","Corticosteroids metabolic pathway process","Supportive care (hydration) antibiotics for STEC avoid antimotility drugs"]'::jsonb, 'Supportive care (hydration) antibiotics for STEC avoid antimotility drugs', 'Typical HUS (STEC): supportive care hydration. Avoid antibiotics (increase Shiga toxin release) and antimotility drugs. Eculizumab for atypical HUS (complement dysregulation).'),
('8ce6980d-dbbd-5520-83ef-7faa2ffc3fcf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Von Willebrand disease (VWD) treatment:', '["Thrombolytics metabolic pathway process","Aspirin metabolic pathway process","Heparin metabolic pathway process","Desmopressin (DDAVP) and VWF-containing concentrates","Warfarin metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Desmopressin (DDAVP) and VWF-containing concentrates', 'VWD: type 1 responds to DDAVP (releases endothelial VWF). Types 2 and 3 need VWF-containing factor concentrates. Cryoprecipitate is second-line (infectious risk). Avoid aspirin.'),
('058031f8-add3-54d4-8eab-5c3d93042bd9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hemophilia A treatment:', '["Factor IX concentrates","Platelets","Cryoprecipitate","Fresh frozen plasma","Factor VIII concentrates"]'::jsonb, 'Factor VIII concentrates', 'Hemophilia A: treat with factor VIII concentrates. Mild disease: DDAVP (releases endogenous factor VIII and VWF). Develop inhibitors (antibodies) in ~30% of severe cases.'),
('dad2cef4-bc93-542d-b085-839e5e020140'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hemophilia B (Christmas disease) treatment:', '["Cryoprecipitate","Platelets","Factor IX concentrates","Factor VIII concentrates","DDAVP"]'::jsonb, 'Factor IX concentrates', 'Hemophilia B (factor IX deficiency) treated with factor IX concentrates. Recombinant factors are available. DDAVP does NOT affect factor IX levels. Inhibitors develop less frequently than hemophilia A.'),
('bccf13f1-731d-5976-be24-6ebdf269a0b8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Warfarin reversal agents:', '["Factor VIII only","Cryoprecipitate only","FFP only","Platelets only","Vitamin K (oral/IV) and PCC"]'::jsonb, 'Vitamin K (oral/IV) and PCC', 'Warfarin reversal: for high INR without bleeding: hold warfarin give low-dose oral vitamin K. For major bleeding: IV vitamin K + 4-factor PCC (immediate reversal). FFP is alternative if PCC unavailable.'),
('dd9705a4-f233-5f03-ba2b-02c95bc5126b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Heparin reversal agent:', '["Vitamin K","Tranexamic acid","FFP","PCC","Protamine sulfate"]'::jsonb, 'Protamine sulfate', 'Protamine (basic protein) binds acidic heparin forming inactive complexes. 1 mg neutralizes ~100 U heparin. Monitor aPTT. Side effects: hypotension bradycardia anaphylaxis (fish allergy).'),
('eec2b314-43d1-5cd8-a25d-7f94b7e2e580'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC) reversal:', '["PCC metabolic pathway process","FFP metabolic pathway process","Andexanet alfa (for factor Xa inhibitors) and idarucizumab","Vitamin K metabolic pathway process","Protamine metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Andexanet alfa (for factor Xa inhibitors) and idarucizumab', 'Idarucizumab (Praxbind) is a monoclonal antibody fragment reversing dabigatran (direct thrombin inhibitor). Andexanet alfa (Andexxa) is a recombinant factor Xa decoy reversing rivaroxaban apixaban edoxaban.'),
('9c2fe4d5-0c90-5bb3-a808-22b74995eae3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Thrombolytic therapy (alteplase tPA) is used for:', '["DIC metabolic pathway process","Bleeding disorders metabolic pathway process","Hemophilia metabolic pathway process","Acute ischemic stroke myocardial infarction and massive PE","Venous thrombosis prophylaxis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Acute ischemic stroke myocardial infarction and massive PE', 'tPA (alteplase) converts plasminogen to plasmin dissolving clots. Time-sensitive: stroke within 4.5 hours MI within 12 hours. Contraindicated in recent surgery bleeding history hypertension.'),
('5ee7b6fc-a4e8-51fd-81c9-c87ad03234cc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Tranexamic acid (TXA) mechanism:', '["Antiplatelet","Blocks plasminogen activation","Vitamin K antagonist","Anticoagulant","Activates plasminogen"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks plasminogen activation', 'TXA is a lysine analog that blocks plasminogen binding to fibrin inhibiting fibrinolysis. Used for trauma (CRASH-2) menorrhagia dental procedures in hemophilia and postpartum hemorrhage.'),
('e83a3d05-e95b-5916-b0d4-7d5d4d4923c4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Aminocaproic acid (EACA) is:', '["Procoagulant","Antiplatelet","Anticoagulant","Thrombolytic","An antifibrinolytic"]'::jsonb, 'An antifibrinolytic', 'EACA is an older antifibrinolytic. Used for: post-prostatectomy bleeding subarachnoid hemorrhage and dental bleeding in hemophilia. Contraindicated in DIC (can cause thrombosis).'),
('11a34cc1-ff58-57cc-9f08-882e758d5fe4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Leukemia diagnosis uses:', '["Only peripheral smear metabolic pathway process","Only complete blood count metabolic pathway process","Only imaging metabolic pathway process","Flow cytometry cytogenetics and molecular testing","Only bone marrow biopsy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Flow cytometry cytogenetics and molecular testing', 'Leukemia diagnosis: CBC with differential bone marrow biopsy flow cytometry (immunophenotyping) cytogenetics (karyotype FISH) and molecular analysis (PCR NGS) for classification.'),
('ad40b7ee-3361-5540-a041-8e063005c3fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acute promyelocytic leukemia (APML) is characterized by:', '["t(8;21) RUNX1-RUNX1T1","t(15;17) PML-RARalpha fusion","inv(16) CBFB-MYH11","t(9;22) BCR-ABL","t"]'::jsonb, 't(15;17) PML-RARalpha fusion', 'APML with coagulation (DIC). PML-RARalpha blocks differentiation at promyelocyte stage. All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) and arsenic trioxide induce differentiation achieving high cure rates.'),
('47d56195-0641-5729-9748-315830af5b74'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is characterized by:', '["t","t(9;22) BCR-ABL1","t","t","inv"]'::jsonb, 't(9;22) BCR-ABL1', 'CML: BCR-ABL fusion causes constitutive tyrosine kinase activity. Chronic phase responsive to tyrosine kinase inhibitors (imatinib). Accelerated/blast crisis have poor prognosis.'),
('15a5f213-2ae8-5d97-bf94-461c17bf205b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) in children features:', '["t(9;22) poor prognosis","t","inv","t(12;21) ETV6-RUNX1 good prognosis","t"]'::jsonb, 't(12;21) ETV6-RUNX1 good prognosis', 'Pediatric ALL: ETV6-RUNX1 (~25% good prognosis). High hyperdiploidy (>50) favorable. MLL rearrangements (infant ALL) poor. Treatment: multi-agent chemotherapy risk-adapted.'),
('1e7ea4a9-0daa-5e92-954b-8965f851f90d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Multiple myeloma features:', '["Polyclonal gammopathy metabolic pathway process","No bone lesions metabolic pathway process","Normal bone marrow metabolic pathway process","Normal immunoglobulins metabolic pathway process","Monoclonal gammopathy lytic bone lesions hypercalcemia renal failure"]'::jsonb, 'Monoclonal gammopathy lytic bone lesions hypercalcemia renal failure', 'Myeloma: malignant plasma cells producing monoclonal immunoglobulin (M protein). CRAB: hyperCalcemia Renal failure Anemia Bone lesions. Diagnosis: serum/urine electrophoresis bone marrow biopsy.'),
('3fe25407-2c3c-5517-8ada-bcbdd235c21b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Amyloidosis (AL type) is associated with:', '["Abeta2 microglobulin","All types equally","Transthyretin amyloid","Monoclonal light chain deposition","AA amyloid"]'::jsonb, 'Monoclonal light chain deposition', 'AL amyloidosis: light chains (lambda > kappa) misfold as beta-pleated sheets. Features: nephrotic syndrome cardiomyopathy hepatomegaly macrophagesia periorbital purpura. Congo red staining shows apple-green birefringence.'),
('9d7b1663-c340-519c-84b1-d30899127a1d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hereditary amyloidosis (ATTR) involves:', '["Light chains","Abeta2 microglobulin","AA amyloid","Transthyretin (TTR) gene mutations","Prion protein"]'::jsonb, 'Transthyretin (TTR) gene mutations', 'ATTR amyloidosis: mutant or wild-type TTR deposits. Familial amyloid polyneuropathy (FAP): progressive neuropathy cardiomyopathy. Wild-type ATTR (senile cardiac amyloidosis): restrictive cardiomyopathy in elderly.'),
('c4e30ac3-612d-506a-bd65-7d8da84aba91'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Cystic fibrosis diagnosis by:', '["Pancreatic function test only","Lung biopsy only","Liver biopsy only","Clinical symptoms only","Sweat chloride >60 mmol/L and CFTR mutation analysis"]'::jsonb, 'Sweat chloride >60 mmol/L and CFTR mutation analysis', 'CF diagnosis: sweat chloride >60 mmol/L (two tests) or identification of two CFTR mutations with clinical features. Newborn screening using immunoreactive trypsinogen (IRT).'),
('0588e4c8-16df-53e0-9b32-497c8e524667'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Primary ciliary dyskinesia (Kartagener syndrome) features:', '["Only situs inversus metabolic pathway process","Only hearing loss metabolic pathway process","Recurrent sinusitis bronchiectasis situs inversus and infertility","Only infertility metabolic pathway process","Only respiratory infections metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Recurrent sinusitis bronchiectasis situs inversus and infertility', 'PCD (autosomal recessive dynein defect) impairs mucociliary clearance. Features: chronic sinusitis bronchiectasis situs inversus (50%) male infertility. Diagnosis: electron microscopy of cilia nasal nitric oxide.'),
('6f2e21dc-302f-5c34-8123-24577e780aec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency diagnosis:', '["Low serum alpha-1-antitrypsin level and PI*ZZ genotype","Normal AAT level metabolic pathway process","Increased elastase activity metabolic pathway process","Increased trypsin activity metabolic pathway process","High AAT level metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Low serum alpha-1-antitrypsin level and PI*ZZ genotype', 'AAT deficiency diagnosis: low serum AAT (<50 mg/dL) and protease inhibitor (PI) genotyping. PI*ZZ is most common severe form. Lung and liver disease risk varies by genotype.'),
('bb2fccab-1ac1-5726-9883-fc472f0718b5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Alpha-1-antitrypsin treatment:', '["Augmentation therapy with purified human AAT","Antibiotics only metabolic pathway process","Liver transplant only metabolic pathway process","Inhaled steroids only metabolic pathway process","Bronchodilators only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Augmentation therapy with purified human AAT', 'Intravenous AAT augmentation (Prolastin Zemaira) slows emphysema progression in PI*ZZ patients by increasing antiprotease activity in lungs. Does not treat liver disease. Liver transplant for cirrhosis.'),
('4aeb1903-d635-5c7e-bf0a-7ea7e8e17851'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Hemochromatosis treatment with phlebotomy:', '["No treatment needed","Remove 500 mL blood weekly until ferritin <50 ng/mL","Remove 500 mL monthly","Remove 100 mL weekly","Lifelong maintenance phlebotomy only"]'::jsonb, 'Remove 500 mL blood weekly until ferritin <50 ng/mL', 'Initial: phlebotomy 500 mL weekly till ferritin <50 TSAT <50%. Maintenance: phlebotomy every 2-4 months to keep ferritin 50-100. Improves survival and organ function.'),
('654392cb-311d-5453-bad0-9a963d5c34c6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Wilson disease treatment monitoring:', '["24-hour urinary copper and serum free copper","Kayser-Fleischer rings only","Liver enzymes only","Serum ceruloplasmin only","Neurologic exam only"]'::jsonb, '24-hour urinary copper and serum free copper', 'Monitor: 24-hr urinary copper (goal >200 mcg/day on chelation) serum free copper (non-ceruloplasmin bound). Aim for free copper <10 mcg/dL. Neurologic and liver function improvement.'),
('78011067-69fe-51cb-a23c-bc030c55b0e7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Menkes disease treatment:', '["Trientine","Zinc acetate","Copper histidine injections","Penicillamine","No treatment"]'::jsonb, 'Copper histidine injections', 'Copper-histidine parenteral therapy bypasses intestinal copper transport defect. Efficacy is limited especially if neurological damage is established. Best if started prenatally or early neonatal period.'),
('f9840100-4aa8-514b-bb6b-3d43f6712031'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Biotin therapy for biotinidase deficiency:', '["5-20 mg oral biotin daily","Folate","Carnitine","Vitamin B12","Low biotin diet"]'::jsonb, '5-20 mg oral biotin daily', 'Biotinidase deficiency treatment: high-dose oral biotin (5-20 mg/day) is life-saving. Rapidly reverses metabolic acidosis skin rash and seizures. Neurologic damage may be irreversible if treated late.'),
('f7f923d2-1661-55a6-afa6-fde77949b170'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Biotin therapy for holocarboxylase synthetase deficiency:', '["High-dose biotin","Carnitine","Biotin restriction","Vitamin B12","Low biotin diet"]'::jsonb, 'High-dose biotin', 'HCS deficiency requires higher biotin doses (10-40 mg/day) than biotinidase deficiency. Biotin supplementation rapidly improves metabolic acidosis and skin lesions. Some patients need protein restriction.'),
('97ad71cd-dde6-58cb-82e3-4512a77578f9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Adrenoleukodystrophy (cerebral) treatment:', '["No treatment metabolic pathway process","Statins only metabolic pathway process","Hematopoietic stem cell transplant (HSCT) for early cerebral disease","Dietary VLCFA restriction only metabolic pathway process","Lorenzo oil only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hematopoietic stem cell transplant (HSCT) for early cerebral disease', 'HSCT can halt demyelination in cerebral X-ALD if applied early. Lorenzo oil (glyceryl trioleate/trierucate) lowers VLCFA but does NOT affect neurologic progression. Adrenal insufficiency requires hormone replacement.'),
('7c077f35-7833-59b6-8f4c-37e2d8db71d4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Nitisinone (NTBC) for tyrosinemia I:', '["Blocks phenylalanine hydroxylase metabolic pathway process","Blocks 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase preventing toxic metabolite accumulation","Inhibits tyrosine aminotransferase metabolic pathway process","Replaces FAH metabolic pathway process","Promotes tyrosine excretion metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Blocks 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase preventing toxic metabolite accumulation', 'NTBC inhibits 4-HPPD preventing accumulation of maleylacetoacetate and fumarylacetoacetate (toxic). Combined with low-tyrosine diet it dramatically improves survival and prevents liver cancer.'),
('2ce890d5-e410-57a2-929b-288eb112415e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Sapropterin (BH4) therapy for PKU:', '["Only for maternal PKU","Effective for all PKU patients","Only for severe PKU","Effective in BH4-responsive PAH mutations","Not effective"]'::jsonb, 'Effective in BH4-responsive PAH mutations', 'Sapropterin dihydrochloride (Kuvan) is a synthetic BH4 that increases residual PAH activity. Effective in patients with specific PAH mutations (about 30-50% with mild-moderate PKU).'),
('c833654a-89c0-5053-9e97-a26e1d9d95b0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pegvaliase (PEGylated phenylalanine ammonia lyase) for PKU:', '["Replaces PAH","Blocks phenylalanine absorption","Enzyme substitution therapy degrades phenylalanine","Promotes phenylalanine excretion","Provides BH4"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme substitution therapy degrades phenylalanine', 'Pegvaliase (Palynziq) is an injectable enzyme that converts phenylalanine to trans-cinnamic acid and ammonia. It is indicated for adults with PKU uncontrolled on other treatments.'),
('ce112ef3-d95e-5d08-8212-2be71337901c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Pegloticase for refractory gout:', '["Xanthine oxidase inhibitor metabolic pathway process","Uricosuric metabolic pathway process","Recombinant uricase converting uric acid to allantoin","Anti-inflammatory metabolic pathway process","Anti-IL-1 metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Recombinant uricase converting uric acid to allantoin', 'Pegloticase (Krystexxa) is a PEGylated recombinant uricase (porcine-baboon chimeric) converting uric acid to water-soluble allantoin. Used for refractory tophaceous gout. Infusion reactions and antibody formation limit use.'),
('d68756b2-43a0-5a23-8d35-c8727cb44534'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Miglustat for Gaucher and NPC:', '["Glucosylceramide synthase inhibitor","Promotes excretion","ERT replacement","Increases enzyme activity","Inhibits acid beta-glucosidase"]'::jsonb, 'Glucosylceramide synthase inhibitor', 'Miglustat (Zavesca) reduces synthesis of glucosylceramide (Gaucher) and glycosphingolipids (NPC). It crosses BBB. Side effects: diarrhea weight loss tremor. Used when ERT not suitable.'),
('de0b31a3-0914-5354-b690-2dab6f93078f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Eliglustat for Gaucher disease:', '["Chaperone therapy metabolic pathway process","Ceramide analog glucosylceramide synthase inhibitor","ERT metabolic pathway process","Substrate reduction agent metabolic pathway process","Gene therapy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Ceramide analog glucosylceramide synthase inhibitor', 'Eliglustat (Cerdelga) is a newer SRT. It is metabolized by CYP2D6 (requires genotyping). Fewer GI side effects than miglustat. Used for type 1 Gaucher disease.'),
('f7e4174c-ad37-5133-b902-a0c7c5dc2674'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Migalastat for Fabry disease:', '["Pharmacological chaperone stabilizing mutant alpha-galactosidase A","Gene therapy metabolic pathway process","Substrate reduction metabolic pathway process","ERT replacement metabolic pathway process","Chaperone for GLA metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Pharmacological chaperone stabilizing mutant alpha-galactosidase A', 'Migalastat (Galafold) binds to and stabilizes amenable GLA mutations (some missense variants) allowing proper trafficking and function. Oral therapy for Fabry disease with amenable mutations.'),
('6b6170c3-3225-5645-abd4-06077380a2fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Chaperone therapy for lysosomal storage diseases:', '["Small molecules that stabilize mutant enzymes enabling proper folding","Enzyme replacement metabolic pathway process","Substrate reduction metabolic pathway process","Cell therapy metabolic pathway process","Gene therapy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Small molecules that stabilize mutant enzymes enabling proper folding', 'Pharmacological chaperones (migalastat) bind mutant lysosomal enzymes stabilizing their structure promoting proper folding and trafficking. Requires residual enzyme activity amenable mutations.'),
('f5d05533-f124-5e32-9a3b-221a5d48b1e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Voretigene neparvovec (Luxturna) gene therapy treats:', '["Hemophilia","SCID","Duchenne muscular dystrophy","Cystic fibrosis","RPE65 mutation"]'::jsonb, 'RPE65 mutation', 'Luxturna is an adeno-associated virus (AAV) vector delivering functional RPE65 gene to retinal pigment epithelium. It improves vision in biallelic RPE65-mediated inherited retinal disease.'),
('7fd0b6bf-2a47-57b9-bc7b-0606359b0c25'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Onasemnogene abeparvovec (Zolgensma) treats:', '["Beta-thalassemia","Severe combined immunodeficiency","Spinal muscular atrophy","Duchenne muscular dystrophy","Hemophilia A"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal muscular atrophy', 'Zolgensma is an AAV9 vector delivering SMN1 for spinal muscular atrophy. One-time IV infusion. Dramatically improves survival and motor function in infants if treated presymptomatically.'),
('db37b21d-246d-5c44-9d4b-574b74c2501a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Betibeglogene autotemcel (Zynteglo) treats:', '["Sickle cell disease metabolic pathway process","Transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia","PKU metabolic pathway process","Hemophilia metabolic pathway process","SCID metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Transfusion-dependent beta-thalassemia', 'Zynteglo uses lentiviral vector to insert functional beta-globin gene into patient-derived hematopoietic stem cells. Reduced transfusion requirements in beta-thalassemia. Suspended in US due to pricing.'),
('589057fb-8460-5ede-ae6e-d755cfcab820'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Strimvelis treats:', '["X-linked SCID","Beta-thalassemia","Hemophilia","Cystic fibrosis","ADA-SCID"]'::jsonb, 'ADA-SCID', 'Strimvelis is ex vivo gene therapy for ADA-SCID. Autologous CD34+ cells transduced with ADA-expressing retroviral vector. First approved gene therapy in Europe. No need for HLA-matched donor.'),
('59cac1ef-3382-571a-930a-325b359afa2b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'CAR-T cell therapy (tisagenlecleucel) is approved for:', '["T-cell leukemia metabolic pathway process","CML metabolic pathway process","B-cell ALL and diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","AML metabolic pathway process","Multiple myeloma metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'B-cell ALL and diffuse large B-cell lymphoma', 'Tisagenlecleucel (Kymriah) is anti-CD19 CAR-T cells for relapsed/refractory B-cell ALL (children) and DLBCL (adults). CRS and neurotoxicity are major toxicities.'),
('1684a681-8d08-585e-9a20-f32f8b19acb9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Ex vivo gene therapy for X-linked SCID uses:', '["SMN1 delivery metabolic pathway process","Gamma-retroviral vector delivering IL2RG to CD34+ cells","CFTR delivery metabolic pathway process","RPE65 delivery metabolic pathway process","ADA delivery metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma-retroviral vector delivering IL2RG to CD34+ cells', 'X-SCID gene therapy: autologous CD34+ cells transduced with IL2RG retroviral vector. Restores T-cell immunity. Early trials had insertional mutagenesis (LMO2) causing leukemia. Newer vectors are safer.'),
('6ad7105e-eaf6-5c20-aa79-e4a2844e6766'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. Hemophilia A gene therapy (valoctocogene roxaparvovec):', '["AAV8 delivering factor IX","Retroviral factor VIII","AAV5 vector delivering factor VIII cDNA","None available","Lentiviral factor VIII"]'::jsonb, 'AAV5 vector delivering factor VIII cDNA', 'Valoctocogene roxaparvovec (Roctavian/BMN270) is AAV5-BDD-FVIII for hemophilia A. Single IV infusion reduces bleeding rates and factor VIII use. Approved in Europe.'),
('ca9b02c0-1038-53a8-ab22-4e36d95e25c4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. Hemophilia B gene therapy (etranacogene dezaparvovec):', '["Lentiviral therapy","Retroviral therapy","AAV5 delivering factor VIII","RISC-based therapy","AAV5 vector delivering factor IX Padua variant"]'::jsonb, 'AAV5 vector delivering factor IX Padua variant', 'Etranacogene dezaparvovec (Hemgenix) is AAV5-FIX-Padua (gain-of-function FIX variant). Achieves sustained factor IX levels reducing bleeding in hemophilia B.'),
('214837cc-4857-50c8-8134-26365f784afc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. Duchenne muscular dystrophy gene therapy:', '["Stem cell therapy","AAV micro-dystrophin delivery","CRISPR correction","None available","Exon 51 skipping only"]'::jsonb, 'AAV micro-dystrophin delivery', 'Elevidys (delandistrogene moxeparvovec) is AAV-rh74 delivering micro-dystrophin for DMD. Approved for ambulatory DMD patients. Exon-skipping agents (eteplirsen) are also available.'),
('244906c1-c093-57e7-824a-0160d486f609'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing clinical trials target:', '["Sickle cell disease and beta-thalassemia","Huntington disease metabolic pathway process","Cystic fibrosis metabolic pathway process","Alzheimer disease metabolic pathway process","All genetic diseases metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Sickle cell disease and beta-thalassemia', 'Exagamglogene autotemcel (Casgevy) uses CRISPR-Cas9 to edit BCL11A enhancer in hematopoietic stem cells reactivating fetal hemoglobin for sickle cell disease and beta-thalassemia.'),
('433d7ab8-6005-55fa-bb51-f3f440cdd9b5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The drug ataluren (Translarna) treats:', '["Insertion mutations metabolic pathway process","Nonsense mutation Duchenne muscular dystrophy","Missense mutations metabolic pathway process","Frameshift mutations metabolic pathway process","Deletion mutations metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Nonsense mutation Duchenne muscular dystrophy', 'Ataluren is an oral readthrough agent promoting ribosomal readthrough of premature stop codons. Approved for nonsense mutation DMD. Efficacy is modest.'),
('ddce3465-7fcb-5f05-9036-9a7b4c1d8f61'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'ASO (antisense oligonucleotide) therapy nusinersen targets:', '["Cystic fibrosis","Familial hypercholesterolemia","Huntington disease","Spinal muscular atrophy","Duchenne muscular dystrophy"]'::jsonb, 'Spinal muscular atrophy', 'Nusinersen (Spinraza) is an ASO that modifies SMN2 splicing to include exon 7 producing more functional SMN protein. Given intrathecally. Improves motor function in SMA.'),
('70574c48-4975-525f-8002-0291cc01bd3c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Eteplirsen is an ASO that:', '["Inhibits transthyretin production","Modifies SMN2 splicing","Knocks down mutant HTT","Reduces LDL","Promotes DMD exon 51 skipping"]'::jsonb, 'Promotes DMD exon 51 skipping', 'Eteplirsen (Exondys 51) is a phosphorodiamidate morpholino ASO that skips DMD exon 51 applicable to ~13% of DMD patients with amenable deletions. Modest benefit on ambulation.'),
('41c4adc6-94ad-50c8-a208-edf9e9da2927'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Patisiran (Onpattro) is an RNAi therapeutic for:', '["Hemophilia metabolic pathway process","Cystic fibrosis metabolic pathway process","Hereditary transthyretin-mediated amyloidosis","SMA metabolic pathway process","Hypercholesterolemia metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Hereditary transthyretin-mediated amyloidosis', 'Patisiran is a lipid nanoparticle-formulated siRNA that degrades mutant and wild-type TTR mRNA. Given IV every 3 weeks. Improves neuropathy and quality of life in hATTR.'),
('8d58a995-a7cc-5eec-bf47-3818c468546b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Inclisiran (Leqvio) is a:', '["HMG-CoA reductase inhibitor","PCSK9 monoclonal antibody","siRNA targeting PCSK9 for LDL reduction","NPC1L1 inhibitor","CETP inhibitor"]'::jsonb, 'siRNA targeting PCSK9 for LDL reduction', 'Inclisiran is a long-acting siRNA that blocks PCSK9 translation. Given as subcutaneous injection initially then every 6 months. Reduces LDL by ~50%. Novel mechanism of LDL-lowering.'),
('ca59a73c-e2fc-506c-bf20-8fcbbff82430'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Lomitapide inhibits:', '["HMG-CoA reductase metabolic pathway process","PCSK9 metabolic pathway process","Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) for homozygous FH","CETP metabolic pathway process","ACAT metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP) for homozygous FH', 'Lomitapide inhibits MTP blocking VLDL assembly in liver and intestine. Used for homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. Side effects: hepatic steatosis diarrhea and elevated transaminases.'),
('a8d817d7-9249-5bfa-8d32-f80f4e0fd979'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Mipomersen is an ASO that targets:', '["PCSK9","HMG-CoA reductase","LDL receptor","CETP","Apolipoprotein B-100"]'::jsonb, 'Apolipoprotein B-100', 'Mipomersen (Kynamro) is an ASO that degrades apo B-100 mRNA reducing VLDL and LDL synthesis. Given SC weekly. Side effects: injection site reactions hepatic steatosis.'),
('ba2b5b19-8841-5cf0-941c-06edb8217c48'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Evinacumab targets:', '["Angiopoietin-like 3 (ANGPTL3) for homozygous FH","CETP metabolic pathway process","LDL receptor metabolic pathway process","PCSK9 metabolic pathway process","ANGPTL4 metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Angiopoietin-like 3 (ANGPTL3) for homozygous FH', 'Evinacumab is a monoclonal antibody against ANGPTL3 which inhibits LPL and EL. Blocking ANGPTL3 reduces LDL triglycerides and HDL. Approved for homozygous FH.'),
('ffbeb94e-8351-57ef-b58d-d44eaeb07c22'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Volanesorsen targets:', '["APOC3","PCSK9","ANGPTL3","APOB","LDLR"]'::jsonb, 'APOC3', 'Volanesorsen (Waylivra) is an ASO against apoC-III reducing triglycerides in familial chylomicronemia (LPL deficiency). It prevents LPL inhibition by apoC-III. Side effect: thrombocytopenia.'),
('f66ad015-f458-5d1a-9ffa-ebba4d32d93b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'The mAb canakinumab targets:', '["TNF-alpha metabolic pathway process","IL-1beta for gout and autoinflammatory diseases","IL-6 metabolic pathway process","IL-17 metabolic pathway process","IL-23 metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'IL-1beta for gout and autoinflammatory diseases', 'Canakinumab is a human anti-IL-1beta mAb. Used for CAPS (cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes) gout flares and systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis. CANTOS trial showed reduced cardiovascular events.'),
('80595cd2-c54b-50fb-b282-7428a54f5f7e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Tocilizumab is an anti-IL-6 receptor mAb used in:', '["Lupus only metabolic pathway process","Osteoarthritis only metabolic pathway process","Gout only metabolic pathway process","Rheumatoid arthritis and cytokine release syndrome","Scleroderma only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Rheumatoid arthritis and cytokine release syndrome', 'Tocilizumab blocks IL-6 signaling. Used for RA giant cell arteritis and severe CRS from CAR-T therapy. Side effects: neutropenia infections hepatotoxicity hyperlipidemia.'),
('b16bb2ba-2619-5af2-be3c-47c1f4ce2815'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Ustekinumab targets:', '["IL-17 metabolic pathway process","IL-23 only metabolic pathway process","IL-1 metabolic pathway process","TNF-alpha metabolic pathway process","IL-12 and IL-23 (p40 subunit) for psoriasis and Crohn disease"]'::jsonb, 'IL-12 and IL-23 (p40 subunit) for psoriasis and Crohn disease', 'Ustekinumab (Stelara) is a human mAb against p40 shared by IL-12 and IL-23. Used for psoriasis psoriatic arthritis and Crohn disease. Given SC every 12 weeks after loading.'),
('ba223bbd-4c0c-5e8c-998f-6e155a46d3c6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 2)', 'Disorders', 'Risankizumab targets:', '["IL-23 (p19 subunit) for psoriasis","TNF-alpha metabolic pathway process","IL-17 metabolic pathway process","IL-12 metabolic pathway process","IL-1 metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'IL-23 (p19 subunit) for psoriasis', 'Risankizumab (Skyrizi) and guselkumab (Tremfya) are selective IL-23 p19 inhibitors. Highly effective for plaque psoriasis. Given SC every 8-12 weeks. Better safety than older biologics.'),
('6405caaa-1083-5e1b-80eb-aa1d549ff1ee'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Spectrophotometry measures:', '["Light scattering metabolic pathway process","Light absorbance by a sample at specific wavelengths","Electrical conductivity metabolic pathway process","Radioactivity metabolic pathway process","Light emission metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Light absorbance by a sample at specific wavelengths', 'Spectrophotometry measures the amount of light absorbed by a sample. The absorbance is proportional to concentration (Beer-Lambert law A = epsilon * l * c).'),
('1dd2c196-d9c7-55cb-a14f-bb389f9b0b70'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'The Beer-Lambert law states that absorbance is:', '["Directly proportional to concentration and path length","Directly proportional to transmittance","Inversely proportional to path length","Inversely proportional to concentration","Independent of concentration"]'::jsonb, 'Directly proportional to concentration and path length', 'A = epsilon * l * c where epsilon is molar absorptivity l is path length and c is concentration. This law is the basis for quantitative spectrophotometric analysis.'),
('d3a1b585-e355-5b0a-b4f2-2805d9377693'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'In spectrophotometry the wavelength of maximum absorbance is called:', '["Lambda min","Cutoff wavelength","Peak width","Lambda max","Isobestic point"]'::jsonb, 'Lambda max', 'Lambda max (λmax) is the wavelength at which a substance absorbs light most strongly. It is used for quantitative analysis and compound identification.'),
('605d079d-6f63-5912-8fc4-ee74efc861d7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'An isosbestic point in spectroscopy is:', '["The point of zero absorbance","The wavelength of minimum absorbance","The baseline point","The wavelength of maximum absorbance","A wavelength where two species have equal absorbance"]'::jsonb, 'A wavelength where two species have equal absorbance', 'An isosbestic point occurs when two interconvertible species (e.g. Hb and HbO2) have the same molar absorptivity at a particular wavelength. It confirms the presence of only two species in equilibrium.'),
('021888f8-a24e-5698-903a-b18cfbd8b2ef'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Fluorimetry measures:', '["Radioactivity metabolic pathway process","Light emitted by a substance after light absorption","Electrical potential metabolic pathway process","Light scattered metabolic pathway process","Light absorbed metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Light emitted by a substance after light absorption', 'Fluorimetry measures fluorescence (light emitted at a longer wavelength after excitation). It is more sensitive than spectrophotometry (10-1000x). Used for drug monitoring and ELISA.'),
('d0f5e5b5-f14c-5128-bb47-2af552493ffc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Fluorescence occurs when an electron:', '["Absorbs energy metabolic pathway process","Returns from triplet state metabolic pathway process","Moves to a higher energy level metabolic pathway process","Returns from excited singlet state to ground state emitting light","Scatters light metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Returns from excited singlet state to ground state emitting light', 'Fluorescence: absorption of light promotes electron to excited singlet state. Return to ground state emits light at longer wavelength. Stokes shift = difference between excitation and emission maxima.'),
('9bede3ad-c4d5-5a1b-b222-ead5aa6e8f47'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Phosphorescence differs from fluorescence by:', '["Faster emission metabolic pathway process","No light absorption metabolic pathway process","Same mechanism metabolic pathway process","Emission from triplet state with longer lifetime","Higher energy emission metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Emission from triplet state with longer lifetime', 'Phosphorescence involves intersystem crossing to triplet state (spin-forbidden). Return to ground state is slow (milliseconds to seconds). Fluorescence is from singlet state (nanoseconds).'),
('0e03f8c3-c03d-5630-9042-c7129ed1d944'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Chemiluminescence is:', '["Light production from a chemical reaction","Light absorption","Electron excitation by light","Bioluminescence in fireflies only","Heat production"]'::jsonb, 'Light production from a chemical reaction', 'Chemiluminescence produces light from chemical reactions (e.g. luminol + H2O2 + HRP). Used in ELISA and Western blotting. High sensitivity without external light source.'),
('fd0a90af-53e7-5bf8-b7b9-05d6b6ada192'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Bioluminescence uses:', '["Only luciferase metabolic pathway process","Luciferase enzyme and luciferin substrate","Only luciferin metabolic pathway process","Mg2+ only metabolic pathway process","ATP only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Luciferase enzyme and luciferin substrate', 'Bioluminescence (e.g. firefly luciferase) catalyzes luciferin + ATP + O2 producing light. Used in reporter assays (gene expression) and BRET (protein interactions).'),
('df577ea9-7cc1-5c8d-ba26-785d6fbe4092'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Turbidimetry measures:', '["Light absorption metabolic pathway process","Light emission metabolic pathway process","Electrical conductivity metabolic pathway process","Reduction in light intensity due to particles scattering light","Radioactivity metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Reduction in light intensity due to particles scattering light', 'Turbidimetry measures the decrease in transmitted light due to particle scattering. Used for bacterial growth curves and immunoprecipitation assays (nephelometry measures scattered light).'),
('9c133f2a-d091-5631-947b-1a9452bc8083'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Nephelometry measures:', '["Light scattered at an angle by particles in solution","Light emission metabolic pathway process","Light absorption metabolic pathway process","Light refraction metabolic pathway process","Light transmission metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Light scattered at an angle by particles in solution', 'Nephelometry measures scattered light (usually at 90 degrees). More sensitive than turbidimetry. Used for quantifying serum proteins immunoglobulins and complement components.'),
('c774cf98-9d5a-5238-a4d6-978b174bf6de'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Atomic absorption spectrophotometry (AAS) measures:', '["Nucleic acids","Organic compounds","Proteins","Carbohydrates","Metal ion concentrations"]'::jsonb, 'Metal ion concentrations', 'AAS: sample is atomized (flame/graphite furnace). Light at characteristic wavelength is absorbed by ground-state atoms. Used for trace metal analysis (lead iron copper zinc calcium).'),
('0fba093c-cf08-5e29-91e8-30a94db8ac35'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Atomic emission spectrophotometry (AES) / ICP uses:', '["Electrochemical detection metabolic pathway process","Light scattering metabolic pathway process","Plasma to excite atoms measuring emitted light at characteristic wavelengths","Flame to measure absorption metabolic pathway process","Radioactive decay metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Plasma to excite atoms measuring emitted light at characteristic wavelengths', 'ICP-AES (inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy) uses argon plasma (6000-10000 K) to excite atoms. Emissions are measured for multi-element analysis. Used for mineral analysis.'),
('e31fbeea-976b-5eb6-9503-04773fa799ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Mass spectrometry (MS) measures:', '["Mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of ions","Absorbance wavelength","Fluorescence intensity","Radioactive decay","Electrochemical signal"]'::jsonb, 'Mass-to-charge ratio (m/z) of ions', 'MS ionizes molecules and separates them by m/z. Provides molecular weight and structural information. Used in proteomics metabolomics and clinical toxicology.'),
('4d876a7f-ddc9-56fa-b302-73f4f4c52a7e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'MALDI-TOF MS is used for:', '["Glycan analysis metabolic pathway process","Lipid analysis metabolic pathway process","RNA analysis metabolic pathway process","DNA sequencing metabolic pathway process","Protein identification by peptide mass fingerprinting"]'::jsonb, 'Protein identification by peptide mass fingerprinting', 'MALDI (matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization) coupled with TOF analyzes large molecules. Soft ionization produces ions without fragmentation. Used for bacterial identification and protein analysis.'),
('f16054c1-4845-54f3-ac12-9ff887ec9b1e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Tandem mass spectrometry (MS/MS) is used for:', '["Fragment ion analysis providing structural information","Elemental analysis only metabolic pathway process","Isotope ratio only metabolic pathway process","Molecular weight only metabolic pathway process","Protein quantitation only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Fragment ion analysis providing structural information', 'MS/MS selects a precursor ion in first MS fragments it and analyzes fragments in second MS. Used in newborn screening (amino acids acylcarnitines) drug testing and proteomics.'),
('1a48d949-436c-5cb2-bda1-8c7a240c4be8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'ELISA (enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay) uses:', '["Enzyme-labeled antibodies for antigen/antibody detection","Radioactive labels metabolic pathway process","Fluorescent labels only metabolic pathway process","Magnetic beads only metabolic pathway process","Chromogenic substrates only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme-labeled antibodies for antigen/antibody detection', 'ELISA: antibody or antigen is immobilized on microplate. Detection uses enzyme-conjugated antibody. Substrate produces color/fluorescence/chemiluminescence. Types: direct indirect sandwich competitive.'),
('944e69c2-605f-5eed-999d-8e28ab7caaf8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Sandwich ELISA detects:', '["Both antigen and antibody","Antigen only","Antigen between two antibodies","Nucleic acids","Antibody only"]'::jsonb, 'Antigen between two antibodies', 'Sandwich ELISA: capture antibody immobilized on plate binds antigen. Detection antibody (enzyme-labeled) binds a different epitope. Highly sensitive and specific for antigen quantification.'),
('4be9b169-d891-5cdc-89e2-c40f2acd4b23'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Competitive ELISA is used for:', '["Small molecules (haptens) like drugs and hormones","Large proteins metabolic pathway process","Cells metabolic pathway process","Tissues metabolic pathway process","Nucleic acids metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Small molecules (haptens) like drugs and hormones', 'Competitive ELISA: labeled antigen competes with sample antigen for limited antibody binding. Signal inversely proportional to analyte concentration. Used for drug monitoring (theophylline digoxin).'),
('e9db8c37-d319-592d-81b1-c91f4f6eff63'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Indirect ELISA detects:', '["Antibodies in patient serum","Antigens","Nucleic acids","Enzymes","Hormones"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies in patient serum', 'Indirect ELISA: antigen immobilized on plate. Patient serum added (primary antibody binds). Enzyme-labeled anti-human IgG added. Used for HIV hepatitis autoimmune disease serology.'),
('bd1a3113-afa2-5688-a5b1-1a257f8612cf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'ELISA substrate for HRP (horseradish peroxidase) includes:', '["TMB (tetramethylbenzidine) producing blue color","ONPG metabolic pathway process","BCIP/NBT metabolic pathway process","X-gal metabolic pathway process","DAB metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'TMB (tetramethylbenzidine) producing blue color', 'HRP uses H2O2 as substrate with chromogenic donors. TMB (blue) is most common ELISA substrate. Stop with acid (yellow). Read at 450 nm. ABTS and OPD are alternatives.'),
('92b76105-6521-557c-82a7-0f7b4abbf9ab'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Alkaline phosphatase (AP) substrate in ELISA:', '["pNPP (p-nitrophenyl phosphate) producing yellow","TMB producing blue metabolic pathway process","DAB producing brown metabolic pathway process","BCIP/NBT producing purple metabolic pathway process","Luminol producing light metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'pNPP (p-nitrophenyl phosphate) producing yellow', 'AP dephosphorylates pNPP to p-nitrophenol (yellow product read at 405 nm). AP is used in Western blotting (BCIP/NBT for purple precipitate).'),
('4bb574a9-f2ce-5dda-88a8-820ba7e53df2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Chemiluminescent ELISA substrate for HRP:', '["BCIP/NBT","DAB","pNPP","Luminol enhanced with phenol","TMB"]'::jsonb, 'Luminol enhanced with phenol', 'Enhanced chemiluminescence (ECL): HRP catalyzes luminol + H2O2 producing light. Phenol derivatives enhance signal. Used in Western blotting. Very sensitive with film or CCD detection.'),
('b5e35b7f-6b46-553e-b70a-501d8161834b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Radioimmunoassay (RIA) uses:', '["Chemiluminescent labels metabolic pathway process","Enzyme labels metabolic pathway process","Radioactively labeled antigens or antibodies","Magnetic labels metabolic pathway process","Fluorescent labels metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Radioactively labeled antigens or antibodies', 'RIA: competitive binding assay using radioisotopes (125I 131I 3H 14C). Developed by Yalow and Berson. Highly sensitive but requires radioactive materials and special handling. Largely replaced by ELISA.'),
('90e9a29a-1dd2-5433-8be1-fb12b406724d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'RIA is based on the principle of:', '["Antigen-antibody precipitation metabolic pathway process","Competitive binding between labeled and unlabeled antigen for limited antibody","Cell lysis metabolic pathway process","Direct antigen detection metabolic pathway process","Enzyme activity measurement metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Competitive binding between labeled and unlabeled antigen for limited antibody', 'RIA: known amount of labeled antigen and unknown sample compete for limited antibody binding. Bound/free separation. Signal inversely proportional to analyte concentration.'),
('e2b2ab64-fb10-55dc-9d49-d9639b66208c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Immunoradiometric assay (IRMA) uses:', '["Radioactive antibodies in a sandwich format","Radioactive antigens metabolic pathway process","Competitive binding metabolic pathway process","Single antibody metabolic pathway process","No antibodies metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Radioactive antibodies in a sandwich format', 'IRMA uses excess labeled antibody in two-site (sandwich) format. Unlike RIA signal is directly proportional to antigen concentration. Used for TSH HCG and tumor markers.'),
('4bd4a4ab-b97b-5a9a-a7b2-26fe8490ae66'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Scintillation counting detects:', '["Chemiluminescence","Scattered light","Absorbance","Fluorescence","Radioactive decay"]'::jsonb, 'Radioactive decay', 'Scintillation counters detect radioactive decay via scintillators (emit light when struck by radiation). Liquid scintillation for beta emitters (3H 14C 32P). Gamma counters for gamma emitters (125I 51Cr).'),
('e8c32bae-a804-538f-8aa9-c96197216d88'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Gamma counter specifically detects:', '["Alpha emitters only","All radioisotopes","Beta emitters only","No radioisotopes","Gamma-emitting radioisotopes"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma-emitting radioisotopes', 'Gamma counters use NaI(Tl) crystal scintillator. Used for RIA and IRMA with 125I-labeled tracers. Well-type detector maximizes counting efficiency for gamma rays.'),
('b7ea6975-fe69-5694-b83a-3dbef18c3ab8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Autoradiography detects:', '["Chemiluminescence metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Light scattering metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Fluorescence metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Radioactive isotopes in gels membranes and tissues via film or phosphor imaging","Absorbance metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Radioactive isotopes in gels membranes and tissues via film or phosphor imaging', 'Autoradiography: radioactive samples (32P 35S 3H 14C) exposure to X-ray film or phosphor screen. Used in Southern/Northern/Western blotting and in situ hybridization.'),
('912dc640-fae1-585e-9935-b88853398691'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Phosphorimaging (storage phosphor) for autoradiography:', '["More sensitive and quantitative than film autoradiography","Only for gamma emitters metabolic pathway process","Requires liquid scintillation metabolic pathway process","Less sensitive metabolic pathway process","Qualitative only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'More sensitive and quantitative than film autoradiography', 'Phosphorimaging uses storage phosphor screens that store energy from radioactive decay. Laser scanning releases light. Dynamic range >5 orders vs film ~1-2 orders. Quantitative and reusable.'),
('4ecde077-97bd-52d2-8d22-f19754547b17'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Microtiter plate readers (ELISA readers) measure:', '["Chemiluminescence only metabolic pathway process","Fluorescence only metabolic pathway process","Radioactivity only metabolic pathway process","Absorbance at specific wavelengths in 96/384-well plates","Turbidity only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Absorbance at specific wavelengths in 96/384-well plates', 'ELISA readers measure absorbance (typically 450 nm for TMB). Multi-mode readers also measure fluorescence time-resolved fluorescence and luminescence. Used for high-throughput immunoassays.'),
('472f725a-8585-5060-b13c-0f23e77e75c2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Electrophoresis separates molecules based on:', '["Charge size and shape","Only shape","Only charge","Only mass","Only size"]'::jsonb, 'Charge size and shape', 'Electrophoresis uses electric field to separate charged molecules. DNA/RNA (negative charge) move to anode. Proteins can be separated by charge (native PAGE) or size (SDS-PAGE).'),
('6d5deeb0-5bc5-5430-8375-c2cb71e85083'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'SDS-PAGE separates proteins primarily based on:', '["Charge","Isoelectric point","Molecular weight","Shape","Hydrophobicity"]'::jsonb, 'Molecular weight', 'SDS (sodium dodecyl sulfate) denatures proteins and gives uniform negative charge proportional to mass. Beta-mercaptoethanol reduces disulfide bonds. Migration is inversely related to log molecular weight.'),
('0717b34b-e669-5ffc-974f-0748b9ed728c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'In SDS-PAGE the stacking gel has:', '["Low pH and low acrylamide concentration","High pH and high acrylamide","Low pH and high acrylamide","High pH and low acrylamide","Neutral pH"]'::jsonb, 'Low pH and low acrylamide concentration', 'Stacking gel (pH 6.8 4% acrylamide) concentrates samples into thin bands. Resolving gel (pH 8.8 6-15% acrylamide) separates by size. Tris-glycine buffer system creates moving boundary.'),
('36c4bcea-0c93-57bb-a697-a5ffdd45542c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'The molecular weight of an unknown protein in SDS-PAGE is determined by:', '["Band intensity metabolic pathway process","Charge of the protein metabolic pathway process","Band width metabolic pathway process","Distance from the well metabolic pathway process","Comparison with molecular weight standards"]'::jsonb, 'Comparison with molecular weight standards', 'Log MW is inversely proportional to relative mobility (Rf). Standards of known MW create a calibration curve. Interpolate unknown MW from its Rf.'),
('98adb081-6164-5584-a826-d05ef6fcb2b3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Native PAGE separates proteins based on:', '["Native charge and size","SDS-bound mass only","Hydrophobicity","Isoelectric point only","Denatured size only"]'::jsonb, 'Native charge and size', 'Native PAGE uses non-denaturing conditions preserving protein structure and activity. Separation depends on intrinsic charge shape and size. Used for enzyme activity assays (zymography) and protein complex analysis.'),
('53e7d2cc-a360-5b50-aa0b-47c2f52a717c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Isoelectric focusing (IEF) separates proteins based on:', '["Isoelectric point","Charge at a single pH","Hydrophobicity","Shape","Molecular weight"]'::jsonb, 'Isoelectric point', 'IEF: proteins migrate in pH gradient until net charge = 0 (pI). Immobilized pH gradients (IPG strips) provide high resolution. First dimension of 2D electrophoresis.'),
('ceddea20-8ec6-5b99-bdc1-2e2237af00b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Two-dimensional electrophoresis (2D-PAGE) separates by:', '["pI (first dimension IEF) then MW","Charge then shape","MW then pI","Hydrophobicity then charge","Size then charge"]'::jsonb, 'pI (first dimension IEF) then MW', '2D electrophoresis: first dimension IEF on IPG strip. Second dimension SDS-PAGE perpendicular to IEF. Resolves thousands of proteins. Used in proteomics for differential expression analysis.'),
('32d7e518-6951-57a8-9111-dfe087047428'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Capillary electrophoresis (CE) offers:', '["High resolution fast separation and small sample volume","Low resolution metabolic pathway process","Only small volume metabolic pathway process","Only high resolution metabolic pathway process","Only fast separation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'High resolution fast separation and small sample volume', 'CE uses narrow capillary (20-100 microm) with high voltage. High resolution speed and sensitivity. Multiple modes: CZE (free solution) CGE (gel) MEKC (micellar) IEF (isoelectric focusing).'),
('89fe2fea-19ad-5b5d-aa11-d4804db13f53'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Capillary zone electrophoresis (CZE) is used for:', '["Protein sequencing only","Cell sorting","Metabolite analysis only","DNA sequencing only","Serum protein separation"]'::jsonb, 'Serum protein separation', 'CZE separates based on electrophoretic mobility in free solution. Used for serum protein electrophoresis (albumin alpha1 alpha2 beta gamma globulins) and hemoglobin variants.'),
('62b280ef-a09a-59a4-8b8b-defaa995b4ee'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Agarose gel electrophoresis is commonly used for:', '["DNA separation and analysis","Protein separation","Lipid analysis","Carbohydrate analysis","Metabolite analysis"]'::jsonb, 'DNA separation and analysis', 'Agarose gels (0.5-2%) separate DNA fragments by size. Ethidium bromide stains DNA. Used for restriction analysis PCR verification and Southern blotting. Pulse-field for large DNA (>20 kb).'),
('562e8b85-0e4f-5f31-a353-e0167c5cfa69'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE) for DNA:', '["Separates double-stranded DNA by shape","Separates proteins","Denaturing sequencing gels separate single-stranded DNA by length","Separates RNA","Separates lipids"]'::jsonb, 'Denaturing sequencing gels separate single-stranded DNA by length', 'Denaturing PAGE (with urea) separates ssDNA fragments differing by one nucleotide. Used for Sanger sequencing. Higher resolution than agarose for small fragments (<500 bp).'),
('25995453-f7d9-51b8-afe2-478517422748'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Pulsed-field gel electrophoresis (PFGE):', '["Separates RNA metabolic pathway process","Separates small DNA only metabolic pathway process","Separates large DNA molecules (50 kb - 10 Mb) by alternating field directions","Separates proteins metabolic pathway process","Separates carbohydrates metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Separates large DNA molecules (50 kb - 10 Mb) by alternating field directions', 'PFGE: alternating electric field at different angles forces large DNA to reorient and migrate. Used for bacterial typing (epidemiology) and chromosome-sized DNA analysis.'),
('a85caad7-b9dc-59e9-8ec9-d1546b9efa76'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Zymography detects:', '["Lipid content","Protein","Enzyme activity in gels","DNA hybridization","RNA expression"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme activity in gels', 'Zymography: substrate (gelatin casein) incorporated in SDS-PAGE gel. After renaturation enzyme digests substrate. Coomassie staining reveals clear bands on blue background. Used for matrix metalloproteinase (MMP) analysis.'),
('8694c49f-10ab-5628-a924-a94a79a47caa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Chromatography separates molecules by:', '["Only solubility metabolic pathway process","Only size metabolic pathway process","Only charge metabolic pathway process","Differential distribution between stationary and mobile phases","Only affinity metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Differential distribution between stationary and mobile phases', 'All chromatography separates based on differential partitioning between stationary phase (solid/liquid on support) and mobile phase (liquid/gas). Types: adsorption partition ion-exchange size-exclusion affinity.'),
('8c705508-4f0f-5845-af24-325574888178'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Thin-layer chromatography (TLC) uses:', '["Gas mobile phase metabolic pathway process","An ion-exchange column metabolic pathway process","A column packed with resin metabolic pathway process","A solid stationary phase on a plate and liquid mobile phase","A paper stationary phase metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'A solid stationary phase on a plate and liquid mobile phase', 'TLC: stationary phase (silica gel alumina) coated on glass/plastic plate. Mobile phase rises by capillary action. Rf = distance moved by compound/distance moved by solvent front.'),
('14748ce1-84bf-5033-8b51-7ac8691fc969'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Paper chromatography uses:', '["Silica gel as stationary phase","Gas as mobile phase","Column as support","Ion-exchange resin","Paper (cellulose) as stationary phase"]'::jsonb, 'Paper (cellulose) as stationary phase', 'Paper chromatography: cellulose paper is the stationary phase (water bound to cellulose). Mobile phase (organic solvent) moves by capillary action. Used for amino acid and sugar analysis.'),
('ca2e3473-0b13-5d5f-ae1c-7266bb44ad8a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Column chromatography:', '["Paper as stationary phase metabolic pathway process","Gas as mobile phase metabolic pathway process","Flat bed system metabolic pathway process","Stationary phase packed in a column mobile phase flows through","Stationary phase on a plate metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Stationary phase packed in a column mobile phase flows through', 'Column chromatography: stationary phase (silica alumina resin) packed in glass/plastic column. Sample applied mobile phase flows by gravity or pump. Fractions collected for analysis.'),
('631a0190-1eee-5f68-b57e-cb297fbb8ead'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Ion-exchange chromatography separates based on:', '["Affinity","Hydrophobicity","Molecular weight","Net charge of molecules","Solubility"]'::jsonb, 'Net charge of molecules', 'Ion-exchange: positively charged (anion exchange DEAE) binds negative molecules. Negatively charged (cation exchange CM SP) binds positive molecules. Eluted by salt gradient (increasing ionic strength).'),
('be1723c4-0310-5379-baab-f32916cfeadd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'DEAE-cellulose is a:', '["Weak anion exchanger","Cation exchanger","Size exclusion resin","Affinity resin","Strong anion exchanger"]'::jsonb, 'Weak anion exchanger', 'DEAE (diethylaminoethyl) is a weak anion exchanger (positively charged) binding negatively charged proteins. Elution with increasing NaCl or pH gradient. Used for protein purification.'),
('da9bf9a1-b3fe-5fe0-87d4-184f63556df2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'CM-cellulose is a:', '["Anion exchanger","Affinity resin","Weak cation exchanger","Strong cation exchanger","Size exclusion resin"]'::jsonb, 'Weak cation exchanger', 'CM (carboxymethyl) is a weak cation exchanger (negatively charged) binding positively charged proteins. Used for basic protein separation and purification.'),
('e4dcaad6-ce4e-5e13-8fc4-47ff7e29eef6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Size-exclusion chromatography (gel filtration) separates by:', '["Net charge","Hydrophobicity","Affinity to ligand","Molecular size and shape","Isoelectric point"]'::jsonb, 'Molecular size and shape', 'Gel filtration: porous beads allow smaller molecules to enter pores (retarded). Large molecules are excluded (elute first). Sephadex Sepharose Sephacryl are common media. Used for desalting and MW estimation.'),
('33f659b3-7679-51b1-84d3-704a1084db19'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Affinity chromatography uses:', '["Charge metabolic pathway process","Size exclusion metabolic pathway process","Specific binding between a molecule and its ligand","Hydrophobicity metabolic pathway process","Solubility metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Specific binding between a molecule and its ligand', 'Affinity chromatography exploits specific biological interactions (antibody-antigen enzyme-substrate lectin-sugar). Ligand immobilized on matrix. Target binds specifically eluted by competition or pH change.'),
('dd5991f1-e56a-5b3d-964f-0699af47323d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Protein A or G affinity chromatography purifies:', '["DNA","Antibodies","Membrane proteins","Enzymes","RNA"]'::jsonb, 'Antibodies', 'Protein A (from S. aureus) and Protein G (from Streptococcus) bind Fc region of IgG. Used for monoclonal/polyclonal antibody purification from serum cell culture ascites.'),
('c74dcd5a-0a4b-5349-a57d-c74a18edba81'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Nickel-NTA affinity chromatography purifies:', '["His-tagged recombinant proteins","GST-tagged proteins","MBP-tagged proteins","FLAG-tagged proteins","GFP fusion proteins"]'::jsonb, 'His-tagged recombinant proteins', 'Ni-NTA (nitrilotriacetic acid) binds polyhistidine tags (6xHis). Imidazole gradient elutes bound proteins. Most common method for recombinant protein purification.'),
('819a303b-9f61-51e2-9103-27e6d9c6ab3a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'GST (glutathione S-transferase) affinity purification uses:', '["Glutathione-Sepharose beads","Nickel-NTA","Protein A","DEAE","CM"]'::jsonb, 'Glutathione-Sepharose beads', 'GST-tagged proteins bind to glutathione immobilized on Sepharose. Elution with free reduced glutathione. Mild conditions preserve native structure. Also increases protein solubility.'),
('c0b4cc41-c96c-55db-a523-8ba9ce8bcd73'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) uses:', '["Open columns metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","High-pressure pumps and small-particle columns for rapid high-resolution separation","Gravity flow columns metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Paper-based system metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Thin-layer plates metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'High-pressure pumps and small-particle columns for rapid high-resolution separation', 'HPLC: high pressure (up to 400 bar) drives mobile phase through columns with small particles (3-5 micron). High resolution speed and sensitivity. Multiple modes: reversed-phase ion-exchange size-exclusion.'),
('44c7c741-30ea-5fa5-8e17-2ed2924f2a7e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Reversed-phase HPLC (RP-HPLC):', '["Polar stationary phase with nonpolar mobile phase","Ion-exchange mode","Nonpolar stationary phase (C18 C8) with polar mobile phase","Affinity mode","Size-exclusion mode"]'::jsonb, 'Nonpolar stationary phase (C18 C8) with polar mobile phase', 'RP-HPLC: alkyl chains (C18 C8 C4) bonded to silica. Hydrophobic molecules bind. Elution with increasing organic solvent (acetonitrile methanol). Most common HPLC mode for peptides and drugs.'),
('7851c6e3-dd12-5450-ba61-bcfd4e6b5426'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Normal-phase HPLC:', '["Nonpolar stationary phase with polar mobile phase","Ion-exchange mode","Polar stationary phase (silica) with nonpolar mobile phase","Size-exclusion mode","Affinity mode"]'::jsonb, 'Polar stationary phase (silica) with nonpolar mobile phase', 'Normal-phase: silica or alumina stationary phase (polar). Mobile phase hexane/isopropanol. Used for lipid separation and geometric isomer separation. Less common than reversed-phase.'),
('1e716d3d-96fe-5e81-807b-5940deb2fb78'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Gas chromatography (GC) separates:', '["Large proteins metabolic pathway process","Volatile compounds separated by partitioning between gas and liquid stationary phase","Nucleic acids metabolic pathway process","Carbohydrates metabolic pathway process","Non-volatile compounds metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Volatile compounds separated by partitioning between gas and liquid stationary phase', 'GC: sample vaporized and carried by inert gas (He N2) through capillary column coated with liquid stationary phase. Separation by boiling point and polarity. Used for fatty acid analysis and toxicology.'),
('f62d7948-adcd-5225-99a9-aa248e3b466e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'GC-MS (gas chromatography-mass spectrometry) combines:', '["Two separations metabolic pathway process","IR and MS metabolic pathway process","Chromatographic separation with mass spectrometric identification","Two mass specs metabolic pathway process","LC and MS metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Chromatographic separation with mass spectrometric identification', 'GC-MS is the gold standard for confirmatory drug testing. Electron impact (EI) ionization produces characteristic fragmentation patterns matching libraries. Used in toxicology doping and metabolomics.'),
('15290265-97df-5623-a35b-470bdccb941a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC-MS):', '["Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Combines GC with MS metabolic pathway process","Used only for metals metabolic pathway process","Used only for volatile compounds metabolic pathway process","Combines HPLC with mass spectrometry for analysis of non-volatile compounds"]'::jsonb, 'Combines HPLC with mass spectrometry for analysis of non-volatile compounds', 'LC-MS analyzes non-volatile polar and thermally labile compounds (drugs metabolites peptides proteins). Electrospray ionization (ESI) is the most common interface. Essential in clinical pharmacology.'),
('85f25994-c9b5-510d-b349-743a822cb45c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplifies:', '["Proteins","RNA directly","Specific DNA sequences","Lipids","Carbohydrates"]'::jsonb, 'Specific DNA sequences', 'PCR uses thermal cycling to amplify specific DNA sequences exponentially. Requires template primers dNTPs and Taq polymerase. Each cycle: denaturation (95°C) annealing (55-65°C) extension (72°C).'),
('a823580a-5996-536e-93d0-7e249d6adf92'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'The three steps of PCR cycling are:', '["Annealing denaturation extension","Annealing extension denaturation","Denaturation annealing extension","Denaturation extension annealing","Extension denaturation annealing"]'::jsonb, 'Denaturation annealing extension', 'Denaturation (94-98°C) separates dsDNA. Annealing (50-65°C) primers bind. Extension (72°C) Taq polymerase extends primers. 25-35 cycles achieve million-fold amplification.'),
('4ad5356a-0f77-56c3-a087-f36c6c629493'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Real-time PCR (qPCR) uses:', '["End-point gel detection metabolic pathway process","Fluorescent detection of PCR product accumulation in real time","Northern blotting metabolic pathway process","Southern blotting metabolic pathway process","Radioactive detection metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Fluorescent detection of PCR product accumulation in real time', 'qPCR monitors amplification each cycle via fluorescent dyes (SYBR Green) or probes (TaqMan). Ct (threshold cycle) relates to initial template quantity. Used for gene expression and viral load.'),
('ba166438-931e-5d44-946a-599440109fdd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'SYBR Green in qPCR:', '["Degrades during amplification","Fluorescent dye that binds double-stranded DNA","Binds RNA only","Binds single-stranded DNA only","Sequence-specific probe"]'::jsonb, 'Fluorescent dye that binds double-stranded DNA', 'SYBR Green fluorescence increases when bound to dsDNA. Non-specific - detects any dsDNA product. Melt curve analysis distinguishes specific from non-specific products. Less specific than probes.'),
('92d759af-7b84-5414-99a3-94116f7fee48'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'TaqMan probes in qPCR:', '["Do not require a quencher metabolic pathway process","Non-specific dyes metabolic pathway process","Only report fluorescence metabolic pathway process","Bind to proteins metabolic pathway process","Sequence-specific hydrolysis probes with quencher and reporter"]'::jsonb, 'Sequence-specific hydrolysis probes with quencher and reporter', 'TaqMan probe: oligonucleotide with reporter (FAM) and quencher (TAMRA). Intact probe has quenched fluorescence. Taq polymerase 5''→3'' exonuclease activity cleaves probe releasing reporter. Sequence-specific.'),
('84b43f6d-a5b6-5ff4-a16e-11d09d80e2cd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Reverse transcription PCR (RT-PCR):', '["Converts RNA to cDNA then amplifies by PCR","Directly amplifies RNA","Does not require reverse transcriptase","Same as qPCR","Same as standard PCR"]'::jsonb, 'Converts RNA to cDNA then amplifies by PCR', 'RT-PCR: reverse transcriptase (MMLV AMV) converts RNA to cDNA. Then standard PCR amplification. Used for RNA virus detection (HIV HCV) and gene expression analysis.'),
('29d891df-15bc-51f6-ac82-21f4ca653ba9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Digital PCR (dPCR) provides:', '["Absolute quantification of nucleic acids without standard curve","Relative quantification metabolic pathway process","Only qualitative results metabolic pathway process","Less sensitivity than qPCR metabolic pathway process","Slower than qPCR metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Absolute quantification of nucleic acids without standard curve', 'dPCR partitions sample into thousands of droplets/wells. Poisson statistics count positive vs negative partitions giving absolute concentration without standards. Higher precision for rare mutations.'),
('59852240-9ee7-5fa7-9e48-01b971fc2182'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Droplet digital PCR (ddPCR) partitions sample into:', '["Agarose gel metabolic pathway process","96 wells metabolic pathway process","384 wells metabolic pathway process","Single tube metabolic pathway process","~20,000 nanoliter droplets in oil emulsion"]'::jsonb, '~20,000 nanoliter droplets in oil emulsion', 'ddPCR: water-oil emulsion creates ~20,000 droplets. Each droplet contains 0/1/2+ template molecules. After PCR droplets are read as positive/negative by fluorescence. Provides absolute quantification.'),
('24270f98-f05b-5f2f-80a6-e003d1b430e4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Multiplex PCR amplifies:', '["Multiple target sequences simultaneously in one reaction","A single target metabolic pathway process","Only two targets metabolic pathway process","Targets in separate tubes metabolic pathway process","Targets sequentially metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple target sequences simultaneously in one reaction', 'Multiplex PCR uses multiple primer pairs in single reaction. Optimized for equal amplification. Used for pathogen detection (respiratory panel) and microsatellite analysis. Primer design critical.'),
('bf01bc56-a399-5a8c-846b-0e1367c3f188'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Nested PCR improves:', '["Specificity by two rounds of amplification with inner and outer primers","Sensitivity only metabolic pathway process","Yield metabolic pathway process","Speed metabolic pathway process","Quantification metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Specificity by two rounds of amplification with inner and outer primers', 'Nested PCR: first round with outer primers. Second round with inner primers (targeting internal sequence). Rarely used now (contamination risk). Replaced by real-time PCR for most applications.'),
('2e713e3d-60e8-513a-b22c-739197113c25'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Allele-specific PCR detects:', '["Single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) and point mutations","Copy number variations metabolic pathway process","Translocations metabolic pathway process","Insertions metabolic pathway process","Deletions metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) and point mutations', 'Allele-specific PCR uses primers with 3'' base matching wild-type or mutant allele. Only perfectly matched primer extends. Used for genotyping (factor V Leiden cystic fibrosis).'),
('50408a56-6a44-5d35-b68d-96a58f638a77'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Methylation-specific PCR (MSP) detects:', '["DNA sequence metabolic pathway process","Histone modification metabolic pathway process","DNA methylation status using bisulfite-treated DNA and specific primers","RNA expression metabolic pathway process","Protein methylation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'DNA methylation status using bisulfite-treated DNA and specific primers', 'Bisulfite treatment converts unmethylated C to U (then T after PCR). Methylated C remains unchanged. Primers designed for methylated vs unmethylated sequences. Used for cancer epigenetics.'),
('0915a293-14c4-59ce-9b33-4d04a18ea051'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Bisulfite sequencing for DNA methylation:', '["Gold standard for methylation analysis at single-base resolution","Only for CpG islands metabolic pathway process","Less accurate than MSP metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Does not require sequencing metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Gold standard for methylation analysis at single-base resolution', 'Bisulfite treatment + Sanger/next-gen sequencing determines methylation status of each CpG. Converts unmethylated cytosines to uracils. Read as Ts after PCR. Methylated Cs remain as Cs.'),
('214d4bf2-1cd7-5507-9201-bdea3a19cb3f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. DNA cloning involves:', '["Direct DNA sequencing metabolic pathway process","Cell culture only metabolic pathway process","Protein expression only metabolic pathway process","PCR amplification only metabolic pathway process","Inserting a DNA fragment into a vector replicating in a host"]'::jsonb, 'Inserting a DNA fragment into a vector replicating in a host', 'Cloning: restriction enzyme digestion ligation into vector (plasmid bacteriophage BAC YAC). Transformation into host (E. coli yeast). Selection and screening. Amplifies specific DNA fragment.'),
('3c9b81eb-76ed-5bff-92c0-e924cc324e10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Plasmid cloning vectors contain:', '["Origin of replication multiple cloning site and selectable marker","Only selectable marker metabolic pathway process","Only antibiotic resistance metabolic pathway process","Only MCS metabolic pathway process","Only origin of replication metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Origin of replication multiple cloning site and selectable marker', 'Essential elements: ori (replication) MCS/ polylinker (restriction sites) selectable marker (ampR kanR). Optional: lacZ (blue-white screening) promoter (protein expression) tag sequences.'),
('434dc09d-d4e5-56a6-82fe-d996f9ccbb40'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Blue-white screening in cloning uses:', '["LacZ alpha-complementation","Antibiotic resistance","GFP fluorescence","PCR screening","Colony hybridization"]'::jsonb, 'LacZ alpha-complementation', 'LacZ fragment on plasmid complements host lacZdeltaM15. IPTG induces beta-galactosidase cleaving X-gal to blue. Insert disrupts lacZ producing white colonies. Distinguishes recombinants from non-recombinants.'),
('2cf97f78-6db9-57df-81b7-782d119f1bf1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Restriction enzyme digestion produces:', '["Sticky ends (overhangs) or blunt ends","Only sticky ends metabolic pathway process","Random fragments metabolic pathway process","RNA fragments metabolic pathway process","Only blunt ends metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Sticky ends (overhangs) or blunt ends', 'Restriction endonucleases recognize palindromic sequences. Staggered cuts produce sticky ends (EcoRI: G^AATTC). Blunt cuts produce flush ends (SmaI: CCC^GGG). Sticky ends facilitate directional cloning.'),
('0e4a8d5d-120c-50e7-8747-cfad07248694'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'DNA ligase joins:', '["DNA fragments by forming phosphodiester bonds","RNA fragments metabolic pathway process","Proteins metabolic pathway process","Carbohydrates metabolic pathway process","Lipids metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'DNA fragments by forming phosphodiester bonds', 'DNA ligase (T4 E. coli) catalyzes phosphodiester bond between 5''-phosphate and 3''-hydroxyl of adjacent DNA fragments. Requires ATP (T4) or NAD+ (E. coli). Seals nicks and joins fragments.'),
('c2f6cde7-2349-56e8-b5ee-f0b44d6e168e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Competent cells for transformation are:', '["Plant cells metabolic pathway process","Bacteria treated to take up foreign DNA","Mammalian cells metabolic pathway process","Yeast cells metabolic pathway process","Animal cells metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Bacteria treated to take up foreign DNA', 'Chemical competence: CaCl2 treatment + heat shock (42°C). Electrocompetence: high-voltage pulse creates pores. Transformation efficiency: 10^6-10^9 CFU/microgram DNA.'),
('24593f43-105f-52cd-ad79-25ecfcb3ffb9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Transformation efficiency is expressed as:', '["CFU per mL","CFU per microgram of DNA","OD600","Colony size","Number of colonies"]'::jsonb, 'CFU per microgram of DNA', 'Transformation efficiency: colony-forming units per microgram of input DNA. E. coli typical: 10^6-10^8 CFU/microgram. High-efficiency: 10^9-10^10. Important for library construction.'),
('201b3d52-a818-59e5-95bc-c2a595da6d5a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. The LacZ gene is commonly used as:', '["A reporter gene in cloning and expression studies","A terminator metabolic pathway process","A selectable marker metabolic pathway process","An origin of replication metabolic pathway process","A promoter metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'A reporter gene in cloning and expression studies', 'LacZ (beta-galactosidase) reporter: produces blue color with X-gal. Expression quantifiable by ONPG (yellow). Used in blue-white screening promoter analysis and transgenics.'),
('b3ef635a-47ed-560d-a959-2d98323d06a7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Green fluorescent protein (GFP) is used as:', '["A reporter for gene expression and protein localization","An antibiotic resistance marker","A cloning site","An origin of replication","A transcription factor"]'::jsonb, 'A reporter for gene expression and protein localization', 'GFP (from Aequorea victoria) fluoresces green (ex 395 nm em 509 nm). Fused to proteins of interest for localization. Variants: EGFP (enhanced) YFP CFP. Used in live-cell imaging.'),
('83b340f5-fdd6-5547-86ab-b2aa17558756'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Southern blotting detects:', '["Specific DNA sequences using labeled probes","Carbohydrates metabolic pathway process","Lipids metabolic pathway process","Specific proteins metabolic pathway process","Specific RNA sequences metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Specific DNA sequences using labeled probes', 'Southern blot (Edwin Southern 1975): DNA restriction fragments separated by gel electrophoresis transferred to membrane. Hybridization with labeled probe detects complementary sequences.'),
('6ced7722-b9b8-546c-ad8b-13dfab7c038a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Southern blotting steps in order:', '["Transfer digestion electrophoresis hybridization","Detection digestion electrophoresis transfer","Hybridization digestion electrophoresis transfer","Digestion electrophoresis transfer hybridization detection","Electrophoresis digestion transfer hybridization"]'::jsonb, 'Digestion electrophoresis transfer hybridization detection', '1) Restriction enzyme digestion 2) Agarose gel electrophoresis 3) Denaturation/neutralization 4) Capillary/electrophoretic transfer to membrane 5) Hybridization with labeled probe 6) Detection.'),
('afd98e28-3b6f-59ed-9afa-ae964b627795'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Southern blotting is used for:', '["Protein expression metabolic pathway process","Gene mapping genomic DNA analysis and mutation detection","Gene expression analysis metabolic pathway process","RNA expression metabolic pathway process","Metabolite analysis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Gene mapping genomic DNA analysis and mutation detection', 'Southern blot: detect restriction fragment length polymorphisms (RFLPs) gene rearrangements (V(D)J) and mutations. Used for sickle cell anemia (beta-globin) and hereditary diseases.'),
('3f16b933-c0c5-52ba-b54a-0da0bbf7c0af'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Northern blotting detects:', '["Lipids","Specific RNA transcripts","Specific DNA sequences","Specific proteins","Carbohydrates"]'::jsonb, 'Specific RNA transcripts', 'Northern blot (Alwine 1977): RNA separated by denaturing agarose gel (formaldehyde) transferred to membrane. Hybridized with labeled probe. Measures transcript size and abundance.'),
('4cfcde5f-6611-5792-8342-d8caa393d84f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Northern blotting is used for:', '["Gene expression analysis","Protein expression","Lipid analysis","Genomic DNA analysis","DNA mutation detection"]'::jsonb, 'Gene expression analysis', 'Northern blot: determine transcript size estimate expression levels detect alternative splicing. Less sensitive than RT-PCR and RNA-seq. Still used for validation.'),
('dcdb3e99-f9ad-5e8e-9cb0-0dd1e3e6d9b4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Western blotting detects:', '["Specific RNA sequences","Lipids","Specific DNA sequences","Specific proteins using antibodies","Carbohydrates"]'::jsonb, 'Specific proteins using antibodies', 'Western blot: proteins separated by SDS-PAGE transferred to membrane (nitrocellulose PVDF). Blocked then probed with primary antibody then enzyme-labeled secondary antibody. Detection by ECL chromogen.'),
('ef08fb0e-42fe-5023-92fd-056c82dd26c1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Western blotting transfer methods include:', '["Electroblotting","Capillary transfer","Heat transfer","UV crosslinking","Chemical transfer"]'::jsonb, 'Electroblotting', 'Electroblotting: electric current moves proteins from gel to membrane. Wet transfer (buffer tank) is efficient but slow. Semi-dry (filter paper sandwich) is faster. PVDF membrane binds proteins better than nitrocellulose.'),
('3abe262e-977c-55fa-bf2e-27ffb7bf69f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Primary antibody in Western blotting:', '["Is fluorescent metabolic pathway process","Specifically binds the target protein","Binds membrane metabolic pathway process","Is enzyme-labeled metabolic pathway process","Binds all proteins metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Specifically binds the target protein', 'Primary antibody recognizes target protein epitope. Monoclonal (specific single epitope) or polyclonal (multiple epitopes). Optimize dilution (1:100-1:10,000) for signal-to-noise ratio.'),
('032f1b32-87d0-55c4-b6ed-d90bd0f92eec'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Secondary antibody in Western blotting:', '["Binds membrane metabolic pathway process","Is fluorescent only metabolic pathway process","Is not needed metabolic pathway process","Enzyme-labeled antibody binding primary antibody Fc region","Binds target protein directly metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Enzyme-labeled antibody binding primary antibody Fc region', 'Secondary antibody: anti-species IgG (goat anti-rabbit). Conjugated to HRP (ECL) or AP (BCIP/NBT). Amplifies signal. Must be species-specific (raised against primary antibody host).'),
('cee0603a-97f6-5af5-9cc2-7485064e2849'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Detection in Western blotting commonly uses:', '["Colorimetric only metabolic pathway process","Enhanced chemiluminescence (ECL) on film or digital imager","Radioactivity only metabolic pathway process","Fluorescence only metabolic pathway process","Mass spectrometry metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Enhanced chemiluminescence (ECL) on film or digital imager', 'ECL: HRP catalyzes luminol + H2O2 producing light captured by X-ray film or CCD camera. More sensitive than colorimetric. Allows stripping and reprobing. Digital imaging provides quantification.'),
('e52c32bf-2e07-5bd9-9c03-358634727fb1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Dot blot is a:', '["Simpler method without electrophoresis sample spotted directly on membrane","Electrophoresis-based method metabolic pathway process","Chromatographic method metabolic pathway process","Column-based method metabolic pathway process","PCR-based method metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Simpler method without electrophoresis sample spotted directly on membrane', 'Dot blot: sample applied directly to membrane as dot. Probe/detect as blotting. Semi-quantitative. Used for screening multiple samples. Cannot distinguish molecular weight.'),
('efe38344-c7be-5749-bbee-e315cf2f77ea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Slot blot:', '["Western blot variant metabolic pathway process","Northern blot variant metabolic pathway process","Similar to dot blot using elongated slot format","Different from dot blot metabolic pathway process","Southern blot variant metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Similar to dot blot using elongated slot format', 'Slot blot uses slotted format for more uniform sample application. Used for quantitative DNA/RNA/Protein detection. Allows multiple samples per membrane.'),
('4dfebe55-4fbe-5a3f-b180-31cc1238f925'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Southwestern blotting detects:', '["DNA-binding proteins using labeled DNA probes","RNA-binding proteins metabolic pathway process","Protein-DNA interactions metabolic pathway process","DNA sequences metabolic pathway process","RNA sequences metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'DNA-binding proteins using labeled DNA probes', 'Southwestern blot: proteins separated by SDS-PAGE renatured on membrane. Probed with labeled DNA sequence. Identifies transcription factors and DNA-binding proteins by MW.'),
('729a6fc7-b5ef-5dc1-adec-10e1ac578872'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Far-Western blotting detects:', '["Protein","RNA","DNA","Lipid","Antibody-antigen interactions"]'::jsonb, 'Protein', 'Far-Western: probe membrane with purified protein (not antibody). Detects interacting proteins. Used for mapping protein interaction domains and binding partners.'),
('8140dfda-41f8-5710-a18a-158023b5dc76'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Eastern blotting is used for:', '["Detection of post-translational modifications","DNA detection metabolic pathway process","RNA detection metabolic pathway process","Lipid detection metabolic pathway process","Carbohydrate detection metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detection of post-translational modifications', 'Eastern blot: detects PTMs. After Western blot membrane is probed with modification-specific reagents (lectins for glycosylation antibodies for phosphorylation). Sometimes called ''Far-Eastern'' blotting.'),
('33ffc1b7-9ecd-5bcb-9040-81bbbefe7ad3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Sanger sequencing (chain termination method) uses:', '["Ribonucleotides metabolic pathway process","Dideoxynucleotides (ddNTPs) that terminate DNA synthesis","Radioactive labels only metabolic pathway process","Random primers metabolic pathway process","Deoxynucleotides only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Dideoxynucleotides (ddNTPs) that terminate DNA synthesis', 'Sanger method: DNA polymerase extends primer with dNTPs. ddNTPs (lack 3''-OH) incorporated randomly causing chain termination. Four separate reactions produce nested fragments for sequence reading.'),
('8667dc95-e091-560d-925c-0ef4d7a8c58b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. Dideoxynucleotides (ddNTPs):', '["Lack 3''-hydroxyl group preventing further chain elongation","Lack 5''-phosphate metabolic pathway process","Lack 2''-hydroxyl metabolic pathway process","Have 3''-hydroxyl metabolic pathway process","Have 2''-hydroxyl metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Lack 3''-hydroxyl group preventing further chain elongation', 'ddNTPs differ from dNTPs by missing 3''-OH. Once incorporated DNA polymerase cannot add more nucleotides. Each ddNTP is labeled with different fluorophore (Sanger sequencing).'),
('b3b1e903-33bf-562f-82c7-5b1a6e226f60'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'In automated Sanger sequencing:', '["Four ddNTPs have distinct fluorescent labels","No labels needed","Sequencing by synthesis","Sequencing by ligation","Each ddNTP is radioactive"]'::jsonb, 'Four ddNTPs have distinct fluorescent labels', 'Dye-terminator Sanger sequencing: each ddNTP (ddATP ddCTP ddGTP ddTTP) has different fluorophore. Single reaction generates all fragments. Detected by capillary electrophoresis laser.'),
('6cf7fda6-fa3c-53d5-861e-b27750f00341'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. Next-generation sequencing (NGS) platforms:', '["Sequence millions of DNA fragments simultaneously in massively parallel fashion","Sequence one fragment at a time metabolic pathway process","Are less accurate metabolic pathway process","Are slower than Sanger metabolic pathway process","Have lower throughput metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Sequence millions of DNA fragments simultaneously in massively parallel fashion', 'NGS (Illumina Ion Torrent PacBio): library preparation clonal amplification massive parallel sequencing. Generates billions of bases per run. Dramatically reduced cost per base vs Sanger.'),
('78270408-df8c-5769-9e84-fb622b6ff0af'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Illumina sequencing uses:', '["Sequencing by synthesis with reversible terminators","Pyrosequencing metabolic pathway process","Ion detection metabolic pathway process","Single-molecule real-time metabolic pathway process","Nanopore metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Sequencing by synthesis with reversible terminators', 'Illumina: bridge PCR on flow cell creates clusters. Reversible terminators incorporate one labeled nucleotide per cycle. Imaging identifies base. Cycles repeated. Most widely used NGS platform.'),
('6d19d729-5cdf-5f62-9618-71cc688778d0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Ion Torrent sequencing detects:', '["Protons (H+) released during nucleotide incorporation","Fluorescent signal metabolic pathway process","Light from luciferase metabolic pathway process","Current change metabolic pathway process","Radioactive decay metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Protons (H+) released during nucleotide incorporation', 'Ion Torrent: semiconductor chip detects pH change from H+ release during DNA polymerization. Natural nucleotides (not modified). Faster than Illumina but homopolymer errors are common.'),
('18eec093-5982-5f89-be84-2bc1f6339f21'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Pyrosequencing uses:', '["pH detection metabolic pathway process","Luciferase to detect pyrophosphate (PPi) release from nucleotide incorporation","Radioactive detection metabolic pathway process","Current detection metabolic pathway process","Fluorescent terminators metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Luciferase to detect pyrophosphate (PPi) release from nucleotide incorporation', 'Pyrosequencing: PPi released during dNTP incorporation is converted to ATP by sulfurylase. Luciferase uses ATP to produce light. Real-time detection. Used for SNP analysis and bacterial typing.'),
('97b7febd-f460-506c-80f8-fc82e88c621f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Pacific Biosciences (PacBio) SMRT sequencing:', '["Chemical detection","Nanopore detection","Single-molecule real-time sequencing using zero-mode waveguides","Sequencing by synthesis on flow cell","pH-based detection"]'::jsonb, 'Single-molecule real-time sequencing using zero-mode waveguides', 'PacBio: single DNA polymerase molecule immobilized at bottom of zero-mode waveguide (ZMW). Fluorescent nucleotides detected in real time. Long reads (10-30 kb) but higher error rate.'),
('fa55a3af-a184-50bd-b0ca-68893bda36a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Oxford Nanopore sequencing detects:', '["Fluorescent signals metabolic pathway process","pH changes metabolic pathway process","Light emission metabolic pathway process","Radioactive decay metabolic pathway process","Ionic current changes as single DNA strands pass through nanopores"]'::jsonb, 'Ionic current changes as single DNA strands pass through nanopores', 'Nanopore: protein nanopore (alpha-hemolysin) in membrane. Voltage applied DNA passes through. Each base causes characteristic current blockade. Portable (MinION) real-time ultra-long reads.'),
('1800d9c1-43d3-5cf6-bd56-23b6a34c5aaf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'The MinION device by Oxford Nanopore:', '["Not portable metabolic pathway process","Portable USB-powered DNA sequencer for real-time field sequencing","Only for Sanger sequencing metabolic pathway process","Requires large infrastructure metabolic pathway process","Tabletop sequencer metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Portable USB-powered DNA sequencer for real-time field sequencing', 'MinION: small device (~100g) plugs into USB3. Generates long reads (>100 kb). Used for outbreak surveillance (Ebola Zika SARS-CoV-2) and rapid pathogen identification in field settings.'),
('296723ad-b6d6-5b4a-8ac4-ede7f1a10604'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. RNA sequencing (RNA-seq) workflow:', '["Metabolite analysis metabolic pathway process","Sequencing directly without processing metabolic pathway process","DNA sequencing only metabolic pathway process","Isolate RNA reverse transcribe to cDNA prepare library sequence analyze","Protein sequencing metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Isolate RNA reverse transcribe to cDNA prepare library sequence analyze', 'RNA-seq: rRNA depletion/polyA selection. Fragmentation. Reverse transcription to cDNA. Adapter ligation. PCR amplification. NGS sequencing. Bioinformatic alignment and quantification.'),
('f953121e-ed6c-564c-908b-3927e8af6cea'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Single-cell RNA-seq (scRNA-seq):', '["Is less sensitive than bulk metabolic pathway process","Measures only one gene metabolic pathway process","Profiles bulk tissue only metabolic pathway process","Requires millions of cells metabolic pathway process","Profiles gene expression of individual cells revealing heterogeneity"]'::jsonb, 'Profiles gene expression of individual cells revealing heterogeneity', 'scRNA-seq uses droplet-based (10x Genomics) or plate-based (Smart-seq2) methods. Each cell''s transcriptome separately barcoded. Reveals cell types states and rare populations.'),
('cf20926a-8093-58d7-bfa0-e6dc92192b38'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'CRISPR-Cas9 for genome editing:', '["Cas9 endonuclease creates double-strand break at guide RNA-targeted sequence","Does not cut DNA metabolic pathway process","Only binds DNA metabolic pathway process","Requires no guide RNA metabolic pathway process","Is a transcription factor metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Cas9 endonuclease creates double-strand break at guide RNA-targeted sequence', 'Cas9 + guide RNA (gRNA) target specific genomic sequence. PAM (NGG) required. DSB repaired by NHEJ (indel knockout) or HDR (with donor template precise editing).'),
('f3434d31-928e-5462-bcfd-39450a7e978e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'CRISPR-Cas12a (Cpf1) differs from Cas9 by:', '["Requires tracrRNA metabolic pathway process","Creates blunt cut metabolic pathway process","Recognizes T-rich PAM and creates staggered cut","Is larger than Cas9 metabolic pathway process","Recognizes G-rich PAM metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Recognizes T-rich PAM and creates staggered cut', 'Cpf1: TTTV PAM. Staggered cut (5'' overhang). Single crRNA (no tracrRNA needed). Also has collateral cleavage activity (used for diagnostic SHERLOCK). Smaller size aids delivery.'),
('d6e8f4b7-eace-596c-adb4-04e64f451712'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'CRISPR-Cas13 targets:', '["RNA","DNA","Proteins","Lipids","Carbohydrates"]'::jsonb, 'RNA', 'Cas13 is an RNA-guided RNase. Binds and cleaves target RNA. Collateral cleavage of nearby RNAs enhances signal. Used for RNA knockdown (CRISPRi) and diagnostics (SHERLOCK Cas13-based).'),
('9676b21f-8d01-50e0-a89b-35f8361ffd26'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'SHERLOCK (Cas13-based diagnostic):', '["Detects nucleic acids at attomolar sensitivity with fluorescent readout","Detects only RNA metabolic pathway process","Requires expensive equipment metabolic pathway process","Detects only DNA metabolic pathway process","Detects proteins metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detects nucleic acids at attomolar sensitivity with fluorescent readout', 'SHERLOCK: Cas13 + gRNA + reporter RNA. Target recognition activates collateral RNase activity cleaving reporter. Fluorescent/lateral flow readout. Sensitive rapid (<1 h) point-of-care pathogen detection.'),
('e55a9b78-dbe5-533e-95d7-0e279ddb297f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'DETECTR (Cas12-based diagnostic):', '["DNA detection using Cas12 collateral cleavage for lateral flow readout","RNA detection metabolic pathway process","Protein detection metabolic pathway process","Cell detection metabolic pathway process","Tissue detection metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'DNA detection using Cas12 collateral cleavage for lateral flow readout', 'DETECTR: Cas12a + gRNA + reporter ssDNA. Target DNA binding activates collateral DNase. Cleavage of reporter detected by lateral flow. Used for HPV and SARS-CoV-2 detection.'),
('d1b04023-7860-5c38-8d0d-3d68b8922531'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH):', '["Detects metabolites metabolic pathway process","Detects carbohydrates metabolic pathway process","Detects proteins metabolic pathway process","Detects specific DNA/RNA sequences in fixed cells or tissue using fluorescent probes","Detects lipids metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detects specific DNA/RNA sequences in fixed cells or tissue using fluorescent probes', 'FISH: denature DNA hybridize with fluorescently labeled probe. Visualize by fluorescence microscopy. Used for chromosome abnormalities gene mapping and pathogen detection.'),
('01225ea5-e7ea-5012-8c7d-272b19d538ca'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'FISH is used in clinical cytogenetics for:', '["Protein expression metabolic pathway process","Gene expression metabolic pathway process","DNA methylation metabolic pathway process","Detecting chromosomal aneuploidies deletions and translocations","Metabolite analysis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detecting chromosomal aneuploidies deletions and translocations', 'FISH detects specific chromosome abnormalities: trisomies (13 18 21) deletions (22q11 DiGeorge) translocations (BCR-ABL Philadelphia). Interphase FISH on uncultured cells (rapid).'),
('308a2ff0-93ba-559f-94f3-e9f27975fd46'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Comparative genomic hybridization (CGH) / microarray:', '["Detects genome-wide copy number variations","Detects sequence mutations","Detects DNA methylation","Detects RNA expression","Detects protein expression"]'::jsonb, 'Detects genome-wide copy number variations', 'Array CGH: test and reference DNA labeled different fluorophores hybridized to array of genomic clones/oligonucleotides. Ratio indicates gain/loss. Resolution depends on probe density.'),
('0a974633-444e-50bb-abe3-e16bef55a92d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Single nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) arrays:', '["Detect only insertions metabolic pathway process","Genotype hundreds of thousands of SNPs for GWAS and CNV detection","Sequence whole genome metabolic pathway process","Detect only deletions metabolic pathway process","Detect rare variants only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Genotype hundreds of thousands of SNPs for GWAS and CNV detection', 'SNP arrays (Illumina Affymetrix) use allele-specific probes. Genotype 500k-2M SNPs. Used for GWAS ancestry chromosomal abnormalities (loss of heterozygosity) and CNVs.'),
('2ce37a57-9eeb-5c2f-880e-2e8721bf095a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. DNA microarrays (gene chips):', '["PCR on solid surface metabolic pathway process","Cell culture array metabolic pathway process","Sequencing on solid surface metabolic pathway process","Thousands of probes immobilized on solid surface for gene expression analysis","Protein array on membrane metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Thousands of probes immobilized on solid surface for gene expression analysis', 'DNA microarrays: probes (oligonucleotides/cDNAs) spotted or synthesized on glass slide/silicon chip. Labeled cDNA from sample hybridizes. Fluorescence intensity reflects expression level.'),
('e9110c43-103f-571d-8b19-f784f77d6b38'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Affymetrix GeneChip technology uses:', '["Bead-based arrays metabolic pathway process","Nylon membrane arrays metabolic pathway process","Photolithographic in situ synthesis of oligonucleotide probes","PCR products metabolic pathway process","Spotted cDNAs metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Photolithographic in situ synthesis of oligonucleotide probes', 'Affymetrix: 25-mer probes synthesized directly on chip using photolithography and photolabile protecting groups. Perfect match (PM) and mismatch (MM) probes reduce cross-hybridization.'),
('c0a6c4c4-5a72-55c7-8a9b-3ba65fcfeb90'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Two-color microarray experiment:', '["No labeling needed metabolic pathway process","Radioactive labeling metabolic pathway process","Three samples hybridized metabolic pathway process","Single sample hybridized metabolic pathway process","Test and reference cDNA labeled with Cy3 and Cy5 co-hybridized"]'::jsonb, 'Test and reference cDNA labeled with Cy3 and Cy5 co-hybridized', 'Cy3 (green 550 nm) and Cy5 (red 649 nm). Ratio of red/green indicates relative expression. Dye-swap replicates control for labeling bias. Normalization corrects for systematic variation.'),
('07c3cd0e-dbc5-56d8-8a83-3cd671169f66'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. Gene expression microarray data is normalized by:', '["RMA (robust multi-array average) quantile normalization","Manual normalization metabolic pathway process","Visual inspection metabolic pathway process","Serial dilution metabolic pathway process","No normalization needed metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'RMA (robust multi-array average) quantile normalization', 'RMA: background correction quantile normalization log2 transformation and probe summarization. Other methods: MAS5.0 (Affymetrix) vsn (variance stabilization). Normalization essential for cross-array comparison.'),
('739bac4c-b953-5f97-9233-d6fe0547c819'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Protein microarrays detect:', '["Protein biochemical reaction","Only protein expression","Only RNA expression","Only lipid binding","Only DNA hybridization"]'::jsonb, 'Protein biochemical reaction', 'Protein microarrays: capture antibodies or proteins immobilized on surface. Used for profiling protein abundance protein interactions and autoantibody detection in autoimmune diseases.'),
('afea4361-bb73-5d28-a54d-dfe1f1b03773'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Antibody microarrays (reverse-phase arrays):', '["Probes are antibodies capturing target proteins from sample","Probes are antigens metabolic pathway process","Probes are DNA metabolic pathway process","Probes are RNA metabolic pathway process","Probes are lipids metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Probes are antibodies capturing target proteins from sample', 'Antibody arrays: pre-printed antibodies capture target proteins from lysate. Detection with labeled secondary antibodies. Multiplexed protein quantification for biomarkers and signaling pathways.'),
('d1411d2b-f091-513a-9b7e-1285532ba15b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Tissue microarrays (TMA):', '["Multiple tissue cores from different specimens arrayed on single slide","Single tissue per slide metabolic pathway process","Cell culture array metabolic pathway process","Protein array metabolic pathway process","DNA array metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Multiple tissue cores from different specimens arrayed on single slide', 'TMA: 0.6-2 mm cores from paraffin blocks arrayed in recipient block. Single slide contains 50-1000 specimens. High-throughput analysis of protein/RNA expression by IHC ISH. Conserves precious tissue.'),
('83f3dee9-9aa0-5e6b-b296-41805686a292'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Protein quantification by Biuret method:', '["Means fluorescence metabolic pathway process","Uses Bradford dye metabolic pathway process","Means absorbance at 260 nm metabolic pathway process","Peptide bonds form purple complex with Cu2+ in alkaline solution","Uses Lowry reagent metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Peptide bonds form purple complex with Cu2+ in alkaline solution', 'Biuret: Cu2+ forms purple complex with peptide bonds in alkaline solution (540 nm). Simple but less sensitive (1-20 mg/mL). Used for total protein in serum/CSF.'),
('711779c6-ddf3-54aa-81ca-6d70e28b7c56'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Lowry method for protein:', '["Does not require standards metabolic pathway process","Uses only copper metabolic pathway process","Uses only phenol reagent metabolic pathway process","Less sensitive than Biuret metabolic pathway process","Folin-Ciocalteu reagent reacts with tyrosine/tryptophan"]'::jsonb, 'Folin-Ciocalteu reagent reacts with tyrosine/tryptophan', 'Lowry: Cu2+ treatment (Biuret reaction) + Folin-Ciocalteu reagent reduction by Tyr/Trp (blue 750 nm). More sensitive (5-100 microgram/mL) but many interfering substances.'),
('cc36f261-eb2e-527e-a534-167d03139642'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Bradford assay (Coomassie Blue):', '["Lowry-based metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Biuret-based metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","SDS-based assay metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No dye binding metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Coomassie Brilliant Blue G-250 binds protein shifting absorbance to 595 nm"]'::jsonb, 'Coomassie Brilliant Blue G-250 binds protein shifting absorbance to 595 nm', 'Bradford: Coomassie Blue G-250 shifts from red (465 nm) to blue (595 nm) upon protein binding. Fast simple. Compatible with reducing agents. Interference from detergents.'),
('f35cbe78-6c9f-54ff-9384-74099b49e75e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'BCA (bicinchoninic acid) assay:', '["Same as Lowry metabolic pathway process","Same as Biuret metabolic pathway process","Fluorescent assay metabolic pathway process","Same as Bradford metabolic pathway process","Cu+ produced from Cu2+ by protein reacts with BCA forming purple complex"]'::jsonb, 'Cu+ produced from Cu2+ by protein reacts with BCA forming purple complex', 'BCA: Cu2+ reduced to Cu+ by protein (Biuret). Cu+ + BCA forms purple complex (562 nm). More sensitive than Biuret. Compatible with detergents (unlike Bradford). Interference from reducing agents.'),
('32ac5994-54eb-5149-9083-75ab5f9a6638'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'UV absorbance at 280 nm estimates protein by:', '["Tryptophan and tyrosine absorbance","Sugar absorbance","Nucleic acid absorbance","Peptide bond absorbance","Lipid absorbance"]'::jsonb, 'Tryptophan and tyrosine absorbance', 'A280: tryptophan (main contributor) and tyrosine absorbance. Extinction coefficient depends on aromatic amino acid content. Used for purified proteins. Contamination by DNA/RNA absorbs at 260 nm.'),
('56d95122-f90d-589c-a7b5-2f6bc153c041'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. Ratio A260/A280 for pure DNA is approximately:', '["1.5","1.8","1.0","2.0","0.5"]'::jsonb, '1.8', 'Pure DNA: A260/A280 ~1.8. Pure RNA: ~2.0. Lower values indicate protein/phenol contamination. Higher values may indicate RNA contamination. Ratio A260/A230 (secondary) indicates other contaminants.'),
('8f956943-5e44-5e25-8138-33b3c32c95bd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Nucleic acid quantification by UV at 260 nm:', '["A260 = 1 = 1 microgram/mL metabolic pathway process","A260 = 1 = 25 microgram/mL metabolic pathway process","A260 = 1 = 10 microgram/mL metabolic pathway process","A260 = 1 = 100 microgram/mL metabolic pathway process","A260 = 1 corresponds to 50 microgram/mL dsDNA or 40 microgram/mL RNA"]'::jsonb, 'A260 = 1 corresponds to 50 microgram/mL dsDNA or 40 microgram/mL RNA', 'Extinction coefficients: 1 OD260 = 50 ng/microliter dsDNA; 40 ng/microliter RNA; 33 ng/microliter ssDNA. OD260/OD280 <1.8 indicates contamination.'),
('da7794c6-8d76-5c05-8d02-7f596eec732b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'NanoDrop spectrophotometer:', '["Measures 1-2 microliter samples without cuvettes","Large sample volume metabolic pathway process","Low sensitivity metabolic pathway process","Requires standard cuvettes metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Measures 1-2 microliter samples without cuvettes', 'NanoDrop uses surface tension to hold sample between pedestals. Path length 1.0-0.05 mm (auto-range). Measures A260 A280 A230 and ratios. Quick (<5 sec) nucleic acid/protein quantification.'),
('035b8089-3947-50ec-a380-4b37404a5bbb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Qubit fluorometer uses:', '["Turbidity measurement metabolic pathway process","Radioactive detection metabolic pathway process","UV absorbance metabolic pathway process","Fluorescent dyes specific for dsDNA RNA protein","Colorimetric detection metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Fluorescent dyes specific for dsDNA RNA protein', 'Qubit: fluorescent dyes (PicoGreen for dsDNA RiboGreen for RNA) have high specificity and sensitivity. Quantification is more accurate than UV for low concentrations. Calibration with standards.'),
('bd3a2a4a-8d3b-5829-be44-0039d73b5987'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'PicoGreen dye specifically binds:', '["Double-stranded DNA","Single-stranded DNA","RNA","Protein","Lipids"]'::jsonb, 'Double-stranded DNA', 'PicoGreen is highly selective for dsDNA. Fluorescence enhanced >1000-fold upon binding. Detection limit ~25 pg/mL. Used for quantifying dsDNA for NGS library preparation.'),
('bb8f8cf3-820f-511e-a1e9-20c83cebf2f2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'RiboGreen dye quantifies:', '["dsDNA","Protein","ssDNA","RNA","Lipids"]'::jsonb, 'RNA', 'RiboGreen is specific for RNA. Linear detection over three orders of magnitude. Used for RNA quantification for RNA-seq and other RNA-based applications.'),
('36fad8aa-8155-5092-ba5d-97a979928d00'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Enzyme-coupled assays measure:', '["Substrate consumption or product formation via coupled enzymatic reactions","Direct absorbance only metabolic pathway process","Fluorescence only metabolic pathway process","Chemiluminescence only metabolic pathway process","Radioactivity only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Substrate consumption or product formation via coupled enzymatic reactions', 'Enzyme-coupled assays use auxiliary enzymes to convert product to detectable compound (e.g. glucose + HK + G6PD + NADP+ measured at 340 nm). Clinically used for glucose cholesterol creatinine.'),
('82471c98-ae4d-580f-a83a-41a588c59c02'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Glucose estimation by glucose oxidase method:', '["Hexokinase method only","Enzyme immunoassay","GOD + POD + chromogen","Reducing method only","NBT reduction"]'::jsonb, 'GOD + POD + chromogen', 'GOD method: glucose + O2 → gluconic acid + H2O2. H2O2 + chromogen (ABTS TMB) → color. Specific for glucose. No interference from other reducing substances (unlike Clinitest).'),
('a923ca4e-ca28-5470-b47f-0851ab7ace79'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Hexokinase method for glucose:', '["Reference method using HK + G6PD producing NADPH","Same as glucose oxidase metabolic pathway process","Less accurate than GOD metabolic pathway process","No longer used metabolic pathway process","Not specific metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Reference method using HK + G6PD producing NADPH', 'HK method: glucose + ATP → G6P (HK). G6P + NADP+ → 6-PG + NADPH (G6PD). NADPH measured at 340 nm. Reference (gold standard) method. More expensive than GOD.'),
('1e217ed2-aac1-5966-b56f-de6c7d69f668'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Cholesterol estimation:', '["Cholesterol esterase + oxidase + POD + chromogen","Biuret method metabolic pathway process","GOD method metabolic pathway process","Hexokinase method metabolic pathway process","Bradford assay metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Cholesterol esterase + oxidase + POD + chromogen', 'Cholesterol assay: cholesteryl ester → free cholesterol + fatty acids (esterase). Cholesterol + O2 → cholestenone + H2O2 (oxidase). H2O2 + chromogen (POD). Measures total cholesterol.'),
('6d606aa6-6b39-5d7f-b6c8-bd91edd23af3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. A middle-aged male is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. HDL cholesterol measurement:', '["Direct measurement without separation metabolic pathway process","Size-exclusion only metabolic pathway process","Electrophoresis only metabolic pathway process","Ultracentrifugation only metabolic pathway process","Precipitation of other lipoproteins then measure cholesterol in supernatant"]'::jsonb, 'Precipitation of other lipoproteins then measure cholesterol in supernatant', 'HDL-C: phosphotungstate/Mg2+ or dextran sulfate/Mg2+ precipitates VLDL and LDL. Centrifuge measure cholesterol in supernatant (HDL). Direct homogeneous assays are now common.'),
('ac9b2edc-3f53-57a6-b7cc-488f3e7b30e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'LDL cholesterol is commonly calculated by:', '["Electrophoresis","Ultracentrifugation","Precipitation","Direct measurement only","Friedewald formula: LDL = Total - HDL -"]'::jsonb, 'Friedewald formula: LDL = Total - HDL -', 'Friedewald: LDL-C = TC - HDL-C - (TG/5) mg/dL. Valid when TG <400 mg/dL. Direct measurement preferred for non-fasting or high TG metabolics.'),
('7db1b77a-6243-51c3-b293-287cfa8c0ca6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Triglyceride estimation:', '["Uricase method metabolic pathway process","Lipase hydrolysis to glycerol then enzymatic measurement","HMG method metabolic pathway process","Cholesterol esterase method metabolic pathway process","Glucose oxidase method metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Lipase hydrolysis to glycerol then enzymatic measurement', 'Triglyceride: lipoprotein lipase releases glycerol. Glycerol + ATP → G3P (GK). G3P + O2 → DAP + H2O2 (GPO). H2O2 + chromogen (POD). Correct for free glycerol.'),
('53fd4728-4bc9-5f57-9ac6-4db25a21ad25'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Uric acid estimation by uricase method:', '["Enzyme immunoassay metabolic pathway process","Uric acid + O2 → allantoin + H2O2 (uricase) then H2O2 detection","Xanthine oxidase method metabolic pathway process","Phosphotungstate reduction metabolic pathway process","Absorbance at 293 nm metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Uric acid + O2 → allantoin + H2O2 (uricase) then H2O2 detection', 'Uricase: specific oxidation of uric acid to allantoin + H2O2. H2O2 measured peroxidase-coupled chromogen (Trinder reaction). Replaced less specific phosphotungstate method.'),
('31cdb571-ceb4-597f-9ce7-de4095065087'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Creatinine estimation using Jaffe reaction:', '["Enzymatic method only metabolic pathway process","Fluorescence metabolic pathway process","ELISA metabolic pathway process","UV absorbance metabolic pathway process","Picrate + creatinine in alkaline solution forms red-orange complex"]'::jsonb, 'Picrate + creatinine in alkaline solution forms red-orange complex', 'Jaffe: alkaline picrate reacts with creatinine (max 520 nm). Simple but interference from ketones bilirubin cephalosporins. Enzymatic methods (creatininase) more specific.'),
('7c180c81-926f-5673-a8ea-308aae445839'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Enzymatic creatinine method:', '["Less specific than Jaffe metabolic pathway process","Same as Jaffe metabolic pathway process","No enzymatic steps metabolic pathway process","Picrate method metabolic pathway process","Creatininase → creatine → sarcosine → H2O2 detected peroxidase"]'::jsonb, 'Creatininase → creatine → sarcosine → H2O2 detected peroxidase', 'Enzymatic: creatinine + H2O → creatine (creatininase). Creatine + H2O → sarcosine + urea (creatinase). Sarcosine + O2 → glycine + H2O2 + formaldehyde (sarcosine oxidase). H2O2 detection.'),
('6d10475c-8a4b-530e-8b43-05a8914b8a9f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Blood urea nitrogen (BUN) estimation:', '["Bradford assay metabolic pathway process","Jaffe reaction metabolic pathway process","Urease converts urea to ammonia then measured","Direct UV absorbance metabolic pathway process","Biuret reaction metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Urease converts urea to ammonia then measured', 'Urease: urea + H2O → 2NH3 + CO2. NH3 measured by Berthelot (phenol-hypochlorite blue) or enzymatic (GLDH coupled reaction NADH consumption at 340 nm).'),
('2384938b-6bb7-5818-bfe1-049cc8e74883'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Serum electrolyte measurement uses:', '["Titration only metabolic pathway process","Atomic absorption only metabolic pathway process","Ion-selective electrodes (ISE) for Na K Cl Ca","Flame photometry only metabolic pathway process","All manual methods metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Ion-selective electrodes (ISE) for Na K Cl Ca', 'ISE: potentiometric measurement using ion-specific membranes. Na (glass electrode) K (valinomycin) Cl (ion exchanger). Direct ISE (undiluted) more accurate than indirect ISE (diluted).'),
('0dd57041-a9fb-540a-b856-f2b0a1ddd81a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Flame photometry measures:', '["All electrolytes metabolic pathway process","Na and K by emission at characteristic wavelengths","Mg and Ca metabolic pathway process","Cl and HCO3 metabolic pathway process","Fe and Cu metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Na and K by emission at characteristic wavelengths', 'Flame photometry: sample aspirated into flame. Excited atoms emit light at characteristic wavelengths. Intensity proportional to concentration. Lithium internal standard. Largely replaced by ISE.'),
('ce4b6526-1eb6-5640-bdf7-65eb66ea4478'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Blood gas analysis measures:', '["Only pCO2 metabolic pathway process","Only pO2 metabolic pathway process","Only HCO3- metabolic pathway process","pH pCO2 pO2 HCO3- and calculated parameters","Only pH metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'pH pCO2 pO2 HCO3- and calculated parameters', 'Blood gas analyzer: pH (glass electrode) pCO2 (Severinghaus electrode) pO2 (Clark electrode). Calculates HCO3- base excess O2 saturation. Essential for acid-base and respiratory assessment.'),
('3b936def-7ba4-5754-87ad-417ca50ff473'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Clark electrode measures:', '["pH","Glucose","pO2","Lactate","pCO2"]'::jsonb, 'pO2', 'Clark electrode (polarographic): platinum cathode Ag/AgCl anode. O2 reduced at cathode producing current proportional to pO2. Covered by O2-permeable membrane. Used in blood gas analyzers.'),
('f1980be2-d8b5-521e-b127-bc16d0d84de3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Severinghaus electrode measures:', '["K+","pH","pCO2","pO2","Na+"]'::jsonb, 'pCO2', 'Severinghaus: CO2 diffuses through membrane into bicarbonate solution changing pH. pH change measured by glass electrode. pCO2 calculated from pH change. Used in blood gas analyzers.'),
('3a13cfaa-c6ed-5824-af3d-176e4e5d0d0a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Recombinant DNA technology involves:', '["Only expression metabolic pathway process","Only cloning metabolic pathway process","Only sequencing metabolic pathway process","Only PCR metabolic pathway process","Combining DNA from different sources into new molecules"]'::jsonb, 'Combining DNA from different sources into new molecules', 'Recombinant DNA: restriction enzymes cut DNA at specific sites. DNA ligase joins fragments. Vector carries recombinant DNA into host. Produces modified organisms/proteins.'),
('85305bed-d18a-50d1-b4f2-04bda624ab8c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. cDNA (complementary DNA) is:', '["Synthetic DNA metabolic pathway process","Bacterial DNA metabolic pathway process","PCR product metabolic pathway process","DNA synthesized from mRNA template by reverse transcriptase","Genomic DNA metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'DNA synthesized from mRNA template by reverse transcriptase', 'cDNA: reverse transcriptase uses mRNA template with oligo-dT or random primers. cDNA lacks introns making it suitable for bacterial expression of eukaryotic proteins.'),
('6d3e5c07-5cda-50e0-bc51-083c8caaa54f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Genomic DNA library:', '["Collection of PCR products metabolic pathway process","Collection of plasmids metabolic pathway process","Collection of cDNA clones metabolic pathway process","Collection of phages metabolic pathway process","Collection of clones containing all genomic DNA fragments"]'::jsonb, 'Collection of clones containing all genomic DNA fragments', 'Genomic library: genomic DNA fragmented partially digested with restriction enzymes. Fragments inserted into vectors. Represents entire genome. Used for genome sequencing and gene discovery.'),
('7bb3d89f-0d47-5a6b-88f4-aa8aa135f334'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. cDNA library represents:', '["Mitochondrial DNA only metabolic pathway process","Intronic sequences only metabolic pathway process","Non-coding sequences only metabolic pathway process","Expressed genes (transcribed sequences) from a specific tissue/cell","All genomic sequences metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Expressed genes (transcribed sequences) from a specific tissue/cell', 'cDNA library: mRNA isolated from specific tissue/cell type converted to cDNA. Represents genes expressed at that time. Enriched for coding sequences (no introns).'),
('c8388422-e9c1-52f4-a141-f6425e35e0ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Screening a library uses:', '["PCR only metabolic pathway process","Spectroscopy only metabolic pathway process","Sequencing only metabolic pathway process","Microscopy only metabolic pathway process","Colony/plaque hybridization with labeled probe"]'::jsonb, 'Colony/plaque hybridization with labeled probe', 'Library screening: transfer colonies/plaques to membrane. Denature and hybridize with labeled probe (radioactive/fluorescent). Identify positive clones. PCR screening (colony PCR) is also common.'),
('ba42bfab-6de0-5271-b0f8-3dd19d9336c3'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Expression vectors allow:', '["Only DNA cloning metabolic pathway process","Only storage metabolic pathway process","Only transformation metabolic pathway process","Only sequencing metabolic pathway process","Production of recombinant proteins in host cells"]'::jsonb, 'Production of recombinant proteins in host cells', 'Expression vectors: promoter (T7 lac) ribosome binding site terminator tag sequences (His GST MBP). Inducible expression (IPTG). Used for producing recombinant proteins for research and therapeutics.'),
('0759c4d3-201c-5ac0-ba6c-fd341f2c887a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Bacterial expression systems (E. coli):', '["Fast growth high yield but no post-translational modifications","Produce eukaryotic PTMs","Cannot express recombinant proteins","Slow growth","Low yield"]'::jsonb, 'Fast growth high yield but no post-translational modifications', 'E. coli: rapid growth (20 min doubling) high yield low cost. Limitations: no glycosylation no disulfide bond formation (cytoplasm). Inclusion bodies common. Used for simple proteins.'),
('0982e1a8-564e-5529-92c4-0a38bde4f997'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Yeast expression systems (S. cerevisiae P. pastoris):', '["Provide some PTMs (glycosylation) and secrete proteins","No PTMs metabolic pathway process","Low yield metabolic pathway process","Slow growth metabolic pathway process","Cannot secrete proteins metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Provide some PTMs (glycosylation) and secrete proteins', 'Yeast: eukaryotic PTMs (N-glycosylation disulfide bonds). P. pastoris (methanol-inducible AOX1 promoter) for high-level secretion. Used for recombinant proteins (hepatitis B vaccine insulin).'),
('ab8981c4-351b-5e1c-864a-4f3744e182e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Mammalian expression systems (CHO HEK293):', '["Full mammalian PTMs proper folding and function","Simple PTMs metabolic pathway process","No secretion metabolic pathway process","Low yield metabolic pathway process","Bacterial glycosylation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Full mammalian PTMs proper folding and function', 'CHO (Chinese hamster ovary) cells: most common for therapeutic proteins. Produce human-like glycosylation. Stable expression. Used for monoclonal antibodies (rituximab trastuzumab).'),
('7149b2d0-3c2b-5163-9a0a-ed70052acbeb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Baculovirus-insect cell expression:', '["Mammalian glycosylation metabolic pathway process","No PTMs metabolic pathway process","High expression with insect-type glycosylation","Bacterial PTMs metabolic pathway process","Low expression metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'High expression with insect-type glycosylation', 'Baculovirus (Autographa californica NPV) infects insect cells (Sf9 Hi5). Strong polyhedrin promoter. High protein yields. Glycosylation is insect-type (high mannose). Used for vaccine production.'),
('d38cd29b-f669-5422-b966-01a63121f0a2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Site-directed mutagenesis:', '["Introduces specific mutations into cloned DNA","Random mutagenesis metabolic pathway process","Insertion only metabolic pathway process","PCR error metabolic pathway process","Deletion only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Introduces specific mutations into cloned DNA', 'Site-directed mutagenesis: primer with desired mutation extended by PCR. Template digested by DpnI (methyl-specific). Mutant plasmid transformed. Used for protein structure-function studies and enzyme engineering.'),
('f7d9cbd6-77fe-55af-99c2-cbf67668dcd7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Phage display technology:', '["Screens libraries in vivo metabolic pathway process","Sequences proteins directly metabolic pathway process","Synthesizes proteins chemically metabolic pathway process","Displays peptides/proteins on phage surface for selection","Expresses proteins in bacteria metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Displays peptides/proteins on phage surface for selection', 'Phage display: foreign DNA fused to phage coat protein (pIII pVIII). Library of variants displayed on phage surface. Panning selects binding variants. Used for antibody engineering (Humira) and peptide discovery.'),
('89fb1f2f-075b-5195-a19a-1fcb85e0b912'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Yeast two-hybrid system detects:', '["Protein","DNA","RNA","Protein-lipid interactions","Protein-sugar interactions"]'::jsonb, 'Protein', 'Yeast two-hybrid: bait (DNA-binding domain fusion) + prey (activation domain fusion). Interaction activates reporter gene (lacZ HIS3). Used for mapping protein interaction networks.'),
('663bc161-eceb-5096-93a3-df082db1a473'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. Chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP):', '["Identifies DNA regions bound by specific proteins in cells","RNA","Protein","DNA methylation","Protein-DNA interactions in vitro"]'::jsonb, 'Identifies DNA regions bound by specific proteins in cells', 'ChIP: crosslink protein-DNA. Sonication shears chromatin. Antibody pulls down target protein + bound DNA. Reverse crosslinks purify DNA. qPCR or sequencing (ChIP-seq) identifies binding sites.'),
('c4d485d2-5942-53b0-b095-cc40f68fa584'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Electrophoretic mobility shift assay (EMSA):', '["Uses antibodies metabolic pathway process","Uses sequencing metabolic pathway process","Detects protein-DNA binding by reduced gel mobility","Detects lipid binding metabolic pathway process","Detects RNA binding only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detects protein-DNA binding by reduced gel mobility', 'EMSA: labeled DNA probe + protein. Complex migrates slower than free probe on native gel. Specificity shown by competitor/supershift. Used for transcription factor binding studies.'),
('d5b05530-02a9-55f4-8237-d691c51b01a1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'DNase I footprinting identifies:', '["Protein-binding DNA regions protected from DNase I digestion","Protein sequence metabolic pathway process","Lipid binding metabolic pathway process","RNA sequence metabolic pathway process","DNA sequence metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Protein-binding DNA regions protected from DNase I digestion', 'Footprinting: labeled DNA + protein partially digested with DNase I. Protected region appears as ''footprint'' on sequencing gel. Identifies precise protein-binding sequences.'),
('970f975c-5870-5410-9f8d-34ca9c62f860'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Reporter gene assays measure:', '["Protein degradation metabolic pathway process","DNA methylation metabolic pathway process","Protein metabolic pathway process","Gene expression (promoter activity) via easily measured product","RNA stability metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Gene expression (promoter activity) via easily measured product', 'Reporter: firefly luciferase Renilla luciferase GFP beta-galactosidase CAT. Promoter drives reporter expression. Normalized to control reporter. Used for studying gene regulation and signaling pathways.'),
('f0152b26-b0d7-5b5a-8ba3-d200850183dc'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Luciferase reporter assay:', '["Produces color metabolic pathway process","Produces fluorescence metabolic pathway process","Produces radioactivity metabolic pathway process","Firefly luciferase produces light with luciferin + ATP","Produces no signal metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Firefly luciferase produces light with luciferin + ATP', 'Firefly luciferase: luciferin + ATP + O2 → oxyluciferin + AMP + light (560 nm). Quantified by luminometer. Dual-luciferase system (firefly + Renilla) normalizes for transfection efficiency.'),
('53d11ac9-5296-5208-a0d4-d976f09d87d8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'siRNA (small interfering RNA):', '["Modifies DNA metabolic pathway process","Transcribes DNA metabolic pathway process","Translates protein metabolic pathway process","Short dsRNA (21-23 nt) that silences complementary mRNA","Enhances expression metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Short dsRNA (21-23 nt) that silences complementary mRNA', 'siRNA: Dicer processes dsRNA to siRNAs. RISC incorporates guide strand cleaving complementary mRNA. Transient gene knockdown. Used for functional genomics and therapeutic development.'),
('59b32dbb-acfc-5c7e-b94f-803e91a6dac4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'shRNA (short hairpin RNA):', '["Expressed from vector forms hairpin processed to siRNA for stable knockdown","DNA editing metabolic pathway process","Transient knockdown metabolic pathway process","No knockdown metabolic pathway process","Protein overexpression metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Expressed from vector forms hairpin processed to siRNA for stable knockdown', 'shRNA: vector (lentivirus) expressed as hairpin RNA. Processed by Dicer to siRNA. Stable integration allows long-term knockdown. Inducible vectors (tet-on) allow controlled expression.'),
('1f89ac7b-8cc3-558f-bc99-5050fa38d2cf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'MiRNA (microRNA) function:', '["Translate protein metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Regulate gene expression by binding 3''UTR of target mRNA inhibiting translation/degradation","Transcribe DNA metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Edit RNA metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Splice RNA metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Regulate gene expression by binding 3''UTR of target mRNA inhibiting translation/degradation', 'miRNAs are ~22 nt endogenous RNAs. Bind seed region (2-7 nt) of target mRNA 3''UTR. Repress translation or promote mRNA decay. Hundreds regulate most cellular processes.'),
('0d826f8b-1f04-5664-9518-13d0da02a343'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'CRISPR interference (CRISPRi):', '["Edits DNA metabolic pathway process","dCas9 fused to activator metabolic pathway process","Cuts DNA metabolic pathway process","dCas9 (catalytically dead) fused to repressor silences gene expression","Knocks out gene metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'dCas9 (catalytically dead) fused to repressor silences gene expression', 'CRISPRi: dCas9 (D10A H840A) + gRNA targets promoter. Fused to KRAB repressor. Blocks transcription initiation. Reversible and specific. Used for gene knockdown without DNA damage.'),
('31d7b6b2-2ca1-5e3d-b7b7-eae6812de78e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'CRISPR activation (CRISPRa):', '["dCas9 fused to repressor metabolic pathway process","dCas9 fused to transcriptional activator (VP64 p65) enhances gene expression","Edits DNA metabolic pathway process","Knocks out gene metabolic pathway process","Cuts DNA metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'dCas9 fused to transcriptional activator (VP64 p65) enhances gene expression', 'CRISPRa: dCas9-VP64/p65/Rta (VPR) + gRNA targeting promoter. Recruits transcriptional machinery. Used for endogenous gene activation (gain-of-function studies).'),
('fd7ca7e6-7e90-541e-a02b-7d17c41bff27'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Base editing (CRISPR base editor):', '["Creates DSB for HDR metabolic pathway process","Deletion only metabolic pathway process","Insertion only metabolic pathway process","Converts one base to another without double-strand break","NHEJ only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Converts one base to another without double-strand break', 'Base editors: Cas9 nickase fused to deaminase. CBE (cytosine deaminase) C→T (G→A). ABE (adenine deaminase) A→G (T→C). Precise point mutations without DSB.'),
('8a905a85-124e-5a6d-806b-a58b1b9b3f7b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Prime editing:', '["Search and replace'' genome editing using Cas9 nickase fused to reverse transcriptase with pegRNA","NHEJ only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Cas9 only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","HDR only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Base editing only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Search and replace'' genome editing using Cas9 nickase fused to reverse transcriptase with pegRNA', 'Prime editing: Cas9 nickase-RT + prime editing guide RNA (pegRNA) with primer binding site + RT template. Precise insertions deletions and point mutations without DSB or donor template.'),
('08fbfb59-162f-5ea0-81a6-579e9c30d6b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Vectors used in gene therapy include:', '["Only bacteriophage metabolic pathway process","Only bacteria metabolic pathway process","Only yeast metabolic pathway process","Only plasmid metabolic pathway process","AAV lentivirus adenovirus and retrovirus"]'::jsonb, 'AAV lentivirus adenovirus and retrovirus', 'AAV (non-pathogenic): low immunogenicity sustained expression (episomal). Lentivirus: integrates into genome for stable expression. Adenovirus: high expression but immunogenic. Retrovirus: integrates infects dividing cells.'),
('cced4090-8763-5a99-b6b9-61ccd4f3a478'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Adenoviral vectors are used for:', '["Transient high-level gene expression in dividing and non-dividing cells","Stable integration metabolic pathway process","Long-term expression metabolic pathway process","Small insert capacity metabolic pathway process","Low immunogenicity metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Transient high-level gene expression in dividing and non-dividing cells', 'Adenovirus: large insert (8-36 kb) high transduction efficiency. Episomal (no integration). High immunogenicity limits repeat dosing. Used for vaccines (COVID-19: AstraZeneca Johnson & Johnson) and cancer gene therapy.'),
('d7d025af-6a60-5c2e-a11b-6dac066abdd2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'AAV (adeno-associated virus) serotypes:', '["All serotypes identical metabolic pathway process","Different serotypes (AAV1-9) have different tissue tropisms","No tropism differences metabolic pathway process","Cannot transduce human cells metabolic pathway process","Only AAV2 used metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Different serotypes (AAV1-9) have different tissue tropisms', 'AAV serotypes: AAV1 (muscle) AAV2 (broad) AAV5 (lung/retina) AAV8 (liver) AAV9 (CNS muscle). Capsid engineering improves targeting. Used for gene therapy (Luxturna Zolgensma).'),
('8a16c81f-ac2c-5f60-95f5-5ff67e97cbcb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Lentiviral vectors:', '["Only transduce dividing cells metabolic pathway process","Integrate into host genome allowing long-term expression in dividing and non-dividing cells","Cause high immunogenicity metabolic pathway process","Small insert capacity metabolic pathway process","Do not integrate metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Integrate into host genome allowing long-term expression in dividing and non-dividing cells', 'Lentivirus (HIV-based): infects non-dividing cells. Integrates into genome. Large insert (8-10 kb). Used for CAR-T cells and gene therapy for SCID beta-thalassemia.'),
('69ff71bb-6544-58fe-a8b7-4db42ccbd10d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. Liposomal delivery for gene therapy uses:', '["Cationic lipids complexed with DNA for cell uptake","Viral vectors metabolic pathway process","Naked DNA metabolic pathway process","Electroporation metabolic pathway process","Gene gun metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Cationic lipids complexed with DNA for cell uptake', 'Cationic liposomes: positively charged lipids complex with negatively charged DNA. Cell uptake by endocytosis. Low immunogenicity. Low efficiency. Used for some vaccines and clinical trials.'),
('2e4cb8aa-3b86-54d6-b37c-c9f2b60639ce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Electroporation for gene delivery:', '["Electric pulses create temporary membrane pores for DNA entry","Viral method metabolic pathway process","Chemical method metabolic pathway process","Heat shock metabolic pathway process","Microinjection metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Electric pulses create temporary membrane pores for DNA entry', 'Electroporation: high-voltage pulses permeabilize cell membrane. DNA enters through pores. Used for bacteria yeast mammalian cells. In vivo electroporation for DNA vaccines.'),
('b0b5210f-a83b-5ab3-965a-1fa28f9885f8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Microinjection:', '["Direct injection of DNA into cell nucleus","Liposomal delivery metabolic pathway process","Viral delivery metabolic pathway process","Heat shock metabolic pathway process","Electroporation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Direct injection of DNA into cell nucleus', 'Microinjection: pronuclear injection into fertilized egg. Used for producing transgenic mice (first transgenic method). DNA randomly integrates. Also used for single-cell manipulation.'),
('4cdd3fc0-867e-579f-936e-635f311e6ec8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with multiple congenital anomalies. Genetic testing is performed. Gene targeting (homologous recombination) in ES cells:', '["No selection needed metabolic pathway process","Replaces endogenous gene with modified version","Random integration metabolic pathway process","Viral transduction metabolic pathway process","Electroporation only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Replaces endogenous gene with modified version', 'Gene targeting in mouse ES cells: targeting vector with homologous arms. Positive (neo) and negative (TK) selection. Germline transmission creates knockout/knockin mice. Nobel Prize 2007 (Capecchi Smithies Evans).'),
('eee16453-af50-53ce-a010-cf839abe856f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Cre-LoxP recombination system:', '["Random recombination metabolic pathway process","Site-specific recombination between LoxP sites by Cre recombinase","LoxP sites only metabolic pathway process","Cre only metabolic pathway process","No specificity metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Site-specific recombination between LoxP sites by Cre recombinase', 'Cre-LoxP: Cre recombinase catalyzes recombination between 34 bp LoxP sites. Conditional knockout: LoxP-flanked (floxed) gene + Cre under tissue-specific promoter. Used for spatiotemporal gene deletion.'),
('bb9faba7-5ae6-5eac-b335-dbd422bc575c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'FLP-FRT recombination:', '["Yeast FLP recombinase recognizes FRT sites","Not site-specific metabolic pathway process","Mammalian recombination metabolic pathway process","Only in plants metabolic pathway process","Bacterial recombination metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Yeast FLP recombinase recognizes FRT sites', 'FLP-FRT: FLP recombinase from yeast. Used with Cre-LoxP for double recombination strategies. FRT sites engineered for optimal mammalian expression (codon-optimized FLPe).'),
('3f6a7940-3142-5fd7-88ee-ee2fb20cb93c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Tet-on/Tet-off systems regulate:', '["Only activation metabolic pathway process","Inducible gene expression using tetracycline/doxycycline","Only repression metabolic pathway process","No regulation metabolic pathway process","Constitutive expression metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Inducible gene expression using tetracycline/doxycycline', 'Tet system: Tet repressor (TetR) binds tetO operator. Tet-off: TetR-VP16 activates in absence of doxycycline. Tet-on: rtTA activates in presence of doxycycline. Tightly regulated expression.'),
('97e21db5-ad14-5534-816a-89bd7b69c834'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'shRNAmir (microRNA-adapted shRNA):', '["Same as shRNA metabolic pathway process","shRNA embedded in miRNA context for more efficient processing","Less efficient metabolic pathway process","No Dicer processing metabolic pathway process","Only for plants metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'shRNA embedded in miRNA context for more efficient processing', 'shRNAmir mimics endogenous miRNA: Drosha and Dicer process more efficiently. Lower toxicity. Used for stable RNAi in vivo with lentiviral vectors.'),
('b689e58d-910e-52be-a531-07fb85e00504'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Aptamers are:', '["Single-stranded DNA/RNA molecules that bind specific targets","Peptides metabolic pathway process","Proteins metabolic pathway process","Lipids metabolic pathway process","Small molecules metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Single-stranded DNA/RNA molecules that bind specific targets', 'Aptamers (SELEX): oligonucleotides selected for target binding. ''Chemical antibodies''. High specificity and affinity. Used for diagnostics (therapeutics: Macugen for AMD).'),
('4f1d3b25-6212-5598-99a9-a346dbf83e4a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'SELEX (systematic evolution of ligands by exponential enrichment):', '["Iterative selection of aptamers from random library","PCR only metabolic pathway process","Cloning only metabolic pathway process","Expression only metabolic pathway process","Sequencing only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Iterative selection of aptamers from random library', 'SELEX: random oligonucleotide library incubated with target. Bound sequences amplified by PCR. Repeated rounds enrich high-affinity aptamers. Counter-selection removes non-specific binders.'),
('efe6d2fa-3e31-51d4-a264-e8faad8ac7ff'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Biosensors combine:', '["Biological recognition element with transducer for analyte detection","Only bio-recognition metabolic pathway process","Only electronics metabolic pathway process","Only transducer metabolic pathway process","Only optics metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Biological recognition element with transducer for analyte detection', 'Biosensors: bioreceptor (enzyme antibody nucleic acid cell) + transducer (electrochemical optical piezoelectric). Signal proportional to analyte. Used for glucose monitoring (glucose oxidase electrode).'),
('d4fcd1e7-bdf8-5cdb-aea0-b3928f0a8f22'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Glucose biosensor (continuous glucose monitor CGM):', '["Glucose oxidase produces H2O2 detected amperometrically","Glucose dehydrogenase metabolic pathway process","Hexokinase metabolic pathway process","Glucose only by colorimetry metabolic pathway process","Not used clinically metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Glucose oxidase produces H2O2 detected amperometrically', 'CGM: subcutaneous sensor with glucose oxidase. Current proportional to glucose. Transmits to receiver/insulin pump. Improves glycemic control in diabetes (reduces hypoglycemia).'),
('236a6f9f-3f03-506a-8ef9-3d3468c7b66b'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Immunosensors detect:', '["Only antigens metabolic pathway process","Only antibodies metabolic pathway process","Antigen-antibody binding at sensor surface producing signal","Enzyme activity metabolic pathway process","No biological recognition metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Antigen-antibody binding at sensor surface producing signal', 'Immunosensors: antibody immobilized on transducer (SPR electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance). Binding detected in real-time. Used for point-of-care testing (cardiac troponin).'),
('83242bc9-5a98-5c78-8f63-a214c794e18a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Surface plasmon resonance (SPR):', '["Detects real-time biomolecular interactions by refractive index change","Detects only proteins metabolic pathway process","Detects only DNA metabolic pathway process","Detects only RNA metabolic pathway process","No real-time metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detects real-time biomolecular interactions by refractive index change', 'SPR: polarized light reflected from gold film. Biomolecular binding changes refractive index. Measures ka kd KD (affinity). Label-free real-time. Used for drug discovery and antibody characterization.'),
('8ca8bd1e-21a0-532e-adfb-7764a8612bb1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Quartz crystal microbalance (QCM):', '["Radioactive sensor metabolic pathway process","Electrochemical sensor metabolic pathway process","Fluorescence sensor metabolic pathway process","Optical sensor metabolic pathway process","Piezoelectric sensor detecting mass change from binding events"]'::jsonb, 'Piezoelectric sensor detecting mass change from binding events', 'QCM: quartz crystal oscillates at frequency dependent on mass. Binding of analyte changes frequency (Sauerbrey equation). Real-time label-free. Used for biosensor development.'),
('bc213920-6b1c-59ef-959b-b869af93abf1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Lab-on-a-chip (microfluidics):', '["Miniaturized devices integrating multiple laboratory functions on single chip","Full-size lab metabolic pathway process","Macro-scale device metabolic pathway process","No integration metabolic pathway process","Only for DNA analysis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Miniaturized devices integrating multiple laboratory functions on single chip', 'Microfluidics: channels (10-100 micrometer) manipulate small volumes (pL-nL). Multifunction integration. Used for POCT DNA analysis and cell sorting. Advantages: speed low sample cost.'),
('8096abab-17a0-56a1-86c6-919db389a320'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Microfluidic PCR:', '["Rapid PCR amplification in microliter/nanoliter volumes on chip","Slower than conventional metabolic pathway process","No advantage metabolic pathway process","Requires more reagent metabolic pathway process","Conventional PCR only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid PCR amplification in microliter/nanoliter volumes on chip', 'Microfluidic PCR: fast thermal cycling (small volume high surface-to-volume). Continuous flow or droplet PCR. Used for rapid point-of-care diagnostics (GeneXpert).'),
('a9f62034-3e02-5b74-9e7e-c41f9edb702e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Digital microfluidics:', '["Only for DNA metabolic pathway process","Only for cells metabolic pathway process","Continuous flow metabolic pathway process","Individual droplets manipulated by electrowetting on dielectric","No droplet control metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Individual droplets manipulated by electrowetting on dielectric', 'Digital microfluidics: droplets (nL) moved by applying voltage to electrode array. No pumps or valves. Mixing splitting dispensing. Used for sample preparation and assays.'),
('3932f788-57b5-565a-8c93-c3929134ab1c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Organ-on-a-chip:', '["Microfluidic device mimicking organ-level physiology","Cell culture plate metabolic pathway process","Animal model metabolic pathway process","Computer model metabolic pathway process","Petri dish metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Microfluidic device mimicking organ-level physiology', 'Organ chips: microchannels lined with living cells. Mimic tissue-tissue interfaces and mechanical forces. Drug testing disease modeling. Lung-on-chip (Harvard) demonstrates breathing motions.'),
('bd967fd0-abeb-5b4c-a5a9-d81d3dcc17c7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Mass cytometry (CyTOF):', '["Time-of-flight mass spectrometry using metal-tagged antibodies","FACS with fluorescence metabolic pathway process","Flow cytometry with 8 colors metabolic pathway process","Mass spectrometry only metabolic pathway process","ELISA format metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Time-of-flight mass spectrometry using metal-tagged antibodies', 'CyTOF: antibodies labeled with rare earth metals (isotopes). Cell analysis by ICP-MS. Up to 50 parameters without spectral overlap. Used for deep immunophenotyping and single-cell proteomics.'),
('9d36ac4a-20c1-55eb-ab39-6b779b4cc712'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Imaging mass cytometry (IMC):', '["ELISPOT metabolic pathway process","FACS metabolic pathway process","Western blot metabolic pathway process","Tissue imaging with metal-tagged antibodies laser ablation and CyTOF","Standard microscopy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Tissue imaging with metal-tagged antibodies laser ablation and CyTOF', 'IMC: laser ablates tissue spot (1 micron). Ablated material analyzed by CyTOF. Multiplexed tissue imaging (40+ markers). Single-cell spatial proteomics for tumor microenvironment.'),
('83180026-ddd1-5fd1-81e3-28c36c36f79d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Flow cytometry uses:', '["Only fluorescence metabolic pathway process","Only microscopy metabolic pathway process","Only scatter metabolic pathway process","Only cell counting metabolic pathway process","Laser-based cell analysis measuring light scatter and fluorescence"]'::jsonb, 'Laser-based cell analysis measuring light scatter and fluorescence', 'Flow cytometry: cells in single file pass laser beam. Forward scatter (size) side scatter (granularity). Fluorescence from antibodies/reporters. High-throughput multiparameter cell analysis and sorting.'),
('14231c24-fba7-5e49-8b0e-014f142ace32'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS):', '["Analyzes only metabolic pathway process","Counts cells only metabolic pathway process","Sorts by size only metabolic pathway process","Sorts cells based on fluorescence and scatter properties","No sorting metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Sorts cells based on fluorescence and scatter properties', 'FACS: droplets containing single cells charged based on fluorescence. Deflected by electric fields into tubes/wells. Purity >99%. Used for collecting specific cell populations (stem cells rare cells).'),
('549d82da-4117-5c6b-affb-361903f64a21'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Magnetic-activated cell sorting (MACS):', '["Sorts by density metabolic pathway process","Uses magnetic beads conjugated to antibodies for cell separation","Fluorescence-based metabolic pathway process","Sorts by size only metabolic pathway process","No labeling metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Uses magnetic beads conjugated to antibodies for cell separation', 'MACS: cells labeled with magnetic microbeads (anti-CD4 anti-CD8). Column in magnetic field retains labeled cells. Elute after removing field. Simpler faster than FACS. Lower purity.'),
('365653ff-973f-53b0-bf41-4cdbf8a928d4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Elispot (enzyme-linked immunospot) assay:', '["Measures cytokine secretion from individual cells","Measures serum cytokines","Measures intracellular cytokines","No single-cell resolution","Measures only IgG"]'::jsonb, 'Measures cytokine secretion from individual cells', 'ELISPOT: capture antibody on membrane. Cells secrete cytokine. Detection antibody enzyme substrate form spot (one spot = one secreting cell). Used for vaccine immunogenicity and TB diagnosis (IFN-gamma).'),
('9b904988-aebe-5fa1-99e4-b7f27b1b81f2'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Fluorospot is a variant of ELISPOT:', '["Detects only one cytokine metabolic pathway process","Only for bacterial assays metabolic pathway process","Detects multiple cytokines simultaneously using different fluorophores","Less sensitive than ELISPOT metabolic pathway process","No color differentiation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detects multiple cytokines simultaneously using different fluorophores', 'Fluorospot: fluorescent detection of multiple cytokines (IFN-gamma IL-2 TNF-alpha) in same well. Higher throughput than ELISPOT. Used for comprehensive T-cell analysis.'),
('91ec3162-d488-598c-8569-6eea60dde894'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Luminex (multiplex bead-based assay):', '["Color-coded beads with different capture antibodies for multiplex protein detection","Single analyte metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No bead coding metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","ELISA only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Western only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Color-coded beads with different capture antibodies for multiplex protein detection', 'Luminex: 100 bead sets with unique dye ratios. Each bead coated with specific capture antibody. Detection with PE-labeled antibody. Flow-based readout. Up to 50 analytes simultaneously (cytokines).'),
('67e9093c-949d-5ad6-a041-cf58ad7f1dcf'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Simoa (single molecule array):', '["Western blot metabolic pathway process","Digital ELISA detecting single protein molecules in femtoliter wells","Bulk ELISA metabolic pathway process","Flow cytometry metabolic pathway process","PCR metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Digital ELISA detecting single protein molecules in femtoliter wells', 'Simoa: capture on paramagnetic beads. Enzyme label. Beads loaded into femtoliter well arrays. Single molecule counting gives attomolar sensitivity (1000x conventional ELISA).'),
('02e6fa30-2483-53b0-a923-2dece66e9a06'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. Cell-free DNA (cfDNA) analysis:', '["Circulating DNA in plasma used for non-invasive prenatal testing and liquid biopsy","Cellular DNA metabolic pathway process","Only from tumors metabolic pathway process","Only for prenatal testing metabolic pathway process","Not clinically used metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Circulating DNA in plasma used for non-invasive prenatal testing and liquid biopsy', 'cfDNA: fragmented DNA released from apoptotic/necrotic cells. NIPT (fetal cfDNA for trisomy). Liquid biopsy (tumor cfDNA for cancer mutations monitoring). Non-invasive.'),
('6f8906be-6513-5df7-bc2f-86053a0cc8bd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Liquid biopsy detects:', '["Only RNA metabolic pathway process","Only solid tumor biopsy metabolic pathway process","Only protein markers metabolic pathway process","Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) and circulating tumor cells (CTCs) from blood sample","Metabolites only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) and circulating tumor cells (CTCs) from blood sample', 'Liquid biopsy: ctDNA (mutations methylation) CTCs exosomes. Used for early detection treatment monitoring resistance detection. Less invasive than tissue biopsy. Serial monitoring possible.'),
('8effa909-868a-5b50-9147-4f0b957fdc02'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Next-generation sequencing (NGS) for cancer:', '["Only germline testing metabolic pathway process","Only single genes metabolic pathway process","Not used clinically metabolic pathway process","No mutations found metabolic pathway process","Targeted panels whole-exome and whole-genome sequencing identify driver mutations"]'::jsonb, 'Targeted panels whole-exome and whole-genome sequencing identify driver mutations', 'NGS panels (50-500 genes) identify actionable mutations (EGFR BRAF KRAS). TMB (tumor mutational burden) predicts immunotherapy response. MSI (microsatellite instability) for immune checkpoint therapy.'),
('3c90df38-743f-5795-b51c-d903d420a9e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Whole-exome sequencing (WES) targets:', '["Only promoter regions metabolic pathway process","Only mitochondria metabolic pathway process","Only non-coding regions metabolic pathway process","All protein-coding exons (~1-2% of genome containing ~85% of disease mutations","Only RNA metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'All protein-coding exons (~1-2% of genome containing ~85% of disease mutations', 'WES: capture-based enrichment of exons. Cost-effective for identifying coding variants. Used for rare disease diagnosis (WES diagnostic yield ~30-40%). Identifies novel disease genes.'),
('fdb36a2f-c439-53f1-afcb-beb992f475ba'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Whole-genome sequencing (WGS):', '["Only exons metabolic pathway process","Only chromosomes metabolic pathway process","Sequences entire genome including non-coding regions","Only coding regions metabolic pathway process","Only mitochondrial DNA metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Sequences entire genome including non-coding regions', 'WGS: comprehensive coverage (coding + non-coding). Detects structural variants and CNVs better than WES. Cost decreasing but data analysis challenging. Used for rare diseases and cancer.'),
('40a902c2-8597-5930-83b9-47beb40efd10'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Long-read sequencing (PacBio Nanopore):', '["Reads <1 kb metabolic pathway process","Reads >10 kb resolving complex structural variants and repetitive regions","Same as Illumina metabolic pathway process","Shorter reads metabolic pathway process","Lower accuracy metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Reads >10 kb resolving complex structural variants and repetitive regions', 'Long-read sequencing: PacBio HiFi (15-20 kb Q30) Nanopore (100+ kb). Resolves structural variants tandem repeats phasing. Genome assembly. Detects previously missed disease variants.'),
('b2e63f76-1cc6-5713-bc74-b331972d7b8a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'RNA-seq applications:', '["Transcriptome profiling differential gene expression fusion detection splicing","Only lipid analysis metabolic pathway process","Only methylation analysis metabolic pathway process","Only protein analysis metabolic pathway process","Only mutation detection metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Transcriptome profiling differential gene expression fusion detection splicing', 'RNA-seq: quantify gene expression identify alternative splicing detect gene fusions (BCR-ABL EML4-ALK) and non-coding RNAs. Used for cancer subtyping and biomarker discovery.'),
('254df4f4-7f6e-5046-a01a-fa9b8be33710'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Small RNA sequencing:', '["Profiles miRNAs piRNAs siRNAs and other small non-coding RNAs","Only mRNA metabolic pathway process","Only lncRNA metabolic pathway process","Only rRNA metabolic pathway process","Only tRNA metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Profiles miRNAs piRNAs siRNAs and other small non-coding RNAs', 'Small RNA-seq: size selection (18-30 nt) adapter ligation reverse transcription. Identifies known/novel small RNAs. Used for miRNA biomarker discovery and regulatory network analysis.'),
('7eb83987-78e3-56f6-967d-7feb70020ba7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'ChIP-seq for transcription factor:', '["Only histone modifications metabolic pathway process","Only protein expression metabolic pathway process","Only RNA binding metabolic pathway process","Only DNA methylation metabolic pathway process","Genome-wide mapping of transcription factor binding sites"]'::jsonb, 'Genome-wide mapping of transcription factor binding sites', 'ChIP-seq: antibody against transcription factor. Purified DNA sequenced. Peak calling identifies binding sites (MACS). Used for regulatory network mapping (TP53 CTCF MYC).'),
('1b494b7b-f45c-59d5-bccf-13f3087a573c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'ChIP-seq for histone modifications:', '["Only RNA","Only DNA sequences","Only transcription factors","Only proteins","Maps genome-wide distribution of histone marks"]'::jsonb, 'Maps genome-wide distribution of histone marks', 'Histone ChIP-seq: identifies active promoters (H3K4me3) enhancers (H3K27ac) repressive domains (H3K27me3). Integrative analysis defines chromatin states. Roadmap Epigenomics project.'),
('3db2ad51-bc13-50e9-8bcf-e77ca71603a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. ATAC-seq (assay for transposase-accessible chromatin):', '["Only euchromatin metabolic pathway process","Identifies open chromatin regions (promoters enhancers) genome-wide","Only heterochromatin metabolic pathway process","Identifies closed chromatin metabolic pathway process","Histone modifications metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Identifies open chromatin regions (promoters enhancers) genome-wide', 'ATAC-seq: Tn5 transposase inserts adapters into accessible DNA. PCR amplify and sequence. Peaks indicate open chromatin. Requires fewer cells than DNase-seq. Fast genome-wide regulatory mapping.'),
('52cf28a5-f8df-501d-8ce8-db9226b43814'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with global developmental delay. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. Hi-C (chromosome conformation capture):', '["No 3D info metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only one locus metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","2D interactions only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only in bacteria metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Maps 3D chromatin interactions genome-wide identifying topologically associating domains"]'::jsonb, 'Maps 3D chromatin interactions genome-wide identifying topologically associating domains', 'Hi-C: crosslink restriction digest ligate labeled biotin. Purify ligated junctions. NGS identifies interacting regions. TADs loops compartments. Alterations in cancer and developmental disorders.'),
('fcfc638e-7528-5ee6-a149-af90a6a1668f'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with growth retardation. Genetic testing is performed. DNA methylation analysis by bisulfite sequencing:', '["Only detects unmethylated metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Only CpG islands metabolic pathway process","Single-base resolution methylation map after bisulfite conversion","Does not detect methylation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Single-base resolution methylation map after bisulfite conversion', 'Bisulfite conversion: unmethylated C → U → T after PCR. Methylated C remains C. WGBS (whole-genome bisulfite sequencing) comprehensive. RRBS (reduced representation) enriches CpG-rich regions.'),
('44b122c1-6021-5de0-a7be-2929989a2d99'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Methylation-specific PCR (MSP):', '["Bisulfite-treated DNA PCR with primers specific for methylated/unmethylated sequences","No bisulfite needed metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No PCR metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only for RNA metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only for proteins metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Bisulfite-treated DNA PCR with primers specific for methylated/unmethylated sequences', 'MSP: separate primers for methylated (M) and unmethylated (U) sequences after bisulfite treatment. Gel-based detection. Sensitive but not quantitative. Used for cancer biomarker (MGMT promoter in glioblastoma).'),
('3c5ec914-d235-5422-9eb7-b1d18c36fdb5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Pyrosequencing for methylation:', '["Quantitative bisulfite sequencing at specific loci using pyrosequencing","Only global methylation metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Only non-CpG metabolic pathway process","Qualitative only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Quantitative bisulfite sequencing at specific loci using pyrosequencing', 'Pyrosequencing after bisulfite treatment: incorporation of nucleotides releases PPi detected by luciferase. Percentage methylation at each CpG site. Used for clinical biomarker analysis.'),
('579a0535-2ef9-5ed8-9590-8ce32663958d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'MassArray (EpiTYPER):', '["Antibody-based metabolic pathway process","MALDI-TOF based quantitative DNA methylation analysis","Array-based metabolic pathway process","PCR-based only metabolic pathway process","Sequencing-based metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'MALDI-TOF based quantitative DNA methylation analysis', 'EpiTYPER: bisulfite PCR in vitro transcription (T7) RNase A cleavage. MALDI-TOF measures mass ratios of CpG-containing fragments. Multiplexes up to 10 CpG sites per reaction.'),
('0fffa360-94cb-5132-83f5-76723c1b33d9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Methylation array (Infinium 450K/EPIC):', '["PCR-based metabolic pathway process","Only targeted regions metabolic pathway process","Reduced representation metabolic pathway process","BeadChip array covering >850,000 CpG sites for genome-wide methylation","Whole-genome bisulfite seq metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'BeadChip array covering >850,000 CpG sites for genome-wide methylation', 'Illumina Infinium: bisulfite-converted DNA hybridized to bead-bound probes. Two bead types (methylated/unmethylated). Beta value = methylation level. EPIC v2.0 covers >935k CpGs.'),
('d48493ea-1b45-53ce-8964-5971ba0ce13a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Immunoprecipitation-based methylation (MeDIP):', '["No antibody metabolic pathway process","Antibody against 5-methylcytosine enriches methylated DNA for sequencing/array","Antibody against unmethylated metabolic pathway process","Only RNA metabolic pathway process","Only histone metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Antibody against 5-methylcytosine enriches methylated DNA for sequencing/array', 'MeDIP-seq: anti-5mC antibody pulls down methylated fragments (single-stranded). Sensitive for low-CpG regions. Semi-quantitative. Resolution limited to fragment size (~300 bp).'),
('d6e48f00-9258-5e08-9e69-d9438c4ff6c0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Oxidative bisulfite sequencing (oxBS-seq):', '["Same as bisulfite metabolic pathway process","No distinction metabolic pathway process","Detects only C metabolic pathway process","Detects only 5mC metabolic pathway process","Specifically detects 5-hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) distinguishing from 5mC"]'::jsonb, 'Specifically detects 5-hydroxymethylcytosine (5hmC) distinguishing from 5mC', 'oxBS-seq: chemical oxidation converts 5hmC to 5fC then bisulfite converts to U. 5mC remains C. Comparing with standard bisulfite identifies 5hmC. Important for active demethylation studies.'),
('da1fda8b-b5fa-577b-8002-df6ba19115b1'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'TapS (TET-assisted pyridine borane sequencing):', '["Only 5mC metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Detects 5hmC and 5fC (5-carboxylcytosine) at single-base resolution","Only U metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only C metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only 5hmC metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detects 5hmC and 5fC (5-carboxylcytosine) at single-base resolution', 'TAPS: TET oxidation + bisulfite. Specifically labels 5mC/5hmC. Produces C→T transition at 5mC/5hmC sites. Non-destructive less DNA degradation. Alternative to bisulfite sequencing.'),
('33803dee-9240-5a6c-a39a-c4c984aaae1c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Optical mapping (Bionano Genomics):', '["Visualizes long DNA molecules (150 kb+) with fluorescent labels at sequence motifs","Short reads metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Sequencing-based metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","PCR-based metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Array-based metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Visualizes long DNA molecules (150 kb+) with fluorescent labels at sequence motifs', 'Optical mapping: nanochannel array linearizes labeled DNA. Label patterns create genome maps. Detects structural variants (50 kb+) and repeat expansions. Complementary to NGS.'),
('fa367cd6-5c30-53a8-bd73-36ee59976314'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Fluorescence microscopy techniques:', '["Only one type metabolic pathway process","No fluorescence metabolic pathway process","Epifluorescence confocal multiphoton TIRF and super-resolution microscopy","Only electron microscopy metabolic pathway process","Only brightfield metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Epifluorescence confocal multiphoton TIRF and super-resolution microscopy', 'Advanced microscopy: confocal (optical sectioning) multiphoton (deep tissue) TIRF (surface) STED PALM STORM (super-resolution beyond diffraction limit ~200 nm).'),
('1fb2ed95-1019-57f8-87ec-6a3b9c9b05c9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Confocal microscopy:', '["Pinhole eliminates out-of-focus light providing optical sections for 3D reconstruction","No sectioning metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Lower resolution metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No fluorescence metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Bulk tissue only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Pinhole eliminates out-of-focus light providing optical sections for 3D reconstruction', 'Confocal: point illumination pinhole before detector. Blocks out-of-focus light. Z-stacks reconstruct 3D. Laser scanning (LSCM) or spinning disk (Nipkow). Used for immunofluorescence live imaging.'),
('ce0278c0-0779-5bed-8758-b59caf558501'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Super-resolution microscopy (STED PALM STORM):', '["Achieves resolution below diffraction limit","Same as confocal metabolic pathway process","Lower resolution metabolic pathway process","No fluorescence metabolic pathway process","No live imaging metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Achieves resolution below diffraction limit', 'STED (stimulated emission depletion): donut-shaped depletion beam. PALM/STORM: single-molecule localization. 10-50 nm resolution. Visualizes subcellular structures and protein clusters.'),
('4e47e022-a9cb-51c7-85f8-85afd7d0d9e0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Light-sheet fluorescence microscopy:', '["Illuminates sample from side with thin sheet of light for rapid 3D imaging","Low speed metabolic pathway process","Point illumination metabolic pathway process","Wide-field only metabolic pathway process","No sectioning metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Illuminates sample from side with thin sheet of light for rapid 3D imaging', 'Light-sheet: orthogonal illumination minimizes photobleaching/phototoxicity. Fast volumetric imaging. Used for developmental biology (zebrafish fly embryo) clearing methods for whole-brain imaging.'),
('e5838359-8f21-5f1d-82f4-132cdab52314'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'CLEM (correlative light and electron microscopy):', '["Same sample imaged by fluorescence microscopy then electron microscopy","No correlation metabolic pathway process","Electron only metabolic pathway process","Light only metabolic pathway process","Only one modality metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Same sample imaged by fluorescence microscopy then electron microscopy', 'CLEM: fluorescent protein/label identifies region of interest. TEM/SEM provides ultrastructure. Overlay images. Precisely localizes proteins in cellular context.'),
('353e7bf1-d8ef-5c6b-a6f5-84c516692ed8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Positron emission tomography (PET):', '["X-ray only metabolic pathway process","Ultrasound only metabolic pathway process","Anatomical only metabolic pathway process","Structural only metabolic pathway process","Radioactive tracer detects metabolic activity"]'::jsonb, 'Radioactive tracer detects metabolic activity', 'PET: positron-emitting isotope (18F) incorporated into tracer (FDG). Detected by coincidence gamma detection. Functional imaging. Used for cancer (high glucose) cardiac viability and neuroimaging.'),
('4eff2c5f-97b4-59ff-9a13-a172ecc390ae'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'SPECT (single photon emission CT):', '["Gamma-emitting tracers (99mTc 123I) for functional imaging","Only PET metabolic pathway process","No radioisotopes metabolic pathway process","Only anatomical metabolic pathway process","Positron emitters metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Gamma-emitting tracers (99mTc 123I) for functional imaging', 'SPECT: gamma camera rotates around patient. Tracers: 99mTc-sestamibi (cardiac) 99mTc-HMPAO (brain blood flow) 123I-FP-CIT (DaTscan Parkinson). Lower resolution than PET but lower cost.'),
('2d5d99d2-4021-5781-a2f9-c83146172e77'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI):', '["Strong magnetic field aligns protons radiofrequency pulses generate tissue-specific contrast","X-rays only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only structure metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No magnetism metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only anatomy metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Strong magnetic field aligns protons radiofrequency pulses generate tissue-specific contrast', 'MRI: hydrogen protons align in magnetic field (1.5-7T). RF pulses excite. T1 T2 relaxation times provide contrast. No ionizing radiation. Excellent soft tissue contrast. Functional MRI (BOLD) maps brain activity.'),
('b051aaac-370e-5d6b-b1a7-d81075df8d12'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Magnetic resonance spectroscopy (MRS):', '["Non-invasive measurement of metabolite concentrations in tissue","Only anatomy metabolic pathway process","Only function metabolic pathway process","No metabolites metabolic pathway process","Only inorganic metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Non-invasive measurement of metabolite concentrations in tissue', 'MRS: chemical shift of metabolites (NAA choline creatine lactate) in selected volume. Clinical: brain tumor (choline/NAA ratio) prostate cancer (citrate) cardiac energetics (PCr/ATP).'),
('1ae55061-c55a-5618-8359-e380a034a22a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Ultrasound imaging uses:', '["Light metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","High-frequency sound waves reflected from tissues producing real-time images","X-rays metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Magnetism metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Radioactivity metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'High-frequency sound waves reflected from tissues producing real-time images', 'Ultrasound: piezoelectric transducer emits sound (2-18 MHz). Echoes create real-time images. Doppler measures blood flow. Portable no radiation. Used for obstetrics abdominal cardiac.'),
('a8b8983a-c64b-5902-aa84-9932ac708555'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Contrast-enhanced ultrasound (CEUS):', '["Microbubble contrast agents for real-time vascular imaging","No contrast metabolic pathway process","Only MRI contrast metabolic pathway process","Only CT contrast metabolic pathway process","Iodine-based metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Microbubble contrast agents for real-time vascular imaging', 'CEUS: gas-filled microbubbles (<10 micron). Resonate at ultrasound frequencies. Visualize blood flow perfusion. Real-time. No nephrotoxicity (unlike CT contrast). Used for liver lesion characterization.'),
('bbb22497-827b-5486-8a69-5140aa7815cb'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Photoacoustic imaging:', '["No ultrasound metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No laser metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Light only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Sound only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Laser pulse induces thermoelastic expansion generating ultrasound waves detected by transducers"]'::jsonb, 'Laser pulse induces thermoelastic expansion generating ultrasound waves detected by transducers', 'Photoacoustic: nanosecond laser pulse absorbed by tissue. Rapid heating generates pressure wave (ultrasound). Combines optical contrast with ultrasound depth. Hemoglobin melanin imaging. Functional and molecular.'),
('d187dd2d-76ce-5acc-9d74-c217b89e7b04'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS):', '["Non-invasive monitoring of tissue oxygenation (HbO2 Hb) using NIR light","Oxygen only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","CO2 only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No hemoglobin metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only invasive metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Non-invasive monitoring of tissue oxygenation (HbO2 Hb) using NIR light', 'NIRS: NIR light (700-1000 nm) penetrates tissue. HbO2 and deoxyhemoglobin have different spectra. Measures regional cerebral/tissue oxygen saturation (rSO2). Used during surgery and neonatal monitoring.'),
('686dbdcb-f7d9-5b42-b362-22b571614737'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Pulse oximetry measures:', '["Only respiration metabolic pathway process","Only blood pressure metabolic pathway process","Only temperature metabolic pathway process","Arterial oxygen saturation (SpO2) by light absorption at two wavelengths","Only heart rate metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Arterial oxygen saturation (SpO2) by light absorption at two wavelengths', 'Pulse oximetry: RED (660 nm) and IR (940 nm) LEDs. Absorbance pulsates with arterial blood. Ratio of absorbances gives SpO2. Quick non-invasive. Common in clinical monitoring.'),
('a4213c0a-2ebc-54e0-ab2f-8eedd0d12ebd'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Bilirubin measurement (transcutaneous):', '["Urine only metabolic pathway process","Measures bilirubin by skin reflectance spectroscopy","Blood only metabolic pathway process","No spectroscopy metabolic pathway process","No light metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Measures bilirubin by skin reflectance spectroscopy', 'Transcutaneous bilirubinometry: multiple wavelengths measure yellow skin color. Correlates with serum bilirubin. Non-invasive jaundice screening in newborns. Reduces blood draws.'),
('58dc3fa8-6984-5a26-a461-c3ff9d35d035'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Glycated hemoglobin (HbA1c) measurement:', '["No clinical use metabolic pathway process","Not standardized metabolic pathway process","Various methods: HPLC immunoassay enzymatic capillary electrophoresis","Only immunoassay metabolic pathway process","Only HPLC metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Various methods: HPLC immunoassay enzymatic capillary electrophoresis', 'HbA1c: NGSP standardized. HPLC (gold standard) separates HbA1c from other hemoglobins. Immunoassay (turbidimetric inhibition). Enzymatic (Fructosyl amino acid oxidase). POCT devices available.'),
('f66f7a96-0c66-5bf5-afed-1df6b1c2f1f5'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Hemoglobin electrophoresis:', '["Only size metabolic pathway process","Only one hemoglobin metabolic pathway process","No separation metabolic pathway process","Separates normal and variant hemoglobins (HbA HbA2 HbF HbS HbC) by charge","No clinical use metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Separates normal and variant hemoglobins (HbA HbA2 HbF HbS HbC) by charge', 'Hb electrophoresis: alkaline (cellulose acetate pH 8.6) separates HbA HbF HbS HbC. Acid (pH 6.2) confirms. High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) is the reference method for thalassemia screening.'),
('c2cf399c-fa44-5f18-9f5c-ee0f9bc0e640'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Isoelectric focusing (IEF) for hemoglobin:', '["High-resolution separation of hemoglobin variants by pI","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Same as electrophoresis metabolic pathway process","No variants detected metabolic pathway process","Lower resolution metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'High-resolution separation of hemoglobin variants by pI', 'IEF: pH gradient (pH 6-8) in polyacrylamide gel. Hb variants focus at their pI. High resolution separates HbS HbC HbE HbD HbO-Arab. Used for confirmatory testing.'),
('c44272ec-deaf-5eb2-a59a-c5445688ef06'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Capillary electrophoresis (CE) for hemoglobin:', '["Rapid high-resolution separation with quantitative results","Slow resolution metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Low throughput metabolic pathway process","No automation metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Rapid high-resolution separation with quantitative results', 'CE: automated instruments (Sebia) separate Hb fractions. Fast (7 min) high resolution quantitative. Detects HbA HbA2 HbF HbS HbC HbE. First-line for thalassemia and hemoglobinopathy screening.'),
('88ac681a-94b8-5c27-849f-48c8fe04d2a9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Transferrin glycosylation (CDG screening):', '["Isoelectric focusing or CE measures transferrin glycoforms for congenital disorders of glycosylation","For diabetes diagnosis metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","For iron deficiency metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not for CDG metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only for Hb variants metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Isoelectric focusing or CE measures transferrin glycoforms for congenital disorders of glycosylation', 'CDG screening: serum transferrin IEF/CE detects abnormal glycosylation (hypo-glycosylated). Type 1 (ER defect) type 2 (Golgi defect). Confirm by gene sequencing. ~40% of diagnostic cases.'),
('9bc3b7d8-bcf4-573d-9af4-747332e09b5d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) measurement:', '["Immunoassay for hepatocellular carcinoma and neural tube defect screening","HPLC method metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Only for hepatitis metabolic pathway process","Electrophoresis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoassay for hepatocellular carcinoma and neural tube defect screening', 'AFP ELISA/CLIA: elevated in HCC germ cell tumors. Maternal serum AFP for NTD screening (open spina bifida anencephaly). Also in ataxia telangiectasia and hereditary tyrosinemia.'),
('a06966f7-7100-55f1-a80c-87933ec7797d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Cancer antigen 125 (CA-125):', '["For lung cancer metabolic pathway process","For breast cancer metabolic pathway process","Serum immunoassay for epithelial ovarian cancer monitoring","For prostate cancer metabolic pathway process","For colon cancer metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Serum immunoassay for epithelial ovarian cancer monitoring', 'CA-125 (MUC16) by ELISA/CLIA. Elevated in ovarian cancer. Also in endometriosis pregnancy pelvic inflammation. Used for monitoring treatment response and recurrence.'),
('8bfff34a-85ed-5be7-b62a-a5c001de5748'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Prostate-specific antigen (PSA):', '["For bladder cancer metabolic pathway process","For penile cancer metabolic pathway process","Serum immunoassay for prostate cancer screening and monitoring","For testicular cancer metabolic pathway process","For renal cancer metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Serum immunoassay for prostate cancer screening and monitoring', 'PSA (kallikrein-3) serum test. Free/total PSA ratio improves specificity. Age-specific reference ranges. Screening controversial (overdiagnosis). Used for post-treatment monitoring.'),
('52d4e365-41cd-5628-8be5-d7f5efeed2f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG):', '["No immunoassay metabolic pathway process","Only urine test metabolic pathway process","Immunoassay for pregnancy test and germ cell tumor marker","Only blood test metabolic pathway process","Pregnancy only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoassay for pregnancy test and germ cell tumor marker', 'hCG beta-subunit: urine (dipstick) serum (ELISA/CLIA). Pregnancy: detectable 10-14 days after conception. Choriocarcinoma testicular germ cell tumors. Point-of-care pregnancy tests widely available.'),
('24cf6aa0-2898-522a-a535-f213f4fc60b6'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Troponin I/T measurement:', '["Non-specific metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Low sensitivity metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No clinical use metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only in research metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","High-sensitivity cardiac troponin immunoassay for myocardial infarction diagnosis"]'::jsonb, 'High-sensitivity cardiac troponin immunoassay for myocardial infarction diagnosis', 'High-sensitivity (hs) troponin assays detect levels 10-100x lower. 99th percentile URL. Early diagnosis MI. 0/1-hour algorithm for rapid rule-in/rule-out. Essential for acute coronary syndrome management.'),
('7f944c5b-f82c-5ffa-9715-6dc76133d7ee'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'BNP/NT-proBNP measurement:', '["Immunoassay for heart failure diagnosis and prognosis","For MI only metabolic pathway process","For arrhythmia only metabolic pathway process","For hypertension only metabolic pathway process","For valve disease only metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoassay for heart failure diagnosis and prognosis', 'BNP (B-type natriuretic peptide) and NT-proBNP. Ventricular stretch releases. <100 pg/mL (BNP) excludes HF in dyspnea. >400 pg/mL suggests HF. Prognostic marker. Guides therapy.'),
('ea08d07f-0429-5199-b552-0fc628611a2e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'C-reactive protein (CRP):', '["Not inflammatory metabolic pathway process","No clinical use metabolic pathway process","Acute phase reactant measured by immunoturbidimetry or high-sensitivity CRP","Liver function test metabolic pathway process","Only chronic metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Acute phase reactant measured by immunoturbidimetry or high-sensitivity CRP', 'CRP: produced by liver (IL-6). Rapid rise (6-8 h). hs-CRP <1 mg/L low 1-3 moderate >3 high cardiovascular risk. Clinical: infection monitoring inflammation CVD risk.'),
('fe13eac5-927e-5e2b-9c32-cdbf31469999'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Procalcitonin (PCT):', '["Marker of bacterial infection guiding antibiotic therapy","Viral infection marker metabolic pathway process","No clinical use metabolic pathway process","Only in sepsis metabolic pathway process","Not specific metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Marker of bacterial infection guiding antibiotic therapy', 'PCT: produced by C-cells (bacterial infection). <0.25 ng/mL bacterial infection unlikely. >0.5 ng/mL suggests bacterial infection. >2 ng/mL severe sepsis. Guides antibiotic initiation/discontinuation.'),
('8408377a-ec40-5f2e-beb5-dfd1ea7498af'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'D-dimer testing:', '["Fibrin degradation product measured by immunoassay for DIC/VTE exclusion","For MI metabolic pathway process","For liver disease metabolic pathway process","For bleeding metabolic pathway process","For stroke metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Fibrin degradation product measured by immunoassay for DIC/VTE exclusion', 'D-dimer: cross-linked fibrin degradation product. Sensitive but not specific for VTE. <0.5 mg/mL (age-adjusted) excludes DVT/PE. Also elevated in DIC pregnancy post-surgery. Age-adjusted cutoff for >50 years.'),
('a5d8111d-c4ef-52fe-844b-6fe3a395a496'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Interleukin-6 (IL-6):', '["Cytokine measured by immunoassay for inflammatory conditions and cytokine release syndrome","Only research metabolic pathway process","Only in autoimmune diseases metabolic pathway process","No assay metabolic pathway process","Not clinical metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Cytokine measured by immunoassay for inflammatory conditions and cytokine release syndrome', 'IL-6 by ELISA/CLIA. Elevated in inflammation sepsis CRS (CAR-T therapy). Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6R) treats CRS. Prognostic marker in COVID-19.'),
('05a93c2a-04a8-5c80-b3ba-fe99f66f6777'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Ferritin measurement:', '["Not for iron metabolic pathway process","For infection only metabolic pathway process","Only in anemia metabolic pathway process","Immunoassay reflecting iron stores","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoassay reflecting iron stores', 'Ferritin: acute phase reactant. Low <30 ng/mL indicates iron deficiency. High >200 (F) >300 (M) suggests iron overload inflammation liver disease. Hyperferritinemia: hemochromatosis Still disease HLH.'),
('627654e7-ba94-5e83-9681-5cbf172936e8'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Vitamin B12 and folate measurement:', '["Only B12 metabolic pathway process","No clinical relevance metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Competitive immunoassays / chemiluminescence for deficiency diagnosis","Only folate metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Competitive immunoassays / chemiluminescence for deficiency diagnosis', 'B12 (competitive binding assay/electrochemiluminescence) normal >300 pg/mL. B12 <200 deficient. Folate (RBC folate more reliable). MMA and homocysteine are functional markers for B12 deficiency.'),
('7fa92845-11c0-564f-b71d-acfd0c1042f0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Immunoassay for thyroid function:', '["TSH FT4 FT3 measured by chemiluminescence immunoassay (CLIA) for thyroid disorders","Only TSH metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only T4 metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not for thyroid metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'TSH FT4 FT3 measured by chemiluminescence immunoassay (CLIA) for thyroid disorders', 'Thyroid tests: TSH (third-generation CLIA) is first-line. FT4 FT3. Diagnose hypo/hyperthyroidism. TSH low + FT4 high = hyperthyroidism. TSH high + FT4 low = hypothyroidism. Monitoring therapy.'),
('81dd8b13-4019-538a-8762-d0cdd6743e75'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Cortisol measurement:', '["Morning serum cortisol by CLIA for adrenal insufficiency and Cushing syndrome","Not for adrenal metabolic pathway process","Only urine metabolic pathway process","No reference ranges metabolic pathway process","Not reliable metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Morning serum cortisol by CLIA for adrenal insufficiency and Cushing syndrome', 'Cortisol: diurnal variation (peak 8 am). <3 mcg/dL suggests adrenal insufficiency. >20 mcg/dL normal. Late-night salivary cortisol for Cushing screening. ACTH stimulation test confirms adrenal insufficiency.'),
('16e42bf4-e3b5-5997-8168-60cb8b1c7242'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Hemoglobin A1c methods:', '["HPLC (reference) immunoassay enzymatic capillary electrophoresis","All same method metabolic pathway process","Only one method metabolic pathway process","Not standardized metabolic pathway process","No quality control metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'HPLC (reference) immunoassay enzymatic capillary electrophoresis', 'HbA1c methods: NGSP certified. HPLC (Bio-Rad Tosoh) separates Hb species. Immunoassay (Roche Tina-quant). Enzymatic (Abbott). CE (Sebia). POCT (DCA Vantage). All standardized to DCCT.'),
('8dbbe7ad-fe8b-5258-8fcb-48243b7cc025'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'G6PD enzyme activity measurement:', '["Not for deficiency metabolic pathway process","No assay metabolic pathway process","Qualitative only metabolic pathway process","Quantitative spectrophotometric assay for G6PD deficiency diagnosis","Only in RBC metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Quantitative spectrophotometric assay for G6PD deficiency diagnosis', 'G6PD: NADPH production measured at 340 nm (rate assay). Normal 8-18 U/g Hb. Deficient <4. Test during steady state not during acute hemolysis. Females with intermediate activity require molecular testing.'),
('1e9eae03-2e57-5095-9b36-4cf75888c1a0'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Pyruvate kinase (PK) deficiency diagnosis:', '["Quantitative enzyme assay in RBCs","No enzyme assay metabolic pathway process","Only molecular metabolic pathway process","Not specific metabolic pathway process","Low PK activity metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Quantitative enzyme assay in RBCs', 'PK: NADH consumption measured at 340 nm (coupled assay). Normal ~15 U/g Hb. Deficient <60% normal. False increase with reticulocytosis. Molecular confirmation needed.'),
('c194ed7c-d70e-59df-a6b4-743eef52d42c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) assay:', '["Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","NADH production at 340 nm measures total LDH and isoenzymes","No isoenzymes metabolic pathway process","Only total metabolic pathway process","No clinical use metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'NADH production at 340 nm measures total LDH and isoenzymes', 'LDH: lactate → pyruvate NAD+ → NADH (340 nm). Total LDH elevation: MI hemolysis liver disease cancer. LDH isoenzymes (LDH1-5) by electrophoresis. LDH1 > LDH2 in MI (''flipped'').'),
('4c5e6468-7d1e-5b10-86a7-84fd55c57678'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) isoenzymes:', '["Not differentiated metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Liver only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No isoenzymes metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Bone only metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Electrophoresis or heat inactivation differentiates liver (heat stable) from bone (heat labile) ALP"]'::jsonb, 'Electrophoresis or heat inactivation differentiates liver (heat stable) from bone (heat labile) ALP', 'ALP isoenzymes: liver (band 1) bone (band 2) intestinal (band 3) placental (band 4). Heat stable: placental > liver > bone > intestinal. Used to determine source of elevated ALP.'),
('67e77186-0289-597f-ad8f-ff6871e8ef0e'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Creatine kinase (CK) isoenzyme analysis:', '["Same isoforms metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only CK-MB metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No isoenzymes metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not clinical metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","CK-MM (skeletal) CK-MB (cardiac) CK-BB (brain) by electrophoresis or immunoassay"]'::jsonb, 'CK-MM (skeletal) CK-MB (cardiac) CK-BB (brain) by electrophoresis or immunoassay', 'CK: total CK elevated in muscle damage. CK-MB mass (immunoassay) >5 ng/mL or CK-MB fraction >5-6% suggests MI. CK-MB/CK ratio. CK-MM in muscle injury. CK-BB in brain injury and cancer.'),
('9035bae1-d72d-59f3-bd9b-4d2512a250fe'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP):', '["Only one band metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No clinical use metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No separation metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Agarose gel or CE separates proteins into albumin alpha1 alpha2 beta gamma globulin","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Agarose gel or CE separates proteins into albumin alpha1 alpha2 beta gamma globulin', 'SPEP: densitometric scan shows fractions. Albumin (60%) alpha1 (alpha1-antitrypsin) alpha2 (haptoglobin) beta (transferrin) gamma (immunoglobulins). Monoclonal spike (M protein) in multiple myeloma.'),
('faf3111b-8143-5539-9229-69ef569482d9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Immunofixation electrophoresis (IFE):', '["Only total protein metabolic pathway process","No heavy/light chains metabolic pathway process","Not specific metabolic pathway process","No identification metabolic pathway process","Identifies specific immunoglobulins in monoclonal bands"]'::jsonb, 'Identifies specific immunoglobulins in monoclonal bands', 'IFE after SPEP detects monoclonal band: apply antisera against IgG IgA IgM kappa lambda. Identifies type (e.g. IgGκ). Used for multiple myeloma Waldenstrom MGUS diagnosis.'),
('efe61818-bfa1-571e-84df-2cab2ef69432'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Serum free light chain (FLC) assay:', '["Immunoassay quantifies free kappa and lambda light chains","Not for light chains metabolic pathway process","Only immunofixation metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process","Only urine metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Immunoassay quantifies free kappa and lambda light chains', 'FLC: kappa/lambda ratio abnormal. Involved/uninvolved ratio for monitoring. Diagnostic criterion for multiple myeloma (clonal FLC >100 mg/L). More sensitive than SPEP for light chain disease.'),
('4207dca6-a27c-5b3e-98a4-128806b4c2e9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Urine protein electrophoresis (UPEP):', '["Detects proteinuria","Same as SPEP","No protein","Not for myeloma","Only 24-hour"]'::jsonb, 'Detects proteinuria', 'UPEP: concentrated urine electrophoresis. Monoclonal light chains (Bence Jones) appear as spikes. Immunofixation confirms. 24-hour urine quantification. Part of myeloma workup.'),
('77c7d2df-7bf7-50a7-ac79-a6736980521d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Cryoglobulin detection:', '["Only room temp metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not serum metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only plasma metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No cold requirement metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Serum collected and maintained at 37°C; cold precipitable proteins indicate cryoglobulinemia"]'::jsonb, 'Serum collected and maintained at 37°C; cold precipitable proteins indicate cryoglobulinemia', 'Cryoglobulins: serum at 37°C precipitates at 4°C (cold). Type I (monoclonal IgM/IgG) in WM/myeloma. Type II/III (mixed) in HCV autoimmune disease. Measure cryocrit and immunofixation.'),
('f111f29c-2f34-5fd5-9b21-6fc00e4a47fa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Coombs test (direct antiglobulin test DAT):', '["In serum metabolic pathway process","Not in RBCs metabolic pathway process","Detects antibody or complement on RBC surface","In plasma metabolic pathway process","Not for hemolysis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detects antibody or complement on RBC surface', 'DAT: anti-human globulin (Coombs reagent) added to washed RBCs. Agglutination indicates bound IgG/complement. Positive in AIHA drug-induced HDN. Indirect (IAT) detects serum antibodies.'),
('e2f4ffe7-8611-53b3-a42d-d6e1e3820c76'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Rapid plasma reagin (RPR) test:', '["Only for HIV metabolic pathway process","No clinical use metabolic pathway process","Non-treponemal cardiolipin antigen test for syphilis screening","Treponemal-specific metabolic pathway process","Not for syphilis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Non-treponemal cardiolipin antigen test for syphilis screening', 'RPR: flocculation test detects anti-cardiolipin antibodies. Quantitated (titer). False positives: SLE anticardiolipin antibodies pregnancy. Confirm with treponemal tests (FTA-ABS TP-PA).'),
('1cd91192-f0d7-5f9f-a1f6-cc2f593ba763'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Tuberculin skin test (PPD/Mantoux):', '["Intradermal injection of purified protein derivative measures delayed hypersensitivity","No injection metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Blood test metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only chest X-ray metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not for TB metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Intradermal injection of purified protein derivative measures delayed hypersensitivity', 'PPD: 0.1 mL (5 TU) intradermal forearm. Read 48-72 h induration (not erythema). Positive ≥5 mm (HIV) ≥10 mm (risk factors) ≥15 mm (no risk). BCG vaccination causes false positive.'),
('cc345ca0-cf38-5b3f-90e5-7650ad7522d4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Interferon-gamma release assay (IGRA) for TB:', '["No blood draw metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Whole blood assay measuring IFN-gamma release after TB antigen stimulation","Not for TB metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only in HIV metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Same as PPD metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Whole blood assay measuring IFN-gamma release after TB antigen stimulation', 'IGRA: QuantiFERON-TB Gold Plus T-SPOT.TB. Blood incubated with TB-specific antigens (ESAT-6 CFP-10). IFN-gamma measured by ELISA/ELISPOT. No BCG cross-reactivity. Preferred for BCG-vaccinated.'),
('7c4e72ef-4aca-5825-b180-b12070674a74'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Autoantibody detection by immunofluorescence:', '["Not for autoantibodies metabolic pathway process","Only in CSF metabolic pathway process","Patient serum on tissue sections (HEp-2 cells) detects autoantibodies","No fluorescence metabolic pathway process","Only ELISA metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Patient serum on tissue sections (HEp-2 cells) detects autoantibodies', 'Indirect immunofluorescence (IIF): HEp-2 cells + patient serum + anti-human IgG-FITC. Pattern: homogeneous (anti-dsDNA) speckled (anti-ENA) nucleolar (anti-Scl-70) centromere (limited scleroderma).'),
('913fe1be-2e4a-5413-889a-cc98e749cefa'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Anti-dsDNA antibody detection:', '["No specific test metabolic pathway process","Not for SLE metabolic pathway process","Only ANA metabolic pathway process","ELISA or immunofluorescence (Crithidia luciliae) for SLE diagnosis","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'ELISA or immunofluorescence (Crithidia luciliae) for SLE diagnosis', 'Anti-dsDNA: specific for SLE (~70%). ELISA (quantitative) Crithidia (IIF kinetoplast). Correlates with disease activity (especially lupus nephritis). High specificity for SLE diagnosis.'),
('65a0a32a-b3cc-5d17-97e5-1f0303f0e937'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Anti-extractable nuclear antigen (ENA) panel:', '["No clinical use metabolic pathway process","Not specific metabolic pathway process","All same pattern metabolic pathway process","Only ANA positive metabolic pathway process","Anti-Ro/SSA La/SSB Sm RNP Scl-70 Jo-1 by ELISA or line immunoassay"]'::jsonb, 'Anti-Ro/SSA La/SSB Sm RNP Scl-70 Jo-1 by ELISA or line immunoassay', 'ENA: anti-Sm (SLE-specific) anti-RNP (MCTD) anti-Ro (SLE Sjogren neonatal lupus) anti-La (Sjogren) anti-Scl-70 (diffuse scleroderma) anti-Jo-1 (polymyositis).'),
('3fc6e9b9-6bf3-52b3-abe6-efad4fedc974'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Anti-CCP (cyclic citrullinated peptide) antibody:', '["Low specificity metabolic pathway process","Same as RF metabolic pathway process","Not for RA metabolic pathway process","No clinical use metabolic pathway process","Highly specific serologic marker for rheumatoid arthritis"]'::jsonb, 'Highly specific serologic marker for rheumatoid arthritis', 'Anti-CCP: sensitivity ~70% specificity >95% for RA. Present early before disease onset. More specific than rheumatoid factor (RF). Predicts erosive disease. Used in RA diagnostic criteria.'),
('c6f62881-ed8d-524b-b9e4-ef28c0a2a7e9'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'HLA-B27 testing:', '["Flow cytometry or PCR for ankylosing spondylitis and related spondyloarthropathies","Only by serology metabolic pathway process","No clinical relevance metabolic pathway process","Only for transplant metabolic pathway process","Not for spondylitis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Flow cytometry or PCR for ankylosing spondylitis and related spondyloarthropathies', 'HLA-B27: strong association with ankylosing spondylitis (90%) reactive arthritis psoriatic arthritis uveitis. Detected by flow cytometry (monoclonal antibody) or PCR (SSP SSO).'),
('7823aa65-ca90-54d1-9233-a691c6a23e50'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'HLA typing for transplantation:', '["Molecular typing (PCR-SSO PCR-SSP NGS) for HLA-A B C DR DQ matching","Serology only metabolic pathway process","Not needed metabolic pathway process","Only race metabolic pathway process","No typing metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Molecular typing (PCR-SSO PCR-SSP NGS) for HLA-A B C DR DQ matching', 'HLA typing: high-resolution NGS is gold standard. Class I (A B C) Class II (DRB1 DQB1 DPB1). Matching improves transplant survival. Also for HLA antibody screening (PRA).'),
('1fa7bced-cf11-5bbc-82b0-445dac78eac7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'NGS-based HLA typing:', '["Intermediate","Full biochemical reaction","Low resolution","No advantage","Only for class I"]'::jsonb, 'Full biochemical reaction', 'NGS: phased sequencing of exons 2-3 (Class I) and exon 2 (Class II). Resolves ambiguous combinations. Detect novel alleles. Cost decreasing. Becoming the standard for unrelated donor registries.'),
('c2d63509-657e-5619-a699-a357b451b02a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'PRA (panel reactive antibody) testing:', '["No clinical use metabolic pathway process","Detects anti-HLA antibodies in transplant candidates","Only crossmatch metabolic pathway process","No antibodies metabolic pathway process","Not for transplant metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Detects anti-HLA antibodies in transplant candidates', 'PRA: patient serum tested against 100+ HLA antigens. Single antigen bead (Luminex) identifies specific antibodies. %PRA predicts crossmatch positivity. Used for organ allocation and desensitization.'),
('7ff44e4d-096b-5f49-ab67-57bced51e05c'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Complement assays:', '["CH50 (classical pathway) AP50 (alternative) and individual complement components","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No function metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only individual metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only CH50 metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'CH50 (classical pathway) AP50 (alternative) and individual complement components', 'CH50: sheep RBC + antibody + patient serum. Hemolytic assay measures classical pathway function. Low in SLE (C3 C4 consumption) complement deficiencies. AP50 for alternative pathway.'),
('7551972c-bf50-51dd-bbe1-1baadc13afa7'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A middle-aged female is found to have elevated cholesterol on routine screening. Lipid profile is ordered. Antiphospholipid antibody testing:', '["Lupus anticoagulant anticardiolipin and anti-beta2 glycoprotein I antibodies","Only one test metabolic pathway process","No clinical syndrome metabolic pathway process","Only in pregnancy metabolic pathway process","Not for thrombosis metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Lupus anticoagulant anticardiolipin and anti-beta2 glycoprotein I antibodies', 'Antiphospholipid syndrome: LA (functional clotting assay) aCL (ELISA) anti-beta2GPI (ELISA). Persistent positivity required. Associated with arterial/venous thrombosis and pregnancy loss.'),
('ad5047d7-4aa8-5140-a565-4e217546646d'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Lupus anticoagulant (LA) detection:', '["Only PTT metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No confirmation metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","DRVVT (dilute Russell viper venom time) and PTT-based assays with mixing and confirmation","Not specific metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only one test metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'DRVVT (dilute Russell viper venom time) and PTT-based assays with mixing and confirmation', 'LA: screening test prolonged (DRVVT or PTT). Mixing with normal plasma does NOT correct (inhibitor). Confirmation with excess phospholipid shortens. Two tests required (DRVVT + PTT-LA).'),
('7f09a423-a1e9-5e99-b3e7-be4955014857'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Factor assays (coagulation):', '["PTT-based (factors VIII IX XI XII) or PT-based (factor VII X V II) one-stage assays","No specific metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","All same metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Any factor metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not quantitative metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'PTT-based (factors VIII IX XI XII) or PT-based (factor VII X V II) one-stage assays', 'Factor activity: patient plasma mixed with factor-deficient plasma. Clot time compared to standard curve. Diagnose hemophilia A (VIII) B (IX) factor XI deficiency. Also factor inhibitors.'),
('153b05ea-36c4-5c22-8628-5e6420fdf344'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Bethesda assay for factor inhibitors:', '["No quantification metabolic pathway process","Only in PT metabolic pathway process","Only in TT metabolic pathway process","Not needed metabolic pathway process","Measures inhibitor titer (Bethesda units) neutralizing factor VIII or IX"]'::jsonb, 'Measures inhibitor titer (Bethesda units) neutralizing factor VIII or IX', 'Bethesda: mixture of patient and normal plasma incubated. Residual factor activity measured. 1 BU = 50% inhibition. Detects acquired hemophilia A (autoantibodies) and congenital hemophilia inhibitors.'),
('4bbd6586-aa53-5c88-8ce8-9cef9d97702a'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Thromboelastography (TEG) / Rotational thromboelastometry (ROTEM):', '["Only PT/PTT metabolic pathway process","Not point-of-care metabolic pathway process","Not global metabolic pathway process","Not for bleeding metabolic pathway process","Whole blood viscoelastic test of clot formation strength and lysis"]'::jsonb, 'Whole blood viscoelastic test of clot formation strength and lysis', 'TEG/ROTEM: measures R time (initiation) K angle (kinetics) MA/MCF (strength) LY30 (lysis). Guides transfusion: FFP (prolonged R) platelets (low MA) cryo (low angle). Point-of-care in surgery/trauma.'),
('47176155-7b81-55c4-9626-e5daaa6ab9f4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with dysmorphic features. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. A family presents for genetic counseling after the birth of a child with intellectual disability. Genetic testing is performed. Genetic testing for BRCA1/BRCA2:', '["No actionable metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not hereditary metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","No preventive metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis for hereditary breast/ovarian cancer","Only one gene metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Sequencing and deletion/duplication analysis for hereditary breast/ovarian cancer', 'BRCA testing: NGS panel (BRCA1 BRCA2 plus others). Sanger confirmation. Detection of large rearrangements (MLPA). Positive: lifetime cancer risks guide surveillance risk-reducing surgery and PARP inhibitors.'),
('9af754d3-6583-5b93-b67a-f44c8794fc53'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Lynch syndrome (HNPCC) testing:', '["Only IHC metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Not for Lynch metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","MSI (microsatellite instability) IHC (mismatch repair proteins) and MLH1 MSH2 etc sequencing","No specific metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process","Only MSI metabolic pathway process metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'MSI (microsatellite instability) IHC (mismatch repair proteins) and MLH1 MSH2 etc sequencing', 'Lynch: tumor MSI testing (Bethesda panel 5 markers). IHC for MLH1 MSH2 MSH6 PMS2 (loss). Germline sequencing. MSI-high/MMR deficient tumors respond to immune checkpoint inhibitors.'),
('f546d7c9-52b8-52ac-b8a2-151bdebc1eb4'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Prenatal screening (first-trimester):', '["No screening metabolic pathway process","Second trimester only metabolic pathway process","Only serum metabolic pathway process","Only ultrasound metabolic pathway process","Combined nuchal translucency (NT) PAPP-A and free beta-hCG"]'::jsonb, 'Combined nuchal translucency (NT) PAPP-A and free beta-hCG', 'First-trimester screen: NT (11-14 weeks) + maternal serum PAPP-A and free beta-hCG. Detection rate ~85% for trisomy 21 (5% FPR). Combined with second-trimester quadruple screen (sequential/integrated).'),
('ec979b98-04b8-5186-b741-01bd6043b0ce'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Non-invasive prenatal testing (NIPT):', '["No DNA metabolic pathway process","No screening metabolic pathway process","Not for trisomy metabolic pathway process","Cell-free fetal DNA from maternal blood analyzed by NGS for aneuploidy","Invasive metabolic pathway process"]'::jsonb, 'Cell-free fetal DNA from maternal blood analyzed by NGS for aneuploidy', 'NIPT: massively parallel sequencing of cfDNA (fetal fraction >4%). Trisomy 21 detection >99% (<0.1% FPR). Sex chromosome aneuploidy and microdeletions. Screening test (not diagnostic).'),
('a411a1b5-c707-5e7e-bdd6-eba21e18f561'::uuid, 'Biochemistry (Paper 1)', 'Biomedical Diagnostic Techniques', 'Prenatal diagnosis by amniocentesis:', '["Not diagnostic metabolic pathway process","Not for chromosomes metabolic pathway process","2nd trimester only metabolic pathway process","Only maternal metabolic pathway process","Amniotic fluid cells (15-20 weeks) for karyotype FISH and microarray"]'::jsonb, 'Amniotic fluid cells (15-20 weeks) for karyotype FISH and microarray', 'Amniocentesis: 20 mL fluid at 15-20 weeks. Cells cultured for karyotype (aneuploidy). FISH for rapid (24 h) aneuploidy. Chromosomal microarray (CMA) detects microdeletions/duplications. Risk of miscarriage ~0.1%.');
